7:T492,Against a malicious receiver the question is settled: if both sets first pass
through a Diffie-Hellman oblivious PRF keyed by the sender, a simulator reads
the receiver's set off its random-oracle queries, and communication stays
\( O(|Y|\log|X|) \) (PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR; [security against a malicious
receiver with privacy against the sender](PSI-OP-002)). Against a malicious
sender the same protocol gives only privacy: the receiver's messages reveal
nothing, but the sender still decides what the receiver outputs, for instance
by returning encryptions of zero so that the receiver accepts its whole set
(PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR, §7.2). Full simulation needs an effective input
extracted from the sender, and the usual tool, zero-knowledge proofs as in
earlier malicious PSI, costs communication linear in \( |X| \); the sender
must also be held to the prescribed homomorphic computation. Making the sender
return, for each match, an encryption of a hash of the item's PRF value
confines it to [removing matches as a function of the receiver's other
items](PSI-OP-004), but only under a heuristic assumption that leveled
homomorphic encryption cannot evaluate the hash.8:T504,With threshold FHE the bound is met: PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR tests the
intersection size homomorphically and then computes the intersection with
threshold additively homomorphic encryption ([the threshold-FHE
case](PSI-OP-006)). Because that second phase needs no FHE, [a cardinality
test of the same cost without FHE](PSI-OP-010) would suffice, but
PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR builds a test from threshold additively homomorphic
encryption only for its other functionality, which releases the intersection
when all elements outside it together number at most \( t \), a stricter
condition. For [two parties](PSI-OP-007) the question is settled.
PSI-PAPER-2023-GS reaches \( O(\lambda^2\ell t\,\mathrm{polylog}\,t) \) bits
from oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption by splitting the
sets into buckets and running a quadratic cardinality test on small
thresholds, but in a model where each broadcast message counts once
([the broadcast case](PSI-OP-009)). It does not state the cost over
point-to-point channels, where the \( \Omega(\ell t) \) bound holds and each
broadcast becomes \( \ell - 1 \) private messages, and it does not call its
protocol optimal. With a broadcast channel the known lower bound is only
\( \Omega(t\log\ell + \ell) \) (PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR, Appendix C).9:T43f,PSI-PAPER-2019-GS reduces two-party threshold PSI to a private intersection
cardinality test followed by an intersection step that already costs
\( \tilde O(t) \) (Theorem 4 and the following paragraph). Its own test, a
singularity test of a Hankel matrix built from the parties' polynomial
encodings, cost \( \tilde O(t^2) \). PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR observes that the
Hankel structure reduces the test to the half-GCD problem, solvable in
quasi-linear time, so that the parties can obtain additive shares of the
matrix and test it with Yao's garbled circuits or additively homomorphic
encryption at quasi-linear cost (§3.3); its second phase needs only additively
homomorphic encryption for two parties (§7.1). The paper states that this
gives two-party threshold PSI with communication \( \tilde O(t) \) from
additively homomorphic encryption and settles the question of
PSI-PAPER-2019-GS (abstract; §1.2), with \( \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \) factors omitted.
PSI-PAPER-2023-GS credits the bound \( O(\lambda t\,\mathrm{polylog}\,t) \)
without FHE to this combination (footnote 5, p. 4).a:T499,PSI-PAPER-2023-GS assumes pairwise private channels and a unit-cost broadcast
channel, against a static passive adversary corrupting all but one party
(§2.1). Each party hashes its set into \( t \) buckets, independently
\( \epsilon \) times for a statistical parameter \( \epsilon = O(\lambda) \);
in every bucket the parties obtain secret shares of the bucket's exact
intersection size when it is within a logarithmic threshold of full, and of a
default value otherwise, and a final secure computation checks whether one of
the \( \epsilon \) sums of these shares shows a large enough intersection
(Theorem 7). The bucket test is built from a quadratic cardinality test of
earlier work run at logarithmic thresholds (Theorem 8), which the paper states
can be rewritten over generic secure computation at
\( O(\lambda\ell\,\mathrm{poly}(t)) \). The result is an \( \ell \)-party
cardinality test and, with the multiparty intersection step that
PSI-PAPER-2019-GS sketches without a theorem (§8.2 there), passively secure
threshold PSI with
\( O(\epsilon^2\ell t\,\mathrm{polylog}\,t) \) bits from oblivious transfer or
additively homomorphic encryption (Corollaries 5 and 6).b:T40d,Protocols secure against both parties are balanced: their communication is at
least 128 bits per item of either set (PSI-PAPER-2022-RR), so a lookup against
\( 10^9 \) users would move at least 16 GB, and the largest measured run has
\( 2^{24} \) items per party. The protocols built for contact discovery give
up security against the server. Precomputed OPRF PSI on a phone checks 1,024
contacts against \( 2^{28} \) users in 2.92 s over WiFi and 6.53 s over LTE
after a one-time 1,072 MiB download, is secure only against a malicious
client, and its authors state that Signal's requirements are not met
(PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW). FHE-based unbalanced PSI keeps communication small but
only keeps the server from learning the client's items, not from changing the
client's output (PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR), and for 2,048 contacts against
\( 2^{28} \) users its sender needs 4.75 s on 24 threads
(PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG). Full simulation against a malicious sender with
communication sublinear in its set is itself open
([PSI-OP-001](PSI-OP-001)).c:T50a,The paper does not use the terms "set intersection" or "PSI". It calls the task private preference matching: the basic function takes two lists of yes/no answers and a threshold and outputs whether the number of matching answers reaches the threshold (§3, p. 81). The protocol it gives in Appendix A instead lets each party count the matches, and the paper says that this leaks the number of matches rather than the one-bit threshold decision, as a trade-off for efficiency (§3, p. 81). Revealing which items are shared appears only as a variation in which the last two lists are not permuted (Appendix A, p. 85). Later papers describe this protocol as the classic Diffie-Hellman PSI protocol (for example PSI-PAPER-2021-RT, §1, p. 1); that description is theirs. The paper states no adversary model and no security argument for the matching protocol. Its assumptions appear only as context: a prime \( p \) with a large prime factor \( q \) of \( p - 1 \) in which discrete logarithms are believed hard (Appendix A, p. 85), and a hash function whose output "cannot be easily influenced or predicted" (§2, p. 79). It adds that a zero-knowledge proof could be incorporated so that active cheating other than strategic choice of inputs is detected, without giving one (Appendix A, p. 85).d:T665,The paper lists its contributions on p. 2. PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI: set intersection from oblivious evaluation of an encrypted polynomial whose roots are the client's items, semi-honest, standard model (§4.1, p. 5; Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6; Lemmas 1-3, p. 8). PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-BALANCED-ALLOCATION-HASHING: balanced-allocation hashing into low-degree bin polynomials, which reduces the server's exponentiations to \( O(k_C + k_S \ln \ln k_C) \) (§4.2, pp. 6-8). PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE: protocols against a malicious client (cut-and-choose, standard model) and a malicious server (random oracle model) (§5, pp. 9-13). PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD: variants that output the intersection size or a threshold decision (§4.4-4.5, p. 9). PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-APPROX-LOWER-BOUND: an \( \Omega(N) \) communication lower bound for multiplicatively approximating the intersection size (§6, pp. 13-14). PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE: a sketched multi-party protocol for semi-honest parties (§7, pp. 15-17). PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OT-REDUCTION: private matching implies oblivious transfer, so it has no black-box reduction from one-way functions (§3.1, pp. 3-4). Not carded separately: the sampling-based private approximation protocol Intersect-Approx, whose expected number of sampling invocations matches the lower bound for distinguishing intersection sizes (§6, pp. 14-15); it is described in PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-APPROX-LOWER-BOUND. Not carded, outside the topic: fuzzy matching and fuzzy search, including a 2-out-of-3 fuzzy matching protocol (§8, p. 17). Fuzzy PSI is excluded by SCOPE.md.e:T442,PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF: the oblivious programmable PRF, its three single-query constructions (polynomial, garbled Bloom filter, table), and the hashing-based extension to many points and queries (§§3-4, pp. 4-10). PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-SYMMETRIC-KEY-MPSI: multiparty PSI against any number of colluding semi-honest parties from conditional zero-sharing and conditional reconstruction over OPPRFs (§5, pp. 10-13; Theorem 1, p. 12). PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING: the augmented semi-honest variant with zero-sharing from pairwise PRF seeds (§6.1, pp. 14-15; Theorem 2, p. 14). Not yet recorded: the optimized three-party protocol with three sequential OPPRF executions instead of four (§6.3, pp. 16-17; Figure 10, p. 16), described in PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-3PARTY; the stash-free cuckoo-hashing variant with three primary and two supplementary hash functions (§4, p. 9; Appendix B, pp. 24-25), which §7.2 (p. 19) reports as 168 ms against 211 ms for two-party PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT at \( 2^{12} \) items; and the hint-size optimizations of §6.2 (pp. 15-16).f:T42b,PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION: malicious security for the OT-based PSI paradigm of Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner by running oblivious encodings in both directions and comparing common encodings, with simple hashing, phasing, and aggregation of encodings across bins (§5.1-5.2, pp. 7-8; §6.1-6.3, pp. 10-15; Figure 4, p. 12; Theorem 2, p. 13). PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-ENCODE-COMMIT: the encode-commit variant, in which Alice commits to her items and sends masked decommitments (§5.3, pp. 8-10; §6.4-6.5, pp. 15-17; Theorem 3, p. 16; Figure 9, p. 27). Not carded separately: the oblivious encoding functionality F_encode and its two instantiations (§4, Figure 2, pp. 5-7); its programmable-random-oracle instantiation is the 1-out-of-N OT extension of Orrù, Orsini, and Scholl. Not carded separately: the experimental comparison with De Cristofaro, Kim, and Tsudik, with Rindal and Rosulek (EUROCRYPT 2017), and with the semi-honest protocol of Kolesnikov et al. (§7, Figures 6-8, pp. 18-22); its figures are recorded in the construction rows.10:T4e9,PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER: OPRF pre-processing before the FHE intersection, giving security against a malicious receiver and removing noise flooding (§4, pp. 8-11; Theorems 1-2, pp. 15-16). PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS: arbitrary-length items through SIMD slots over extension fields and a lazy encoding algorithm (§3, pp. 7-8; Table 1, p. 9). PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI: labeled PSI with communication sublinear in the sender's set from label-interpolation polynomials (§5, pp. 11-12; Figures 1-2, pp. 13-14). PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LEAKY-MALICIOUS-SENDER: security against a malicious sender up to a sender-chosen leakage function, from hash-valued labels and an H-limited leveled FHE assumption (§7.3, pp. 17-18; Appendix A, pp. 25-26). Not recorded separately: PSI with computation, which returns additive secret shares of the per-bin results and labels for a further secure computation with communication \( O(|Y| \log |X| + |f|) \), described without a security theorem (§6, pp. 13-15); the symmetric-key BFV variant that nearly halves fresh ciphertexts (§8.1, p. 18). The complete protocols are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR (unlabeled), PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-LABELED, and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-MAL-LEAKAGE.11:T531,The paper lists its contributions in §1.3 (pp. 5-6) and its additions to the conference versions in §1.4 (pp. 6-7). PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-BIT-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-OT-PSI: the OT-based PSI protocol with per-bin OPRFs from 1-out-of-N random OT over a fixed 512-bit BCH code, whose communication no longer depends on item bit length (§2.2.3, p. 11; §5, Protocol 3, pp. 21-23). The construction is PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSZ-OT. PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-HASHING-PARAMETERS: concrete parameters for simple and cuckoo hashing in PSI (§3, pp. 12-18). Not carded here because they first appeared in the conference papers, which are separate identities without full text in this release: permutation-based hashing for PSI (§3.3, pp. 17-18; from PSI-PAPER-2015-PSSZ, whose title names it); circuit-based PSI with the sort-compare-shuffle circuit on GMW and the pairwise-comparison circuit with hashing (§4.1-4.2, pp. 18-20). Not carded: the description and benchmark of the circuit-based OPRF protocol of Pinkas, Schneider, Smart, and Williams (§4.3, pp. 20-21, marked ADDED), which the paper finds close to server-aided PSI in run-time for unequal set sizes (§6.2.3, p. 30; Table 12, p. 30); and the same-platform comparison of naive hashing, server-aided, public-key, circuit-based, and OT-based protocols (§6, Tables 8-13, pp. 24-31).12:T558,The paper optimizes two precomputed unbalanced PSI protocols of Kiss, Liu, Schneider, Asokan, and Pinkas (PoPETs 2017): GC-PSI, an OPRF evaluated by garbled circuits, and NR-PSI, an oblivious evaluation of the Naor-Reingold PRF (§1.1, p. 2; §3.5, p. 6). PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION: correlated-OT precomputation that halves online OT communication (§4.3, p. 8; §4.4, p. 10). PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION: a compressed cuckoo-filter encoding of the server set, smaller updates, and realistic false-positive parameters (§4.1, pp. 6-7). PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-LOWMC-OPRF: LowMC parameters for the garbled-circuit OPRF with 8.2 times fewer AND gates than AES-128 (§4.2, pp. 7-8). PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION: native ARMv8 implementations with security against malicious clients and their measurement (§4.5, pp. 10-11; §§5-6, pp. 11-14). Not recorded separately: the survey of contact discovery in twelve mobile messengers (§1.2, p. 3; Table 1, p. 3); the proposed combinations with multi-server PIR and with FHE-based PSI, a dedicated filter server, and database partitioning, none of them implemented (§4.6, p. 11; Appendix A, pp. 18-19); the Signal prototype (Appendix C, p. 19). The complete protocols are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-KRSSW-GC (Figure 1, p. 9) and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-KRSSW-NR (Figure 2, p. 10).13:T45e,PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES: two multiparty threshold PSI functionalities, equal for two parties and different for more (§1.1, p. 2; §2.3, p. 6; Figures 1-4). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-LOWER-BOUND: \( \Omega(nT) \) communication for both functionalities in point-to-point networks, and \( \Omega(T \log n + n) \) for the first in the broadcast model (§4, pp. 15-17; Appendix C, pp. 40-42). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND: matching \( O(nT) \) protocols for both functionalities from threshold FHE (§5, pp. 17-23; §7, pp. 28-30; Appendix B, pp. 39-41). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI: an \( \tilde O(nT) \) protocol for the second functionality from threshold additively homomorphic encryption, whose two-party case gives \( \tilde O(T) \) from additively homomorphic encryption (§6, pp. 24-28; §7, pp. 28-30; §1.2, p. 4). Not listed as separate contributions: the use of repeated threshold PSI with \( T = 1, 2, 4, \dots \) to obtain multiparty PSI with communication independent of the set sizes, and the observation that the protocols are compact MPC in the plain model (§1.2, p. 4).14:T817,All protocols are semi-honest with up to \( n - 1 \) corruptions and run in a star network (§1.1, p. 3; §2.3, p. 6). Upper bounds omit a \( \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \) factor and \( \tilde O \) hides polylogarithmic factors (footnote 1, p. 3). Whether the first functionality admits a protocol from assumptions weaker than threshold FHE is marked unknown (Table 1, p. 3). On the claim to settle the open problem of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS: the abstract says that, as a direct implication, the paper designs a two-party protocol with communication \( \tilde O(T) \) from assumptions weaker than FHE (p. 1); §1.2 says the case \( n = 2 \) gives \( \tilde O(T) \) from additively homomorphic encryption, referring to §§6-7 (p. 4); §3.3 attributes the gain to testing Hankel-matrix singularity through the half-GCD connection of Brent, Gustavson, and Yun, quasi-linear instead of the quadratic protocol of Kiltz et al. (p. 13). In the two-party case the singularity test is instantiated with Yao's garbled circuits at communication \( \tilde O(k \cdot \mathrm{poly}(\lambda)) \) for a \( (k + 1) \times (k + 1) \) Hankel matrix (§6.1, p. 26), and the intersection step uses additively homomorphic encryption alone with communication \( O(T \cdot \mathrm{poly}(\lambda)) \) (§7.1, p. 29). The later paper of Ghosh and Simkin, PSI-PAPER-2023-GS (ePrint 2023/919, PDF dated 2023-06-12), accepts the two-party result: it states that communication \( O(\lambda t\,\mathrm{polylog}\,t) \) without fully homomorphic encryption follows from the construction of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS combined with an observation of Badrinarayanan et al., and that its own two-party protocol does not improve on this (paper outline and footnote 5, p. 4). For more than two parties it notes that this paper's quasilinear protocol without FHE additionally requires fewer than \( t \) distinct elements outside the intersection among all sets (§1, pp. 1-2), and it counts this paper among the works that leave asymptotically optimal multiparty threshold PSI from assumptions other than FHE open (§1, p. 2).15:T479,PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER: evaluation of each bin's intersection polynomial with \( O(\sqrt{B}) \) ciphertext-ciphertext multiplications (§3.1, pp. 7-9; Appendix A). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES: extremal postage-stamp bases in place of windowing for the receiver's encrypted powers (§3.1, pp. 10-11). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS-POWERS: depth-free Frobenius powers giving communication nearly constant in the larger set (§3.1, pp. 11-12; §5.4, pp. 18-20). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS: arbitrary-length items and labels over prime-field SIMD slots, with OPRF hashing made mandatory (§3.1, p. 7; §4.2, pp. 13-14). Not recorded separately: a hash-to-curve map for the FourQ curve used by the OMGDH-based OPRF, measured at about 12,000 cycles (§3.1, p. 12); the partitioning of very large sender sets into several protocol runs (§5.2, p. 18); and the security sketch for the labeled case (§4.2, p. 14). The complete protocols are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG (unlabeled, SEAL), PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG-LABELED (labeled, SEAL), and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS (HElib proof of concept).16:T52b,PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS: the definition of oblivious key-value stores, with linear, binary, and overfitting notions (§2, pp. 5-7), and the catalog of existing instances (§3, pp. 7-9). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT: the three-hash garbled cuckoo table (§4.1, pp. 9-11). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION: replication and star architectures for amplifying OKVS correctness, with a concrete instantiation (§5, pp. 13-18). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-LINEAR-OKVS-VOLE: PSI from any linear OKVS and vector OLE (§7.1, pp. 20-22). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-MALICIOUS-MULTIPARTY: malicious security for the zero-sharing multiparty PSI of Kolesnikov et al. (§7.2, pp. 21-22; Appendix B, pp. 29-39). Not separately recorded: the OKVS from simple hashing into bins with dense matrices and its sharper failure analysis (§4.2, pp. 11-12); the observation that OKVS can replace polynomials in sparse OT extension, OPPRF, and covert computation (§6.1, §6.2, §6.4, pp. 18-20); the benchmarks of OKVS constructions (Appendix A.2, pp. 28-29). The complete protocols are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-3HGCT, PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-STAR, PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-VOLE, and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MULTIPARTY. The semi-honest PaXoS-PSI rows with the new encodings in Table 1 (p. 24) are not recorded as separate rows.17:T467,PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER: a transformation from any private intersection cardinality test with secret-shared output and communication \( O(\lambda \ell\,\mathrm{poly}(t)) \) to one with communication \( O(\lambda^{2} \ell t\,\mathrm{polylog}\,t) \), by random partitioning into \( t \) buckets (abstract, p. 1; §1.2, p. 2; §§3-4, pp. 6-16). PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY-QUASILINEAR: passively secure \( \ell \)-party threshold PSI, releasing the intersection when it has at least \( n - t \) elements, with communication \( O(\epsilon^{2} \ell t\,\mathrm{polylog}\,t) \) bits from oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption (Corollaries 5-6, p. 16). Not listed as separate contributions: the two-party instantiation (Corollaries 2-3, p. 10), which the authors say does not improve the two-party state of the art asymptotically (p. 4), and the use of threshold PSI with thresholds \( 2, 2^{2}, 2^{4}, \dots \) to compute plain PSI with communication sublinear in the set sizes for large intersections (§1.1, p. 2). The two-party instantiation is the comparison row PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2023-GS-TWO-PARTY.18:T471,Security is against a static passive adversary corrupting all but one party, with pairwise authenticated private channels and a broadcast channel of unit cost (§2.1, p. 4). The partitioning uses random functions, which the authors say may be replaced by pseudorandom functions with a key known to all parties (footnote 4, p. 3). The authors consider the protocols too inefficient for most real-world parameters (§1.2, p. 3). On the earlier claim of PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR: the paper states that two-party threshold PSI with communication \( O(\lambda t\,\mathrm{polylog}\,t) \) can be obtained without fully homomorphic encryption from the construction of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS combined with an observation of Badrinarayanan et al., and that its own two-party protocol does not improve on this (p. 4 and footnote 5). Its abstract sentence that current protocols rely on FHE or depend at least quadratically on \( t \) concerns the \( \ell \)-party setting (abstract, p. 1); for \( \ell > 2 \) it notes that Badrinarayanan et al. additionally require fewer than \( t \) distinct elements outside the intersection among all sets (§1, pp. 1-2).19:T5ca,The paper covers multiparty PSI in which only the leader \( P_1 \) learns the intersection, at most \( t \) parties collude, and no external party helps; it excludes server-aided protocols and treats only the semi-honest model, noting whether malicious versions exist (§1, pp. 1-2; Definition 1, p. 2). It defines size-hiding and size-revealing privacy and exact protocols with error at most \( 2^{-40} \) (Definitions 2-4, p. 3), and notes that augmented semi-honest security neither implies nor follows from semi-honest security, while malicious security implies it (§2.3, pp. 3-4). Network topologies are star, mesh, wheel, and tree (§3.1, p. 4). It sorts all protocols into three constructions (§4, pp. 5-9): private homomorphic set representations (bitsets, hash sets, sorted multisets, polynomial roots), leaky homomorphic set representations (Bloom filters and garbled Bloom filters), and aggregatable membership queries (payloads in polynomial roots, oblivious programmable PRFs, oblivious key-value stores). Table 2 (p. 10) lists the protocols without known attacks in these classes, with communication, computation, rounds, topology, collusion resistance, and whether a malicious version exists; PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT and PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY appear under aggregatable membership queries. Section 6 (p. 12) collects recurring flaws: revealing a Bloom filter of the intersection, randomness contributed by only one party, and difficulties of moving to the malicious model.1a:T5b0,The receiver cuckoo-hashes its items into m bins with three hash functions and no stash, and the sender inserts each of its items into all three candidate bins of a simple hash table with bin size B (Figure 2, p. 8). Permutation-based hashing stores only part of each item, and items longer than \( 2\log_2(N_x + N_y) + \lambda - 1 \) bits are first hashed to that length (§4.2, pp. 7-9). The receiver batches its table into \( m/n \) plaintexts of the FV scheme, encrypts the powers \( y^{i \cdot 2^{\ell j}} \) of each slot value, and sends them (Figure 4, step 5, p. 12). The sender, holding precomputed coefficients of the polynomial with roots in each partition of each bin, computes encryptions of all needed powers and a plaintext-ciphertext dot product per partition, optionally switches the results to a smaller modulus, and returns \( \alpha \cdot m/n \) ciphertexts (Figure 4, steps 4 and 6, p. 12). A slot decrypts to zero exactly when the receiver's item lies in the sender's bin (§3, pp. 5-6). Windowing raises the receiver-to-sender communication by a factor \( (2^{\ell} - 1)(\lfloor \log_2(B)/\ell \rfloor + 1) \) to lower the depth, and partitioning multiplies the reply by α (§4.3, pp. 9-10). Modulus switching reduces reply ciphertexts to about 15-20% of their size (§4.4, p. 10). The paper gives the concrete communication as about \( 1.5C\sigma N_y \log_2 N_x \) bits, with C a small ciphertext-expansion constant (§6.3, p. 17).1b:T43d,For a programmable PRF built from a PRF F, the sender masks each programmed value \( y_i \) with \( F(k, x_i) \) and publishes the masked points in a structure that the receiver can evaluate anywhere: the coefficients of an interpolated polynomial p, so that the output on q is \( F(k, q) \oplus p(q) \) (§3.2, p. 6); a garbled Bloom filter (§3.3, pp. 6-7); or, for one query, a table T of size the next power of two above n together with a nonce v chosen so that the positions \( H(F(k, x_i) \| v) \) are distinct (§3.4, pp. 7-9). When the \( y_i \) are random the hint is uniform, so it hides which points were programmed (§§3.2-3.4). To handle many queries the receiver cuckoo-hashes its items into bins with three primary and two supplementary hash functions and no stash, the sender maps every item under all five functions, and the parties run one single-query OPPRF per bin (§4, pp. 9-10; Figure 7, p. 11). The polynomial and garbled Bloom filter constructions use no random oracle in their description; the table construction models H as a random oracle (Figure 6, p. 8).1c:T422,Each party \( P_i \) chooses, for each of its items x, random strings \( s^{i,1}, \ldots, s^{i,n} \) that XOR to zero and runs a multi-query OPPRF with every other party \( P_j \) as sender programmed on \( (x, s^{i,j}) \); \( P_j \) queries its own set (Figure 8, steps 1-2, p. 12). Each party XORs its own share with the outputs it received into a value \( S_i(x) \), and in the second phase every \( P_i \) with \( i > 1 \) programs an OPPRF to P1 on \( (x, S_i(x)) \); P1 announces its items x with \( S_1(x) \) equal to the XOR of the received outputs (steps 3-5, p. 12). If some party lacks x, at least one term is pseudorandom and x is excluded except with probability \( 2^{-\lambda} \) (§5.3, p. 12). The proof of Theorem 1 argues that a party without x leaves the honest parties' values for x independent of the coalition's view, and that programming an OPPRF on a random value is indistinguishable from not programming it (pp. 12-13). All OPPRF instances of a phase run in parallel, so the protocol has a constant number of rounds (§5.3, p. 13).1d:T40c,Both parties act as receiver of an oblivious encoding for every position of every bin, so each obtains one-sided encodings of its own items; for each of her items Alice sends the µ common encodings that combine her encoding under Bob's instance with her encodings under Alice's instances for the positions of the item's bin, and Bob outputs his items whose own common encodings appear. With simple hashing into \( m = O(n / \log n) \) bins of \( \mu = O(\log n) \) positions, phasing to shorten items, and Alice's encodings aggregated across bins, the protocol UC-realizes PSI with output to Bob against malicious adversaries in the \( F_{\mathrm{encode}} \)-hybrid model at cost \( O(Cn \log n) \), where \( C \approx \kappa \) is the cost of one oblivious encoding. \( F_{\mathrm{encode}} \) is realized in the programmable random oracle model by 1-out-of-N OT extension, or in the standard model by σ oblivious transfers per encoding; a corrupt party's set may hold up to \( m\mu \) items, fewer than \( 4n \) for \( n = 2^{20} \).1e:T40c,Both parties act as receiver of an oblivious encoding for every position of every bin, so each obtains one-sided encodings of its own items; for each of her items Alice sends the µ common encodings that combine her encoding under Bob's instance with her encodings under Alice's instances for the positions of the item's bin, and Bob outputs his items whose own common encodings appear. With simple hashing into \( m = O(n / \log n) \) bins of \( \mu = O(\log n) \) positions, phasing to shorten items, and Alice's encodings aggregated across bins, the protocol UC-realizes PSI with output to Bob against malicious adversaries in the \( F_{\mathrm{encode}} \)-hybrid model at cost \( O(Cn \log n) \), where \( C \approx \kappa \) is the cost of one oblivious encoding. \( F_{\mathrm{encode}} \) is realized in the programmable random oracle model by 1-out-of-N OT extension, or in the standard model by σ oblivious transfers per encoding; a corrupt party's set may hold up to \( m\mu \) items, fewer than \( 4n \) for \( n = 2^{20} \).1f:T485,The table has m main positions and \( d + \lambda \) extra positions. A key x has two main positions \( h_1(x), h_2(x) \) and a random extra vector r(x); its value is the XOR of the two main positions and of the extra positions selected by r(x) (Figure 6, p. 19). Encoding builds the cuckoo graph whose edges are the keys, solves the linear constraints of the edges in its 2-core with Gaussian elimination using the extra positions, picks a root value for every tree component, and then sets each remaining position while traversing edges directed away from the 2-core or the root (Figure 6, steps 1-5, p. 19). The rows of a cycle are linearly dependent in the main part, which is why the random extra part is needed; with at most d cycles the kernel has dimension at most d, and \( d + \lambda \) random columns make the system solvable except with probability \( 2^{-\lambda} \) (Theorem 8 and proof, p. 20). The DFS variant instead adds, for each back edge, one constraint on the extra positions equal to the XOR of the values on that cycle (Appendix B, pp. 29-31). This is the encoding used in PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI's implementation.20:T555,The OOS functionality gives the sender a secret s and strings \( q_i \), and the receiver \( r_i = q_i \oplus C(d_i) \wedge s \) for its choice strings \( d_i \), where C is a linear code of minimum distance at least κ (§2, pp. 4-5). If the receiver's choice strings form a PaXoS D (PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-PAXOS) encoding \( y \mapsto H_1(y) \), then decoding R at x equals decoding Q at x XOR \( C(\mathrm{Decode}(D, x)) \wedge s \), so the two parties obtain the same string for every x in the receiver's set (§4.1, pp. 9-10). For any other x, the difference \( C(\mathrm{Decode}(D, x) \oplus H_1(x)) \) is a nonzero codeword, and the hashed value looks random by the code-correlation robustness of a random oracle (Lemma 1, p. 4; Lemma 5, Hybrid 4, p. 13). The sender sends one hash per item, randomly permuted, and the receiver outputs the items whose own hash appears (Figure 3, p. 11). Semi-honest security needs only H1, H2 outputs of \( \lambda + 2 \log n \) bits; the malicious instantiation lengthens H1 to bound the receiver's effective set (PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND) and sets H2 to \( 2\kappa \) bits to bound the sender's (§4.4, pp. 14-16). The implementation uses the garbled cuckoo table of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO; the complete configurations are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-SH and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-MAL.21:T439,A PaXoS is given by a probe function v that maps each string to an m-bit vector (Definition 3, p. 6). To encode pairs \( (x_i, y_i) \), the encoder forms the matrix whose rows are \( v(x_i) \) and solves for \( D = (d_1, \ldots, d_m) \) with \( M \cdot D = (y_1, \ldots, y_n) \); to decode x, one XORs the positions \( d_j \) with \( v(x)_j = 1 \) (§3.2, pp. 6-7). Decoding can be applied to any string, not only to encoded keys. Because decoding is linear over \( \mathrm{GF}(2) \), it commutes with XOR of two encodings, with any linear map applied position by position, and with bitwise AND by a fixed string (§3.3, p. 7); these are the properties that the PSI protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI combines with the outputs of OT extension. The paper measures a PaXoS by its rate \( n/m \), its encoding cost, and its decoding cost (§3.4, pp. 7-8), and lists random matrices and garbled Bloom filters as instances (§3.5, p. 8). The construction of the same paper that achieves constant rate with linear-time encoding is PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO.22:T463,In vector OLE the sender holds a secret s and a vector Q, and the receiver holds its chosen vector D and R with \( q_i = r_i \oplus d_i \cdot s \), where \( \cdot \) is multiplication in \( \mathrm{GF}(2^{\ell_1}) \) (Figure 3, step 1, p. 22). If D is a linear OKVS (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS) that decodes to \( H_1(y) \) on the receiver's set, decoding commutes with the correlation, so for such y the receiver's \( \mathrm{Decode}(R, y) \) equals the sender's \( \mathrm{Decode}(Q, y) \oplus H_1(y) \cdot s \), while for other x the two differ by a nonzero multiple of s (§7.1, pp. 20-21). The sender hashes its values with the item and sends them permuted; the receiver outputs the items whose hash appears (Figure 3, steps 2-3, p. 22). Compared with PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI, the code that made the AND-based correlation hide s disappears, and any linear OKVS can be used (§7.1, p. 21). The paper also observes that the VOLE-based OPRF of Rindal and Schoppmann, PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF, can use any OKVS in place of PaXoS (§6.2, p. 19). The complete row is PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-VOLE.23:T4cc,Every pair of parties shares a PRF key, from which each party derives a share \( sh_i(h) \) of zero for any item h, with the shares of all parties summing to zero (Figure 5, step 1, p. 39). P0 learns \( \mathrm{PRF}_i(h) \) for its items from the OPRF with \( P_i \), and \( P_i \) sends an OKVS that decodes, on each of its items, to the oracle hash of the PRF value plus its zero share; P0 builds its own share in the same way and keeps the items on which all decoded values sum to zero (Figure 5, steps 2-4, p. 39). The only change from Kolesnikov et al.'s augmented semi-honest protocol is the oracle hash around the PRF value, which the simulator for a corrupt P0 or corrupt non-central parties uses to extract inputs (Appendix B.2, p. 30; B.4, pp. 31-33). Hazay and Lindell's example of a protocol that is malicious-secure but not semi-honest-secure explains the apparent paradox: the semi-honest simulator may not change a corrupt party's input, while the malicious one may (Appendix B.6, pp. 36-37). The paper compares communication \( O(n^2 \kappa + nm(\kappa + \lambda + \log m)) + o(nm) \) and four rounds with other multiparty protocols in Table 4 (p. 36). The complete row is PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MULTIPARTY.24:T472,An OKVS encodes a list of key-value pairs into a data structure S from which Decode recovers the value of any encoded key; Decode can be called on any key, and when the values are random, S reveals nothing about which keys were encoded (Definitions 1-2, p. 5). A linear OKVS over a field F stores a vector \( S \in F^m \) and decodes by an inner product with a key-dependent vector, so encoding solves a linear system, and choosing a uniform solution gives obliviousness automatically (Definition 3, p. 5; §2.2, p. 6). A binary OKVS restricts the key vectors to 0/1 entries; over \( \mathrm{GF}(2^{\ell}) \) decoding is an XOR of positions and commutes with bitwise AND, and this is exactly the PaXoS of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-PAXOS (§2.3, p. 6). For malicious protocols, the overfitting game measures how many pairs (k, H(k)) an adversary can fit into an encoding sized for n pairs (Definition 4, pp. 6-7); the information-theoretic bound of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND applies to any key-value store (Lemma 5, p. 7). The paper measures an OKVS by rate, encoding time, and single and batch decoding time (§2.5, p. 7).25:T491,The OPPRF lets the sender program values \( z_i \) at points \( y_i \): the parties run an OPRF on the receiver's points, and the sender sends an XoPaXoS encoding P with \( \mathrm{Decode}(P, y_i) = z_i - F_k(y_i) \), so the receiver's output \( F_k(x) + \mathrm{Decode}(P, x) \) equals \( z_i \) at \( x = y_i \) and is random elsewhere (§5, p. 21; Figure 8, p. 38). Security against the receiver needs P to reveal nothing about the sender's points outside the intersection, which plain PaXoS does not guarantee because it fixes some positions to zero; XoPaXoS samples those positions at random (§2, pp. 10-11; Theorem 5.1 proof, pp. 21-22). In the circuit-PSI protocol the sender programs, for every bin and every item y hashed there with function j, the point \( H(y, j) \) to a random bin value \( r_i \); the receiver queries the item in its cuckoo bin and obtains \( r_i \) exactly when that item is in the sender's set, and a two-party circuit then outputs shares of the equality bit and of the associated values (§6, pp. 23-24; Figure 10, pp. 38-39). The OPRF underneath is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF. The complete row is PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-CIRCUIT.26:T439,The random VOLE functionality gives the sender Δ and B and the receiver \( A' \) and \( C = A'\Delta + B \) (Figure 2, p. 14). The receiver encodes its set with the PaXoS solver so that the encoding P decodes to \( H_F(x) \) on its items (§3.2, p. 14), and derandomizes the VOLE by sending \( A = P + A' \) (Figure 4, steps 2-4, p. 36). For x in the receiver's set, linearity gives \( \mathrm{Decode}(K, x) - \Delta H_F(x) = \mathrm{Decode}(C, x) \); for other x the difference is a nonzero multiple of the uniform Δ, so the value is unpredictable to the receiver (§3.2, p. 15; Lemma 3.3, Hybrid 4, p. 18). The sender commits to its half of the offset w before the receiver's message (Figure 4, steps 1 and 5, p. 36), and x is hashed together with the value to extract a malicious sender's queries (§3.2, p. 15). The PaXoS solver is the one of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO with \( 2.4n \) main positions (§2, p. 9). With the transformation of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS this gives the PSI rows PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-SH and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-MAL.27:T55e,The encoder builds the matrix H whose rows are \( \mathrm{row}(k_i) \) and looks for row and column permutations that put it in the form \( \begin{bmatrix} A & B & C \\ D & E & F \end{bmatrix} \), with F a large lower-triangular sparse block and B, E the dense columns (§2, pp. 5-6). After eliminating C, only a g-row system on the dense columns remains; if it has full row rank, which holds with probability \( 1 - 2^{-\lambda} \) for λ extra binary columns (Lemma 1, p. 8) or with overwhelming probability for Vandermonde-style columns over a large field (Lemma 2, p. 8), the solution follows by back-substitution (§2, p. 6). For \( w = 2 \) the rows that can be triangulated form the independent sets of a graphic matroid, so the greedy choice minimizes the gap (Lemmas 3-4, pp. 11-12); duplicate rows, which occur with probability \( O(n^{2-w}) \), are the main failure mode and motivate \( w \ge 3 \) (Lemma 6, p. 13; §3.2, p. 16). An exhaustive variant searches subsets of the 2-core and is optimal when the gap is bounded (Figure 10, p. 30; Lemma 10, p. 20). The construction improves on PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT and replaces the amplification of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION by direct parameter choice; §5.1 (p. 24) argues that the empirical 2-core bound used there overstates security by a few bits (for example \( \lambda = 27 \) rather than 29).28:T594,Regev (2005) gave a quantum reduction from the worst-case approximate lattice
problems GapSVP and SIVP to LWE with approximation factor
\( \tilde O(n/\alpha) \). Peikert (2009) gave a classical reduction from
GapSVP for exponential modulus \( q \ge 2^{n/2} \), and Brakerski, Langlois,
Peikert, Regev and Stehlé (2013) extended classical hardness to polynomial
modulus by trading dimension for modulus.

The best known attacks are lattice reduction (primal and dual attacks with
BKZ), combinatorial BKW-type algorithms, and hybrid attacks; all take time
\( 2^{\Theta(n)} \) for the parameters used in practice. Concrete security is
estimated with the lattice estimator introduced by Albrecht, Player and Scott
(2015) and its later versions; the authors of that paper note that its cost
models have since been superseded. Arora and Ge (2011) solve LWE in time
\( 2^{\tilde O(n^{2\varepsilon})} \) when \( \alpha q = n^{\varepsilon} \) with
\( \varepsilon < 1/2 \), and in polynomial time when the error takes only a few
known values and many samples are available; this rules out very narrow or
few-valued error distributions, not the regime with \( \alpha q > 2\sqrt n \).

In April 2024 Chen posted a claimed polynomial-time quantum algorithm for LWE
with certain polynomial approximation factors (ePrint 2024/555); on 18 April
2024 the author reported a bug in a step of the algorithm and stated that the
claim does not hold.29:T42b,Two parties hold sets \(X\) and \(Y\) and want \(X\cap Y\) without revealing
anything about the items outside it (PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ). Usually one party,
the receiver, learns the intersection and the other, the sender, learns
nothing; the set sizes are typically public. With more parties, all of them
compute the intersection of their sets and learn nothing else beyond bounds on
the set sizes (PSI-PAPER-2018-EKR). Variants release only a function of the
intersection: its size, a sum of values attached to its items, a decision on
whether it is large, or secret shares for a further secure computation. Generic
secure computation can compute PSI, and garbled-circuit PSI ran as fast as the
public-key protocols of its time, or faster at higher security levels, at much
higher bandwidth (PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK); protocols that exploit the structure of
the problem are more efficient (PSI-PAPER-2018-EKR). Applications include
private contact discovery from a phone (PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR,
PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW) and aggregate ad-conversion statistics
(PSI-PAPER-2020-ION).2a:Ta46,- **Adversaries**: semi-honest parties follow the protocol; augmented
  semi-honest parties may change their inputs and then follow it
  (PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT); malicious parties deviate arbitrarily. Security can
  differ by party, as in protocols that are simulation-secure against a
  malicious receiver but only private against a malicious sender
  (PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR).
- **Set-size slack**: the fastest malicious protocols realize a relaxed
  functionality in which a corrupt party may effectively input more items than
  it declared, up to a constant factor (PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS,
  PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY, PSI-PAPER-2021-RT).
- **Balanced and unbalanced**: sets of similar size, or a small receiver set
  against a much larger sender set, where communication can be sublinear in
  the larger set (PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR).
- **Outputs**: the intersection; its cardinality; an intersection-sum, the sum
  of values attached to matching items (PSI-PAPER-2020-ION); threshold PSI,
  which releases the intersection only when the sets differ in few items
  (PSI-PAPER-2019-GS); circuit-PSI, secret shares of per-item results that feed
  a further secure computation (PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW, PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY); and
  labeled PSI, which returns labels the sender attaches to matching items
  (PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR).
- **Hashing to bins**: items are mapped into bins by simple hashing or by
  cuckoo hashing, which places each item in one of a few candidate bins,
  possibly with a stash, so that per-bin work is small; parameters are set
  for a hashing failure probability such as \(2^{-40}\) (PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ).
- **Oblivious PRF (OPRF)**: the receiver learns \(F_k(y)\) for its items while
  the sender holds \(k\) and sends \(F_k(x)\) for its own items, so the
  receiver compares values (PSI-PAPER-2018-EKR); many instances can be
  produced at once from OT extension (PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT).
- **Oblivious key-value store (OKVS)**: a data structure that encodes
  key-value pairs and hides the keys when the values are random; a polynomial
  through the pairs is the simplest one (PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY), and PaXoS is a
  binary one decoded by XOR (PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY).
- **Lower bounds**: computing the intersection needs communication linear in
  the set size, by the complexity of disjointness (PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP;
  PSI-PAPER-2019-GS); threshold PSI needs \(\Omega(t)\) for two parties
  (PSI-PAPER-2019-GS) and \(\Omega(\ell t)\) for \(\ell\) parties over
  point-to-point channels (PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR); and PSI implies oblivious
  transfer, so it has no black-box construction from one-way functions
  (PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP).2b:T876,- **Public-key-based**: commutative Diffie-Hellman exponentiation of hashed
  items (PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH), oblivious polynomial evaluation under additively
  homomorphic encryption (PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP), RSA blind signatures as the
  OPRF (PSI-PAPER-2010-DT), and PSI from any two-round key agreement with
  responses encoded in a polynomial, which suits small sets
  (PSI-PAPER-2021-RT).
- **OT- and VOLE-based**: OPRFs from OT extension combined with hashing to
  bins (PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ, PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT); malicious security by dual
  execution (PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS) and by encoding the receiver's set in a
  PaXoS, with linear communication (PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY); general OKVS and
  amplification of their failure probability (PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY); OPRFs
  from vector oblivious linear evaluation (VOLE) (PSI-PAPER-2021-RS); and
  subfield VOLE with a triangulation OKVS (PSI-PAPER-2022-RR).
- **Circuit-based and computing on the intersection**: a sort-compare-shuffle
  garbled circuit (PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK); hashing-based circuits with
  \(\omega(n)\) comparisons (PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW) and with \(O(n)\)
  communication through batched oblivious programmable PRFs (OPPRF)
  (PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY, PSI-PAPER-2021-RS); intersection-sum with cardinality
  from Diffie-Hellman double masking (PSI-PAPER-2020-ION); and threshold PSI
  from polynomial encodings and cardinality tests (PSI-PAPER-2019-GS,
  PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR, PSI-PAPER-2023-GS).
- **Unbalanced and labeled**: fully homomorphic encryption with batching, so
  that the sender evaluates the intersection on the receiver's encrypted set
  (PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR), with OPRF preprocessing and labels
  (PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR) and lower computation and communication
  (PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG); alternatively the server's OPRF-encrypted set is
  transferred once and queried online (PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW).
- **Multiparty**: zero-sharing delivered through OPPRFs, secure against any
  number of colluding semi-honest parties (PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT), malicious
  versions over OKVS (PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY, PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY), and protocols
  from homomorphic set representations surveyed in PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE.1:["$","$L6",null,{"catalog":{"catalogVersion":"psi-dossier-v1-seed","constructions":[{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"discrete_log","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-NOT-STATED-DISCRETE-LOG-SETTING-MODULO-A-PRIME-WITH-AN-UNPREDICTABLE-HASH","assumption_name":"not stated; discrete-log setting modulo a prime with an unpredictable hash","authors":["Bernardo A. Huberman","Matt Franklin","Tad Hogg"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["no-trusted-third-party","matching-count"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"four lists modulo p: \\( H(x_i)^a \\) for Alice's n items, \\( H(y_j)^b \\) for Bob's m items, then \\( H(y_j)^{ab} \\) and \\( H(x_i)^{ab} \\), each randomly permuted (Appendix A, steps 1-4, p. 85)","computation":"each party exponentiates its own n (or m) hashed items and the other party's m (or n) values with its secret exponent modulo p (Appendix A, steps 1-4, p. 85)"},"construction_family":"commutative_exponentiation","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-1999-HFH","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH"],"paper_title":"Enhancing Privacy and Trust in Electronic Communities","paper_url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/336992.337012","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quan