RPHIC-ENCRYPTION-SCHEME-PAILLIER-NAMED-AS-AN-INSTANCE","assumption_name":"semantic security of an additively homomorphic encryption scheme (Paillier named as an instance)","authors":["Michael J. Freedman","Kobbi Nissim","Benny Pinkas"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":[],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"as in PM-Semi-Honest; only the server's payload changes, to a fixed string \\( 0^{\\ell} \\) (§4.4, p. 9)","computation":"as in PM-Semi-Honest; the client counts the decryptions equal to \\( 0^{\\ell} \\) (§4.4, p. 9)"},"construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"paper_title":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","paper_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"PSI cardinality","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest client and server; the proof follows from that of PM-Semi-Honest (§4.4, p. 9)","output":["cardinality"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§3.1, p. 3; §4.4, p. 9)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"as in PM-Semi-Honest: one message from the client and one reply (§4.4, p. 9; Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"as in PM-Semi-Honest: the client generates the homomorphic key pair (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, step 1(a), p. 6)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest, standard model, by the proof of PM-Semi-Honest (§4.4, p. 9)","security_notion":"the client learns \\( |X \\cap Y| \\) only","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"The server encodes a fixed string instead of its item as the payload, so the client learns how many of its decryptions equal the string, that is the intersection size (§4.4, p. 9). The variant is part of PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD. The threshold variant of §4.5 (p. 9) adds an unspecified secure circuit evaluation and has no row.","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"additively_homomorphic","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-SEMANTICALLY-SECURE-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-ENCRYPTION-PSEUDORANDOM-FUNCTION-AND-COLLISION-RESISTANT-HASH","assumption_name":"semantically secure additively homomorphic encryption, pseudorandom function, and collision-resistant hash","authors":["Michael J. Freedman","Kobbi Nissim","Benny Pinkas"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":[],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"L times that of the semi-honest protocol (§5.1, p. 11)","computation":"L times that of the semi-honest protocol, for error probability exponentially small in L (§5.1, p. 11)"},"construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MAL-CLIENT","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"paper_title":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","paper_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["malicious","semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"malicious client (§5.1, pp. 10-11); the server's deviations are treated by a separate protocol (§5.2, p. 11), and a malicious server cannot affect the client's privacy here (footnote 11, p. 11)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§3.1, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"client sends L copies, server chooses \\( L/2 \\) to open, client opens them and sends the keys of the rest, server replies (Protocol PM-Malicious-Client, steps 1-4, p. 10)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"PRF keys for the balanced-allocation hash h and for F, with a collision-resistant hash G, all chosen by the client (Protocol PM-Malicious-Client, step 1, p. 10)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","cut_and_choose"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"malicious client, standard model (footnote 4, p. 2; §5, p. 10; proof sketch, p. 11)","security_notion":"private matching with the client's effective input bounded by \\( k_C \\) items","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"The cut-and-choose of Protocol PM-Malicious-Client (p. 10), from PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE, wraps the balanced-allocation protocol of PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-SH. The client learns an item only if it is a root of all \\( L/2 \\) evaluated copies, so a client with more than \\( k_C \\) roots is caught except with probability exponentially small in L (proof sketch, p. 11).","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"additively_homomorphic","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-SEMANTICALLY-SECURE-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-ENCRYPTION-AND-RANDOM-ORACLES","assumption_name":"semantically secure additively homomorphic encryption and random oracles","authors":["Michael J. Freedman","Kobbi Nissim","Benny Pinkas"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":[],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"the encrypted coefficients of a degree-\\( k_C \\) polynomial from the client and \\( k_S \\) pairs \\( (\\mathrm{Enc}(r' \\cdot P(y) + s), H2(r'', y)) \\) from the server (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, steps 1-2, p. 12)","computation":"for each received pair the client decrypts s, recomputes \\( r = H1(s) \\), and checks consistency with its items (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, step 3, p. 12)"},"construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MAL-SERVER","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"paper_title":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","paper_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["malicious","semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"malicious server (Lemma 4, p. 12); the server's privacy is preserved as in the semi-honest protocol, and a malicious client is treated by a separate protocol (§5.2, p. 11; §5.1, p. 10)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§3.1, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"one message from the client and one reply from the server (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, steps 1-2, p. 12)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"random oracles H1 and H2; the client generates the homomorphic key pair (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"malicious server, random oracle model (Lemma 4 and proof sketch, pp. 12-13)","security_notion":"private matching against a malicious server, with the server's input extracted from its random-oracle queries","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"For each item y the server picks a random s, sets \\( r = H1(s) \\), and uses r to fix all randomness of its homomorphic evaluation, returning \\( (\\mathrm{Enc}(r' \\cdot P(y) + s), H2(r'', y)) \\); the client decrypts s and accepts x if the pair is what an honest server would produce for x (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12). The mechanism is the server half of PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE.","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"additively_homomorphic","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-SEMANTIC-SECURITY-OF-AN-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-ENCRYPTION-SCHEME-PAILLIER-NAMED-AS-AN-INSTANCE","assumption_name":"semantic security of an additively homomorphic encryption scheme (Paillier named as an instance)","authors":["Michael J. Freedman","Kobbi Nissim","Benny Pinkas"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":[],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"each client posts the encrypted coefficients of a degree-k polynomial and \\( k(n - 1) \\) encrypted shares; the leader posts k tuples of \\( n - 1 \\) ciphertexts; each client posts k values (§7, steps 1-3, p. 16)","computation":"the leader evaluates each of the \\( n - 1 \\) encrypted polynomials on each of its k items; the paper says hashing to bins can improve this (§7, step 2, p. 16; p. 17)"},"construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"paper_title":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","paper_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"multiparty PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties; the protocol is sketched (§7, p. 15)","corruption_threshold":"not stated; for items outside the intersection the published XOR values look random to any coalition (§7, p. 16)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"every party checks the published row values against its own items (§7, step 4, p. 16)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"two rounds in which \\( P_1, \\ldots, P_{n-1} \\) submit data and one round in which \\( P_n \\) submits data (§7, p. 17)","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"each client has its own homomorphic key pair; data is posted to a public bulletin board or to the leader acting as one (§7, step 1, p. 16)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","secret_sharing"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest; no proof is given (§7, pp. 15-17)","security_notion":"each party learns the intersection of all n lists","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"Clients post encrypted polynomials and encrypted zero-XOR share matrices; the leader evaluates each client's polynomial on its items with XOR shares of the item as payloads; clients decrypt their columns and post row XORs; an item is in the intersection if a row's published values XOR to it (§7, steps 1-4, p. 16). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE.","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"additively_homomorphic","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-SEMANTIC-SECURITY-OF-AN-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-ENCRYPTION-SCHEME-PAILLIER-NAMED-AS-AN-INSTANCE","assumption_name":"semantic security of an additively homomorphic encryption scheme (Paillier named as an instance)","authors":["Michael J. Freedman","Kobbi Nissim","Benny Pinkas"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["payload-transfer"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-BALANCED-ALLOCATION-HASHING"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"\\( B \\cdot M \\) encrypted coefficients from the client, \\( O(k_C) \\) for \\( B = k_C / \\ln \\ln k_C \\), plus two ciphertexts per server item, one per candidate bin (§4.2, pp. 7-8)","computation":"server: for each item, M short exponentiations and one full-length exponentiation per candidate bin, \\( O(k_S + k_S \\ln \\ln k_C \\cdot n / \\lambda) \\) full-length exponentiations in total, dominated in practice by \\( k_S \\) full-length exponentiations; client: equal to the number of coefficients (§4.2, p. 8)"},"construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-SH","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"paper_title":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","paper_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest client and server (§3.2, p. 4; §4.3, pp. 8-9)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§3.1, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"one message from the client (encrypted bin polynomials and the bin mapping) and one reply from the server (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, steps 1-2, p. 6; §4.2, p. 7)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"the client generates the homomorphic key pair (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, step 1(a), p. 6) and chooses the balanced-allocation hash h (§4.2, p. 7); items are mapped to about \\( 2 \\log(\\max(k_C, k_S)) \\) bits by pairwise-independent hashing (§4.2, p. 6)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","balanced_allocation_hashing"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest, standard model (Lemmas 1-3, p. 8; §4.3, p. 9)","security_notion":"private matching: the client learns \\( X \\cap Y \\); client privacy by indistinguishability, server privacy by comparison with the ideal model","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"The client hashes her items into bins by balanced allocations, encodes each bin's items as roots of a degree-M polynomial padded with zero roots, and sends the encrypted coefficients; the server evaluates the two candidate bin polynomials for each item y and returns \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + y) \\), permuted (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6; §4.2, p. 7). Mechanisms: PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI and…","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"factoring","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-ONE-MORE-RSA-INVERSION-AND-RANDOM-ORACLES","assumption_name":"one-more RSA inversion and random oracles","authors":["Emiliano De Cristofaro","Gene Tsudik"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["server-precomputation","no-online-client-exponentiation"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(v + w) \\): v blinded values to the server, v signed values and w tags to the client (Figure 4, p. 9; Table 2, p. 11)","computation":"online, server \\( O(v) \\) 1024-bit exponentiations and client \\( O(v) \\) 1024-bit modular multiplications; precomputation, server \\( O(w) \\) 1024-bit exponentiations (RSA signatures) and client \\( O(v) \\) multiplications (§5.6, p. 9; Table 2, p. 11)","reported_measurement":"online computation 4,662 ms (server) and 1,422 ms (client) for 5,000 items per party, precomputation excluded, 160-bit items, 1024-bit modulus, ANSI C with OpenSSL on a 2.33 GHz Dell Precision PC with 8 GB RAM (Table 3 and §6.1, p. 11)"},"construction_family":"rsa_blind_signature","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2010-DT"],"paper_title":"Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Computational and Bandwidth Complexity","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"honest-but-curious client and server (§5.1, p. 6; Appendix B.3, p. 16)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only; mutual PSI by two one-way runs (§2, p. 2)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"online, one client message and one server reply (Figure 4, steps 3-5, p. 9)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"the server's RSA key \\( (n, e, d) \\), set up a priori by the server; offline, the server computes w RSA signatures and tags and the client computes its blinded values (Figure 4, steps 1-2, p. 9)","technique":["rsa_blind_signature"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"honest-but-curious, random oracle model, one-more-RSA (Appendix B.3, p. 16)","security_notion":"server privacy, client privacy, and client unlinkability; no server unlinkability","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"The server publishes hashes of RSA signatures on its hashed items; the client obtains signatures on its own hashed items by blinding, unblinds, hashes, and compares (Figure 4, p. 9). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA.","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","year":2010},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"discrete_log","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-ONE-MORE-GAP-DH-AND-RANDOM-ORACLES","assumption_name":"One-More-Gap-DH and random oracles","authors":["Emiliano De Cristofaro","Gene Tsudik"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["server-unlinkability","server-precomputation"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(v + w) \\): X and v values from the client; Z, v values, and w tags from the server (Figure 3, p. 8; Table 2, p. 11)","computation":"server \\( O(v) \\) 160-bit exponentiations online and \\( O(w) \\) 160-bit exponentiations precomputed, client \\( O(v) \\) 160-bit exponentiations, 1024-bit modulus (Table 2, p. 11; §5.5, p. 8)","reported_measurement":"online computation 2,072 ms (server) and 5,344 ms (client) for 5,000 items per party, precomputation excluded, on the platform of §6.1 (Table 3, p. 11)"},"construction_family":"prime_order_group_exponentiation","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2010-DT-FIG3","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2010-DT"],"paper_title":"Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Computational and Bandwidth Complexity","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"honest-but-curious client and server (§5.1, p. 6; Appendix B.2, p. 15)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§2, p. 2)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"one client message and one server reply (Figure 3, steps 2 and 4, p. 8)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"public p, q, and g generating a subgroup of order q of \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p^* \\), hash functions H and H' (Figure 3, p. 8); the server can precompute the values \\( (h_{sj})^{-R_s} \\) (§5.5, p. 8)","technique":["diffie_hellman"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"honest-but-curious, random oracle model; the server-privacy reduction to One-More-Gap-DH uses one additional DH-oracle query (Appendix B.2, pp. 15-16)","security_notion":"server privacy, client privacy, client and server unlinkability","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"The client sends \\( X = \\mathrm{PCH} \\cdot g^{R_c} \\) and \\( y_i = (\\mathrm{PCH}/h_{ci}) \\cdot g^{R_{c:i}} \\); the server returns \\( Z = g^{R_s} \\), the values \\( y_i^{R_s} \\), and tags \\( H'((X/h_{sj})^{R_s}) \\); the client unblinds each \\( y_i^{R_s} \\) with Z and compares (Figure 3, p. 8). The row is the PSI version described in PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI. The paper does not call this protocol Diffie-Hellman; the…","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","year":2010},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"discrete_log","assumption_id":"ASM-DDH","assumption_ids":["ASM-DDH","ASM-ROM"],"assumption_name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model","authors":["Yan Huang","David Evans","Jonathan Katz"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["small-universe"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM","PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 2^{\\sigma} \\) non-free gates, one AND per element of the universe (Table 1, p. 2; §3, p. 4); with size auditing \\( \\sigma \\cdot 2^{\\sigma} \\) non-free gates (§6, p. 11)","computation":"grows linearly in the universe size \\( 2^{\\sigma} \\) (§4, p. 5); the garbled-circuit part is 35 to 45% of the running time (§6, p. 11)","reported_measurement":"\\( \\sigma = 16 \\): 2.48 s without auditing and 12.22 s with auditing (§6, p. 11); faster than SCS-WN for \\( n > 40 \\) at \\( \\sigma = 12 \\) and \\( n > 500 \\) at \\( \\sigma = 16 \\) (§7.1 and Figure 8, p. 11); two Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 desktops, 100 Mbps LAN"},"construction_family":"garbled_circuit_bit_vector","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-BWA","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"],"paper_title":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","paper_url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties; because set sizes are not restricted, a dishonest party could input all 1s and learn the other set, so the protocol should not be used standalone (§3, pp. 4-5)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the output is the bit-vector of the intersection; either or both parties may learn it (§3, p. 4; §2, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"not_applicable","setup":"Naor-Pinkas base OTs with OT extension (§2, p. 4)","technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest two-party; no proof given (§2, p. 4)","security_notion":"PSI as a bit-vector intersection","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"The bit-vector protocol of §3 (pp. 4-5). Its cost does not depend on the set sizes, which is why set_size_regime is not applicable, and it is the fastest protocol of the paper for small element spaces (§7.1, p. 11). Its measurements belong to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM and its audited version to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING.","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"discrete_log","assumption_id":"ASM-DDH","assumption_ids":["ASM-DDH","ASM-ROM"],"assumption_name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model","authors":["Yan Huang","David Evans","Jonathan Katz"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["early-reveal"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"Equal circuits of \\( \\sigma - 1 \\) non-free gates, evaluated about \\( ((2n - \\hat{n})^{2} + \\hat{n})/4 \\) times for intersection size \\( \\hat{n} \\), giving \\( ((2n - \\hat{n})^{2} + \\hat{n})(\\sigma - 1)/4 \\) non-free gates (Table 1, p. 2; §4, p. 5)","computation":"worst case \\( \\Theta(n^{2}) \\) comparisons; early reveal saves about 75% of comparisons for identical sets, 45% when half the elements match, and 30% when a third match (§4, p. 5)","reported_measurement":"for 128 to 8192 elements of 32 bits and \\( \\hat{n}/n = 0.5 \\), SCS-WN was 10 to 70 times faster than PWC (§7.2, p. 12; Figure 10, p. 13); two Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 desktops, 100 Mbps LAN"},"construction_family":"garbled_circuit_pairwise_comparison","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-PWC","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"],"paper_title":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","paper_url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties (§1.1, p. 2; §2, p. 3)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"each match is revealed as soon as it is found (Algorithm 1, p. 5); either or both parties may learn the result (§2, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"each reveal adds a round; the implementation overlaps reveals with the comparisons in a second thread (§4, p. 5)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"each party randomly permutes its set before the protocol so that the order of revealed matches leaks nothing (§4, p. 5); Naor-Pinkas base OTs with OT extension (§2, p. 4)","technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest two-party; positional leakage of early reveal is removed by random permutation of the inputs (§4, p. 5); no proof given (§2, p. 4)","security_notion":"PSI (intersection revealed, nothing else)","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"The pairwise-comparison protocol of §4 (p. 5). The paper recommends it for large universes when n is small (§1.2, p. 2). Its measurements belong to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM.","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"discrete_log","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-DECISIONAL-DIFFIE-HELLMAN-FOR-THE-OBLIVIOUS-TRANSFER-IN-THE-RANDOM-ORACLE-MODEL-WITH-AN-UNNAMED-HOMOMORPHIC-ENCRYPTION-SCHEME","assumption_name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model, with an unnamed homomorphic encryption scheme","authors":["Yan Huang","David Evans","Jonathan Katz"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["linear-shuffle"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE","PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"garbled circuit of \\( 2\\sigma n \\log(2n) + ((3n - 1)\\sigma - n) + (\\sigma + 32)n \\) non-free gates (Table 1, p. 2), plus \\( 2n \\) ciphertexts in the shuffle (§5.3.2, p. 9)","computation":"\\( O(n) \\) public-key operations in the shuffle (§5.3.2, p. 9); the paper notes that for BWA and SCS-HE gate counts do not capture the full cost (§1.2, p. 3)","reported_measurement":"for 128 to 8192 elements of 32 bits, SCS-WN was more than 7 times faster, and SCS-HE saved no bandwidth relative to SCS-WN; two Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 desktops, 100 Mbps LAN (§7.2, p. 12; Figure 10, p. 13)"},"construction_family":"garbled_circuit_sort_compare_shuffle","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-HE","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"],"paper_title":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","paper_url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties (§1.1, p. 2; §2, p. 3)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"Alice receives the shuffled intersection elements; Bob receives nothing or, if desired, the sorted elements from Alice (Figure 6, p. 9)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"the shuffle uses two rounds, each sending n ciphertexts (§5.3.2, p. 9); the rounds of the garbled-circuit part are not stated","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"Alice's homomorphic key pair (Figure 6, p. 9); Naor-Pinkas base OTs with OT extension (§2, p. 4)","technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension","additively_homomorphic_encryption","sorting_network"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest two-party; no proof given (§2, p. 4); statistical masking \\( O(n \\cdot 2^{-k}) \\) in the secret-sharing step (§5.3.2, p. 8)","security_notion":"PSI (intersection revealed, nothing else)","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"This is the SCS-HE variant of §5.3.2 (pp. 8-9): the sort-compare-shuffle circuit of PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE up to filtering, followed by the homomorphic shuffle of Figure 6. The shuffle is linear in n but slower in practice than the Waksman network, and it gives up the goal of using only generic secure computation (§5.3.2, p. 9).","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"discrete_log","assumption_id":"ASM-DDH","assumption_ids":["ASM-DDH","ASM-ROM"],"assumption_name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model","authors":["Yan Huang","David Evans","Jonathan Katz"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["quasi-linear-circuit","generic-composition"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE","PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"garbled circuit of \\( 2\\sigma n \\log(2n) + ((3n - 1)\\sigma - n) + \\sigma(n \\log n - n + 1)/3 \\) non-free gates for two sets of n σ-bit elements (Table 1, p. 2), with free XOR and garbled-row reduction (§2, p. 4); measured bandwidth 147 to 470 MB for \\( n = 1024 \\) depending on the security level (§7.3, p. 13)","computation":"for \\( n = 1024 \\) and \\( \\sigma = 160 \\): \\( 3k \\) medium-exponent exponentiations, \\( 2k \\) modular inverses, \\( 2k \\) modular multiplications, and 18.34 million SHA calls, with k the OT-extension security parameter from 80 to 256 (Table 2, p. 13)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 1024 \\): 10.5 s (\\( \\sigma = 32 \\)) and 51.5 s (\\( \\sigma = 160 \\)) at the 80-bit level, 22.7 s and 122.7 s at the 256-bit level (Figure 11, p. 13); \\( n = 2^{20} \\), \\( \\sigma = 32 \\): about 6 hours for 1.7 billion non-free gates (§7.2, p. 12); two Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 desktops, 100 Mbps LAN, one core per party, setup excluded (§7, p. 11)"},"construction_family":"garbled_circuit_sort_compare_shuffle","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-WN","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"],"paper_title":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","paper_url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties; stronger threat models are left to future work (§1.1, p. 2; §2, p. 3)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the evaluator receives the n permuted outputs; if the generator should learn them too, the evaluator permutes or sorts them again and sends them back (§5.3.3, p. 9); either or both parties may learn the result (§2, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"each party sorts its set locally; Naor-Pinkas base OTs extended by OT extension, whose setup is a one-time cost per pair of parties (§2, p. 4; §7, p. 11); the circuit generator chooses a random permutation and configures the Waksman network locally (§5.3.3, pp. 9-10)","technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension","sorting_network"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest two-party; Yao's protocol with Naor-Pinkas OT and OT extension; the paper states provability from DDH in the random oracle model without a proof (§2, p. 4)","security_notion":"PSI (intersection revealed, nothing else)","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"This is the SCS-WN protocol of §5.3.3 (pp. 9-10), the sort-compare-shuffle circuit of PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE evaluated with Yao's garbled-circuit protocol. The Waksman network is about 15% of the cost of the sorting stage (§5.3.3, p. 10). Its measured comparison with custom PSI is PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM.","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"symmetric","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-HAMMING-CORRELATION-ROBUST-HASH-AND-PSEUDORANDOM-CODE-WITH-IDEAL-BASE-OTS","assumption_name":"Hamming correlation-robust hash and pseudorandom code, with ideal base OTs","authors":["Vladimir Kolesnikov","Ranjit Kumaresan","Mike Rosulek","Ni Trieu"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["item-length-independent-oprf-cost"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"\\( k(1.2n + s) + (3 + s)nv \\) bits for n items per party, excluding base OTs (Table 5, p. 15), with \\( k = 424\\text{-}448 \\) and \\( v = 56\\text{-}88 \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{8} \\) to \\( 2^{24} \\) (Table 2, p. 15)","computation":"k base OTs plus symmetric-key operations, with an AES-based pseudorandom code (§2, p. 6; §6.3, p. 16)","reported_measurement":"3,780 ms (LAN) and 7,455 ms (WAN) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and items of 32 to 128 bits, one thread per party on an Intel Xeon E5-2699 v3 (Table 3, p. 15; §6.2, p. 16)"},"construction_family":"oprf_cuckoo_hashing","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2016-KKRT","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT"],"paper_title":"Efficient Batched Oblivious PRF with Applications to Private Set Intersection","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/799","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"two-party PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest Alice and Bob (§1.1, p. 3; §5.2, p. 11)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"both parties: Bob sends the intersection to Alice (Figure 3, step 6, p. 13)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"k base OTs, which the implementation obtains by extending 128 Naor-Pinkas OTs with IKNP (§6.3, p. 16); BaRK-OPRF is proved with an ideal OT functionality (Figure 2, p. 10)","technique":["batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest two-party; BaRK-OPRF proved with ideal base OTs (Theorem 6, p. 9); PSI security argued in §5.2, p. 11","security_notion":"PSI with output to both parties","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"This is the protocol of Figure 3 (p. 13), recorded as PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI: the hashing paradigm of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner, with BaRK-OPRF (PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF) in place of their OPRF and with the hash index appended to each bin input so that Alice never evaluates the PRF twice on the same key and input (§5.2, pp. 11-12). Correctness fails only on a spurious PRF…","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","year":2016},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"lattice","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-IND-CPA-LEVELED-FHE-WITH-CIRCUIT-PRIVACY-FV-OVER-RING-LWE","assumption_name":"IND-CPA leveled FHE with circuit privacy (FV over Ring-LWE)","authors":["Hao Chen","Kim Laine","Peter Rindal"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","light-receiver-computation","offline-sender-preprocessing"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(N_y \\log N_x) \\) (p. 4); about \\( 1.5 C \\sigma N_y \\log_2 N_x \\) bits with C a small ciphertext-expansion constant (§6.3, p. 17); receiver to sender \\( (2^{\\ell} - 1)(\\lfloor \\log_2(B/\\alpha)/\\ell \\rfloor + 1) \\cdot m/n \\) ciphertexts and sender to receiver \\( \\alpha \\cdot m/n \\) ciphertexts (§4.3, p. 10; Figure 4, p. 12)","computation":"sender work linear in \\( N_x \\), with circuit depth \\( \\lceil \\log_2(\\lfloor \\log_2(B/\\alpha)/\\ell \\rfloor + 1) \\rceil + 1 \\); the receiver performs only encryptions and decryptions linear in \\( N_y \\) (p. 4; §4.3, p. 10)","reported_measurement":"\\( N_x = 2^{24} \\), \\( N_y = 5535 \\), \\( \\sigma = 32 \\), \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), SEAL8192-1, \\( \\alpha = 128 \\), \\( \\ell = 2 \\): sender pre-processing 71.2 s and sender online 36.1 s single-threaded, receiver encryption 0.3 s and decryption 2.4 s, communication 4.1 MB receiver to sender and 8.4 MB sender to receiver; one machine with two 18-core Intel Xeon E5-2699 v3, network simulated with tc (Tables 3-4, pp. 14-15; §6.1, p. 13; abstract, p. 1)"},"construction_family":"fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-CLR","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"],"paper_title":"Fast Private Set Intersection from Homomorphic Encryption","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/299","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"unbalanced PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest sender and receiver (§2.2, p. 3; Theorem 2, p. 11)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only; the sender outputs nothing (Figure 3, p. 11; Figure 4, p. 12)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"the receiver sends its encrypted powers and the sender replies with \\( \\alpha \\cdot m/n \\) ciphertexts (Figure 4, steps 5-6, p. 12); no round count is stated","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"the parties agree on hashing, FHE, window, and partition parameters, and the receiver generates the FHE key pair (Figure 1, step 1, p. 5; Figure 4, steps 1-3, p. 12); the sender's pre-processing (hashing, polynomial coefficients, NTT) runs offline without the receiver, but simulation-based security needs fresh hash functions in each execution (§6.1, p. 14)","technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest two-party, simulation-based (Theorem 2, p. 11)","security_notion":"PSI with output to the receiver only (FPSI, Figure 3)","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"This row is the full protocol of Figure 4 (p. 12), which realizes the contribution PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION. The sender partitions its simple hash table into α subtables, replaces each bin of each subtable by the coefficients of the polynomial whose roots are the bin's items, and batches the coefficients (step 4). The receiver batches its cuckoo table, computes the window powers of each batched…","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","year":2017},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"symmetric","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-PRF-AND-RANDOM-ORACLE-WITH-THE-OPPRF-INSTANTIATED-FROM-THE-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF","assumption_name":"PRF and random oracle, with the OPPRF instantiated from the KKRT batched OPRF","authors":["Vladimir Kolesnikov","Naor Matania","Benny Pinkas","Mike Rosulek","Ni Trieu"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["three-opprf-executions"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"three OPPRF executions against four for the general protocol of §5 (§6.3, p. 17)","computation":"symmetric-key work of three multi-query OPPRFs plus base OTs (§6.3, p. 17; §7, p. 17)","reported_measurement":"25.81 s total and 25.23 s online for \\( m = 2^{20} \\) on LAN, against 41.64 s for the general three-party protocol; 129.45 s on WAN (Table 4, p. 18; §7.2, pp. 19-20); simulated WAN with 96 ms RTT and 200 Mbps, same machine and parameters as the other rows (§7, p. 17)"},"construction_family":"opprf_incremental_chain","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-3PARTY","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT"],"paper_title":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"multiparty PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties, standard semi-honest model as the section title states (§6.3, p. 16); security is argued in prose for each colluding pair (§6.3, p. 17)","corruption_threshold":"three parties with \\( t \\in \\{1, 2\\} \\) (Table 4, p. 18); the protocol supports the full corrupted majority in the three-party case (§7.2, p. 19)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the leader P1 announces the intersection (Figure 10, step 4, p. 16)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"three OPPRF executions run in turn, each waiting for the previous one (§6.3, p. 17)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"ideal OPPRF functionality in Figure 10 (p. 16); 128 Naor-Pinkas base OTs in an offline phase (§7, p. 17); evaluated with the table-based OPPRF, which models H as a random oracle (§7.2, p. 18; Figure 6, p. 8)","technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"semi-honest, any two of three parties may collude; argued in prose, no theorem (§6.3, p. 17)","security_notion":"three-party PSI","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"The three parties compute the intersection incrementally: P2 obtains encodings of \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\), P3 of \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\cap X_3 \\), and P1 decodes (§6.3, p. 16). Each step is one multi-query OPPRF from PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF. Collusion of P1 and P2 reveals only \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\), which they can compute anyway; P2 and P3 learn nothing about P1's encodings; P1 and P3 learn the output (§6.3, p. 17).","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"update_model":null,"verification_cost":null,"work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","year":2017},{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"symmetric","assumption_id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-PRF-AND-RANDOM-ORACLE-WITH-THE-OPPRF-INSTANTIATED-FROM-THE-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF","assumption_name":"PRF and random oracle, with the OPPRF instantiated from the KKRT batched OPRF","authors":["Vladimir Kolesnikov","Naor Matania","Benny Pinkas","Mike Rosulek","Ni Trieu"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["symmetric-key-techniques","unconditional-zero-sharing"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING","PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF"],"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(n m \\lambda) \\) bits for the leader and \\( O(m \\lambda) \\) bits per client (Table 1, p. 3)","computation":"\\( O(n \\kappa) \\) exponentiations for the leader and \\( O(\\kappa) \\) per client, in an offline preprocessing phase (Table 1, p. 3); zero-sharing costs PRF evaluations only (Figure 9, p. 14)","reported_measurement":"21.93 s and 467.66 MB of client communication excluding base OTs for 5 parties with \\( m = 2^{20} \\) on LAN, table-based OPPRF, leader with up to 4 threads (Table 3, p. 18; §7.1, p. 17); 128-bit items, \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), 2x 36-core Intel Xeon 2.30 GHz with 256 GB RAM, simulated LAN with 0.02 ms RTT and 10 Gbps (§7, p. 17)"},"construction_family":"opprf_unconditional_zero_sharing","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-AUG","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":null,"nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":null,"object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT"],"paper_title":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"multiparty PSI","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":null,"properties":{"adversary":["augmented_semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"augmented semi-honest adversary that may change the corrupted parties' inputs before running the protocol honestly (§2.1, p. 4; Theorem 2, p. 14)","corruption_threshold":"Table 1 lists \\( n - 1 \\) (p. 3); Theorem 2 states no threshold (p. 14), and the zero-sharing argument considers \\( t < n - 1 \\) corrupt parties (§6.1, p. 14)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the leader P1 announces the intersection (Figure 8, step 5, p. 12, which the protocol keeps; §6.1, p. 14)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"a one-time seed exchange, then the parallel OPPRF reconstruction phase of Figure 8, step 4 (§6.1, p. 14; §5.3, p. 13)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"pairwise PRF seeds exchanged in an initialization step (Figure 9, p. 14); the proof sketch assumes a secure OPRF protocol (p. 14); 128 Naor-Pinkas base OTs in an offline phase and the KKRT OPRF code (§7, p. 17); the table-based OPPRF models H as a random oracle (Figure 6, p. 8)","technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing","secret_sharing"]},"quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"computational","security_model":"augmented semi-honest (Theorem 2 with proof sketch, pp. 14-15)","security_notion":"multi-party PSI (Figure 1, p. 5)","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":null,"signing_cost":null,"signing_rounds":null,"sizes":{},"statefulness":null,"summary":"Unconditional zero-sharing from pairwise PRF seeds (PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING) replaces the pairwise OPPRF phase, and each party then programs one OPPRF (PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF) to the leader with its share for each of its items (§6.1, p. 14). The OPPRFs run on the batched OPRF PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF (§3.1, p. 4).","supported_gates":null,"tag_size":null,"threshold_policy":null,"transform":null,"