ASSUMPTION-SEMANTICALLY-SECURE-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-ENCRYPTION-PSEUDORANDOM-FUNCTION-AND-COLLISION-RESISTANT-HASH"},{"evidence":"scheme_declared","id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-SEMI-HONEST-GENERIC-TWO-PARTY-COMPUTATION-GMW-OR-YAO-NO-ASSUMPTION-STATED","keywords":["generic"],"metadata":{"family":"generic","name":"Semi-honest generic two-party computation (GMW or Yao); no assumption stated"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[],"slug":"semi-honest-generic-two","status":"catalogued","subtitle":"generic","summary":"Assumption used by one or more PSI construction records: Semi-honest generic two-party computation (GMW or Yao); no assumption stated.","title":"Semi-honest generic two-party computation (GMW or Yao); no assumption stated","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-ASSUMPTION-SEMI-HONEST-GENERIC-TWO-PARTY-COMPUTATION-GMW-OR-YAO-NO-ASSUMPTION-STATED"},{"evidence":"scheme_declared","id":"PSI-ASSUMPTION-THRESHOLD-FHE-WITH-DISTRIBUTED-SETUP-FROM-CIRCULAR-SECURE-LWE-WITH-THRESHOLD-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-ENCRYPTION","keywords":["lattice"],"metadata":{"family":"lattice","name":"Threshold FHE with distributed setup from circular-secure LWE, with threshold additively homomorphic encryption"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[],"slug":"threshold-fhe-with-distribut","status":"catalogued","subtitle":"lattice","summary":"Assumption used by one or more PSI construction records: Threshold FHE with distributed setup from circular-secure LWE, with threshold additively homomorphic encryption.","title":"Threshold FHE with distributed setup from circular-secure LWE, with threshold additively homomorphic encryption","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-ASSUMPTION-THRESHOLD-FHE-WITH-DISTRIBUTED-SETUP-FROM-CIRCULAR-SECURE-LWE-WITH-THRESHOLD-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-ENCRYPTION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-1999-HFH","keywords":["two-party matching count","commutative_exponentiation","no-trusted-third-party","matching-count"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"not stated; discrete-log setting modulo a prime with an unpredictable hash"},"capabilities":["no-trusted-third-party","matching-count"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING"],"complexity":{"communication":"four lists modulo p: \\( H(x_i)^a \\) for Alice's n items, \\( H(y_j)^b \\) for Bob's m items, then \\( H(y_j)^{ab} \\) and \\( H(x_i)^{ab} \\), each randomly permuted (Appendix A, steps 1-4, p. 85)","computation":"each party exponentiates its own n (or m) hashed items and the other party's m (or n) values with its secret exponent modulo p (Appendix A, steps 1-4, p. 85)"},"configuration_note":"The protocol of Appendix A, steps 1-5 (p. 85), with all four lists permuted, so that each party learns the number of shared items. The variation without permutation in steps 3 and 4, which reveals the shared items, is described in the same paragraph but is not this row.","construction_family":"commutative_exponentiation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-1999-HFH","name":"HFH preference matching","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH"],"primitive":"two-party matching count","properties":{"adversary":"not_reported","adversary_scope":"not stated; the paper says a zero-knowledge proof could be added so that active cheating other than strategic input choice is detected, without specifying it (Appendix A, p. 85)","output":["cardinality"],"output_recipients":"both parties: each counts the matches (Appendix A, step 5, p. 85)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"four messages, two in each direction (Appendix A, steps 1-4, p. 85)","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"not_reported","setup":"system-wide large prime p with a large prime factor q of \\( p - 1 \\) (Appendix A, p. 85); a cryptographic hash whose output cannot be easily influenced or predicted (§2, p. 79)","technique":["diffie_hellman"]},"security":{"mode":"not_reported","model":"no adversary model or proof is given for the protocol (Appendix A, p. 85)","notion":"not stated in the paper"},"status":"published","title":"HFH private preference matching by exponentiation of hashed items modulo a prime","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH","year":1999},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1145/336992.337012","sections":[{"content":"Alice and Bob exchange randomly permuted lists of hashed items raised to their secret exponents a and b, re-exponentiate what they receive, return it permuted, and count equal values (Appendix A, p. 85). The authors relate it to Diffie-Hellman key exchange (Appendix A, p. 85). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The paper frames the task as preference matching, not set intersection, and states no security model, so adversary, security model, and set-size regime are recorded as not reported. No measurement is reported.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"hfh99-hfh-preference-matching","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party matching count · 1999","summary":"Alice and Bob exchange randomly permuted lists of hashed items raised to their secret exponents a and b, re-exponentiate what they receive, return it permuted, and count equal values (Appendix A, p. 85). The authors relate it to Diffie-Hellman key exchange (Appendix A, p. 85). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING.","title":"HFH preference matching","type":"construction","venue":"ACM EC 1999","year":1999,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-1999-HFH"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY","keywords":["psi cardinality","oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"additively_homomorphic","name":"semantic security of an additively homomorphic encryption scheme (Paillier named as an instance)"},"capabilities":[],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD"],"complexity":{"communication":"as in PM-Semi-Honest; only the server's payload changes, to a fixed string \\( 0^{\\ell} \\) (§4.4, p. 9)","computation":"as in PM-Semi-Honest; the client counts the decryptions equal to \\( 0^{\\ell} \\) (§4.4, p. 9)"},"configuration_note":"Private cardinality matching \\( \\mathrm{PM}_C \\) (§4.4, p. 9): PM-Semi-Honest with \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + 0^{\\ell}) \\) in place of \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + y) \\).","construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY","name":"FNP \\( \\mathrm{PM}_C \\) (cardinality)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"primitive":"PSI cardinality","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest client and server; the proof follows from that of PM-Semi-Honest (§4.4, p. 9)","output":["cardinality"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§3.1, p. 3; §4.4, p. 9)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"as in PM-Semi-Honest: one message from the client and one reply (§4.4, p. 9; Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"as in PM-Semi-Honest: the client generates the homomorphic key pair (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, step 1(a), p. 6)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest, standard model, by the proof of PM-Semi-Honest (§4.4, p. 9)","notion":"the client learns \\( |X \\cap Y| \\) only"},"status":"published","title":"FNP semi-honest PSI cardinality from oblivious polynomial evaluation with a constant payload","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"The server encodes a fixed string instead of its item as the payload, so the client learns how many of its decryptions equal the string, that is the intersection size (§4.4, p. 9). The variant is part of PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD. The threshold variant of §4.5 (p. 9) adds an unspecified secure circuit evaluation and has no row.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation or measurement is reported.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"fnp04-fnp-mathrm-pm-c","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"PSI cardinality · 2004","summary":"The server encodes a fixed string instead of its item as the payload, so the client learns how many of its decryptions equal the string, that is the intersection size (§4.4, p. 9). The variant is part of PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD. The threshold variant of §4.5 (p. 9) adds an unspecified secure circuit evaluation and has no row.","title":"FNP \\( \\mathrm{PM}_C \\) (cardinality)","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MAL-CLIENT","keywords":["two-party psi","oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"additively_homomorphic","name":"semantically secure additively homomorphic encryption, pseudorandom function, and collision-resistant hash"},"capabilities":[],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE"],"complexity":{"communication":"L times that of the semi-honest protocol (§5.1, p. 11)","computation":"L times that of the semi-honest protocol, for error probability exponentially small in L (§5.1, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol PM-Malicious-Client (p. 10): L copies of the B bin polynomials over pseudorandom identities \\( F_s(G(x)) \\), \\( L/2 \\) opened and checked for \\( k_C \\) nonzero roots, the other \\( L/2 \\) evaluated with XOR shares of each server item as payloads.","construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MAL-CLIENT","name":"FNP PM-Malicious-Client","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious","semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"malicious client (§5.1, pp. 10-11); the server's deviations are treated by a separate protocol (§5.2, p. 11), and a malicious server cannot affect the client's privacy here (footnote 11, p. 11)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§3.1, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"client sends L copies, server chooses \\( L/2 \\) to open, client opens them and sends the keys of the rest, server replies (Protocol PM-Malicious-Client, steps 1-4, p. 10)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"PRF keys for the balanced-allocation hash h and for F, with a collision-resistant hash G, all chosen by the client (Protocol PM-Malicious-Client, step 1, p. 10)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","cut_and_choose"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious client, standard model (footnote 4, p. 2; §5, p. 10; proof sketch, p. 11)","notion":"private matching with the client's effective input bounded by \\( k_C \\) items"},"status":"published","title":"FNP PSI secure against a malicious client by cut-and-choose over bin polynomials","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"The cut-and-choose of Protocol PM-Malicious-Client (p. 10), from PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE, wraps the balanced-allocation protocol of PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-SH. The client learns an item only if it is a root of all \\( L/2 \\) evaluated copies, so a client with more than \\( k_C \\) roots is caught except with probability exponentially small in L (proof sketch, p. 11).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The proof is a sketch. No measurement is reported.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"fnp04-fnp-pm-malicious-client","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2004","summary":"The cut-and-choose of Protocol PM-Malicious-Client (p. 10), from PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE, wraps the balanced-allocation protocol of PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-SH. The client learns an item only if it is a root of all \\( L/2 \\) evaluated copies, so a client with more than \\( k_C \\) roots is caught except with probability exponentially small in L (proof sketch, p. 11).","title":"FNP PM-Malicious-Client","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MAL-CLIENT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MAL-SERVER","keywords":["two-party psi","oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"additively_homomorphic","name":"semantically secure additively homomorphic encryption and random oracles"},"capabilities":[],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE"],"complexity":{"communication":"the encrypted coefficients of a degree-\\( k_C \\) polynomial from the client and \\( k_S \\) pairs \\( (\\mathrm{Enc}(r' \\cdot P(y) + s), H2(r'', y)) \\) from the server (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, steps 1-2, p. 12)","computation":"for each received pair the client decrypts s, recomputes \\( r = H1(s) \\), and checks consistency with its items (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, step 3, p. 12)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol PM-Malicious-Server (p. 12), described for a single polynomial without hashing; the paper says the balanced-allocation version may be modified similarly (§5.2, p. 11).","construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MAL-SERVER","name":"FNP PM-Malicious-Server","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious","semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"malicious server (Lemma 4, p. 12); the server's privacy is preserved as in the semi-honest protocol, and a malicious client is treated by a separate protocol (§5.2, p. 11; §5.1, p. 10)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§3.1, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"one message from the client and one reply from the server (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, steps 1-2, p. 12)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"random oracles H1 and H2; the client generates the homomorphic key pair (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious server, random oracle model (Lemma 4 and proof sketch, pp. 12-13)","notion":"private matching against a malicious server, with the server's input extracted from its random-oracle queries"},"status":"published","title":"FNP PSI secure against a malicious server by random-oracle derandomization of replies","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"For each item y the server picks a random s, sets \\( r = H1(s) \\), and uses r to fix all randomness of its homomorphic evaluation, returning \\( (\\mathrm{Enc}(r' \\cdot P(y) + s), H2(r'', y)) \\); the client decrypts s and accepts x if the pair is what an honest server would produce for x (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12). The mechanism is the server half of PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The proof is a sketch. The paper's protocol against both malicious parties is only outlined (§5.3, p. 13) and has no row. No measurement is reported.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"fnp04-fnp-pm-malicious-server","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2004","summary":"For each item y the server picks a random s, sets \\( r = H1(s) \\), and uses r to fix all randomness of its homomorphic evaluation, returning \\( (\\mathrm{Enc}(r' \\cdot P(y) + s), H2(r'', y)) \\); the client decrypts s and accepts x if the pair is what an honest server would produce for x (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12). The mechanism is the server half of PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE.","title":"FNP PM-Malicious-Server","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MAL-SERVER"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY","keywords":["multiparty psi","oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"additively_homomorphic","name":"semantic security of an additively homomorphic encryption scheme (Paillier named as an instance)"},"capabilities":[],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE"],"complexity":{"communication":"each client posts the encrypted coefficients of a degree-k polynomial and \\( k(n - 1) \\) encrypted shares; the leader posts k tuples of \\( n - 1 \\) ciphertexts; each client posts k values (§7, steps 1-3, p. 16)","computation":"the leader evaluates each of the \\( n - 1 \\) encrypted polynomials on each of its k items; the paper says hashing to bins can improve this (§7, step 2, p. 16; p. 17)"},"configuration_note":"The protocol for semi-honest parties of §7, steps 1-4 (p. 16), with n parties holding k items each; not the strawman protocol of pp. 15-16.","construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY","name":"FNP multi-party PM","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"primitive":"multiparty PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties; the protocol is sketched (§7, p. 15)","corruption_threshold":"not stated; for items outside the intersection the published XOR values look random to any coalition (§7, p. 16)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"every party checks the published row values against its own items (§7, step 4, p. 16)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"two rounds in which \\( P_1, \\ldots, P_{n-1} \\) submit data and one round in which \\( P_n \\) submits data (§7, p. 17)","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"each client has its own homomorphic key pair; data is posted to a public bulletin board or to the leader acting as one (§7, step 1, p. 16)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","secret_sharing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest; no proof is given (§7, pp. 15-17)","notion":"each party learns the intersection of all n lists"},"status":"published","title":"FNP semi-honest multi-party PSI from encrypted client polynomials and XOR-shared payloads","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Clients post encrypted polynomials and encrypted zero-XOR share matrices; the leader evaluates each client's polynomial on its items with XOR shares of the item as payloads; clients decrypt their columns and post row XORs; an item is in the intersection if a row's published values XOR to it (§7, steps 1-4, p. 16). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"A sketch without proof or formal collusion bound. No measurement is reported.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"fnp04-fnp-multi-party-pm","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty PSI · 2004","summary":"Clients post encrypted polynomials and encrypted zero-XOR share matrices; the leader evaluates each client's polynomial on its items with XOR shares of the item as payloads; clients decrypt their columns and post row XORs; an item is in the intersection if a row's published values XOR to it (§7, steps 1-4, p. 16). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE.","title":"FNP multi-party PM","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-SH","keywords":["two-party psi","oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","payload-transfer"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"additively_homomorphic","name":"semantic security of an additively homomorphic encryption scheme (Paillier named as an instance)"},"capabilities":["payload-transfer"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-BALANCED-ALLOCATION-HASHING"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( B \\cdot M \\) encrypted coefficients from the client, \\( O(k_C) \\) for \\( B = k_C / \\ln \\ln k_C \\), plus two ciphertexts per server item, one per candidate bin (§4.2, pp. 7-8)","computation":"server: for each item, M short exponentiations and one full-length exponentiation per candidate bin, \\( O(k_S + k_S \\ln \\ln k_C \\cdot n / \\lambda) \\) full-length exponentiations in total, dominated in practice by \\( k_S \\) full-length exponentiations; client: equal to the number of coefficients (§4.2, p. 8)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol PM-Semi-Honest (p. 6) with short-domain exponents, Horner's rule, and balanced-allocation hashing into \\( B = k_C / \\ln \\ln k_C \\) bins with polynomials of degree M (§4.2, pp. 6-8); this is the configuration behind the \\( O(k) \\) communication and \\( O(k \\ln \\ln k) \\) computation of the abstract (p. 1).","construction_family":"oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-SH","name":"FNP PM-Semi-Honest with balanced allocations","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest client and server (§3.2, p. 4; §4.3, pp. 8-9)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§3.1, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"one message from the client (encrypted bin polynomials and the bin mapping) and one reply from the server (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, steps 1-2, p. 6; §4.2, p. 7)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"the client generates the homomorphic key pair (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, step 1(a), p. 6) and chooses the balanced-allocation hash h (§4.2, p. 7); items are mapped to about \\( 2 \\log(\\max(k_C, k_S)) \\) bits by pairwise-independent hashing (§4.2, p. 6)","technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","balanced_allocation_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest, standard model (Lemmas 1-3, p. 8; §4.3, p. 9)","notion":"private matching: the client learns \\( X \\cap Y \\); client privacy by indistinguishability, server privacy by comparison with the ideal model"},"status":"published","title":"FNP semi-honest PSI from oblivious polynomial evaluation with balanced-allocation hashing","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"The client hashes her items into bins by balanced allocations, encodes each bin's items as roots of a degree-M polynomial padded with zero roots, and sends the encrypted coefficients; the server evaluates the two candidate bin polynomials for each item y and returns \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + y) \\), permuted (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6; §4.2, p. 7). Mechanisms: PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI and PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-BALANCED-ALLOCATION-HASHING. The server may attach payload data \\( p_y \\) to each item (step 2(b), p. 6).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation or measurement is reported. The costs are asymptotic, with constants discussed only through the cited balanced-allocation bounds (§4.2, p. 8).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"fnp04-fnp-pm-semi-honest","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2004","summary":"The client hashes her items into bins by balanced allocations, encodes each bin's items as roots of a degree-M polynomial padded with zero roots, and sends the encrypted coefficients; the server evaluates the two candidate bin polynomials for each item y and returns \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + y) \\), permuted (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6; §4.2, p. 7). Mechanisms: PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI and…","title":"FNP PM-Semi-Honest with balanced allocations","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2004-FNP-SH"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA","keywords":["two-party psi","rsa_blind_signature","server-precomputation","no-online-client-exponentiation"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"factoring","name":"one-more RSA inversion and random oracles"},"capabilities":["server-precomputation","no-online-client-exponentiation"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(v + w) \\): v blinded values to the server, v signed values and w tags to the client (Figure 4, p. 9; Table 2, p. 11)","computation":"online, server \\( O(v) \\) 1024-bit exponentiations and client \\( O(v) \\) 1024-bit modular multiplications; precomputation, server \\( O(w) \\) 1024-bit exponentiations (RSA signatures) and client \\( O(v) \\) multiplications (§5.6, p. 9; Table 2, p. 11)","reported_measurement":"online computation 4,662 ms (server) and 1,422 ms (client) for 5,000 items per party, precomputation excluded, 160-bit items, 1024-bit modulus, ANSI C with OpenSSL on a 2.33 GHz Dell Precision PC with 8 GB RAM (Table 3 and §6.1, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 4, 'Blind RSA-based PSI Protocol with linear complexity' (p. 9), without the data-transfer extension of §5.7.","construction_family":"rsa_blind_signature","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA","name":"DT10 blind-RSA PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2010-DT"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"honest-but-curious client and server (§5.1, p. 6; Appendix B.3, p. 16)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only; mutual PSI by two one-way runs (§2, p. 2)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"online, one client message and one server reply (Figure 4, steps 3-5, p. 9)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"the server's RSA key \\( (n, e, d) \\), set up a priori by the server; offline, the server computes w RSA signatures and tags and the client computes its blinded values (Figure 4, steps 1-2, p. 9)","technique":["rsa_blind_signature"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"honest-but-curious, random oracle model, one-more-RSA (Appendix B.3, p. 16)","notion":"server privacy, client privacy, and client unlinkability; no server unlinkability"},"status":"published","title":"De Cristofaro-Tsudik semi-honest PSI from RSA blind signatures with server precomputation","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","year":2010},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","sections":[{"content":"The server publishes hashes of RSA signatures on its hashed items; the client obtains signatures on its own hashed items by blinding, unblinds, hashes, and compares (Figure 4, p. 9). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The measurement is the authors' online computation time on their machine, with network time and precomputation excluded (§6.1, p. 11). Because the tags repeat across runs, the row does not provide server unlinkability (§5.6, p. 10).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"ct10-dt10-blind-rsa-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2010","summary":"The server publishes hashes of RSA signatures on its hashed items; the client obtains signatures on its own hashed items by blinding, unblinds, hashes, and compares (Figure 4, p. 9). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA.","title":"DT10 blind-RSA PSI","type":"construction","venue":"FC 2010","year":2010,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2010-DT-FIG3","keywords":["two-party psi","prime_order_group_exponentiation","server-unlinkability","server-precomputation"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"One-More-Gap-DH and random oracles"},"capabilities":["server-unlinkability","server-precomputation"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(v + w) \\): X and v values from the client; Z, v values, and w tags from the server (Figure 3, p. 8; Table 2, p. 11)","computation":"server \\( O(v) \\) 160-bit exponentiations online and \\( O(w) \\) 160-bit exponentiations precomputed, client \\( O(v) \\) 160-bit exponentiations, 1024-bit modulus (Table 2, p. 11; §5.5, p. 8)","reported_measurement":"online computation 2,072 ms (server) and 5,344 ms (client) for 5,000 items per party, precomputation excluded, on the platform of §6.1 (Table 3, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 3, 'PSI Protocol with linear complexity' (p. 8), with the product of hashes PCH; Figure 6 (p. 17) gives a simplified version for honest-but-curious parties.","construction_family":"prime_order_group_exponentiation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2010-DT-FIG3","name":"DT10 PSI (Figure 3)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2010-DT"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"honest-but-curious client and server (§5.1, p. 6; Appendix B.2, p. 15)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the client only (§2, p. 2)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"one client message and one server reply (Figure 3, steps 2 and 4, p. 8)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"public p, q, and g generating a subgroup of order q of \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p^* \\), hash functions H and H' (Figure 3, p. 8); the server can precompute the values \\( (h_{sj})^{-R_s} \\) (§5.5, p. 8)","technique":["diffie_hellman"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"honest-but-curious, random oracle model; the server-privacy reduction to One-More-Gap-DH uses one additional DH-oracle query (Appendix B.2, pp. 15-16)","notion":"server privacy, client privacy, client and server unlinkability"},"status":"published","title":"De Cristofaro-Tsudik semi-honest PSI with linear complexity in a prime-order subgroup","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","year":2010},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","sections":[{"content":"The client sends \\( X = \\mathrm{PCH} \\cdot g^{R_c} \\) and \\( y_i = (\\mathrm{PCH}/h_{ci}) \\cdot g^{R_{c:i}} \\); the server returns \\( Z = g^{R_s} \\), the values \\( y_i^{R_s} \\), and tags \\( H'((X/h_{sj})^{R_s}) \\); the client unblinds each \\( y_i^{R_s} \\) with Z and compares (Figure 3, p. 8). The row is the PSI version described in PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI. The paper does not call this protocol Diffie-Hellman; the technique value reflects the exponentiation of hashed items by the server's secret exponent in a prime-order group and the One-More-Gap-DH proof (Appendix B.2, p. 15).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The measurement is the authors' online computation time on their machine (§6.1, p. 11). Table 2 lists the Jarecki-Liu UPF protocol with the same asymptotic costs and malicious security (Table 2, p. 11); on the same machine its client time is lower (4,249 ms) and its server time similar (2,108 ms) for 5,000 items per party (Table 3, p. 11).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"ct10-dt10-psi-figure-3","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2010","summary":"The client sends \\( X = \\mathrm{PCH} \\cdot g^{R_c} \\) and \\( y_i = (\\mathrm{PCH}/h_{ci}) \\cdot g^{R_{c:i}} \\); the server returns \\( Z = g^{R_s} \\), the values \\( y_i^{R_s} \\), and tags \\( H'((X/h_{sj})^{R_s}) \\); the client unblinds each \\( y_i^{R_s} \\) with Z and compares (Figure 3, p. 8). The row is the PSI version described in PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI. The paper does not call this protocol Diffie-Hellman; the…","title":"DT10 PSI (Figure 3)","type":"construction","venue":"FC 2010","year":2010,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2010-DT-FIG3"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-BWA","keywords":["two-party psi","garbled_circuit_bit_vector","small-universe"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model"},"capabilities":["small-universe"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM","PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 2^{\\sigma} \\) non-free gates, one AND per element of the universe (Table 1, p. 2; §3, p. 4); with size auditing \\( \\sigma \\cdot 2^{\\sigma} \\) non-free gates (§6, p. 11)","computation":"grows linearly in the universe size \\( 2^{\\sigma} \\) (§4, p. 5); the garbled-circuit part is 35 to 45% of the running time (§6, p. 11)","reported_measurement":"\\( \\sigma = 16 \\): 2.48 s without auditing and 12.22 s with auditing (§6, p. 11); faster than SCS-WN for \\( n > 40 \\) at \\( \\sigma = 12 \\) and \\( n > 500 \\) at \\( \\sigma = 16 \\) (§7.1 and Figure 8, p. 11); two Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 desktops, 100 Mbps LAN"},"configuration_note":"Each set is a bit-vector of length \\( 2^{\\sigma} \\) and the circuit ANDs the two vectors bit by bit (§3, p. 4).","construction_family":"garbled_circuit_bit_vector","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-BWA","name":"HEK BWA","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties; because set sizes are not restricted, a dishonest party could input all 1s and learn the other set, so the protocol should not be used standalone (§3, pp. 4-5)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the output is the bit-vector of the intersection; either or both parties may learn it (§3, p. 4; §2, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"not_applicable","setup":"Naor-Pinkas base OTs with OT extension (§2, p. 4)","technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; no proof given (§2, p. 4)","notion":"PSI as a bit-vector intersection"},"status":"published","title":"HEK bitwise-AND PSI over a bit-vector of the universe, semi-honest two-party, Yao garbled circuits","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},"primaryUrl":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","sections":[{"content":"The bit-vector protocol of §3 (pp. 4-5). Its cost does not depend on the set sizes, which is why set_size_regime is not applicable, and it is the fastest protocol of the paper for small element spaces (§7.1, p. 11). Its measurements belong to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM and its audited version to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Practical only for small universes, up to about \\( \\sigma = 20 \\) (§7.1, p. 11).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"hek12-hek-bwa","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2012","summary":"The bit-vector protocol of §3 (pp. 4-5). Its cost does not depend on the set sizes, which is why set_size_regime is not applicable, and it is the fastest protocol of the paper for small element spaces (§7.1, p. 11). Its measurements belong to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM and its audited version to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING.","title":"HEK BWA","type":"construction","venue":"NDSS 2012","year":2012,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-BWA"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-PWC","keywords":["two-party psi","garbled_circuit_pairwise_comparison","early-reveal"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model"},"capabilities":["early-reveal"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM"],"complexity":{"communication":"Equal circuits of \\( \\sigma - 1 \\) non-free gates, evaluated about \\( ((2n - \\hat{n})^{2} + \\hat{n})/4 \\) times for intersection size \\( \\hat{n} \\), giving \\( ((2n - \\hat{n})^{2} + \\hat{n})(\\sigma - 1)/4 \\) non-free gates (Table 1, p. 2; §4, p. 5)","computation":"worst case \\( \\Theta(n^{2}) \\) comparisons; early reveal saves about 75% of comparisons for identical sets, 45% when half the elements match, and 30% when a third match (§4, p. 5)","reported_measurement":"for 128 to 8192 elements of 32 bits and \\( \\hat{n}/n = 0.5 \\), SCS-WN was 10 to 70 times faster than PWC (§7.2, p. 12; Figure 10, p. 13); two Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 desktops, 100 Mbps LAN"},"configuration_note":"Algorithm 1 (p. 5): compare every pending pair with an Equal circuit that XORs the inputs and computes a negated OR, reveal each match at once, and skip elements already matched.","construction_family":"garbled_circuit_pairwise_comparison","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-PWC","name":"HEK PWC","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties (§1.1, p. 2; §2, p. 3)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"each match is revealed as soon as it is found (Algorithm 1, p. 5); either or both parties may learn the result (§2, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"each reveal adds a round; the implementation overlaps reveals with the comparisons in a second thread (§4, p. 5)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"each party randomly permutes its set before the protocol so that the order of revealed matches leaks nothing (§4, p. 5); Naor-Pinkas base OTs with OT extension (§2, p. 4)","technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; positional leakage of early reveal is removed by random permutation of the inputs (§4, p. 5); no proof given (§2, p. 4)","notion":"PSI (intersection revealed, nothing else)"},"status":"published","title":"HEK pairwise-comparison PSI with early reveal of matches, semi-honest two-party, Yao garbled circuits","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},"primaryUrl":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","sections":[{"content":"The pairwise-comparison protocol of §4 (p. 5). The paper recommends it for large universes when n is small (§1.2, p. 2). Its measurements belong to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The expected gate count is a heuristic estimate that assumes matches are evenly spread after shuffling; the experiments are consistent with it (§4, p. 5).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"hek12-hek-pwc","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2012","summary":"The pairwise-comparison protocol of §4 (p. 5). The paper recommends it for large universes when n is small (§1.2, p. 2). Its measurements belong to PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM.","title":"HEK PWC","type":"construction","venue":"NDSS 2012","year":2012,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-PWC"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-HE","keywords":["two-party psi","garbled_circuit_sort_compare_shuffle","linear-shuffle"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model, with an unnamed homomorphic encryption scheme"},"capabilities":["linear-shuffle"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE","PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM"],"complexity":{"communication":"garbled circuit of \\( 2\\sigma n \\log(2n) + ((3n - 1)\\sigma - n) + (\\sigma + 32)n \\) non-free gates (Table 1, p. 2), plus \\( 2n \\) ciphertexts in the shuffle (§5.3.2, p. 9)","computation":"\\( O(n) \\) public-key operations in the shuffle (§5.3.2, p. 9); the paper notes that for BWA and SCS-HE gate counts do not capture the full cost (§1.2, p. 3)","reported_measurement":"for 128 to 8192 elements of 32 bits, SCS-WN was more than 7 times faster, and SCS-HE saved no bandwidth relative to SCS-WN; two Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 desktops, 100 Mbps LAN (§7.2, p. 12; Figure 10, p. 13)"},"configuration_note":"Sorting and filtering as in SCS-WN; the n filtered outputs \\( m_i \\) are secret-shared inside the circuit as \\( r_i \\) and \\( r_i + m_i \\) with \\( (\\sigma + k) \\)-bit masks, and Bob homomorphically computes and permutes the encryptions of \\( m_i \\) under Alice's key before Alice decrypts (§5.3.2, pp. 8-9; Figure 6, p. 9).","construction_family":"garbled_circuit_sort_compare_shuffle","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-HE","name":"HEK SCS-HE","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties (§1.1, p. 2; §2, p. 3)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"Alice receives the shuffled intersection elements; Bob receives nothing or, if desired, the sorted elements from Alice (Figure 6, p. 9)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"the shuffle uses two rounds, each sending n ciphertexts (§5.3.2, p. 9); the rounds of the garbled-circuit part are not stated","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"Alice's homomorphic key pair (Figure 6, p. 9); Naor-Pinkas base OTs with OT extension (§2, p. 4)","technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension","additively_homomorphic_encryption","sorting_network"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; no proof given (§2, p. 4); statistical masking \\( O(n \\cdot 2^{-k}) \\) in the secret-sharing step (§5.3.2, p. 8)","notion":"PSI (intersection revealed, nothing else)"},"status":"published","title":"HEK sort-compare-shuffle PSI with homomorphic-encryption shuffling, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},"primaryUrl":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","sections":[{"content":"This is the SCS-HE variant of §5.3.2 (pp. 8-9): the sort-compare-shuffle circuit of PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE up to filtering, followed by the homomorphic shuffle of Figure 6. The shuffle is linear in n but slower in practice than the Waksman network, and it gives up the goal of using only generic secure computation (§5.3.2, p. 9).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The homomorphic encryption scheme used is not named in the paper. Times are for the authors' setting (§7, p. 11).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"hek12-hek-scs-he","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2012","summary":"This is the SCS-HE variant of §5.3.2 (pp. 8-9): the sort-compare-shuffle circuit of PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE up to filtering, followed by the homomorphic shuffle of Figure 6. The shuffle is linear in n but slower in practice than the Waksman network, and it gives up the goal of using only generic secure computation (§5.3.2, p. 9).","title":"HEK SCS-HE","type":"construction","venue":"NDSS 2012","year":2012,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-HE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-WN","keywords":["two-party psi","garbled_circuit_sort_compare_shuffle","quasi-linear-circuit","generic-composition"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model"},"capabilities":["quasi-linear-circuit","generic-composition"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE","PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM"],"complexity":{"communication":"garbled circuit of \\( 2\\sigma n \\log(2n) + ((3n - 1)\\sigma - n) + \\sigma(n \\log n - n + 1)/3 \\) non-free gates for two sets of n σ-bit elements (Table 1, p. 2), with free XOR and garbled-row reduction (§2, p. 4); measured bandwidth 147 to 470 MB for \\( n = 1024 \\) depending on the security level (§7.3, p. 13)","computation":"for \\( n = 1024 \\) and \\( \\sigma = 160 \\): \\( 3k \\) medium-exponent exponentiations, \\( 2k \\) modular inverses, \\( 2k \\) modular multiplications, and 18.34 million SHA calls, with k the OT-extension security parameter from 80 to 256 (Table 2, p. 13)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 1024 \\): 10.5 s (\\( \\sigma = 32 \\)) and 51.5 s (\\( \\sigma = 160 \\)) at the 80-bit level, 22.7 s and 122.7 s at the 256-bit level (Figure 11, p. 13); \\( n = 2^{20} \\), \\( \\sigma = 32 \\): about 6 hours for 1.7 billion non-free gates (§7.2, p. 12); two Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 desktops, 100 Mbps LAN, one core per party, setup excluded (§7, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Local sorting, bitonic merging, DupSelect-3 filtering, and shuffling of the n filtered outputs by a Waksman network of \\( n \\log n - n + 1 \\) programmed 2-Swappers (§5, pp. 5-10; Figure 1, p. 6; Figure 7 and Algorithm 2, p. 10).","construction_family":"garbled_circuit_sort_compare_shuffle","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-WN","name":"HEK SCS-WN","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties; stronger threat models are left to future work (§1.1, p. 2; §2, p. 3)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the evaluator receives the n permuted outputs; if the generator should learn them too, the evaluator permutes or sorts them again and sends them back (§5.3.3, p. 9); either or both parties may learn the result (§2, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"each party sorts its set locally; Naor-Pinkas base OTs extended by OT extension, whose setup is a one-time cost per pair of parties (§2, p. 4; §7, p. 11); the circuit generator chooses a random permutation and configures the Waksman network locally (§5.3.3, pp. 9-10)","technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension","sorting_network"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; Yao's protocol with Naor-Pinkas OT and OT extension; the paper states provability from DDH in the random oracle model without a proof (§2, p. 4)","notion":"PSI (intersection revealed, nothing else)"},"status":"published","title":"HEK sort-compare-shuffle PSI with Waksman-network shuffling, semi-honest two-party, Yao garbled circuits","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},"primaryUrl":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","sections":[{"content":"This is the SCS-WN protocol of §5.3.3 (pp. 9-10), the sort-compare-shuffle circuit of PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE evaluated with Yao's garbled-circuit protocol. The Waksman network is about 15% of the cost of the sorting stage (§5.3.3, p. 10). Its measured comparison with custom PSI is PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Times are for the authors' Java framework on 2011 desktop hardware with a 100 Mbps LAN and exclude one-time setup (§7, p. 11). They are not comparable with measurements from other papers unless a source makes the comparison.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"hek12-hek-scs-wn","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2012","summary":"This is the SCS-WN protocol of §5.3.3 (pp. 9-10), the sort-compare-shuffle circuit of PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE evaluated with Yao's garbled-circuit protocol. The Waksman network is about 15% of the cost of the sorting stage (§5.3.3, p. 10). Its measured comparison with custom PSI is PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM.","title":"HEK SCS-WN","type":"construction","venue":"NDSS 2012","year":2012,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-WN"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2016-KKRT","keywords":["two-party psi","oprf_cuckoo_hashing","item-length-independent-oprf-cost"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"Hamming correlation-robust hash and pseudorandom code, with ideal base OTs"},"capabilities":["item-length-independent-oprf-cost"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( k(1.2n + s) + (3 + s)nv \\) bits for n items per party, excluding base OTs (Table 5, p. 15), with \\( k = 424\\text{-}448 \\) and \\( v = 56\\text{-}88 \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{8} \\) to \\( 2^{24} \\) (Table 2, p. 15)","computation":"k base OTs plus symmetric-key operations, with an AES-based pseudorandom code (§2, p. 6; §6.3, p. 16)","reported_measurement":"3,780 ms (LAN) and 7,455 ms (WAN) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and items of 32 to 128 bits, one thread per party on an Intel Xeon E5-2699 v3 (Table 3, p. 15; §6.2, p. 16)"},"configuration_note":"Bob hashes his items into \\( 1.2n \\) bins with 3-way cuckoo hashing and a stash of size s; the parties run \\( 1.2n + s \\) BaRK-OPRF instances with Bob as receiver, each bin input tagged with its hash index; Alice sends permuted PRF values of her items for each hash function and stash position (Figure 3, p. 13).","construction_family":"oprf_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2016-KKRT","name":"KKRT PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest Alice and Bob (§1.1, p. 3; §5.2, p. 11)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"both parties: Bob sends the intersection to Alice (Figure 3, step 6, p. 13)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"k base OTs, which the implementation obtains by extending 128 Naor-Pinkas OTs with IKNP (§6.3, p. 16); BaRK-OPRF is proved with an ideal OT functionality (Figure 2, p. 10)","technique":["batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; BaRK-OPRF proved with ideal base OTs (Theorem 6, p. 9); PSI security argued in §5.2, p. 11","notion":"PSI with output to both parties"},"status":"published","title":"KKRT semi-honest two-party PSI from BaRK-OPRF and cuckoo hashing","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","year":2016},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/799","sections":[{"content":"This is the protocol of Figure 3 (p. 13), recorded as PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI: the hashing paradigm of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner, with BaRK-OPRF (PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF) in place of their OPRF and with the hash index appended to each bin input so that Alice never evaluates the PRF twice on the same key and input (§5.2, pp. 11-12). Correctness fails only on a spurious PRF collision, which outputs of \\( \\sigma + 2 \\log_2 n \\) bits bound by \\( 2^{-\\sigma} \\) (§5.2, p. 12).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Rows of this kind are compared as published. The reported times are for equal set sizes and the authors' machine and network settings (§6.2, p. 16); they are not comparable with measurements from other papers unless a source makes the comparison.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"kkrt16-kkrt-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2016","summary":"This is the protocol of Figure 3 (p. 13), recorded as PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI: the hashing paradigm of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner, with BaRK-OPRF (PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF) in place of their OPRF and with the hash index appended to each bin input so that Alice never evaluates the PRF twice on the same key and input (§5.2, pp. 11-12). Correctness fails only on a spurious PRF…","title":"KKRT PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2016","year":2016,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2016-KKRT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-CLR","keywords":["unbalanced psi","fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","light-receiver-computation","offline-sender-preprocessing"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"lattice","name":"IND-CPA leveled FHE with circuit privacy (FV over Ring-LWE)"},"capabilities":["communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","light-receiver-computation","offline-sender-preprocessing"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(N_y \\log N_x) \\) (p. 4); about \\( 1.5 C \\sigma N_y \\log_2 N_x \\) bits with C a small ciphertext-expansion constant (§6.3, p. 17); receiver to sender \\( (2^{\\ell} - 1)(\\lfloor \\log_2(B/\\alpha)/\\ell \\rfloor + 1) \\cdot m/n \\) ciphertexts and sender to receiver \\( \\alpha \\cdot m/n \\) ciphertexts (§4.3, p. 10; Figure 4, p. 12)","computation":"sender work linear in \\( N_x \\), with circuit depth \\( \\lceil \\log_2(\\lfloor \\log_2(B/\\alpha)/\\ell \\rfloor + 1) \\rceil + 1 \\); the receiver performs only encryptions and decryptions linear in \\( N_y \\) (p. 4; §4.3, p. 10)","reported_measurement":"\\( N_x = 2^{24} \\), \\( N_y = 5535 \\), \\( \\sigma = 32 \\), \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), SEAL8192-1, \\( \\alpha = 128 \\), \\( \\ell = 2 \\): sender pre-processing 71.2 s and sender online 36.1 s single-threaded, receiver encryption 0.3 s and decryption 2.4 s, communication 4.1 MB receiver to sender and 8.4 MB sender to receiver; one machine with two 18-core Intel Xeon E5-2699 v3, network simulated with tc (Tables 3-4, pp. 14-15; §6.1, p. 13; abstract, p. 1)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 4 (p. 12): permutation-based cuckoo hashing with \\( h = 3 \\) and no stash on the receiver side and simple hashing into bins of size B on the sender side (Figure 2, p. 8); items hashed to \\( 2 \\log_2(N_x + N_y) + \\lambda - 1 \\) bits when longer; FV with batching (SEAL v2.1, §6.1, p. 13); window parameter ℓ, partition parameter α, optional modulus switching of the reply.","construction_family":"fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-CLR","name":"CLR17 FHE PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"],"primitive":"unbalanced PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest sender and receiver (§2.2, p. 3; Theorem 2, p. 11)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only; the sender outputs nothing (Figure 3, p. 11; Figure 4, p. 12)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"the receiver sends its encrypted powers and the sender replies with \\( \\alpha \\cdot m/n \\) ciphertexts (Figure 4, steps 5-6, p. 12); no round count is stated","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"the parties agree on hashing, FHE, window, and partition parameters, and the receiver generates the FHE key pair (Figure 1, step 1, p. 5; Figure 4, steps 1-3, p. 12); the sender's pre-processing (hashing, polynomial coefficients, NTT) runs offline without the receiver, but simulation-based security needs fresh hash functions in each execution (§6.1, p. 14)","technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party, simulation-based (Theorem 2, p. 11)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver only (FPSI, Figure 3)"},"status":"published","title":"CLR17 semi-honest unbalanced PSI from leveled FV with batching, cuckoo hashing, windowing, and partitioning","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/299","sections":[{"content":"This row is the full protocol of Figure 4 (p. 12), which realizes the contribution PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION. The sender partitions its simple hash table into α subtables, replaces each bin of each subtable by the coefficients of the polynomial whose roots are the bin's items, and batches the coefficients (step 4). The receiver batches its cuckoo table, computes the window powers of each batched plaintext, encrypts them, and sends them (step 5). The sender computes encryptions of all powers up to \\( B/\\alpha \\), takes the dot product with each subtable's coefficients, optionally switches modulus, and returns the results; the receiver outputs the items whose slot decrypts to zero in some partition (steps 6-7). Function privacy is obtained by noise flooding before the reply (§5.2, p. 11).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The reported measurement is for 32-bit items, one parameter choice, and the authors' machine with a simulated LAN (§6.1, p. 13); Table 3 (p. 14) gives other choices of α, ℓ, and thread counts. The paper compares with the protocols of Pinkas et al. and Kolesnikov et al. on the same machine (Table 5, p. 16); those comparisons are not repeated here.","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"he_bfv12: the protocol row runs on FV (SEAL v2.1) (CLR17 §6.1, p. 13). HE-OPT-SIMD-PACKING-2012: batching of the receiver's table and the sender's coefficients (CLR17 Figure 4, steps 4c and 5a, p. 12; §4.1, p. 6).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"clr17-clr17-fhe-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"unbalanced PSI · 2017","summary":"This row is the full protocol of Figure 4 (p. 12), which realizes the contribution PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION. The sender partitions its simple hash table into α subtables, replaces each bin of each subtable by the coefficients of the polynomial whose roots are the bin's items, and batches the coefficients (step 4). The receiver batches its cuckoo table, computes the window powers of each batched…","title":"CLR17 FHE PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-CLR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-3PARTY","keywords":["multiparty psi","opprf_incremental_chain","three-opprf-executions"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"PRF and random oracle, with the OPPRF instantiated from the KKRT batched OPRF"},"capabilities":["three-opprf-executions"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"three OPPRF executions against four for the general protocol of §5 (§6.3, p. 17)","computation":"symmetric-key work of three multi-query OPPRFs plus base OTs (§6.3, p. 17; §7, p. 17)","reported_measurement":"25.81 s total and 25.23 s online for \\( m = 2^{20} \\) on LAN, against 41.64 s for the general three-party protocol; 129.45 s on WAN (Table 4, p. 18; §7.2, pp. 19-20); simulated WAN with 96 ms RTT and 200 Mbps, same machine and parameters as the other rows (§7, p. 17)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 10 (p. 16): P1 programs an OPPRF to P2 on random encodings of its items, P2 reprograms the outputs it received on its own items to P3, and P3 does the same back to P1, who outputs items whose encoding returns (§6.3, pp. 16-17).","construction_family":"opprf_incremental_chain","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-3PARTY","name":"KMPRT three-party PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT"],"primitive":"multiparty PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest parties, standard semi-honest model as the section title states (§6.3, p. 16); security is argued in prose for each colluding pair (§6.3, p. 17)","corruption_threshold":"three parties with \\( t \\in \\{1, 2\\} \\) (Table 4, p. 18); the protocol supports the full corrupted majority in the three-party case (§7.2, p. 19)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the leader P1 announces the intersection (Figure 10, step 4, p. 16)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"three OPPRF executions run in turn, each waiting for the previous one (§6.3, p. 17)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"ideal OPPRF functionality in Figure 10 (p. 16); 128 Naor-Pinkas base OTs in an offline phase (§7, p. 17); evaluated with the table-based OPPRF, which models H as a random oracle (§7.2, p. 18; Figure 6, p. 8)","technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest, any two of three parties may collude; argued in prose, no theorem (§6.3, p. 17)","notion":"three-party PSI"},"status":"published","title":"KMPRT optimized semi-honest three-party PSI from a chain of three OPPRFs","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","sections":[{"content":"The three parties compute the intersection incrementally: P2 obtains encodings of \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\), P3 of \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\cap X_3 \\), and P1 decodes (§6.3, p. 16). Each step is one multi-query OPPRF from PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF. Collusion of P1 and P2 reveals only \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\), which they can compute anyway; P2 and P3 learn nothing about P1's encodings; P1 and P3 learn the output (§6.3, p. 17).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The protocol does not extend to more than three parties: colluding P1 and \\( P_j \\) would learn the partial intersection \\( X_1 \\cap \\cdots \\cap X_j \\) (§6.3, pp. 16-17). The gain over the general protocol is small on slow networks because the OPPRFs run in sequence (§6.3, p. 17). The paper reports the communication of the general protocol only (Table 5, p. 19).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"kmprt17-kmprt-three-party-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty PSI · 2017","summary":"The three parties compute the intersection incrementally: P2 obtains encodings of \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\), P3 of \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\cap X_3 \\), and P1 decodes (§6.3, p. 16). Each step is one multi-query OPPRF from PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF. Collusion of P1 and P2 reveals only \\( X_1 \\cap X_2 \\), which they can compute anyway; P2 and P3 learn nothing about P1's encodings; P1 and P3 learn the output (§6.3, p. 17).","title":"KMPRT three-party PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-3PARTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-AUG","keywords":["multiparty psi","opprf_unconditional_zero_sharing","symmetric-key-techniques","unconditional-zero-sharing"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"PRF and random oracle, with the OPPRF instantiated from the KKRT batched OPRF"},"capabilities":["symmetric-key-techniques","unconditional-zero-sharing"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING","PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(n m \\lambda) \\) bits for the leader and \\( O(m \\lambda) \\) bits per client (Table 1, p. 3)","computation":"\\( O(n \\kappa) \\) exponentiations for the leader and \\( O(\\kappa) \\) per client, in an offline preprocessing phase (Table 1, p. 3); zero-sharing costs PRF evaluations only (Figure 9, p. 14)","reported_measurement":"21.93 s and 467.66 MB of client communication excluding base OTs for 5 parties with \\( m = 2^{20} \\) on LAN, table-based OPPRF, leader with up to 4 threads (Table 3, p. 18; §7.1, p. 17); 128-bit items, \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), 2x 36-core Intel Xeon 2.30 GHz with 256 GB RAM, simulated LAN with 0.02 ms RTT and 10 Gbps (§7, p. 17)"},"configuration_note":"The zero-sharing protocol of Figure 9 (p. 14) in place of steps 1-3 of Figure 8, followed by steps 4-5 of Figure 8 (p. 12), with the table-based OPPRF (Figure 6, p. 8) inside the hashing-based OPPRF (Figure 7, p. 11), as in the TABLE row of Table 3 (p. 18).","construction_family":"opprf_unconditional_zero_sharing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-AUG","name":"KMPRT augmented semi-honest multiparty PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT"],"primitive":"multiparty PSI","properties":{"adversary":["augmented_semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"augmented semi-honest adversary that may change the corrupted parties' inputs before running the protocol honestly (§2.1, p. 4; Theorem 2, p. 14)","corruption_threshold":"Table 1 lists \\( n - 1 \\) (p. 3); Theorem 2 states no threshold (p. 14), and the zero-sharing argument considers \\( t < n - 1 \\) corrupt parties (§6.1, p. 14)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the leader P1 announces the intersection (Figure 8, step 5, p. 12, which the protocol keeps; §6.1, p. 14)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"a one-time seed exchange, then the parallel OPPRF reconstruction phase of Figure 8, step 4 (§6.1, p. 14; §5.3, p. 13)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"pairwise PRF seeds exchanged in an initialization step (Figure 9, p. 14); the proof sketch assumes a secure OPRF protocol (p. 14); 128 Naor-Pinkas base OTs in an offline phase and the KKRT OPRF code (§7, p. 17); the table-based OPPRF models H as a random oracle (Figure 6, p. 8)","technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing","secret_sharing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"augmented semi-honest (Theorem 2 with proof sketch, pp. 14-15)","notion":"multi-party PSI (Figure 1, p. 5)"},"status":"published","title":"KMPRT augmented semi-honest multiparty PSI from unconditional zero-sharing and table-based OPPRFs","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","sections":[{"content":"Unconditional zero-sharing from pairwise PRF seeds (PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING) replaces the pairwise OPPRF phase, and each party then programs one OPPRF (PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF) to the leader with its share for each of its items (§6.1, p. 14). The OPPRFs run on the batched OPRF PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF (§3.1, p. 4).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Security holds in the augmented semi-honest model only (§6.1, pp. 14-15). Of the four hint variants in Table 3, the table-based OPPRF is the fastest; the polynomial hint with separate bins has 447.44 MB of communication at \\( 2^{20} \\) items and the combined polynomial hint the smallest communication at small sizes (Table 3, p. 18). Reported times are for the authors' setting only.","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-BASEOT: 128 base OTs obtained with the Naor-Pinkas construction in an offline phase (KMPRT §7, p. 17).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"kmprt17-kmprt-augmented-semi-honest","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty PSI · 2017","summary":"Unconditional zero-sharing from pairwise PRF seeds (PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING) replaces the pairwise OPPRF phase, and each party then programs one OPPRF (PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF) to the leader with its share for each of its items (§6.1, p. 14). The OPPRFs run on the batched OPRF PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF (§3.1, p. 4).","title":"KMPRT augmented semi-honest multiparty PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-AUG"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-SH","keywords":["multiparty psi","opprf_conditional_zero_sharing","symmetric-key-techniques","no-honest-majority","corruption-threshold-parameter"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"PRF and random oracle, with the OPPRF instantiated from the KKRT batched OPRF"},"capabilities":["symmetric-key-techniques","no-honest-majority","corruption-threshold-parameter"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-SYMMETRIC-KEY-MPSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(n m \\lambda) \\) bits for the leader and \\( O(m t \\lambda) \\) bits per client (Table 1, p. 3; §7.2, p. 20)","computation":"\\( O(n \\kappa) \\) exponentiations for the leader and \\( O(t \\kappa) \\) per client, all in an offline preprocessing phase (Table 1, p. 3); the rest is symmetric-key work, with a random-oracle nonce search per bin in the table-based OPPRF (§3.4, p. 9)","reported_measurement":"71.28 s total and 70.60 s online for \\( n = 5 \\), \\( t = 4 \\), \\( m = 2^{20} \\) on LAN (Table 4, p. 18), with 1,870 MB client communication excluding base OTs (Table 5, p. 19); 128-bit items, \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), one machine with 2x 36-core Intel Xeon 2.30 GHz and 256 GB RAM, simulated LAN with 0.02 ms RTT and 10 Gbps, leader with \\( n - 1 \\) threads and other parties with \\( \\min\\{t + 1, n - 1\\} \\) threads (§7, p. 17; §7.2, p. 19)"},"configuration_note":"The protocol of Figure 8 (p. 12), with every pairwise OPPRF realized as the hashing-based multi-query OPPRF of Figure 7 (p. 11) over the table-based single-query OPPRF of Figure 6 (p. 8), as in the evaluation of §7.2 (p. 18); OPPRF outputs have \\( \\lambda + \\log_2 m \\) bits (§5.3, p. 12).","construction_family":"opprf_conditional_zero_sharing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-SH","name":"KMPRT semi-honest multiparty PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT"],"primitive":"multiparty PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"any number of corrupt, colluding, semi-honest parties (Theorem 1, p. 12)","corruption_threshold":"any number of colluding parties, \\( t = n - 1 \\) by default; a variant tolerates at most t corrupt parties by sharing with \\( t + 1 \\) parties only (§5.3, p. 13); Table 1 lists \\( n - 1 \\) (p. 3)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the leader P1 announces the intersection (Figure 8, step 5, p. 12); the ideal functionality gives it to all parties (Figure 1, p. 5)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"constant: all OPPRF instances of the sharing phase run in parallel, as do those of the reconstruction phase (§5.3, p. 13)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"Theorem 1 assumes an ideal OPPRF functionality (Figure 8, p. 12); the implementation obtains 128 base OTs with the Naor-Pinkas construction in an offline phase and uses the KKRT OPRF code (§7, p. 17); the table-based OPPRF models H as a random oracle (Figure 6, p. 8)","technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing","secret_sharing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest, any number of colluding parties, ideal OPPRF functionality (Theorem 1, p. 12)","notion":"multi-party PSI (Figure 1, p. 5)"},"status":"published","title":"KMPRT semi-honest multiparty PSI from conditional zero-sharing over table-based OPPRFs","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","sections":[{"content":"Conditional zero-sharing over pairwise OPPRFs followed by conditional reconstruction at the leader (PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-SYMMETRIC-KEY-MPSI), with the OPPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF built on the batched OPRF PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF (§3.1, p. 4). All pairs of parties use the same cuckoo hash functions, so each party hashes its set twice, once with cuckoo hashing as receiver and once with simple hashing as sender (§5.3, p. 13). Placing the party with the smallest set as leader reduces cost, since the number of OPPRF instances follows the receiver's cuckoo table (§5.3, p. 13).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The row describes the table-based instantiation evaluated in §7.2. The generic protocol of Figure 8 can also use the polynomial or garbled Bloom filter OPPRFs, which need no random oracle (§§3.2-3.3). Reported times are for the authors' single machine with simulated networks and are not comparable with measurements from other papers unless a source makes the comparison.","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-BASEOT: 128 base OTs obtained with the Naor-Pinkas construction in an offline phase (KMPRT §7, p. 17).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"kmprt17-kmprt-semi-honest-multiparty","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty PSI · 2017","summary":"Conditional zero-sharing over pairwise OPPRFs followed by conditional reconstruction at the leader (PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-SYMMETRIC-KEY-MPSI), with the OPPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF built on the batched OPRF PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF (§3.1, p. 4). All pairs of parties use the same cuckoo hash functions, so each party hashes its set twice, once with cuckoo hashing as receiver and once with simple hashing as…","title":"KMPRT semi-honest multiparty PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-KMPRT-SH"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-DE-ROM","keywords":["two-party psi","dual_execution_oblivious_encoding","symmetric-key-only","parallelizable"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"Orrù-Orsini-Scholl OT extension in the programmable random oracle model"},"capabilities":["symmetric-key-only","parallelizable"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 2\\kappa^{2} \\) bits offline and \\( 6\\kappa n + \\beta n \\log n \\) bits online, \\( \\beta \\approx \\lambda + 2 \\log n - 1 \\) (Figure 8, p. 20); 1,092 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) in the WAN configuration (Figure 8, p. 20)","computation":"\\( O(Cn \\log n) \\) with \\( C \\approx \\kappa \\) the cost of one F_encode call (Theorem 2, p. 13); \\( m\\mu = O(n) \\) F_encode instances (§7.1, p. 22)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 2^{20} \\), one thread: 18 s total in LAN (10 Gbps) and 106 s in WAN (40 Mbps, 80 ms RTT); 2.9 s with 64 threads in LAN; two multi-core Intel Xeon processors with 256 GB RAM, \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) (Figure 6, p. 19; Figure 7, p. 19; §7, p. 20)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 4 (p. 12) with F_encode instantiated by Orrù-Orsini-Scholl OT extension, \\( m = n/4 \\) bins in LAN and \\( m = n/10 \\) in WAN with µ from Figure 5 (p. 18; §6.6, p. 17).","construction_family":"dual_execution_oblivious_encoding","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-DE-ROM","name":"RR17 DE-ROM","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious Alice or malicious Bob, UC (Theorem 2, p. 13); a corrupt party may input up to \\( m\\mu \\) items, fewer than \\( 4n \\) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (§6.3, p. 15)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"Bob only; two-party output is not supported (Figure 1, p. 4; §6.7, pp. 17-18)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"F_encode realized by the Orrù-Orsini-Scholl 1-out-of-N OT extension with a BCH-(511, 76, 171) code, proven in the programmable random oracle model (§4, p. 6; §7, p. 18); hash function agreed by coin tossing (Figure 4, step 1, p. 12); items first hashed to 128 bits, then phased (§7, p. 18)","technique":["dual_execution","ot_extension","permutation_based_hashing","simple_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious, UC, F_encode-hybrid; F_encode in the programmable random oracle model (Theorem 2, p. 13; §4, p. 6)","notion":"PSI with output to Bob; corrupt party's set bounded by \\( m\\mu \\)"},"status":"published","title":"Rindal-Rosulek malicious dual-execution PSI with random-oracle oblivious encodings","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","sections":[{"content":"Each bin position runs two oblivious encodings, one per direction; Alice sends common encodings for her items aggregated across bins, and Bob matches his own (Figure 4, p. 12). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Measurements are the authors', with both parties on one server over a simulated network (§7, p. 20). Figure 6 also lists a semi-honest protocol for comparison, which the paper marks as such (p. 19).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"rr17-rr17-de-rom","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2017","summary":"Each bin position runs two oblivious encodings, one per direction; Alice sends common encodings for her items aggregated across bins, and Bob matches his own (Figure 4, p. 12). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION.","title":"RR17 DE-ROM","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-DE-ROM"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-EC-ROM","keywords":["two-party psi","commitment_oblivious_encoding","symmetric-key-only","exact-sender-set-size"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"random-oracle commitment \\( H(x \\| r) \\) and Orrù-Orsini-Scholl OT extension in the programmable random oracle model"},"capabilities":["symmetric-key-only","exact-sender-set-size"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-ENCODE-COMMIT"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 2\\kappa^{2} \\) bits offline and \\( 3\\kappa n + n(C + D \\log n + \\log^{2} n) \\) bits online, with \\( C \\approx 2\\kappa \\) for a commitment and \\( D \\approx \\kappa \\) for a decommitment (Figure 8, p. 20); 1,322 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) in the WAN configuration (Figure 8, p. 20)","computation":"\\( O(Cn \\log n) \\) with \\( C \\approx \\kappa \\) the cost of one sender F_encode call (Theorem 3, p. 16)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 2^{20} \\), one thread: 12.6 s total in LAN (10 Gbps) and 255 s in WAN (40 Mbps, 80 ms RTT); 3.0 s with 64 threads in LAN (Figure 6, p. 19; Figure 7, p. 19); same machine as the other variants (§7, p. 20)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 9 (p. 27) with \\( H(x \\| r) \\) commitments and Orrù-Orsini-Scholl oblivious encodings; \\( m = n/4 \\) bins in LAN and \\( n/10 \\) in WAN (§6.6, p. 17; Figure 5, p. 18).","construction_family":"commitment_oblivious_encoding","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-EC-ROM","name":"RR17 EC-ROM","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious Alice or malicious Bob, UC (Theorem 3, p. 16); Alice is restricted to exactly n items (§6.5, p. 17)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"Bob only (Figure 1, p. 4; Figure 9, step 4, p. 27)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"commitment \\( \\mathrm{Comm}(x, r) = H(x \\| r) \\) in the non-programmable random oracle model (Appendix A, p. 26); F_encode by Orrù-Orsini-Scholl OT extension in the programmable random oracle model (§4, p. 6; §7, p. 18); hash function and commitment parameters by coin tossing (Figure 9, step 1, p. 27)","technique":["ot_extension","permutation_based_hashing","encode_commit","simple_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious, UC, F_encode-hybrid with an extractable hiding commitment, random oracle model (Theorem 3, p. 16; Appendix A, p. 26)","notion":"PSI with output to Bob; Alice restricted to exactly n items"},"status":"published","title":"Rindal-Rosulek malicious encode-commit PSI with random-oracle commitments and oblivious encodings","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","sections":[{"content":"Bob acts as encoding receiver for every bin position; Alice sends a commitment per item with tagged decommitments masked under PRFs of her encodings under Bob's instances, and Bob opens those whose tags match his own (Figure 9, p. 27). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-ENCODE-COMMIT.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Measurements are the authors', with both parties on one server over a simulated network (§7, p. 20). The abstract's 13 seconds and §1.2's 12.6 seconds for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) match this variant's single-thread LAN time (abstract, p. 1; §1.2, p. 3; Figure 6, p. 19).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"rr17-rr17-ec-rom","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2017","summary":"Bob acts as encoding receiver for every bin position; Alice sends a commitment per item with tagged decommitments masked under PRFs of her encodings under Bob's instances, and Bob opens those whose tags match his own (Figure 9, p. 27). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-ENCODE-COMMIT.","title":"RR17 EC-ROM","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-EC-ROM"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-SM","keywords":["two-party psi","dual_execution_oblivious_encoding","no-random-oracle"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"PRF, correlation-robust hash, and actively secure OT extension, without random oracles"},"capabilities":["no-random-oracle"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 2\\kappa^{2} + 6\\sigma\\kappa n \\) bits offline and \\( \\sigma n + \\beta n \\log n \\) bits online (Figure 8, p. 20); 7,708 MB for \\( \\sigma = 32 \\) and 22,183 MB for \\( \\sigma = 64 \\) at \\( n = 2^{20} \\) in the WAN configuration (Figure 8, p. 20)","computation":"σ OT instances per oblivious encoding (§4, p. 7); \\( O(Cn \\log n) \\) overall (Theorem 2, p. 13)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 2^{20} \\), one thread: 56 s (\\( \\sigma = 32 \\)) and 134 s (\\( \\sigma = 64 \\)) total in LAN, 1,322 s and 3,782 s in WAN (Figure 6, p. 19); same machine and networks as the random-oracle variant (§7, p. 20)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 4 (p. 12) with the standard-model F_encode of §4 (pp. 6-7), for \\( \\sigma = 32 \\) or 64 bit items.","construction_family":"dual_execution_oblivious_encoding","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-SM","name":"RR17 SM","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious Alice or malicious Bob, UC (Theorem 2, p. 13); a corrupt party may input up to \\( m\\mu \\) items (§6.3, p. 15)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"Bob only (Figure 1, p. 4; §6.7, pp. 17-18)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"F_encode realized by σ chosen-message 1-out-of-2 OTs with the Keller-Orsini-Scholl OT extension and a PRF per encoding (§4, pp. 6-7; §7, p. 18); items of 32 or 64 bits, since hashing to a smaller domain needs a random oracle for extraction (§7, p. 18)","technique":["dual_execution","ot_extension","permutation_based_hashing","simple_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious, UC, F_encode-hybrid, standard model (Theorem 2, p. 13; §4, pp. 6-7)","notion":"PSI with output to Bob; corrupt party's set bounded by \\( m\\mu \\)"},"status":"published","title":"Rindal-Rosulek malicious dual-execution PSI with standard-model oblivious encodings","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","sections":[{"content":"The dual-execution protocol of Figure 4 (p. 12), with each oblivious encoding built from σ chosen-message OTs whose outputs are combined through a PRF (§4, pp. 6-7). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The cost grows with the item length σ, and the paper evaluates only 32- and 64-bit items (§7, p. 18). Figure 6 marks the correlation-robust hash operations of this variant as \"(crh)\" (p. 19). Measurements are the authors'.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"rr17-rr17-sm","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2017","summary":"The dual-execution protocol of Figure 4 (p. 12), with each oblivious encoding built from σ chosen-message OTs whose outputs are combined through a PRF (§4, pp. 6-7). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION.","title":"RR17 SM","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-SM"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR","keywords":["unbalanced psi","oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","arbitrary-length-items","reusable-sender-preprocessing","malicious-receiver-security","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"mixed","name":"One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman in the random oracle model, and semantically secure leveled FHE (BFV)"},"capabilities":["arbitrary-length-items","reusable-sender-preprocessing","malicious-receiver-security","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS","PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(|Y| \\log |X|) \\) (§4.2, p. 9); the query is \\( m/k \\cdot \\log(|X|/m) \\) ciphertexts with \\( k = n/d \\) slots per ciphertext and the reply \\( m/k \\cdot \\alpha \\) ciphertexts (§3.1, p. 7)","computation":"sender online \\( \\Theta(|X|/k) \\) homomorphic multiplications (§3.1, p. 8); receiver work \\( O(|Y| \\log |X|) \\) (§6, p. 13); sender offline computation of symmetric-polynomial coefficients per bin (Figure 1, step 5, p. 13)","reported_measurement":"\\( |X| = 2^{20} \\), \\( |Y| = 512 \\), arbitrary-length items: online 1.0 s single-threaded, 1.5 MB receiver to sender and 2.5 MB sender to receiver, sender offline 36 s; \\( |X| = 2^{28} \\), \\( |Y| = 1024 \\): online 12.1 s with 32 sender threads, 8.2 MB and 10.2 MB, sender offline 4,628 s on 32 threads; 32-core Intel Xeon with 256 GB RAM, LAN with 10 Gbps and sub-millisecond latency, receiver limited to at most 4 threads (Figure 4, p. 20; §8, p. 18; abstract, p. 1)"},"configuration_note":"Figures 1-2 (pp. 13-14) without labels: the sender applies the Jarecki-Liu Diffie-Hellman OPRF and a random-oracle hash to \\( \\sigma = \\log_2(N_x N_y) + \\lambda \\) bits and simple-hashes with three hash functions; the receiver obtains its OPRF values interactively and cuckoo-hashes them into m bins; BFV in SEAL v2.3.0-4 with SIMD slots in \\( \\mathbb{F}_{t^{d}} \\) (\\( d \\log_2 t \\ge \\sigma \\)), split parameter B, window parameter w, optional modulus switching; the implementation uses symmetric-key BFV for fresh ciphertexts (§8.1, p. 18).","construction_family":"oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR","name":"CHLR18 PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"],"primitive":"unbalanced PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"one-sided simulation security against a malicious receiver (Theorem 1, p. 15); privacy, not simulation, against a malicious sender (Theorem 2, p. 16)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only; the sender outputs nothing (Figure 2, step 9, p. 14; Figure 3, p. 15)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"an OPRF exchange (the receiver sends \\( H(y)^{\\beta} \\), the sender answers), then the receiver's encrypted powers and the sender's reply (Figure 2, steps 7-8, p. 14); no round count is stated","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"the sender's pre-processing of its OPRF-processed set is non-interactive and reusable with many receivers (Figure 1, p. 13; Figure 4 caption, p. 20); hash functions are agreed by coin flipping (Figure 1, steps 1-2, p. 13); H is a random oracle (Figure 1, step 1, p. 13)","technique":["oprf","fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"one-sided simulation against a malicious receiver in the random oracle hybrid (Theorem 1, p. 15); privacy against a malicious sender (Theorem 2, p. 16)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver (Figure 3)"},"status":"published","title":"CHLR18 unbalanced PSI from Diffie-Hellman OPRF pre-processing and leveled BFV with extension-field SIMD slots","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","sections":[{"content":"This row is the protocol of Figures 1-2 (pp. 13-14) without labels. It combines OPRF pre-processing (PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER) and extension-field SIMD slots for long items (PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS) with the batched homomorphic intersection, windowing, splitting, and modulus switching of PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION. The sender splits each bin into α subsets of size at most B, computes the coefficients of each subset's symmetric polynomial over \\( \\mathbb{F}_{t^{d}} \\), and batches them (Figure 1, step 5). The receiver batches its cuckoo table of OPRF values, encrypts window powers, and sends them; the sender computes all powers up to B and returns one dot product per subset; the receiver outputs the items whose slot decrypts to zero in some subset (Figure 2, steps 7-9). Noise flooding, padding, and randomization of the polynomial are not used (§4.2, pp. 9-10).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The measurements are for the authors' LAN and machine, with FHE parameters chosen per set size and not listed (§8.2, p. 19; Figure 4, p. 20). The comparison with other unbalanced protocols in Figure 5 (p. 22) mixes numbers measured by the authors and numbers taken from the earlier paper (§8, p. 18).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"he_bfv12: the protocol row runs on BFV (SEAL v2.3.0-4) (CHLR18 §1.2, p. 2; §8, p. 18).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"chlr18-chlr18-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"unbalanced PSI · 2018","summary":"This row is the protocol of Figures 1-2 (pp. 13-14) without labels. It combines OPRF pre-processing (PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER) and extension-field SIMD slots for long items (PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS) with the batched homomorphic intersection, windowing, splitting, and modulus switching of PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION. The sender splits each bin into α subsets of size at most…","title":"CHLR18 PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-LABELED","keywords":["labeled psi","oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","labels-for-matching-items","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","reusable-sender-preprocessing","batched-keyword-pir"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"mixed","name":"One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman in the random oracle model, and semantically secure leveled FHE (BFV)"},"capabilities":["labels-for-matching-items","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","reusable-sender-preprocessing","batched-keyword-pir"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(|Y| \\log |X| \\sigma + |Y| \\ell) \\) for items of σ bits and labels of ℓ bits (§5.1, p. 12)","computation":"sender online \\( O(|X| \\ell) \\), a factor \\( \\ell/\\sigma \\) above unlabeled PSI; sender offline Newton interpolation of degree about \\( |X|/(m\\alpha) \\) per subset, \\( O(|X|^{2}/(m\\alpha)) \\cdot \\ell/\\sigma \\) in total (§5.1, p. 12)","reported_measurement":"\\( |X| = 2^{20} \\), \\( |Y| = 256 \\), 288-byte labels: server online 4.6 s, client encryption 0.77 s, communication 17.6 MB, against 20.5 s, 4.92 s, and 120 MB for multi-query SealPIR in the Pung retrieval setting; thread counts not stated (Figure 6, p. 22; §8.4, p. 21)"},"configuration_note":"Figures 1-2 (pp. 13-14) with labels, in the OPRF-optimized form of §5.2 (p. 12): each label is one-time-padded with the second part of its item's OPRF output and interpolated per subset; the receiver reads the label from the subset whose intersection polynomial decrypts to zero. Labels longer than items are split into chunks (§5.1, p. 11).","construction_family":"oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-LABELED","name":"CHLR18 labeled PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"],"primitive":"labeled PSI","properties":{"adversary":"conditional","adversary_scope":"Theorems 1 and 2 (pp. 15-16) cover the protocol of Figure 2 for the unlabeled functionality of Figure 3 (p. 15): one-sided simulation against a malicious receiver and privacy against a malicious sender; no theorem is stated for the labeled functionality","output":["labeled"],"output_recipients":"receiver only, which learns the labels of matching items (Figure 2, step 9, p. 14)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"as in the unlabeled protocol, with label-polynomial evaluations returned alongside the intersection results (Figure 2, steps 7-9, p. 14); no round count is stated","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"the sender interpolates one label polynomial per subset offline and batches it (Figure 1, step 5(b)ii and 5(c), p. 13); the pre-processing is reusable with many receivers (Figure 4 caption, p. 20); random oracle H and coin-flipped hash functions (Figure 1, steps 1-2, p. 13)","technique":["oprf","fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"Theorems 1-2 (pp. 15-16) stated for the unlabeled functionality","notion":"labeled PSI with output to the receiver (no ideal functionality stated for labels)"},"status":"published","title":"CHLR18 labeled PSI from OPRF-padded label-interpolation polynomials under leveled BFV","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","sections":[{"content":"This row is the labeled configuration of Figures 1-2 (pp. 13-14), realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI on top of the unlabeled row PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR. In the sender's pre-processing each subset gets a symmetric polynomial and a label polynomial over \\( \\mathbb{F}_{t^d} \\), both batched (Figure 1, step 5). In the online phase the sender evaluates both on the receiver's encrypted powers and returns both results; the receiver takes the label at each position where the intersection result is zero (Figure 2, steps 8-9).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The only reported measurement is the Pung comparison of Figure 6 (p. 22), for one set-size pair and one label length. The paper does not separate the cost of the label polynomials from that of the intersection polynomials in that experiment.","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"PIR-PAPER-2018-SEALPIR: measured against multi-query SealPIR in the Pung retrieval setting (CHLR18 Figure 6, p. 22).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"chlr18-chlr18-labeled-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"labeled PSI · 2018","summary":"This row is the labeled configuration of Figures 1-2 (pp. 13-14), realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI on top of the unlabeled row PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR. In the sender's pre-processing each subset gets a symmetric polynomial and a label polynomial over \\( \\mathbb{F}_{t^d} \\), both batched (Figure 1, step 5). In the online phase the sender evaluates both on the receiver's encrypted powers and returns both results;…","title":"CHLR18 labeled PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-LABELED"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-MAL-LEAKAGE","keywords":["unbalanced psi","oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","malicious-sender-with-leakage","malicious-receiver-security","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"mixed","name":"One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman in the random oracle model, and semantically secure H-limited leveled FHE"},"capabilities":["malicious-sender-with-leakage","malicious-receiver-security","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LEAKY-MALICIOUS-SENDER","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER"],"complexity":{"communication":"the sender returns only the label-polynomial ciphertexts, since Step 8 of Figure 2 runs without the symmetric polynomials (Figure 8, step 2, p. 25); no separate communication figure is reported","computation":"no separate figure is reported; §5.2 (p. 12) notes that not computing the symmetric polynomial saves work in this setting"},"configuration_note":"Figure 8 (p. 25): steps 1-7 of Figure 2 with each processed item's label set to \\( H(x') \\) for a random oracle H chosen so that the FHE scheme is \\( H' \\)-limited for the window powers, a zero-knowledge proof of one OPRF key, label polynomials only, and acceptance of \\( y' \\) when its decrypted label equals \\( H(y') \\).","construction_family":"oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-MAL-LEAKAGE","name":"CHLR18 leaky malicious PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR"],"primitive":"unbalanced PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious receiver or malicious sender, with non-black-box simulation for the sender and a malicious sender's set of polynomial size; the realized functionality is leaky (Theorem 3, p. 25; Figure 7, p. 25)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only: \\( X \\cap Y \\) with an honest sender, \\( X \\cap Y \\cap \\mathrm{leakage}(Y) \\) otherwise (Figure 8, p. 25)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"zero-knowledge proof that all OPRF answers use the same key (Figure 8, step 1b, p. 25), replaceable by aborting on degenerate answers (remarks, p. 26); jointly sampled hash functions, since sender-chosen ones with reused pre-processing leak one bit (§7.3, p. 18); random oracles H and the label hash (Figure 8, step 1a, p. 25)","technique":["oprf","fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"non-black-box simulation in the random oracle hybrid (Theorem 3, p. 25)","notion":"leaky PSI functionality \\( X \\cap Y \\cap \\mathrm{leakage}(Y) \\) (Figure 7)"},"status":"published","title":"CHLR18 malicious PSI with leakage: hash-valued labels under an H-limited leveled FHE assumption","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","sections":[{"content":"This row is the protocol of Figure 8 (p. 25), realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LEAKY-MALICIOUS-SENDER. It reuses the OPRF pre-processing of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER and the label polynomials of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI, with hash-valued labels as the only output channel, so that a correct label for y requires knowing y under the H-limited assumption (Definition 1, p. 17).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation or measurement of this variant is reported. The H-limited assumption is argued heuristically and supported by experiments, not reduced to a standard assumption (§7.3, p. 17).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"chlr18-chlr18-leaky-malicious-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"unbalanced PSI · 2018","summary":"This row is the protocol of Figure 8 (p. 25), realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LEAKY-MALICIOUS-SENDER. It reuses the OPRF pre-processing of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER and the label polynomials of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI, with hash-valued labels as the only output channel, so that a correct label for y requires knowing y under the H-limited assumption (Definition 1, p. 17).","title":"CHLR18 leaky malicious PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-MAL-LEAKAGE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSWW-2D","keywords":["circuit-psi","cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","any-symmetric-function-of-intersection","combined-stash","associated-data"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"Semi-honest generic two-party computation (GMW or Yao); no assumption stated"},"capabilities":["any-symmetric-function-of-intersection","combined-stash","associated-data"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-2D-CUCKOO-HASHING"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 4 \\cdot 2.4n(\\sigma - \\log_2(2.4n) + 1) + s'n(\\sigma - 1) \\) AND gates, \\( s' \\) the combined stash size (§6.2, p. 22), at two ciphertexts per AND gate in GMW with OT extension (§2, p. 6); lookup-table evaluation reduces communication by up to 4.1 times (§6.2, p. 23)","computation":"symmetric-key operations of GMW; hashing for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) takes 873.19 ms for 2D cuckoo hashing and 782.32 ms for cuckoo hashing into two tables (Table 5, p. 25)","reported_measurement":"PSI-CAT on \\( n = 2^{20} \\) items of 32 bits, one thread: 86,648 ms (LAN) and 389,891 ms (WAN), 6,950.6 MB with GMW; two Intel Core i7-4790 machines, LAN 1 Gbit/s with 1 ms RTT, WAN 100 Mbit/s with 100 ms RTT, \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), statistical parameter 40 (Tables 4 and 6, pp. 25-26; §6.3, p. 24)"},"configuration_note":"Alice uses iterative 2D cuckoo hashing with two tables of \\( 2.4n \\) bins of capacity two and no stash (Algorithm 3, p. 16; §6.1, pp. 19-20); Bob uses standard cuckoo hashing into both tables with one combined stash (§5.2, p. 17; Table 1); items are shortened by permutation-based hashing; the circuit compares Bob's item with Alice's two items per bin and each stashed item with all of Alice's items, then computes f (§6.2, p. 22).","construction_family":"cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSWW-2D","name":"PSWW 2D cuckoo circuit-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW"],"primitive":"circuit-PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest Alice and Bob (§2, p. 6); no extension to malicious adversaries is seen (§5.4, p. 18)","output":["circuit"],"output_recipients":"both parties agree on a symmetric function f and a circuit for it and compute \\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) (§2, p. 5); the measured instance outputs whether the intersection size exceeds a threshold (PSI-CAT) (§6.3, p. 24)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"the GMW online phase needs one round per layer of AND gates; the circuit depth is \\( \\log_2 \\sigma \\), as for Circuit-Phasing (§2, p. 6; §6.2, p. 23)","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"hash functions agreed in advance, chosen independently of the inputs (§5.2, p. 16); GMW precomputes multiplication triples with OT extension in an input-independent setup phase (§2, p. 6); the circuit is evaluated with GMW in the ABY framework (§6.3, p. 24)","technique":["cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing","gmw"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; security is inherited from the generic protocol, no proof given (§5.2, p. 16)","notion":"\\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for a symmetric function f, revealing nothing else"},"status":"published","title":"PSWW circuit-based PSI from iterative 2D cuckoo hashing with two-item bins and a combined stash, semi-honest two-party, GMW","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/120","sections":[{"content":"The iterative 2D cuckoo hashing protocol with a combined stash (\"Ours Iterative Combined\", §5.2, pp. 16-17; §6.2, p. 22), built on PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-2D-CUCKOO-HASHING. For the measured PSI-CAT instance the per-element match bits feed a Hamming-weight circuit and a depth-optimized comparison with the threshold (§6.3, p. 24). The circuit may also output a function of data associated with each item (§1.3, p. 4).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The parameters rest on simulations with failure probability below \\( 2^{-40} \\) for \\( n \\ge 2^{13} \\) (§6.1, p. 20). The run times are for the authors' machines and networks (§6.3, p. 24) and are not comparable with measurements from other papers unless a source makes the comparison.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"psww18-psww-2d-cuckoo-circuit","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"circuit-PSI · 2018","summary":"The iterative 2D cuckoo hashing protocol with a combined stash (\"Ours Iterative Combined\", §5.2, pp. 16-17; §6.2, p. 22), built on PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-2D-CUCKOO-HASHING. For the measured PSI-CAT instance the per-element match bits feed a Hamming-weight circuit and a depth-optimized comparison with the threshold (§6.3, p. 24). The circuit may also output a function of data associated with each item (§1.3, p. 4).","title":"PSWW 2D cuckoo circuit-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSWW-2D"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSWW-MIRROR","keywords":["circuit-psi","cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","any-symmetric-function-of-intersection","non-constant-stash"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"Semi-honest generic two-party computation (GMW or Yao); no assumption stated"},"capabilities":["any-symmetric-function-of-intersection","non-constant-stash"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-ALMOST-LINEAR-CIRCUIT"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 4 \\cdot 2.4n(\\sigma - \\log_2(2.4n) + 1) + 3s'n(\\sigma - 1) \\) AND gates with six stashes combined into \\( s' \\le 2s \\) (§6.2, p. 22); asymptotically \\( \\omega(\\sigma n) \\) (§6.2, p. 22)","computation":"Bob runs four cuckoo insertions, Alice two, then relocates items that landed in different columns without further insertions (§4.1, pp. 11-12; Algorithm 1, p. 12)","reported_measurement":"not implemented; computed circuit size for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and \\( \\sigma = 32 \\) is 410,700,868 AND gates with a combined stash, against 215,665,732 for the 2D construction (Table 3, p. 23)"},"configuration_note":"Four tables of \\( 2(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) bins, two subtables each, with the right tables reusing the left hash functions and mirroring the lower row (§4.1, pp. 10-12; Figure 1, p. 11); stashes of size \\( \\omega(1) \\); the circuit of §4.2 (p. 13).","construction_family":"cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSWW-MIRROR","name":"PSWW mirror cuckoo circuit-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW"],"primitive":"circuit-PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest Alice and Bob (§2, p. 6)","output":["circuit"],"output_recipients":"both parties compute \\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for an agreed symmetric function f (§2, p. 5)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"table sizes, stash sizes, and hash functions agreed in advance (§4.2, p. 13); a generic two-party computation protocol evaluates the circuit (§4.2, p. 13)","technique":["cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; failure probability negligible in n with stashes of size \\( \\omega(1) \\) (§4.1, p. 13; Appendix C, p. 34)","notion":"\\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for a symmetric function f, revealing nothing else"},"status":"published","title":"PSWW circuit-based PSI from mirror cuckoo hashing with \\( \\omega(1) \\) stashes, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/120","sections":[{"content":"The protocol of §4.2 (p. 13) on top of the hashing of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-ALMOST-LINEAR-CIRCUIT. The authors did not implement it, because §6.2 shows it is always worse than the iterative 2D construction (§6.3, p. 24).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"An asymptotic construction; the authors state that its analysis is not intended to be tight (§4, p. 10).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"psww18-psww-mirror-cuckoo-circuit","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"circuit-PSI · 2018","summary":"The protocol of §4.2 (p. 13) on top of the hashing of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-ALMOST-LINEAR-CIRCUIT. The authors did not implement it, because §6.2 shows it is always worse than the iterative 2D construction (§6.3, p. 24).","title":"PSWW mirror cuckoo circuit-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSWW-MIRROR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSZ-OT","keywords":["two-party psi","oprf_cuckoo_hashing","item-length-independent-oprf-cost"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"random OT extension with a hash modeled as a random oracle"},"capabilities":["item-length-independent-oprf-cost"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-BIT-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-OT-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-HASHING-PARAMETERS"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 512(s + b) \\) bits from P2 and \\( k \\cdot \\ell \\cdot n_1 \\) bits from P1, with \\( \\ell = \\lambda + \\log_2(k n_1) + \\log_2(n_2) \\) (§5, 'Efficiency', p. 23); \\( 512 n_2 + (k + s) n_1 \\ell \\) bits in Table 8 (p. 26)","computation":"\\( 3 n_2 + (k + s) n_1 \\) symmetric-key operations (Table 8, p. 26); P1 performs \\( (k + s) n_1 \\) correlation-robust function evaluations, independent of µ (§5, 'Efficiency', p. 23)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 2^{20} \\) items per party: 5,847 ms in LAN (Gigabit, Intel Haswell i7-4770K at 3.5 GHz) and 58,418 ms in WAN (98 Mbit/s, 94 ms RTT), 111.299 MB, one thread, no precomputation (Tables 9-10, p. 29; §6.2.1, p. 26); two sets of a billion 128-bit items with data on SSD: 34.2 hours (§6.2.4, pp. 30-31)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol 3 (p. 22): P1 simple hashing and P2 cuckoo hashing with k hash functions, \\( b = \\varepsilon n_2 \\) bins and a stash of size s, items shortened by permutation-based hashing, and one 1-out-of-\\( 2^{\\mu} \\) random OT per bin and stash position; parameters from §3 (Tables 5-7; Table 11, p. 30 for unequal set sizes).","construction_family":"oprf_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSZ-OT","name":"PSZ18 OT + hashing","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest P1 and P2 (§2.1, p. 8; §5, 'Security', p. 23)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"P2 only (Protocol 3, 'Output', p. 22)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"512 base OTs for the 1-out-of-N OT extension with a \\( [2^{77}, 512, 129] \\) BCH code (§2.2.3, p. 11; §6.2.2, pp. 28-29); the proof assumes an ideal 1-out-of-N random OT functionality, whose efficient instantiation uses a hash modeled as a random oracle (§5, p. 23; §2.1, p. 8)","technique":["batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing","simple_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest, simulation-based, with an ideal 1-out-of-N random OT functionality (§5, 'Security', p. 23)","notion":"PSI with output to P2"},"status":"published","title":"Pinkas-Schneider-Zohner semi-honest OT-based PSI with cuckoo hashing, permutation-based hashing, and 1-out-of-N random OT","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/930","sections":[{"content":"Hashing, OPRF evaluation via 1-out-of-N random OT for each bin and stash position, and a plaintext intersection of P1's permuted OPRF outputs with P2's (Protocol 3, p. 22). Mechanism and parameters: PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-BIT-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-OT-PSI and PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-HASHING-PARAMETERS.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Measurements are the authors', from a same-platform comparison of many PSI paradigms with \\( \\sigma = 32 \\)-bit elements and 128-bit security (§6.2, pp. 26-29). The paper also runs the protocol on unequal set sizes (Tables 12-13, pp. 30-31); the protocol does not exploit the imbalance and stays balanced here.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"psz18-psz18-ot-hashing","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2018","summary":"Hashing, OPRF evaluation via 1-out-of-N random OT for each bin and stash position, and a plaintext intersection of P1's permuted OPRF outputs with P2's (Protocol 3, p. 22). Mechanism and parameters: PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-BIT-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-OT-PSI and PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-HASHING-PARAMETERS.","title":"PSZ18 OT + hashing","type":"construction","venue":"ACM Transactions on Privacy and Security 21(2)","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-PSZ-OT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-GS-AHE","keywords":["two-party threshold psi","polynomial_threshold_psi","sublinear-in-set-size","size-hiding-cardinality-test"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"IND-CPA additively homomorphic encryption (Paillier or Damgård-Jurik) and passively secure OLE"},"capabilities":["sublinear-in-set-size","size-hiding-cardinality-test"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( \\tilde O(t^{2}) \\) in the plain model (Lemma 4, p. 14), from the private cardinality test with \\( \\tilde O(t^{2}) \\) (Lemma 3, p. 12; Theorem 2, p. 10) and noisy polynomial addition with \\( O(\\ell \\log p) \\), \\( \\ell = 3t + 1 \\) (§7.2, p. 17)","computation":"each party interpolates a rational function from \\( 3t + 1 \\) points in the clear (Figure 7, p. 13); described as computationally more efficient than the FHE-based protocol (§3, p. 7)"},"configuration_note":"Π_TPSI of Figure 7 (p. 13) with F_PICT instantiated by Π_PICT (Figure 5, p. 11) and the singularity test of Kiltz et al., and F_NPA by Π_NPA (Figure 10, p. 17) over OLE.","construction_family":"polynomial_threshold_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-GS-AHE","name":"Ghosh-Simkin AHE threshold PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"],"primitive":"two-party threshold PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"static passive adversary corrupting Alice or Bob (§2.2, p. 6; Theorem 4, p. 14)","output":["threshold"],"output_recipients":"both parties receive the intersection if the symmetric difference is at most \\( 2t \\), otherwise ⊥ (Figure 6, p. 12)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"the singularity test of Kiltz et al. uses \\( O(\\log k) \\) rounds for \\( k \\times k \\) matrices (Theorem 2, p. 10); the round count of the whole protocol is not stated","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"Alice's homomorphic key pair (Figure 5, p. 11); public evaluation points \\( \\alpha_1, \\dots, \\alpha_{3t+1} \\) (Figure 7, p. 13); OLE instances for noisy polynomial addition, passively secure with constant overhead (§7.2, p. 17)","technique":["additively_homomorphic_encryption","ole","polynomial_sparsity_test","set_reconciliation"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"UC, static passive adversary; Theorem 4 in the (F_PICT, F_NPA)-hybrid model and Theorem 5 in the F_OLE-hybrid model (pp. 14, 16)","notion":"threshold PSI (intersection if the symmetric difference is at most \\( 2t \\))"},"status":"published","title":"Ghosh-Simkin two-party threshold PSI from additively homomorphic encryption and OLE, semi-honest, \\( \\tilde O(t^{2}) \\) communication","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","sections":[{"content":"The main protocol of the paper, PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI: a private intersection cardinality test, then noisy polynomial addition and local interpolation (§§5-7, pp. 10-17).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation or measurement is reported; the authors do not claim practicality (§1.1, p. 2).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"gs19-ghosh-simkin-ahe-threshold","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party threshold PSI · 2019","summary":"The main protocol of the paper, PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI: a private intersection cardinality test, then noisy polynomial addition and local interpolation (§§5-7, pp. 10-17).","title":"Ghosh-Simkin AHE threshold PSI","type":"construction","venue":"CRYPTO 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-GS-AHE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-GS-FHE","keywords":["two-party threshold psi","polynomial_threshold_psi","sublinear-in-set-size"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"lattice","name":"LWE or Ring-LWE"},"capabilities":["sublinear-in-set-size"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-FHE-UPPER-BOUND"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 2t + 1 \\) ciphertexts from Alice and t ciphertexts from Bob, \\( O(t \\log p) \\) bits for elements of \\( \\mathbb{F}_p \\) (§3, p. 8)","computation":"Bob interpolates a rational function homomorphically from \\( 2t \\) encrypted points, which the authors call far from practical (§3, p. 8)"},"configuration_note":"Set reconciliation of Minsky, Trachtenberg, and Zippel evaluated under FHE, sketched in §3 (p. 8).","construction_family":"polynomial_threshold_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-GS-FHE","name":"Ghosh-Simkin FHE threshold PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"],"primitive":"two-party threshold PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"static passive adversary (§2.2, p. 6)","output":["threshold"],"output_recipients":"Alice receives the numerator polynomial \\( p_{A \\setminus B} \\), from which she computes the intersection, or ⊥ (§3, p. 8)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"two messages, Alice to Bob and Bob to Alice (§3, p. 8)","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"Alice's FHE key pair; public evaluation points \\( \\alpha_1, \\dots, \\alpha_{2t} \\) and a uniformly random z sent in the clear (§3, p. 8)","technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption","set_reconciliation"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"passive adversary; security follows from the security of FHE, stated without a formal proof (§3, p. 8)","notion":"threshold PSI"},"status":"published","title":"Ghosh-Simkin two-party threshold PSI from FHE-evaluated set reconciliation, semi-honest, \\( O(t \\log p) \\) communication","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","sections":[{"content":"The FHE upper bound of §3 (p. 8), PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-FHE-UPPER-BOUND.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"A sketch whose details are left to the reader (§3, p. 8). The lattice assumption is the one the paper names for known FHE instantiations (§3, p. 8).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"gs19-ghosh-simkin-fhe-threshold","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party threshold PSI · 2019","summary":"The FHE upper bound of §3 (p. 8), PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-FHE-UPPER-BOUND.","title":"Ghosh-Simkin FHE threshold PSI","type":"construction","venue":"CRYPTO 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-GS-FHE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-KRSSW-GC","keywords":["unbalanced psi","oprf_cuckoo_filter_precomputation","precomputation","server-set-updates","malicious-client-security","no-online-server-cryptography","client-storage-linear-in-server-set"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"LowMC as a pseudorandom function and maliciously secure OT, without a proof"},"capabilities":["precomputation","server-set-updates","malicious-client-security","no-online-server-cryptography","client-storage-linear-in-server-set"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-LOWMC-OPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION"],"complexity":{"communication":"online: one correction bit per input bit from the client and one masked label per input bit from the server, 2 KiB server to client per client item (Figure 1, p. 9; Table 5, p. 13); base: one garbled circuit of 624 AND gates per precomputed item (Table 2, p. 8); setup: a filter linear in the server set, 1,072 MiB for \\( 2^{28} \\) items (Table 4, p. 13)","computation":"online: the client evaluates one garbled circuit per item and looks it up in the filter, and the server performs no cryptographic operations (§6.2, p. 13); setup: the server evaluates LowMC on every item of its set (Table 4, p. 13)","reported_measurement":"\\( N_s = 2^{28} \\), \\( N_c = 1024 \\), 128-bit items: base plus online 3.54 s (WiFi) and 8.59 s (LTE), 22.01 MiB server to client and 2.02 MiB client to server; setup transfer 1,072 MiB and server setup 1,869.13 s; one thread each, server Intel Core i7-4600U, client Google Pixel XL 2 (Table 6, p. 14; §6, p. 12)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 1 (p. 9): LowMC instance (3) of Table 2 (p. 8) as the PRF, Yao garbling with Free-XOR and half-gates and fixed-key AES (§5.2, p. 12), correlated-OT precomputation with per-label masks (§4.3, p. 8), cuckoo filter with \\( \\varepsilon_{\\max} = 2^{-29.4} \\), 32-bit tags, and bucket size 3 (Table 4, p. 13). An AES-128 variant, AES-GC-PSI, is measured alongside (Tables 3-5, p. 13).","construction_family":"oprf_cuckoo_filter_precomputation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-KRSSW-GC","name":"LowMC-GC-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW"],"primitive":"unbalanced PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious","semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"malicious client, using maliciously secure base OTs and OT extension; semi-honest server; argued in prose without a proof (§4.5, pp. 10-11; §5.1, pp. 11-12)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"client only (Figure 1, p. 9)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"online phase: the client sends correction bits and the server answers with masked labels, after which the client evaluates the precomputed circuits and queries the filter locally (Figure 1, p. 9); no round count is stated","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"base phase: random correlated OTs by OT extension and one garbled LowMC circuit per future client item, repeated once the precomputed items are used up; setup phase: the server sends a compressed cuckoo filter of PRF values of its whole set, which the client stores; update phase: one tag and bucket index per changed item (Figure 1, p. 9; §3.5, p. 6)","technique":["garbled_circuits","oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_filter"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious client and semi-honest server, argued informally (§4.5, pp. 10-11)","notion":"PSI with output to the client, with precomputation"},"status":"published","title":"KRSSW19 LowMC-GC-PSI: precomputed unbalanced PSI with a garbled LowMC OPRF, correlated-OT precomputation, and a compressed cuckoo filter","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","sections":[{"content":"This row is the GC-PSI protocol of Figure 1 (p. 9), based on the precomputation protocol of Kiss et al. and the cuckoo filters of Resende and Aranha. It uses the LowMC parameters of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-LOWMC-OPRF, the correlated-OT precomputation of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION, and the filter encoding of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION, as implemented in PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION. The server inserts \\( \\mathrm{PRF}_k(x) \\) for every item into the filter; online, the client obtains the input labels of its items through the precomputed correlated OTs, evaluates the garbled circuits to get \\( \\mathrm{PRF}_k(y) \\), and outputs the items found in the filter.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The filter has a false-positive probability near \\( 2^{-29} \\) per item (§4.1, p. 6). Measurements are single-threaded on the authors' phone and laptop over real WiFi and LTE (§6, p. 12) and are not comparable with server-class LAN measurements in other papers unless a source makes the comparison.","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-YAO: the OPRF is a precomputed Yao garbled circuit (KRSSW19 §3.2, p. 5; Figure 1, p. 9). MPC-COMP-CORR-OTEXT: base phase runs correlated OTs by OT extension (KRSSW19 Figure 1, p. 9).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"krssw19-lowmc-gc-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"unbalanced PSI · 2019","summary":"This row is the GC-PSI protocol of Figure 1 (p. 9), based on the precomputation protocol of Kiss et al. and the cuckoo filters of Resende and Aranha. It uses the LowMC parameters of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-LOWMC-OPRF, the correlated-OT precomputation of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION, and the filter encoding of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION, as implemented in…","title":"LowMC-GC-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"USENIX Security 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-KRSSW-GC"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-KRSSW-NR","keywords":["unbalanced psi","oprf_cuckoo_filter_precomputation","precomputation","server-set-updates","malicious-client-security","client-storage-linear-in-server-set"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"group","name":"Naor-Reingold PRF over P-256 and maliciously secure OT, without a proof"},"capabilities":["precomputation","server-set-updates","malicious-client-security","client-storage-linear-in-server-set"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION"],"complexity":{"communication":"online: per client item one correction bit and one correlated message per input bit and one group element \\( \\tilde g_i \\) (Figure 2, p. 10), 4,147 KiB server to client for \\( 2^{10} \\) items (Table 5, p. 13); base: 0.01 MiB server to client for \\( 2^{10} \\) items (Table 3, p. 13); setup: 1,072 MiB filter for \\( 2^{28} \\) items (Table 4, p. 13)","computation":"online: per client item the server computes \\( g^{a_0 \\cdot r_{\\mathrm{inv}}} \\) and the client one exponentiation (point multiplication) and a filter lookup (Figure 2, p. 10); setup: the server computes the Naor-Reingold PRF on every item, 52,332.38 s for \\( 2^{28} \\) items single-threaded (Table 4, p. 13)","reported_measurement":"\\( N_s = 2^{28} \\), \\( N_c = 1024 \\), 128-bit items: base plus online 2.92 s (WiFi) and 6.53 s (LTE), 4.07 MiB server to client and 2.00 MiB client to server; setup transfer 1,072 MiB and server setup 52,332.38 s; one thread each, server Intel Core i7-4600U, client Google Pixel XL 2 (Table 6, p. 14; §6, p. 12)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 2 (p. 10): Naor-Reingold PRF \\( f_k(x) = g^{a_0 \\cdot \\prod a_j^{x_j}} \\) evaluated obliviously with one OT per input bit as proposed by Hazay and Lindell, with correlated-OT precomputation (§4.4, p. 10), instantiated over the elliptic curve P-256 with MIRACL (§5.3, p. 12), and the cuckoo filter of Table 4 (p. 13).","construction_family":"oprf_cuckoo_filter_precomputation","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-KRSSW-NR","name":"ECC-NR-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW"],"primitive":"unbalanced PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious","semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"malicious client, using maliciously secure base OTs and OT extension; semi-honest server; argued in prose without a proof (§4.5, pp. 10-11; §5.1, pp. 11-12)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"client only (Figure 2, p. 10)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"online phase: the client sends correction bits, the server answers with one correlated message per input bit and one group element per item, and the client queries the filter locally (Figure 2, p. 10); no round count is stated","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"base phase: random OTs by OT extension, α per future client item; setup phase: the server sends a compressed cuckoo filter of Naor-Reingold PRF values of its whole set, which the client stores; updates as in GC-PSI (Figure 2, p. 10; §3.5, p. 6)","technique":["oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_filter"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious client and semi-honest server, argued informally (§4.5, pp. 10-11)","notion":"PSI with output to the client, with precomputation"},"status":"published","title":"KRSSW19 ECC-NR-PSI: precomputed unbalanced PSI with an oblivious Naor-Reingold PRF over P-256, correlated-OT precomputation, and a compressed cuckoo filter","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","sections":[{"content":"This row is the NR-PSI protocol of Figure 2 (p. 10), based on the protocols of Hazay and Lindell and of Kiss et al. and on the cuckoo filters of Resende and Aranha. It uses the correlated-OT precomputation of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION and the filter encoding of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION, as implemented in PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION. For each input bit the client receives either \\( r \\) or \\( r \\cdot a_j \\) masked through the precomputed OTs, multiplies the received values, and raises the server's \\( g^{a_0 \\cdot r_{\\mathrm{inv}}} \\) to that product to obtain \\( f_k(y) \\), which it looks up in the filter.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The base phase sends 12 times less than LowMC-GC-PSI, the online phase is slightly slower except for a single item, and the one-time setup is much slower because of elliptic-curve operations (§6.1, p. 13). Measurements are single-threaded on the authors' devices over real networks (§6, p. 12).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-OTEXT: base phase runs random OTs by OT extension (KRSSW19 Figure 2, p. 10).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"krssw19-ecc-nr-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"unbalanced PSI · 2019","summary":"This row is the NR-PSI protocol of Figure 2 (p. 10), based on the protocols of Hazay and Lindell and of Kiss et al. and on the cuckoo filters of Resende and Aranha. It uses the correlated-OT precomputation of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION and the filter encoding of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION, as implemented in PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION. For each input bit the client…","title":"ECC-NR-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"USENIX Security 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-KRSSW-NR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-ADVANCED","keywords":["circuit-psi","opprf_cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","linear-communication","role-reversal-for-stash"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"Pseudorandom functions"},"capabilities":["linear-communication","role-reversal-for-stash"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(n\\kappa) \\) for the two OPPRF phases and \\( 2n + s^{2} \\) comparisons of γ-bit values, \\( O(n) \\) comparisons for a stash of size \\( O(\\log n) \\) (§5.1, p. 19); concretely the stash step of the basic protocol is replaced by \\( B \\cdot (450 + (\\gamma - 1) \\cdot 256) \\) bits (§7.3, p. 25); 10,277 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 3, p. 25)","computation":"two batched OPPRF executions and their polynomial interpolations (Protocol 10, p. 20)","reported_measurement":"estimated, not measured: 148,436 ms (LAN) and 597,885 ms (WAN) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) with arbitrary bitlength and one thread (Table 5, p. 27; §7.4, p. 26)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol 10 (p. 20): \\( K = 2 \\) cuckoo hashing with a stash of size s; phase 1 covers P1's table items, phase 2 maps P1's stashed items by simple hashing against P2's cuckoo table, and phase 3 compares the two stashes pairwise in \\( s^{2} \\) circuit sections.","construction_family":"opprf_cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-ADVANCED","name":"PSTY advanced (dual-execution) OPPRF circuit-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY"],"primitive":"circuit-PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest P1 and P2 (§2.2, p. 8)","output":["circuit"],"output_recipients":"both parties obtain \\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for the agreed function f (§1, p. 1)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"three phases: OPPRF with P1 as receiver, OPPRF with roles reversed on P1's stashed items, then one circuit (Protocol 10, p. 20); circuit depth \\( \\max(\\log \\sigma, \\log \\gamma) \\) (§7.3, p. 26)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"batched OPPRF in the F_OPRF-hybrid model (Theorem 8, p. 15); generic two-party computation (Functionality 1, p. 9); cost estimates assume the OPRF of Kolesnikov et al. at 450 bits per evaluation and GMW (§7.3, p. 24; §7.4, p. 26)","technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","cuckoo_hashing","gmw"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; security from the OPPRF and 2PC functionalities (§5.1, p. 20); failure negligible in the statistical parameter (§5, p. 19)","notion":"\\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for a symmetric function f"},"status":"published","title":"PSTY circuit-based PSI with linear communication via a role-reversed OPPRF phase for the stash, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","sections":[{"content":"The asymptotically linear protocol of §5.1 (pp. 19-20), which the paper calls dual execution; here the second execution handles the stash and is not a malicious-security technique. It realizes the asymptotic part of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Not implemented; its run times are estimates from the interpolation experiments and earlier measurements (§7.4, p. 26). It computes at least two copies of the basic circuit, which is why the stash-less variant is concretely cheaper (§5.2, p. 20).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"psty19-psty-advanced-dual-execution","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"circuit-PSI · 2019","summary":"The asymptotically linear protocol of §5.1 (pp. 19-20), which the paper calls dual execution; here the second execution handles the stash and is not a malicious-security technique. It realizes the asymptotic part of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION.","title":"PSTY advanced (dual-execution) OPPRF circuit-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-ADVANCED"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-BASIC","keywords":["circuit-psi","opprf_cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","payloads"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"Pseudorandom functions"},"capabilities":["payloads"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( B \\cdot 450 \\) bits for the OPRFs, hints of \\( B \\cdot \\max_b \\cdot \\gamma \\) bits, \\( B(\\gamma - 1) \\cdot 256 \\) bits for the bin comparisons, and \\( sn(\\sigma - 1) \\cdot 256 \\) bits for comparing the s stashed items with all n items of P2 (§7.3, p. 24); super-linear because a negligible failure probability needs \\( s = O(\\log n / \\log \\log n) \\) (§4.2, p. 18); 8,123 MB (\\( \\sigma = 32 \\)) and 12,731 MB (arbitrary σ) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 3, p. 25)","computation":"polynomial interpolation over mega-bins, \\( O(n \\log n) \\) asymptotically with Lagrange interpolation for \\( n/\\log n \\) mega-bins (§4.3, p. 18)","reported_measurement":"estimated, not measured: 182,204 ms (LAN) and 752,695 ms (WAN) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) with arbitrary bitlength and one thread (Table 5, p. 27; §7.4, p. 26)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol 9 (p. 17): \\( K = 2 \\) cuckoo hashing into \\( \\beta = 2.4n \\) bins with a stash of size s from Table 1 (p. 22), one batched OPPRF over all bins, and a circuit with β bin sections and \\( s \\cdot n \\) stash sections.","construction_family":"opprf_cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-BASIC","name":"PSTY basic OPPRF circuit-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY"],"primitive":"circuit-PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest P1 and P2 (§2.2, p. 8)","output":["circuit"],"output_recipients":"both parties obtain \\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for the agreed function f (§1, p. 1)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"OPPRF phase followed by one circuit evaluation (Protocol 9, p. 17); circuit depth \\( \\max(\\log \\sigma, \\log \\gamma) \\) (§7.3, p. 26)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"batched OPPRF in the F_OPRF-hybrid model (Theorem 8, p. 15); generic two-party computation (Functionality 1, p. 9); cost estimates assume the OPRF of Kolesnikov et al. and GMW (§7.3, p. 24)","technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","cuckoo_hashing","gmw"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; security follows from the OPPRF and 2PC functionalities (§4.1, p. 17); fails if the stash overflows (§4.2, pp. 17-18)","notion":"\\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for a symmetric function f"},"status":"published","title":"PSTY basic circuit-based PSI from batched OPPRF with a compared stash, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","sections":[{"content":"The basic protocol of §4.1 (pp. 16-17), built on PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF. Each bin needs one comparison, but every stashed item is still compared with all of P2's items, which keeps the communication super-linear (§4, p. 16; §5, p. 19).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Not implemented; run times are estimates (§7.4, p. 26).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"psty19-psty-basic-opprf-circuit","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"circuit-PSI · 2019","summary":"The basic protocol of §4.1 (pp. 16-17), built on PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF. Each bin needs one comparison, but every stashed item is still compared with all of P2's items, which keeps the communication super-linear (§4, p. 16; §5, p. 19).","title":"PSTY basic OPPRF circuit-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-BASIC"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-NO-STASH","keywords":["circuit-psi","opprf_cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","linear-communication","no-stash","payloads"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"Pseudorandom functions"},"capabilities":["linear-communication","no-stash","payloads"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( B \\cdot 450 \\) bits for the OPRFs, hints of \\( B \\cdot \\max_b \\cdot \\gamma \\) bits, and \\( B(\\gamma - 1) \\cdot 256 \\) bits for the bin comparisons, with \\( B = 1.27n \\) bins (§7.3, p. 24; §5.2, p. 20); 2,540 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) for both fixed and arbitrary bitlengths, 96% of it the circuit (Table 3, p. 25)","computation":"Lagrange interpolation of hint polynomials of degree at most 1,024 per mega-bin, 45,335 ms estimated for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) with \\( K = 3 \\) (§7.2 and Table 2, pp. 23-24)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 2^{20} \\), arbitrary bitlength, one thread: 120,731 ms (LAN) and 261,481 ms (WAN); two Intel Core i7-4770K PCs, \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), \\( K = 3 \\), \\( 1.27n \\) bins, no stash, LAN 1 Gbit/s with 1 ms RTT and WAN 100 Mbit/s with 100 ms RTT, averaged over 50 runs (Table 5, p. 27; §7.4, pp. 26-27)"},"configuration_note":"Cuckoo hashing with \\( K = 3 \\) hash functions into \\( \\beta = 1.27n \\) bins and no stash for P1, simple hashing with the same three functions for P2, one batched OPPRF over all bins with mega-bins of at most 1,024 elements, and one γ-bit equality per bin in the circuit (§5.2, pp. 20-21; §7.2, pp. 23-24; Protocol 9, p. 17).","construction_family":"opprf_cuckoo_hashing_circuit_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-NO-STASH","name":"PSTY no-stash OPPRF circuit-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY"],"primitive":"circuit-PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest P1 (receiver) and P2 (sender) (§2.1-2.2, p. 8)","output":["circuit"],"output_recipients":"both parties obtain \\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for the agreed function f (§1, p. 1; §2.1, p. 8)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"the circuit depth is \\( \\max(\\log \\sigma, \\log \\gamma) \\), 6 or 7 for the parameters of Table 4; GMW needs one online round per layer of AND gates (§7.3, pp. 25-26; Table 4, p. 26; §2.3, p. 9)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"batched OPPRF in the F_OPRF-hybrid model (Theorem 8, p. 15), implemented on the OPRF of Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner (§7.4 and footnote 9, p. 27); generic two-party computation (Functionality 1, p. 9) by GMW in the ABY framework (§7.4, p. 27)","technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","cuckoo_hashing","gmw"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; OPPRF from a PRF (Theorem 5) in the F_OPRF-hybrid model (Theorem 8); protocol security from the OPPRF and 2PC functionalities (§4.1, p. 17); hashing failure below \\( 2^{-40} \\) by the experiments of Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner (§5.2, p. 20)","notion":"\\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) for a symmetric function f"},"status":"published","title":"PSTY stash-less circuit-based PSI from batched OPPRF with three-function cuckoo hashing, semi-honest two-party, GMW","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","sections":[{"content":"The stash-less protocol of §5.2 (pp. 20-21), the only one the authors implemented (§7.4, p. 26). It instantiates PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION with the batched OPPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF. Payloads can be added as in PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-PAYLOAD at the same asymptotic cost.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The absence of a stash rests on empirical failure estimates, not an analysis (§5.2, p. 20). Times are for the authors' setting and are not comparable with other papers unless a source makes the comparison; the comparisons in Table 5 extrapolate earlier protocols to arbitrary bitlength (§7.4, p. 26).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"psty19-psty-no-stash-opprf","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"circuit-PSI · 2019","summary":"The stash-less protocol of §5.2 (pp. 20-21), the only one the authors implemented (§7.4, p. 26). It instantiates PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION with the batched OPPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF. Payloads can be added as in PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-PAYLOAD at the same asymptotic cost.","title":"PSTY no-stash OPPRF circuit-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-NO-STASH"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-BF","keywords":["psi cardinality and intersection-sum","encrypted_bloom_filter_homomorphic_tags","encrypted-bloom-filter"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"CPA-secure additively homomorphic encryption (exponential ElGamal and Ring-LWE in the measurements)"},"capabilities":["encrypted-bloom-filter"],"claim_ids":[],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( M(61 + 1.44 \\log_2 M) + 1 \\) homomorphic ciphertexts for M items per party (Table 4, p. 12)","computation":"\\( M(104 + 2.44 \\log_2 M) + 1 \\) homomorphic operations and Bloom-filter insertions of M items twice (Table 3, p. 12)","reported_measurement":"100,000 items per party: 1,988.47 s and 925.98 MB with exponential ElGamal over a 256-bit curve for the Bloom filter and slotted Ring-LWE for the values; single thread of an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3, network time excluded; 7.7865 US cents in the GCP cost model (Tables 5-6, p. 13; §5.1, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 4 (p. 11): P1 sends its Bloom filter encrypted bit by bit; for each item P2 computes an encryption of \\( r(\\sum \\mathrm{bits} - k) \\), zero exactly on a match, sends these with encrypted values in permuted order, and P1 sums the values of zero entries.","construction_family":"encrypted_bloom_filter_homomorphic_tags","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-BF","name":"Ion et al. encrypted Bloom filter intersection-sum","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2020-ION"],"primitive":"PSI cardinality and intersection-sum","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest P1 and P2 (§1, p. 2; security proof in Appendix F)","output":["cardinality","sum"],"output_recipients":"P1 learns the intersection size; P2 learns the intersection-sum (Figure 4, p. 11)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"both parties generate homomorphic key pairs and exchange public keys; Bloom-filter size and hash functions agreed so that a false match over all items has probability at most \\( 2^{-40} \\) (Figure 4, p. 11; §5.1, p. 11)","technique":["bloom_filter","additively_homomorphic_encryption"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; simulation from the intersection size and sum, given the encryption scheme and negligible Bloom-filter collisions (§4.3.2, p. 10; Appendix F)","notion":"private intersection-sum with cardinality"},"status":"published","title":"Ion et al. encrypted-Bloom-filter private intersection-sum with cardinality, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","sections":[{"content":"The encrypted-Bloom-filter protocol of §4.3.2 (pp. 10-11), the second of the paper's two new protocols. Like PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM it follows the paper's \"Tag, Shuffle, Aggregate\" recipe (§4.2, pp. 8-9); it is not listed here as a separate contribution.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Correctness fails only on Bloom-filter collisions or an accidental zero, both made negligible by parameter choice (§4.3.2, p. 10). It was the most expensive of the paper's protocols in communication and monetary cost (§5.2, p. 12).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"ikn20-ion-et-al-encrypted","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"PSI cardinality and intersection-sum · 2020","summary":"The encrypted-Bloom-filter protocol of §4.3.2 (pp. 10-11), the second of the paper's two new protocols. Like PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM it follows the paper's \"Tag, Shuffle, Aggregate\" recipe (§4.2, pp. 8-9); it is not listed here as a separate contribution.","title":"Ion et al. encrypted Bloom filter intersection-sum","type":"construction","venue":"IEEE EuroS&P 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-BF"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-DDH","keywords":["psi cardinality and intersection-sum","diffie_hellman_homomorphic_tags","deployed","threshold-abort-variant","reverse-variant","segmented-inputs"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"DDH and CPA-secure additively homomorphic encryption, random oracle model (Paillier with Damgård-Jurik in deployment)"},"capabilities":["deployed","threshold-abort-variant","reverse-variant","segmented-inputs"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-DDH-INTERSECTION-SUM"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 3M \\) group elements and \\( M + 1 \\) homomorphic ciphertexts for M items per party, before slotting (Table 4, p. 12)","computation":"\\( 4M \\) exponentiations and \\( 2M + 1 \\) homomorphic operations in total (Table 3, p. 12)","reported_measurement":"100,000 items per party: 74.46 s and 8.11 MB with slotted Paillier (Damgård-Jurik, \\( s = 3 \\)), 47.40 s and 8.34 MB with slotted Ring-LWE; single thread of an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3, network time excluded; 0.0840 and 0.0753 US cents in the GCP cost model (Tables 5-6, p. 13; §5.1, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 2 (p. 6) with the NIST curve prime256v1, SHA-256 hashing to the curve, and Paillier with 768-bit primes and the Damgård-Jurik optimization, 65 values per ciphertext (§3.2, pp. 6-7).","construction_family":"diffie_hellman_homomorphic_tags","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-DDH","name":"Ion et al. DDH intersection-sum (deployed)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2020-ION"],"primitive":"PSI cardinality and intersection-sum","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest P1 and P2 (§2.3, p. 4; Theorems 1-2, p. 6)","output":["cardinality","sum"],"output_recipients":"P1 learns the intersection size; P2 learns the intersection size and the intersection-sum (Figure 1, p. 3); a reverse variant gives the sum to P1 (§3.4, p. 7; Appendix G)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"three rounds: P1, then P2, then P1 (Figure 2, p. 6)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"each party picks a secret exponent; P2 generates the homomorphic key pair and sends the public key; a common random seed prepended to inputs simulates a fresh random oracle per execution (Figure 2, p. 6; §3.2, pp. 6-7)","technique":["diffie_hellman","additively_homomorphic_encryption"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; simulation-based proofs from DDH and the security of the homomorphic encryption with H as a random oracle (Theorems 1-2, p. 6; Appendix D)","notion":"private intersection-sum with cardinality"},"status":"published","title":"Ion et al. DDH-based private intersection-sum with cardinality with Paillier tags, semi-honest two-party, random oracle model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","sections":[{"content":"The deployed protocol of §3 (pp. 5-7), PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-DDH-INTERSECTION-SUM. A typical day ran about 1,000 executions of about 100,000 items per party between each pair of parties (§2.4, p. 4), at 84 cents per day in total (Table 2, p. 8).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Measurements exclude network transfer time and use a single core (§5.1, p. 11); monetary costs use GCP prices for pre-emptible machines and internet egress (§2.5, pp. 4-5). The comparison with garbled-circuit protocols draws their numbers from another paper and the authors list its caveats (§5.3, p. 12).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"he_paillier99: Paillier with 768-bit primes and Damgård-Jurik \\( s = 3 \\) in the deployed configuration (§3.2, p. 7).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"ikn20-ion-et-al-ddh","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"PSI cardinality and intersection-sum · 2020","summary":"The deployed protocol of §3 (pp. 5-7), PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-DDH-INTERSECTION-SUM. A typical day ran about 1,000 executions of about 100,000 items per party between each pair of parties (§2.4, p. 4), at 84 cents per day in total (Table 2, p. 8).","title":"Ion et al. DDH intersection-sum (deployed)","type":"construction","venue":"IEEE EuroS&P 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-DDH"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-ROT","keywords":["psi cardinality and intersection-sum","oprf_shuffle_homomorphic_tags","shuffled-oprf","reverse-variant"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"CPA-secure additively homomorphic encryption and batched random OT"},"capabilities":["shuffled-oprf","reverse-variant"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM","PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 3.6M + 1 \\) homomorphic ciphertexts, random OTs for \\( 1.3M \\) instances, and \\( 96M \\) bytes for M items per party (Table 4, p. 12)","computation":"\\( 5.9M + 1 \\) homomorphic operations, random OTs for \\( 1.3M \\) instances, and cuckoo hashing of M items (Table 3, p. 12)","reported_measurement":"100,000 items per party: 989.88 s and 123.21 MB with Paillier for the OPRF values and slotted Ring-LWE for the associated values; single thread of an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3, network time excluded; 1.237 US cents in the GCP cost model (Tables 5-6, p. 13; §5.1, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 3 (p. 10) with three hash functions, no stash, at most \\( 1.5n \\) bins, a SHA-256-based pseudorandom code of 448 bits, and Paillier with 768-bit primes (§5.1, p. 11).","construction_family":"oprf_shuffle_homomorphic_tags","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-ROT","name":"Ion et al. random-OT intersection-sum","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT"],"primitive":"PSI cardinality and intersection-sum","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest P1 and P2 (Theorems 3-4, pp. 9-10)","output":["cardinality","sum"],"output_recipients":"P1 learns the intersection size; P2 learns the intersection-sum (Figure 3, p. 10; Figure 1, p. 3)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"both parties generate homomorphic key pairs and exchange public keys; batched random OT of Kolesnikov et al. with 128 base public-key OTs (Figure 3, p. 10; §5.1, p. 11)","technique":["batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing","additively_homomorphic_encryption"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; simulation-based proofs from the encryption scheme and the random-OT functionality (Theorems 3-4, pp. 9-10; Appendix E)","notion":"private intersection-sum with cardinality"},"status":"published","title":"Ion et al. random-OT-based private intersection-sum with cardinality with shuffled OPRF outputs, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","sections":[{"content":"The protocol of §4.3.1 (pp. 9-10), PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM, built on the batched OPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF with a shuffle and re-masking step.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The security model label is not stated: the proofs rely on the random-OT functionality and the encryption scheme (§4.3.1, p. 9). Measurements exclude network time and use a single core (§5.1, p. 11).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"ikn20-ion-et-al-random","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"PSI cardinality and intersection-sum · 2020","summary":"The protocol of §4.3.1 (pp. 9-10), PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM, built on the batched OPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF with a shuffle and re-masking step.","title":"Ion et al. random-OT intersection-sum","type":"construction","venue":"IEEE EuroS&P 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-ROT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-MAL","keywords":["two-party psi","okvs_ot_extension","linear-communication"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Non-programmable random oracles, in the OOS OT-extension hybrid model"},"capabilities":["linear-communication"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( (\\lambda + \\log_2(n_1 n_2)) n_1 + \\ell(2.4 n_2 + 2\\lambda + \\chi) + \\lambda(2.4 n_2 + 2\\ell) \\) bits excluding base OTs; about \\( 1623n \\), \\( 1621n \\), and \\( 1602n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) with \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) (Table 1, p. 21); the recommended parameters are \\( \\ell_2 = 2\\kappa \\) and \\( \\ell_1 \\) of 129 to 233 bits with codewords of 583 to 776 bits, depending on the table size m and the extraction bound \\( n' \\) (§4.4, p. 15; Figure 5, p. 16)","computation":"garbled cuckoo encoding in \\( O((d + \\lambda)^{3} + n\\lambda) \\) with \\( d \\) about \\( \\log n \\), decoding by XOR of about \\( (\\lambda + d + 2)/2 \\) positions per key (§5.1, p. 18; Figure 2, p. 9); OOS OT extension on the table positions, with AES-based hash and PRF (§7.1, p. 22)","reported_measurement":"202.04 MB; 5,598 ms (LAN), 18,621 ms (WAN1, 155 MiB/s), 40,810 ms (WAN2, 55 MiB/s) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and 128-bit items, one thread per party on AWS M5.large (Table 2, p. 24; §7.2, p. 23)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol of Figure 3 (p. 11) with the 2-core garbled cuckoo table of \\( 2.4n \\) main positions (§7.1, p. 22), \\( H_2 \\) outputs of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits, and \\( \\ell_1 \\) and the code taken from Figure 5 (p. 16) for the chosen extraction bound \\( n' \\).","construction_family":"okvs_ot_extension","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-MAL","name":"PaXoS-PSI (malicious, 2-core table)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious sender or receiver, static corruption of one party (§2, p. 5; Theorem 4, p. 10); a corrupt party may use a set of size \\( n' > n \\), bounded for the receiver by Claim 7 (p. 15) and Figure 5 (p. 16) and for the sender by \\( H_2 \\) outputs of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits (§4.4, p. 15)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (Figure 1, p. 6; Figure 3, step 4, p. 11)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"non-programmable random oracles \\( H_1, H_2 \\) (abstract, p. 1; §4.2, p. 10); OOS as an ideal functionality (Figure 3, p. 11); base OTs instantiated with Naor-Pinkas OTs from libOTe (§7.1, p. 22)","technique":["paxos","ot_extension"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious two-party, random oracle model, OOS-hybrid (Theorem 4, p. 10; Lemmas 5-6, pp. 12-14)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver; corrupt parties may use larger sets"},"status":"published","title":"PaXoS-PSI, malicious two-party PSI from a garbled cuckoo PaXoS and OOS OT extension, random oracle model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","sections":[{"content":"This row is the protocol of Figure 3 (p. 11) with the malicious parameters of §4.4 (pp. 15-16), as described in PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI. The longer \\( H_1 \\) output and code bound a malicious receiver's effective set (PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND), and \\( H_2 \\) outputs of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits make collisions among a malicious sender's queries negligible. In the authors' experiments the malicious variant uses about 25% more communication than the semi-honest one and runs about 4% slower on a LAN (§7.3, p. 23). The DFS-based table sends 170.04 MB and runs in 17,903 ms on a LAN for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 2, p. 24); that configuration is not a separate row.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Table 1 (p. 21) writes the sender's term as \\( (\\lambda + \\log_2(n_1 n_2)) n_1 \\) even for the malicious protocol, while §4.4 (p. 15) recommends \\( H_2 \\) outputs of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits; the measured communication is in Table 2 (p. 24). Measurements are for equal set sizes and the authors' AWS setting (§7.2, p. 23).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-BASEOT: the base OTs of the OT extension are Naor-Pinkas OTs (§7.1, p. 22).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"prty20-paxos-psi-malicious-2","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2020","summary":"This row is the protocol of Figure 3 (p. 11) with the malicious parameters of §4.4 (pp. 15-16), as described in PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI. The longer \\( H_1 \\) output and code bound a malicious receiver's effective set (PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND), and \\( H_2 \\) outputs of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits make collisions among a malicious sender's queries negligible. In the authors' experiments the…","title":"PaXoS-PSI (malicious, 2-core table)","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-MAL"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-SH","keywords":["two-party psi","okvs_ot_extension","linear-communication"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"Code-correlation-robust hash, in the OOS OT-extension hybrid model"},"capabilities":["linear-communication"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-PAXOS"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( (\\lambda + \\log_2(n_1 n_2)) n_1 + \\ell(2.4 n_2 + \\lambda + \\chi) \\) bits excluding base OTs, with \\( n_1, n_2 \\) the sender's and receiver's set sizes and \\( \\ell \\) the OT-extension width; about \\( 1207n \\), \\( 1268n \\), and \\( 1302n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) with \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) (Table 1, p. 21); codeword length 448 to 506 bits for \\( n = 2^{12} \\) to \\( 2^{24} \\) (Figure 4, p. 15)","computation":"garbled cuckoo encoding in \\( O((d + \\lambda)^{3} + n\\lambda) \\) with \\( d \\) about \\( \\log n \\), decoding by XOR of about \\( (\\lambda + d + 2)/2 \\) positions per key (§5.1, p. 18; Figure 2, p. 9); OOS OT extension on the \\( 2.4n + d + \\lambda \\) table positions, with AES-based hash and PRF (§7.1, p. 22)","reported_measurement":"163.63 MB; 5,378 ms (LAN), 17,628 ms (WAN1, 155 MiB/s), 40,524 ms (WAN2, 55 MiB/s) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and 128-bit items, one thread per party on AWS M5.large (Table 2, p. 24; §7.2, p. 23)"},"configuration_note":"The receiver encodes \\( \\{(y, H_1(y))\\} \\) into the 2-core garbled cuckoo table with \\( 2.4n \\) main positions (§5; §7.1, p. 22) and uses it as its OOS choice strings; the sender sends \\( H_2(x, \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, x) \\oplus C(H_1(x)) \\wedge s) \\) for each item, randomly permuted (Figure 3, p. 11); \\( \\ell_1 = \\ell_2 = \\lambda + 2 \\log n \\) with the concatenated Reed-Solomon codes of Figure 4 (p. 15).","construction_family":"okvs_ot_extension","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-SH","name":"PaXoS-PSI (semi-honest, 2-core table)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest sender and receiver; obtained from the protocol of Figure 3 by setting \\( \\ell_1 = \\ell_2 = \\lambda + 2 \\log n \\) (§4.4, p. 15)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (Figure 1, p. 6; Figure 3, step 4, p. 11)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"base OTs for OOS, instantiated with Naor-Pinkas OTs from libOTe (§7.1, p. 22); OOS is treated as an ideal functionality (Figure 3, p. 11); the paper states standard-model security for the semi-honest case (Appendix D, p. 33), while the proof it gives is the malicious random-oracle proof (Theorem 4, p. 10)","technique":["paxos","ot_extension"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; standard model as stated in Appendix D (p. 33)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"PaXoS-PSI, semi-honest two-party PSI from a garbled cuckoo PaXoS and OOS OT extension","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","sections":[{"content":"This row is the protocol of Figure 3 (p. 11), described in PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI, with the semi-honest parameters of §4.4 (p. 15): the shortest hash outputs that keep false matches below \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) and a code of minimum distance κ. The encoding is the garbled cuckoo table of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO in its 2-core form. The authors also measure a DFS-based table (Appendix B) with \\( 2n \\) main positions, which sends 138.03 MB and runs in 17,140 ms on a LAN for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 2, p. 24); that configuration is not a separate row.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Measurements are for equal set sizes and the authors' AWS setting (§7.2, p. 23); they are not comparable with other papers' measurements unless a source makes the comparison. The introduction's 25% communication improvement over KKRT (§1.1, p. 2) is not supported by Table 1 (p. 21) or §7.3 (p. 23).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-BASEOT: the base OTs of the OT extension are Naor-Pinkas OTs (§7.1, p. 22).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"prty20-paxos-psi-semi-honest","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2020","summary":"This row is the protocol of Figure 3 (p. 11), described in PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI, with the semi-honest parameters of §4.4 (p. 15): the shortest hash outputs that keep false matches below \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) and a code of minimum distance κ. The encoding is the garbled cuckoo table of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO in its 2-core form. The authors also measure a DFS-based table (Appendix B) with \\( 2n…","title":"PaXoS-PSI (semi-honest, 2-core table)","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-SH"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-AHE-TWO-PARTY","keywords":["two-party threshold psi","polynomial_threshold_psi","sublinear-in-set-size","no-fhe"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"Additively homomorphic encryption and Yao garbled circuits; no further assumption stated for the two-party case"},"capabilities":["sublinear-in-set-size","no-fhe"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( \\tilde O(T) \\) (§1.2, p. 4): the singularity test via Yao's garbled circuits costs \\( \\tilde O(k \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) (§6.1, p. 26) and the second phase \\( O(T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) (§7.1, p. 29)","computation":"half-GCD-based singularity test of a \\( (T + 1) \\times (T + 1) \\) Hankel matrix in \\( O(T \\log^{2} T) \\) (Imported Theorem 6.3, p. 25), evaluated as a garbled circuit (§6.1, p. 26)"},"configuration_note":"The \\( n = 2 \\) case of Π_CTest-diff (Figure 8, p. 27) with a two-party garbled-circuit F_SingTest (§6.1, p. 26), followed by the two-party second phase of §7.1 (p. 29), in which Bob returns encryptions of \\( p_A(x) R_1^B(x) + p_B(x)(R_2^A(x) + R_2^B(x)) \\) for Alice to decrypt.","construction_family":"polynomial_threshold_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-AHE-TWO-PARTY","name":"BMRR two-party AHE threshold PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"],"primitive":"two-party threshold PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest Alice or Bob, as the \\( n = 2 \\) case of Theorem 7.1 (p. 28; §1.2, p. 4)","output":["threshold"],"output_recipients":"both parties receive the intersection if the symmetric difference is at most T, otherwise ⊥ (Figure 2, p. 6; §2.3, p. 6)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"Alice holds the secret key of the additively homomorphic encryption in the second phase (§7.1, p. 29); F_SingTest is realized by Yao's garbled circuits (§6.1, p. 26)","technique":["additively_homomorphic_encryption","garbled_circuits","polynomial_sparsity_test"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest; the two-party specialization is described in prose (§1.2, p. 4; §6.1, p. 26; §7.1, p. 29) rather than in a separate theorem","notion":"two-party threshold PSI"},"status":"published","title":"BMRR two-party threshold PSI from additive HE with a garbled-circuit Hankel singularity test, semi-honest, \\( \\tilde O(T) \\) communication","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"The two-party specialization that the paper offers as the solution to the open problem of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS (§1.2, p. 4; §3.3, p. 13). It keeps the monomial encoding and sparsity test of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI and replaces the general singularity test with the Hankel-specific one of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation is reported, and the two-party round count is not stated separately. Whether later work accepts the resolution was not checked.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"bmrr21-bmrr-two-party-ahe","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party threshold PSI · 2021","summary":"The two-party specialization that the paper offers as the solution to the open problem of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS (§1.2, p. 4; §3.3, p. 13). It keeps the monomial encoding and sparsity test of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI and replaces the general singularity test with the Hankel-specific one of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI.","title":"BMRR two-party AHE threshold PSI","type":"construction","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-AHE-TWO-PARTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TAHE-DIFF","keywords":["multiparty threshold psi","polynomial_threshold_psi","sublinear-in-set-size","star-network","no-fhe"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"Threshold additively homomorphic encryption with distributed setup (ElGamal under DDH or Paillier under DCR) and a PRG in \\( \\mathrm{NC}^{1} \\)"},"capabilities":["sublinear-in-set-size","star-network","no-fhe"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( \\tilde O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) (Corollary 6.6 and Corollary 7.2, p. 28), with F_SingTest at \\( \\tilde O(k \\cdot n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) for a \\( (k + 1) \\times (k + 1) \\) Hankel matrix (§6.1, p. 26)","computation":"Hankel singularity reduces to a half-GCD computation of \\( O(k \\log^{2} k) \\) (Imported Theorem 6.3, p. 25); the constant-round instantiation evaluates a circuit of size \\( \\tilde O(k \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) and constant depth assuming a PRG in \\( \\mathrm{NC}^{1} \\) (§6.1, p. 26)"},"configuration_note":"Π_CTest-diff (Figure 8, p. 27) with F_SingTest realized by the second protocol of §6.1 (p. 26), followed by Π_TPSI-diff (Figure 9, p. 30).","construction_family":"polynomial_threshold_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TAHE-DIFF","name":"BMRR TAHE threshold PSI (diff)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"],"primitive":"multiparty threshold PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest environment corrupting any proper subset of the parties (§2.2, p. 5; Theorem 7.1, p. 28)","corruption_threshold":"semi-honest corruption of up to \\( n - 1 \\) of n parties (Theorem 7.1, p. 28)","output":["threshold"],"output_recipients":"every party receives I if \\( |(S_1 \\cup \\dots \\cup S_n) \\setminus I| \\le T \\), otherwise ⊥ (Figure 2, p. 6; Figure 9, p. 30)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"\\( O(1) \\) when F_SingTest is realized by a jointly generated garbled circuit; \\( O(T \\log^{2} T) \\) with the homomorphic-evaluation instantiation (§6.1, p. 26; Corollary 7.2, p. 28)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"n-out-of-n threshold additively homomorphic encryption with distributed setup; a prime \\( q > T(T + 1)(p - 1) 2^{\\kappa} \\) for the sparsity test (Theorem 6.5, p. 27); star network with central party P1 (§2.3, p. 6)","technique":["additively_homomorphic_encryption","garbled_circuits","polynomial_sparsity_test"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"UC, semi-honest, up to \\( n - 1 \\) corruptions; Theorem 6.5 in the F_SingTest-hybrid model and Theorem 7.1 in the F_CTest-diff-hybrid model (pp. 27-28)","notion":"multiparty threshold PSI, functionality F_TPSI-diff"},"status":"published","title":"BMRR multiparty threshold PSI for F_TPSI-diff from threshold additive HE with Hankel singularity testing, semi-honest, \\( \\tilde O(nT) \\) communication","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"The TAHE instantiation of Corollary 7.2 (p. 28), realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI for F_TPSI-diff as defined in PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation is reported. \\( \\tilde O \\) hides polylogarithmic factors and the bound omits \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) (footnote 1, p. 3). No such protocol is given for F_TPSI-int (Table 1, p. 3).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"bmrr21-bmrr-tahe-threshold-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty threshold PSI · 2021","summary":"The TAHE instantiation of Corollary 7.2 (p. 28), realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI for F_TPSI-diff as defined in PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES.","title":"BMRR TAHE threshold PSI (diff)","type":"construction","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TAHE-DIFF"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TFHE-DIFF","keywords":["multiparty threshold psi","polynomial_threshold_psi","sublinear-in-set-size","star-network"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"lattice","name":"Threshold FHE with distributed setup from circular-secure LWE, with threshold additively homomorphic encryption"},"capabilities":["sublinear-in-set-size","star-network"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) (Corollary 7.2, p. 28; §5.2, p. 22)","computation":"the central party homomorphically interpolates \\( p_i/p_1 \\) for every other party and computes an encryption of the predicate \\( |(\\bigcup S_i) \\setminus I| \\le T \\) (§3.2, p. 12; §5.2, pp. 21-22)"},"configuration_note":"Π_TFHE-CTest-diff (Figure 6, §5.2, pp. 21-23) followed by Π_TPSI-diff (Figure 9, p. 30).","construction_family":"polynomial_threshold_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TFHE-DIFF","name":"BMRR TFHE threshold PSI (diff)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"],"primitive":"multiparty threshold PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest environment corrupting any proper subset of the parties (§2.2, p. 5; Theorem 7.1, p. 28)","corruption_threshold":"semi-honest corruption of up to \\( n - 1 \\) of n parties (Theorem 7.1, p. 28)","output":["threshold"],"output_recipients":"every party receives I if \\( |(S_1 \\cup \\dots \\cup S_n) \\setminus I| \\le T \\), otherwise ⊥ (Figure 2, p. 6; Figure 9, p. 30)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"\\( O(1) \\) in a star network (§5.2, p. 22; Corollary 7.2, p. 28)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"n-out-of-n threshold FHE with distributed setup for the cardinality test (Figure 6, §5.2) and threshold additively homomorphic encryption with distributed setup for the second phase (Figure 9, p. 30); star network with central party P1 (§2.3, p. 6)","technique":["threshold_fhe","additively_homomorphic_encryption","set_reconciliation"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"UC, semi-honest, up to \\( n - 1 \\) corruptions; Theorem 7.1 in the F_CTest-diff-hybrid model, instantiated by Theorem 5.4 (pp. 21, 28)","notion":"multiparty threshold PSI, functionality F_TPSI-diff"},"status":"published","title":"BMRR multiparty threshold PSI for F_TPSI-diff from threshold FHE and threshold additive HE, semi-honest, \\( O(nT) \\) communication","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"The TFHE instantiation of Corollary 7.2 (p. 28) for F_TPSI-diff, realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND for the functionality of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation is reported. The \\( O(nT) \\) bound omits a \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) factor (footnote 1, p. 3).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"bmrr21-bmrr-tfhe-threshold-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty threshold PSI · 2021","summary":"The TFHE instantiation of Corollary 7.2 (p. 28) for F_TPSI-diff, realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND for the functionality of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES.","title":"BMRR TFHE threshold PSI (diff)","type":"construction","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TFHE-DIFF"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TFHE-INT","keywords":["multiparty threshold psi","polynomial_threshold_psi","sublinear-in-set-size","star-network"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"lattice","name":"Threshold FHE with distributed setup from circular-secure LWE, with threshold additively homomorphic encryption"},"capabilities":["sublinear-in-set-size","star-network"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\): each party sends \\( 2T + 4 \\) TFHE ciphertexts and one partial decryption in the cardinality test (§5.1, p. 19), and the second phase costs \\( O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) (Theorem B.1 and Corollary B.2, p. 40)","computation":"the central party homomorphically interpolates a rational function from \\( 2T + 3 \\) encrypted evaluations under TFHE (§5.1, p. 17); each party interpolates a degree-\\( (3T + 3) \\) rational function in the clear (Figure 10, p. 41)"},"configuration_note":"Π_TFHE-CTest-int (Figure 5, p. 18) followed by Π_TPSI-int (Figure 10, p. 41).","construction_family":"polynomial_threshold_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TFHE-INT","name":"BMRR TFHE threshold PSI (int)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"],"primitive":"multiparty threshold PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest environment corrupting any proper subset of the parties (§2.2, p. 5; Theorem B.1, p. 40)","corruption_threshold":"semi-honest corruption of up to \\( n - 1 \\) of n parties (Theorem B.1, p. 40)","output":["threshold"],"output_recipients":"every party receives I if \\( |S_i \\setminus I| \\le T \\) for all i, otherwise ⊥ (Figure 1, p. 6; Figure 10, p. 41)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"\\( O(1) \\) in a star network (§5.1, p. 19; Theorem B.1, p. 40)","security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"n-out-of-n threshold FHE with distributed setup for the cardinality test (Figure 5, p. 18) and threshold additively homomorphic encryption with distributed setup for the second phase (Figure 10, p. 41); star network with central party P1 (§2.3, p. 6)","technique":["threshold_fhe","additively_homomorphic_encryption","set_reconciliation"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"UC, semi-honest, up to \\( n - 1 \\) corruptions; Theorem B.1 in the F_CTest-int-hybrid model, instantiated by Theorem 5.1 (pp. 17, 40)","notion":"multiparty threshold PSI, functionality F_TPSI-int"},"status":"published","title":"BMRR multiparty threshold PSI for F_TPSI-int from threshold FHE and threshold additive HE, semi-honest, \\( O(nT) \\) communication","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"The F_TPSI-int protocol of Appendix B (pp. 39-41), with the cardinality test of §5.1. It realizes PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND for the functionality defined in PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation is reported. The \\( O(nT) \\) bound omits a \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) factor (footnote 1, p. 3).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"bmrr21-bmrr-tfhe-threshold-psi-2","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty threshold PSI · 2021","summary":"The F_TPSI-int protocol of Appendix B (pp. 39-41), with the cardinality test of §5.1. It realizes PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND for the functionality defined in PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES.","title":"BMRR TFHE threshold PSI (int)","type":"construction","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TFHE-INT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG","keywords":["unbalanced psi","oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","square-root-homomorphic-multiplications","updatable-sender-preprocessing","malicious-receiver-security"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"mixed","name":"One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman in the random oracle model, and semantically secure leveled FHE (BFV)"},"capabilities":["communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","square-root-homomorphic-multiplications","updatable-sender-preprocessing","malicious-receiver-security"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER"],"complexity":{"communication":"sublinear in \\( |X| \\) (abstract, p. 1); \\( O(1) \\) ciphertexts from the sender per bin set before partitioning, and receiver-to-sender powers bounded by Lemma 1 for a target depth (§3.1, p. 9)","computation":"\\( O(\\sqrt{|X|}) \\) homomorphic multiplications (abstract, p. 1); per bin \\( L_\\alpha + (\\alpha + 1) H_\\alpha - (\\alpha + 3) \\) non-scalar multiplications with \\( \\alpha \\) partitions (eq. (4), p. 8)","reported_measurement":"\\( |X| = 2^{28} \\), \\( |Y| = 2048 \\): sender online 4.75 s on 24 threads, online communication 15.09 MB, sender offline 2,211 s; \\( |X| = 2^{24} \\), \\( |Y| = 4096 \\): sender online 11.0 s single-threaded, 5.1 MB receiver to sender and 2.5 MB sender to receiver; Intel Xeon Platinum 8272CL with 24 cores and 192 GB RAM, false-positive probability at most \\( 2^{-40} \\) per execution (Tables 1-2, pp. 16-17; §5, p. 14; §5.1, p. 16)"},"configuration_note":"SEAL-based implementation (APSI) in unlabeled mode (§5, pp. 14-15; §5.1, p. 15): OPRF hashing of both sets, three cuckoo hash functions, BFV with SIMD packing over prime fields and items split across slots, partitioning, Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation, query powers from windowing or extremal postage-stamp bases; low-depth Frobenius exponentiation is not used in SEAL (§5, p. 15). Parameters are chosen per set size, optimizing more for computation than communication (§5, p. 15).","construction_family":"oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG","name":"CMGD21 PSI (SEAL)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"],"primitive":"unbalanced PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"security against a malicious receiver and privacy against a malicious sender, with the security proof of the earlier labeled-PSI paper carried over unchanged (§3, p. 6)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (§2.2, p. 4; §3, p. 5)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"an OPRF exchange, then the receiver's encrypted query powers and the sender's reply (§3, pp. 5-6); no round count is stated","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"the sender samples the OPRF key and pre-processes its OPRF-processed set offline; the precomputation is updatable (§3, p. 6; §4.2, p. 13); OMGDH-based OPRF over the FourQ curve with a random-oracle hash to the curve (§3.1, p. 12)","technique":["oprf","fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"as in the earlier labeled-PSI paper's Theorem 1: malicious receiver; privacy against a malicious sender (§3, p. 6)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"CMGD21 unbalanced PSI (APSI over SEAL BFV): OPRF hashing, prime-field slots, Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation, and postage-stamp query powers","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","sections":[{"content":"This row is the unlabeled protocol of §3 (pp. 5-6) with the optimizations of §3.1 as configured in the SEAL implementation. Both sets pass through the OPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER, now mandatory; items are split over prime-field slots (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS); the receiver sends encrypted query powers chosen by postage-stamp bases (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES); and the sender evaluates each partition's intersection polynomial by the Paterson-Stockmeyer method (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER). The receiver outputs the items whose slots decrypt to zero.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Table 1 (p. 16) gives one parameterization per set-size pair; the authors note that running time can be traded for communication and vice versa (§5.1, p. 15). Comparisons in Table 2 (p. 17) include numbers for other protocols taken from earlier papers and different thread counts.","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"he_bfv12: the SEAL implementation uses BFV (CMGD21 §5, p. 14).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"cmg21-cmgd21-psi-seal","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"unbalanced PSI · 2021","summary":"This row is the unlabeled protocol of §3 (pp. 5-6) with the optimizations of §3.1 as configured in the SEAL implementation. Both sets pass through the OPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER, now mandatory; items are split over prime-field slots (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS); the receiver sends encrypted query powers chosen by postage-stamp bases (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES); and the…","title":"CMGD21 PSI (SEAL)","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS","keywords":["unbalanced psi","oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","communication-nearly-constant-in-larger-set","cacheable-evaluation-keys"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"mixed","name":"One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman in the random oracle model, and semantically secure leveled FHE (BGV)"},"capabilities":["communication-nearly-constant-in-larger-set","cacheable-evaluation-keys"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS-POWERS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( \\tilde O(C^{2}) \\) with \\( C = \\log \\log |X| \\) (§1.2, p. 3); \\( O(1) \\) query ciphertexts for depth \\( O(\\log \\log |X|) \\) (§3.1, p. 11)","computation":"Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation with powers obtained by Frobenius operations and non-scalar multiplications (§3.1, pp. 11-12); impractically high on current CPUs (abstract, p. 1)","reported_measurement":"\\( |Y| = 210 \\): online communication 0.72 MB at \\( |X| = 2^{20} \\) and 0.76 MB at \\( 2^{22} \\), \\( 2^{24} \\), and \\( 2^{26} \\); evaluation keys 6.22 to 9.17 MB sent once; at \\( |X| = 2^{24} \\) sender online 354 s and offline 305 s on 24 threads; 80-bit hashed items, at least 128-bit security for this parameter set, without OPRF or networking (Tables 5-6, pp. 19-20; Table 8, p. 27; §5, p. 15)"},"configuration_note":"HElib implementation over BGV (§5, p. 15; §5.4, pp. 18-19): cyclotomic orders chosen freely so that small plaintext moduli \\( t \\) (from 3 to 2789, Table 7, p. 26) give useful Frobenius powers; no partitioning (\\( \\alpha = 1 \\)), no windowing or postage-stamp bases; SIMD packing and Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation.","construction_family":"oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS","name":"CMGD21 PSI (HElib, Frobenius)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG"],"primitive":"unbalanced PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"the protocol keeps the OPRF step and its guarantees, a malicious receiver and privacy against a malicious sender (§3, p. 6); the proof-of-concept implementation omits the OPRF (§5, p. 15)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (§3, p. 5)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"the receiver sends Frobenius evaluation keys once, independent of its set and cacheable by the sender (§3.1, p. 12; §5.4, p. 19)","technique":["oprf","fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"as in the SEAL protocol (§3, p. 6); the measured implementation omits the OPRF (§5, p. 15)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"CMGD21 low-communication unbalanced PSI over HElib BGV with depth-free Frobenius powers (proof of concept)","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","sections":[{"content":"This row is the low-communication configuration of §3.1 (pp. 11-12) and §5.4 (pp. 18-20), realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS-POWERS. The receiver sends a constant number of encrypted powers; the sender obtains the low and high powers of Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER) with Frobenius operations and a few non-scalar multiplications, and returns the intersection results.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The implementation is a proof of concept written to measure communication; it implements neither the OPRF nor networking, and the OPRF communication is added at 32 bytes per receiver item (§5, p. 15). The authors state the variant is not practical to use today (§6, p. 20).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"he_bgv12: the HElib implementation uses BGV (CMGD21 §5, p. 14).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"cmg21-cmgd21-psi-helib-frobenius","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"unbalanced PSI · 2021","summary":"This row is the low-communication configuration of §3.1 (pp. 11-12) and §5.4 (pp. 18-20), realizing PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS-POWERS. The receiver sends a constant number of encrypted powers; the sender obtains the low and high powers of Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER) with Frobenius operations and a few non-scalar multiplications, and returns the intersection results.","title":"CMGD21 PSI (HElib, Frobenius)","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG-LABELED","keywords":["labeled psi","oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","labels-for-matching-items","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","updatable-labels"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"mixed","name":"One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman in the random oracle model, and semantically secure leveled FHE (BFV)"},"capabilities":["labels-for-matching-items","communication-sublinear-in-larger-set","updatable-labels"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"receiver to sender as in unlabeled PSI; sender to receiver grows linearly in the number of item-length label fragments (§5.3, p. 18)","computation":"offline interpolation \\( O(B^{2}) \\) field operations per bin (§4.2, p. 13); online one polynomial evaluation per label fragment on the same query powers (§5.3, p. 18)","reported_measurement":"label length 288 bytes, \\( |X| = 2^{20} \\), \\( |Y| = 256 \\): sender online 0.66 s with 16 sender threads, client encryption 0.05 s single-threaded, communication 11.2 MB, against 4.6 s, 0.77 s, and 17.6 MB for the earlier labeled protocol (Table 3, p. 18); Table 4 (p. 19) adds 16- and 32-byte labels"},"configuration_note":"SEAL-based implementation in labeled mode (§5.3, p. 18): the unlabeled configuration of PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG plus labels encrypted under a key from the extended OPRF output, split into item-length fragments and interpolated per prime-field chunk (§4.2, pp. 13-14; Figure 2, p. 13).","construction_family":"oprf_fhe_polynomial_evaluation_cuckoo_hashing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG-LABELED","name":"CMGD21 labeled PSI (SEAL)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR"],"primitive":"labeled PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"security against a malicious receiver, argued as a straightforward extension of the earlier unlabeled proof with labels encrypted under OPRF-derived keys (§4.2, p. 14); privacy against a malicious sender as in the unlabeled case (§3, p. 6)","output":["labeled"],"output_recipients":"receiver only, which learns the labels of matching items together with the intersection result (§2.2, p. 4; §4.2, p. 14)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"as in the unlabeled protocol, with one or more label results returned per item fragment (§4.2, p. 14); no round count is stated","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"unbalanced","setup":"offline interpolation of encrypted-label chunks per bin and partition, with collision-aware insertion; updates use a fresh random nonce per label (§4.2, pp. 13-14)","technique":["oprf","fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"proof sketch extending the earlier unlabeled proof (§4.2, p. 14)","notion":"labeled PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"CMGD21 labeled PSI (APSI over SEAL BFV): OPRF-keyed label encryption with chunk-wise interpolation over prime fields","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","sections":[{"content":"This row is the labeled protocol of §4 (pp. 12-14) as configured in the SEAL implementation. It replaces the extension-field label interpolation of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI by prime-field chunks (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS) and evaluates the label polynomials with the Paterson-Stockmeyer method on the same query powers as the intersection polynomials (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The label-length experiments of Tables 3-4 (pp. 18-19) are the only labeled measurements; the authors note that there are few meaningful points of comparison (§5.3, p. 18).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"he_bfv12: the SEAL implementation uses BFV (CMGD21 §5, p. 14). PIR-PAPER-2018-SEALPIR: Table 3 compares with the SealPIR paper's keyword retrieval (CMGD21 §5.3, p. 18; Table 3, p. 18).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"cmg21-cmgd21-labeled-psi-seal","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"labeled PSI · 2021","summary":"This row is the labeled protocol of §4 (pp. 12-14) as configured in the SEAL implementation. It replaces the extension-field label interpolation of PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI by prime-field chunks (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS) and evaluates the label polynomials with the Paterson-Stockmeyer method on the same query powers as the intersection polynomials (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER).","title":"CMGD21 labeled PSI (SEAL)","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-CONG-LABELED"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-3HGCT","keywords":["two-party psi","okvs_ot_extension","heuristic-error-analysis"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the OOS OT-extension hybrid model"},"capabilities":["heuristic-error-analysis"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"136.66 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 1, p. 24); the protocol invokes \\( 1.3n \\) OTs, against \\( 2.4n \\) for PaXoS-PSI, and sends 1.61 times less than PaXoS-PSI (§8.2, p. 23)","computation":"three-hash table encoding and batch decoding in \\( O(n\\lambda) \\) (Figure 1, p. 8); 4,996 ms to encode and 1,034 ms to decode \\( 2^{20} \\) elements of \\( \\mathrm{GF}(2^{128}) \\) (Table 2, p. 29)","reported_measurement":"136.66 MB; 5,855 ms (4.6 Gbit/s), 10,417 ms (260 Mbit/s), 37,695 ms (33 Mbit/s) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and 128-bit items, one thread per party on AWS c5d.2xlarge (Table 1, p. 24; §8, pp. 22-23)"},"configuration_note":"PaXoS-PSI (Figure 3 of PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY) with the binary three-hash garbled cuckoo table of §4.1 in place of the two-hash PaXoS (§6.3, p. 19; §8.1-8.2, p. 23).","construction_family":"okvs_ot_extension","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-3HGCT","name":"PaXoS-PSI with 3H-GCT (malicious)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious sender or receiver, through the PaXoS-PSI protocol and proofs, which are written for an arbitrary binary OKVS (§6.3, p. 19)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only, as in PaXoS-PSI (§6.3, p. 19)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"OOS OT extension with codewords of 627, 616, and 605 bits and \\( H_2 \\) outputs of 256 bits for \\( n = 2^{12}, 2^{16}, 2^{20} \\) (Figure 4, p. 23); three-hash table with \\( 1.3n \\) nodes, a heuristic parameter choice (§8.1, p. 23; Table 2, p. 29)","technique":["okvs","ot_extension"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious two-party, random oracle model, OOS-hybrid, via the PaXoS-PSI proofs (§6.3, p. 19)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver; corrupt parties may use larger sets"},"status":"published","title":"PaXoS-PSI with a three-hash garbled cuckoo table, malicious two-party PSI","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"The protocol is PaXoS-PSI (PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI) with the three-hash garbled cuckoo table of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT as its binary OKVS. Because PaXoS-PSI's protocol and proofs are written for an arbitrary binary OKVS, a smaller table directly reduces the number of OTs and the receiver's communication (§6.3, p. 19; §8.2, p. 23).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The failure probability of the \\( 1.3n \\) table is not established analytically or empirically in the paper; Table 2 (p. 29) lists its error analysis as heuristic. Measurements are for equal set sizes and the authors' AWS setting (§8, pp. 22-23). The authors report that the networking library of several compared implementations ran slower over a real network than over a simulated one (Table 1 note, p. 24).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"gprty21-paxos-psi-with-3h","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2021","summary":"The protocol is PaXoS-PSI (PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI) with the three-hash garbled cuckoo table of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT as its binary OKVS. Because PaXoS-PSI's protocol and proofs are written for an arbitrary binary OKVS, a smaller table directly reduces the number of OTs and the receiver's communication (§6.3, p. 19; §8.2, p. 23).","title":"PaXoS-PSI with 3H-GCT (malicious)","type":"construction","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-3HGCT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-STAR","keywords":["two-party psi","okvs_ot_extension","empirical-error-analysis"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the OOS OT-extension hybrid model"},"capabilities":["empirical-error-analysis"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"145.42 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 1, p. 24); the protocol invokes \\( 1.41n \\) OTs and sends 1.43 times less than PaXoS-PSI (§8.2, p. 23); OKVS size about \\( 1.3n \\) (§5.4, p. 18)","computation":"2,915 ms to encode and 1,625 ms to decode \\( 2^{20} \\) elements of \\( \\mathrm{GF}(2^{128}) \\) (Table 2, p. 29); encoding makes up to \\( 2q + 1 \\) calls to the leaf encoder (§5.2, p. 16)","reported_measurement":"145.42 MB; 4,987 ms (4.6 Gbit/s), 9,631 ms (260 Mbit/s), 36,871 ms (33 Mbit/s) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and 128-bit items, one thread per party on AWS c5d.2xlarge (Table 1, p. 24; §8, pp. 22-23)"},"configuration_note":"PaXoS-PSI (Figure 3 of PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY) with the star-architecture OKVS of §5.4 (pp. 17-18), whose leaves and center are three-hash garbled cuckoo tables (§6.3, p. 19; §8.1, p. 23).","construction_family":"okvs_ot_extension","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-STAR","name":"PaXoS-PSI with star-architecture OKVS (malicious)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious sender or receiver, through the PaXoS-PSI protocol and proofs, which are written for an arbitrary binary OKVS (§6.3, p. 19)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only, as in PaXoS-PSI (§6.3, p. 19)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"OOS OT extension with codewords of 627, 616, and 605 bits and \\( H_2 \\) outputs of 256 bits for \\( n = 2^{12}, 2^{16}, 2^{20} \\) (Figure 4, p. 23); star architecture with \\( q = 10 \\) leaves for \\( n = 2^{16} \\) and \\( q = 160 \\) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\), leaves of \\( 1.3n' + \\lambda + 0.5 \\log n' \\) positions, OKVS failure probability \\( 2^{-52.9} \\) and \\( 2^{-45.05} \\) from an empirically measured leaf failure rate of \\( 2^{-29.355} \\) (§5.4, pp. 17-18)","technique":["okvs","ot_extension"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious two-party, random oracle model, OOS-hybrid, via the PaXoS-PSI proofs (§6.3, p. 19)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver; corrupt parties may use larger sets"},"status":"published","title":"PaXoS-PSI with a star-architecture OKVS over three-hash tables, malicious two-party PSI","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"The protocol is PaXoS-PSI (PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI) with the star-architecture OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION, whose components are the three-hash tables of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT. The authors describe the star construction as the only OKVS in their comparison with an empirically proven error probability (Appendix A.2, p. 28).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The OKVS failure probability rests on an empirical measurement at confidence 0.9999 (§5.4, p. 17); PSI-PAPER-2022-RR (§5.1, p. 24) later argues that the measured bound overstates the security level by a few bits, which was not checked here. Measurements are for equal set sizes and the authors' AWS setting (§8, pp. 22-23).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"gprty21-paxos-psi-with-star","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2021","summary":"The protocol is PaXoS-PSI (PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI) with the star-architecture OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION, whose components are the three-hash tables of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT. The authors describe the star construction as the only OKVS in their comparison with an empirically proven error probability (Appendix A.2, p. 28).","title":"PaXoS-PSI with star-architecture OKVS (malicious)","type":"construction","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-STAR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MULTIPARTY","keywords":["multiparty psi","oprf_okvs_zero_sharing","any-okvs"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracle and PRF-based zero sharing, in the malicious two-party OPRF hybrid model"},"capabilities":["any-okvs"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-MALICIOUS-MULTIPARTY","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(n^{2} \\kappa + nm(\\kappa + \\lambda + \\log m)) + o(nm) \\) for \\( n \\) parties with sets of size \\( m \\) (Table 4, p. 36); \\( O(n^{2} \\kappa + n \\cdot |\\mathrm{OPRF}| + n \\cdot m \\cdot \\log |\\mathbb{F}|) \\) (Appendix B.3, p. 30)","computation":"\\( \\Omega(nm) \\) field multiplications (Table 4, p. 36); each party encodes one OKVS of \\( m \\) points, and \\( P_0 \\) decodes \\( m \\) points in each received OKVS (Appendix B.3, p. 30)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 5 (p. 39): zero-sharing setup; one OPRF instance between \\( P_0 \\) (receiver) and each \\( P_i \\) (sender); each \\( P_i \\) sends \\( \\mathrm{Encode}(\\{(h, H(\\mathrm{PRF}_i(h), h) + sh_i(h))\\}) \\); \\( P_0 \\) outputs the \\( h \\) in its set with \\( \\sum_i \\mathrm{Decode}(Q_i, h) = 0 \\).","construction_family":"oprf_okvs_zero_sharing","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MULTIPARTY","name":"Malicious multiparty PSI from OPRF and OKVS","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY"],"primitive":"multiparty PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious; corrupt parties may contribute sets of up to about \\( 2m \\) items (Theorem 9, p. 31; Appendix B.4, p. 34)","corruption_threshold":"robust against \\( n - 1 \\) corruptions among \\( n \\) parties (Table 4 caption, p. 36); any subset of parties, including the central party, may be corrupt (Appendix B.4, p. 31)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the central party \\( P_0 \\) only (Figure 5, p. 39); an extension gives output to all parties under a weaker functionality (Appendix B.7, p. 38)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"\\( |\\mathrm{OPRF}| + 1 \\) rounds (Appendix B.3, p. 30); 4 in the comparison of Table 4 (p. 36)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"F_oprf-hybrid model with a malicious-secure two-party OPRF between \\( P_0 \\) and each party, instantiated with vOLE in Table 4 (p. 36); pairwise PRF keys for zero sharing exchanged in one input-independent round (Figure 5, step 1, p. 39; Appendix B.5, p. 35); exponentially large field \\( \\mathbb{F} \\)","technique":["okvs","vole","secret_sharing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious, F_oprf-hybrid, random oracle model (Theorem 9, p. 31)","notion":"UC-secure multiparty PSI with output to a central party; corrupt parties may use larger sets"},"status":"published","title":"Malicious multiparty PSI from two-party OPRFs, zero sharing, and OKVS, star topology","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"This is the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-MALICIOUS-MULTIPARTY, written over an arbitrary OKVS (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS): Kolesnikov et al.'s zero-sharing construction with each OPRF output hashed together with the item by a random oracle (Appendix B.2, p. 30; Figure 5, p. 39).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"All parties hold sets of the same size \\( m \\) (Figure 5, p. 39); corrupt parties are not held to their advertised sizes (Appendix B.4, p. 34; B.7, p. 37). The paper reports no implementation of this protocol; the asymptotic comparison of Table 4 (p. 36) is the authors'.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"gprty21-malicious-multiparty-psi-fro","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty PSI · 2021","summary":"This is the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-MALICIOUS-MULTIPARTY, written over an arbitrary OKVS (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS): Kolesnikov et al.'s zero-sharing construction with each OPRF output hashed together with the item by a random oracle (Appendix B.2, p. 30; Figure 5, p. 39).","title":"Malicious multiparty PSI from OPRF and OKVS","type":"construction","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MULTIPARTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-VOLE","keywords":["two-party psi","okvs_vole","any-linear-okvs"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the vector-OLE hybrid model"},"capabilities":["any-linear-okvs"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-LINEAR-OKVS-VOLE"],"complexity":{"communication":"not quantified; the paper states that, since no error-correcting code is needed, communication is reduced relative to PaXoS-PSI (§7.1, p. 21)","computation":"not reported; the protocol was not implemented because no malicious-secure vOLE implementation existed (§7.1, p. 21)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 3 (p. 22): vOLE of length \\( m \\) between the sender's random \\( s \\) and the receiver's linear OKVS \\( D = \\mathrm{Encode}(\\{(y, H_1(y))\\}) \\) over \\( \\mathrm{GF}(2^{\\ell_1}) \\); the sender sends a random permutation of \\( \\{H_2(x, \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, x) \\oplus H_1(x) \\cdot s)\\} \\); the receiver outputs the \\( y \\) whose \\( H_2(y, \\mathrm{Decode}(R, y)) \\) appears.","construction_family":"okvs_vole","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-VOLE","name":"Generalized PaXoS-PSI over vOLE","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious sender or receiver (Theorem 7, p. 21)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (Figure 3, step 3, p. 22)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"vOLE functionality in which the sender inputs a random \\( s \\in \\{0,1\\}^{\\ell_1} \\) and the receiver its OKVS (Figure 3, step 1, p. 22); requires a malicious-secure vOLE protocol (§7.1, p. 21); random oracles \\( H_1 \\), \\( H_2 \\) with \\( \\ell_1 \\), \\( \\ell_2 \\) as in PaXoS-PSI (Theorem 7, p. 21)","technique":["okvs","vole"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious two-party, random oracle model, vOLE-hybrid (Theorem 7, p. 21)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"Generalized PaXoS-PSI from a linear OKVS and vector OLE, malicious two-party PSI","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"This is the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-LINEAR-OKVS-VOLE: PaXoS-PSI with the OOS correlation replaced by vector OLE, so that the receiver's encoding may be any linear OKVS and no error-correcting code is used (§7.1, pp. 20-22).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The paper gives no implementation, measurement, or concrete communication count, and no written proof beyond Theorem 7's statement (§7.1, p. 21). It leaves to future work whether this approach is competitive with the OT-extension version (§7.1, p. 21).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"gprty21-generalized-paxos-psi-over","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2021","summary":"This is the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-LINEAR-OKVS-VOLE: PaXoS-PSI with the OOS correlation replaced by vector OLE, so that the receiver's encoding may be any linear OKVS and no error-correcting code is used (§7.1, pp. 20-22).","title":"Generalized PaXoS-PSI over vOLE","type":"construction","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-VOLE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-NTY-ARBITRARY-COLLUSION","keywords":["multiparty psi","okvs_zeroxor","corruption-threshold-calibration","lightweight-clients"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"PRF and random oracle, with OKVS obliviousness and ideal OPPRF and zero-sharing functionalities"},"capabilities":["corruption-threshold-calibration","lightweight-clients"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-THRESHOLD-CALIBRATED","PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(m \\kappa) \\) per client and \\( O(m \\kappa \\max\\{t, n - t\\}) \\) for the leader (Table 1, p. 2); servers \\( P_{v+1}, \\ldots, P_{n-1} \\) communicate \\( O(m) \\) and \\( P_n \\) \\( O(mt) \\), with \\( \\kappa \\) omitted (§4.2.1, pp. 10-11)","computation":"\\( O(m t \\kappa) \\) per client and \\( O(m \\kappa (n - t)) \\) for the leader (Table 1, p. 2); pivot and servers \\( O(m (n - t)) \\), with \\( \\kappa \\) omitted (§4.2.1, pp. 10-11)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 15, t = 7, m = 2^{20} \\) on LAN: client 3.48 s and total 32.56 s; client 41.94 MB and total 1,416.9 MB (Tables 3-4, p. 12); \\( n = 15, m = 2^{20} \\): 7.2, 22.8, 32.5, and 58.23 s for \\( t = 1, 4, 7, 14 \\) (§1.2, p. 4); 128-bit items, \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), PaXoS with expansion 2.5, table-based OPPRF code of Kolesnikov et al., 2x 36-core Intel Xeon 2.30 GHz with 256 GB RAM, simulated LAN with 0.02 ms RTT and 10 Gbps, one thread per peer (§5, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol 4.4 (p. 10) with ZeroXOR realized by Protocol 4.2 (p. 10), PaXoS as the OKVS, and AES-NI as the PRF (§5, p. 11); used in the evaluation for \\( t > 1 \\), while \\( t = 1 \\) uses Protocol 3.3 (§5, p. 11).","construction_family":"okvs_zeroxor","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-NTY-ARBITRARY-COLLUSION","name":"NTY multiparty PSI, arbitrary collusion","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT"],"primitive":"multiparty PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious adversary statically corrupting any strict subset of the parties (abstract, p. 1; §1, p. 1; Theorem 4.5, p. 11)","corruption_threshold":"\\( t < n \\), chosen as a parameter; clients \\( n - t - 1 \\), one pivot, \\( t \\) servers (Protocol 4.4, p. 10; Theorem 4.5, p. 11)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"\\( P_n \\) only (Functionality 2.1, p. 4; Protocol 4.4, step 6, p. 10)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"4 rounds: keys and OKVSs in parallel, then ZeroXOR with one zero-sharing round plus the OPPRF rounds (§4.2.1, p. 11; Table 1, p. 2)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"Theorem 4.5 is in the F_zeroXOR-hybrid and random oracle model (p. 11); ZeroXOR is in the F_zeroShare and F_opprf hybrid model (Theorem 4.3, p. 10); unconditional zero-sharing seeds are exchanged once (§2.0.5, p. 5; Protocol C.1, p. 14)","technique":["okvs","paxos","opprf","secret_sharing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious, \\( t < n \\) corrupted, F_zeroXOR-hybrid and random oracle model (Theorem 4.5, p. 11)","notion":"\\( n \\)-party PSI with output to \\( P_n \\) (Functionality 2.1, p. 4)"},"status":"published","title":"NTY malicious multiparty PSI against \\( t < n \\) colluding parties from OKVS encodings and ZeroXOR over OPPRFs","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1221","sections":[{"content":"Clients deliver a conditional zero-sharing to the pivot and the \\( t \\) servers through one OKVS and \\( t \\) PRF keys each, and the \\( t + 1 \\) parties run ZeroXOR (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-THRESHOLD-CALIBRATED; Protocol 4.4, p. 10). ZeroXOR masks each programmed value with the unconditional zero-sharing of PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING (§2.0.5, p. 5; §4.1, p. 10). At \\( t = n - 1 \\) there are no clients and only ZeroXOR among all \\( n \\) parties remains (§5, p. 11).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The protocol specifies the malicious PaXoS-based OPPRF (Appendix B, p. 14), but the measured implementation uses the table-based OPPRF code of PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT, which footnote 2 calls semi-honest secure and about three times slower than the malicious OPPRF (§5, p. 11). Reported times are for the authors' setting only.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"nty21-nty-multiparty-psi-arbitrary","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty PSI · 2021","summary":"Clients deliver a conditional zero-sharing to the pivot and the \\( t \\) servers through one OKVS and \\( t \\) PRF keys each, and the \\( t + 1 \\) parties run ZeroXOR (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-THRESHOLD-CALIBRATED; Protocol 4.4, p. 10). ZeroXOR masks each programmed value with the unconditional zero-sharing of PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING (§2.0.5, p. 5; §4.1, p. 10). At \\( t = n - 1 \\) there are no…","title":"NTY multiparty PSI, arbitrary collusion","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-NTY-ARBITRARY-COLLUSION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-NTY-NO-COLLUSION","keywords":["multiparty psi","okvs_reduction_to_server_aided_psi","symmetric-key-primitives-only","no-collusion"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"symmetric","name":"PRF and random oracle, with OKVS obliviousness and an ideal two-party PSI functionality"},"capabilities":["symmetric-key-primitives-only","no-collusion"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-NO-COLLUSION"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(m \\kappa) \\) per client and \\( O((m + n) \\kappa) \\) for the leader (Table 1, p. 2); each of \\( P_1, \\ldots, P_{n-2} \\) sends one OKVS of \\( O(m) \\) values and \\( P_1 \\) also sends \\( O(n) \\) keys of \\( \\kappa \\) bits (§3.2.2, p. 8)","computation":"\\( O(m \\kappa) \\) per client and \\( O(n m \\kappa) \\) for the leader (Table 1, p. 2); \\( P_1 \\) makes \\( O(nm) \\) PRF calls and \\( P_{n-1} \\) decodes \\( O(n) \\) OKVSs on \\( O(m) \\) values (§3.2.2, p. 8)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 15, t = 1, m = 2^{20} \\) on LAN: client 3.95 s and total 7.25 s; client 41.94 MB and total 662.7 MB (Tables 3-4, p. 12); \\( n = 32, t = 1, m = 2^{20} \\): 10.71 s for \\( P_n \\) on LAN (Table 5, p. 12); 128-bit items, \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), PaXoS with expansion 2.5, AES-NI PRF, 2x 36-core Intel Xeon 2.30 GHz with 256 GB RAM, simulated LAN with 0.02 ms RTT and 10 Gbps (§5, p. 11)"},"configuration_note":"Protocol 3.3 (p. 8) with the two-party PSI functionality instantiated by the server-aided PSI of Kamara et al. (§3.2.2, p. 8), as used in the evaluation for \\( t = 1 \\) (§5, p. 11).","construction_family":"okvs_reduction_to_server_aided_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-NTY-NO-COLLUSION","name":"NTY multiparty PSI, no collusion","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY"],"primitive":"multiparty PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious adversary corrupting at most one party (§3, p. 6; Theorem 3.4, p. 8)","corruption_threshold":"no collusion, \\( t \\le 1 \\) (§1.2, p. 4); \\( t = 1 \\) (Table 1, p. 2); a collusion of two parties such as \\( P_1 \\) and \\( P_{n-1} \\) breaks the protocol (§3.2.1, p. 8)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"\\( P_n \\) only (Functionality 2.1, p. 4; Protocol 3.3, step 6, p. 8)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"4 rounds, two plus the two rounds of the server-aided PSI of Kamara et al. (§3.2.2, p. 8); Table 1 lists 5 (p. 2)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"Theorem 3.4 is in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{psi}}^{2,1,m} \\)-hybrid model (p. 8); the malicious variant hashes items with a random oracle (Protocol 3.3, note a, p. 8); the two-party PSI is instantiated with the server-aided PSI of Kamara et al., whose server is another party assumed not to collude (§3.1.1, p. 6; §3.2.2, p. 8; Appendix A, pp. 13-14); no oblivious transfer is used (§1.2, p. 4)","technique":["okvs","paxos","server_aided_psi"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious, at most one corrupted party, \\( F_{\\mathrm{psi}}^{2,1,m} \\)-hybrid (Theorem 3.4, p. 8)","notion":"\\( n \\)-party PSI with output to \\( P_n \\) (Functionality 2.1, p. 4)"},"status":"published","title":"NTY malicious multiparty PSI without collusion from OKVS encodings and server-aided two-party PSI","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1221","sections":[{"content":"PRF keys from \\( P_1 \\) and OKVS encodings reduce the \\( n \\)-party intersection to a two-party PSI between \\( P_{n-1} \\) and \\( P_n \\) (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-NO-COLLUSION; Protocol 3.3, p. 8). Each party appends its decoded value to its plain item so that a malicious \\( P_1 \\) cannot force false matches (§3.2, p. 7). The server-aided two-party PSI of Kamara et al. replicates every item \\( \\lambda \\) times and adds agreed dummy items so that a cheating server is detected (Appendix A, pp. 13-14).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The row covers the constant-round Protocol 3.3; the recursive Protocol 3.1 has \\( O(n) \\) rounds (§3.1.4, p. 6) and is not evaluated. Reported times are for the authors' single machine with simulated networks and are not comparable with other papers' measurements unless a source makes the comparison.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"nty21-nty-multiparty-psi-no","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty PSI · 2021","summary":"PRF keys from \\( P_1 \\) and OKVS encodings reduce the \\( n \\)-party intersection to a two-party PSI between \\( P_{n-1} \\) and \\( P_n \\) (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-NO-COLLUSION; Protocol 3.3, p. 8). Each party appends its decoded value to its plain item so that a malicious \\( P_1 \\) cannot force false matches (§3.2, p. 7). The server-aided two-party PSI of Kamara et al. replicates every item \\( \\lambda \\) times and adds…","title":"NTY multiparty PSI, no collusion","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-NTY-NO-COLLUSION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-CIRCUIT","keywords":["circuit-psi","opprf_cuckoo_circuit","associated-values","secret-shared-output"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the OPPRF and generic two-party computation hybrid models; VOLE from LPN with regular noise"},"capabilities":["associated-values","secret-shared-output"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"13.4 MB (IKNP) and 4.79 MB (SilentOT) for \\( n = 2^{12} \\); 171 MB and 21.1 MB for \\( n = 2^{16} \\); 2,830 MB and 277 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 4, p. 29)","computation":"one OPPRF over the cuckoo-table bins and one equality circuit per bin (§6, pp. 23-24; Figure 10, pp. 38-39)","reported_measurement":"for \\( n = 2^{20} \\): 23.3 s (IKNP) and 103 s (SilentOT) at 5 Gbps, 149 s and 120 s at 100 Mbps, one core per party on AWS M5.2xlarge (Table 4, p. 29; §7.2, p. 26)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 10 (pp. 38-39): receiver cuckoo table, sender simple hash table, OPPRF programmed with \\( H(y, j) \\) mapped to bin values \\( r_i \\) (and masked associated values), then F2pc comparing \\( r_i' \\) with \\( r_i \\) per bin; the IKNP and SilentOT measurements differ only in how the GMW offline OTs are generated (§7.2, p. 30).","construction_family":"opprf_cuckoo_circuit","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-CIRCUIT","name":"VOLE-PSI circuit-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-RS"],"primitive":"circuit-PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest (abstract, p. 1); the OPPRF is proved in the F_oprf-hybrid model (Theorem 5.1, p. 21), and the circuit-PSI proof is deferred to Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai (§6, p. 23)","output":["circuit"],"output_recipients":"both parties receive secret shares of a membership bit and of associated values for each cuckoo-table bin (Figure 9, p. 38)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"cuckoo hashing with expansion 1.27 and three hash functions, following Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai (§7.2, pp. 29-30); OPPRF from the VOLE-based OPRF, whose security uses random oracles, and XoPaXoS (Figure 8, p. 38); generic two-party computation for the comparison, implemented with GMW whose OTs come from IKNP or SilentOT (§7.2, p. 30)","technique":["opprf","vole","paxos","cuckoo_hashing","gmw"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party; OPPRF in the F_oprf-hybrid model (Theorem 5.1, p. 21); composition argued by reference to Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai (§6, p. 23)","notion":"circuit-PSI with secret-shared output"},"status":"published","title":"VOLE-PSI circuit-PSI, semi-honest, from a VOLE-based OPPRF with XoPaXoS, cuckoo hashing, and a GMW comparison circuit","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","sections":[{"content":"This is the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI: the circuit-PSI of Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai with their OPPRF replaced by one built from PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF and XoPaXoS (§6, pp. 22-24).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Table 4 (p. 29) compares with the earlier protocol's measured running times and with communication computed from its theoretical costs. The authors note disagreement in the literature about cuckoo-hashing parameters for a given statistical security, and keep the earlier protocol's parameters for comparability (§7.2, pp. 29-30).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-GMW: the comparison circuit is evaluated with GMW (§7.2, pp. 26 and 30). MPC-CONTRIB-2019-PCG-SILENT: the SilentOT variant (§7.2, p. 30; Table 4, p. 29), cited to a CCS 2019 paper that has no record of its own.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rs21-vole-psi-circuit-psi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"circuit-PSI · 2021","summary":"This is the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI: the circuit-PSI of Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai with their OPPRF replaced by one built from PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF and XoPaXoS (§6, pp. 22-24).","title":"VOLE-PSI circuit-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-CIRCUIT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-MAL","keywords":["two-party psi","vole_okvs_oprf","data-independent-setup"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Programmable random oracles, in the vector-OLE hybrid model; VOLE from LPN with regular noise"},"capabilities":["data-independent-setup"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 2^{24} n_x^{0.05} + 307n_x + 128n_y \\) bits for \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), about \\( 960n \\), \\( 474n \\), and \\( 438n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) (Table 1, p. 25); the sender sends \\( \\kappa \\) bits per item (§7.1, p. 26)","computation":"\\( O(n) \\) (abstract, p. 1): PaXoS encoding by a linear pass plus \\( O(\\lambda^{3}) \\) Gaussian elimination on the 2-core (§2, pp. 9-10), and an LPN-based VOLE expansion with a consistency check (§3.1, p. 14)","reported_measurement":"57.86 MB; 5.150 s (LAN, 5 Gbps), 9.599 s (100 Mbps), 54.09 s (10 Mbps), 495.0 s (1 Mbps) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) with setup excluded, and 58.79 MB and 6.157 s (LAN) with setup; one core per party on AWS M5.2xlarge (Table 3, p. 29; §7.2, p. 26)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 6 (p. 37) with κ-bit outputs over the malicious OPRF of Figure 4 (p. 36), using the PaXoS solver with \\( 2.4n \\) main positions (§2, p. 9).","construction_family":"vole_okvs_oprf","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-MAL","name":"VOLE-PSI (malicious, PaXoS)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-RS"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious sender or receiver (Theorem 3.1, p. 16; Theorem 4.1, p. 20); a malicious receiver's effective set is bounded by \\( n' = O(n) \\) through the PaXoS size bound, and by \\( m \\) under the authors' Strong OKVS Extraction Conjecture (§2, pp. 11-12; Lemma 3.3, p. 18)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (Figure 5, p. 37; Figure 6, step 4, p. 37)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"programmable random oracles (the simulators program \\( H \\), Lemmas 3.2-3.3, pp. 16-18); coin tossing for the offset \\( w \\) (Figure 4, steps 1 and 5, p. 36); F_vole-hybrid, implemented with LPN-based VOLE and the malicious consistency check of Weng et al. (§3.1, p. 14; §7.2, p. 26); OPRF outputs of \\( \\kappa \\) bits (Figure 6, p. 37)","technique":["batched_oprf","vole","paxos"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious two-party, programmable random oracle model, F_vole-hybrid (Theorem 3.1, p. 16; Theorem 4.1, p. 20)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver; a corrupt receiver may use a larger set"},"status":"published","title":"VOLE-PSI, malicious two-party PSI from a VOLE-based OPRF with a PaXoS encoding and κ-bit OPRF outputs","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","sections":[{"content":"The OPRF is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF, and the transformation uses the analysis of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS, so the sender's values are \\( \\kappa \\) bits long (§4, pp. 19-20). The authors attribute the gap to the malicious PaXoS-PSI protocol to the \\( \\kappa \\) bits per encoding position and the κ-bit outputs (§7.1, p. 26).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Table 1 (p. 25) prints the general malicious formula with a \\( (\\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y)) n_y \\) term but uses \\( 128n_y \\) in its numeric column; the numeric column and the measurements of Table 3 (p. 29) are recorded. The bound on a malicious receiver's effective set depends on the 2024 revision's treatment of extraction (§2, pp. 11-13). Measurements are for equal set sizes and the authors' AWS setting (§7.2, pp. 26-27).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-PCG: the VOLE is produced by an LPN-based generator that expands a short seed correlation (§3.1, pp. 13-14; §7.2, p. 26).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rs21-vole-psi-malicious-paxos","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2021","summary":"The OPRF is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF, and the transformation uses the analysis of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS, so the sender's values are \\( \\kappa \\) bits long (§4, pp. 19-20). The authors attribute the gap to the malicious PaXoS-PSI protocol to the \\( \\kappa \\) bits per encoding position and the κ-bit outputs (§7.1, p. 26).","title":"VOLE-PSI (malicious, PaXoS)","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-MAL"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-SH","keywords":["two-party psi","vole_okvs_oprf","data-independent-setup"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the vector-OLE hybrid model; VOLE from LPN with regular noise"},"capabilities":["data-independent-setup"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( \\rho\\kappa n_x + (\\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y)) n_y \\) bits plus the VOLE, with \\( \\rho = 2.4 \\) for PaXoS (§7.1, p. 24); \\( 2.4\\kappa n_x + (\\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y)) n_y + 2^{17} \\kappa n_x^{0.05} \\) bits in total, about \\( 914n \\), \\( 426n \\), and \\( 398n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) with \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) (Table 1, p. 25)","computation":"\\( O(n) \\) (abstract, p. 1): PaXoS encoding by a linear pass over the cuckoo graph plus Gaussian elimination of \\( O(\\lambda^{3}) \\) on its 2-core (§2, pp. 9-10), and an LPN-based VOLE expansion (§3.1, pp. 13-14)","reported_measurement":"52.61 MB; 4.398 s (LAN, 5 Gbps), 8.496 s (100 Mbps), 48.69 s (10 Mbps), 449.7 s (1 Mbps) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) with setup excluded, and 53.55 MB and 5.396 s (LAN) with setup; one core per party on AWS M5.2xlarge (Table 2, p. 28; §7.2, p. 26)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 6 (p. 37) over the OPRF of Figure 4 (p. 36): the receiver encodes \\( \\{(x, H_F(x))\\} \\) with the PaXoS solver (\\( 2.4n \\) main positions), derandomizes a random VOLE, and learns its OPRF values; the sender sends its OPRF values for \\( Y \\) in random order with semi-honest output length.","construction_family":"vole_okvs_oprf","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-SH","name":"VOLE-PSI (semi-honest, PaXoS)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-RS"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest sender and receiver; OPRF outputs of \\( \\lambda + \\log_2(n_x) + \\log_2(n_y) \\) bits (Figure 6, p. 37)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (Figure 5, p. 37; Figure 6, step 4, p. 37)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"F_vole-hybrid with a random VOLE of length \\( m = 2.4n + d + \\lambda \\) over a field of about \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\) elements (Figure 1, p. 7; Figure 4, p. 36); the implementation uses LPN-based VOLE with iterative bootstrapping, part of which runs in a one-time, data-independent setup (§3.1, p. 14; §7.2, pp. 26-27); random oracles \\( H \\) and \\( H_F \\)","technique":["batched_oprf","vole","paxos"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party, random oracle model, F_vole-hybrid (Theorem 3.1, p. 16; Theorem 4.1, p. 20)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"VOLE-PSI, semi-honest two-party PSI from a VOLE-based OPRF with a PaXoS encoding","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","sections":[{"content":"The OPRF is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF with the PaXoS solver of PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY; the PSI step is the standard OPRF-to-PSI transformation with outputs just long enough to make false matches unlikely (§4, p. 19; Figure 6, p. 37). The authors observe that the VOLE overhead is highly sublinear, so the protocol is best suited to large sets, for example \\( n_x \\ge 2^{16} \\) (§7.1, p. 25).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The rate-1 variant with polynomial interpolation (about \\( 245n \\) bits at \\( n = 2^{20} \\) in Table 1, p. 25) is not implemented and is not a separate row. Measurements are for equal set sizes and the authors' bandwidth-limited AWS setting (§7.2, pp. 26-27).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-PCG: the VOLE is produced by an LPN-based generator that expands a short seed correlation (§3.1, pp. 13-14; §7.2, p. 26).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rs21-vole-psi-semi-honest","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2021","summary":"The OPRF is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF with the PaXoS solver of PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY; the PSI step is the standard OPRF-to-PSI transformation with outputs just long enough to make false matches unlikely (§4, p. 19; Figure 6, p. 37). The authors observe that the VOLE overhead is highly sublinear, so the protocol is best suited to large sets, for example \\( n_x \\ge 2^{16} \\) (§7.1, p. 25).","title":"VOLE-PSI (semi-honest, PaXoS)","type":"construction","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-SH"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-MAL","keywords":["two-party psi","key_agreement_polynomial_encoding","low-communication","small-sets"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"oracle Diffie-Hellman with elligator encoding, ideal permutation, and random oracles"},"capabilities":["low-communication","small-sets"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-KA-TO-MALICIOUS-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 2\\kappa n_1 + \\phi n_2 + \\phi \\) bits, with \\( \\kappa = 128 \\) and \\( \\phi = 256 \\): one key-agreement message, \\( n \\) field elements for \\( P \\), and \\( n \\) outputs of \\( H_2 \\) of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits (Table 1, p. 4; §4, 'Costs', p. 15)","computation":"sender one key-agreement message and \\( n \\) keys; receiver \\( n \\) responses and \\( n \\) keys; interpolation and multi-point evaluation of a degree-\\( n \\) polynomial in \\( O(n \\log^{2} n) \\) field operations (§4, 'Costs', p. 15)","reported_measurement":"\\( n = 2^{8} \\): 16.38 KB and 119.5 ms total on a simulated 10 Gbps network; \\( n = 2^{20} \\): 67.11 MB and 251.06 s total on 10 Gbps; 128-bit items, \\( \\kappa = 128 \\), \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), one Intel Xeon at 2.30 GHz with 256 GB RAM (Table 2, p. 17; Table 3, p. 18; §5.1-5.2, p. 19)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 4 (p. 11) instantiated with hashed Diffie-Hellman over Curve25519 with elligator encodings, \\( F = \\{0,1\\}^{256} \\), and the sender's two messages combined (§4, p. 15; §5.1, pp. 16-18).","construction_family":"key_agreement_polynomial_encoding","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-MAL","name":"RT21 malicious","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-RT"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious sender or malicious receiver, UC security (Lemma 5, p. 10; Lemma 6, p. 12)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the receiver only (Figure 2, p. 6; Figure 4, step 7, p. 11)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"two rounds for a one-round key agreement such as Diffie-Hellman: \\( P \\) from the receiver, then \\( m \\) and \\( K \\) from the sender (§4, 'Optimizations', p. 15)","security_model":["ideal_permutation_model","random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"ideal permutation \\( \\Pi \\) on \\( \\{0,1\\}^{256} \\) instantiated with fixed-key Rijndael-256; random oracles instantiated with SHA2; Curve25519 with elligator encoding of the receiver's messages (§5.1, pp. 16-18; Appendix B, p. 28)","technique":["key_agreement"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious, UC, ideal permutation plus random oracle model (abstract, p. 1; Lemmas 5-7, pp. 10-14; §4.1, pp. 15-16)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver; a corrupt receiver may input more than \\( n \\) items (\\( n' < 8n + 4 \\) for \\( n = 2^{10} \\), \\( |F| = 2^{256} \\))"},"status":"published","title":"Rosulek-Trieu malicious PSI from hashed elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman with polynomial-encoded responses","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","sections":[{"content":"The receiver encodes \\( \\Pi^{-1} \\) of fresh Diffie-Hellman responses at the points \\( H_1(y_i) \\) of a polynomial; the sender sends its single Diffie-Hellman message and \\( H_2(x_i, k_i) \\) for the key \\( k_i \\) it derives from \\( \\Pi(P(H_1(x_i))) \\) (Figure 4, p. 11). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-KA-TO-MALICIOUS-PSI.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Measurements are the authors' and use their machine and simulated networks (§5.2, p. 19). Table 2 and Table 3 compare protocols that differ in security (semi-honest, one-sided, and two-sided malicious) as the paper labels them; they are not normalized here.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"rt21-rt21-malicious","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2021","summary":"The receiver encodes \\( \\Pi^{-1} \\) of fresh Diffie-Hellman responses at the points \\( H_1(y_i) \\) of a polynomial; the sender sends its single Diffie-Hellman message and \\( H_2(x_i, k_i) \\) for the key \\( k_i \\) it derives from \\( \\Pi(P(H_1(x_i))) \\) (Figure 4, p. 11). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-KA-TO-MALICIOUS-PSI.","title":"RT21 malicious","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-MAL"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-SH-V1","keywords":["two-party psi","key_agreement_polynomial_encoding","low-communication","small-sets"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"CDH with elligator encoding, ideal permutation, and random oracles"},"capabilities":["low-communication","small-sets"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( (\\lambda + \\log(n_1 n_2)) n_1 + \\phi n_2 + \\phi \\) bits, with \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) and \\( \\phi = 256 \\) (Table 1, p. 4)","computation":"\\( 3n \\) fixed-base and \\( 2n \\) variable-base exponentiations and two degree-\\( n \\) interpolation or multi-point evaluation steps in total (§1.2, p. 6)","reported_measurement":"semi-honest 'Ours' rows: \\( n = 2^{8} \\), 9.98 KB and 116.5 ms total on 10 Gbps; \\( n_1 = n_2 = 2^{20} \\), 44.04 MB and 245.06 s total on 10 Gbps (Table 2, p. 17; Table 3, p. 18). The paper does not say which semi-honest variant it benchmarked; its implementation section describes an ideal permutation, which variant 2 does not use (§5.1, p. 18)."},"configuration_note":"Appendix A.1, Figure 5 (p. 24), instantiated with hashed Diffie-Hellman over Curve25519 with elligator encodings (§5.1, pp. 16-18).","construction_family":"key_agreement_polynomial_encoding","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-SH-V1","name":"RT21 semi-honest variant 1","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-RT"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest sender and receiver (Lemmas 11-12, pp. 24-25)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the receiver only (Figure 5, step 7, p. 24)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"three messages \\( m \\), \\( P \\), \\( K \\) (Figure 5, p. 24); for a one-round key agreement the paper combines \\( m \\) and \\( K \\) in the malicious protocol (§4, p. 15)","security_model":["ideal_permutation_model","random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"ideal permutation \\( \\Pi \\) on the field \\( F \\); hashed Diffie-Hellman with elligator encoding (Appendix A.1, Figure 5, p. 24; §3.2, pp. 8-9)","technique":["key_agreement"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest; ideal permutation; hashed Diffie-Hellman in the random oracle model under CDH (Lemmas 11-12, pp. 24-25; §3.2, p. 8; Table 1, p. 4)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"Rosulek-Trieu semi-honest PSI, variant 1, from hashed Diffie-Hellman with an ideal permutation","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","sections":[{"content":"As in the malicious protocol, but without \\( H_1 \\) and \\( H_2 \\): the receiver interpolates \\( P \\) on \\( (y_i, \\Pi^{-1}(m'_i)) \\) and the sender returns keys of \\( \\lambda + 2 \\log n \\) bits (Figure 5, p. 24). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The attribution of the semi-honest measurements to this variant is an inference recorded in reported_measurement. Measurements use the authors' machine and simulated networks (§5.2, p. 19).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"rt21-rt21-semi-honest-variant","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2021","summary":"As in the malicious protocol, but without \\( H_1 \\) and \\( H_2 \\): the receiver interpolates \\( P \\) on \\( (y_i, \\Pi^{-1}(m'_i)) \\) and the sender returns keys of \\( \\lambda + 2 \\log n \\) bits (Figure 5, p. 24). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION.","title":"RT21 semi-honest variant 1","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-SH-V1"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-SH-V2","keywords":["two-party psi","key_agreement_polynomial_encoding","low-communication","no-ideal-permutation"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"discrete_log","name":"oracle Diffie-Hellman with elligator encoding and random oracles"},"capabilities":["low-communication","no-ideal-permutation"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( (\\lambda + \\log(n_1 n_2)) n_1 + \\phi n_2 + 2\\phi \\) bits, with \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) and \\( \\phi = 256 \\) (Table 1, p. 4)"},"configuration_note":"Appendix A.2, Figure 6 (p. 26): the receiver interpolates \\( P \\) on the points \\( (y_i, m'_i) \\) and \\( (0, R) \\); no implementation of this variant is reported.","construction_family":"key_agreement_polynomial_encoding","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-SH-V2","name":"RT21 semi-honest variant 2","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2021-RT"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest sender and receiver (Appendix A.2 and Lemma 13, pp. 26-27)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"the receiver only (Figure 6, step 7, p. 26)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"three messages \\( m \\), \\( P \\), \\( K \\) (Figure 6, p. 26)","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"no ideal permutation; the receiver fixes \\( P(0) \\) to a random \\( R \\), assuming 0 is not a valid item; hashed Diffie-Hellman with elligator encoding (Appendix A.2, p. 26; §3.2, pp. 8-9)","technique":["key_agreement"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest; non-malleable key agreement with pseudorandom second messages, which for hashed Diffie-Hellman is the oracle Diffie-Hellman assumption (Lemma 13, p. 27; §3.2, p. 9; Table 1, p. 4)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"Rosulek-Trieu semi-honest PSI, variant 2, from non-malleable hashed Diffie-Hellman without an ideal permutation","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","sections":[{"content":"The receiver encodes its key-agreement responses directly as \\( P(y_i) \\) and adds the point \\( P(0) = R \\), which lets the simulator program one evaluation at a time by solving for \\( R \\) (Appendix A.2, p. 26). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Only the theoretical communication of Table 1 is reported for this variant.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"rt21-rt21-semi-honest-variant-2","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2021","summary":"The receiver encodes its key-agreement responses directly as \\( P(y_i) \\) and adds the point \\( P(0) = R \\), which lets the simulator program one evaluation at a time by solving for \\( R \\) (Appendix A.2, p. 26). The mechanism is PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION.","title":"RT21 semi-honest variant 2","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RT-SH-V2"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-CIRCUIT","keywords":["circuit-psi","opprf_cuckoo_circuit","secret-shared-output"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the OPPRF and generic two-party computation hybrid models; Silver for silent OT and VOLE"},"capabilities":["secret-shared-output"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 931n \\) and \\( 921n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{16} \\) and \\( 2^{20} \\); asymptotically \\( O(n\\ell) \\) (Table 2, p. 25)","computation":"not stated beyond the measurements; the OKVS encodes in \\( O(m + g^{2}) \\) after linear-time triangulation (§2, p. 6)","reported_measurement":"1,112 ms and 15,557 ms for \\( n = 2^{16} \\) and \\( 2^{20} \\), on an Intel i7 9750H laptop over a local network of about 1 Gbps (Table 2, p. 25; §5, p. 23)"},"configuration_note":"Table 2 row 'Ours (\\( w = 3 \\), Silver)': the VOLE-PSI circuit-PSI with the \\( w = 3 \\) doubly oblivious OKVS and Silver (§4, p. 23; Table 2, p. 25).","construction_family":"opprf_cuckoo_circuit","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-CIRCUIT","name":"Raghuraman-Rindal circuit-PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","PSI-PAPER-2021-RS"],"primitive":"circuit-PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest, as in the circuit-PSI of VOLE-PSI that the paper uses (§4, p. 23; Table 2, p. 25)","output":["circuit"],"output_recipients":"both parties receive secret shares, as in the circuit-PSI of VOLE-PSI (§1, p. 2; §4, p. 23)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"the circuit-PSI protocol of PSI-PAPER-2021-RS with the OKVS in its doubly oblivious form (Figure 1, step 5, p. 7; Theorem 2, p. 31; §4, p. 23) and Silver (Table 2, p. 25)","technique":["opprf","okvs","vole","cuckoo_hashing"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party, by reference to the circuit-PSI of PSI-PAPER-2021-RS (§4, p. 23)","notion":"circuit-PSI with secret-shared output"},"status":"published","title":"VOLE-PSI circuit-PSI with the doubly oblivious triangulation OKVS and Silver, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","year":2022},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","sections":[{"content":"The row applies PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS, in the doubly oblivious form that circuit PSI needs, to the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI, with Silver for the remaining correlations (§4, p. 23; Table 2, p. 25).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The paper describes this protocol only through a reference to VOLE-PSI and one row of Table 2 (p. 25), and its improvement factors differ by passage (abstract, p. 1; §1, p. 2; §1.1, p. 3). Measurements are the authors' single-laptop setting (§5, p. 23).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-2019-PCG-SILENT: the Silver construction of Couteau, Rindal, and Raghuraman supplies silent correlations here (Table 2, p. 25; §5.2, p. 26); Silver has no record of its own.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rr22-raghuraman-rindal-circuit-ps","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"circuit-PSI · 2022","summary":"The row applies PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS, in the doubly oblivious form that circuit PSI needs, to the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI, with Silver for the remaining correlations (§4, p. 23; Table 2, p. 25).","title":"Raghuraman-Rindal circuit-PSI","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2022","year":2022,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-CIRCUIT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-FAST","keywords":["two-party psi","vole_okvs_oprf","multi-threaded"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the subfield-VOLE hybrid model; VOLE following Couteau-Rindal-Raghuraman 2021 and Boyle et al. 2019"},"capabilities":["multi-threaded"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 1.3\\kappa n_x + (\\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y)) n_y + 2^{14.5} \\kappa \\) bits; \\( 241n \\), \\( 251n \\), and \\( 260n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) (Table 2, p. 25)","computation":"OKVS encoding in \\( O(m + g^{2}) \\) after linear-time triangulation (§2, p. 6); 143 ms to encode \\( 2^{20} \\) items with clusters of \\( 2^{14} \\) (Table 1, p. 25)","reported_measurement":"51 ms, 369 ms, and 6,987 ms for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) on one thread, and 49 ms, 163 ms, and 3,145 ms with 4 threads, on an Intel i7 9750H laptop with 16 GB RAM over a local network of about 1 Gbps (Table 2, p. 25; §5, p. 23)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 9 (p. 23) with \\( B = F \\), the \\( w = 3 \\) OKVS with clusters of \\( 2^{14} \\) columns, and \\( m \\) about \\( 1.28n \\) (§4, p. 22).","construction_family":"vole_okvs_oprf","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-FAST","name":"Raghuraman-Rindal PSI (semi-honest, fast)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","PSI-PAPER-2021-RS"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest sender and receiver (Theorem 3, p. 33)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (Figure 9, step 5, p. 23; Figure 11, p. 32)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"F_sub-vole-hybrid with base field equal to the extension (no subfield), a field of about \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\) elements (§4, p. 22; Figure 12, p. 32); VOLE following Couteau, Rindal, and Raghuraman and Boyle et al. (§4, p. 22); random oracles \\( H_B \\) and \\( H_o \\)","technique":["vole","okvs","batched_oprf"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party, random oracle model, F_sub-vole-hybrid (Theorem 3, p. 33)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"Fast instantiation of VOLE-based semi-honest PSI with the triangulation OKVS (row weight 3, clustered)","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","year":2022},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","sections":[{"content":"This is the semi-honest protocol of Figure 9 (p. 23), which is the VOLE-based PSI of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF with the OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS, instantiated without a subfield to avoid its running-time overhead (§4, p. 22).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"All protocols in the comparison were run by the authors on the same laptop with \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) and \\( \\kappa = 128 \\) (§5, p. 23); the measurements are not comparable with other papers' settings. The paper text writes the base field as \"\\( \\mathrm{GF}(\\kappa) \\)\" (§4, p. 22), read here as a field of about \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\) elements, as Theorem 3 (p. 33) requires.","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-PCG: the VOLE follows the silent constructions of Couteau, Rindal, and Raghuraman and of Boyle et al. (§4, p. 22).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rr22-raghuraman-rindal-psi-semi","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2022","summary":"This is the semi-honest protocol of Figure 9 (p. 23), which is the VOLE-based PSI of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF with the OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS, instantiated without a subfield to avoid its running-time overhead (§4, p. 22).","title":"Raghuraman-Rindal PSI (semi-honest, fast)","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2022","year":2022,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-FAST"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-MAL","keywords":["two-party psi","vole_okvs_oprf","multi-threaded"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the vector-OLE hybrid model (malicious protocol of VOLE-PSI)"},"capabilities":["multi-threaded"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 1.3\\kappa n_x + \\kappa n_y + 2^{14.5} \\kappa \\) bits; \\( 343n \\), \\( 302n \\), and \\( 300n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) (Table 2, p. 25)","computation":"OKVS encoding in \\( O(m + g^{2}) \\) after linear-time triangulation (§2, p. 6), plus the VOLE","reported_measurement":"62 ms, 439 ms, and 8,055 ms for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) on one thread, and 65 ms, 222 ms, and 3,984 ms with 4 threads, on an Intel i7 9750H laptop over a local network of about 1 Gbps (Table 2, p. 25; §5, p. 23)"},"configuration_note":"The malicious VOLE-PSI protocol (Figures 4 and 6 of PSI-PAPER-2021-RS) with the triangulation OKVS in place of PaXoS (§4, p. 23); the OKVS parameters for this row are not stated separately.","construction_family":"vole_okvs_oprf","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-MAL","name":"Raghuraman-Rindal PSI (malicious)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","PSI-PAPER-2021-RS"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["malicious"],"adversary_scope":"malicious sender or receiver, through the malicious protocol of VOLE-PSI, which the paper uses directly (§4, p. 23)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (Figure 11, p. 32)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"the malicious protocol of PSI-PAPER-2021-RS with the new OKVS (§4, p. 23); no separate malicious proof is given in this paper","technique":["vole","okvs","batched_oprf"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"malicious two-party, via the VOLE-PSI proofs (§4, p. 23)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver; a corrupt receiver may use a larger set"},"status":"published","title":"Malicious VOLE-PSI with the triangulation OKVS","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","year":2022},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","sections":[{"content":"The row combines the malicious OPRF and transformation of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF and PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS with the OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS (§4, p. 23). The authors observe almost no overhead over their semi-honest protocol (§5.2, p. 26).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Security rests on the VOLE-PSI proofs, including its treatment of a malicious receiver's effective set; this paper does not restate it for the new OKVS. Measurements are the authors' single-laptop setting (§5, p. 23).","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"rr22-raghuraman-rindal-psi-malici","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2022","summary":"The row combines the malicious OPRF and transformation of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF and PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS with the OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS (§4, p. 23). The authors observe almost no overhead over their semi-honest protocol (§5.2, p. 26).","title":"Raghuraman-Rindal PSI (malicious)","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2022","year":2022,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-MAL"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-SD","keywords":["two-party psi","subfield_vole_okvs","multi-threaded"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracles, in the subfield-VOLE hybrid model; VOLE following Couteau-Rindal-Raghuraman 2021 and Boyle et al. 2019"},"capabilities":["multi-threaded"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-SUBFIELD-VOLE","PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( 1.2 \\log(n_x n_y) n_x + (\\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y)) n_y + 2^{14.5} \\kappa \\) bits as printed; \\( 206n \\), \\( 180n \\), and \\( 196n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) (Table 2, p. 25); \\( O(n\\lambda + n \\log n) \\) (abstract, p. 1)","computation":"OKVS encoding in \\( O(m + g^{2}) \\) after linear-time triangulation (§2, p. 6), without clustering (§4, p. 22)","reported_measurement":"63 ms, 1,353 ms, and 27,681 ms for \\( n = 2^{16}, 2^{20}, 2^{24} \\) on one thread, and 61 ms, 1,107 ms, and 25,325 ms with 4 threads, on an Intel i7 9750H laptop over a local network of about 1 Gbps (Table 2, p. 25; §5, p. 23)"},"configuration_note":"Figure 9 (p. 23) with subfield VOLE, the \\( w = 3 \\) OKVS without clustering, and \\( m = 1.23n \\) (§4, p. 22).","construction_family":"subfield_vole_okvs","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-SD","name":"Raghuraman-Rindal PSI (semi-honest, subfield VOLE)","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","PSI-PAPER-2021-RS"],"primitive":"two-party PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"semi-honest sender and receiver (Theorem 3, p. 33)","output":["intersection"],"output_recipients":"receiver only (Figure 9, step 5, p. 23; Figure 11, p. 32)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"F_sub-vole-hybrid with base field \\( B \\) the smallest field with \\( |B| \\ge 2^{\\lambda + \\log_2(n_x n_y)} \\) and extension \\( F \\) the smallest with \\( |F| \\ge 2^{\\kappa} \\) (§4, p. 22; Theorem 3, p. 33); VOLE following Couteau, Rindal, and Raghuraman and Boyle et al. (§4, p. 22); random oracles \\( H_B \\) and \\( H_o \\)","technique":["subfield_vole","okvs","batched_oprf"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"semi-honest two-party, random oracle model, F_sub-vole-hybrid (Theorem 3, p. 33)","notion":"PSI with output to the receiver"},"status":"published","title":"Low-communication instantiation of semi-honest PSI with subfield VOLE and the triangulation OKVS (row weight 3, no clustering)","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","year":2022},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","sections":[{"content":"This is the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-SUBFIELD-VOLE (Figure 9, p. 23) with the OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS tuned for size (§4, p. 22).","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"Table 2 (p. 25) prints the dominant receiver term as \\( 1.2 \\log(n_x n_y) n_x \\), without \\( \\lambda \\), while the base-field size in §4 (p. 22) suggests about \\( 1.23(\\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y)) \\) bits per position; the numeric values of Table 2 are recorded. The abstract reports \\( 187n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{20} \\), while Table 2 and §1.1 (p. 3) give \\( 180n \\). Measurements are the authors' single-laptop setting (§5, p. 23).","heading":"Scope"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-PCG: the subfield VOLE follows the silent constructions of Couteau, Rindal, and Raghuraman and of Boyle et al. (§4, p. 22).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rr22-raghuraman-rindal-psi-semi-2","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party PSI · 2022","summary":"This is the protocol of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-SUBFIELD-VOLE (Figure 9, p. 23) with the OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS tuned for size (§4, p. 22).","title":"Raghuraman-Rindal PSI (semi-honest, subfield VOLE)","type":"construction","venue":"ACM CCS 2022","year":2022,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-SD"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY","keywords":["multiparty threshold psi","bucketed_cardinality_test_threshold_psi","sublinear-in-set-size","no-fhe","large-intersection-condition"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"Oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption (any assumption implying secure computation)"},"capabilities":["sublinear-in-set-size","no-fhe","large-intersection-condition"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY-QUASILINEAR","PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(\\epsilon^{2} \\ell t\\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits (Corollaries 5-6, p. 16), from \\( \\epsilon t \\) buckets each running the inner test \\( O(\\ln t) \\) times at threshold \\( O(\\ln t) \\), with the inner test at \\( O(\\lambda \\ell \\,\\mathrm{poly}(t)) \\) (§4.1, p. 16); the intersection step costs \\( \\tilde O(\\lambda \\ell t) \\) (§1.2, p. 2)","computation":"not_reported"},"configuration_note":"Π_ℓ-pict (Figure 11, p. 15) with F_ℓ-∩ realized by Π_ℓ-∩ (Figure 12, p. 16) over the Branco-Döttling-Pu cardinality test, followed by the Ghosh-Simkin intersection step (Corollary 6, p. 16).","construction_family":"bucketed_cardinality_test_threshold_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY","name":"Ghosh-Simkin 2023 multiparty threshold PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"],"primitive":"multiparty threshold PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"passive adversary corrupting up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) parties (§2.1, p. 4; Corollary 6, p. 16)","corruption_threshold":"static passive corruption of all but one of \\( \\ell \\) parties (§2.1, p. 4)","output":["threshold"],"output_recipients":"the parties learn the intersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) elements, otherwise \\( \\bot \\) (abstract and §1, p. 1); the cardinality test outputs \\( \\ell \\)-out-of-\\( \\ell \\) secret shares (Figure 9, p. 11)","parties":"multiparty","rounds":"not_reported","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"pairwise authenticated private channels and a broadcast channel of unit cost (§2.1, p. 4); \\( \\epsilon \\) public random functions into \\( t \\) buckets, replaceable by PRFs with a public key (footnote 4, p. 3); inner multiparty cardinality test of Branco, Döttling, and Pu with generic secure computation (§4.1, p. 16)","technique":["secret_sharing","simple_hashing","additively_homomorphic_encryption"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"standalone, static passive adversary corrupting all but one party; Theorems 7-8 in hybrid models with ideal functionalities (pp. 15-16); correctness except with probability \\( 2^{-\\epsilon} \\) (Theorem 6, p. 14)","notion":"multiparty threshold PSI (intersection of at least \\( n - t \\) elements)"},"status":"published","title":"Ghosh-Simkin 2023 multiparty threshold PSI from bucketed cardinality tests, passive security, \\( O(\\epsilon^{2} \\ell t\\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) communication","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","year":2023},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/919","sections":[{"content":"The \\( \\ell \\)-party protocol of Corollary 6 (p. 16): the cardinality-test compiler of PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER applied to the Branco-Döttling-Pu test, then the intersection step of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS. It realizes PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY-QUASILINEAR.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"No implementation or measurement is reported, and the round complexity is not stated. The output condition is the large-intersection condition, not the union-based condition of PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-BMRR-TAHE-DIFF.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"gs23-ghosh-simkin-2023-multiparty","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"multiparty threshold PSI · 2023","summary":"The \\( \\ell \\)-party protocol of Corollary 6 (p. 16): the cardinality-test compiler of PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER applied to the Branco-Döttling-Pu test, then the intersection step of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS. It realizes PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY-QUASILINEAR.","title":"Ghosh-Simkin 2023 multiparty threshold PSI","type":"construction","venue":"PKC 2023","year":2023,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2023-GS-TWO-PARTY","keywords":["two-party threshold psi","bucketed_cardinality_test_threshold_psi","sublinear-in-set-size","no-fhe"],"metadata":{"assumption":{"family":"generic","name":"Oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption (any assumption implying secure computation)"},"capabilities":["sublinear-in-set-size","no-fhe"],"claim_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI"],"complexity":{"communication":"\\( O(\\epsilon^{2} t\\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits (Corollaries 2-3, p. 10): \\( \\epsilon t \\) buckets, each running the Ghosh-Simkin test \\( O(\\ln t) \\) times at threshold \\( O(\\ln t) \\), at \\( O(\\lambda \\ln^{3} t) \\) per test with the Cramer-Damgård determinant protocol (p. 10)","computation":"not_reported"},"configuration_note":"Π_pict (Figure 7, p. 9) with F_△ realized by Π_△ (Figure 8, p. 10) over the two-party Ghosh-Simkin cardinality test, followed by the Ghosh-Simkin intersection step (Corollary 3, p. 10).","construction_family":"bucketed_cardinality_test_threshold_psi","dossier_type":"construction","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2023-GS-TWO-PARTY","name":"Ghosh-Simkin 2023 two-party threshold PSI","paper_ids":["PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"],"primitive":"two-party threshold PSI","properties":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"adversary_scope":"static passive adversary corrupting one party (§2.1, p. 4; Theorem 3, p. 9)","output":["threshold"],"output_recipients":"both parties learn the intersection if the symmetric difference is small, otherwise \\( \\bot \\); the cardinality test outputs secret shares (Figure 1, p. 5; Corollary 3, p. 10)","parties":"two_party","rounds":"constant (Corollaries 2-3, p. 10)","security_model":"not_reported","set_size_regime":"balanced","setup":"\\( \\epsilon \\) public random functions into \\( t \\) buckets, replaceable by PRFs with a public key (footnote 4, p. 3); inner Ghosh-Simkin cardinality test with Cramer-Damgård constant-round determinant computation (p. 10)","technique":["secret_sharing","simple_hashing","polynomial_sparsity_test","additively_homomorphic_encryption"]},"security":{"mode":"computational","model":"standalone, static passive adversary; Theorem 3 in the (F_△, F_vec-leq)-hybrid model and Theorem 4 in the F_pict-hybrid model (pp. 9-10); correctness except with probability \\( 2^{-\\epsilon} \\) (Theorem 3, p. 9)","notion":"two-party threshold PSI"},"status":"published","title":"Ghosh-Simkin 2023 two-party threshold PSI from bucketed cardinality tests, passive security, constant rounds, \\( O(\\epsilon^{2} t\\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) communication","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","year":2023},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/919","sections":[{"content":"The two-party protocol of Corollary 3 (p. 10), the two-party case of PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER with the cardinality test and intersection step of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI.","heading":"Protocol"},{"content":"The authors present it to explain the main ideas and state that it does not improve asymptotically on the existing \\( O(\\lambda t\\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) two-party bound, which they attribute to the earlier Ghosh-Simkin construction combined with an observation of Badrinarayanan et al. (p. 4 and footnote 5). No implementation is reported.","heading":"Scope"}],"slug":"gs23-ghosh-simkin-2023-two","status":"fulltext_checked","subtitle":"two-party threshold PSI · 2023","summary":"The two-party protocol of Corollary 3 (p. 10), the two-party case of PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER with the cardinality test and intersection step of PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI.","title":"Ghosh-Simkin 2023 two-party threshold PSI","type":"construction","venue":"PKC 2023","year":2023,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2023-GS-TWO-PARTY"},{"evidence":"synthesized_from_sources","id":"PSI-GOAL-001","keywords":["two-party","malicious","unbalanced","contact-discovery","communication","concrete-efficiency"],"metadata":{"basis":"synthesized","dossier_type":"goal","evidence":"synthesized_from_sources","frontier":[{"assumption":"correlation-robust hashing and a pseudorandom code, with base OTs","cells":{"deployable":"no","malicious_both":"no","smaller_set_comm":"no"},"line":"Semi-honest OPRF PSI from OT extension","note":"Secure only against parties that follow the protocol, with communication linear in both sets, so a lookup pays for the whole user base. Two sets of \\( 2^{20} \\) items already take 3.8 s, beyond a 2 s lookup.","sources":[{"locator":"abstract, p. 1; Tables 3 and 5, p. 15","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT"}]},{"assumption":"random oracles, with OT extension or VOLE","cells":{"deployable":"no","malicious_both":"yes","smaller_set_comm":"no"},"line":"Malicious OT- and VOLE-based PSI","note":"Simulation against either party, a corrupt party's set being bounded only up to a constant factor (below \\( 4n \\) for \\( 2^{20} \\) items in 2017). Runs are balanced: \\( 2^{24} \\) items per party take 8.1 s on one laptop thread (2022). Communication is at least 128 bits per item of either set, so a lookup against \\( 10^{9} \\) users would move at least 16 GB.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 2, p. 13; §6.3, p. 15; Figure 6, p. 19","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS"},{"locator":"abstract, p. 1; Theorem 4, p. 10","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY"},{"locator":"Table 2, p. 25; §5, p. 23","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR"}]},{"assumption":"oracle Diffie-Hellman, ideal permutation, random oracles","cells":{"deployable":"no","malicious_both":"yes","smaller_set_comm":"no"},"line":"Malicious key-agreement PSI","note":"UC security against either party at about one group element per item; a corrupt receiver may use fewer than \\( 8n + 4 \\) items for \\( n = 2^{20} \\). Fastest in its authors' comparison for sets of at most 512 items; for \\( 2^{20} \\) items it takes 251 s, and its communication grows with both sets.","sources":[{"locator":"Lemmas 5-6, pp. 10-12; Table 1, p. 4; §4.1, pp. 15-16; Table 3, p. 18; §5.2, pp. 19-20","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT"}]},{"assumption":"leveled fully homomorphic encryption with circuit privacy","cells":{"deployable":"no","malicious_both":"no","smaller_set_comm":"yes"},"line":"Semi-honest FHE-based unbalanced PSI","note":"Communication \\( O(|Y| \\log |X|) \\): 11.0 to 20.1 MB for 5,535 receiver items against \\( 2^{24} \\) sender items. That run takes 36.1 s of single-threaded sender online time, about 150 contacts per core-second against a sixtieth of the scenario's users. A malicious sender can steer the receiver's output, for example to the receiver's whole set.","sources":[{"locator":"abstract, p. 1; Theorem 2, p. 11; Tables 3-5, pp. 14-16","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"}]},{"assumption":"leveled FHE, One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman, random oracles","cells":{"deployable":"no","malicious_both":"partial","smaller_set_comm":"yes"},"line":"FHE-based unbalanced PSI with OPRF preprocessing","note":"Simulation against a malicious receiver but only privacy against a malicious sender, which still decides what the receiver outputs; a variant confines the sender to dropping matches through a leakage function, under a heuristic assumption on leveled FHE. For 2,048 contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) users the sender needs 4.75 s online on 24 threads, about 18 contacts per core-second.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorems 1-2, pp. 15-16; §7.2-7.3, pp. 16-18","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR"},{"locator":"§3, p. 6; Tables 1-2, pp. 16-17","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG"}]},{"assumption":"LowMC or Naor-Reingold PRF, maliciously secure OT; no proof","cells":{"deployable":"no","malicious_both":"partial","smaller_set_comm":"no"},"line":"Precomputed OPRF PSI for mobile contact discovery","note":"Secure against a malicious client but not a malicious server, argued in prose. On a phone, 1,024 contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) users take 2.92 s over WiFi and 6.53 s over LTE, after a one-time download of a 1,072 MiB filter linear in the user base. The authors state that Signal's requirements are not yet met.","sources":[{"locator":"§4.5, pp. 10-11; Tables 4 and 6, pp. 13-14; §7, p. 14","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW"}]}],"id":"PSI-GOAL-001","keywords":["two-party","malicious","unbalanced","contact-discovery","communication","concrete-efficiency"],"layers":[{"best":"UC security, set-size slack allowed","distance":"reached","distance_note":"Reached in 2017 from OT extension; the other dimensions measure what it costs.","layer":"feasibility","rungs":[{"kind":"impossibility","level":"PSI implies oblivious transfer, so it has no black-box construction from one-way functions.","sources":[{"locator":"§3.1, pp. 3-4","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"}],"year":2004},{"kind":"construction","level":"Security against a malicious client or a malicious server; a protocol against both is only outlined.","sources":[{"locator":"Protocol PM-Malicious-Client, p. 10; Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12; §5.3, p. 13","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"}],"status":"reached","year":2004},{"kind":"construction","level":"UC security against either party; a corrupt party may use fewer than \\( 4n \\) items for \\( n = 2^{20} \\).","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 2, p. 13; §6.3, p. 15","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS"}],"status":"reached","year":2017}],"target":"full security against either party"},{"layer":"assumptions","not_applicable":"The open questions here concern security against the sender and cost; they accept any computational assumption, random oracles included.","not_applicable_label":"Any assumption allowed; security and cost are the question"},{"best":"communication linear in both sets","distance":"partway","distance_note":"Malicious protocols communicate linearly in both sets; \\( O(|Y| \\log |X|) \\) is reached only with weaker security against the sender.","layer":"asymptotic","near_misses":[{"gives_up":"malicious_both","level":"Semi-honest parties: \\( O(|Y| \\log |X|) \\) communication from leveled FHE.","problem":"PSI-OP-003","sources":[{"locator":"abstract, p. 1; Theorem 2, p. 11","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"}],"year":2017},{"gives_up":"malicious_both","level":"Simulation against a malicious receiver, only privacy against a malicious sender.","problem":"PSI-OP-002","sources":[{"locator":"Theorems 1-2, pp. 15-16; §4.2, p. 9","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR"}],"year":2018},{"gives_up":"malicious_both","level":"A malicious sender limited to dropping matches by a leakage function, under a heuristic assumption.","problem":"PSI-OP-004","sources":[{"locator":"§7.3, pp. 17-18; Theorem 3, p. 25","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR"}],"year":2018}],"rungs":[{"kind":"lower_bound","level":"For sets of equal size, computing the intersection needs communication linear in the set size.","sources":[{"locator":"§3, pp. 7-8","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"}],"year":2019},{"kind":"construction","level":"Malicious security with \\( O(\\kappa n \\log n) \\) communication.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 2, p. 13","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS"}],"status":"reached","year":2017},{"kind":"construction","level":"Malicious security with communication linear in the set size.","sources":[{"locator":"abstract, p. 1; Theorem 4, p. 10; §6, p. 22","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY"}],"status":"reached","year":2020},{"kind":"construction","level":"Full security against a malicious sender with communication sublinear in its set.","problem":"PSI-OP-001","status":"open"}],"target":"communication sublinear in the larger set"},{"best":"\\( 2^{24} \\) items per party, balanced sets only","distance":"far","distance_note":"Against Signal's \\( 10^{9} \\) users: malicious protocols run only on balanced sets of at most \\( 2^{24} \\) items, sending 128+ bits per item; trusting the server, 1,024 contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) take 2.92 s.","layer":"concrete","near_misses":[{"gives_up":"malicious_both","level":"Malicious client, semi-honest server: 1,024 contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) users in 2.92 s on a phone over WiFi, after a one-time 1,072 MiB download.","sources":[{"locator":"§4.5, pp. 10-11; Table 4, p. 13; Table 6, p. 14","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW"}],"year":2019},{"gives_up":"malicious_both","level":"Only privacy against the sender: 2,048 contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) users in 4.75 s of sender time on 24 threads, 15.09 MB online.","sources":[{"locator":"§3, p. 6; Tables 1-2, pp. 16-17","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG"}],"year":2021}],"rungs":[{"kind":"construction","level":"\\( 2^{20} \\) items per party in 18 s on one thread (LAN); 1,092 MB in the WAN configuration.","sources":[{"locator":"Figure 6, p. 19; Figure 8, p. 20","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS"}],"status":"reached","year":2017},{"kind":"construction","level":"\\( 2^{20} \\) items per party in 5.6 s on one thread (LAN) with 202 MB of communication.","sources":[{"locator":"Table 2, p. 24","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY"}],"status":"reached","year":2020},{"kind":"construction","level":"\\( 2^{24} \\) items per party in 8.1 s on one laptop thread, at about 300 bits per item.","sources":[{"locator":"Table 2, p. 25; §5, p. 23","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR"}],"status":"reached","year":2022},{"kind":"construction","level":"Contact discovery at Signal's scale: 10,000 contacts against \\( 10^{9} \\) users in under 2 s, 1,600 contacts per second per server core.","problem":"PSI-OP-011","status":"open"}],"target":"Efficient enough to deploy, measured"}],"recognized_by":[{"locator":"§1.4, p. 3; §2.2, p. 4","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","statement":"States that all earlier PSI protocols send data proportional to both entire sets, whereas the insecure solution sends only the smaller set, and presents private contact discovery from a phone as the motivating application."},{"locator":"§7, p. 14","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","statement":"Reports that Signal's developers would deploy PSI-based contact discovery only at \\( 10^{9} \\) users, address books of up to 10,000 contacts, lookups under 2 s and 1,600 contacts per second on one core, and states that its protocols, secure against malicious clients only, do not yet meet this."},{"locator":"§7.2, p. 16","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","statement":"Calls full simulation-based security against a malicious sender extremely challenging when communication must stay sublinear in the sender's set."},{"locator":"Table 2, p. 25; §5, p. 23","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","statement":"Measures malicious PSI up to \\( 2^{24} \\) items per party on one laptop thread, the largest malicious runs recorded here."}],"requirements":[{"axis":"Security","key":"malicious_both","label":"Malicious security for both","layer":"feasibility","target":"full simulation against a malicious sender and a malicious receiver; a corrupt party's set may exceed its declared size by a constant factor"},{"axis":"Efficiency","key":"smaller_set_comm","label":"Sublinear in the larger set","layer":"asymptotic","target":"communication sublinear in the larger set, as for the insecure solution, which sends only the smaller set"},{"axis":"Efficiency","key":"deployable","label":"Efficient enough to deploy","layer":"concrete","target":"latency and server computation within the budget of a real deployment on unbalanced sets, measured"}],"reviewed_by":[],"status":"open","summary":"Two parties learn their intersection with full security against either party acting maliciously, communicating sublinearly in the larger set, and efficient enough to deploy. Every known line of work gives up at least one of these.","title":"Efficient malicious two-party PSI"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"The application that practical two-party PSI keeps returning to is private\ncontact discovery: a phone checks which of its contacts use a messaging\nservice without revealing its address book (PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR,\nPSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW). The sets are unbalanced, so communication should\ndepend on the phone's set rather than on the user base, as it does without\nprivacy (PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR); and either side may cheat. Security against\nmalicious parties is the property that the fastest and the most\ncommunication-efficient protocols each weaken (PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR,\nPSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW). The goal is all three at once.","heading":"Why this goal"},{"content":"Rows group protocols by technique and by the security they prove. A malicious\nrow may let a corrupt party use a constant factor more items than it\ndeclared, as every fast malicious protocol does; tighter bounds on that slack\nfollow only from unproven conjectures that overfitting a polynomial or a\nkey-value encoding to a random oracle is hard (PSI-PAPER-2021-RS,\nPSI-PAPER-2021-RT, PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY). The last column asks whether a\nprotocol fits the deployment Signal's developers described, as reported by\nKales et al. (PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW, §7): a server database of \\( 10^{9} \\) users,\naddress books of up to 10,000 contacts, lookups in under 2 s, and one server\ncore handling 1,600 contacts per second. Balanced protocols fail it because\ntheir communication grows with the user base; the unbalanced ones are too\nslow at a quarter of that scale and do not resist a malicious server. Times\ncome from different machines and are compared only with the budget, not with\neach other.","heading":"Reading the frontier"}],"slug":"goal-1","status":"open","subtitle":"","summary":"Efficient malicious two-party PSI","title":"Efficient malicious two-party PSI","type":"goal","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-GOAL-001"},{"evidence":"synthesized_from_sources","id":"PSI-GOAL-002","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","communication-complexity","lower-bound","cardinality-testing","threshold-fhe"],"metadata":{"basis":"synthesized","dossier_type":"goal","evidence":"synthesized_from_sources","frontier":[{"assumption":"fully homomorphic encryption","cells":{"independent_of_n":"yes","many_parties":"no","no_fhe":"no","optimal_comm":"yes"},"line":"Two-party threshold PSI from FHE","note":"\\( O(t \\log p) \\) bits for two parties, matching the \\( \\Omega(t) \\) lower bound; given as a sketch.","sources":[{"locator":"§3, pp. 7-8","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"}]},{"assumption":"additively homomorphic encryption","cells":{"independent_of_n":"yes","many_parties":"no","no_fhe":"yes","optimal_comm":"yes"},"line":"Two-party threshold PSI from additively homomorphic encryption","note":"\\( \\tilde O(t^{2}) \\) bits in 2019 through a quadratic cardinality test; \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) in 2021 by testing a Hankel matrix at quasi-linear cost. Only two parties.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorems 4-5, pp. 14-16; §6.3, p. 14","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"},{"locator":"§3.3, p. 13; §7.1, p. 29","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}]},{"assumption":"threshold fully homomorphic encryption","cells":{"independent_of_n":"yes","many_parties":"yes","no_fhe":"no","optimal_comm":"yes"},"line":"Multiparty threshold PSI from threshold FHE","note":"\\( O(\\ell t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) bits in a constant number of rounds, matching the \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) bound for point-to-point networks.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 5.1, p. 17; Corollary B.2, p. 40; Theorem 4.1 and Corollary 4.2, pp. 15-16","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}]},{"assumption":"threshold additively homomorphic encryption","cells":{"independent_of_n":"yes","many_parties":"partial","no_fhe":"yes","optimal_comm":"yes"},"line":"Multiparty threshold PSI from threshold additively homomorphic encryption","note":"\\( \\tilde O(\\ell t) \\) bits, but only for a stricter functionality that releases the intersection when all elements outside it, over every set together, number at most t.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 6.5 and Corollary 6.6, pp. 27-28; Table 1, p. 3","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}]},{"assumption":"oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption","cells":{"independent_of_n":"yes","many_parties":"yes","no_fhe":"yes","optimal_comm":"partial"},"line":"Multiparty threshold PSI from oblivious transfer","note":"\\( O(\\lambda^{2} \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits when each broadcast message counts once; the cost over point-to-point channels, where the \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) bound holds, is not stated.","sources":[{"locator":"§2.1, p. 4; Corollaries 5-6, p. 16","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS"}]}],"id":"PSI-GOAL-002","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","communication-complexity","lower-bound","cardinality-testing","threshold-fhe"],"layers":[{"best":"ℓ parties via threshold FHE","distance":"reached","distance_note":"Reached in 2021 from threshold FHE; without a threshold condition, communication must grow linearly with the set size.","layer":"feasibility","rungs":[{"kind":"construction","level":"Two parties, \\( O(t \\log p) \\) bits from FHE.","sources":[{"locator":"§3, pp. 7-8","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"}],"status":"reached","year":2019},{"kind":"construction","level":"Any number of parties, \\( O(\\ell t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) bits from threshold FHE.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 5.1, p. 17; Corollary B.2, p. 40","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}],"status":"reached","year":2021}],"target":"ℓ parties, cost independent of set size"},{"best":"\\( O(\\lambda^{2} \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) from OT","distance":"near","distance_note":"One step: a cardinality test of this cost without FHE over point-to-point channels would complete the protocol, because the intersection step already avoids FHE.","layer":"assumptions","near_misses":[{"gives_up":"many_parties","level":"Two parties: a cardinality test with quasi-linear communication from additively homomorphic encryption.","problem":"PSI-OP-008","sources":[{"locator":"§3.3, p. 13; Theorem 6.5 and Corollary 6.6, pp. 27-28","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}],"year":2021},{"gives_up":"many_parties","level":"Two parties: threshold PSI with \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) communication from additively homomorphic encryption.","problem":"PSI-OP-007","sources":[{"locator":"abstract, p. 1; §1.2, p. 4; §7.1, p. 29","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}],"year":2021},{"gives_up":"many_parties","level":"\\( \\tilde O(\\ell t) \\) without FHE when the elements outside the intersection number at most t over all sets together.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 6.5 and Corollary 6.6, pp. 27-28","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}],"year":2021}],"rungs":[{"kind":"construction","level":"From oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption, \\( O(\\lambda^{2} \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits when a broadcast counts once.","problem":"PSI-OP-009","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 7, p. 15; Corollaries 5-6, p. 16","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS"}],"status":"reached","year":2023},{"kind":"construction","level":"An ℓ-party cardinality test at \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits without FHE over point-to-point channels.","problem":"PSI-OP-010","status":"open"},{"kind":"construction","level":"ℓ-party threshold PSI at \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits without FHE over point-to-point channels.","problem":"PSI-OP-005","status":"open"}],"target":"no FHE, \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits"},{"best":"\\( O(\\ell t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\), matching \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\)","distance":"reached","distance_note":"Reached in 2021 from threshold FHE, matching the lower bound; the assumptions ladder asks for the same cost without FHE.","layer":"asymptotic","rungs":[{"kind":"lower_bound","level":"Two parties need \\( \\Omega(t) \\) bits, by the linear lower bound for computing an intersection.","sources":[{"locator":"§3, pp. 7-8","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"}],"year":2019},{"kind":"lower_bound","level":"ℓ parties need \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) bits over point-to-point channels.","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 4.1 and Corollary 4.2, pp. 15-16","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}],"year":2021},{"kind":"construction","level":"\\( O(\\ell t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) bits in a constant number of rounds from threshold FHE.","problem":"PSI-OP-006","sources":[{"locator":"Theorem 5.1, p. 17; Corollary B.2, p. 40","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}],"status":"reached","year":2021}],"target":"\\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits, point to point"},{"layer":"concrete","not_applicable":"The covered threshold PSI papers describe no deployment scenario, workload or budget: they present communication bounds and do not claim practicality.","not_applicable_label":"The literature gives no deployment target"}],"recognized_by":[{"locator":"§9, pp. 19-20","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","statement":"Calls threshold PSI with communication quasi-linear in t from assumptions weaker than fully homomorphic encryption an open problem."},{"locator":"Tables 1-2, p. 3; §1.1, p. 4","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","statement":"Marks multiparty threshold PSI from threshold additively homomorphic encryption as unknown for the functionality its threshold-FHE protocol solves, and suggests building the cardinality test from weaker assumptions."},{"locator":"§1, p. 2","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","statement":"States that earlier work leaves asymptotically optimal multiparty threshold PSI from assumptions other than fully homomorphic encryption open."}],"requirements":[{"axis":"Setting","key":"many_parties","label":"Any number of parties","layer":"feasibility","target":"\\( \\ell \\ge 2 \\) semi-honest parties, up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) corrupted, learn the intersection when it holds at least \\( n - t \\) of each party's n elements"},{"axis":"Efficiency","key":"independent_of_n","label":"Independent of set size","layer":"feasibility","target":"communication polynomial in t, ℓ and λ, independent of the set size n"},{"axis":"Assumption","key":"no_fhe","label":"No FHE","layer":"assumptions","target":"no fully homomorphic or threshold fully homomorphic encryption, for example oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption"},{"axis":"Efficiency","key":"optimal_comm","label":"Optimal communication","layer":"asymptotic","target":"\\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits over point-to-point channels, matching the \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) lower bound"}],"reviewed_by":[],"status":"open","summary":"Any number of parties learn their intersection exactly when it is large, communicating \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\) bits up to polylogarithmic and security-parameter factors over point-to-point channels, without fully homomorphic encryption. Every known protocol gives up at least one of these.","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI at optimal communication without FHE"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Computing an intersection needs communication linear in the set size, but\nwhen parties only want the intersection if it is large, communication can\ndepend on the threshold \\( t \\) instead (PSI-PAPER-2019-GS). For any number of\nparties the cost is now pinned down: at least \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) bits over\npoint-to-point channels, and that much suffices with threshold fully\nhomomorphic encryption (PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR). The area asks for the same cost\nfrom the weaker assumptions behind ordinary PSI (PSI-PAPER-2023-GS).","heading":"Why this goal"},{"content":"Each row gives up one requirement: more than two parties, an assumption weaker\nthan FHE, the exact functionality, or communication counted over\npoint-to-point channels. The row for threshold additively homomorphic\nencryption is marked partial on the first column because it solves a stricter\nfunctionality, which bounds the elements outside the intersection over all\nsets together rather than per party. The last row counts a broadcast message\nonce; over point-to-point channels each broadcast is sent to every party, and\nthe source does not state the resulting cost.","heading":"Reading the frontier"}],"slug":"goal-2","status":"open","subtitle":"","summary":"Multiparty threshold PSI at optimal communication without FHE","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI at optimal communication without FHE","type":"goal","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-GOAL-002"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-001","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","malicious-sender","fully-homomorphic-encryption","sublinear-communication","simulation-security"],"metadata":{"barriers":[],"basis":"named","caveat":null,"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-001","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","malicious-sender","fully-homomorphic-encryption","sublinear-communication","simulation-security"],"named_open_by":[{"locator":"§5.2 'Discussion', paragraph 'Malicious behavior', PDF pp. 12-13 (ePrint 2017/299, PDF dated 2017-09-06)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","statement":"Notes that a malicious sender can evaluate an arbitrary function on the receiver's encrypted set and so steer the receiver's output, for example to the receiver's whole set, and says that efficiently preventing such behavior by the sender appears extremely challenging."},{"locator":"§7.2 'Receiver Privacy', PDF p. 16 (ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","statement":"Calls full simulation-based security against a malicious sender extremely challenging in its setting; names the requirement of communication sublinear in the sender's set as the main barrier, because it rules out extracting that set with zero-knowledge proofs of linear size, and enforcing correct computation by the sender as the second; it therefore proves only privacy against a malicious sender."}],"profile":{"Efficiency":"total communication sublinear in the sender's set size","Functionality":"two-party PSI: the receiver learns \\( X \\cap Y \\), the sender learns nothing","Model":"random oracle model permitted","Security":"full simulation-based security against a malicious sender and against a malicious receiver","Setting":"unbalanced: the receiver's set much smaller than the sender's"},"refuted_by":[],"related":[],"resolution_condition":"A two-party protocol in which the receiver, holding \\( Y \\) with \\( |Y| = N_y \\), learns \\( X^* \\cap Y \\) and the sender, holding \\( X \\) with \\( |X| = N_x \\), learns nothing, that is fully simulation-secure (stand-alone, static corruption, in the plain or the random oracle model) against a malicious receiver whose effective set has at most \\( N_y \\) items and against a malicious sender, for whom the simulator extracts an effective set \\( X^* \\) of polynomial size; and whose total communication is at most \\( p(\\lambda, N_y) \\cdot g(N_x) \\) for some polynomial \\( p \\) and some function \\( g \\) with \\( g(N) = o(N) \\). Any computational assumption may be used.","resolved_by":[],"status":"open","summary":"Can a receiver with a small set learn its intersection with a much larger sender set, with full simulation-based security against either party acting maliciously, while communication stays sublinear in the sender's set? Solved for a malicious receiver; open for a malicious sender.","title":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious sender with sublinear communication"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"In unbalanced PSI a receiver with a small set \\( Y \\), such as a phone's\ncontact list, learns which of its items lie in a sender's much larger set\n\\( X \\), as in private contact discovery. Protocols built on fully homomorphic\nencryption let the receiver send its encrypted set and the sender evaluate the\nintersection on it, so that communication grows with \\( |Y| \\) and only\nlogarithmically with \\( |X| \\) (PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR). The sender then holds an\nencrypted copy of the receiver's set and may compute on it whatever it likes,\nwhich makes security against a malicious sender the hard direction.","heading":"Background"},{"content":"Is there a two-party protocol in which the receiver learns \\( X \\cap Y \\) and\nthe sender learns nothing, that is fully simulation-secure against a malicious\nreceiver and against a malicious sender, possibly in the random oracle model,\nand whose total communication is sublinear in \\( |X| \\) for every fixed\nreceiver set size and security parameter?","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"$7","heading":"Why it is open"},{"content":"- Privacy against a malicious sender, which guarantees neither input\n  independence nor a correct output for the receiver.\n- Leaky functionalities, in which the receiver's output may depend on its items\n  outside the intersection.\n- Protocols whose communication is linear in the sender's set, such as\n  balanced malicious-secure PSI, or protocols that transfer an encoding of the\n  whole sender set in a setup phase (PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW).","heading":"What would not resolve it"},{"content":"Parties: a sender with \\( X \\), \\( |X| = N_x \\), and a receiver with \\( Y \\),\n\\( |Y| = N_y \\); \\( N_x \\), \\( N_y \\) and the item length are public. Ideal\nfunctionality: the receiver obtains \\( X^* \\cap Y \\), where \\( X^* \\) is the\nsender's input, and the sender obtains nothing. Security: stand-alone\nsimulation against a static malicious adversary corrupting either party; the\nsimulator for a corrupt receiver submits a set of at most \\( N_y \\) items, and\nthe simulator for a corrupt sender submits a set of polynomial size. Model:\nplain or random oracle model; any computational assumption. Efficiency: total\ncommunication at most \\( p(\\lambda, N_y)\\cdot g(N_x) \\) for a polynomial\n\\( p \\) and some \\( g(N) = o(N) \\).","heading":"Formal target"},{"content":"Hashing gives a malicious sender further ways to make the receiver's output\ndepend on items outside the intersection: it can perform its simple hashing\nincorrectly, and when it reuses its preprocessing across receivers and\nchooses the hash functions it can provoke cuckoo-hashing failures that depend\non the receiver's set, which leaks one bit (PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR, §7.2 and\n§7.3).","heading":"Known obstacles"},{"content":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR introduced the homomorphic approach with communication\n\\( O(|Y|\\log|X|) \\) against semi-honest parties and noted that a malicious\nsender can steer the output. PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR added oblivious PRF\npreprocessing, which gives one-sided simulation against a malicious receiver\nand privacy against a malicious sender, and a variant that limits a malicious\nsender to a leakage function under the H-limited assumption.\nPSI-PAPER-2021-CONG reduced computation and communication while keeping the\nsame guarantees.","heading":"Research history"}],"slug":"op-1","status":"open","subtitle":"","summary":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious sender with sublinear communication","title":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious sender with sublinear communication","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-001"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-002","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","malicious-receiver","oprf-preprocessing","one-sided-simulation","random-oracle"],"metadata":{"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-002","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","malicious-receiver","oprf-preprocessing","one-sided-simulation","random-oracle"],"named_open_by":[{"locator":"§5.2 'Discussion', paragraph 'Malicious behavior', PDF p. 13 (ePrint 2017/299, PDF dated 2017-09-06)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","statement":"Notes that a malicious receiver can fill vacant cuckoo-hash slots to enlarge its set and add noise to its ciphertexts so that noise flooding no longer hides the sender's set, and leaves a formal analysis of the malicious setting and countermeasures to future work."}],"profile":{"Efficiency":"total communication sublinear in the sender's set size","Functionality":"two-party PSI: the receiver learns \\( X \\cap Y \\), the sender learns nothing","Model":"random oracle model permitted","Security":"one-sided simulation against a malicious receiver; privacy only against a malicious sender","Setting":"unbalanced: the receiver's set much smaller than the sender's"},"resolved_by":[{"locator":"Theorem 1, §7.1, PDF p. 15; Theorem 2, §7.2, PDF p. 16; communication \\( O(|Y| \\log |X|) \\), §4.2, PDF p. 9 (ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR"}],"status":"resolved","summary":"Unbalanced PSI with communication sublinear in the sender's set, secure against a malicious receiver and only private against a malicious sender: the sender learns nothing about the receiver's set, but the receiver's output need not be correct. Solved with oblivious PRF preprocessing in the random oracle model.","title":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious receiver with sublinear communication"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Give an unbalanced PSI protocol with communication sublinear in the sender's\nset that is simulation-secure against a malicious receiver and keeps the\nreceiver's set private from a malicious sender, in the sense of Hazay and\nLindell: the sender's view reveals nothing about the receiver's input, while\ninput independence and output correctness are not required.","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR first maps both sets through the Diffie-Hellman oblivious\nPRF \\( F_\\alpha(x) = H'(H(x)^\\alpha) \\), whose key the sender holds, and then\nruns the homomorphic intersection of PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR on the outputs. In the\nrandom oracle model and in a group where One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman is hard,\na simulator extracts a malicious receiver's set, of the honest size, from its\noracle queries (Theorem 1). Semantic security of the homomorphic encryption\nand the same group assumption give privacy against a malicious sender\n(Theorem 2). Communication is \\( O(|Y|\\log|X|) \\) (§4.2). Because the sender's\nnon-matching items look random to the receiver, noise flooding is no longer\nneeded, and the sender can reuse its processed set across receivers.\nPSI-PAPER-2021-CONG keeps these guarantees with lower cost.","heading":"Resolution"}],"slug":"op-2","status":"resolved","subtitle":"","summary":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious receiver with sublinear communication","title":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious receiver with sublinear communication","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-002"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-003","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","semi-honest","fully-homomorphic-encryption","simd-batching","cuckoo-hashing"],"metadata":{"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-003","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","semi-honest","fully-homomorphic-encryption","simd-batching","cuckoo-hashing"],"profile":{"Efficiency":"total communication sublinear in the sender's set size","Functionality":"two-party PSI: the receiver learns \\( X \\cap Y \\), the sender learns nothing","Model":"random oracle model permitted","Security":"semi-honest sender and receiver","Setting":"unbalanced: the receiver's set much smaller than the sender's"},"resolved_by":[{"locator":"Abstract, PDF p. 1; Theorem 2, §5.1, PDF p. 11; Figure 4, PDF p. 12 (ePrint 2017/299, PDF dated 2017-09-06)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"}],"status":"resolved","summary":"Unbalanced PSI with communication sublinear in the sender's set, for parties that follow the protocol. Solved with leveled homomorphic encryption, batching, and hashing: communication \\( O(|Y| \\log |X|) \\).","title":"Semi-honest unbalanced PSI with sublinear communication"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Give a two-party PSI protocol, secure against semi-honest parties, in which\nthe receiver learns \\( X \\cap Y \\) and total communication is sublinear in the\nsender's set size \\( |X| \\).","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR has the receiver cuckoo-hash its items, pack the table into\nbatched ciphertexts of a leveled fully homomorphic scheme, and send them with\nsome encrypted powers; the sender, holding its items in simple-hashed bins,\nevaluates for each bin a polynomial that vanishes on the bin's items and\nreturns the results, and the receiver reads its matches off the zero slots\n(Figure 4). Windowing and partitioning of the bins keep the multiplicative\ndepth low. Communication is \\( O(|Y|\\log|X|) \\) (abstract), and the protocol\nis secure against semi-honest parties in the standard model, assuming an\nIND-CPA scheme with circuit privacy, which noise flooding provides in the\nleveled setting (Theorem 2).","heading":"Resolution"}],"slug":"op-3","status":"resolved","subtitle":"","summary":"Semi-honest unbalanced PSI with sublinear communication","title":"Semi-honest unbalanced PSI with sublinear communication","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-003"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-004","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","malicious-sender","leaky-functionality","h-limited","labeled-psi","random-oracle"],"metadata":{"caveat":"The H-limited property of leveled homomorphic encryption is a heuristic assumption supported by experiments, not by a reduction to a standard problem.","dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-004","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","malicious-sender","leaky-functionality","h-limited","labeled-psi","random-oracle"],"profile":{"Efficiency":"total communication sublinear in the sender's set size","Functionality":"two-party PSI: the receiver learns \\( X \\cap Y \\), the sender learns nothing","Model":"random oracle model permitted","Security":"simulation against a malicious receiver, and against a malicious sender for output \\( X \\cap Y \\cap L(Y) \\)","Setting":"unbalanced: the receiver's set much smaller than the sender's"},"resolved_by":[{"locator":"§7.3 and Definition 1, PDF pp. 17-18; Figures 7-8 and Theorem 3, Appendix A, PDF p. 25 (ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR"}],"status":"resolved","summary":"Unbalanced PSI with sublinear communication, secure against a malicious sender when the receiver's output may be \\( X \\cap Y \\cap L(Y) \\) for a leakage function \\( L \\) that the sender chooses. Solved under a heuristic assumption that leveled homomorphic encryption cannot evaluate a deep hash function.","title":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious sender with bounded leakage"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Give an unbalanced PSI protocol with communication sublinear in the sender's\nset that is fully secure against a malicious receiver and simulation-secure\nagainst a malicious sender for a weaker functionality: the sender submits a\nset \\( X^* \\) and a leakage function \\( L \\), and the receiver obtains\n\\( X^* \\cap Y \\cap L(Y) \\). A malicious sender can thus withhold matches\ndepending on the receiver's other items, but cannot add items it does not\nhold.","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR runs its labeled protocol, whose communication is\nsublinear in the sender's set (§1.3, p. 3), with the label of each sender item\nset to a hash \\( H(\\mathrm{OPRF}_k(x)) \\); the receiver accepts \\( y \\) only\nif the decrypted label at its position equals \\( H(\\mathrm{OPRF}_k(y)) \\), and\nthe sender proves in zero knowledge that it used one PRF key (Figure 8).\nDefinition 1 calls a leveled scheme H-limited if no efficient party can turn an\nencryption of \\( z \\) into an encryption of \\( H(z) \\). In the random oracle\nmodel, with a One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman group and a semantically secure\nH-limited leveled scheme, the protocol realizes the leaky functionality with\nnon-black-box simulation, a malicious receiver held to its declared set size,\nand a malicious sender's set of polynomial size (Theorem 3). The theorem\ncovers a sender that does not reuse its preprocessing; with reuse and\nsender-chosen hash functions, selective cuckoo-hashing failures leak one bit\n(§7.3, p. 18).","heading":"Resolution"}],"slug":"op-4","status":"resolved","subtitle":"","summary":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious sender with bounded leakage","title":"Unbalanced PSI against a malicious sender with bounded leakage","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-004"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-005","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","communication-complexity","lower-bound","cardinality-testing"],"metadata":{"barriers":[],"basis":"named","caveat":null,"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-005","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","communication-complexity","lower-bound","cardinality-testing"],"named_open_by":[{"locator":"Tables 1 and 2, PDF p. 3; §1.1, PDF p. 4 (ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","statement":"Marks as unknown a protocol from threshold additively homomorphic encryption for the functionality that releases the intersection when every set is close to it, and for the matching cardinality test, while its threshold-FHE protocols meet its \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) lower bound; suggests that future work build that cardinality test from assumptions weaker than threshold FHE and reuse the paper's second phase."},{"locator":"§1, PDF p. 2 (ePrint 2023/919, PDF dated 2023-06-12)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","statement":"States that Ghosh and Simkin (2019), Branco, Döttling and Pu, and Badrinarayanan et al. all leave constructing asymptotically optimal multiparty threshold PSI protocols from assumptions other than fully homomorphic encryption as an open problem, after noting that the latter two prove \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) and match it up to polylogarithmic factors with FHE."}],"profile":{"Assumption":"no fully homomorphic encryption, e.g. oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption","Efficiency":"total communication \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits, independent of \\( n \\)","Functionality":"threshold PSI: every party learns the intersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) of their \\( n \\) elements","Model":"point-to-point private channels","Security":"semi-honest, static, up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) of \\( \\ell \\) parties corrupted","Setting":"any number \\( \\ell \\ge 2 \\) of parties"},"refuted_by":[],"related":[],"resolution_condition":"A family of protocols, one for every number of parties \\( \\ell \\ge 2 \\), in which each party holds a set of \\( n \\) elements and every party obtains the intersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) elements and nothing otherwise; secure against a static semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) parties; over pairwise private point-to-point channels, each message counted at its length; with total communication at most \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits for every \\( n \\); and relying on no fully homomorphic or threshold fully homomorphic encryption.","resolved_by":[],"status":"open","summary":"Can many parties learn the intersection of their sets exactly when it is large, with communication matching the \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) lower bound up to polylogarithmic and security-parameter factors, without fully homomorphic encryption? Known from threshold FHE, for two parties, and when broadcast messages count once.","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI with optimal communication without FHE"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"In threshold PSI the parties learn the intersection of their sets only if it is\nlarge, here if it contains at least \\( n - t \\) of each party's \\( n \\)\nelements, so for small \\( t \\) communication can fall far below the set size,\nwhich plain PSI cannot achieve in general (PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR, §1). For two\nparties PSI-PAPER-2019-GS proved an \\( \\Omega(t) \\) lower bound and an almost\nmatching protocol from fully homomorphic encryption (FHE). PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR\nextended both to \\( \\ell \\) parties: every protocol communicates\n\\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) over point-to-point channels, and threshold FHE achieves\n\\( O(\\ell t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\). Whether that cost is reachable from\nweaker assumptions is the remaining question.","heading":"Background"},{"content":"Let \\( \\ell \\ge 2 \\) parties each hold a set of \\( n \\) elements, and let\n\\( t < n \\). Without fully homomorphic or threshold fully homomorphic\nencryption, is there a protocol, secure against a semi-honest adversary\ncorrupting up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) parties, that gives every party the\nintersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) elements and nothing otherwise,\nover point-to-point channels, with total communication\n\\( \\ell t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)\\cdot\\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits?","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"$8","heading":"Why it is open"},{"content":"- Protocols that use plain or threshold fully homomorphic encryption.\n- Protocols for the functionality that bounds all elements outside the\n  intersection together, rather than each party's.\n- Bounds that count a broadcast message once, unless they are also shown over\n  point-to-point channels.\n- Communication of order \\( \\ell t^2 \\), or with factors in \\( \\ell \\) or\n  \\( t \\) beyond \\( \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\).","heading":"What would not resolve it"},{"content":"Parties \\( P_1,\\dots,P_\\ell \\), \\( \\ell \\ge 2 \\), hold sets\n\\( X_1,\\dots,X_\\ell \\) of exactly \\( n \\) elements from a universe of\n\\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\)-bit strings; \\( t < n \\) is public. Ideal\nfunctionality: if \\( |\\bigcap_i X_i| \\ge n - t \\), every party receives\n\\( \\bigcap_i X_i \\); otherwise every party receives \\( \\bot \\). Security:\nsimulation against a static semi-honest adversary corrupting any \\( \\ell - 1 \\)\nparties. Network: pairwise private channels, each message counted at its\nlength; a star topology is allowed. Efficiency: total communication\n\\( O(\\ell t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)\\cdot\\mathrm{polylog}(t)) \\) bits for\nevery \\( n \\); rounds unrestricted. Assumptions: any that do not include\nfully homomorphic or threshold fully homomorphic encryption.","heading":"Formal target"},{"content":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS initiated sublinear threshold PSI for two parties, with an\n\\( \\Omega(t) \\) bound, an almost matching FHE protocol, an\n\\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\) protocol from additively homomorphic encryption, and a\nmultiparty extension. PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR defined two multiparty\nfunctionalities, proved \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) for both, met it with threshold\nFHE, gave \\( \\tilde O(\\ell t) \\) without FHE for the stricter functionality,\nand thereby settled the two-party case. PSI-PAPER-2023-GS compiled quadratic\ncardinality tests into quasilinear ones, counting broadcast messages once.","heading":"Research history"}],"slug":"op-5","status":"open","subtitle":"","summary":"Multiparty threshold PSI with optimal communication without FHE","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI with optimal communication without FHE","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-005"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-006","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","threshold-fhe","communication-complexity"],"metadata":{"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-006","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","threshold-fhe","communication-complexity"],"profile":{"Assumption":"threshold fully homomorphic encryption with distributed setup","Efficiency":"total communication \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits, independent of \\( n \\)","Functionality":"threshold PSI: every party learns the intersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) of their \\( n \\) elements","Model":"point-to-point private channels","Security":"semi-honest, static, up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) of \\( \\ell \\) parties corrupted","Setting":"any number \\( \\ell \\ge 2 \\) of parties"},"resolved_by":[{"locator":"Theorem 5.1, §5.1, PDF p. 17, with its communication analysis, PDF p. 19; Theorem B.1 and Corollary B.2, Appendix B, PDF p. 40; lower bound Theorem 4.1 and Corollary 4.2, PDF pp. 15-16 (ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}],"status":"resolved","summary":"Multiparty threshold PSI at the optimal communication, with threshold fully homomorphic encryption allowed. Solved with \\( O(\\ell t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) communication in a constant number of rounds, matching the \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) lower bound.","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI with optimal communication from threshold FHE"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Give an \\( \\ell \\)-party threshold PSI protocol, secure against a semi-honest\nadversary corrupting up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) parties, that releases the\nintersection exactly when it has at least \\( n - t \\) elements, with\ncommunication \\( \\ell t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) up to polylogarithmic\nfactors over point-to-point channels, assuming threshold fully homomorphic\nencryption.","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR works in a star network. In the first phase each party\nsends a central party \\( O(t) \\) threshold-FHE encryptions of evaluations of\nits set polynomial; the central party homomorphically interpolates a rational\nfunction and tests it at a random point, and the parties jointly decrypt one\nbit (Theorem 5.1, with \\( O(\\ell t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\)\ncommunication, p. 19). If the test passes, a second phase from threshold\nadditively homomorphic encryption gives every party \\( 3t + 4 \\) evaluations\nof a randomized combination of all set polynomials, from which it interpolates\nthe elements of its own set outside the intersection (Theorem B.1). The whole\nprotocol communicates \\( O(\\ell t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) bits in a\nconstant number of rounds (Corollary B.2, whose statement names the other\nfunctionality although it instantiates this protocol), matching the\n\\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) bound for point-to-point networks (Theorem 4.1,\nCorollary 4.2).","heading":"Resolution"}],"slug":"op-6","status":"resolved","subtitle":"","summary":"Multiparty threshold PSI with optimal communication from threshold FHE","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI with optimal communication from threshold FHE","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-006"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-007","keywords":["threshold-psi","two-party","hankel-matrix","cardinality-testing","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"metadata":{"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-007","keywords":["threshold-psi","two-party","hankel-matrix","cardinality-testing","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"named_open_by":[{"locator":"§9 'Conclusion and Open Problems', PDF pp. 19-20 (ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","statement":"Calls it an open problem to construct a threshold PSI protocol with communication quasi-linear in \\( t \\) from assumptions weaker than fully homomorphic encryption, and notes that its intersection step already has this cost, so only a cardinality test of the same cost is missing."}],"profile":{"Assumption":"no fully homomorphic encryption, e.g. oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption","Efficiency":"total communication \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits, independent of \\( n \\)","Functionality":"threshold PSI: every party learns the intersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) of their \\( n \\) elements","Model":"point-to-point private channels","Security":"semi-honest, static, up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) of \\( \\ell \\) parties corrupted","Setting":"two parties"},"resolved_by":[{"locator":"Abstract, PDF p. 1; §1.2, PDF p. 4; §3.3, PDF p. 13; §7.1 'Two-party case', PDF p. 29 (ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"},{"locator":"Theorem 4 and the paragraph before Lemma 4, §6.3, PDF p. 14 (ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"}],"status":"resolved","summary":"Threshold PSI without FHE at quasi-linear communication, for two parties, posed by Ghosh and Simkin. Solved: a Hankel-matrix singularity test of quasi-linear cost gives communication quasi-linear in \\( t \\) from additively homomorphic encryption.","title":"Two-party threshold PSI with quasi-linear communication without FHE"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Give a two-party protocol, secure against semi-honest parties and without\nfully homomorphic encryption, that reveals the intersection of two sets of\n\\( n \\) elements exactly when it has at least \\( n - t \\) elements, with\ncommunication \\( \\tilde O(t) \\), that is \\( t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\)\nup to polylogarithmic factors.","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"$9","heading":"Resolution"}],"slug":"op-7","status":"resolved","subtitle":"","summary":"Two-party threshold PSI with quasi-linear communication without FHE","title":"Two-party threshold PSI with quasi-linear communication without FHE","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-007"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-008","keywords":["cardinality-testing","threshold-psi","hankel-matrix","half-gcd"],"metadata":{"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-008","keywords":["cardinality-testing","threshold-psi","hankel-matrix","half-gcd"],"named_open_by":[{"locator":"§9 'Conclusion and Open Problems', PDF pp. 19-20 (ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","statement":"Says that, since its intersection step already has the desired cost, one only needs a private intersection cardinality test with communication quasi-linear in \\( t \\) from assumptions weaker than fully homomorphic encryption."}],"profile":{"Assumption":"no fully homomorphic encryption","Efficiency":"communication \\( t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits, independent of \\( n \\)","Functionality":"two-party intersection cardinality test: whether \\( |X \\cap Y| \\ge n - t \\)","Security":"semi-honest"},"resolved_by":[{"locator":"§3.3, PDF p. 13; Theorem 6.5 and Corollary 6.6, §6.2, PDF pp. 27-28, for two parties (ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"}],"status":"resolved","summary":"A two-party protocol, without FHE, that decides whether two sets of \\( n \\) elements share at least \\( n - t \\) of them, with communication quasi-linear in \\( t \\). By the Ghosh-Simkin reduction it settles two-party threshold PSI without FHE; Badrinarayanan et al. gave one.","title":"Two-party intersection cardinality test with quasi-linear communication"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Is there a two-party protocol, secure against semi-honest parties and without\nfully homomorphic encryption, that outputs whether two sets of \\( n \\)\nelements share at least \\( n - t \\) of them, with communication\n\\( t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)\\cdot\\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits?","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"In the test of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS the sets are encoded so that the size of\ntheir symmetric difference is the number of monomials of a polynomial, whose\nsparsity is decided by the singularity of a Hankel matrix; testing it with a\nprotocol for arbitrary matrices costs \\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\).\nPSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR observes that a Hankel matrix has only linearly many\ndistinct entries and that its singularity reduces to the half-GCD problem,\nsolvable in quasi-linear time, so the parties can obtain additive shares of the\nmatrix and test it with additively homomorphic encryption or Yao's garbled\ncircuits at quasi-linear communication (§3.3). Theorem 6.5 and Corollary 6.6\ngive the protocol for any number of parties from threshold additively\nhomomorphic encryption; for more than two parties it decides whether all\nelements outside the intersection together number at most \\( t \\).","heading":"Resolution"}],"slug":"op-8","status":"resolved","subtitle":"","summary":"Two-party intersection cardinality test with quasi-linear communication","title":"Two-party intersection cardinality test with quasi-linear communication","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-008"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-009","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","broadcast-channel","bucketing","cardinality-testing","oblivious-transfer"],"metadata":{"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-009","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","broadcast-channel","bucketing","cardinality-testing","oblivious-transfer"],"profile":{"Assumption":"no fully homomorphic encryption, e.g. oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption","Efficiency":"total communication \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits, independent of \\( n \\)","Functionality":"threshold PSI: every party learns the intersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) of their \\( n \\) elements","Model":"private channels plus a broadcast channel whose messages count once","Security":"semi-honest, static, up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) of \\( \\ell \\) parties corrupted","Setting":"any number \\( \\ell \\ge 2 \\) of parties"},"resolved_by":[{"locator":"§2.1, PDF p. 4; Theorem 7, §4, PDF p. 15; Theorem 8 and Corollaries 5-6, §4.1, PDF p. 16 (ePrint 2023/919, PDF dated 2023-06-12)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS"}],"status":"resolved","summary":"Multiparty threshold PSI without FHE at near-optimal communication, in a network with a broadcast channel whose messages count once. Solved from oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption with \\( O(\\lambda^{2}\\ell t\\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\,t) \\) bits by a bucketing compiler, against passive corruption of all but one party.","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI without FHE when broadcasts count once"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Give an \\( \\ell \\)-party threshold PSI protocol, without fully homomorphic\nencryption and secure against a semi-honest adversary corrupting up to\n\\( \\ell - 1 \\) parties, with communication\n\\( \\ell t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)\\cdot\\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits, in a\nnetwork of private channels together with a broadcast channel on which\nsending a message costs its length once, whatever the number of recipients.","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"$a","heading":"Resolution"}],"slug":"op-9","status":"resolved","subtitle":"","summary":"Multiparty threshold PSI without FHE when broadcasts count once","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI without FHE when broadcasts count once","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-009"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-010","keywords":["cardinality-testing","threshold-psi","multiparty","communication-complexity"],"metadata":{"basis":"named","dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-OP-010","keywords":["cardinality-testing","threshold-psi","multiparty","communication-complexity"],"named_open_by":[{"locator":"Table 2, PDF p. 3; §1.1, PDF p. 4 (ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","statement":"Marks a cardinality test for this functionality from threshold additively homomorphic encryption as unknown, and suggests that future work build one from assumptions weaker than threshold FHE and plug in the paper's second phase."},{"locator":"§8.2, PDF p. 19 (ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27)","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","statement":"Separates the multiparty cardinality test, which it builds from threshold FHE, from the intersection protocol to stress that a cardinality test from simpler assumptions would immediately give the whole multiparty protocol from those assumptions."}],"profile":{"Assumption":"no fully homomorphic encryption","Efficiency":"total communication \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits, independent of \\( n \\)","Functionality":"multiparty intersection cardinality test: all parties learn whether the intersection has at least \\( n - t \\) elements","Model":"point-to-point private channels","Security":"semi-honest, static, up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) of \\( \\ell \\) parties corrupted"},"resolution_condition":"A protocol for every number of parties \\( \\ell \\ge 2 \\), each holding \\( n \\) elements, that gives every party the bit [the intersection has at least \\( n - t \\) elements] (or shares of it), secure against a static semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) parties, over pairwise private point-to-point channels, with total communication at most \\( \\ell \\cdot t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits for every \\( n \\), and relying on no fully homomorphic or threshold fully homomorphic encryption.","status":"open","summary":"An \\( \\ell \\)-party protocol, without FHE, that tells all parties whether their intersection has at least \\( n - t \\) elements, with \\( \\ell t \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) communication over point-to-point channels. A published reduction turns it into multiparty threshold PSI of the same cost without FHE.","title":"Multiparty intersection cardinality test with optimal communication without FHE"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Is there an \\( \\ell \\)-party protocol, secure against a semi-honest adversary\ncorrupting up to \\( \\ell - 1 \\) parties and without fully homomorphic\nencryption, that tells every party whether the intersection of their sets of\n\\( n \\) elements has at least \\( n - t \\) elements, with total communication\n\\( \\ell t\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)\\cdot\\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) bits over\npoint-to-point channels?","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR builds this test only from threshold FHE (Theorem 5.1); its\ntest from threshold additively homomorphic encryption decides the stricter\nunion-based condition instead (Corollary 6.6). PSI-PAPER-2023-GS gives an\n\\( \\ell \\)-party test with secret-shared output and\n\\( O(\\epsilon^2\\ell t\\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\,t) \\) bits from oblivious transfer or\nadditively homomorphic encryption (Corollary 5), but in a model where a\nbroadcast message counts once (§2.1), and it does not state the cost over\npoint-to-point channels.","heading":"Why it is open"}],"slug":"op-10","status":"open","subtitle":"","summary":"Multiparty intersection cardinality test with optimal communication without FHE","title":"Multiparty intersection cardinality test with optimal communication without FHE","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-010"},{"evidence":"claim_audited","id":"PSI-OP-011","keywords":["malicious","contact-discovery","unbalanced","deployment","concrete-efficiency","signal"],"metadata":{"barriers":[],"basis":"derived","caveat":"The budget is Signal's requirement as reported by Kales et al. (§7, p. 14); reading the 2 s bound as covering a whole address book is ours, no network is stated, and timings date with hardware.","derived_from":{"gap":"Protocols secure against both parties communicate linearly in both sets; contact-discovery protocols measured at scale trust the server and still miss the 2 s bound.","goal":"PSI-GOAL-001","requirements":["deployable","malicious_both"]},"dossier_type":"open_problem","evidence":"claim_audited","id":"PSI-OP-011","keywords":["malicious","contact-discovery","unbalanced","deployment","concrete-efficiency","signal"],"named_open_by":[],"profile":{"Efficiency":"each lookup under 2 s end to end, not averaged; at most 1/1,600 server core-second per contact, precomputation included","Model":"random oracles allowed; one-time server-set processing and downloads not bounded","Security":"full simulation against a malicious client and a malicious server; constant-factor slack in a corrupt party's set size","Setting":"a phone client with an address book of up to 10,000 contacts; a server holding \\( 10^{9} \\) registered users"},"recognized_by":[{"locator":"§7, p. 14","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","statement":"Reports Signal's developers' requirements for deploying PSI-based contact discovery (\\( 10^{9} \\) users, address books of up to 10,000 contacts, lookups under 2 s, 1,600 contacts per second on one core) and states that its protocols, secure against malicious clients only, do not yet meet them."},{"locator":"§1.4, p. 3; §2.2, p. 4","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","statement":"Presents private contact discovery from a phone as the motivating application of unbalanced PSI, where earlier protocols send data proportional to both entire sets."},{"locator":"§7.2, p. 16","paper":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","statement":"Calls full simulation-based security against a malicious sender extremely challenging when communication must stay sublinear in the sender's set."}],"refuted_by":[],"related":[],"resolution_condition":"An implementation of a two-party PSI protocol with a proof of simulation-based security against a malicious client and against a malicious server (a corrupt party's effective set may exceed its declared size by a constant factor; random oracles allowed), together with a measurement in which a client on a smartphone checks 10,000 contacts against a server set of \\( 10^{9} \\) items in under 2 s end to end, and the server's computation per contact, including any per-client precomputation and amortized over clients, is at most 1/1,600 core-second. One-time processing of the server's set, one-time downloads, and the network are reported but not bounded.","resolved_by":[],"status":"open","summary":"Can PSI secure against a malicious client and a malicious server check 10,000 contacts against \\( 10^{9} \\) users in under 2 s, with one server core handling 1,600 contacts per second, as Signal's developers require? Protocols secure against both parties run only on balanced sets; those measured at this scale trust the server.","title":"Malicious PSI deployable for contact discovery"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"Messaging services find which of a user's contacts are registered by\ncomparing the phone's address book with the user base. Signal's developers\nconsidered PSI for this and, as Kales et al. report, stated what a deployable\nprotocol must handle: a server database of \\( 10^{9} \\) users, address books of up to\n10,000 contacts, lookups in under 2 s, and one server core handling 1,600\ncontacts per second (PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW, §7). Private contact discovery from\na phone is also the application that motivates unbalanced PSI\n(PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR). A server that runs the protocol can cheat as well as a\nclient, so the goal asks for security against both.","heading":"Background"},{"content":"Build and measure a two-party PSI protocol, simulation-secure against a\nmalicious client and against a malicious server (a corrupt party may use a\nconstant factor more items than it declared; random oracles allowed), in\nwhich a phone checks 10,000 contacts against \\( 10^9 \\) registered users in\nunder 2 s, while the server spends at most 1/1,600 core-second per contact.","heading":"Problem"},{"content":"$b","heading":"Why it is open"},{"content":"- Protocols secure only against a malicious client, or that trust the server\n  in any other way, however fast.\n- Measurements at a smaller user base or address book, or extrapolations.\n- Latency amortized over several lookups, or server throughput that leaves\n  out per-client precomputation.\n- Designs that add servers assumed not to collude, which change the setting.","heading":"What would not resolve it"}],"slug":"op-11","status":"open","subtitle":"","summary":"Malicious PSI deployable for contact discovery","title":"Malicious PSI deployable for contact discovery","type":"open_problem","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-OP-011"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH","keywords":["private-preference-matching","diffie-hellman","commutative-exponentiation","matching-count","no-trusted-third-party","two-party"],"metadata":{"authors":["Bernardo A. Huberman","Matt Franklin","Tad Hogg"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH","keywords":["private-preference-matching","diffie-hellman","commutative-exponentiation","matching-count","no-trusted-third-party","two-party"],"primary_url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/336992.337012","review_scope":"The 9-page ACM proceedings PDF (printed pages 78-86) was read in full. Locators cite the printed page numbers; PDF page \\( k \\) is printed page \\( 77 + k \\). Only the private preference matching protocol (§3, p. 81; Appendix A, p. 85) is within the PSI atlas. Community discovery (§3, pp. 80-81), deniable signatures (§4, pp. 81-82), and anonymous group membership (§5, pp. 82-83) are outside the topic and were read only to confirm that they contain no further matching protocol. The paper gives no security definition, adversary model, or proof for the matching protocol, so nothing of that kind could be checked.","status":"published","title":"Enhancing Privacy and Trust in Electronic Communities","venue":"ACM EC 1999","versions":["ACM Conference on Electronic Commerce (E-COMMERCE 99), Denver, Colorado, pp. 78-86, doi:10.1145/336992.337012; 9-page ACM PDF with printed page numbers 78-86, read 2026-10-11"],"year":1999},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1145/336992.337012","sections":[{"content":"Enhancing Privacy and Trust in Electronic Communities","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title, authors, venue, and pages were checked against the ACM PDF (first page, p. 78). The PDF carries the ACM copyright line \"E-COMMERCE 99, Denver, Colorado\" and printed pages 78-86. No other version was compared.","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING: a protocol, related by the authors to Diffie-Hellman key exchange and to encrypted key exchange, in which two parties exponentiate hashes of their list items with secret exponents and count the matches (Appendix A, p. 85). The protocol is the construction PSI-CONSTRUCTION-1999-HFH. Not recorded, outside the topic: community discovery by posting key pairs for yes/no answers (§3, p. 80; Appendix A, p. 85), deniable and convertible signatures (§4, pp. 81-82; Appendix A, p. 86), and 1-out-of-n and t-out-of-n proofs of knowledge for anonymous group membership (§5, pp. 82-83; Appendix A, p. 86).","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"$c","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"hfh99","status":"published","subtitle":"Bernardo A. Huberman, Matt Franklin, Tad Hogg · 1999","summary":"Enhancing Privacy and Trust in Electronic Communities","title":"Enhancing Privacy and Trust in Electronic Communities","type":"paper","venue":"ACM EC 1999","year":1999,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","keywords":["private-matching","oblivious-polynomial-evaluation","homomorphic-encryption","balanced-allocations","cut-and-choose","psi-cardinality","threshold","multiparty","lower-bound"],"metadata":{"authors":["Michael J. Freedman","Kobbi Nissim","Benny Pinkas"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-BALANCED-ALLOCATION-HASHING","PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE","PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD","PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-APPROX-LOWER-BOUND","PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE","PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OT-REDUCTION"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","keywords":["private-matching","oblivious-polynomial-evaluation","homomorphic-encryption","balanced-allocations","cut-and-choose","psi-cardinality","threshold","multiparty","lower-bound"],"primary_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","review_scope":"The Springer chapter of the EUROCRYPT 2004 version (LNCS 3027, pp. 1-19) was read in full: §§1-8 and the protocols PM-Semi-Honest (p. 6), PM-Malicious-Client (p. 10), PM-Malicious-Server (p. 12), Private-Sample-B (p. 14), and the multi-party protocol (p. 16). Locators cite the printed LNCS page numbers, which coincide with the PDF pages of the chapter. Security arguments in this version are lemma statements with proof sketches (pp. 8, 11-13); they were read but not re-verified. The fully malicious protocol is only sketched, with details deferred to the full version (§5.3, p. 13). The 2016 journal version was not compared.","status":"published","title":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","versions":["EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, pp. 1-19, doi:10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1; 19-page Springer chapter PDF, read 2026-10-11","Journal version with Carmit Hazay, Journal of Cryptology 29 (2016), doi:10.1007/s00145-014-9190-0 (not read)"],"year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title, authors, venue, and pages were checked against the chapter's first page (p. 1: \"C. Cachin and J. Camenisch (Eds.): EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, pp. 1-19, 2004\"). LITERATURE_COVERAGE.md records the 2016 Journal of Cryptology version with Hazay as a second version of this identity; it was not read, and no claim here depends on it. The paper calls set intersection \"private matching\" (PM), with the client learning \\( X \\cap Y \\) (§3.1, p. 3).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$d","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"Only the client learns the output (§3.1, p. 3). Security definitions are informal in this version (§3.2, p. 4), and the security lemmas carry proof sketches. The protocol against both malicious parties is described only in outline (§5.3, p. 13). The authors note that the number of rounds of Intersect-Approx depends on its output, which the security definitions do not capture (§6, \"Caveat\", p. 15). The paper leaves open more efficient fuzzy matching and malicious-secure fuzzy matching (§8, p. 17).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"fnp04","status":"published","subtitle":"Michael J. Freedman, Kobbi Nissim, Benny Pinkas · 2004","summary":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","title":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","type":"paper","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","keywords":["blind-rsa","authorized-psi","rsa-ppit","one-more-rsa","one-more-gap-dh","linear-complexity","data-transfer","semi-honest"],"metadata":{"authors":["Emiliano De Cristofaro","Gene Tsudik"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA","PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","keywords":["blind-rsa","authorized-psi","rsa-ppit","one-more-rsa","one-more-gap-dh","linear-complexity","data-transfer","semi-honest"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2009/491, PDF dated 2010-08-04, read in full: §§1-7, Figures 1-6, Tables 1-3, and Appendices A-C. The proof sketches of Appendix B were read but not re-verified. The FC 2010 proceedings version was not compared; the ePrint footnote on p. 1 says an earlier version appeared at FC 2010. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Computational and Bandwidth Complexity","venue":"FC 2010","versions":["IACR ePrint 2009/491, PDF dated 2010-08-04, 17 pages (read)","Financial Cryptography and Data Security (FC 2010), LNCS 6052, pp. 143-159, titled 'Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Complexity' (bibliographic data as cited in reference [17] of IACR ePrint 2016/930 and reference [DT10] of IACR ePrint 2021/1159; not checked against Springer)"],"year":2010},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","sections":[{"content":"Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Computational and Bandwidth Complexity","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF. The FC 2010 venue, volume, and pages are taken from the reference lists of PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ and PSI-PAPER-2021-RT, which give the shorter conference title. The paper uses \"client\" for the party that learns the intersection and \"server\" for the other (§2, p. 2).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"The paper lists its contributions in §3 (p. 2). PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA: PSI from RSA blind signatures with server precomputation, in which the client performs no online exponentiations (§5.6, Figure 4, p. 9; Appendix B.3, p. 16). PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI: authorized PSI with linear computation and communication, obtained from the quadratic APSI protocol derived from RSA-PPIT, and its PSI version in a prime-order group (§5.3-5.5, Figures 1-3, pp. 7-9; Appendices B.1-B.2, pp. 14-16). Not carded separately: adding data transfer (PSI-DT, APSI-DT) to all four protocols by deriving symmetric keys from the matching values, following the earlier PPIT work (§5.7, p. 10). Not carded separately: the asymptotic and experimental comparison with prior PSI and APSI protocols (§6, Tables 2-3, pp. 10-11); its figures are recorded in the construction rows.","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"All PSI protocols assume an honest-but-curious client and server; in APSI the server is honest-but-curious, the certification authority is not malicious, and the client may lack authorizations for some items (§5.1, p. 6). Malicious security is left to future work (§5.1, p. 6; §7, p. 12), as are formal proofs of unlinkability (Appendix B.1-B.3, pp. 14-16), a security proof for shorter exponents in the APSI protocol (§6, p. 11), and removing an extra oracle query from the One-More-Gap-DH reduction (Appendix B.2, p. 16). The APSI protocol has no comparison row: authorization by a certification authority has no facet in the topic vocabulary, and the APSI rows of Table 2 are not comparable with PSI rows.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"ct10","status":"published","subtitle":"Emiliano De Cristofaro, Gene Tsudik · 2010","summary":"Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Computational and Bandwidth Complexity","title":"Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Computational and Bandwidth Complexity","type":"paper","venue":"FC 2010","year":2010,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2010-DT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","keywords":["garbled-circuits","yao","sort-compare-shuffle","bitonic-merger","waksman-network","pairwise-comparison","bit-vector","auditing","semi-honest","two-party","benchmark"],"metadata":{"authors":["Yan Huang","David Evans","Jonathan Katz"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE","PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM","PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","keywords":["garbled-circuits","yao","sort-compare-shuffle","bitonic-merger","waksman-network","pairwise-comparison","bit-vector","auditing","semi-honest","two-party","benchmark"],"primary_url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","review_scope":"The 15-page NDSS 2012 paper (PDF dated 2011-11-28) was read in full: §§1-8, Tables 1-3, Figures 1-11, and Algorithms 1-3. The paper gives no security proofs and relies on the known proof of Yao's protocol (§1.2, p. 2; §2, p. 4), so no proof was checked. The PDF names no venue; NDSS 2012 is taken from the reference lists of IACR ePrint 2018/120 and 2019/241 and from the NDSS programme page. All locators cite this PDF's pages, which carry no printed page numbers.","status":"published","title":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","venue":"NDSS 2012","versions":["NDSS 2012 paper, 15-page PDF dated 2011-11-28","NDSS 2012 programme page at ndss-symposium.org (found by web search on 2026-10-11; the PDF hosted there was not compared)"],"year":2012},"primaryUrl":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","sections":[{"content":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the 15-page PDF dated 2011-11-28. The PDF itself does not name the venue. NDSS 2012 is the venue given in the reference lists of PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW (reference [HEK12]) and PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY (reference [HEK12]) and on the NDSS 2012 programme page. The authors' framework and protocol implementations are announced at www.MightBeEvil.com (p. 14).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE: the sort-compare-shuffle circuit, with a bitonic merger of the locally sorted sets, DupSelect-3 filtering, and shuffling by a Waksman network, so that the circuit has \\( \\Theta(n \\log n) \\) non-free gates (§5, pp. 5-10; Table 1, p. 2). PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM: the measured comparison of the garbled-circuit protocols with the one-more-DL-based protocol of De Cristofaro and Tsudik on the same hardware (§7, pp. 11-14; Figure 11 and Table 2, p. 13). PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING: an extension of the circuits that withholds the intersection when its size exceeds a threshold (§6, pp. 10-11). Not listed as separate contributions: the bit-vector protocol (Bitwise-AND, §3, pp. 4-5), the pairwise-comparison protocol with early reveal (§4, p. 5), and the shuffling by sorting or by homomorphic encryption (§§5.3.1-5.3.2, pp. 8-9). They are comparison rows PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-BWA, PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-PWC, and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2012-HEK-SCS-HE.","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"All protocols are semi-honest and two-party; stronger threat models are left to future work (§1.1, p. 2). The paper states that the protocols can be proven secure from decisional Diffie-Hellman, used for the oblivious transfer, in the random oracle model, without giving proofs (§2, p. 4). The bit-vector protocol does not bound the set sizes, so a dishonest party could input the whole universe; the paper says it should not be used standalone (§3, pp. 4-5). The introduction says the SCS-WN protocol on 160-bit elements is faster than the De Cristofaro-Tsudik protocol except at the ultra-short security level (§1.1, p. 2), while §7.3 calls it competitive at all security levels (p. 13); the plotted times (Figure 11, p. 13) show it about five times slower at the ultra-short level and faster at every other level.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"hek12","status":"published","subtitle":"Yan Huang, David Evans, Jonathan Katz · 2012","summary":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","title":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","type":"paper","venue":"NDSS 2012","year":2012,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","keywords":["batched-oprf","bark-oprf","ot-extension","pseudorandom-code","cuckoo-hashing","semi-honest","two-party"],"metadata":{"authors":["Vladimir Kolesnikov","Ranjit Kumaresan","Mike Rosulek","Ni Trieu"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","keywords":["batched-oprf","bark-oprf","ot-extension","pseudorandom-code","cuckoo-hashing","semi-honest","two-party"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/799","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2016/799, PDF dated 2016-08-20, read in full (§§1-6, Figures 1-3, Tables 1-5). The proof of Theorem 6 was read but not re-verified, and the CCS proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Efficient Batched Oblivious PRF with Applications to Private Set Intersection","venue":"ACM CCS 2016","versions":["IACR ePrint 2016/799, PDF dated 2016-08-20","ACM CCS 2016, pp. 818-829 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [43] of IACR ePrint 2016/930)"],"year":2016},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/799","sections":[{"content":"Efficient Batched Oblivious PRF with Applications to Private Set Intersection","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2016-08-20. Venue and pages are taken from the reference list of PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ (reference [43]) and were not checked against the ACM Digital Library. The authors' implementation is at github.com/osu-crypto/BaRK-OPRF (§6, p. 14).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF: the batched, related-key OPRF (BaRK-OPRF) from OT extension with a pseudorandom code. PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI: PSI in the hashing paradigm of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner with BaRK-OPRF in place of their OPRF, so that the OT cost per bin no longer grows with item length, and with the hash index appended to each bin input (§5, pp. 10-13; Figure 3, p. 13; Table 1, p. 14). The abstract reports 3.1-3.6 times faster PSI than that protocol for 128-bit items and sufficiently large sets, 3.8 seconds for sets of \\( 2^{20} \\) items, and a slowdown of 4.3 times relative to insecure naive hashing for very large sets (p. 1). The protocol itself is PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2016-KKRT.","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The paper is strictly in the semi-honest model (§1.1, p. 3). Theorem 6 (p. 9) covers BaRK-OPRF; the security of the PSI protocol is argued in prose (§5.2, p. 11). Footnote 3 (p. 12) notes that the earlier protocol did not account for collisions among the cuckoo hash functions, which the hash-index optimization of §5.2 (Figure 3, p. 13) handles.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"kkrt16","status":"published","subtitle":"Vladimir Kolesnikov, Ranjit Kumaresan, Mike Rosulek et al. · 2016","summary":"Efficient Batched Oblivious PRF with Applications to Private Set Intersection","title":"Efficient Batched Oblivious PRF with Applications to Private Set Intersection","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2016","year":2016,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","fv","seal","simd-batching","cuckoo-hashing","permutation-based-hashing","windowing","partitioning","modulus-switching","semi-honest","private-contact-discovery"],"metadata":{"authors":["Hao Chen","Kim Laine","Peter Rindal"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","fv","seal","simd-batching","cuckoo-hashing","permutation-based-hashing","windowing","partitioning","modulus-switching","semi-honest","private-contact-discovery"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/299","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2017/299, PDF dated 2017-09-06, read in full (§§1-7, Figures 1-4, Tables 1-5). The proofs of Theorems 1 and 2 were read but not re-verified; the hashing parameters of Table 1 and equation (1), the empirical cuckoo-hashing failure estimates of §4.2, and the SEAL parameter security estimates of Table 2 were not recomputed. The CCS proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Fast Private Set Intersection from Homomorphic Encryption","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","versions":["IACR ePrint 2017/299, PDF dated 2017-09-06 (PDF metadata)","ACM CCS 2017, pp. 1243-1255 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [12] of IACR ePrint 2018/787)"],"year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/299","sections":[{"content":"Fast Private Set Intersection from Homomorphic Encryption","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2017-09-06 (PDF metadata; the PDF itself carries no date). Venue and pages are taken from the reference list of PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR (reference [12]) and were not checked against the ACM Digital Library. The paper calls the protocol of Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner \"[PSZ16]\" and cites it as IACR ePrint 2016/930, which is PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ. The implementation uses SEAL v2.1 (§6.1, p. 13).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION: semi-honest PSI from leveled fully homomorphic encryption with communication linear in the smaller set and logarithmic in the larger set, made practical by batching, cuckoo and permutation-based hashing, windowing, partitioning, and modulus switching. The complete protocol of Figure 4 (p. 12) is PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-CLR. Not recorded separately: the basic protocol of Figure 1 (p. 5), which the paper calls a strawman with \\( O(N_x N_y) \\) homomorphic operations (§3, p. 6); the variant for a receiver holding the larger set, in which the parties switch roles and the result is returned masked (§5.2, p. 13); and the comparison with the protocols of Pinkas et al. and Kolesnikov et al. (§§6.2-6.3, pp. 14-17).","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"Security is proved only against semi-honest parties (Theorem 2, p. 11), with output to the receiver only (Figure 3, p. 11). The paper discusses but does not address malicious behavior: a malicious sender can influence the receiver's output, and a malicious receiver could add noise to its ciphertexts so that noise flooding no longer hides the sender's set (§5.2, pp. 12-13). Experiments use 32-bit items; longer items require larger FHE parameters, which the authors leave to future work (§6.1, p. 14). Cuckoo-hashing failure probabilities were determined empirically, without a stash (§4.2, pp. 7-8). Reusing the sender's pre-processing across executions is safe only against adversaries that do not force hashing failures (§6.1, p. 14).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"clr17","status":"published","subtitle":"Hao Chen, Kim Laine, Peter Rindal · 2017","summary":"Fast Private Set Intersection from Homomorphic Encryption","title":"Fast Private Set Intersection from Homomorphic Encryption","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","keywords":["multiparty-psi","opprf","programmable-prf","conditional-zero-sharing","augmented-semi-honest","cuckoo-hashing","garbled-bloom-filter","semi-honest","dishonest-majority"],"metadata":{"authors":["Vladimir Kolesnikov","Naor Matania","Benny Pinkas","Mike Rosulek","Ni Trieu"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-SYMMETRIC-KEY-MPSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","keywords":["multiparty-psi","opprf","programmable-prf","conditional-zero-sharing","augmented-semi-honest","cuckoo-hashing","garbled-bloom-filter","semi-honest","dishonest-majority"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2017/799, PDF dated 2017-08-24, read in full (§§1-7, Appendices A-B, Figures 1-12, Tables 1-5). The proofs of Theorem 1 (pp. 12-13) and Theorem 2 (proof sketch, pp. 14-15) were read but not re-verified; the security of the hashing-based OPPRF extension is argued informally (§4, p. 10). The CCS proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","versions":["IACR ePrint 2017/799, PDF dated 2017-08-24 (full version, as its first footnote states)","ACM CCS 2017, pp. 1257-1272 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [3] of IACR ePrint 2023/1777)"],"year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","sections":[{"content":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2017-08-24, whose first footnote calls it the full version of an ACM CCS 2017 paper (p. 1). The page range is taken from reference [3] of PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE and was not checked against the ACM Digital Library. The implementation is at github.com/osu-crypto/MultipartyPSI (§7, p. 17).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$e","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"All protocols are semi-honest or augmented semi-honest (§2.1, p. 4); no malicious security is claimed. Theorem 1 is stated for the protocol of Figure 8 with an ideal OPPRF functionality (p. 12). The OPPRF constructions are built on the KKRT OPRF, which has related keys and leaks slightly more than the PRF output; the paper works with cleaner definitions and states that the PSI protocol remains secure in the standard sense when built from the relaxed building blocks (§3.1, p. 5). Table 1 (p. 3) compares asymptotic costs with Kissner-Song, Cheon-Jarecki-Seo, and Hazay-Venkitasubramaniam, none of which is recorded yet.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"kmprt17","status":"published","subtitle":"Vladimir Kolesnikov, Naor Matania, Benny Pinkas et al. · 2017","summary":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","title":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","keywords":["dual-execution","malicious-security","oblivious-encoding","simple-hashing","phasing","encode-commit","ot-extension","uc-security"],"metadata":{"authors":["Peter Rindal","Mike Rosulek"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION","PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-ENCODE-COMMIT"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","keywords":["dual-execution","malicious-security","oblivious-encoding","simple-hashing","phasing","encode-commit","ot-extension","uc-security"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2017/769, full version dated August 9, 2017, read in full: §§1-7, Figures 1-9, and Appendices A-B. The proof of Theorem 2 was read but not re-verified; Theorem 1 is stated with a proof sketch and Theorem 3 with simulators only, as in the paper. The CCS proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Malicious-Secure Private Set Intersection via Dual Execution","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","versions":["IACR ePrint 2017/769, full version dated August 9, 2017 (PDF dated 2017-08-09), 27 pages (read)","ACM CCS 2017, pp. 1229-1242 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [RR17b] of IACR ePrint 2021/1159; not checked against the ACM Digital Library)"],"year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","sections":[{"content":"Malicious-Secure Private Set Intersection via Dual Execution","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF, which describes itself as the full version of the CCS 2017 paper (p. 1). Alice is the sender and Bob the receiver who learns the intersection (§2.1, p. 3). The implementations are in github.com/osu-crypto/libPSI (§7, p. 18).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$f","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"Both protocols give output only to Bob; §6.7 (pp. 17-18) explains why the natural symmetric extension to two-party output fails against a malicious Bob. The ideal functionality allows a corrupt party up to \\( n' > n \\) items, with \\( n' < 4n \\) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) in the dual-execution protocol (Figure 1, p. 4; §6.3, p. 15); the encode-commit protocol restricts Alice to exactly \\( n \\) items (§6.5, p. 17). Footnote 1 (p. 7) states that it is not known how to make OT of random secrets cheaper without the programmable random oracle model.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"rr17","status":"published","subtitle":"Peter Rindal, Mike Rosulek · 2017","summary":"Malicious-Secure Private Set Intersection via Dual Execution","title":"Malicious-Secure Private Set Intersection via Dual Execution","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","labeled-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","bfv","seal","oprf","diffie-hellman-oprf","omgdh","malicious-receiver","malicious-sender","keyword-pir","extension-field-simd","private-contact-discovery"],"metadata":{"authors":["Hao Chen","Zhicong Huang","Kim Laine","Peter Rindal"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LEAKY-MALICIOUS-SENDER"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","labeled-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","bfv","seal","oprf","diffie-hellman-oprf","omgdh","malicious-receiver","malicious-sender","keyword-pir","extension-field-simd","private-contact-discovery"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27, read in full (§§1-8, Appendix A, Figures 1-8, Table 1). The proofs of Theorems 1-3 were read but not re-verified; the heuristic argument and experiments for the H-limited property (§7.3) were not reproduced; FHE parameter choices behind Figure 4 are not listed in the paper and were not checked. The CCS proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Labeled PSI from Fully Homomorphic Encryption with Malicious Security","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","versions":["IACR ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27 (PDF metadata)","ACM CCS 2018, pp. 1223-1237, doi:10.1145/3243734.3243836 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [14] of IACR ePrint 2021/1116)"],"year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","sections":[{"content":"Labeled PSI from Fully Homomorphic Encryption with Malicious Security","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2018-08-27 (PDF metadata). Venue, pages, and DOI are taken from reference [14] of PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG and were not checked against the ACM Digital Library. The paper builds on PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR, which it cites as [12] and reviews in §2 (pp. 6-7). The implementation is built on SEAL v2.3.0-4 with BFV (§1.2, p. 2; §8, p. 18).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$10","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"Theorem 1 (p. 15) gives one-sided simulation security against a malicious receiver and Theorem 2 (p. 16) privacy, not simulation, against a malicious sender, both for the unlabeled PSI functionality of Figure 3 (p. 15); no theorem is stated for the labeled functionality. Theorem 3 (p. 25) relies on a leveled FHE scheme being H-limited (Definition 1, p. 17), which the authors argue heuristically and support by experiments, without a reduction (§7.3, p. 17). When the sender reuses its pre-processing across receivers and picks the hash functions, a selective cuckoo-hashing failure leaks one bit of the receiver's set (§7.3, p. 18). The authors call full simulation-based security against a malicious sender extremely challenging when communication must stay sublinear in the sender's set (§7.2, p. 16). The OT-based OPRF alternative of §4.4 (pp. 10-11) is discussed but not part of the full protocol.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"chlr18","status":"published","subtitle":"Hao Chen, Zhicong Huang, Kim Laine et al. · 2018","summary":"Labeled PSI from Fully Homomorphic Encryption with Malicious Security","title":"Labeled PSI from Fully Homomorphic Encryption with Malicious Security","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR"},{"evidence":"bibliographic_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-EKR","keywords":["secure-computation","monograph","custom-protocols","oprf","cuckoo-hashing"],"metadata":{"authors":["David Evans","Vladimir Kolesnikov","Mike Rosulek"],"contribution_ids":[],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"bibliographic_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-EKR","keywords":["secure-computation","monograph","custom-protocols","oprf","cuckoo-hashing"],"primary_url":"https://securecomputation.org/","review_scope":"Basis source for the PSI subfield division. In the authors' PDF dated 2022-06-11, §1.2 (generic versus specialized MPC), §3.8 \"Custom Protocols\" (printed p. 59), §3.8.1 \"Private Set Intersection (PSI)\" (printed pp. 60-63), and §3.9 \"Further Reading\" were read in full. Locators cite the printed page numbers of that PDF. The book makes no new PSI contribution; no contribution card is planned.","status":"published","title":"A Pragmatic Introduction to Secure Multi-Party Computation","venue":"NOW Publishers, 2018","versions":["NOW Publishers, 2018","Authors' PDF at securecomputation.org, version of 2022-06-11"],"year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://securecomputation.org/","sections":[{"content":"A Pragmatic Introduction to Secure Multi-Party Computation","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The PDF gives the citation as NOW Publishers, 2018, and identifies itself as the version of June 11, 2022. Locators cite its printed page numbers.","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"§3.8.1 (pp. 60-63) defines the goal of PSI for a group of parties (p. 60), contrasts PSI on generic MPC with custom protocols (§3.8, pp. 59-60; §3.8.1, p. 60), presents two-party PSI from an OPRF with 3-way cuckoo hashing (\"PSI from OPRF\", pp. 60-62) and the OPRF from 1-out-of-infinity OT (\"More efficient OPRF from 1-out-of-∞ OT\", pp. 62-63), and notes that the extension to more than two parties is not immediate (p. 63).","heading":"Source role"}],"slug":"ekr18","status":"published","subtitle":"David Evans, Vladimir Kolesnikov, Mike Rosulek · 2018","summary":"A Pragmatic Introduction to Secure Multi-Party Computation","title":"A Pragmatic Introduction to Secure Multi-Party Computation","type":"paper","venue":"NOW Publishers, 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2018-EKR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","keywords":["circuit-based-psi","cuckoo-hashing","two-dimensional-cuckoo-hashing","mirror-cuckoo-hashing","permutation-based-hashing","stash","psi-cat","gmw","aby","semi-honest"],"metadata":{"authors":["Benny Pinkas","Thomas Schneider","Christian Weinert","Udi Wieder"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-ALMOST-LINEAR-CIRCUIT","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-2D-CUCKOO-HASHING"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","keywords":["circuit-based-psi","cuckoo-hashing","two-dimensional-cuckoo-hashing","mirror-cuckoo-hashing","permutation-based-hashing","stash","psi-cat","gmw","aby","semi-honest"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/120","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2018/120, full version, PDF dated 2019-01-24, read in full: §§1-6, Appendices A-C, Algorithms 1-3, Figures 1-4, and Tables 1-6. The proof of Theorem 1 (Appendix A, pp. 29-33) was read but not re-verified; Appendix B is a heuristic explanation. The EUROCRYPT proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI via Cuckoo Hashing","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2018","versions":["IACR ePrint 2018/120, full version, PDF dated 2019-01-24 (ePrint lists it as a major revision of the EUROCRYPT 2018 paper)","EUROCRYPT 2018, LNCS Part III, pp. 125-157 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [PSWW18] of IACR ePrint 2019/175)"],"year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/120","sections":[{"content":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI via Cuckoo Hashing","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The ePrint PDF asks to be cited as the EUROCRYPT 2018 paper (footnote on p. 1). Its first page gives no proceedings pages; those are taken from the reference list of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS. The hashing simulation code and the PSI implementation in the ABY framework are announced in §6.1 (p. 19) and §6.3 (p. 24).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-ALMOST-LINEAR-CIRCUIT: mirror cuckoo hashing and the resulting circuit-based PSI with \\( \\omega(n) \\) comparisons and negligible failure probability, including the stash bound for non-constant stash size (§4, pp. 10-13; Appendix C, p. 34). PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-2D-CUCKOO-HASHING: two-dimensional cuckoo hashing, its \\( O(1/n) \\) failure analysis, and the concretely efficient PSI protocol with two-item bins and a combined stash (§5, pp. 13-18; §6, pp. 19-26; Appendix A, pp. 29-33). Not listed as separate contributions: the generalization of 2D cuckoo hashing to more parties with \\( 2^{m} \\) tables (§5.3, p. 18), the use of lookup-table evaluation to cut communication by up to 4.1 times (§6.2, p. 23), and the extension of PSI-CAT to privacy-preserving ridesharing (§6.3, p. 26).","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"Security is semi-honest only; the authors see no way to extend their hashing to malicious adversaries (§5.4, p. 18). The parameters of the concrete protocol rest on about 5.5 million core hours of simulations rather than on an analysis of two-item bins (§3.3, p. 10; §6.1, p. 20; Appendix A, p. 29). No security proof is given; security follows from the generic two-party computation protocol (§5.2, p. 16).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"psww18","status":"published","subtitle":"Benny Pinkas, Thomas Schneider, Christian Weinert et al. · 2018","summary":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI via Cuckoo Hashing","title":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI via Cuckoo Hashing","type":"paper","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","keywords":["ot-extension","oprf","cuckoo-hashing","permutation-based-hashing","bch-code","circuit-based-psi","naive-hashing","unequal-set-sizes","survey"],"metadata":{"authors":["Benny Pinkas","Thomas Schneider","Michael Zohner"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-BIT-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-OT-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-HASHING-PARAMETERS"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","keywords":["ot-extension","oprf","cuckoo-hashing","permutation-based-hashing","bch-code","circuit-based-psi","naive-hashing","unequal-set-sizes","survey"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/930","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2016/930, PDF dated 2019-07-28, read in full: §§1-6, Protocols 1-3, Tables 1-13, Figures 1-3, and the reference list. It is also a basis source for the PSI subfield division, through §1.2 \"Classification of PSI Protocols\" (pp. 3-5) and §6.2.3 \"PSI with Unequal Set Sizes\" (pp. 29-30). The security argument of §5 (p. 23) was read but not re-verified; the empirical hashing results of §3 were not reproduced. The journal citation is the one given in the paper's first footnote and reference [65]; the ACM TOPS version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Scalable Private Set Intersection Based on OT Extension","venue":"ACM Transactions on Privacy and Security 21(2)","versions":["ACM TOPS 21(2):7:1-7:35, January 2018, doi:10.1145/3154794","IACR ePrint 2016/930, PDF dated 2019-07-28"],"year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/930","sections":[{"content":"Scalable Private Set Intersection Based on OT Extension","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The paper asks to be cited as the ACM TOPS 2018 journal version and describes itself as a combined and extended version of the USENIX Security 2014 and 2015 papers (PSI-PAPER-2014-PSZ and PSI-PAPER-2015-PSSZ), with the additions listed in §1.4 and marked \"ADDED\" or \"EXTENDED\" in section headings (first footnote, p. 1; §1.4, pp. 6-7). Locators cite the ePrint PDF dated 2019-07-28.","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"§1.2 (pp. 3-5) discusses PSI protocols under the headings \"A naive solution\" (insecure hashing), \"Third Party-Based PSI\", \"Public-Key-Based PSI\", \"Circuit-Based PSI\", and \"OT-Based PSI\". §6.2.3 (pp. 29-30) runs the best protocol of each class on unequal set sizes. Three of the five PSI subfields of this atlas take their scope from the public-key-based, circuit-based, and OT-based classes.","heading":"Source role"},{"content":"$11","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"All protocols compared are semi-honest (§2.1, p. 8). The paper observes that existing two-party PSI protocols all seem to have communication linear in both set sizes (§6.2.3, p. 30) and that dependence on the larger set seems inevitable if brute-force searches are to be prevented (§1.1, p. 3). The text of §6.2.3 gives client sets of \\( 2^{8} \\) and \\( 2^{16} \\) items while Tables 11-13 use \\( 2^{8} \\) and \\( 2^{12} \\) (p. 29; pp. 30-31).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"psz18","status":"published","subtitle":"Benny Pinkas, Thomas Schneider, Michael Zohner · 2018","summary":"Scalable Private Set Intersection Based on OT Extension","title":"Scalable Private Set Intersection Based on OT Extension","type":"paper","venue":"ACM Transactions on Privacy and Security 21(2)","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","keywords":["threshold-psi","communication-complexity","lower-bound","set-reconciliation","sparse-polynomial","hankel-matrix","intersection-cardinality-testing","noisy-polynomial-addition","ole","fhe","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"metadata":{"authors":["Satrajit Ghosh","Mark Simkin"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-LOWER-BOUND","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-FHE-UPPER-BOUND","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","keywords":["threshold-psi","communication-complexity","lower-bound","set-reconciliation","sparse-polynomial","hankel-matrix","intersection-cardinality-testing","noisy-polynomial-addition","ole","fhe","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27, read in full: §§1-9, Theorems 1-5, Lemmas 1-4, and Figures 1-11. Proofs were read but not re-verified; the FHE upper bound and the garbled-circuit bound in §3 are sketches. The CRYPTO proceedings version was not compared; it lacks §8 (§1.3, p. 4). All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"The Communication Complexity of Threshold Private Set Intersection","venue":"CRYPTO 2019","versions":["IACR ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27 (last of seven revisions; adds the multiparty extension of §8 after the CRYPTO 2019 extended abstract, see §1.3)","CRYPTO 2019, LNCS, pp. 3-29 (as given in the paper's own reference [GS19])"],"year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","sections":[{"content":"The Communication Complexity of Threshold Private Set Intersection","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The ePrint page describes the PDF as a minor revision of the CRYPTO 2019 paper. The version read includes §8, multiparty threshold PSI, which §1.3 (p. 4) says was added after the CRYPTO extended abstract; footnote 7 (p. 18) thanks Badrinarayanan, Miao, and Rindal for pointing out that numerator and denominator polynomials may share roots in the multiparty cardinality test.","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-LOWER-BOUND: an \\( \\Omega(t) \\) communication lower bound for any two-party threshold PSI protocol (§3, pp. 7-8). PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-FHE-UPPER-BOUND: an \\( O(t \\log p) \\) protocol from fully homomorphic encryption and set reconciliation (§3, p. 8). PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI: the main protocol with communication \\( \\tilde O(t^{2}) \\) from additively homomorphic encryption and OLE, through private intersection cardinality testing and noisy polynomial addition (§§4-7, pp. 9-17). Not listed as separate contributions: the garbled-circuit sketch with communication at least \\( \\tilde O(t^{3}) \\) (§3, p. 9); the treatment of unequal set sizes by padding (§6.4, p. 15); and the multiparty cardinality test from threshold FHE with communication \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) and the multiparty threshold PSI from generic MPC (§8, pp. 17-19), which are sketches without theorems.","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"Security is against static passive adversaries in the UC framework (§2.2, p. 6). The authors present the results as feasibility results of theoretical interest and do not claim practicality (§1.1, p. 2). \\( \\tilde O \\) hides polylogarithmic factors (§2, p. 4); set elements lie in a field \\( \\mathbb{F}_p \\) with \\( p \\) a \\( \\Theta(\\kappa) \\)-bit prime (§2, p. 4).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"gs19","status":"published","subtitle":"Satrajit Ghosh, Mark Simkin · 2019","summary":"The Communication Complexity of Threshold Private Set Intersection","title":"The Communication Complexity of Threshold Private Set Intersection","type":"paper","venue":"CRYPTO 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2019-GS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","private-contact-discovery","precomputation","oprf","garbled-circuits","naor-reingold-prf","lowmc","cuckoo-filter","correlated-ot","ot-extension","malicious-client","android","armv8","signal"],"metadata":{"authors":["Daniel Kales","Christian Rechberger","Thomas Schneider","Matthias Senker","Christian Weinert"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-LOWMC-OPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","private-contact-discovery","precomputation","oprf","garbled-circuits","naor-reingold-prf","lowmc","cuckoo-filter","correlated-ot","ot-extension","malicious-client","android","armv8","signal"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2019/517, full version, PDF dated 2019-05-24, read in full (§§1-7, Appendices A-D, Figures 1-3, Tables 1-8). The paper gives no security proofs; security against malicious clients is argued in prose (§4.5). LowMC round counts (Table 2) and cuckoo-filter false-positive rates (§4.1) were not recomputed. The USENIX proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Mobile Private Contact Discovery at Scale","venue":"USENIX Security 2019","versions":["IACR ePrint 2019/517 (full version), PDF dated 2019-05-24 (PDF metadata)","USENIX Security 2019 (cited in the ePrint as reference [KRS+19], 'to appear', with author order Kales, Rechberger, Senker, Schneider, Weinert)"],"year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","sections":[{"content":"Mobile Private Contact Discovery at Scale","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint full version (PDF metadata dated 2019-05-24). The title page lists the authors as Kales, Rechberger, Schneider, Senker, Weinert; the paper's own reference to the USENIX Security 2019 version lists Kales, Rechberger, Senker, Schneider, Weinert. Both orders give the same initials. Venue details were not checked against the USENIX proceedings. The implementation is at contact-discovery.github.io (§5, footnote 6, p. 11).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$12","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The server is assumed semi-honest; a malicious server could learn about client items whenever the client reveals the intersection to it, as messaging apps do (§4.5, p. 11). Enumeration attacks by repeated queries remain possible and are left to rate limiting (§4.5, p. 11). The setup phase sends the client a filter linear in the server set, about 1 GiB for \\( 2^{28} \\) items, which the authors name as the bottleneck for very large sets (§4.6, p. 11). Cuckoo filters give a nonzero false-positive probability, about \\( 2^{-29} \\) per item in the experiments (§4.1, p. 6; Table 4, p. 13). The authors report Signal's stated requirements for deploying PSI-based contact discovery, a server database of \\( 10^{9} \\) users, address books of up to 10,000 contacts, lookups under 2 s, and 1,600 contacts per second on one core, and state that these are not yet met (§7, p. 14; the figures were rechecked on 2026-10-12 in the §7 of the USENIX Security 2019 version).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"krssw19","status":"published","subtitle":"Daniel Kales, Christian Rechberger, Thomas Schneider et al. · 2019","summary":"Mobile Private Contact Discovery at Scale","title":"Mobile Private Contact Discovery at Scale","type":"paper","venue":"USENIX Security 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","keywords":["circuit-based-psi","opprf","batched-opprf","programmable-prf","cuckoo-hashing","linear-communication","dual-execution","stash","payload","gmw","aby","semi-honest"],"metadata":{"authors":["Benny Pinkas","Thomas Schneider","Oleksandr Tkachenko","Avishay Yanai"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION","PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-PAYLOAD"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","keywords":["circuit-based-psi","opprf","batched-opprf","programmable-prf","cuckoo-hashing","linear-communication","dual-execution","stash","payload","gmw","aby","semi-honest"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2019/241, PDF dated 2019-02-28, read in full: §§1-7, Definitions 1-2, Constructions 2 and 4, Theorems 3, 5, and 8, Functionalities 1 and 6, Protocols 7, 9, and 10, and Tables 1-5. The proofs of Theorems 3, 5, and 8 were read but not re-verified. The ePrint revision of 2019-03-13 and the EUROCRYPT proceedings version were not compared. All locators cite the PDF pages of the 2019-02-28 version.","status":"published","title":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI with Linear Communication","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2019","versions":["IACR ePrint 2019/241, PDF dated 2019-02-28 (the ePrint page lists a revision of 2019-03-13, which was not compared)","EUROCRYPT 2019, LNCS 11478, pp. 122-153 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [50] of IACR ePrint 2019/723)"],"year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","sections":[{"content":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI with Linear Communication","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the PDF dated 2019-02-28. Venue and pages are taken from the reference list of PSI-PAPER-2020-ION and the ePrint page, which says the paper was published by the IACR in EUROCRYPT 2019. The implementation is at github.com/encryptogroup/OPPRF-PSI (footnote 8, p. 26).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF: the one-time programmable PRF with correlated targets and its batched version, whose single polynomial hint over all bins has size linear in the total number of programmed points (§3, pp. 10-16). PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION: circuit-based PSI with \\( O(n) \\) communication, through one comparison per bin and either a role-reversed phase for the stash or stash-less cuckoo hashing with three hash functions (§§4-5, pp. 16-21). PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-PAYLOAD: injection of the sender's payloads into the circuit through a second batched OPPRF (§6, pp. 21-22). Not listed as separate contributions: the mega-bin split that lowers the cost of polynomial interpolation (§4.3, p. 18; §7.2, pp. 23-24) and the PSI-CAT comparison with earlier public-key threshold protocols (§7.4, pp. 27-28).","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"Security is semi-honest (§2.2, p. 8). The protocol's security is stated to follow from the OPPRF and generic two-party computation (§4.1, p. 17); the OPPRF is proved in the F_OPRF-hybrid model (Theorem 8, p. 15). Only the stash-less protocol was implemented; the run times of the basic and dual-execution protocols are estimates (§7.4, p. 26; Table 5, p. 27).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"psty19","status":"published","subtitle":"Benny Pinkas, Thomas Schneider, Oleksandr Tkachenko et al. · 2019","summary":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI with Linear Communication","title":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI with Linear Communication","type":"paper","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","keywords":["intersection-sum","intersection-cardinality","diffie-hellman","additively-homomorphic-encryption","paillier","random-ot","bloom-filter","deployment","monetary-cost","ad-conversion","semi-honest"],"metadata":{"authors":["Mihaela Ion","Ben Kreuter","Ahmet Erhan Nergiz","Sarvar Patel","Shobhit Saxena","Karn Seth","Mariana Raykova","David Shanahan","Moti Yung"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-DDH-INTERSECTION-SUM","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","keywords":["intersection-sum","intersection-cardinality","diffie-hellman","additively-homomorphic-encryption","paillier","random-ot","bloom-filter","deployment","monetary-cost","ad-conversion","semi-honest"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2019/723, PDF dated 2020-09-05, read in full in §§1-6, Figures 1-4, Tables 1-7, and Appendices A-B; Appendices C-H (proofs of Theorems 1-4, the reverse variants, and further encryption comparisons) were skimmed, not verified. The EuroS&P proceedings version and the 2017 technical report were not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"On Deploying Secure Computing: Private Intersection-Sum-with-Cardinality","venue":"IEEE EuroS&P 2020","versions":["IACR ePrint 2019/723, PDF dated 2020-09-05 (ePrint page: received 2019-06-18, revised 2020-09-07, noted as published in Euro S&P 2020)","An earlier technical report by most of the same authors is cited by later papers as IACR ePrint 2017/738; it was not read"],"year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","sections":[{"content":"On Deploying Secure Computing: Private Intersection-Sum-with-Cardinality","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors follow the PDF dated 2020-09-05; the ePrint page lists the same nine authors with Mariana Raykova placed after Sarvar Patel. The venue is taken from the ePrint page. PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW and PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY cite an earlier version of the deployed protocol as the 2017 ePrint report \"Private intersection-sum protocol with applications to attributing aggregate ad conversions\" (reference [IKN+17] in both).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-DDH-INTERSECTION-SUM: the deployed private intersection-sum-with-cardinality protocol from Diffie-Hellman double masking and additively homomorphic encryption (§3, pp. 5-7; Figure 2, p. 6). PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM: the protocol from batched random OT with a shuffle and re-masking of the OPRF outputs (§4.3.1, pp. 9-10; Figure 3, p. 10). Not listed as separate contributions: the \"Tag, Shuffle, Aggregate\" recipe for turning a PSI protocol into an intersection-sum-with-cardinality protocol (§4.2, pp. 8-9); the encrypted-Bloom-filter protocol (§4.3.2, pp. 10-11; Figure 4), which is the comparison row PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-BF; the monetary cost model for batch computation (§2.5, p. 4; Table 1, p. 5); and the reverse variants in which the identifier-only party receives the sum (§3.4, p. 7; Appendix G).","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"All protocols are semi-honest (§1, p. 2; §2.3, p. 4) and reveal the intersection size besides the sum, which the authors note may be undesirable leakage in other settings (§4.2, p. 9). The comparison with garbled-circuit protocols mixes measurement settings, as the authors state (§5.3, p. 12).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"ikn20","status":"published","subtitle":"Mihaela Ion, Ben Kreuter, Ahmet Erhan Nergiz et al. · 2020","summary":"On Deploying Secure Computing: Private Intersection-Sum-with-Cardinality","title":"On Deploying Secure Computing: Private Intersection-Sum-with-Cardinality","type":"paper","venue":"IEEE EuroS&P 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2020-ION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","keywords":["paxos","probe-and-xor-of-strings","garbled-cuckoo-table","cuckoo-graph","oos-ot-extension","malicious","linear-communication","two-party"],"metadata":{"authors":["Benny Pinkas","Mike Rosulek","Ni Trieu","Avishay Yanai"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-PAXOS","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","keywords":["paxos","probe-and-xor-of-strings","garbled-cuckoo-table","cuckoo-graph","oos-ot-extension","malicious","linear-communication","two-party"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2020/193, PDF dated 2020-02-16 (36 pages), read in full: §§1-7, Figures 1-7, Tables 1-2, and Appendices A-D. The proofs of Lemmas 5-6, Theorem 8, Lemma 10, Theorem 11, and Lemma 12 were read but not re-verified. The EUROCRYPT proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages, which equal the printed page numbers.","status":"published","title":"PSI from PaXoS: Fast, Malicious Private Set Intersection","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2020","versions":["IACR ePrint 2020/193, PDF dated 2020-02-16","EUROCRYPT 2020, Part II, LNCS 12106, pp. 739-767 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [PRTY20] of IACR ePrint 2021/883)"],"year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","sections":[{"content":"PSI from PaXoS: Fast, Malicious Private Set Intersection","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2020-02-16. Venue, volume, and pages are taken from the reference list of PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY and were not checked against the publisher. The authors point to their source code under github.com/cryptobiu; the repository name is split across a page break in the PDF (§1.1, pp. 2-3).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-PAXOS: the probe-and-XOR of strings, an input-independent binary key-value encoding with XOR-homomorphic decoding (Definition 3, p. 6; §3, pp. 5-9). PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO: the garbled cuckoo table, a PaXoS of constant rate with linear-time encoding (§5, pp. 16-21), and its DFS-based variant (Appendix B, pp. 29-31). PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI: two-party PSI from any PaXoS and the OOS OT extension, malicious or semi-honest by the choice of code (Figure 3, p. 11; Theorem 4, p. 10). PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND: the incompressibility bound on a malicious receiver's effective set (Claim 7, p. 15; Appendix A, pp. 27-29). Not separately recorded: the bound on the number of cycles in a cuckoo graph (Lemma 12, Appendix C, pp. 31-32), which is part of the garbled cuckoo table's analysis, and the qualitative comparison of PSI paradigms (Appendix D, pp. 32-36). The complete protocols are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-SH and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-PRTY-MAL.","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The adversary corrupts one of two parties statically (§2, p. 5), and only the receiver learns the intersection (Figure 1, p. 6). The ideal functionality lets a corrupt party use a set larger than advertised (§2, p. 5). Theorem 4 (p. 10) is proved in the random oracle model with OOS as an ideal functionality; Appendix D (p. 33) states standard-model security for the semi-honest case without a separate proof. The introduction's claim that the semi-honest instantiation improves on KKRT's communication by 25% (§1.1, p. 2) does not match Table 1 (p. 21) or §7.3 (p. 23), which put PaXoS-PSI above KKRT; this was not resolved. The protocol targets sets of roughly equal size (Appendix D, p. 36).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"prty20","status":"published","subtitle":"Benny Pinkas, Mike Rosulek, Ni Trieu et al. · 2020","summary":"PSI from PaXoS: Fast, Malicious Private Set Intersection","title":"PSI from PaXoS: Fast, Malicious Private Set Intersection","type":"paper","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty-psi","communication-complexity","lower-bound","threshold-fhe","threshold-additive-homomorphic-encryption","hankel-matrix","half-gcd","cardinality-testing","star-network"],"metadata":{"authors":["Saikrishna Badrinarayanan","Peihan Miao","Srinivasan Raghuraman","Peter Rindal"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-LOWER-BOUND","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty-psi","communication-complexity","lower-bound","threshold-fhe","threshold-additive-homomorphic-encryption","hankel-matrix","half-gcd","cardinality-testing","star-network"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25, read in full: §§1-7 and Appendices A-C, Theorems 4.1, 4.3, 5.1, 5.4, 6.5, 7.1, B.1, and C.1, Corollaries 4.2, 4.4, 6.6, 7.2, and B.2, and Figures 1-10. Proofs were read but not re-verified; Imported Theorems 2.2 and 6.1-6.4 were not checked against their sources. The PKC 2021 proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Multi-Party Threshold Private Set Intersection with Sublinear Communication","venue":"PKC 2021","versions":["IACR ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25 (ePrint page: received 2020-05-22, last of two revisions 2021-03-01, noted as a minor revision of the PKC 2021 paper; the PDF date precedes the last listed revision and the two were not compared)"],"year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"Multi-Party Threshold Private Set Intersection with Sublinear Communication","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the PDF dated 2021-02-25; the venue is taken from the ePrint page. The paper treats the multiparty extension in the full version of PSI-PAPER-2019-GS as concurrent work and reports that it initially overlooked an unexpected degree reduction, fixed after notification (§1.3, p. 5; footnote 6, p. 11).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$13","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"$14","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"bmrr21","status":"published","subtitle":"Saikrishna Badrinarayanan, Peihan Miao, Srinivasan Raghuraman et al. · 2021","summary":"Multi-Party Threshold Private Set Intersection with Sublinear Communication","title":"Multi-Party Threshold Private Set Intersection with Sublinear Communication","type":"paper","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","labeled-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","bfv","bgv","seal","helib","apsi","paterson-stockmeyer","postage-stamp-bases","frobenius-automorphism","oprf","fourq","omgdh"],"metadata":{"authors":["Kelong Cong","Radames Cruz Moreno","Mariana Botelho da Gama","Wei Dai","Ilia Iliashenko","Kim Laine","Michael Rosenberg"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS-POWERS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","labeled-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","bfv","bgv","seal","helib","apsi","paterson-stockmeyer","postage-stamp-bases","frobenius-automorphism","oprf","fourq","omgdh"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2021/1116, PDF dated 2021-11-14, read in full (§§1-6, Appendices A-D, Figures 1-2, Tables 1-8). The proofs of Lemmas 1 and 2 (Appendices B and C) were read but not re-verified; the security argument is a reference to the earlier paper's Theorem 1 plus a sketch for the labeled case (§3, p. 6; §4.2, p. 14). FHE parameter choices were not rechecked with the LWE estimator. The CCS proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Labeled PSI from Homomorphic Encryption with Reduced Computation and Communication","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","versions":["IACR ePrint 2021/1116, PDF dated 2021-11-14 (PDF metadata)","ACM CCS 2021 (the ePrint PDF names no venue; not checked against the proceedings)"],"year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","sections":[{"content":"Labeled PSI from Homomorphic Encryption with Reduced Computation and Communication","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2021-11-14 (PDF metadata). The PDF names no venue; ACM CCS 2021 was not checked against the proceedings. The paper refines PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR and PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR, cited as [15] and [14] (§1.1, p. 3; §1.2, p. 3). The SEAL-based implementation is the open-source APSI library (§5, footnote 7, p. 15); the HElib implementation is a proof of concept without OPRF or networking (§5, p. 15).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$15","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The paper states that its security guarantees and proof are those of the earlier labeled-PSI paper: security against a malicious receiver and privacy against a malicious sender in the random oracle model (§3, p. 6); the labeled case is argued as a straightforward extension (§4.2, p. 14). The low-communication variant has impractically high computation on current CPUs (abstract, p. 1; §6, p. 20). Experiments use a false-positive probability of at most \\( 2^{-40} \\) per execution (§5.1, p. 16). Comparisons with the earlier protocol use a 24-core machine that the authors assume comparable to the earlier 32-core one (§5, p. 14).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"cmg21","status":"published","subtitle":"Kelong Cong, Radames Cruz Moreno, Mariana Botelho da Gama et al. · 2021","summary":"Labeled PSI from Homomorphic Encryption with Reduced Computation and Communication","title":"Labeled PSI from Homomorphic Encryption with Reduced Computation and Communication","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","keywords":["okvs","oblivious-key-value-store","garbled-cuckoo-table","hypergraph-peeling","correctness-amplification","paxos-psi","vector-ole","multiparty-psi","malicious"],"metadata":{"authors":["Gayathri Garimella","Benny Pinkas","Mike Rosulek","Ni Trieu","Avishay Yanai"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-LINEAR-OKVS-VOLE","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-MALICIOUS-MULTIPARTY"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","keywords":["okvs","oblivious-key-value-store","garbled-cuckoo-table","hypergraph-peeling","correctness-amplification","paxos-psi","vector-ole","multiparty-psi","malicious"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2021/883, PDF dated 2021-11-30 (39 pages), read in full: §§1-8, Figures 1-5, Tables 1-4, and Appendices A-B. The proofs of Lemma 6 (sketched in Appendix A.1) and Theorem 9 were read but not re-verified; Theorem 7 is stated without a written proof. The CRYPTO proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages, which equal the printed page numbers.","status":"published","title":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","versions":["IACR ePrint 2021/883, PDF dated 2021-11-30","CRYPTO 2021, Part II, LNCS 12826, pp. 395-425 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [GPR+21] of IACR ePrint 2022/320)"],"year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title, authors, and date were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2021-11-30. Venue, volume, and pages are taken from the reference list of PSI-PAPER-2022-RR and were not checked against the publisher.","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$16","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The two-party protocols are PaXoS-PSI of PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY with a different binary OKVS (§6.3, p. 19) or with vector OLE (§7.1, pp. 20-22); the paper does not re-prove the former. The parameter \\( 1.3n \\) of the three-hash table is a heuristic choice (§8.1, p. 23), and the failure probability of the star instantiation rests on an empirical measurement (§5.4, pp. 17-18). A later analysis in PSI-PAPER-2022-RR (§5.1, p. 24) argues that this measurement overstates the security level by a few bits; that claim was not checked here. The vector-OLE protocol and the multiparty protocol are not implemented.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"gprty21","status":"published","subtitle":"Gayathri Garimella, Benny Pinkas, Mike Rosulek et al. · 2021","summary":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","title":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","type":"paper","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","keywords":["multiparty-psi","malicious","okvs","paxos","opprf","zeroxor","server-aided-psi","corruption-threshold","client-server"],"metadata":{"authors":["Ofri Nevo","Ni Trieu","Avishay Yanai"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-NO-COLLUSION","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-THRESHOLD-CALIBRATED"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","keywords":["multiparty-psi","malicious","okvs","paxos","opprf","zeroxor","server-aided-psi","corruption-threshold","client-server"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1221","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2021/1221, PDF dated 2021-09-17, read in full (§§1-5, Appendices A-D, Protocols 3.1-4.4, Tables 1-5). Proofs of Theorems 3.2 and 3.4 were read but not re-verified; Theorem 4.5 has a proof sketch and the proof of Theorem 4.3 is omitted in the paper (p. 10). The CCS proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Simple, Fast Malicious Multiparty Private Set Intersection","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","versions":["IACR ePrint 2021/1221, PDF dated 2021-09-17","ACM CCS 2021, pp. 1151-1165 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [32] of IACR ePrint 2023/1777)"],"year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1221","sections":[{"content":"Simple, Fast Malicious Multiparty Private Set Intersection","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2021-09-17, whose acknowledgments thank the CCS 2021 reviewers (p. 12). The page range is taken from reference [32] of PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE and was not checked against the ACM Digital Library. The implementation is at github.com/asu-crypto/mPSI (§5, p. 11).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-NO-COLLUSION: malicious multiparty PSI when at most one party is corrupted, from PRF keys, OKVS encodings, and a two-party PSI between two designated parties instantiated as server-aided PSI (§3, pp. 5-9; Protocols 3.1 and 3.3; Theorems 3.2 and 3.4). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-THRESHOLD-CALIBRATED: malicious multiparty PSI against \\( t < n \\) colluding parties with clients, a pivot, and \\( t \\) servers, whose cost decreases with \\( t \\) (§4, pp. 9-11; Protocol 4.4; Theorem 4.5). Not yet recorded separately: the ZeroXOR functionality and protocol (Functionality 4.1, Protocol 4.2, Theorem 4.3, pp. 9-10), which the second contribution uses; the observation that replacing the OKVS by an OPPRF in Protocol 3.1 gives a three-party protocol against two colluding parties (§3.1.3, p. 6).","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"Both main protocols are proved against a malicious adversary in the random oracle model with ideal subfunctionalities (Theorems 3.2, 3.4, 4.5; pp. 7-11), and the ideal functionality gives the output to \\( P_n \\) only (Functionality 2.1, p. 4). The implementation uses the table-based OPPRF code of PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT, which footnote 2 calls semi-honest secure and about three times slower than the malicious PaXoS-based OPPRF (p. 11). Runtimes of the protocol of Ben Efraim et al. are copied from that paper rather than rerun (§5.1, p. 11). Table 1 lists five rounds for Protocol 3.3 while §3.2.2 (p. 8) counts four.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"nty21","status":"published","subtitle":"Ofri Nevo, Ni Trieu, Avishay Yanai · 2021","summary":"Simple, Fast Malicious Multiparty Private Set Intersection","title":"Simple, Fast Malicious Multiparty Private Set Intersection","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","keywords":["vole-psi","vector-ole","batched-oprf","paxos","xopaxos","opprf","circuit-psi","malicious","lpn"],"metadata":{"authors":["Peter Rindal","Phillipp Schoppmann"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","keywords":["vole-psi","vector-ole","batched-oprf","paxos","xopaxos","opprf","circuit-psi","malicious","lpn"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2021/266, PDF dated 2024-08-08 (39 pages), read in full: §§1-8, Figures 1-11, and Tables 1-4. The proofs of Theorems 3.1, 4.1, and 5.1 were read but not re-verified. Earlier ePrint versions and the EUROCRYPT proceedings version were not compared; the revision says that the prior version did not correctly address extracting a malicious receiver's effective input (§2, p. 13), so statements about malicious extraction cite the 2024 revision only. All locators cite this PDF's pages, which equal the printed page numbers.","status":"published","title":"VOLE-PSI: Fast OPRF and Circuit-PSI from Vector-OLE","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2021","versions":["IACR ePrint 2021/266, PDF dated 2024-08-08 (revised version)","EUROCRYPT 2021, Part II, LNCS 12697, pp. 901-930 (bibliographic data as cited in reference [RS21] of IACR ePrint 2022/320)"],"year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","sections":[{"content":"VOLE-PSI: Fast OPRF and Circuit-PSI from Vector-OLE","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and date were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2024-08-08. The title page spells the second author \"Phillipp Schoppman\"; the spelling \"Schoppmann\" follows the paper's own reference [SGRR19] and the citations in PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY and PSI-PAPER-2022-RR. Venue, volume, and pages are taken from the reference list of PSI-PAPER-2022-RR and were not checked against the publisher. The revision adds the Strong OKVS Extraction Conjecture and its discussion (§2, pp. 11-13).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF: a batched OPRF from vector OLE and a PaXoS encoding, malicious-secure (§3, pp. 13-18; Figure 4, p. 36). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS: a tighter analysis of the malicious OPRF-to-PSI transformation that allows κ-bit outputs (§4, pp. 18-20). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI: circuit-PSI from an OPPRF built on the VOLE OPRF and XoPaXoS (§2, pp. 10-11; §§5-6, pp. 21-24). Not separately recorded: the variant with a polynomial-interpolation encoding of rate 1, analyzed only in theory (§7.1, pp. 24-25; Table 1, p. 25), and the Strong OKVS Extraction Conjecture (Conjecture 2.1, p. 12), which the authors leave open. The complete protocols are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-SH, PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-MAL, and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-CIRCUIT.","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The OPRF and PSI are proved in the random oracle and F_vole-hybrid model; the implementation instantiates VOLE from LPN with regular noise (§3.1, p. 14; §7.2, p. 26). Circuit-PSI is semi-honest, and its proof is deferred to Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai (§6, p. 23). Table 1 (p. 25) prints the malicious communication formula with a \\( (\\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y)) n_y \\) term while its numeric column uses \\( 128 n_y \\); the numeric column and the measurements of Table 3 (p. 29) are recorded.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"rs21","status":"published","subtitle":"Peter Rindal, Phillipp Schoppmann · 2021","summary":"VOLE-PSI: Fast OPRF and Circuit-PSI from Vector-OLE","title":"VOLE-PSI: Fast OPRF and Circuit-PSI from Vector-OLE","type":"paper","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2021-RS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","keywords":["key-agreement","diffie-hellman","polynomial-encoding","ideal-permutation","elligator","oracle-diffie-hellman","malicious","small-sets","communication"],"metadata":{"authors":["Mike Rosulek","Ni Trieu"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-KA-TO-MALICIOUS-PSI","PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","keywords":["key-agreement","diffie-hellman","polynomial-encoding","ideal-permutation","elligator","oracle-diffie-hellman","malicious","small-sets","communication"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2021/1159, author version of October 17, 2021, read in full: §§1-5, Figures 1-6, Tables 1-3, and Appendices A-C. The proofs of Lemmas 5-7 and 11-13 and the compression argument of Lemma 16 were read but not re-verified. The ACM CCS 2021 version was not compared; §1.3 (p. 6) says the October 2021 revision repaired a bug in the semi-honest proof and added a second semi-honest variant. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Compact and Malicious Private Set Intersection for Small Sets","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","versions":["IACR ePrint 2021/1159, author version dated October 17, 2021 (PDF dated 2021-10-17), 29 pages (read)","ACM CCS 2021, doi:10.1145/3460120.3484778 (as stated on p. 1 of the author version; not checked against the ACM Digital Library)"],"year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","sections":[{"content":"Compact and Malicious Private Set Intersection for Small Sets","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title, authors, and the CCS DOI were checked against the author version (p. 1). The protocol has a sender and a receiver; only the receiver learns \\( X \\cap Y \\) (Figure 2, p. 6). The implementation is at github.com/osu-crypto/MiniPSI (§5.1, p. 16).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-KA-TO-MALICIOUS-PSI: a transformation from two-round key agreement with pseudorandom second messages and non-malleability to malicious PSI, by encoding the receiver's key-agreement responses in a polynomial, in the ideal permutation and random oracle model (§4, Figure 4, pp. 10-16). PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION: two semi-honest variants, instantiated with Diffie-Hellman, that lower the communication of Diffie-Hellman PSI (Appendix A, Figures 5-6, pp. 23-28; Table 1, p. 4). Not carded separately: the unconditional bound on how many items a corrupt receiver can effectively input (§4.1, pp. 15-16; Appendix C, pp. 28-29); it is recorded in the qualifiers of the malicious contribution. Not carded separately: the optimizations of Appendix B (sender without elligator encoding; OKVS instead of polynomials), whose security the paper leaves to the reader (p. 28).","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The paper compares protocols on small sets and records that OT-based protocols overtake it between 500 and 1,000 items on a 10 Gbps network (§1, p. 2). It conjectures, without proof, that overfitting polynomials are hard to find even when they exist, which would tighten the enforcement of a corrupt receiver's set size (§4.1, p. 16). It describes De Cristofaro-Tsudik 2010 and its improvement as RSA-accumulator protocols (§1.1, p. 3); PSI-PAPER-2010-DT's own protocols use blind RSA signatures and RSA-PPIT, not accumulators.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"rt21","status":"published","subtitle":"Mike Rosulek, Ni Trieu · 2021","summary":"Compact and Malicious Private Set Intersection for Small Sets","title":"Compact and Malicious Private Set Intersection for Small Sets","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2021-RT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","keywords":["okvs","triangulation","gap","phase-transition","subfield-vole","vole-psi","circuit-psi","clustering","malicious"],"metadata":{"authors":["Srinivasan Raghuraman","Peter Rindal"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS","PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-SUBFIELD-VOLE"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","keywords":["okvs","triangulation","gap","phase-transition","subfield-vole","vole-psi","circuit-psi","clustering","malicious"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2022/320, PDF dated 2022-11-02 (35 pages), read in full: §§1-5, Figures 1-12, Tables 1-2, and Appendices A-C. The proofs of Lemmas 1-10 and Theorem 3 were read but not re-verified, and the empirical parameter fits of §3 (Figures 2-8, Equations (1)-(3)) were not reproduced. The criticism of the failure-probability estimate of PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY (§5.1, p. 24) is recorded as the authors' claim. The ACM CCS proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages, which equal the printed page numbers.","status":"published","title":"Blazing Fast PSI from Improved OKVS and Subfield VOLE","venue":"ACM CCS 2022","versions":["IACR ePrint 2022/320, PDF dated 2022-11-02","ACM CCS 2022 (venue as listed in LITERATURE_COVERAGE.md; the ePrint PDF names no venue, and proceedings pages were not checked)"],"year":2022},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","sections":[{"content":"Blazing Fast PSI from Improved OKVS and Subfield VOLE","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2022-11-02, whose header gives only the year 2022. The venue follows LITERATURE_COVERAGE.md and was not checked against the ACM Digital Library. The implementation is at github.com/Visa-Research/volepsi (§5, p. 23).","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS: the OKVS by sparse triangulation with a small gap, its clustering, and its failure-probability analysis and parameter selection (§§2-3, pp. 5-21; Figure 1, p. 7; Appendix A, pp. 30-31). PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-SUBFIELD-VOLE: semi-honest VOLE-based PSI with subfield VOLE and communication \\( O(n\\lambda + n \\log n) \\) (§4, pp. 21-23; Figure 9, p. 23; Theorem 3, pp. 33-35). Not separately recorded: the application of the new OKVS, in its doubly oblivious form, to the circuit-PSI of PSI-PAPER-2021-RS (§4, p. 23; Table 2, p. 25), which appears as a construction row, and the argument that the greedy triangulation is not a matroid algorithm for \\( w > 2 \\) (Appendix B, pp. 32-33). The complete protocols are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-FAST, PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-SD, PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-MAL, and PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-CIRCUIT.","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The malicious and circuit-PSI protocols are those of PSI-PAPER-2021-RS with the new OKVS; the paper proves only the semi-honest subfield-VOLE protocol (Theorem 3, p. 33). Concrete failure probabilities rest on empirical fits (§3.1-3.2, pp. 13-19). The paper's improvement factors differ by passage: the abstract reports \\( 187n \\) bits and a 6× circuit-PSI speedup, §1 a 6.8× and 2.3× reduction, §1.1 \\( 180n \\) bits and 1.5× and 23× improvements; Table 2 (p. 25) is recorded. All measurements were taken on one laptop over a local network of about 1 Gbps (§5, p. 23).","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"rr22","status":"published","subtitle":"Srinivasan Raghuraman, Peter Rindal · 2022","summary":"Blazing Fast PSI from Improved OKVS and Subfield VOLE","title":"Blazing Fast PSI from Improved OKVS and Subfield VOLE","type":"paper","venue":"ACM CCS 2022","year":2022,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2022-RR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty-psi","intersection-cardinality-testing","communication-complexity","divide-and-conquer","secret-sharing","oblivious-transfer","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"metadata":{"authors":["Satrajit Ghosh","Mark Simkin"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER","PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY-QUASILINEAR"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty-psi","intersection-cardinality-testing","communication-complexity","divide-and-conquer","secret-sharing","oblivious-transfer","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/919","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2023/919, PDF dated 2023-06-12, read in full: §§1-4, Lemmas 1-5, Theorems 1-8, Corollaries 1-6, and Figures 1-12. Proofs were read but not re-verified. The PDF itself names no venue; PKC 2023 is taken from the ePrint page. The PKC proceedings version was not compared. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"Threshold Private Set Intersection with Better Communication Complexity","venue":"PKC 2023","versions":["IACR ePrint 2023/919, PDF dated 2023-06-12, 18 pages (the ePrint page, received 2023-06-12, calls it a minor revision of an IACR publication in PKC 2023)"],"year":2023},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/919","sections":[{"content":"Threshold Private Set Intersection with Better Communication Complexity","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title, authors, and affiliations (Indian Institute of Technology Kharagpur; Ethereum Foundation) were checked against the PDF dated 2023-06-12. The venue is taken from the ePrint page, which describes the PDF as a minor revision of the PKC 2023 paper.","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$17","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"$18","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"gs23","status":"published","subtitle":"Satrajit Ghosh, Mark Simkin · 2023","summary":"Threshold Private Set Intersection with Better Communication Complexity","title":"Threshold Private Set Intersection with Better Communication Complexity","type":"paper","venue":"PKC 2023","year":2023,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2023-GS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE","keywords":["sok","multiparty-psi","collusion-resistance","semi-honest","augmented-semi-honest","set-representation","network-topology","operation-count"],"metadata":{"authors":["Jelle Vos","Mauro Conti","Zekeriya Erkin"],"contribution_ids":["PSI-CONTRIB-2024-VCE-ANALYTICAL-COST-COMPARISON"],"dossier_type":"paper","evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE","keywords":["sok","multiparty-psi","collusion-resistance","semi-honest","augmented-semi-honest","set-representation","network-topology","operation-count"],"primary_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1777","review_scope":"IACR ePrint 2023/1777, PDF dated 2023-11-16, read in full (§§1-9, Tables 1-3, Appendices A-C, where Appendix C is the meta-review). The derivations of Appendices A-B were followed but not recomputed. The complexities and protocol descriptions it reports for other papers were not checked against those papers. All locators cite the ePrint PDF pages.","status":"published","title":"SoK: Collusion-resistant Multi-party Private Set Intersections in the Semi-honest Model","venue":"IEEE S&P 2024","versions":["IACR ePrint 2023/1777, PDF dated 2023-11-16","IEEE S&P 2024 (venue as recorded in LITERATURE_COVERAGE.md; the ePrint PDF does not name it, and the IEEE proceedings were not checked)"],"year":2024},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1777","sections":[{"content":"SoK: Collusion-resistant Multi-party Private Set Intersections in the Semi-honest Model","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Title and authors were checked against the ePrint PDF dated 2023-11-16. The PDF ends with a meta-review (Appendix C, p. 19) but does not name the venue.","heading":"Version and bibliographic notes"},{"content":"$19","heading":"What it classifies"},{"content":"PSI-CONTRIB-2024-VCE-ANALYTICAL-COST-COMPARISON: worst-case counts of elliptic-curve multiplications, OTs, and OLEs for semi-honest collusion-resistant protocols, and the conclusion that no single protocol is the state of the art (§7, pp. 12-13; Table 3, p. 13; Appendices A-B, pp. 16-18). Not yet recorded: the argument that the multiparty protocol of Bay and Kayan is insecure because the leader can enumerate the universe to invert hashed PRF values (§5.2.1, p. 11); the paper states it as an argument, without proof. Section 6.2 (p. 12) also describes an error in the protocol of Wei et al. without attributing the finding to another source.","heading":"Contributions"},{"content":"The complexities in Table 2 are derived by the authors (Appendix A, pp. 16-17), partly copied from the original papers, and in some cases after modifying a protocol so that only the leader receives the output. Table 3 counts sub-protocol calls, not running time, and the authors caution that experiments in the surveyed papers ran on different hardware (§5.1.1, p. 10). The open questions the paper names (abstract, p. 1; §1, p. 2; §§7-9, pp. 12-14) are not restated here.","heading":"Claim boundary"}],"slug":"vce24","status":"published","subtitle":"Jelle Vos, Mauro Conti, Zekeriya Erkin · 2024","summary":"SoK: Collusion-resistant Multi-party Private Set Intersections in the Semi-honest Model","title":"SoK: Collusion-resistant Multi-party Private Set Intersections in the Semi-honest Model","type":"paper","venue":"IEEE S&P 2024","year":2024,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING","keywords":["atomic-result","private-preference-matching","commutative-exponentiation","diffie-hellman","matching-count","hashed-items"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"dh-preference-matching","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["cardinality","intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"technique":["diffie_hellman"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Huberman, Franklin, and Hogg wanted members of online communities to find others who share their preferences without handing their answers to a trusted intermediary, since such a party is hard to agree on and becomes a single point of failure. Generic methods for comparing private data were already known, but the authors chose a direct scheme modelled on Diffie-Hellman key exchange. Each side hashes its entries and applies a secret exponent; after a second exchange, values that coincide mark the entries both sides hold. Counting coincidences gives the size of the overlap, and skipping a shuffle reveals the overlap itself. The authors presented it as simple and efficient, accepting that it reveals a count rather than only whether a threshold is met, and gave no formal security analysis.","prior_boundary":"The paper motivates the protocol by the difficulty of agreeing on a trusted third party in an open community and by the single point of failure such a party creates (§1, p. 79); it notes that techniques for two or more parties to compute preference-matching functions exist under various fault and leakage assumptions, citing Fagin-Naor-Winkler and Naor-Pinkas (§3, p. 81).","significance_at_publication":"The authors present it as one technique for private preference matching that is easier to describe and quite efficient, at the price of leaking the number of matches instead of a threshold bit (§3, p. 81), and as part of mechanisms that avoid trusted third parties (abstract, p. 78).","technical_delta":"Each party hashes its items, exponentiates them with its own secret exponent modulo p, and re-exponentiates the other party's values, so equal items yield equal values \\( H(x)^{ab} \\); the parties then count matches (Appendix A, steps 1-5, p. 85). Permuting the exchanged lists hides positions; dropping the final permutations reveals the shared items instead of only their number (Appendix A, p. 85)."},"historical_context_status":"curator_synthesis","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING","keywords":["private-preference-matching","commutative-exponentiation","diffie-hellman","matching-count","hashed-items"],"limitations":["no adversary model, security definition, or proof is given for the protocol (Appendix A, p. 85)","the terms 'set intersection' and 'PSI' do not appear; the task is called private preference matching (§3, p. 81)","protection against active cheating is only mentioned as a possible zero-knowledge addition, not specified (Appendix A, p. 85)","no costs are stated beyond the four messages of the protocol (Appendix A, p. 85)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH","qualifiers":["both parties receive output: each counts the matches (Appendix A, step 5, p. 85)","the computation is modulo a large prime p; the appendix sets up a prime q dividing \\( p - 1 \\) and an element g of order q, but does not say into which group H maps (Appendix A, p. 85)","the hash function is required to have output that cannot be easily influenced or predicted ahead of time (§2, p. 79)","the basic preference-matching function in §3 takes a threshold, but the appendix protocol reveals the count of matches instead of the threshold bit (§3, p. 81)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 78); §1 (p. 79); §2 (p. 79); §3 'Private Preference Matching' (p. 81); Appendix A 'Discrete Log Problem' and 'Private Preference Matching' (p. 85). ACM EC 1999 proceedings PDF, printed pages.","primary_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/336992.337012","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Two parties holding lists \\( x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\) and \\( y_1, \\ldots, y_m \\) each raise hashes of their own items modulo a prime p to a secret random exponent, exchange the permuted results, raise the other party's values to their own exponent, and return them permuted, so that both parties learn how many items the lists share; if the last two lists are not permuted, both learn which items are shared. The paper relates the protocol to Diffie-Hellman key exchange and states no adversary model or security proof for it.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Two-party count of shared list items by commutative exponentiation of hashed items modulo a prime, without a trusted third party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH","year":1999},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1145/336992.337012","sections":[{"content":"Diffie-Hellman preference matching","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Alice holds \\( x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\) and Bob holds \\( y_1, \\ldots, y_m \\). Alice sends \\( H(x_1)^a, \\ldots, H(x_n)^a \\bmod p \\) in random order; Bob sends \\( H(y_1)^b, \\ldots, H(y_m)^b \\bmod p \\) in random order; Alice returns the values \\( H(y_j)^{ab} \\) and Bob returns the values \\( H(x_i)^{ab} \\), each list permuted; each party then counts the matches (Appendix A, steps 1-5, p. 85). The paper lists variations: without the permutations in steps 3 and 4 both parties learn which elements they have in common; without the permutations in steps 1 and 2 they learn the positions of matches; with a fresh exponent per element they learn only matches in equal positions (Appendix A, p. 85). The authors relate the protocol to the Diffie-Hellman key exchange and to Bellovin and Merritt's encrypted key exchange (Appendix A, p. 85).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"PKE-RESULT-1976-DH-INTERACTIVE-PUBLIC-CHANNEL-KEY-AGREEMENT: Diffie-Hellman key agreement, which the paper names as the protocol's relative (Appendix A, p. 85, reference [10]). Bellovin-Merritt encrypted key exchange (reference [7]) has no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"hfh99-diffie-hellman-preference-ma","status":"published","subtitle":"Enhancing Privacy and Trust in Electronic Communities","summary":"Two parties holding lists \\( x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\) and \\( y_1, \\ldots, y_m \\) each raise hashes of their own items modulo a prime p to a secret random exponent, exchange the permuted results, raise the other party's values to their own exponent, and return them permuted, so that both parties learn how many items the lists share; if the last two lists are not permuted, both learn which items are shared. The paper relates the protocol to Diffie-Hellman key exchange and states no adversary model or security proof for it.","title":"Two-party count of shared list items by commutative exponentiation of hashed items modulo a prime, without a trusted third party","type":"result","venue":"ACM EC 1999","year":1999,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-1999-HFH-DH-PREFERENCE-MATCHING"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-APPROX-LOWER-BOUND","keywords":["atomic-result","lower-bound","communication-complexity","disjointness","private-approximation","intersection-size"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"approx-lower-bound","contribution_kind":"boundary_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["cardinality"],"parties":["two_party"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Exact computation of an intersection's size inherits the linear communication cost of set disjointness, a classical communication complexity result, while approximations with additive error are cheap. That raised the hope that a multiplicative approximation could be private and sublinear at once. Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas ruled this out by a short reduction: padding disjointness instances so that disjoint and intersecting inputs land on intersection sizes a factor \\( (1 + \\varepsilon) \\) squared apart, which any good approximation separates. The resulting linear lower bound holds whether or not privacy is required. They pair it with a sampling protocol whose expected number of rounds adapts to the true size and is optimal for telling such sizes apart, while noting that the round count itself reveals information about the result.","prior_boundary":"The randomized communication complexity of disjointness is \\( \\Theta(N) \\), so computing \\( |a \\cap b| \\) exactly needs \\( \\Theta(N) \\) communication; with additive error \\( \\varepsilon N \\), very efficient protocols exist, namely \\( O(\\log N) \\) bits in the private randomness model (§2, pp. 2-3).","significance_at_publication":"The bound shows that approximation does not circumvent the linear communication of computing the intersection size (§2, p. 3); the paper's sampling protocol Intersect-Approx then matches the bound for distinguishing sizes k and \\( k(1 + \\varepsilon) \\) in an expected \\( O(N/k) \\) sampling invocations (§6, p. 15).","technical_delta":"Padding the disjointness inputs a and b with η leading ones and \\( (2 + \\varepsilon^2)\\eta \\) copies of themselves maps disjoint inputs to intersection size η and intersecting inputs to size at least \\( \\eta(1 + \\varepsilon)^2 \\), so an \\( (\\varepsilon, \\delta) \\)-approximation decides disjointness (§6, pp. 13-14)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-APPROX-LOWER-BOUND","keywords":["lower-bound","communication-complexity","disjointness","private-approximation","intersection-size"],"limitations":["approximation with additive error \\( \\varepsilon N \\) is not covered; it admits \\( O(\\log N) \\)-bit protocols (§2, p. 3)","the matching protocol Intersect-Approx is for semi-honest parties, and its number of rounds depends on the output, which the security definitions do not capture (§6, p. 15)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","qualifiers":["inputs are N-bit strings X, Y with \\( \\alpha = |X \\cap Y| \\) (§6, p. 13)","multiplicative error with constant ε; the bound \\( \\Omega(N/\\eta) \\) becomes \\( \\Theta(N) \\) when η is a constant (§6, p. 14)","the bound is a communication complexity bound and holds regardless of privacy (§2, p. 3)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); contributions (p. 2); §2 'Disjointness and set intersection' (pp. 2-3); §6 'A lower bound' (pp. 13-14); Protocol Private-Sample-B (p. 14); Intersect-Approx and 'Caveat' (p. 15). EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, printed pages.","primary_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"For sets given as N-bit strings, any protocol that outputs an \\( (\\varepsilon, \\delta) \\)-approximation of \\( |X \\cap Y| \\), that is a value within a factor \\( 1 \\pm \\varepsilon \\) with probability at least \\( 1 - \\delta \\), can distinguish intersection size at most η from at least \\( \\eta(1 + \\varepsilon)^2 \\) and hence decide disjointness on inputs of length about \\( N/\\eta \\); for constant ε and constant η its randomized communication complexity is therefore \\( \\Theta(N) \\), with or without privacy. Intersection size thus has no private approximation with sublinear worst-case communication under multiplicative error.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Linear communication lower bound for approximating intersection size within a multiplicative factor, by reduction from disjointness","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Communication lower bound for approximate intersection size","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Given disjointness inputs a, b of length m, the client sets \\( X = 1^{\\eta} | a^{(2 + \\varepsilon^2)\\eta} \\) and the server sets \\( Y = 1^{\\eta} | b^{(2 + \\varepsilon^2)\\eta} \\), so \\( |X \\cap Y| = \\eta \\) if a and b are disjoint and at least \\( \\eta(1 + \\varepsilon)^2 \\) otherwise; an \\( (\\varepsilon, \\delta) \\)-approximation separates the two cases, so its randomized communication is \\( \\Omega(m) = \\Omega(N/\\eta) \\) (§6, pp. 13-14). The accompanying protocol Intersect-Approx repeats the private sampler Private-Sample-B with the AND predicate until T positive samples occur and outputs \\( T/t \\); it is a private approximation for semi-honest parties whose communication is dominated by 1-out-of-N oblivious transfers (Protocol Private-Sample-B, p. 14; §6, p. 15).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"PIR-RESULT-1999-CMS-POLYLOGARITHMIC-SINGLE-SERVER-COMMUNICATION: the paper instantiates the 1-out-of-N oblivious transfer of Private-Sample-B with a computational PIR scheme of Cachin, Micali, and Stadler, through the Naor-Pinkas combination, for \\( \\lambda \\cdot \\mathrm{polylog}(N) \\) communication (§6, p. 14).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"fnp04-lower-bound-for-approximate","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","summary":"For sets given as N-bit strings, any protocol that outputs an \\( (\\varepsilon, \\delta) \\)-approximation of \\( |X \\cap Y| \\), that is a value within a factor \\( 1 \\pm \\varepsilon \\) with probability at least \\( 1 - \\delta \\), can distinguish intersection size at most η from at least \\( \\eta(1 + \\varepsilon)^2 \\) and hence decide disjointness on inputs of length about \\( N/\\eta \\); for constant ε and constant η its randomized communication complexity is therefore \\( \\Theta(N) \\), with or without privacy. Intersection size thus has no private approximation with sublinear worst-case communication under multiplicative error.","title":"Linear communication lower bound for approximating intersection size within a multiplicative factor, by reduction from disjointness","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-APPROX-LOWER-BOUND"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-BALANCED-ALLOCATION-HASHING","keywords":["atomic-result","balanced-allocations","hashing-to-bins","horner-rule","polynomial-degree","server-computation"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"balanced-allocation-hashing","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","balanced_allocation_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The basic polynomial protocol makes the server evaluate a polynomial whose degree equals the client's set size at every one of its own items, so its work grows with the product of the two set sizes. Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas cut this by letting the client spread her items over many bins and send a short polynomial for each. They used balanced allocations, placing each item in the emptier of two random bins, which keeps the fullest bin at about \\( \\ln\\ln k \\) items. The server then evaluates just two short polynomials per item. With short exponents and Horner's rule on top, the construction reaches the linear communication and near-linear computation advertised in the abstract, and the authors note that full-length exponentiations, one per server item, dominate in practice.","prior_boundary":"Evaluating the client's encrypted degree-\\( k_C \\) polynomial naively costs \\( O(k_C) \\) exponentiations per server item, \\( O(k_C k_S) \\) in total, for Protocol PM-Semi-Honest (§4.2, p. 6).","significance_at_publication":"These steps give the \\( O(k) \\) communication and \\( O(k \\ln\\ln k) \\) computation stated in the abstract (p. 1); in practice the server's cost is dominated by \\( k_S \\) full-length exponentiations (§4.2, p. 8).","technical_delta":"Items are hashed to short representations so that Horner's rule uses short exponents (§4.2, pp. 6-7); the client then splits her set into B bins by balanced allocations, where each item goes to the less loaded of two random bins, and sends B encrypted polynomials of degree M; the server evaluates two of them per item (§4.2, p. 7). By Azar et al., the maximum load is \\( (1 + o(1)) \\ln\\ln B / \\ln 2 + \\Theta(k_C / B) \\) (§4.2, p. 7)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-BALANCED-ALLOCATION-HASHING","keywords":["balanced-allocations","hashing-to-bins","horner-rule","polynomial-degree","server-computation"],"limitations":["the maximum-load bound holds with probability \\( 1 - o(1) \\); for concrete constants the paper cites Broder and Mitzenmacher (\\( M = 5 \\) fails with probability about \\( 10^{-58} \\)) and says simple experimentation can give finer bounds (§4.2, p. 8)","the protocol does not specify what happens if a bin overflows (§4.2, p. 8)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties; the hashing-based protocol's security is argued informally (§4.3, p. 9)","items are first mapped to about \\( 2 \\log(\\max(k_C, k_S)) \\) bits by pairwise-independent hashing, with a small collision probability (§4.2, p. 6)","the server returns, for every item, the encryptions for both candidate bins (§4.2, p. 7)","the mapping to bins may be chosen pseudorandomly, jointly or by either party (footnote 9, p. 7)","with \\( B = k_C \\) bins and \\( k \\le 2^{24} \\), communication is at most four times that of the protocol without hashing (§4.2, p. 8)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §4.2 'Efficiently Evaluating the Polynomial', including 'Exponents from a small domain', 'Using Horner's rule', 'Using hashing for bucket allocation', 'Throwing elements into bins - balanced allocations', 'A note on correctness and on constants', and 'Efficiency' (pp. 6-8); §4.3, 'Security of the hashing-based protocol' (p. 9). EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, printed pages.","primary_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"In polynomial-evaluation PSI, the client maps her items into B bins with the two-choice balanced-allocation hash of Azar et al., pads each bin with zero roots to a common degree M, and sends one encrypted polynomial per bin; the server evaluates, for each of its items, only the two polynomials of the bins the item could occupy. With \\( B = k_C / \\ln\\ln k_C \\), \\( M = O(\\ln\\ln k_C) \\), so communication and client work remain \\( O(k_C) \\) coefficients while the server's work falls from \\( O(k_C k_S) \\) to \\( O(k_C + k_S \\ln\\ln k_C) \\) exponentiations; Horner's rule and hashing items to short representations reduce the constant further.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Server exponentiations \\( O(k_C + k_S \\ln\\ln k_C) \\) in polynomial-evaluation PSI via balanced-allocation hashing into low-degree bin polynomials, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Balanced-allocation hashing for polynomial-evaluation PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The client chooses a random hash h with range B; each item goes to the less occupied of its two candidate bins, and every bin polynomial is padded with the root 0 to degree M (0 is assumed not to be a valid input). The client sends the B encrypted polynomials and the mapping; for each item y, the server evaluates the polynomials of both candidate bins and returns \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + y) \\) for each (§4.2, p. 7). Communication and the client's work equal the number of coefficients \\( B \\cdot M \\), which is \\( O(k_C) \\) for \\( B = k_C / \\ln\\ln k_C \\) (§4.2, p. 8).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None. Balanced allocations (Azar, Broder, Karlin, Upfal) has no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"fnp04-server-exponentiations-o-k","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","summary":"In polynomial-evaluation PSI, the client maps her items into B bins with the two-choice balanced-allocation hash of Azar et al., pads each bin with zero roots to a common degree M, and sends one encrypted polynomial per bin; the server evaluates, for each of its items, only the two polynomials of the bins the item could occupy. With \\( B = k_C / \\ln\\ln k_C \\), \\( M = O(\\ln\\ln k_C) \\), so communication and client work remain \\( O(k_C) \\) coefficients while the server's work falls from \\( O(k_C k_S) \\) to \\( O(k_C + k_S \\ln\\ln k_C) \\) exponentiations; Horner's rule and hashing items to short representations reduce the constant further.","title":"Server exponentiations \\( O(k_C + k_S \\ln\\ln k_C) \\) in polynomial-evaluation PSI via balanced-allocation hashing into low-degree bin polynomials, semi-honest","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-BALANCED-ALLOCATION-HASHING"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD","keywords":["atomic-result","psi-cardinality","threshold","function-of-intersection","private-matching-variants"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"cardinality-threshold","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["cardinality","threshold"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Some uses of set intersection need less than the shared items: only how many there are, or whether their number passes a threshold. Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas observed that their polynomial protocol adapts with almost no change. If the server attaches a fixed string instead of its item, the client can count matches but cannot tell which items matched. If the server attaches random values, the client ends up holding values that agree with the server's exactly on the matches, and a small secure circuit over those values can then compute a threshold test or another function of the matches. The authors point out that this circuit depends only on the server's set size and a statistical parameter, not on the size of the domain from which items are drawn.","prior_boundary":"The paper defines private cardinality matching and private threshold matching, and more generally simple functions of the intersection, as variants of private matching (§3.1, p. 3); its basic protocol reveals the intersection itself to the client (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6).","significance_at_publication":"The circuit's cost depends on \\( k_S \\) and a statistical parameter, not on the domain size N, and the same approach computes any function of the intersection, such as whether its size lies in a range (§4.5, p. 9).","technical_delta":"Only the server's payload changes: \\( 0^{\\ell} \\) for the size (§4.4, p. 9); random values \\( r_y \\) followed by a circuit with \\( k_S \\cdot \\lambda \\) gates for the bitwise equality tests and a threshold function for the threshold decision (§4.5, p. 9)."},"historical_context_status":"curator_synthesis","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD","keywords":["psi-cardinality","threshold","function-of-intersection","private-matching-variants"],"limitations":["no separate security argument is given for the threshold variant beyond its composition with a secure circuit evaluation (§4.5, p. 9)","no costs are stated beyond the polynomial protocol plus \\( k_S \\cdot \\lambda \\) equality gates and the threshold circuit (§4.5, p. 9)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties; for the cardinality variant the paper says the proof follows trivially from that of PM-Semi-Honest (§4.4, p. 9)","the threshold variant adds a generic secure circuit evaluation protocol after the polynomial step, whose engine is not specified (§4.5, p. 9)","the client is the only party with output (§3.1, p. 3)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP § Contributions","primary_source":"Contributions (p. 2); §3.1 'PM Variants' (p. 3); §4.4 'Variant: Private Matching for Set Cardinality' (p. 9); §4.5 'Variants: Private Matching for Cardinality Threshold and Other Functions' (p. 9). EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, printed pages.","primary_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"In polynomial-evaluation PSI, if the server encodes a fixed string \\( 0^{\\ell} \\) instead of its item as the payload, the client learns only how many of its decryptions equal that string, that is \\( |X \\cap Y| \\), with security following from the semi-honest protocol. If the server instead encodes fresh random values \\( r_y \\) and the parties then run a secure circuit evaluation that compares the client's \\( k_S \\) decryptions with the server's \\( r_y \\) values and applies a threshold, the client learns only whether \\( |X \\cap Y| > t \\); the same circuit step can compute other functions of the matches.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Intersection size or a threshold decision on it, instead of the intersection, from polynomial-evaluation PSI, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Cardinality and threshold variants of polynomial-evaluation PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Cardinality (\\( \\mathrm{PM}_C \\)): the server computes \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + 0^{\\ell}) \\), and the client outputs the number of decryptions equal to \\( 0^{\\ell} \\) (§4.4, p. 9). Threshold (\\( \\mathrm{PM}_t \\)): the server computes \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + r_y) \\) with random \\( r_y \\); the client's ordered decryptions and the server's ordered \\( r_y \\) values enter a circuit that tests equality bit by bit (\\( k_S \\cdot \\lambda \\) gates) and then applies the threshold function (§4.5, p. 9).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None. The secure circuit evaluation protocol is not named.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"fnp04-intersection-size-or-a","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","summary":"In polynomial-evaluation PSI, if the server encodes a fixed string \\( 0^{\\ell} \\) instead of its item as the payload, the client learns only how many of its decryptions equal that string, that is \\( |X \\cap Y| \\), with security following from the semi-honest protocol. If the server instead encodes fresh random values \\( r_y \\) and the parties then run a secure circuit evaluation that compares the client's \\( k_S \\) decryptions with the server's \\( r_y \\) values and applies a threshold, the client learns only whether \\( |X \\cap Y| > t \\); the same circuit step can compute other functions of the matches.","title":"Intersection size or a threshold decision on it, instead of the intersection, from polynomial-evaluation PSI, semi-honest","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-CARDINALITY-THRESHOLD"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE","keywords":["atomic-result","malicious-security","cut-and-choose","random-oracle","pseudo-identities","oblivious-polynomial-evaluation"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"malicious-ope","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model","random_oracle_model"],"technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","cut_and_choose"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The polynomial protocol is fragile against deviation. A cheating client can hide extra roots, especially when its items are spread over many bins, and a cheating server can blend two of its items into one reply so that the client's verdict depends on both. Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas treated the two sides separately. The client commits to several copies of its bin polynomials built from pseudorandom item identities, and the server has half of them opened before answering with the rest, so that cheating is caught except with probability exponentially small in the number of copies. The server, in turn, must derive all of its randomness from a seed it encrypts, letting the client replay each answer. Only the first fix avoids random oracles, and the combination is outlined only.","prior_boundary":"In the semi-honest protocol a client could send a polynomial with more than \\( k_C \\) roots, which with hashing means more nonzero roots across bins than allowed (§5.1, pp. 10-11), and a server could use two different inputs for the evaluation and the payload, for example encrypting \\( r \\cdot (P(y) + P(y')) + y' \\) so that y' is reported only if both y and y' are client items (§5.2, p. 11).","significance_at_publication":"The paper presents these as protocols for malicious environments with the same \\( O(k) \\) communication and \\( O(k \\ln\\ln k) \\) computation as the semi-honest case up to the factor L, the client-side protocol in the standard model and the server-side one in the random oracle model (abstract, p. 1; footnote 4, p. 2; §5, p. 10).","technical_delta":"Cut-and-choose over L copies with pseudorandom item identities bounds the client's effective set (Protocol PM-Malicious-Client, p. 10; proof sketch, p. 11). Deriving the server's evaluation randomness and a hash tag from an encrypted seed lets the client verify each reply against her own items (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12; Lemma 4, p. 12)."},"historical_context_status":"curator_synthesis","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE","keywords":["malicious-security","cut-and-choose","random-oracle","pseudo-identities","oblivious-polynomial-evaluation"],"limitations":["the protocol against both malicious parties is only outlined; its detailed description is deferred to the full version (§5.3, p. 13)","the malicious-server protocol is given without hashing; the paper says the balanced-allocation version may be modified similarly (§5.2, p. 11)","security arguments are proof sketches (pp. 11-13)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","qualifiers":["malicious client: standard model, overhead L times the semi-honest protocol, error exponentially small in L (§5.1, p. 11)","malicious server: random oracle model, random oracles H1 and H2 (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12)","without hashing, a client polynomial with more than \\( k_C \\) roots is excluded by fixing the constant coefficient to 1 (§5.1, p. 11)","the client protocol uses a pseudorandom function F and a collision-resistant hash G (Protocol PM-Malicious-Client, step 1(b), p. 10; footnote 10, p. 11)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); contributions and footnote 4 (p. 2); §3.2 'Malicious adversaries' (p. 4); §5 introduction (pp. 9-10); Protocol PM-Malicious-Client (p. 10); §5.1 and proof sketch (pp. 10-11); §5.2 (p. 11); Protocol PM-Malicious-Server and Lemma 4 (p. 12); proof sketch (pp. 12-13); §5.3 (p. 13). EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, printed pages.","primary_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Against a malicious client, the client builds L copies of her bin polynomials over pseudorandom identities \\( F_s(G(x)) \\) of her items, the server opens \\( L/2 \\) copies to check that each has \\( k_C \\) nonzero roots and evaluates the rest with XOR shares of each item as payloads, giving error exponentially small in L at L times the semi-honest cost, in the standard model. Against a malicious server, the server derives all randomness for item y from \\( r = H1(s) \\) for a random s, returns \\( (\\mathrm{Enc}(r' \\cdot P(y) + s), H2(r'', y)) \\), and the client recomputes each reply to check consistency, in the random oracle model. A protocol secure against both is only outlined.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Polynomial-evaluation PSI secure against a malicious client by cut-and-choose (standard model) and against a malicious server by derandomized replies (random oracle model)","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Malicious-secure polynomial-evaluation PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"PM-Malicious-Client: the client sends L copies, the server asks to open \\( L/2 \\) of them, the client opens their encrypted coefficients without revealing their PRF keys and reveals the keys of the other \\( L/2 \\) copies, and the server, after checking \\( k_C \\) roots per opened copy, evaluates the unopened copies with \\( L/2 \\) random shares whose XOR is its item as payloads (Protocol PM-Malicious-Client, p. 10). PM-Malicious-Server: the server computes \\( r = H1(s) \\) for a random s and returns \\( (\\mathrm{Enc}(r' \\cdot P(y) + s), H2(r'', y)) \\); the client decrypts s, recomputes r, and accepts x if the pair is consistent with x (Protocol PM-Malicious-Server, p. 12). The simulator extracts the server's input from its random-oracle queries (Lemma 4 and proof sketch, pp. 12-13).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"fnp04-polynomial-evaluation-psi-se","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","summary":"Against a malicious client, the client builds L copies of her bin polynomials over pseudorandom identities \\( F_s(G(x)) \\) of her items, the server opens \\( L/2 \\) copies to check that each has \\( k_C \\) nonzero roots and evaluates the rest with XOR shares of each item as payloads, giving error exponentially small in L at L times the semi-honest cost, in the standard model. Against a malicious server, the server derives all randomness for item y from \\( r = H1(s) \\) for a random s, returns \\( (\\mathrm{Enc}(r' \\cdot P(y) + s), H2(r'', y)) \\), and the client recomputes each reply to check consistency, in the random oracle model. A protocol secure against both is only outlined.","title":"Polynomial-evaluation PSI secure against a malicious client by cut-and-choose (standard model) and against a malicious server by derandomized replies (random oracle model)","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MALICIOUS-OPE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE","keywords":["atomic-result","multiparty","leader","xor-shares","oblivious-polynomial-evaluation","bulletin-board"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"multiparty-ope","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["multiparty"],"technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation","secret_sharing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Extending the polynomial protocol to many parties looks easy: let one party, the leader, evaluate every other party's encrypted polynomial on its own items, splitting each item into shares that only a matching client can unlock. Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas show that this naive version leaks, since the leader created the shares and can see which clients return the right ones even for items that are not shared by everyone. Their fix has every client contribute additional random shares that cancel out, so the values clients publish look random unless an item lies in every set. The resulting protocol for honest-but-curious parties needs only three rounds of postings, which the authors contrast with many-round protocols whose communication grows quadratically in the number of parties.","prior_boundary":"The paper's two-party protocol gives the intersection to one client (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6). A strawman extension in which the leader shares each item among the clients fails, because the leader can tell which clients hold an item outside the intersection from which of them publish correct shares (§7, pp. 15-16).","significance_at_publication":"The authors note that the communication involves two rounds of client submissions and one of the leader, which they prefer to protocols with many rounds and \\( n^2 \\) communication, and that hashing to bins can lower the leader's computation (§7, p. 17).","technical_delta":"Each client adds, for each leader item, random shares that XOR to zero, encrypted to the other clients, so that published values look random to the leader unless the item is in the intersection (§7, steps 1-4, p. 16)."},"historical_context_status":"curator_synthesis","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE","keywords":["multiparty","leader","xor-shares","oblivious-polynomial-evaluation","bulletin-board"],"limitations":["no proof and no formal collusion threshold are given; the text says that for items outside the intersection the published XOR looks random to any coalition (§7, p. 16)","no costs are stated beyond the round count; the leader's computation can be reduced by the hashing of §4.2 (§7, p. 17)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties only; the protocol is sketched (§7, p. 15)","each list is assumed to contain k items without loss of generality (§7, p. 15)","data is posted to a public bulletin board, or to the leader acting as one (§7, step 1, p. 16)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); contributions (p. 2); §7 'The Multi-party Case', 'A strawman protocol', and 'Achieving security with respect to semi-honest parties', steps 1-4 (pp. 15-17). EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, printed pages.","primary_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"For n parties with k items each, \\( n - 1 \\) clients publish polynomials encoding their items under their own additively homomorphic keys, together with encrypted random shares that XOR to zero for each of the leader's k items; the leader evaluates every client polynomial on each of its items with one XOR share of the item as payload; each client decrypts what is encrypted under its key and publishes the XOR of the leader's share with the other clients' zero shares, and an item is in the intersection if all published values for its row XOR to it. The protocol is sketched for semi-honest parties and uses two rounds of client submissions and one round of leader submissions.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Multi-party set intersection from encrypted client polynomials and XOR-shared payloads with re-randomizing zero shares, semi-honest (protocol sketch)","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Multi-party polynomial-evaluation PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Client \\( P_i \\) (\\( 1 \\le i \\le n - 1 \\)) posts encrypted coefficients of a degree-k polynomial \\( Q_i \\) with its items as roots, and a \\( k \\times (n - 1) \\) matrix of random numbers whose rows XOR to zero, column l encrypted under \\( P_l \\)'s key. The leader \\( P_n \\) splits each of its items y into \\( n - 1 \\) XOR shares \\( \\sigma_{y,l} \\) and posts, for each y, the tuple of encryptions of \\( r_{y,l} \\cdot Q_l(y) + \\sigma_{y,l} \\), in permuted order. Each client decrypts its column from the leader and from the other clients, XORs each row, and posts the k results; every party checks whether a row's published values XOR to one of its items (§7, steps 1-4, p. 16).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"fnp04-multi-party-set-intersection","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","summary":"For n parties with k items each, \\( n - 1 \\) clients publish polynomials encoding their items under their own additively homomorphic keys, together with encrypted random shares that XOR to zero for each of the leader's k items; the leader evaluates every client polynomial on each of its items with one XOR share of the item as payload; each client decrypts what is encrypted under its key and publishes the XOR of the leader's share with the other clients' zero shares, and an item is in the intersection if all published values for its row XOR to it. The protocol is sketched for semi-honest parties and uses two rounds of client submissions and one round of leader submissions.","title":"Multi-party set intersection from encrypted client polynomials and XOR-shared payloads with re-randomizing zero shares, semi-honest (protocol sketch)","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-MULTIPARTY-OPE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","oblivious-polynomial-evaluation","homomorphic-encryption","private-matching","paillier","standard-model"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"ope-psi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"technique":["oblivious_polynomial_evaluation"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Before this work, two parties could compute a set intersection privately with generic circuits or with many pairwise equality tests, both quadratic in communication, while cheaper constructions using a linear number of exponentiations had been argued only with random oracles and only for honest-but-curious parties. Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas turned the client's set into the roots of a polynomial and sent its coefficients under additively homomorphic encryption, so that the server could evaluate it blindly on each of its own items and hide everything except matches behind a random multiplier. Security against semi-honest parties then rests on semantic security alone, in the standard model, and communication is linear in the set sizes, which the authors present as an improvement over earlier solutions.","prior_boundary":"A generic circuit for private matching of length-k lists needs \\( O(k^2 \\log N) \\) communication and \\( O(k \\log N) \\) oblivious transfers, and comparing all pairs with private equality tests costs \\( O(k^2 \\log N) \\) communication; constructions with only \\( O(k) \\) exponentiations had been analyzed only in the random oracle model against semi-honest parties (§2, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"The authors state that their protocols are more efficient than previous solutions, with \\( O(k) \\) communication (p. 2), and that the semi-honest protocol is secure in the standard model, unlike the earlier \\( O(k) \\)-exponentiation constructions (abstract, p. 1; §2, p. 2).","technical_delta":"The client's set becomes the root set of a polynomial sent under additively homomorphic encryption; the server evaluates it on each of its items, masks the result with a random multiplier, and adds the item, so shared items decrypt to themselves (§4.1, p. 5; Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6). Client privacy follows from semantic security and server privacy from simulating with the polynomial's roots (Lemmas 2-3, p. 8)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI","keywords":["oblivious-polynomial-evaluation","homomorphic-encryption","private-matching","paillier","standard-model"],"limitations":["security is argued by lemma statements with proof sketches (§4.3, p. 8)","a malicious server can break correctness, for example by encrypting \\( r \\cdot (P(y) + P(y')) + y' \\) (§5.2, p. 11); malicious security is a separate contribution","the \\( O(k_C k_S) \\) server cost of this basic protocol is reduced only by the hashing of §4.2 (p. 6)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","qualifiers":["only the client learns the intersection (§3.1, p. 3)","the plaintext space is a field that represents the input domain but is exponentially larger (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, step 1(a), p. 6)","the server may attach a payload \\( p_y \\) by computing \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + (y|p_y)) \\); the client obtains \\( p_y \\) iff y is shared (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, step 2(b), p. 6)","deciding whether a ciphertext encrypts a client item suffices, so exponential ElGamal can replace Paillier (footnote 8, p. 5)","re-randomization of the result ciphertexts is neglected in the description (footnote 6, p. 5)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); contributions (p. 2); §2 (p. 2); §3.1 (p. 3); §3.3 (pp. 4-5); §4.1 (p. 5); Protocol PM-Semi-Honest (p. 6); §4.3, Lemmas 1-3 (p. 8). EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, printed pages.","primary_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"The client encodes her \\( k_C \\) items as the roots of a polynomial P and sends its coefficients encrypted under a semantically secure additively homomorphic scheme; for each of his \\( k_S \\) items y the server homomorphically computes \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + y) \\) with a fresh random r and returns the permuted ciphertexts, so the client decrypts exactly the shared items and random values otherwise. The protocol computes the intersection for the client with security against semi-honest parties in the standard model, assuming semantic security; without the hashing optimization the server performs \\( O(k_C k_S) \\) exponentiations.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Set intersection for the client from oblivious evaluation of an encrypted polynomial whose roots are the client's items, semi-honest, standard model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Polynomial-evaluation PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The client interpolates \\( P(y) = (x_1 - y) \\cdots (x_{k_C} - y) \\), encrypts its \\( k_C + 1 \\) coefficients, and sends them; the server computes \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(P(y)) \\) by homomorphic operations for each of its items, then \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + y) \\), and sends the permuted results; the client outputs every x in its set that appears among the decryptions (Protocol PM-Semi-Honest, p. 6). The authors note that the construction generalizes oblivious transfer protocols in which the client sends a predicate that is zero exactly at its index (footnote 7, p. 5).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"HE-RESULT-1999-PAILLIER-PRACTICAL-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-PUBLIC-KEY-ENCRYPTION: Paillier encryption, named as a scheme with the required homomorphism (§3.3, p. 5, reference [20]).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"fnp04-polynomial-evaluation-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","summary":"The client encodes her \\( k_C \\) items as the roots of a polynomial P and sends its coefficients encrypted under a semantically secure additively homomorphic scheme; for each of his \\( k_S \\) items y the server homomorphically computes \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(r \\cdot P(y) + y) \\) with a fresh random r and returns the permuted ciphertexts, so the client decrypts exactly the shared items and random values otherwise. The protocol computes the intersection for the client with security against semi-honest parties in the standard model, assuming semantic security; without the hashing optimization the server performs \\( O(k_C k_S) \\) exponentiations.","title":"Set intersection for the client from oblivious evaluation of an encrypted polynomial whose roots are the client's items, semi-honest, standard model","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OPE-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OT-REDUCTION","keywords":["atomic-result","oblivious-transfer","black-box-separation","one-way-functions","impagliazzo-rudich","private-matching"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"ot-reduction","contribution_kind":"boundary_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"A natural question about any new secure-computation task is how weak an assumption could possibly suffice. Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas answered it for set intersection with a two-line encoding. If the sender's set holds each of its two bits tagged with its position, and the chooser's set holds both possible bits tagged with its choice, then the one common element tells the chooser exactly the bit it selected and nothing more. Any private matching protocol thus yields oblivious transfer, and the known black-box separation between one-way functions and oblivious transfer carries over. Set intersection therefore cannot be built from one-way functions alone in a black-box way, which the authors state as an impossibility result and do not develop further.","prior_boundary":"Impagliazzo and Rudich bounded what can be proved from one-way permutations in a black-box way (reference [13], cited in §3.1, p. 3); the paper uses this result through oblivious transfer.","significance_at_publication":"It follows that no black-box reduction of private matching from one-way functions exists (§3.1, p. 3); the authors use the reduction only for this impossibility statement (§3.1, p. 4).","technical_delta":"A two-element set encoding of the sender's bits and the chooser's choice bit turns any private matching protocol into a 1-out-of-2 bit oblivious transfer protocol (§3.1, p. 3); the semi-honest PM protocol also supports oblivious transfer of strings, and 1-out-of-N bit transfer can be built directly (§3.1, p. 4)."},"historical_context_status":"curator_synthesis","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OT-REDUCTION","keywords":["oblivious-transfer","black-box-separation","one-way-functions","impagliazzo-rudich","private-matching"],"limitations":["no non-black-box impossibility is claimed (§3.1, p. 3)","the reduction is given in prose without a formal proof (§3.1, pp. 3-4)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","qualifiers":["the reduction is stated for semi-honest 1-out-of-2 bit oblivious transfer (§3.1, p. 3)","the impossibility concerns black-box reductions only (§3.1, p. 3)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP § Contributions","primary_source":"§3.1 'Private Matching and Oblivious Transfer' (pp. 3-4). EUROCRYPT 2004, LNCS 3027, printed pages.","primary_source_url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Semi-honest 1-out-of-2 bit oblivious transfer reduces to private matching: the sender inputs the set \\( \\{0|b_0, 1|b_1\\} \\), the chooser inputs \\( \\{\\sigma|0, \\sigma|1\\} \\), and the chooser's output of the matching protocol is \\( \\sigma|b_\\sigma \\). By the result of Impagliazzo and Rudich, private matching therefore has no black-box reduction from one-way functions.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Private matching implies oblivious transfer, so set intersection has no black-box construction from one-way functions","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","year":2004},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-24676-3_1","sections":[{"content":"Private matching implies oblivious transfer","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"From a private matching protocol in which the chooser learns \\( X \\cap Y \\), the sender's set \\( \\{0|b_0, 1|b_1\\} \\) and the chooser's set \\( \\{\\sigma|0, \\sigma|1\\} \\) intersect in exactly \\( \\sigma|b_\\sigma \\), giving the chooser \\( b_\\sigma \\) and the sender nothing (§3.1, p. 3). The authors combine this with Impagliazzo and Rudich to conclude that private matching cannot be reduced to one-way functions in a black-box manner (§3.1, p. 3).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-BASEOT: oblivious transfer, the primitive that private matching is shown to imply (§3.1, p. 3).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"fnp04-private-matching-implies-obl","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Private Matching and Set Intersection","summary":"Semi-honest 1-out-of-2 bit oblivious transfer reduces to private matching: the sender inputs the set \\( \\{0|b_0, 1|b_1\\} \\), the chooser inputs \\( \\{\\sigma|0, \\sigma|1\\} \\), and the chooser's output of the matching protocol is \\( \\sigma|b_\\sigma \\). By the result of Impagliazzo and Rudich, private matching therefore has no black-box reduction from one-way functions.","title":"Private matching implies oblivious transfer, so set intersection has no black-box construction from one-way functions","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2004","year":2004,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2004-FNP-OT-REDUCTION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA","keywords":["atomic-result","blind-rsa","rsa-signatures","one-more-rsa","server-precomputation","weak-client","oblivious-keyword-search"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"blind-rsa","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"technique":["rsa_blind_signature"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Linear-cost PSI protocols existed by 2009, but they made the client perform one modular exponentiation per item, which is heavy for a phone, and the best known oblivious-PRF protocol carried extra setup. De Cristofaro and Tsudik borrowed an idea from adaptive oblivious keyword search: the server signs the hashes of its items with an RSA key ahead of time and publishes hashes of those signatures, and the client obtains signatures on its own items by blind signing, so that matching items produce equal tags. Online, the client multiplies and divides but never exponentiates, and the server performs one exponentiation per client item. The authors recommend the protocol when the client is weak and the server can precompute, and they note that reusing tags lets a client track changes to the server's set.","prior_boundary":"Polynomial-evaluation PSI cost \\( O(vw) \\) or \\( O(w \\log\\log v) \\) server exponentiations; the OPRF-based protocol of Jarecki and Liu needed a common reference string with a safe RSA modulus and a polynomial input domain; the UPF-based protocol of Jarecki and Liu used \\( O(w + v) \\) short server exponentiations and \\( O(v) \\) client exponentiations (§4.1, pp. 3-4; Table 2, p. 11). The paper's own prime-order protocol also needs \\( O(v) \\) client exponentiations (§5.5, p. 8).","significance_at_publication":"The client performs no online exponentiations, which suits resource-poor clients (§5.6, p. 9); in the paper's measurements the client's online time for 5,000 items per party is 1,422 ms against 5,344 ms for the prime-order protocol, while the server's is 4,662 ms against 2,072 ms (Table 3, p. 11).","technical_delta":"Adapting Ogata and Kurosawa's adaptive oblivious keyword search, the server reveals as keys the hashes of RSA signatures on its items, and the client obtains signatures on its own items by blinding (§5.6, Figure 4, p. 9); server privacy is reduced to one-more-RSA in the random oracle model (Appendix B.3, p. 16)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA","keywords":["blind-rsa","rsa-signatures","one-more-rsa","server-precomputation","weak-client","oblivious-keyword-search"],"limitations":["no server unlinkability: tags \\( H'(H(s_j)^d) \\) repeat across runs, so a client observes changes in the server's set; the paper sketches patches such as fresh RSA keys or per-run hash functions (§5.6, pp. 9-10)","without precomputation it is slower than the prime-order protocol, which uses shorter server exponents (§5.6, p. 10)","client privacy and unlinkability proofs are deferred; server privacy is a proof sketch (Appendix B.3, p. 16)","no security against malicious parties is claimed (§5.1, p. 6; §7, p. 12)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","qualifiers":["honest-but-curious client and server (§5.1, p. 6)","the server sets up its RSA parameters a priori and precomputes w signatures and tags; the client precomputes its blinded values (Figure 4, steps 1-2, p. 9)","only the client learns the intersection; mutual PSI is obtained by two one-way runs if neither party aborts (§2, p. 2)","data transfer can be added by deriving symmetric keys from the matching values (§5.7, p. 10)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT § Contributions","primary_source":"§3, item 4 (p. 2); §4.1 (pp. 3-4); §5.1 (p. 6); §5.6, Figure 4 and 'Drawbacks' (pp. 9-10); §5.7 (p. 10); Table 2 (p. 11); §6.1 and Table 3 (p. 11); Appendix A, One-More-RSA (pp. 13-14); Appendix B.3 (p. 16). IACR ePrint 2009/491, PDF dated 2010-08-04.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"The server signs the full-domain hash of each of its w items with its RSA key offline and sends the tags \\( H'(H(s_j)^d) \\); the client blinds each hashed item as \\( H(c_i) \\cdot R_i^e \\), the server returns \\( (H(c_i) \\cdot R_i^e)^d \\), and the client divides by \\( R_i \\) to obtain \\( H(c_i)^d \\) and compares tags. Against honest-but-curious parties, in the random oracle model under the one-more-RSA assumption, the protocol is server-private, client-private, and client-unlinkable; online the server performs \\( O(v) \\) exponentiations and the client \\( O(v) \\) modular multiplications, after w RSA signatures of server precomputation, with \\( O(v + w) \\) communication.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"PSI from RSA blind signatures with server precomputation and no online client exponentiations, semi-honest, random oracle model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","year":2010},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","sections":[{"content":"Blind-RSA PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Offline, the server computes \\( K_{s:j} = H(s_j)^d \\bmod n \\) and \\( t_j = H'(K_{s:j}) \\) for each of its items, and the client computes \\( y_i = H(c_i) \\cdot (R_{c:i})^e \\bmod n \\). Online, the client sends the \\( y_i \\), the server returns \\( y_i^d \\) together with the tags \\( t_j \\), and the client computes \\( K_{c:i} = y_i^d / R_{c:i} = H(c_i)^d \\) and outputs the items whose tag \\( H'(K_{c:i}) \\) appears among the \\( t_j \\) (Figure 4, p. 9). The simulator for an honest-but-curious client answers random-oracle queries and reduces any client that learns more than v outputs to the one-more-RSA problem (Appendix B.3, p. 16).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"SIG-CONSTRUCTION-1978-RSA: RSA signatures, computed obliviously by blinding (Figure 4, p. 9; Appendix B.3, p. 16, which cites Chaum's blind signatures). Ogata-Kurosawa adaptive oblivious keyword search, the stated source of the idea (§5.6, p. 9), has no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"ct10-psi-from-rsa-blind","status":"published","subtitle":"Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Computational and Bandwidth Complexity","summary":"The server signs the full-domain hash of each of its w items with its RSA key offline and sends the tags \\( H'(H(s_j)^d) \\); the client blinds each hashed item as \\( H(c_i) \\cdot R_i^e \\), the server returns \\( (H(c_i) \\cdot R_i^e)^d \\), and the client divides by \\( R_i \\) to obtain \\( H(c_i)^d \\) and compares tags. Against honest-but-curious parties, in the random oracle model under the one-more-RSA assumption, the protocol is server-private, client-private, and client-unlinkable; online the server performs \\( O(v) \\) exponentiations and the client \\( O(v) \\) modular multiplications, after w RSA signatures of server precomputation, with \\( O(v + w) \\) communication.","title":"PSI from RSA blind signatures with server precomputation and no online client exponentiations, semi-honest, random oracle model","type":"result","venue":"FC 2010","year":2010,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-BLIND-RSA"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI","keywords":["atomic-result","authorized-psi","apsi","rsa-ppit","linear-complexity","one-more-gap-dh","product-of-hashes","certification-authority"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"linear-apsi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Authorized set intersection asks that a client may only match items that a trusted authority has signed, which suits queries such as checking vetted suspects against a bank's records. The available construction, extended from a policy-based information transfer protocol, compared every client authorization with every server item, so its cost grew with the product of the set sizes; identity-based alternatives were linear but lost forward security. De Cristofaro and Tsudik restructured the RSA protocol so that the client folds all its signatures into one aggregate value, letting the server derive a single key per item of its own. Costs become linear, and moving the same pattern into a prime-order group gives a plain set-intersection protocol with short exponents, which the authors prove secure only for honest-but-curious parties.","prior_boundary":"APSI obtained by extending RSA-PPIT to clients with several authorizations has quadratic computation and communication (§4.2, p. 5; §5.3, Figure 1, p. 7); IBE-based PPIT is linear but not forward secure, and PEKS-based APSI needs a quadratic number of tests and bilinear maps (§4.2, p. 5).","significance_at_publication":"The authors report that the linear APSI protocol needs 12,037 ms of server time against 99,118 ms for the RSA-PPIT protocol for 5,000 items per party, with the gap growing for larger sets (Table 3 and §6.1, p. 11), and that it relies on RSA rather than bilinear maps (§4.2, p. 5).","technical_delta":"The client aggregates its authorizations into \\( \\mathrm{PCH}^* \\) and sends \\( X = (\\mathrm{PCH}^*)^2 \\cdot g^{R_c} \\) plus, for each item, a value built from \\( \\mathrm{PCH}^*/\\sigma_i \\), so the server computes one key per own item from X instead of one per pair (§5.4, Figure 2, pp. 7-8). Dropping the RSA setting for a subgroup of order q of \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p^* \\) gives the PSI version, with \\( |q| \\)-bit exponents and precomputable \\( (h_{sj})^{-R_s} \\) (§5.5, Figure 3, p. 8)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI","keywords":["authorized-psi","apsi","rsa-ppit","linear-complexity","one-more-gap-dh","product-of-hashes","certification-authority"],"limitations":["no security against malicious parties is claimed; the authors' work-in-progress malicious proofs seem to depend on the product-of-hashes structure (§5.5, p. 9)","unlinkability is argued informally, with formal proofs deferred (Appendix B.1-B.2, pp. 14-15)","shorter exponents for the APSI protocol are suggested without a security proof (§6, p. 11)","authorization by a certification authority has no facet value in this topic; it is stated here in prose"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","qualifiers":["APSI involves an offline certification authority that is assumed not to behave maliciously; the server is honest-but-curious and the client may lack authorizations for some items (§5.1, p. 6)","in the PSI version both client and server are honest-but-curious (§5.1, p. 6)","the server privacy reduction for the PSI version gives the reduction one additional DH-oracle query; removing it is stated as ongoing work (Appendix B.2, p. 16)","simplified versions for honest-but-curious parties that drop the product of hashes are given in Appendix C (Figures 5-6, p. 17)","only the client learns the intersection (§2, p. 2)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT § Contributions","primary_source":"§2 (p. 2); §3, items 2-3 (p. 2); §4.2 (p. 5); §5.1 Definitions 1-2 and assumptions (p. 6); §5.2 (p. 6); §5.3, Figure 1 (p. 7); §5.4, Figure 2 (pp. 7-8); §5.5, Figure 3 (pp. 8-9); Table 2 (p. 11); Table 3 (p. 11); Appendix B.1 (pp. 14-15); Appendix B.2 (pp. 15-16); Appendix C, Figures 5-6 (p. 17). IACR ePrint 2009/491, PDF dated 2010-08-04.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"In authorized PSI, where each client item must carry an RSA signature issued by an offline certification authority, the client sends one aggregate value X built from the product of its signatures and one blinded value per item; the server returns one exponentiated value per client value and one tag per own item, and the client recomputes the tags of its authorized items. Computation and communication are \\( O(v + w) \\), against \\( O(vw) \\) for the APSI protocol derived from RSA-PPIT, with an honest-but-curious server and a client that may lack authorizations, proved server-private in the random oracle model under the RSA assumption. The same structure in a prime-order subgroup of \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p^* \\) gives a PSI protocol with \\( O(v + w) \\) costs and short exponents, whose server privacy is argued under the One-More-Gap-DH assumption in the random oracle model.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Authorized PSI with computation and communication linear in both set sizes, and its prime-order-group PSI version, semi-honest, random oracle model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","year":2010},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/491","sections":[{"content":"Linear-cost authorized PSI and its PSI version","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"APSI (Figure 2, p. 8): the client computes \\( \\mathrm{PCH}^* = \\prod \\sigma_i \\), sends \\( X = (\\mathrm{PCH}^*)^2 \\cdot g^{R_c} \\) and \\( y_i = (\\mathrm{PCH}^*/\\sigma_i)^2 \\cdot g^{R_{c:i}} \\); the server picks \\( R_s \\), sends \\( Z = g^{eR_s} \\), \\( y_i' = y_i^{eR_s} \\), and tags \\( t_j = H'((X^e / h_{sj}^2)^{R_s}) \\); the client computes \\( K_{c:i} = y_i' \\cdot Z^{R_c} \\cdot Z^{-R_{c:i}} \\) and matches \\( H'(K_{c:i}) \\) against the tags. Server privacy reduces to RSA in the random oracle model (Appendix B.1, pp. 14-15). PSI (Figure 3, p. 8): the same flow in a subgroup of order q of \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p^* \\) with \\( \\mathrm{PCH} = \\prod H(c_i) \\), \\( X = \\mathrm{PCH} \\cdot g^{R_c} \\), and \\( t_j = H'((X/h_{sj})^{R_s}) \\); server privacy reduces to One-More-Gap-DH in the random oracle model (Appendix B.2, pp. 15-16).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"SIG-CONSTRUCTION-1978-RSA: the certification authority's authorizations are RSA signatures on hashed items (Figure 2, p. 8). The RSA-PPIT protocol of De Cristofaro, Jarecki, Kim, and Tsudik (PETS 2009), the stated starting point (§5.3, p. 7), has no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"ct10-authorized-psi-with-computat","status":"published","subtitle":"Practical Private Set Intersection Protocols with Linear Computational and Bandwidth Complexity","summary":"In authorized PSI, where each client item must carry an RSA signature issued by an offline certification authority, the client sends one aggregate value X built from the product of its signatures and one blinded value per item; the server returns one exponentiated value per client value and one tag per own item, and the client recomputes the tags of its authorized items. Computation and communication are \\( O(v + w) \\), against \\( O(vw) \\) for the APSI protocol derived from RSA-PPIT, with an honest-but-curious server and a client that may lack authorizations, proved server-private in the random oracle model under the RSA assumption. The same structure in a prime-order subgroup of \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p^* \\) gives a PSI protocol with \\( O(v + w) \\) costs and short exponents, whose server privacy is argued under the One-More-Gap-DH assumption in the random oracle model.","title":"Authorized PSI with computation and communication linear in both set sizes, and its prime-order-group PSI version, semi-honest, random oracle model","type":"result","venue":"FC 2010","year":2010,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2010-DT-LINEAR-APSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM","keywords":["atomic-result","benchmark","garbled-circuits","custom-protocols","one-more-discrete-log","bandwidth","security-levels","nist"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"gc-versus-custom","contribution_kind":"measurement_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Through the late 2000s, designers of set-intersection protocols built them from public-key primitives and justified this by the presumed slowness of generic secure computation, without measuring the alternative. Huang, Evans, and Katz tested the presumption directly. They rebuilt the fastest custom semi-honest protocol as a real client-server program in the same Java framework as their own garbled circuits and timed both on one pair of desktop machines at five security levels. The garbled circuit used vastly more bandwidth, hundreds of megabytes against one or two, but almost no exponentiations, so its running time grew slowly with the security level while the public-key protocol grew steeply. The measurements turned an untested assumption about generic computation into a comparison with stated settings.","prior_boundary":"Custom PSI protocols from homomorphic encryption and other public-key techniques were designed on the stated belief that generic garbled-circuit solutions would be impractical, a claim the authors say had never been substantiated (abstract and §1, p. 1). The fastest semi-honest PSI protocol reported was that of De Cristofaro and Tsudik (§1.3, p. 3).","significance_at_publication":"For 32-bit elements SCS-WN was always comparable to or faster than the custom protocol, and for 160-bit elements more than ten times faster at the highest security level, at the cost of two to three orders of magnitude more bandwidth (§7.3, pp. 12-13). The conclusion states that generic protocols can be competitive with the best custom PSI protocols (§8, p. 14).","technical_delta":"The authors reimplemented the De Cristofaro-Tsudik protocols in Java as client-server protocols with the same tools as their garbled-circuit framework (§7.3 and footnote 3, p. 12) and measured both at five NIST security levels, scaling OT parameters, OT-extension security, wire-label length, and hash function together (Table 3, p. 12; Figure 11, p. 13). They also counted the expensive operations of each protocol for \\( n = 1024 \\) and \\( \\sigma = 160 \\) (Table 2, p. 13)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM","keywords":["benchmark","garbled-circuits","custom-protocols","one-more-discrete-log","bandwidth","security-levels","nist"],"limitations":["for 160-bit elements at the ultra-short level SCS-WN took 51.5 s against 10.9 s for the custom protocol (Figure 11, p. 13); the introduction says it is faster except at that level (§1.1, p. 2)","the comparison covers semi-honest security only (§1.1, p. 2)","timings depend on the implementations of the cryptographic operations, which is why Table 2 also counts operations (§7.3, pp. 13-14)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","qualifiers":["two Dell desktops with Intel Core 2 Duo E8400 at 3 GHz, 100 Mbps LAN, one core per party (§7, p. 11; §7.2, p. 12)","times include OT and garbled-circuit execution but exclude one-time setup, about 1.2 s for circuit construction and under 1 s for OT-extension initialization, which would add about two seconds to the first run per pair (§7, p. 11; §7.3, p. 12)","the custom protocol is the one-more-DL-based variant, which the authors found faster in total running time than the one-more-RSA-based one (footnote 4, p. 12)","both sides are the authors' Java implementations; De Cristofaro and Tsudik's own C code was single-process and not used for timing (footnote 3, p. 12)","times in this statement are read from the bar labels of Figure 11 for \\( n = 1024 \\) (p. 13)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract and §1 (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 2); §7 (p. 11); §7.1 and Figure 8 (p. 11); §7.2 and Figure 9 (p. 12); §7.3, footnotes 3-4, and Table 3 (p. 12); Figures 10-11 and Table 2 (p. 13); §8 (p. 14). NDSS 2012 PDF dated 2011-11-28.","primary_source_url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"With both protocols implemented on the same two desktops (Intel Core 2 Duo E8400, 100 Mbps LAN), the garbled-circuit SCS-WN protocol computed the intersection of two sets of 1024 elements in 10.5 to 22.7 seconds for 32-bit elements and 51.5 to 122.7 seconds for 160-bit elements across NIST security levels from 80 to 256 bits, while the one-more-DL-based protocol of De Cristofaro and Tsudik took 10.9 to 1972.0 seconds; SCS-WN used 147 to 470 MB of bandwidth against 0.4 to 2.0 MB, and needs far fewer public-key operations but about 2000 times as many operations in total.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Measured running time and bandwidth of garbled-circuit PSI against the fastest custom public-key PSI, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},"primaryUrl":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","sections":[{"content":"Garbled circuits against custom PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Besides the comparison with the custom protocol, the paper measures its own protocols against each other. The bit-vector protocol is the fastest for element spaces up to about \\( \\sigma = 20 \\), for example for \\( n > 40 \\) at \\( \\sigma = 12 \\) and \\( n > 500 \\) at \\( \\sigma = 16 \\) (§7.1 and Figure 8, p. 11). For \\( \\sigma = 32 \\) and 128 to 8192 elements, SCS-WN is more than seven times faster than SCS-HE and 10 to 70 times faster than pairwise comparison, and the oblivious transfers take about 5% of the cost (§7.2, p. 12; Figure 10, p. 13). SCS-WN intersected two sets of \\( 2^{20} \\) 32-bit elements in about six hours (§7.2, p. 12).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-YAO: all measured generic protocols use Yao's garbled-circuit protocol, cited as reference [39] (§1.2, p. 2). MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: the implementation uses OT extension, whose statistical parameter k is varied with the security level (§2, p. 4; Table 2, p. 13).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"hek12-measured-running-time-and","status":"published","subtitle":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","summary":"With both protocols implemented on the same two desktops (Intel Core 2 Duo E8400, 100 Mbps LAN), the garbled-circuit SCS-WN protocol computed the intersection of two sets of 1024 elements in 10.5 to 22.7 seconds for 32-bit elements and 51.5 to 122.7 seconds for 160-bit elements across NIST security levels from 80 to 256 bits, while the one-more-DL-based protocol of De Cristofaro and Tsudik took 10.9 to 1972.0 seconds; SCS-WN used 147 to 470 MB of bandwidth against 0.4 to 2.0 MB, and needs far fewer public-key operations but about 2000 times as many operations in total.","title":"Measured running time and bandwidth of garbled-circuit PSI against the fastest custom public-key PSI, semi-honest two-party","type":"result","venue":"NDSS 2012","year":2012,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-GC-VERSUS-CUSTOM"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING","keywords":["atomic-result","auditing","intersection-size","counter-circuit","garbled-circuits","composability"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"size-auditing","contribution_kind":"capability_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"technique":["garbled_circuits"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"A protocol that returns the intersection reveals a great deal, and when the universe of possible elements is small a dishonest participant can simply submit everything and read off the other set. Custom PSI protocols offered no easy way to restrict what is released, since any change to the output means redesigning the protocol. Huang, Evans, and Katz used the fact that their protocols are ordinary garbled circuits: a few extra gates can count the matches and suppress the result when there are too many. For the sort-based circuits the counter rides on signals the circuit already computes and adds no measurable time, while for the very cheap bit-vector circuit it multiplies the cost several times over. The example illustrates the argument that generic protocols compose with further computation.","prior_boundary":"A PSI output leaks much about the inputs, and for small universes a dishonest party can input every value and learn the other set (§6, p. 10; §3, pp. 4-5). The authors judge that such self-auditing appears difficult to add to custom-designed PSI protocols (§6, p. 10).","significance_at_publication":"It is the paper's demonstration that generic PSI protocols can be combined with further computation at little cost, the modularity argument for generic protocols in the introduction and conclusion (§1, pp. 1-2; §6, p. 10; §8, p. 14).","technical_delta":"Auditing logic is appended to the garbled circuit: for the sort-compare-shuffle family, a Counter circuit that lazily widens its state sums the MUX selection signals of the duplicate-selection circuits, and a comparison circuit tests the threshold; for the bit-vector protocol, a Counter sums the outputs of the AND gates (§6, pp. 10-11)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING","keywords":["auditing","intersection-size","counter-circuit","garbled-circuits","composability"],"limitations":["the paper calls the implementations prototypes and an initial study (§6, p. 10)","no security argument specific to the audited circuits is given (§2, p. 4)","the released condition is an upper bound on the intersection size (§6, p. 10), so it should not be read as threshold PSI in the sense of releasing the intersection only when it is large"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","qualifiers":["the policy studied is an upper bound on the intersection size; above it no output is revealed except that the bound was exceeded (§6, p. 10)","semi-honest parties, as for all protocols of the paper (§1.1, p. 2)","when the threshold is known for an application the Counter can be made smaller (§6, p. 11)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.1 (p. 2); §3 (pp. 4-5); §6 (pp. 10-11); §7.1 (p. 11). NDSS 2012 PDF dated 2011-11-28.","primary_source_url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Because the PSI protocols are pure garbled circuits, the circuit can obliviously count the intersection size \\( \\hat{n} \\) and compare it with a threshold, revealing nothing but that fact when \\( \\hat{n} \\) exceeds it; in the sort-compare-shuffle protocols a Counter circuit of \\( n \\log n - n \\) non-free gates sums the duplicate-selection signals with no measurable overhead in the authors' experiments, while in the bit-vector protocol the count uses \\( \\sigma \\cdot 2^{\\sigma} \\) non-free gates instead of \\( 2^{\\sigma} \\) and raised the running time for \\( \\sigma = 16 \\) from 2.48 s to 12.22 s.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Size-based auditing inside garbled-circuit PSI: the intersection is withheld when its size exceeds a threshold","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},"primaryUrl":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","sections":[{"content":"Size-based auditing","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The auditing circuit computes \\( \\hat{n} \\) obliviously, which is the main cost, and compares it with a threshold, which is negligible (§6, p. 10). In the sort-compare-shuffle protocols the Counter takes as inputs the selection signals of the MUX circuits inside DupSelect-2 and DupSelect-3 (§6, p. 11; Figure 5, p. 8). The authors measured no performance overhead for those protocols and a roughly fivefold slowdown for the bit-vector protocol at \\( 12 \\le \\sigma \\le 16 \\), because its garbled-circuit part is only 35 to 45% of its running time (§6, p. 11).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-YAO: the audit is added to the Boolean circuit evaluated with Yao's protocol (§6, p. 10).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"hek12-size-based-auditing-inside","status":"published","subtitle":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","summary":"Because the PSI protocols are pure garbled circuits, the circuit can obliviously count the intersection size \\( \\hat{n} \\) and compare it with a threshold, revealing nothing but that fact when \\( \\hat{n} \\) exceeds it; in the sort-compare-shuffle protocols a Counter circuit of \\( n \\log n - n \\) non-free gates sums the duplicate-selection signals with no measurable overhead in the authors' experiments, while in the bit-vector protocol the count uses \\( \\sigma \\cdot 2^{\\sigma} \\) non-free gates instead of \\( 2^{\\sigma} \\) and raised the running time for \\( \\sigma = 16 \\) from 2.48 s to 12.22 s.","title":"Size-based auditing inside garbled-circuit PSI: the intersection is withheld when its size exceeds a threshold","type":"result","venue":"NDSS 2012","year":2012,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SIZE-AUDITING"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE","keywords":["atomic-result","sort-compare-shuffle","bitonic-merger","duplicate-selection","waksman-network","garbled-circuits","yao","free-xor","garbled-row-reduction"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"sort-compare-shuffle","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension","sorting_network"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Before 2012 nearly every semi-honest PSI protocol was a custom design from public-key techniques, and generic secure computation was dismissed, in part because comparing all pairs of elements seemed to force a quadratic circuit. Huang, Evans, and Katz observed that each party may sort its own set before the computation starts. The garbled circuit then only has to merge two sorted lists, which a bitonic merger does with \\( n \\log 2n \\) small sorters, keep any element equal to a neighbour, and finally permute the surviving values, because their sorted positions would reveal information about elements outside the intersection. A Waksman network programmed by the circuit generator performs that permutation cheaply. The resulting quasi-linear circuit ran on million-element sets on ordinary desktops and became the generic baseline for computing PSI with secure computation.","prior_boundary":"The custom semi-honest PSI protocols of the time used public-key techniques, and the most efficient was the De Cristofaro-Tsudik protocol, secure under one-more-DL or one-more-RSA in the random oracle model (§1.3, p. 3; §2, p. 3). Generic protocols were held impractical, partly from the belief that a circuit for the intersection of two n-sets needs \\( \\Theta(n^{2}) \\) size (§1, p. 1); the generic weighted-PSI circuit of Jónsson et al. had \\( \\Theta(n \\log^{2} n) \\) gates and required an honest majority (§1.3, p. 3).","significance_at_publication":"The SCS-WN variant computed the intersection of two sets of \\( 2^{20} \\) 32-bit elements in about six hours with 1.7 billion non-free gates (§7.2, p. 12), was faster than every other variant of the paper for 128 to 8192 elements (Figure 10, p. 13), and is the protocol the paper compares with the De Cristofaro-Tsudik protocol (§7.3, p. 12).","technical_delta":"Each party sorts its set locally, so the circuit only merges two sorted lists with a bitonic merger built from 2-Sorters of \\( 2\\sigma \\) non-free gates (§5.1, pp. 6-7; Figures 2-4), filters matches with DupSelect-3 using that three consecutive sorted elements contain at most one match (§5.2, p. 7; Figure 5), and shuffles with a Waksman network whose swapper gates the generator programs, each garbled as a single ciphertext with garbled-row reduction (§5.3.3, pp. 9-10; Algorithm 2). The total is \\( 2\\sigma n \\log(2n) + ((3n - 1)\\sigma - n) + \\sigma(n \\log n - n + 1)/3 \\) gates (Table 1, p. 2)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE","keywords":["sort-compare-shuffle","bitonic-merger","duplicate-selection","waksman-network","garbled-circuits","yao","free-xor","garbled-row-reduction"],"limitations":["no security against malicious parties is claimed; stronger threat models are left to future work (§1.1, p. 2)","the \\( \\Theta(n \\log n) \\) count is in non-free gates with free XOR; the Waksman stage is counted as \\( \\sigma(n \\log n - n + 1)/3 \\) gate equivalents because each programmed swapper gate costs one ciphertext (Table 1, p. 2; §5.3.3, p. 9)","the gate counts and timings concern the plain intersection, not a function of it (§2, p. 3)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties; security follows from Yao's protocol in the semi-honest model, with oblivious transfer from decisional Diffie-Hellman in the random oracle model; no proof is given (§1.2, p. 2; §2, p. 4)","both sets have the same known size n, contain no duplicates, and use σ-bit elements; padding handles other cases (§2, p. 3)","\\( 0^{\\sigma} \\) must not be a valid element, since DupSelect outputs it for non-matches (§5.2, p. 7)","the circuit generator chooses the random permutation and configures the Waksman network locally (§5.3.3, pp. 9-10)","either or both parties may receive the intersection (§2, p. 3)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK § Contributions","primary_source":"Table 1 (p. 2); §1.2 (pp. 2-3); §5 and Figure 1 (pp. 5-6); §5.1 and Figures 2-4 (pp. 6-7); §5.2 and Figure 5 (pp. 7-8); §5.3 (pp. 8-10); §5.3.3, Figure 7, and Algorithms 2-3 (pp. 9-10); §7.2 (p. 12). NDSS 2012 PDF dated 2011-11-28.","primary_source_url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Against semi-honest parties holding n elements of σ bits each, the intersection can be computed by evaluating with Yao's garbled-circuit protocol a circuit that merges the two locally sorted sets with a bitonic merger of \\( 2\\sigma n \\log(2n) \\) non-free gates, keeps each element that equals a neighbour with DupSelect-3 circuits of \\( (3n - 1)\\sigma - n \\) non-free gates in total, and destroys positional information with a Waksman switching network whose control bits the circuit generator sets from a random permutation; the circuit has \\( \\Theta(n \\log n) \\) non-free gates, whereas pairwise comparison needs \\( \\Theta(n^{2}) \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Sort-compare-shuffle garbled circuit for two-party PSI with \\( \\Theta(n \\log n) \\) non-free gates, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","year":2012},"primaryUrl":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2012/private-set-intersection-are-garbled-circuits-better-custom-protocols","sections":[{"content":"Sort-compare-shuffle circuit","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The circuit has three stages (Figure 1, p. 6). Sorting: the two locally sorted lists form a bitonic sequence, and a bitonic merger of \\( n \\log(2n) \\) 2-Sorters sorts their union; each 2-Sorter is a comparison plus a conditional swap with \\( 2\\sigma \\) non-free gates (§5.1, pp. 6-7). Filtering: DupSelect-3 outputs the middle of three consecutive elements if it equals a neighbour and \\( 0^{\\sigma} \\) otherwise, which leaves n outputs instead of \\( 2n - 1 \\) (§5.2, p. 7). Shuffling: the n outputs are permuted before they are revealed, because their positions leak information (§5.3, p. 8). Of the three shuffles studied, the Waksman network (SCS-WN) costs about 15% of the sorting stage (§5.3.3, p. 10), sorting the outputs again (SCS-SORT) is less efficient (§5.3.1, p. 8), and a homomorphic-encryption shuffle (SCS-HE) is linear in n but slower in practice (§5.3.2, p. 9).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-YAO and MPC-COMP-ENC-GARBLED: the circuit is evaluated with Yao's garbled-circuit protocol, which the paper cites as its reference [39] (§1.2, p. 2; §2, pp. 3-4). MPC-CONTRIB-2008-FREEXOR: XOR gates are free and not counted (§1.2, p. 3; §2, p. 4). MPC-CONTRIB-2009-PRACTICAL2PC: garbled-row reduction to three ciphertexts per gate, and to one for the programmed swapper gates (§2, p. 4; §5.3.3, p. 9). MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: input-wire labels are transferred with OT extension on top of Naor-Pinkas OT (§2, p. 4).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"hek12-sort-compare-shuffle-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"Private Set Intersection: Are Garbled Circuits Better than Custom Protocols?","summary":"Against semi-honest parties holding n elements of σ bits each, the intersection can be computed by evaluating with Yao's garbled-circuit protocol a circuit that merges the two locally sorted sets with a bitonic merger of \\( 2\\sigma n \\log(2n) \\) non-free gates, keeps each element that equals a neighbour with DupSelect-3 circuits of \\( (3n - 1)\\sigma - n \\) non-free gates in total, and destroys positional information with a Waksman switching network whose control bits the circuit generator sets from a random permutation; the circuit has \\( \\Theta(n \\log n) \\) non-free gates, whereas pairwise comparison needs \\( \\Theta(n^{2}) \\).","title":"Sort-compare-shuffle garbled circuit for two-party PSI with \\( \\Theta(n \\log n) \\) non-free gates, semi-honest","type":"result","venue":"NDSS 2012","year":2012,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2012-HEK-SORT-COMPARE-SHUFFLE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF","keywords":["atomic-result","bark-oprf","batched-oprf","related-key-oprf","pseudorandom-code","ot-extension","kolesnikov-kumaresan","iknp"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"batched-oprf","contribution_kind":"mechanism","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"technique":["batched_oprf","ot_extension"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Oblivious transfer extension had made large batches of 1-out-of-2 transfers cheap, and Kolesnikov and Kumaresan had read its matrix as a repetition code that a better code could replace, reaching 1-out-of-n transfer for n up to about 256. Oblivious PRFs, by contrast, were still built from algebraic PRFs, and the OPRF hidden inside the PSI protocol of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner, which this paper calls the fastest at the time, spent one random OT on every byte of an item. Kolesnikov, Kumaresan, Rosulek, and Trieu observed that the code is never decoded and needs only large pairwise distance with overwhelming probability, so a pseudorandom code suffices. Each row of the extension matrix then gives an OPRF evaluation on an arbitrary string, with keys related across the batch, and item length drops out of the cost.","prior_boundary":"IKNP OT extension produced many 1-out-of-2 OTs from kappa base OTs and symmetric-key work, and the Kolesnikov-Kumaresan coding view of IKNP gave 1-out-of-n OT for n up to about 256 per matrix row (§1, p. 2; §1.1, p. 3). Earlier OPRF protocols evaluated algebraic PRFs with exponentiations (§1.1, p. 3), and the OPRF implicit in the PSI protocol of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner used one 1-out-of-256 random OT per byte of the effective item length (§5.1, pp. 10-11; §5.3, p. 12).","significance_at_publication":"The OT cost of one OPRF instance became 424-448 bits for the parameters considered, independent of item length (§5.3, p. 12; Table 2, p. 15). The authors use this to replace the OPRF inside the PSI protocol that their abstract calls the fastest at the time, which is the source of their PSI speedup for long items (abstract, p. 1; §5.1, p. 11).","technical_delta":"The error-correcting code of the Kolesnikov-Kumaresan framework is replaced by a pseudorandom code of minimum distance kappa that is never decoded (§2, p. 5; Definition 2, p. 7), so one row of a width-k OT-extension matrix yields one OPRF instance on arbitrary strings. The m instances share the code and the sender's secret, so their keys are related and the receiver learns a relaxed output (Definition 4 and Lemma 5, p. 8); the protocol realizes this functionality against semi-honest adversaries (Theorem 6, p. 9; Figure 2, p. 10) at about the cost of \\( 3.5m \\) 1-out-of-2 OTs (abstract, p. 1)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF","keywords":["bark-oprf","batched-oprf","related-key-oprf","pseudorandom-code","ot-extension","kolesnikov-kumaresan","iknp"],"limitations":["no security against malicious parties is claimed (§1.1, p. 3)","the related-key batch is not a standard OPRF; §5.2 (p. 11) argues that it suffices for the PSI protocol of Figure 3","the matrix width k is 424-448 bits for the parameters of Table 2 (p. 15), against \\( \\kappa = 128 \\) for IKNP, so communication grows with \\( km \\) rather than \\( \\kappa m \\) (footnote 1, p. 6)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","qualifiers":["semi-honest sender and receiver (Theorem 6, p. 9)","related keys: every instance shares the pseudorandom code C and the sender's secret s (§3.3, pp. 7-8)","the receiver learns a relaxed output, the row of the extension matrix, rather than only the PRF value (§3.3, p. 8)","one receiver input per instance (§1, p. 2)","proved with an ideal functionality for the k base OTs (Figure 2, p. 10)","assumes a kappa-Hamming correlation-robust hash H and a pseudorandom code C; the implementation derives C from AES used as a PRF (Lemma 3, p. 7; §6.3, p. 16)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 3); §2 (pp. 4-6); Definitions 1-2 and Lemma 3 (pp. 6-7); Definition 4 and Lemma 5 (p. 8); Figure 1 and Theorem 6 (p. 9); Figure 2 (p. 10); §5.1 and §5.3 (pp. 10-12); Table 2 (p. 15). IACR ePrint 2016/799, PDF dated 2016-08-20.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/799","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Against semi-honest adversaries, the BaRK-OPRF protocol produces a batch of m oblivious PRF instances with related keys, each of which the receiver evaluates on one input from \\( \\{0,1\\}^* \\), using k base OTs and symmetric-key operations; it replaces the error-correcting code of Kolesnikov-Kumaresan OT extension with a pseudorandom code of minimum distance kappa, and m instances cost roughly as much as \\( 3.5m \\) 1-out-of-2 OTs.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Batched related-key OPRF from OT extension, with per-instance cost independent of input length","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","year":2016},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/799","sections":[{"content":"BaRK-OPRF","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The sender picks a pseudorandom code C and a random k-bit string s. For each of m instances the receiver secret-shares the codeword \\( C(r_j) \\) of its input \\( r_j \\) across two matrices, and the parties run k base OTs with their roles reversed, so that the sender obtains the row \\( q_j = t_j \\oplus (C(r_j) \\wedge s) \\) (Figure 2, p. 10). The sender can then evaluate \\( H(j, q_j \\oplus (C(r) \\wedge s)) \\) on any string r, while the receiver learns only the value for its own \\( r_j \\). Recovering another value requires guessing at least kappa bits of s, because two codewords differ in at least kappa positions with overwhelming probability (§2, pp. 4-5).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: IKNP OT extension. BaRK-OPRF starts from the IKNP paradigm (§2, p. 4), and the implementation uses IKNP to extend 128 Naor-Pinkas base OTs to the k base OTs it needs (footnote 1, p. 6; §6.3, p. 16). The Kolesnikov-Kumaresan 1-out-of-n OT extension, the direct predecessor, has no record in any topic yet.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"kkrt16-batched-related-key-oprf","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Batched Oblivious PRF with Applications to Private Set Intersection","summary":"Against semi-honest adversaries, the BaRK-OPRF protocol produces a batch of m oblivious PRF instances with related keys, each of which the receiver evaluates on one input from \\( \\{0,1\\}^* \\), using k base OTs and symmetric-key operations; it replaces the error-correcting code of Kolesnikov-Kumaresan OT extension with a pseudorandom code of minimum distance kappa, and m instances cost roughly as much as \\( 3.5m \\) 1-out-of-2 OTs.","title":"Batched related-key OPRF from OT extension, with per-instance cost independent of input length","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2016","year":2016,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-BATCHED-OPRF"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","bark-oprf-psi","cuckoo-hashing","hash-index","pssz","item-length","ot-extension","semi-honest"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"item-length-independent-psi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The fastest semi-honest PSI protocol of 2015, from Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner, hashed items into bins and ran an oblivious PRF in each bin, but that PRF consumed one oblivious transfer per byte of an item, so long identifiers made the protocol slower. Kolesnikov, Kumaresan, Rosulek, and Trieu kept the hashing structure and swapped in their batched OPRF, whose cost per bin is one fixed-width row of the extension matrix. They also fixed a subtle leak: when two cuckoo hash functions sent an item to the same bin, duplicated values revealed it, so they tag each bin input with its hash index. The result runs in the same time for 32-, 64-, and 128-bit items and was three to four times faster than its predecessor on long items.","prior_boundary":"In the PSSZ protocol, which the paper calls the fastest PSI protocol at the time, each bin's OPRF uses one 1-out-of-256 random OT per byte of the effective item length \\( \\ell^* = \\ell - \\log n \\), an OT cost of \\( 32\\ell^* \\) bits (abstract, p. 1; §5.1, pp. 10-11; §5.3, p. 12). Its protocol and implementation do not account for collisions among the cuckoo hash functions, which leak through duplicate PRF values (§5.2, p. 12, footnote 3).","significance_at_publication":"Communication is lower than PSSZ whenever \\( \\ell^* > 14 \\), which covers every reported PSSZ configuration except \\( n \\ge 2^{20} \\) with 32-bit items (§5.3, p. 13); PSI of \\( 2^{20} \\) items takes 3.8 s regardless of item length, and \\( 2^{24} \\) 128-bit items take about one minute against 214 s for PSSZ (abstract, p. 1; §1, p. 2; Table 3, p. 15).","technical_delta":"Each bin consumes one BaRK-OPRF instance, one row of a k-bit-wide OT-extension matrix with \\( k = 424\\text{--}448 \\), so the OT cost no longer depends on ℓ (§5.3, p. 12; Table 1, p. 14). Appending the hash index z to each bin input makes Alice's PRF calls distinct and lets Bob search a single set \\( H_z \\) or \\( S_j \\) of n values (§5.2, pp. 11-12; Figure 3, p. 13)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI","keywords":["bark-oprf-psi","cuckoo-hashing","hash-index","pssz","item-length","ot-extension","semi-honest"],"limitations":["for short items PSSZ can be faster: its OT cost is lower when \\( \\ell^* < 14 \\), as for 32-bit items with \\( n \\ge 2^{20} \\) (§5.3, p. 13; Table 1, p. 14; §6.2, pp. 16-17)","no security against malicious parties is claimed (§1.1, p. 3)","communication grows with the code width k of 424-448 bits per bin rather than with \\( \\kappa = 128 \\) (Table 1, p. 14; Table 5, p. 15)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","qualifiers":["semi-honest Alice and Bob; PSI security is argued in prose from the RK-PRF security of BaRK-OPRF, Definition 4 (§5.2, p. 11)","both parties learn the intersection: Bob sends it to Alice (Figure 3, step 6, p. 13)","described for equal set sizes \\( |X| = |Y| = n \\) (§5.2, p. 11; Figure 3, p. 13)","stash sizes are taken from PSSZ for a hashing failure probability of \\( 2^{-40} \\) (§6.1, p. 14)","PRF outputs of \\( \\sigma + \\log_2(n^2) \\) bits bound spurious collisions by \\( 2^{-\\sigma} \\) (§5.2, p. 12)","the hash-index optimization reduces running time by about 10% in the implementation (§5.2, p. 11)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1 (p. 2); §1.1 (p. 3); §5 intro and §5.1 (pp. 10-11); §5.2 including 'An Optimization' and footnote 3 (pp. 11-12); §5.3 and 'Remark on pre-hashing long PSI inputs' (pp. 12-13); Figure 3 (p. 13); Table 1 (p. 14); §6.1 and Table 2 (pp. 14-15); Tables 3-5 (p. 15); §6.2-6.3 (pp. 16-17). IACR ePrint 2016/799, PDF dated 2016-08-20.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/799","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Replacing the OPRF implicit in the PSSZ protocol, which spends one 1-out-of-256 random OT per byte of the effective item length, with BaRK-OPRF gives a semi-honest PSI protocol whose OT-extension cost is 424-448 bits per bin for \\( n = 2^{8} \\) to \\( 2^{24} \\), independent of item length. Bob cuckoo-hashes his items into \\( 1.2n \\) bins with three hash functions and a stash and appends the hash index to each bin input, so that Alice evaluates the PRF only on distinct key-input pairs and Bob searches one set of n values per item. Communication is \\( k(1.2n + s) + (3 + s)nv \\) bits excluding base OTs, and the authors report PSI 3.1-3.6 times faster than PSSZ for 128-bit items and sufficiently large sets.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Semi-honest two-party PSI with OT-extension cost per bin independent of item length, from BaRK-OPRF in the cuckoo-hashing paradigm of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","year":2016},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/799","sections":[{"content":"BaRK-OPRF PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Bob places his items in \\( 1.2n \\) bins by three-way cuckoo hashing with a stash of size s and uses \\( y \\| z(y) \\) as the OPRF input of the bin holding y (or y itself in the stash); the parties run \\( 1.2n + s \\) BaRK-OPRF instances with Bob as receiver; Alice sends permutations of \\( H_i = \\{F(k_{h_i(x)}, x \\| i)\\} \\) for \\( i = 1, 2, 3 \\) and \\( S_j = \\{F(k_{1.2n+j}, x)\\} \\) for each stash position; Bob outputs his items whose PRF value lies in the one set that corresponds to its hash index or stash position (Figure 3, p. 13). The paper notes that hashing long items to \\( \\sigma + \\log n \\) bits before PSI cannot remove its advantage, since effective item lengths stay above 14 bits for \\( \\sigma \\ge 40 \\) (§5.3, p. 13).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: the implementation obtains the k base OTs of BaRK-OPRF by extending 128 Naor-Pinkas OTs with IKNP (§6.3, p. 16).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"kkrt16-semi-honest-two-party","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Batched Oblivious PRF with Applications to Private Set Intersection","summary":"Replacing the OPRF implicit in the PSSZ protocol, which spends one 1-out-of-256 random OT per byte of the effective item length, with BaRK-OPRF gives a semi-honest PSI protocol whose OT-extension cost is 424-448 bits per bin for \\( n = 2^{8} \\) to \\( 2^{24} \\), independent of item length. Bob cuckoo-hashes his items into \\( 1.2n \\) bins with three hash functions and a stash and appends the hash index to each bin input, so that Alice evaluates the PRF only on distinct key-input pairs and Bob searches one set of n values per item. Communication is \\( k(1.2n + s) + (3 + s)nv \\) bits excluding base OTs, and the authors report PSI 3.1-3.6 times faster than PSSZ for 128-bit items and sufficiently large sets.","title":"Semi-honest two-party PSI with OT-extension cost per bin independent of item length, from BaRK-OPRF in the cuckoo-hashing paradigm of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2016","year":2016,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2016-KKRT-ITEM-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION","keywords":["atomic-result","unbalanced-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","fv","simd-batching","cuckoo-hashing","permutation-based-hashing","windowing","partitioning","modulus-switching","noise-flooding"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"fhe-sublinear-communication","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"In 2017 the fastest PSI protocols, built from oblivious transfer and hashing, made both parties send encodings of their entire sets, so a phone checking a few thousand contacts against a service with millions of users paid for the size of the service's set. Encrypting the small set under fully homomorphic encryption and letting the server compute on it was an obvious idea, but the depth of the intersection circuit made it impractical. Chen, Laine, and Rindal made it practical by combining SIMD batching with cuckoo and permutation-based hashing, sending extra encrypted powers to flatten the circuit, splitting the server's bins, and shrinking replies by modulus switching. Communication fell to the small set times a logarithm of the large one, and nearly all computation moved to the server, at the price of semi-honest security and 32-bit items.","prior_boundary":"The most efficient PSI protocols at the time, the OT-based protocols of Pinkas et al. and Kolesnikov et al., have communication linear in both set sizes (§1.1, p. 1; §1.3, p. 2), and the paper states that all prior PSI protocols send data proportional to both entire sets, whereas the insecure solution sends only the smaller set (§1.4, p. 3). A naive FHE solution would send only twice the ciphertext expansion times the smaller set, but its computation grows with the size and depth of the intersection circuit (§1.2, pp. 1-2); the concurrent online/offline protocol of Kiss et al. still needs communication or storage linear in the larger set (§1.3, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"For 5535 receiver items against \\( 2^{24} \\) sender items of 32 bits, the paper reports 11.0 to 20.1 MB of total communication depending on parameters, against 480.4 MB for the protocol of Pinkas et al. and 962.1 MB computed from the formula of Kolesnikov et al. (Table 5, p. 16), and 1.7 s of receiver computation in one setting (§6.2, p. 16). The authors present private contact discovery from a phone as the motivating application (§2.2, p. 4) and describe the work as a first step in applying FHE to PSI (§7, p. 17).","technical_delta":"The receiver encrypts its permutation-based cuckoo hash table with SIMD batching, and the sender evaluates per bin the polynomial \\( r\\prod(y - x) \\) from its simple-hashed bins, using encrypted powers of y supplied by the receiver (windowing) and splitting its bins into α partitions, then switches the reply ciphertexts to a smaller modulus (§4, pp. 6-10; Figure 4, p. 12). Communication becomes \\( O(N_y \\log N_x) \\), only the sender performs work linear in \\( N_x \\) (p. 4), and the protocol is proved secure against semi-honest parties assuming an IND-CPA FHE scheme with circuit privacy (Theorem 2, p. 11)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION","keywords":["unbalanced-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","fv","simd-batching","cuckoo-hashing","permutation-based-hashing","windowing","partitioning","modulus-switching","noise-flooding"],"limitations":["no security against malicious parties is claimed: a malicious sender can steer the receiver's output, for example to the receiver's full set, and a malicious receiver could add noise to its ciphertexts so that noise flooding no longer hides the sender's set (§5.2, pp. 12-13)","the implementation restricts items to 32 bits; arbitrary lengths are possible in principle but need much larger FHE parameters (§4.2, p. 9; §6.1, p. 14)","communication is lower than that of the protocol of Pinkas et al. only when the sender's set exceeds about \\( 2^{16} \\) items, and in a single-threaded LAN run with \\( N_x = 2^{20} \\) and \\( N_y \\le 5535 \\) that protocol is faster (3.1 s against 8.6 s) (§6.2, p. 15; Table 5, p. 16)","simulation-based security requires fresh hash functions in each execution; reusing the sender's pre-processing is safe only against adversaries that do not force hashing failures (§6.1, p. 14)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","qualifiers":["semi-honest sender and receiver (§2.2, p. 3; Theorem 2, p. 11)","one-sided output: the receiver learns \\( X \\cap Y \\) and the sender learns nothing (Figure 3, p. 11)","the set sizes \\( N_x \\) and \\( N_y \\) and the item length σ are public (§2.2, p. 3)","security assumes an IND-CPA FHE scheme with circuit privacy; with leveled parameters the sender obtains statistical function privacy by adding encryptions of zero with large noise (noise flooding) (Theorem 2, p. 11; §5.2, p. 11)","the proof of Theorem 2 uses no random oracle; the hash functions are sampled at random and treated as public parameters (p. 11)","correctness holds when hashing succeeds, which happens with probability \\( 1 - 2^{-\\lambda} \\); cuckoo hashing uses three hash functions and no stash, and its failure probability was estimated empirically (§4.2, pp. 7-8; p. 11)","the communication bound assumes the sender holds the larger set; a role-switched variant handles the opposite case with the same asymptotic communication (§2.2, p. 4; §5.2, p. 13)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.4 (p. 3); §2.2 (pp. 3-4); Figure 1 and Theorem 1 (p. 5); §4.1-4.4 (pp. 6-10); Figure 2 and Table 1 (p. 8); Figure 3 and Theorem 2 (p. 11); §5.2 (pp. 11-13); Figure 4 (p. 12); §6 (pp. 13-17); Tables 3-5 (pp. 14-16). IACR ePrint 2017/299, PDF dated 2017-09-06.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/299","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Against semi-honest adversaries, a two-party PSI protocol in which the receiver, holding \\( N_y \\) items, encrypts its cuckoo hash table under a leveled fully homomorphic encryption scheme with SIMD batching, and the sender, holding \\( N_x \\) items in simple-hashed bins, homomorphically evaluates for each bin the product of differences \\( r\\prod(y - x) \\) and returns it, has communication \\( O(N_y \\log N_x) \\), requires work linear in \\( N_x \\) only from the sender, and gives the intersection to the receiver only; windowing (the receiver also sends encrypted powers of its items) and partitioning of the sender's bins reduce the homomorphic circuit depth to \\( \\lceil \\log_2(\\lfloor \\log_2(B/\\alpha)/\\ell \\rfloor + 1) \\rceil + 1 \\) for bin size B, window ℓ, and α partitions.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Communication linear in the smaller set and logarithmic in the larger set for semi-honest two-party PSI, from leveled FHE with batching and hashing","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/299","sections":[{"content":"FHE-based PSI with communication logarithmic in the larger set","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$1a","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"HE-PAPER-2012-FV and HE-RESULT-2012-FV-RLWE-EXACT-ARITHMETIC-SCHEME-WITH-RELINEARIZATION: the implementation uses SEAL v2.1, which implements the Fan-Vercauteren scheme, and §2.3 names FV as a scheme of the required plaintext and ciphertext form (§2.3, p. 4; §6.1, p. 13). HE-RESULT-2012-SV-SIMD-CIPHERTEXT-SLOT-PACKING-AND-PARALLEL-RECRYPTION: the batching step cites Smart-Vercauteren SIMD operations among its sources (§4.1, p. 6). HE-RESULT-2012-BGV-MODULUS-SWITCHING-NOISE-MANAGEMENT: replies are shrunk by modulus switching, citing Brakerski-Gentry-Vaikuntanathan (§4.4, p. 10). HE-PAPER-2016-DS: the paper notes that circuit privacy can be obtained with the sanitization techniques of Ducas and Stehlé, and uses the weaker statistical function privacy by noise flooding instead (§5.2, p. 11).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"clr17-fhe-based-psi-communication","status":"published","subtitle":"Fast Private Set Intersection from Homomorphic Encryption","summary":"Against semi-honest adversaries, a two-party PSI protocol in which the receiver, holding \\( N_y \\) items, encrypts its cuckoo hash table under a leveled fully homomorphic encryption scheme with SIMD batching, and the sender, holding \\( N_x \\) items in simple-hashed bins, homomorphically evaluates for each bin the product of differences \\( r\\prod(y - x) \\) and returns it, has communication \\( O(N_y \\log N_x) \\), requires work linear in \\( N_x \\) only from the sender, and gives the intersection to the receiver only; windowing (the receiver also sends encrypted powers of its items) and partitioning of the sender's bins reduce the homomorphic circuit depth to \\( \\lceil \\log_2(\\lfloor \\log_2(B/\\alpha)/\\ell \\rfloor + 1) \\rceil + 1 \\) for bin size B, window ℓ, and α partitions.","title":"Communication linear in the smaller set and logarithmic in the larger set for semi-honest two-party PSI, from leveled FHE with batching and hashing","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2017-CLR-FHE-SUBLINEAR-COMMUNICATION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING","keywords":["atomic-result","unconditional-zero-sharing","augmented-semi-honest","prf-seeds","multiparty-psi","opprf"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"augmented-zero-sharing","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["augmented_semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["multiparty"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["opprf","secret_sharing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"In the semi-honest protocol, the step that hands out shares of zero is the expensive one: every pair of parties runs a programmable OPRF so that a share reaches a party only if both hold the item. Kolesnikov, Matania, Pinkas, Rosulek, and Trieu observed that if parties may fix their inputs before the run, as the augmented semi-honest model allows, the shares need not be conditional at all. Pairs exchange short PRF seeds once, and each party derives its share of zero for any item locally, so only the reconstruction with the leader still uses programmable OPRFs. The price is that a corrupt party can compute the share for an item it does not hold, which standard semi-honest security forbids; the gain was a run on million-item sets roughly three times faster.","prior_boundary":"In the semi-honest protocol of the same paper, conditional zero-sharing runs a multi-query OPPRF between every pair of parties and is the most expensive part of the protocol (§5.3, p. 13; §6.1, p. 14), giving per-client communication \\( O(m t \\lambda) \\) bits (Table 1, p. 3).","significance_at_publication":"The abstract reports 22 seconds for 5 parties with \\( 2^{20} \\) items each, against 72 seconds for the semi-honest protocol (abstract, p. 1); Table 3 (p. 18) gives 21.93 seconds and 467.66 MB of client communication with the table-based OPPRF on a LAN. The authors argue that in PSI the only useful choice for a coalition is a common input set, so the extra power is relatively harmless (§2.1, p. 4; Appendix A, pp. 23-24).","technical_delta":"The zero-sharing protocol of Figure 9 (p. 14) derives unlimited shares of zero from seeds exchanged in a one-time initialization, and the PSI protocol replaces steps 1-3 of Figure 8 by it, keeping step 4 onward (§6.1, p. 14). Theorem 2 states security in the augmented semi-honest model, defined in §2.1 (p. 4) and Appendix A (pp. 23-24), with a proof sketch (pp. 14-15); the authors explain that a corrupt party can compute the share it would have held for an item it lacks, which rules out standard semi-honest security (§6.1, p. 15)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING","keywords":["unconditional-zero-sharing","augmented-semi-honest","prf-seeds","multiparty-psi","opprf"],"limitations":["not secure in the standard semi-honest model; §2.3 of PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE (p. 3) notes that augmented semi-honest security does not imply semi-honest security","unlike the semi-honest protocol, its cost does not decrease with a smaller tolerated coalition, as PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY observes (§1.1.1, p. 1)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","qualifiers":["augmented semi-honest model: the adversary may change the corrupted parties' inputs once, then runs the protocol honestly (§2.1, p. 4; Definition 3, p. 24)","Theorem 2 is given with a proof sketch that assumes the underlying OPRF protocol is secure (pp. 14-15)","the security argument for the zero-sharing considers a coalition of \\( t < n - 1 \\) corrupt parties (§6.1, p. 14); Table 1 (p. 3) lists corruption threshold \\( n - 1 \\) for both variants","the reported 22 seconds uses the table-based OPPRF, which models a hash function as a random oracle (Figure 6, p. 8; §7.1, pp. 17-18)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); Table 1 (p. 3); §2.1 (p. 4); §6.1 with Figure 9, Theorem 2, and proof sketch (pp. 14-15); §7.1 with Table 3 (pp. 17-18); Appendix A with Definition 3 (pp. 23-24). IACR ePrint 2017/799, PDF dated 2017-08-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"In the multiparty PSI protocol with conditional zero-sharing, the pairwise OPPRF phase is replaced by unconditional zero-sharing: each pair of parties exchanges a PRF seed once, and each party derives its share of zero for any index as the XOR of PRF values under its \\( n - 1 \\) seeds. The parties then run only the conditional-reconstruction phase, in which each party programs an OPPRF to the leader. The modified protocol is secure in the augmented semi-honest model, where corrupt parties may change their inputs before running the protocol honestly, and not in the standard semi-honest model; per-client communication becomes \\( O(m \\lambda) \\) bits against \\( O(m t \\lambda) \\) for the semi-honest protocol.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Augmented semi-honest multiparty PSI with per-client communication \\( O(m \\lambda) \\), by replacing the pairwise OPPRF zero-sharing phase with zero-sharing from pairwise PRF seeds","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","sections":[{"content":"Augmented semi-honest multiparty PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Each party \\( P_i \\) picks a random seed \\( r_{i,j} \\) for every \\( j > i \\) and sends it to \\( P_j \\); on index ind, \\( P_i \\) computes \\( S_i(\\mathrm{ind}) \\) as the XOR of \\( F(r_{j,i}, \\mathrm{ind}) \\) over \\( j < i \\) and \\( F(r_{i,j}, \\mathrm{ind}) \\) over \\( j > i \\), so every term appears twice across all parties and the shares XOR to zero (Figure 9, p. 14). Used as the mapping \\( S_i \\) of Figure 8, these shares feed directly into the reconstruction phase with the leader (§6.1, p. 14). Correctness is unchanged: a party without x does not program its OPPRF at x, so the leader receives a random value (§6.1, p. 14). In the proof sketch, a simulator for a coalition that contains the leader submits the leader's set as every corrupt party's input, which is the extra power the augmented model grants (p. 14).","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"kmprt17-augmented-semi-honest-multip","status":"published","subtitle":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","summary":"In the multiparty PSI protocol with conditional zero-sharing, the pairwise OPPRF phase is replaced by unconditional zero-sharing: each pair of parties exchanges a PRF seed once, and each party derives its share of zero for any index as the XOR of PRF values under its \\( n - 1 \\) seeds. The parties then run only the conditional-reconstruction phase, in which each party programs an OPPRF to the leader. The modified protocol is secure in the augmented semi-honest model, where corrupt parties may change their inputs before running the protocol honestly, and not in the standard semi-honest model; per-client communication becomes \\( O(m \\lambda) \\) bits against \\( O(m t \\lambda) \\) for the semi-honest protocol.","title":"Augmented semi-honest multiparty PSI with per-client communication \\( O(m \\lambda) \\), by replacing the pairwise OPPRF zero-sharing phase with zero-sharing from pairwise PRF seeds","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-AUGMENTED-ZERO-SHARING"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF","keywords":["atomic-result","opprf","programmable-prf","oprf-hint","garbled-bloom-filter","polynomial-interpolation","table-based-opprf","cuckoo-hashing","stash-free-hashing"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"opprf","contribution_kind":"mechanism","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model","random_oracle_model"],"technique":["opprf","batched_oprf","garbled_bloom_filter","cuckoo_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"An oblivious PRF lets a receiver learn PRF values on inputs of its choice while the sender keeps the key, and the batched construction of Kolesnikov, Kumaresan, Rosulek, and Trieu had made such instances cost a few hundred bits each. This paper asked for more: the sender should be able to fix the outputs on chosen inputs, and the receiver should not learn whether its query hit one of them. The authors obtained this with a public hint that corrects the OPRF output on the programmed points, stored as an interpolated polynomial, a garbled Bloom filter, or a small table indexed through a random oracle, and they scaled it to many points and queries by hashing items into bins. A party could now pass a secret value to another party only when both hold the same item.","prior_boundary":"An oblivious PRF gives the sender a key and the receiver PRF values on its chosen inputs (§3.1, p. 4), and the batched OPRF of Kolesnikov, Kumaresan, Rosulek, and Trieu produces many instances from OT extension at roughly 500 bits of communication and a few symmetric-key operations each (§3.1, pp. 4-5). The paper presents programmability, the sender fixing outputs on chosen points without the receiver learning whether its query was programmed, as a new notion (§1.3, p. 3; §3.1, p. 5).","significance_at_publication":"The abstract calls the OPPRF the technical core of the multiparty PSI protocol and suggests it may be of independent interest (abstract, p. 1). In the evaluation the table-based OPPRF gives the fastest PSI and the polynomial hint with separate bins the lowest communication for practical sizes (Table 3, p. 18; §7.1, pp. 17-18).","technical_delta":"The programmable PRF adds a public hint to the syntax, with \\( (n, t) \\)-security defined by an indistinguishability experiment (§3.1, pp. 5-6), and the OPPRF functionality hands the hint to the receiver (Figure 3, p. 5). The hint masks programmed values with OPRF outputs and is stored as a polynomial (Figure 4, p. 7), a garbled Bloom filter (Figure 5, p. 8), or a random-oracle-indexed table for \\( t = 1 \\) (Figure 6, p. 8). Bins built with stash-free cuckoo hashing for the receiver and simple hashing for the sender scale it to large n and t (§4, pp. 9-10; Figure 7, p. 11; Table 2, p. 10)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF","keywords":["opprf","programmable-prf","oprf-hint","garbled-bloom-filter","polynomial-interpolation","table-based-opprf","cuckoo-hashing","stash-free-hashing"],"limitations":["no security against malicious senders or receivers is claimed (§2.1, p. 4)","no theorem covers the OPPRF constructions or the hashing extension; their security is argued in prose (§§3.2-3.4, pp. 6-9; §4, p. 10)","the polynomial hint combined over all bins grows quadratically and did not finish within an hour at \\( 2^{16} \\) items (Table 3, p. 18)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","qualifiers":["semi-honest sender and receiver; the OPPRF protocols are given in the OPRF-hybrid model, and simulation of the polynomial protocol is called trivial (§3.2, p. 6)","the table-based construction supports one query (\\( t = 1 \\)) and models H as a random oracle (Figure 6, p. 8; §3.4, pp. 8-9)","built on the KKRT OPRF, whose instances have related keys and leak slightly more than the PRF value; the paper uses cleaner definitions and says the relaxations can be incorporated (§3.1, p. 5)","the hashing extension programs the same point in several bins and relies on the receiver never querying two bins on the same item (§4, Caveats, p. 10)","hashing parameters, including the stash-free scheme, were determined empirically with failure probability below \\( 2^{-30} \\) or \\( 2^{-40} \\) (§4, p. 9; Table 2, p. 10; Appendix B, pp. 24-25)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.3 (p. 3); §3.1 with Figures 2-3 (pp. 4-6); §3.2 and Figure 4 (pp. 6-7); §3.3 and Figure 5 (pp. 6-8); §3.4 and Figure 6 (pp. 7-9); §4 with Table 2 and Figure 7 (pp. 9-11); Appendix B (pp. 24-25); Table 3 (p. 18). IACR ePrint 2017/799, PDF dated 2017-08-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"A programmable PRF takes a set of n points at key generation and outputs a key and a public hint; it is \\( (n, t) \\)-secure if the hint and t outputs do not reveal which points were programmed when the programmed values are random. The OPPRF functionality gives the sender the key and hint and gives the receiver the hint and the PRF values on its t queries. From an OPRF, three semi-honest OPPRFs are built: an interpolated polynomial hint of n values with \\( O(n^2) \\) interpolation, a garbled Bloom filter hint of about \\( 60n \\) entries with \\( O(n) \\) insertion, and, for a single query, a table of at most \\( 2n \\) entries indexed through a random oracle. Receiver-side cuckoo hashing and sender-side simple hashing extend single-query OPPRFs to many points and queries with one single-query instance per bin.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Oblivious programmable PRF: sender-programmed OPRF outputs that the receiver cannot tell apart from unprogrammed ones, from an OPRF plus a hint, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","sections":[{"content":"Oblivious programmable PRF","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$1b","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-OTEXT: the OPPRF constructions are instantiated with the KKRT OPRF, which the paper describes as based on OT extension with a constant number of base-OT public-key operations (§3.1, p. 4).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"kmprt17-oblivious-programmable-prf","status":"published","subtitle":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","summary":"A programmable PRF takes a set of n points at key generation and outputs a key and a public hint; it is \\( (n, t) \\)-secure if the hint and t outputs do not reveal which points were programmed when the programmed values are random. The OPPRF functionality gives the sender the key and hint and gives the receiver the hint and the PRF values on its t queries. From an OPRF, three semi-honest OPPRFs are built: an interpolated polynomial hint of n values with \\( O(n^2) \\) interpolation, a garbled Bloom filter hint of about \\( 60n \\) entries with \\( O(n) \\) insertion, and, for a single query, a table of at most \\( 2n \\) entries indexed through a random oracle. Receiver-side cuckoo hashing and sender-side simple hashing extend single-query OPPRFs to many points and queries with one single-query instance per bin.","title":"Oblivious programmable PRF: sender-programmed OPRF outputs that the receiver cannot tell apart from unprogrammed ones, from an OPRF plus a hint, semi-honest","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-OPPRF"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-SYMMETRIC-KEY-MPSI","keywords":["atomic-result","multiparty-psi","conditional-zero-sharing","conditional-reconstruction","opprf","dishonest-majority","semi-honest","leader"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"symmetric-key-mpsi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["multiparty"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["opprf","secret_sharing","batched_oprf","cuckoo_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Multiparty PSI had been built from oblivious polynomial evaluation under homomorphic encryption or from bilinear groups, so every item cost public-key operations, and no protocol had been implemented. Kolesnikov, Matania, Pinkas, Rosulek, and Trieu replaced that machinery with oblivious programmable PRFs running on OT extension. Every party splits zero into shares for each of its items and lets another party receive its share only if that party holds the same item; a designated leader then collects, again through programmable PRFs, one combined value per party and keeps the items whose values cancel. Security holds against any number of colluding semi-honest parties, and a smaller tolerated coalition means fewer pairwise instances. Their implementation intersected million-item sets among five parties in about a minute.","prior_boundary":"Multiparty PSI protocols were built from oblivious polynomial evaluation with additively homomorphic encryption or from bilinear groups. Kissner and Song have computation and communication quadratic in the set size and the number of parties; Cheon, Jarecki, and Seo reach quasi-linear complexity but assume at most \\( n/2 \\) corrupted parties; Hazay and Venkitasubramaniam need a linear number of exponentiations in the input sets and give no implementation (§1.2, p. 2; Table 1, p. 3).","significance_at_publication":"Public-key operations no longer depend on the set size (§1.2, p. 2; Table 1, p. 3), and the authors call their implementation the first of a multiparty PSI protocol (abstract, p. 1). It computes PSI of \\( 2^{20} \\) items among 5 parties in 72 seconds and among 15 parties in about 5 minutes on a LAN, with cost linear in the number of parties (abstract, p. 1; §7.2, p. 20; Figure 11, p. 19).","technical_delta":"Conditional zero-sharing uses an OPPRF between every pair of parties so that a party receives another's share of zero for an item only if it holds the item (§5.1, p. 11); conditional reconstruction uses one OPPRF from each party to the leader, which outputs items whose values XOR to zero (§5.2, p. 12; Figure 8, p. 12). Theorem 1 gives security against any number of colluding semi-honest parties (p. 12), outputs of \\( \\lambda + \\log_2 m \\) bits bound false positives by \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (§5.3, p. 12), and a bound t on corruptions limits sharing to \\( t + 1 \\) parties (§5.3, p. 13)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-SYMMETRIC-KEY-MPSI","keywords":["multiparty-psi","conditional-zero-sharing","conditional-reconstruction","opprf","dishonest-majority","semi-honest","leader"],"limitations":["no security against malicious parties is claimed (§1, p. 1; §2.1, p. 4)","communication \\( O(m t \\lambda) \\) per client grows with the tolerated coalition and reaches gigabytes for \\( m = 2^{20} \\) (§7.2, p. 20; Table 5, p. 19)","measurements are for one machine with simulated LAN and WAN links and 128-bit items (§7, p. 17)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties, any number of whom may be corrupt and collude (Theorem 1, p. 12)","proved with an ideal OPPRF functionality (Figure 8, p. 12); the evaluated instantiation uses the table-based OPPRF, which models a hash function as a random oracle (Figure 6, p. 8; §7.2, p. 18)","exponentiation counts are for an offline preprocessing phase (Table 1, p. 3)","the generalization to at most t corrupt parties has each party \\( P_i \\) deal shares only to \\( P_{i+1}, \\ldots, P_{i+t+1} \\) (§5.3, p. 13); in the evaluation each party runs OPPRFs with \\( \\min\\{t + 1, n - 1\\} \\) others, so \\( t = n - 1 \\) and \\( t = n - 2 \\) cost the same (§7.2, p. 20)","the leader P1 announces the intersection (Figure 8, step 5, p. 12); the ideal functionality gives it to all parties (Figure 1, p. 5)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.2 and Table 1 (pp. 2-3); §1.3 (p. 3); §5 with Figure 8 (pp. 10-12); Theorem 1 and proof (pp. 12-13); §5.3 (pp. 12-13); §7.2 with Tables 4-5 and Figure 11 (pp. 18-20). IACR ePrint 2017/799, PDF dated 2017-08-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"For n parties with m items each, every party deals XOR-shares of zero for each of its items and, through a pairwise multi-query OPPRF, lets each other party obtain its share only for items both hold (conditional zero-sharing); each party then programs an OPPRF to the leader P1 with the XOR of the shares it holds per item, and P1 announces the items of its set whose values XOR to zero (conditional reconstruction). With an ideal OPPRF functionality the protocol is secure in the semi-honest model against any number of corrupt, colluding parties, and to tolerate at most t corruptions each party deals shares only to the next \\( t + 1 \\) parties. Exponentiations are confined to an offline phase, \\( O(n \\kappa) \\) for the leader and \\( O(t \\kappa) \\) per client, and communication is \\( O(n m \\lambda) \\) bits for the leader and \\( O(m t \\lambda) \\) bits per client.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Multiparty PSI secure against any number of colluding semi-honest parties with public-key operations independent of set size, via conditional zero-sharing and reconstruction over OPPRFs","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/799","sections":[{"content":"Multiparty PSI from conditional zero-sharing","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$1c","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-OTEXT: every OPPRF instance runs on the KKRT OPRF, which the paper describes as based on OT extension (§3.1, p. 4).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"kmprt17-multiparty-psi-from-opprf","status":"published","subtitle":"Practical Multi-party Private Set Intersection from Symmetric-Key Techniques","summary":"For n parties with m items each, every party deals XOR-shares of zero for each of its items and, through a pairwise multi-query OPPRF, lets each other party obtain its share only for items both hold (conditional zero-sharing); each party then programs an OPPRF to the leader P1 with the XOR of the shares it holds per item, and P1 announces the items of its set whose values XOR to zero (conditional reconstruction). With an ideal OPPRF functionality the protocol is secure in the semi-honest model against any number of corrupt, colluding parties, and to tolerate at most t corruptions each party deals shares only to the next \\( t + 1 \\) parties. Exponentiations are confined to an offline phase, \\( O(n \\kappa) \\) for the leader and \\( O(t \\kappa) \\) per client, and communication is \\( O(n m \\lambda) \\) bits for the leader and \\( O(m t \\lambda) \\) bits per client.","title":"Multiparty PSI secure against any number of colluding semi-honest parties with public-key operations independent of set size, via conditional zero-sharing and reconstruction over OPPRFs","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2017-KMPRT-SYMMETRIC-KEY-MPSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-ENCODE-COMMIT","keywords":["atomic-result","encode-commit","commitments","oblivious-encoding","malicious","uc-security","phasing","tags"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"encode-commit","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model","standard_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["ot_extension","permutation_based_hashing","encode_commit","simple_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Dual execution forces the sender to bind herself to her items by obtaining encodings of them, which works but costs encoding work and lets a cheating sender spread up to a few times n candidate items over the bins. Rindal and Rosulek noted that those encodings act as commitments and asked what happens if an ordinary commitment takes their place. The sender commits to each item and attaches openings hidden under the receiver's encodings, together with short tags so the receiver knows which opening to try. Security needs only an extractable, hiding commitment, which a hash function provides in the random oracle model. The variant computes less and sends more, so it was the faster of the two on a fast local network and the slower one over a wide-area link.","prior_boundary":"In the dual-execution protocol of the same paper, Alice's encodings under Bob's instances act as commitments to her items, which bounds her effective set only by \\( m\\mu \\) items (§5.3, pp. 8-9; §6.3, p. 15), and Alice must act as encoding receiver for every bin position (Figure 4, p. 12).","significance_at_publication":"The commitment-based approach needs less computation and more communication, making it faster in LAN (§5.3, p. 10): 12.6 s against 18 s for dual execution at \\( n = 2^{20} \\) on one thread, but 255 s against 106 s in WAN (Figure 6, p. 19), with 1,322 MB against 1,092 MB of communication (Figure 8, p. 20); it also restricts Alice to exactly n items (§6.5, p. 17).","technical_delta":"Alice commits with a standard non-interactive commitment and sends decommitments masked under Bob's encodings (§5.3, p. 9); PRF-derived tags let Bob find the right decommitment to try without \\( O(n^2) \\) trial openings (§6.4, pp. 15-16; Figure 9, p. 27). Security needs straight-line extraction and standalone hiding, not equivocation (Appendix A, Definition 4, p. 26; Theorem 3, p. 16)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-ENCODE-COMMIT","keywords":["encode-commit","commitments","oblivious-encoding","malicious","uc-security","phasing","tags"],"limitations":["communication is higher than dual execution because \\( O(n \\log n) \\) tag-decommitment pairs are sent (§7.1, p. 21)","the paper states the exact bound only for Alice; Bob still acts as encoding receiver for all \\( m\\mu \\) bin positions and the simulator extracts his set from those inputs, as in the dual-execution protocol where this gives up to \\( m\\mu \\) items (Figure 9, step 3, p. 27; §6.5, p. 16; §6.3, p. 15)","Theorem 3 is supported by simulator descriptions without hybrids (§6.5, pp. 16-17)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","qualifiers":["UC security in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{encode}} \\)-hybrid model with a commitment satisfying Definition 4 (Theorem 3, p. 16); only Bob receives output (Figure 1, p. 4)","Alice's set is restricted to exactly n items, unlike the dual-execution protocol (§6.5, p. 17)","the standard model needs a UC-secure non-interactive commitment, which the authors did not implement because of its communication (Appendix A, p. 26; §7, p. 18)","uses the same simple hashing and phasing as the dual-execution protocol (§6.4, p. 16; Figure 9, step 2, p. 27)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS § Contributions","primary_source":"§5.3 (pp. 8-10); §6.4 (pp. 15-16); §6.5, Theorem 3 (pp. 16-17); §7 'Implementation Details' (p. 18); Figure 6 (p. 19); Figure 8 (p. 20); §7.1 'Communication cost' (p. 21); Appendix A, Definition 4 (p. 26); Appendix B, Figure 9 (p. 27). IACR ePrint 2017/769, full version of August 9, 2017.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Instead of acting as encoding receiver, Alice sends for each item a commitment \\( \\mathrm{Comm}(x; r_x) \\) and, for each position of the item's bin, a tag and the decommitment \\( r_x \\) masked under two PRF values derived from her encoding under Bob's instance; Bob builds a hash table from the tags of his own encodings and outputs his item when the unmasked value opens the commitment. With an extractable and hiding commitment, such as \\( H(x \\| r) \\) in the non-programmable random oracle model, the protocol UC-realizes PSI with output to Bob against malicious adversaries in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{encode}} \\)-hybrid model at cost \\( O(Cn \\log n) \\), and restricts Alice to exactly n items.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Encode-commit malicious PSI with commitments to the sender's items and decommitments masked under oblivious encodings, trading communication for computation and fixing the sender's set size, UC","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","sections":[{"content":"Encode-commit malicious PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Bob acts as encoding receiver for every bin position and stores \\( \\llbracket y' \\rrbracket^{\\mathrm{tag}}_{b,p} = \\mathrm{PRF}(\\llbracket y' \\rrbracket^{A}_{b,p}, \\mathrm{tag}) \\) together with \\( \\llbracket y' \\rrbracket^{\\mathrm{enc}}_{b,p} \\) in a hash table. For each item x, in random order, Alice sends \\( \\mathrm{Comm}(x; r_x) \\) and, for \\( j = 1, \\ldots, \\mu \\), the pair \\( \\llbracket x' \\rrbracket^{\\mathrm{tag}}_{b,j} \\| (\\llbracket x' \\rrbracket^{\\mathrm{enc}}_{b,j} \\oplus r_x) \\); Bob looks up each tag and adds y to the output if the recovered value opens the commitment to y (Figure 9, p. 27). The simulator extracts Alice's items from her commitments in extraction mode and Bob's from his \\( F_{\\mathrm{encode}} \\) inputs (§6.5, pp. 16-17).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None. The commitment scheme H(x||r) and the Orrù-Orsini-Scholl OT extension have no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rr17-encode-commit-malicious-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"Malicious-Secure Private Set Intersection via Dual Execution","summary":"Instead of acting as encoding receiver, Alice sends for each item a commitment \\( \\mathrm{Comm}(x; r_x) \\) and, for each position of the item's bin, a tag and the decommitment \\( r_x \\) masked under two PRF values derived from her encoding under Bob's instance; Bob builds a hash table from the tags of his own encodings and outputs his item when the unmasked value opens the commitment. With an extractable and hiding commitment, such as \\( H(x \\| r) \\) in the non-programmable random oracle model, the protocol UC-realizes PSI with output to Bob against malicious adversaries in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{encode}} \\)-hybrid model at cost \\( O(Cn \\log n) \\), and restricts Alice to exactly n items.","title":"Encode-commit malicious PSI with commitments to the sender's items and decommitments masked under oblivious encodings, trading communication for computation and fixing the sender's set size, UC","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-ENCODE-COMMIT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION","keywords":["atomic-result","dual-execution","oblivious-encoding","common-encoding","simple-hashing","phasing","malicious","uc-security","psz-paradigm"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"malicious-dual-execution","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model","standard_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["dual_execution","ot_extension","permutation_based_hashing","simple_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"By 2017 the fastest semi-honest PSI protocols all followed one pattern: hash items into bins, let one party learn an oblivious encoding of its item in each bin, and compare encodings. Making this pattern resist a cheating sender had failed, partly because the sender's encoding cannot be inverted to extract its input and partly because cuckoo hashing makes item placement depend on the whole set. Rindal and Rosulek adapted dual execution: each party encodes its own items under the other's keys, and only a combination of both encodings is compared, so neither side can count an item it never encoded. Simple hashing and a proof that phasing remains secure complete the protocol. It needs only symmetric-key operations, can avoid random oracles, and ran far faster than the previous malicious protocols in their experiments.","prior_boundary":"The OT-based paradigm of Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner, fastest against semi-honest adversaries, had a variant secure only against a malicious Alice (§1.1, p. 2); three features block malicious security: the encoding map is not invertible for extraction, cuckoo-hashing placement depends on Bob's whole set, and Alice may use different sets in different instances (§3.2, p. 5). The fastest malicious protocol, Rindal-Rosulek's Bloom-filter protocol, appears to depend inherently on the random oracle model (§1.1, p. 2; §1.2, p. 3).","significance_at_publication":"The paper presents this as the first fully malicious variant of the fastest semi-honest paradigm (§1.2, p. 3); the dual-execution random-oracle protocol takes 18 s in LAN and 106 s in WAN for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) on one thread, against 148 s and 1,080 s for the Bloom-filter protocol (Figure 6, p. 19), and a standard-model instantiation is faster than that protocol in LAN (§7.1, p. 20).","technical_delta":"Inspired by Mohassel-Franklin dual execution, each party is encoding receiver once, and the XOR of the two one-sided encodings forms a common encoding that a party can compute only for values it encoded (§5.1, pp. 7-8; Theorem 1, p. 8). Simple hashing replaces cuckoo hashing, phasing is shown secure against malicious adversaries, and aggregating Alice's encodings across bins removes dummy encodings (§6.1-6.2, pp. 10-12; Figure 4, p. 12; Theorem 2, p. 13)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION","keywords":["dual-execution","oblivious-encoding","common-encoding","simple-hashing","phasing","malicious","uc-security","psz-paradigm"],"limitations":["two-party output is not supported: a malicious Bob could make Alice learn \\( X \\cap Y' \\) for any \\( Y' \\subseteq Y \\) (§6.7, pp. 17-18)","the protocol does not strictly enforce the size of a corrupt party's set (Figure 1, p. 4; §6.3, p. 15)","the protocols carry a \\( \\log n \\) factor more communication and computation than the semi-honest protocol of Kolesnikov et al., which the paper reports as a 3.3-4 times running-time gap in LAN for its fastest variant and 25 times in WAN (§7.1, p. 22)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","qualifiers":["UC security in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{encode}} \\)-hybrid model (Theorem 2, p. 13); only Bob receives output (Figure 1, p. 4)","a corrupt party may input up to \\( n' = m\\mu \\) items, \\( n' < 4n \\) for \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), \\( n = 2^{20} \\), \\( \\mu = 68 \\) (§6.3, p. 15)","the programmable-random-oracle instantiation of \\( F_{\\mathrm{encode}} \\) is the 1-out-of-N OT extension of Orrù, Orsini, and Scholl, at about three semi-honest 1-out-of-2 OTs per encoding (§4, p. 6)","the standard-model instantiation uses σ chosen-message OTs and a PRF per encoding, so its cost grows with the item length σ (§4, pp. 6-7)","bins are padded to µ positions and each bin position uses its own \\( F_{\\mathrm{encode}} \\) session (§6.6, p. 17)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1-1.2 (pp. 1-3); Figure 1 (p. 4); §3.1-3.2 (pp. 4-5); §4 and Figure 2 (pp. 5-7); §5.1-5.2, Figure 3, Theorem 1 (pp. 7-9); §6.1-6.2 (pp. 10-12); Figure 4 (p. 12); §6.3, Theorem 2 and 'Set Size for Malicious Parties' (pp. 13-15); §6.6 and Figure 5 (pp. 17-18); §7, Figures 6-8 (pp. 18-20); §7.1 (pp. 20-22). IACR ePrint 2017/769, full version of August 9, 2017.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"$1d","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious security for OT-based PSI in the Pinkas-Schneider-Zohner paradigm by dual execution of oblivious encodings, with simple hashing and phasing, UC in the oblivious-encoding hybrid model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","year":2017},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/769","sections":[{"content":"Dual-execution malicious PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"For each bin b and position p, Alice encodes her item x' under the instance \\( (B, b, p) \\), and Bob encodes his item y' under \\( (A, b, p) \\). For each of her items, Alice sends \\( \\llbracket x' \\rrbracket^{A}_{b,j} \\oplus \\llbracket x' \\rrbracket^{B}_{b,p} \\) for \\( j = 1, \\ldots, \\mu \\), in random order across all bins; Bob outputs y if \\( \\llbracket y' \\rrbracket^{A}_{b,p} \\oplus \\llbracket y' \\rrbracket^{B}_{b,j} \\) lies in this set for some j (Figure 4, p. 12). Encodings have \\( \\lambda + 2\\log(n\\mu) \\) bits so that spurious matches occur with probability at most \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (§6.2, p. 12). The simulator extracts each party's candidate set from its \\( F_{\\mathrm{encode}} \\) inputs and, for a corrupt Alice, keeps the candidates whose common encodings appear in her message (Theorem 2 proof, pp. 13-15).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"Dual execution of Mohassel and Franklin (PKC 2006), the Orrù-Orsini-Scholl 1-out-of-N OT extension, and the Keller-Orsini-Scholl actively secure OT extension used in the standard-model instantiation (§4, pp. 6-7; §7, p. 18) have no record in any topic. MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: IKNP is cited among the OT-extension protocols on which the paradigm relies (§1.1, p. 2); the paper's instantiations use the later protocols named above.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rr17-dual-execution-malicious-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"Malicious-Secure Private Set Intersection via Dual Execution","summary":"$1e","title":"Malicious security for OT-based PSI in the Pinkas-Schneider-Zohner paradigm by dual execution of oblivious encodings, with simple hashing and phasing, UC in the oblivious-encoding hybrid model","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2017","year":2017,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2017-RR-CCS-MALICIOUS-DUAL-EXECUTION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","labeled-psi","keyword-pir","symmetric-pir","interpolation-polynomial","unbalanced-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","pung"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"labeled-psi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["labeled"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing","oprf"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Returning data attached to matching items, such as a public key for each contact found on a messaging service, was easy in OPRF-based PSI: encrypt each label under its item's PRF value and ship them all. But shipping them all costs communication linear in the server's set, and the keyword-search protocol of Freedman, Ishai, Pinkas, and Reingold, which used an interpolating polynomial, was linear as well. Chen, Huang, Laine, and Rindal let the server evaluate, next to the homomorphic intersection polynomial, a second polynomial that interpolates the labels of each bin. A match decrypts to its label, a miss to a random value, and the cost stays logarithmic in the server's set. They observe that the result is a batched symmetric PIR by keywords.","prior_boundary":"Existing PSI protocols could return labels by encrypting each label under its item's OPRF value, but their communication is linear in the sender's set (§1.3, p. 3). The single-server PIR by keywords of Freedman, Ishai, Pinkas, and Reingold uses the same interpolation polynomial under additively homomorphic encryption with communication \\( |X| \\cdot \\ell \\), and the Diffie-Hellman labeled PSI of Jarecki and Liu is also linear in the larger set (§1.5, p. 5).","significance_at_publication":"The paper notes that labeled PSI with a single receiver item is single-server symmetric PIR by keywords, so the protocol is a batched keyword PIR (§1.1, p. 2; §1.5, p. 5). Replacing the multi-query keyword retrieval of the Pung messaging system, for \\( 2^{20} \\) messages of 288 bytes and 256 retrievals, it reports 4.6 s server online time and 17.6 MB against 20.5 s and 120 MB for multi-query SealPIR (§8.4, p. 21; Figure 6, p. 22).","technical_delta":"For each bin the sender interpolates H with \\( H(x_i) = \\ell_i \\) and evaluates \\( G = H + rF \\), where F is the randomized intersection polynomial, so \\( G(y) \\) is the label on a match and uniformly random otherwise; longer labels are split into chunks (§5.1, pp. 11-12). With OPRF pre-processing the sender interpolates labels padded with the second half of each item's OPRF output, which needs no randomization and no help from the FHE scheme to hide non-matching labels (§5.2, p. 12; Figures 1-2, pp. 13-14)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI","keywords":["labeled-psi","keyword-pir","symmetric-pir","interpolation-polynomial","unbalanced-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption","pung"],"limitations":["the security theorems (Theorems 1-2, pp. 15-16) are stated for the unlabeled PSI functionality of Figure 3 (p. 15); no theorem covers the labeled functionality, which PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG later notes and sketches (§4.2, p. 14 of that paper)","Figure 6 (p. 22) does not state thread counts for this comparison"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","qualifiers":["items and labels are first assumed to lie in one finite field; longer labels are split into item-size chunks and the sender repeats the evaluation per chunk (§5.1, p. 11)","complexity figures are for the CLR17-compatible variant of §5.1, with items hashed to \\( \\sigma = \\lambda + \\log|X| + \\log|Y| \\) bits (§5.1, p. 12)","offline, the sender interpolates polynomials of degree about \\( |X|/(m\\alpha) \\) per bin, \\( O(|X|^2/(m\\alpha)) \\cdot \\ell/\\sigma \\) operations in total (§5.1, p. 12)","in the OPRF-optimized variant the one-time pad can be extended to longer labels with a PRG (§5.2, footnote 7, p. 12)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 2); §1.3 (p. 3); §1.5 (p. 5); §5.1-5.3 (pp. 11-12); Figures 1-2 (pp. 13-14); §8.4 (p. 21); Figure 6 (p. 22). IACR ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"In labeled PSI the sender holds a label \\( \\ell_i \\) for each item \\( x_i \\) and the receiver learns the labels of the items in the intersection; if, besides the intersection polynomial, the sender homomorphically evaluates on each receiver bin a polynomial that interpolates the labels of the sender's bin (as \\( G = H + rF \\), or, after OPRF pre-processing, interpolating labels one-time-padded with a second part of each item's OPRF output), the receiver obtains the label exactly for matching items, with communication \\( O(|Y| \\log|X| \\sigma + |Y| \\ell) \\) and sender online computation \\( O(|X| \\ell) \\), a factor \\( \\ell/\\sigma \\) above unlabeled PSI.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Labeled PSI with communication sublinear in the sender's set, from homomorphically evaluated label-interpolation polynomials","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","sections":[{"content":"Labeled PSI from FHE","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Without OPRF pre-processing the sender evaluates, for a receiver item y in a bin, both \\( F(y) = r^* \\prod(x - y) \\) over the bin and \\( G(y) = H(y) + rF(y) \\) with H the interpolation of the bin's labels; the receiver decrypts \\( (F(y), G(y)) \\), which is \\( (0, \\ell_i) \\) when \\( y = x_i \\) and uniformly random in \\( F^* \\times F \\) otherwise (§5.1, p. 11). With OPRF pre-processing the sender computes \\( (x'_i, x''_i) = \\mathrm{OPRF}_k(x_i) \\), uses \\( x'_i \\) as the item and \\( \\ell'_i = \\ell_i + x''_i \\) as the label, and interpolates G with \\( G(x'_i) = \\ell'_i \\) (§5.2, p. 12). The full protocol batches the label polynomials like the intersection polynomials and returns their evaluations on the receiver's encrypted powers (Figure 1, step 5(b)ii, p. 13; Figure 2, steps 8-9, p. 14).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"PIR-PAPER-2018-SEALPIR: the labeled-PSI experiment replaces the multi-query PIR by keywords of the Pung system, built on the multi-query SealPIR of Angel, Chen, Laine, and Setty, and compares with it (§8, p. 18; §8.4, p. 21; Figure 6, p. 22). PIR by keywords (Chor, Gilboa, and Naor, 1997), to which the paper relates labeled PSI, has no record in any topic (§1.1, p. 2; §1.5, p. 5).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"chlr18-labeled-psi-sublinear-in","status":"published","subtitle":"Labeled PSI from Fully Homomorphic Encryption with Malicious Security","summary":"In labeled PSI the sender holds a label \\( \\ell_i \\) for each item \\( x_i \\) and the receiver learns the labels of the items in the intersection; if, besides the intersection polynomial, the sender homomorphically evaluates on each receiver bin a polynomial that interpolates the labels of the sender's bin (as \\( G = H + rF \\), or, after OPRF pre-processing, interpolating labels one-time-padded with a second part of each item's OPRF output), the receiver obtains the label exactly for matching items, with communication \\( O(|Y| \\log|X| \\sigma + |Y| \\ell) \\) and sender online computation \\( O(|X| \\ell) \\), a factor \\( \\ell/\\sigma \\) above unlabeled PSI.","title":"Labeled PSI with communication sublinear in the sender's set, from homomorphically evaluated label-interpolation polynomials","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LABELED-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LEAKY-MALICIOUS-SENDER","keywords":["atomic-result","malicious-sender","leaky-psi","h-limited-fhe","f-limited","labeled-psi","random-oracle","non-black-box-simulation","unbalanced-psi"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"leaky-malicious-sender","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption","oprf","cuckoo_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Homomorphic PSI with a small client set gave a malicious server a lot of room: it held the client's encrypted items and could simply return encryptions of zero, making the client believe that its whole set was in the intersection. Full simulation against such a server looked out of reach, because extracting the server's set seemed to need communication linear in it. Chen, Huang, Laine, and Rindal instead asked the server to prove each match by returning, as a label, a hash of the item's oblivious PRF value. Under the assumption that leveled homomorphic encryption cannot evaluate a deep hash on encrypted data, the server must actually know an item to produce its label, so it can only remove matches as a function of the client's other items, not invent them.","prior_boundary":"In the protocol of Chen, Laine, and Rindal, and in the semi-honest version of this paper, a sender can return encryptions of zero and make the receiver output its full set (§1.3, p. 3; §7.2, p. 16). With OPRF pre-processing the paper proves only privacy against a malicious sender, without input independence or output correctness (Theorem 2, p. 16), and it names two obstacles to full simulation: communication sublinear in the sender's set rules out extracting that set by zero-knowledge proofs, and the sender holds the encrypted receiver set and may evaluate any circuit on it (§7.2, p. 16).","significance_at_publication":"A malicious sender can no longer add receiver items it does not hold; it can only drop matches depending on the receiver's other items, which the authors call significantly less leakage than in the earlier protocol (§7.3, pp. 17-18). Labeled PSI is what makes this possible, and the variant saves work by not evaluating the intersection polynomial (§5.2, p. 12).","technical_delta":"The label of each processed item \\( x' \\) is set to \\( H(x') \\), the receiver accepts y only if the decrypted label at its bin equals \\( H(y') \\), the symmetric polynomial is neither evaluated nor sent, and the sender proves that one OPRF key was used (Figure 8, p. 25). Definition 1 (p. 17) states the F-limited property, and Theorem 3 (p. 25) proves that the protocol realizes the leaky functionality of Figure 7 (p. 25): the simulator extracts the OPRF key from the proof, takes the sender's set from its random-oracle queries, and reads the leakage function off the sender's code (pp. 25-26)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LEAKY-MALICIOUS-SENDER","keywords":["malicious-sender","leaky-psi","h-limited-fhe","f-limited","labeled-psi","random-oracle","non-black-box-simulation","unbalanced-psi"],"limitations":["this is not full simulation-based security against a malicious sender: the receiver's output may depend on items in \\( Y \\setminus X \\) through the sender-chosen leakage function (§7.3, p. 17)","if the sender reuses its pre-processing with many receivers and picks the hash functions, cuckoo-hashing failures can leak one bit of the receiver's set; sampling hash functions from a public reference string is suggested as a countermeasure (§7.3, p. 18)","no measurement of this variant is reported"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","qualifiers":["the H-limited property is an assumption about the leveled FHE scheme and its fixed parameters, supported by a heuristic argument and experiments in which noise overflows after a few extra multiplications, not by a reduction (§7.3, p. 17)","the depth of H can be increased by iterating the hash after the FHE parameters are fixed (§7.3, p. 17)","the sender is not committed to its set and may use any subset of it in each execution (§7.3, p. 17)","the theorem first assumes the sender does not reuse pre-processing across receivers; the zero-knowledge proof of one OPRF key may be replaced by aborting on degenerate OPRF answers, with extra cases in the leakage function (§7.3, p. 17; remarks, p. 26)","simulation against a malicious sender is non-black-box (Theorem 3, p. 25)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.3 (pp. 3-4); §5.2 (p. 12); §7.2 (p. 16); §7.3 and Definition 1 (pp. 17-18); Appendix A, Figures 7-8, and Theorem 3 (pp. 25-26). IACR ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"If the receiver accepts an item y only when the sender returns, as its label, an encryption of \\( H(\\mathrm{OPRF}_k(y)) \\) for a hash function H modeled as a random oracle, the intersection polynomial is no longer returned, and the sender proves in zero knowledge that all OPRF answers use one key, then, assuming One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman and a semantically secure leveled FHE scheme that is H-limited (no efficient sender can compute an encryption of \\( H(z) \\) from encryptions of the fixed powers of z), the protocol securely computes, with non-black-box simulation in the random oracle model, a leaky PSI functionality that gives the receiver \\( X \\cap Y \\cap \\mathrm{leakage}(Y) \\) for a leakage function chosen by a malicious sender whose set may have polynomial size.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious-sender FHE-based unbalanced PSI with bounded leakage \\( X \\cap Y \\cap \\mathrm{leakage}(Y) \\), from hash-valued labels and an H-limited leveled FHE assumption","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","sections":[{"content":"Leaky security against a malicious sender","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Figure 8 (p. 25) runs steps 1-7 of the labeled protocol with labels \\( H(x') \\) and a zero-knowledge proof of one OPRF key, evaluates and returns only the label polynomials, and lets the receiver output y when the decrypted label at \\( y' \\)'s cuckoo position equals \\( H(y') \\). The ideal functionality of Figure 7 (p. 25) takes the sender's set and a leakage function and outputs \\( X \\cap Y \\cap \\mathrm{leakage}(Y) \\). In the proof, a sender that produced a correct label for an item outside its extracted set would contradict the H-limited property, because outputs of the OPRF and the random oracle on such items are uniform (pp. 25-26).","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"chlr18-malicious-sender-fhe-based","status":"published","subtitle":"Labeled PSI from Fully Homomorphic Encryption with Malicious Security","summary":"If the receiver accepts an item y only when the sender returns, as its label, an encryption of \\( H(\\mathrm{OPRF}_k(y)) \\) for a hash function H modeled as a random oracle, the intersection polynomial is no longer returned, and the sender proves in zero knowledge that all OPRF answers use one key, then, assuming One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman and a semantically secure leveled FHE scheme that is H-limited (no efficient sender can compute an encryption of \\( H(z) \\) from encryptions of the fixed powers of z), the protocol securely computes, with non-black-box simulation in the random oracle model, a leaky PSI functionality that gives the receiver \\( X \\cap Y \\cap \\mathrm{leakage}(Y) \\) for a leakage function chosen by a malicious sender whose set may have polynomial size.","title":"Malicious-sender FHE-based unbalanced PSI with bounded leakage \\( X \\cap Y \\cap \\mathrm{leakage}(Y) \\), from hash-valued labels and an H-limited leveled FHE assumption","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LEAKY-MALICIOUS-SENDER"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS","keywords":["atomic-result","extension-field-simd","generalized-simd-encoding","lazy-simd-encoding","arbitrary-length-items","bfv","unbalanced-psi"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"long-items","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Homomorphic PSI with a small client set had been demonstrated only on 32-bit strings, because the plaintext modulus had to be as large as an item, and a modulus of 80 bits, the length needed for hashed items of any length, inflated every parameter and cost. Chen, Huang, Laine, and Rindal used the more general form of SIMD packing in which each slot is an extension field with \\( t^d \\) elements, so a long item occupies one slot while the modulus stays small. They encode with only the Chinese remainder map, skipping the slot isomorphisms that the protocol never needs, and show that wider slots trade a slightly larger query for less server computation. Arbitrary-length items became practical at roughly the cost of the earlier 32-bit runs.","prior_boundary":"Chen, Laine, and Rindal set the plaintext modulus to about \\( 2^{\\sigma} \\) for σ-bit items, so their communication grows linearly and computation quadratically with σ, and larger t and q force a larger ring dimension that can leave slots unused (§3, p. 7). Their examples compare 22-bit items extended to 32 bits by permutation-based hashing (§8.3, p. 21), while hashing arbitrary items to \\( \\lambda + \\log|X| + \\log|Y| \\) bits needs t of about 80 bits, which the authors say hurts that protocol severely (§3, p. 8).","significance_at_publication":"With this encoding the protocol compares 80-bit items inside FHE; for \\( 2^{24} \\) sender items and 5535 receiver items the paper reports 16 MB and 22 s online against 20 MB and 40 s for the 32-bit protocol (§8.3, p. 21; Figure 5, p. 22). The lazy encoder ran 2.2 to 21.8 times faster than FFT-based encoding for d between 8 and 64 (Table 1, p. 9). The earlier paper had left efficient support for long items to future work (PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR, §6.1, p. 14).","technical_delta":"For a prime t such that \\( x^n + 1 \\) factors modulo t into irreducible factors of degree d, the plaintext ring splits into \\( n/d \\) copies of \\( \\mathbb{F}_{t^d} \\), so one slot holds a \\( d \\cdot \\log t \\)-bit item (§3, p. 8). The encoder applies only the CRT map φ and omits the field isomorphisms ψ, which are needed only to permute slots (§3.2, p. 8). The cost analysis gives query size growing with \\( \\log d \\) and online computation \\( \\Theta(1/d) \\), with only logarithmic dependence on n (§3.1, pp. 7-8)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS","keywords":["extension-field-simd","generalized-simd-encoding","lazy-simd-encoding","arbitrary-length-items","bfv","unbalanced-psi"],"limitations":["the trade-off analysis of §3.1 is heuristic, with constants hidden (§3.1, p. 8)","the reported comparison with the 32-bit protocol also reflects the removal of noise flooding by OPRF pre-processing, so the speedup is not attributable to the encoding alone (§8.3, p. 21)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","qualifiers":["items are first hashed to \\( \\lambda + \\log|X| + \\log|Y| \\) bits, about 80 bits for \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) (§3, p. 8; Figure 1, step 1, p. 13)","requires t prime and d the smallest positive integer with \\( t^d \\equiv 1 \\bmod 2n \\) (§3, p. 8)","the cost model treats \\( |Y| \\), \\( |X| \\), n, and the depth L as constants (§3.1, p. 8)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.3 (p. 3); §3 (pp. 7-8); §3.1 (pp. 7-8); §3.2 (p. 8); Table 1 (p. 9); §8.3 (pp. 19-21); Figure 5 (p. 22). IACR ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Encoding each hashed item into one SIMD slot holding an element of an extension field \\( \\mathbb{F}_{t^d} \\), with \\( n/d \\) slots per BFV plaintext and an encoder that skips the slot-permutation isomorphism, lets FHE-based unbalanced PSI compare items of about 80 bits inside the homomorphic computation, enough for arbitrary-length inputs at 40-bit statistical security, without the plaintext modulus \\( t \\approx 2^{\\sigma} \\) that the 32-bit protocol of Chen, Laine, and Rindal would need; larger d increases the query size and reduces the sender's online computation in proportion to \\( 1/d \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Arbitrary-length items in FHE-based unbalanced PSI without a plaintext modulus of the item length, via SIMD slots over extension fields","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","sections":[{"content":"Long items through extension-field SIMD slots","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The BFV plaintext ring \\( \\mathbb{Z}_t[x]/(x^n + 1) \\) is mapped by CRT to the product of the fields \\( \\mathbb{Z}_t[x]/(F_j(x)) \\) and then to \\( n/d \\) copies of \\( \\mathbb{F}_{t^d} \\) (§3, p. 8). Only the first map is used: items are placed directly in the factor rings, which saves the time and storage of the field isomorphisms (§3.2, p. 8). The full protocol chooses t and d with \\( d \\log_2 t \\ge \\sigma \\) (Figure 1, step 3, p. 13).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"HE-RESULT-2012-SV-SIMD-CIPHERTEXT-SLOT-PACKING-AND-PARALLEL-RECRYPTION: the general SIMD encoding with slots of flexible width is that of Smart and Vercauteren, cited as [50] (§3, p. 8; §3.2, p. 8). HE-PAPER-2012-FV: BFV as implemented in SEAL (§1.2, p. 2).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"chlr18-arbitrary-length-items-in","status":"published","subtitle":"Labeled PSI from Fully Homomorphic Encryption with Malicious Security","summary":"Encoding each hashed item into one SIMD slot holding an element of an extension field \\( \\mathbb{F}_{t^d} \\), with \\( n/d \\) slots per BFV plaintext and an encoder that skips the slot-permutation isomorphism, lets FHE-based unbalanced PSI compare items of about 80 bits inside the homomorphic computation, enough for arbitrary-length inputs at 40-bit statistical security, without the plaintext modulus \\( t \\approx 2^{\\sigma} \\) that the 32-bit protocol of Chen, Laine, and Rindal would need; larger d increases the query size and reduces the sender's online computation in proportion to \\( 1/d \\).","title":"Arbitrary-length items in FHE-based unbalanced PSI without a plaintext modulus of the item length, via SIMD slots over extension fields","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-LONG-ITEMS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER","keywords":["atomic-result","oprf-preprocessing","diffie-hellman-oprf","omgdh","malicious-receiver","one-sided-simulation","noise-flooding","unbalanced-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"oprf-malicious-receiver","contribution_kind":"transform","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["oprf","fully_homomorphic_encryption","cuckoo_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The first FHE-based unbalanced PSI protocol protected the server's set only against an honest client, and it did so by flooding reply ciphertexts with noise, padding every bin, and randomizing the evaluated polynomial. Those countermeasures cost depth and parameter size, and a client that tampered with the noise in its own ciphertexts could defeat them. Chen, Huang, Laine, and Rindal moved the protection into a preprocessing step: both sets pass through an oblivious PRF keyed by the server, so the server's non-matching items look random to the client whatever it decrypts. The homomorphic part can then run with smaller parameters, and a simulator reads the client's inputs off its random-oracle queries, which yields security against a malicious client and privacy against a malicious server.","prior_boundary":"The FHE-based protocol of Chen, Laine, and Rindal was proved secure only against semi-honest parties and protected the sender's set by noise flooding, padding of every bin, and a random multiplier on the evaluated polynomial (§2, pp. 6-7; §4.1, p. 9; §4.2, p. 10). Flooding needs a heuristic noise bound and spare noise room, which rules out small FHE parameters, and a receiver that inserts extra noise into its ciphertexts can defeat it (§4.1, p. 9). The OPRF-based unbalanced protocols of Resende and Aranha and of Kiss et al. send the sender's processed set, so their communication is linear in it (§1.4, p. 4; §4.2, p. 9).","significance_at_publication":"The authors state that dropping noise flooding lets them choose smaller FHE parameters (§1.3, p. 3). For \\( 2^{24} \\) sender items and 5535 receiver items they report 16 MB and 22 s online, single-threaded, against 20 MB and 40 s for the earlier protocol, while also comparing 80-bit instead of 22-bit items (§8.3, p. 21; Figure 5, p. 22). The sender's Diffie-Hellman pre-processing is non-interactive and reusable across receivers, which suits contact discovery with a large, slowly changing server set (§4.3, p. 10; Figure 4, p. 20).","technical_delta":"Both sets are first hashed with an OPRF keyed by the sender, either the Diffie-Hellman OPRF of Jarecki and Liu, reusable across receivers, or an OT-based OPRF applied after cuckoo hashing that cannot be reused and keeps the padding (§4.2-4.4, pp. 9-11). The FHE intersection then runs on OPRF outputs without flooding, padding, or multiplier (§4.2, pp. 9-10). A simulator that observes the receiver's random-oracle queries extracts its set, giving one-sided simulation security against a malicious receiver (Theorem 1, p. 15), and the receiver's messages are hidden by OMGDH and FHE semantic security, giving privacy against a malicious sender (Theorem 2, p. 16)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER","keywords":["oprf-preprocessing","diffie-hellman-oprf","omgdh","malicious-receiver","one-sided-simulation","noise-flooding","unbalanced-psi","fully-homomorphic-encryption"],"limitations":["full simulation-based security against a malicious sender is not achieved; a malicious sender can still make the receiver output its full set (§7.2, p. 16)","with the OT-based OPRF the sender cannot reuse OPRF values across receivers and must still pad its bins (§4.2, p. 10; §4.4, p. 11)","when the sender reuses its pre-processing and chooses the hash functions, a selective cuckoo-hashing failure leaks one bit of the receiver's set (§7.3, p. 18)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","qualifiers":["one-sided simulation in the sense of Hazay and Lindell against a malicious receiver, with the malicious receiver's set size equal to the honest one (Theorem 1, p. 15)","against a malicious sender only privacy holds: the receiver's messages reveal nothing about its set, but input independence and output correctness are not guaranteed (§7.2, p. 16; Theorem 2, p. 16)","random oracle model: H is a random oracle into the group, \\( H' \\) an outer hash, and the group must be One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman hard (§4.3, p. 10; Theorem 1, p. 15)","the OPRF output is hashed to \\( \\sigma = \\log_2(N_x N_y) + \\lambda \\) bits with a random-oracle hash sampled by coin flipping (Figure 1, step 1, p. 13)","simulation extracts the receiver's input only before it learns OPRF values, not when it sends its first message, which the authors state suffices (§4.3, p. 10)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.3 (pp. 3-4); §4.1-4.4 (pp. 8-11); Figures 1-2 (pp. 13-14); §7 and Figure 3 (p. 15); Theorem 1 (p. 15); §7.2 and Theorem 2 (pp. 16-17); §8.3 (pp. 19-21); Figure 5 (p. 22). IACR ePrint 2018/787, PDF dated 2018-08-27.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"If both sets are first mapped through a Diffie-Hellman-based oblivious PRF \\( F_\\alpha(x) = H'(H(x)^\\alpha) \\) whose key the sender holds, and the FHE-based intersection of Chen, Laine, and Rindal is run on the outputs, the resulting protocol securely computes PSI with one-sided simulation against a malicious receiver whose set size is unchanged, and with privacy against a malicious sender, in the random oracle model assuming One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman and semantically secure FHE; since the sender's processed items are pseudorandom to the receiver, noise flooding, padding of the sender's bins, and the random multiplier of the evaluated polynomial are no longer needed, and the sender can reuse its processed set with many receivers.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"OPRF pre-processing for FHE-based unbalanced PSI: one-sided simulation security against a malicious receiver without noise flooding, random oracle model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/787","sections":[{"content":"OPRF pre-processing for FHE-based unbalanced PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The sender samples a key k and replaces its set by \\( X' = \\{H(F_k(x))\\} \\); the receiver obtains \\( F_k(y) \\) for its items interactively, by sending \\( H(y)^\\beta \\) and unblinding \\( (H(y)^{\\beta\\alpha})^{1/\\beta} \\) (§4.3, p. 10; Figure 1, step 1, p. 13; Figure 2, step 7a, p. 14). Because \\( X' \\setminus Y' \\) is pseudorandom to the receiver, the number of items in each bin can be public, the evaluated polynomial need not be randomized, and the sender does not flood noise (§4.2, pp. 9-10). In the proof of Theorem 1 the simulator collects the receiver's random-oracle queries to H, extracts its input set when the receiver queries \\( H' \\), obtains \\( X \\cap Y^* \\) from the ideal functionality, pads it with random values to size \\( N_x \\), and plays the honest sender (pp. 15-16).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"HE-PAPER-2012-FV: the FHE scheme is BFV as implemented in SEAL v2.3.0-4 (§1.2, p. 2; §8, p. 18). The Diffie-Hellman OPRF of Jarecki and Liu (SCN 2010), which the protocol uses, has no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"chlr18-oprf-pre-processing-for","status":"published","subtitle":"Labeled PSI from Fully Homomorphic Encryption with Malicious Security","summary":"If both sets are first mapped through a Diffie-Hellman-based oblivious PRF \\( F_\\alpha(x) = H'(H(x)^\\alpha) \\) whose key the sender holds, and the FHE-based intersection of Chen, Laine, and Rindal is run on the outputs, the resulting protocol securely computes PSI with one-sided simulation against a malicious receiver whose set size is unchanged, and with privacy against a malicious sender, in the random oracle model assuming One-More-Gap-Diffie-Hellman and semantically secure FHE; since the sender's processed items are pseudorandom to the receiver, noise flooding, padding of the sender's bins, and the random multiplier of the evaluated polynomial are no longer needed, and the sender can reuse its processed set with many receivers.","title":"OPRF pre-processing for FHE-based unbalanced PSI: one-sided simulation security against a malicious receiver without noise flooding, random oracle model","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2018-CHLR-OPRF-MALICIOUS-RECEIVER"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-2D-CUCKOO-HASHING","keywords":["atomic-result","two-dimensional-cuckoo-hashing","inference-graph","combined-stash","permutation-based-hashing","psi-cat","failure-probability"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"2d-cuckoo-hashing","contribution_kind":"mechanism","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["circuit"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Hashing makes circuit-based PSI cheaper only if a common item lands where both parties look for it. Earlier constructions guaranteed this by letting one party copy each item into every candidate bin, which forced the bins, and the circuit, to grow. Pinkas, Schneider, Weinert, and Wieder arranged two tables of two subtables each so that one party fills whole sides while the other fills one subtable per side; the two choices always cross in exactly one place. They proved that placing items fails with probability inversely proportional to n when each table is somewhat more than four times the input size, and set concrete parameters for bins holding two items by running about \\( 2^{40} \\) simulated insertions. The resulting circuits were several times smaller than both earlier circuit-based approaches.","prior_boundary":"Hashing-based circuit PSI let one party use cuckoo hashing and the other simple hashing, because two cuckoo-hashing parties may place a common item in different bins; simple hashing forces bins of \\( O(\\log n / \\log\\log n) \\) items and \\( O(n \\log n / \\log\\log n) \\) comparisons (§2, p. 7). A related layout with items in two of three tables, by Amossen and Pagh, had no tight stash analysis, and its follow-up needed stashes that grew with n (§2, pp. 6-7).","significance_at_publication":"With two-item bins, no stash for Alice, a combined stash for Bob, and permutation-based hashing, the circuit has \\( 4 \\cdot 2.4n(\\sigma - \\log_2(2.4n) + 1) + s'n(\\sigma - 1) \\) AND gates, 1.8 to 3.8 times fewer than the best earlier circuit for \\( n = 2^{12} \\) to \\( 2^{20} \\) and fixed or arbitrary bitlength (§6.2, pp. 22-23; Table 3), and PSI-CAT ran 1.4 to 2.8 times faster than Circuit-Phasing (§6.3, p. 24; Table 4).","technical_delta":"Alice's items occupy both subtables of one side and Bob's items one subtable on each side, so a common item shares exactly one subtable (§5, pp. 13-14; Figures 2-3). Alice inserts with Algorithm 2 or, with two items per bin, Algorithm 3 (pp. 15-16). An inference-graph argument bounds the probability that an item is bad by \\( ((1 + \\varepsilon)/\\varepsilon)^3 \\cdot 2/m^2 \\) per item (Theorem 1, p. 32), and also yields the tight space bound for standard cuckoo hashing and a bound of \\( k^2 n \\) per table for k copies (Appendix A.4, p. 33)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-2D-CUCKOO-HASHING","keywords":["two-dimensional-cuckoo-hashing","inference-graph","combined-stash","permutation-based-hashing","psi-cat","failure-probability"],"limitations":["the \\( 2^{-40} \\) bound for \\( n \\ge 2^{13} \\) is extrapolated from experiments up to \\( n = 2^{12} \\) and the observed \\( n^{-3} \\) dependence, for which Appendix B gives only a heuristic explanation (§6.1, p. 20; Appendix B, pp. 33-34)","the authors see no way to make the construction secure against malicious parties, since item placement depends on the whole input set (§5.4, p. 18)","the PSI-CAT protocol remained 19 to 36 times slower than the specialized BaRK-OPRF PSI protocol, which computes only the intersection (§6.3, p. 25)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","qualifiers":["Theorem 1 covers single-item bins with tables larger than \\( 4(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) each (Appendix A.3, p. 32)","the two-item-bin variant has no analysis; its failure probability and stash size rest on simulations (§3.3, p. 10; Appendix A, p. 29)","the hash functions must be chosen independently of the inputs and cannot be replaced after a failure without leaking information (§3, pp. 7-8; §5.2, p. 16)","semi-honest parties (§2, p. 6)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.2 (p. 3); §2 (pp. 6-7); §3.3 (pp. 9-10); §5 and Figures 2-3 (pp. 13-14); §5.1 and Algorithms 2-3 (pp. 14-16); §5.2 and Table 1 (pp. 16-17); §6.1, Table 2, and Figure 4 (pp. 19-21); §6.2 and Table 3 (pp. 21-23); §6.3 and Tables 4-6 (pp. 23-26); Appendix A with Theorem 1 (pp. 29-33); Appendix B (pp. 33-34). IACR ePrint 2018/120, PDF dated 2019-01-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/120","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"In two-dimensional cuckoo hashing one party places each item in both subtables of one of two tables, using an iterative insertion that moves evicted items to the other table, and the other party places each item in one subtable of each table by standard cuckoo hashing, so that an item held by both meets in exactly one subtable; with one item per bin and tables of more than \\( 4(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) bins the probability that some item cannot be placed is \\( O(1/n) \\) (Theorem 1); the variant with two-item bins and tables of \\( 2.4n \\) bins was measured to fail with probability at most \\( 2^{-37} \\) for \\( n = 2^{12} \\) at 99.9% confidence, with failure scaling as \\( n^{-3} \\), which the authors take as at most \\( 2^{-40} \\) for \\( n \\ge 2^{13} \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Two-dimensional cuckoo hashing for circuit-based PSI: one shared bin per common item with \\( O(1/n) \\) failure above \\( 4(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) bins per table","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/120","sections":[{"content":"Two-dimensional cuckoo hashing","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Two tables \\( T_L \\) and \\( T_R \\) of \\( 4(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) entries each are used, each with two hash functions (§5.1, pp. 14-15). Bob maps an item to one location in each table by standard cuckoo hashing; Alice maps it to both locations in one table, evicting occupants and their second copies to the other table (Algorithm 2, p. 15). With bins of two items the tables have \\( 2.4n \\) bins of capacity two, the same memory (Algorithm 3, p. 16). For PSI, Bob keeps one combined stash instead of one per table, which roughly halves the stash comparisons (§5.2 and Table 1, p. 17), and permutation-based hashing shortens the stored items (§5.2, pp. 16-17).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-GMW: the measured protocol evaluates the circuit with GMW, which the authors chose because GMW ran faster than Yao in earlier experiments (§6.3, p. 24; §2, p. 6). MPC-PAPER-2015-ABY and MPC-CONTRIB-2015-ABY-MIXED: the implementation is part of the ABY framework (§1.2, p. 3; §6.3, p. 24). MPC-PAPER-1987-GMW: GMW protocol, cited as [GMW87], for the measured protocol (§2, p. 6; Table 4, p. 25).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"psww18-two-dimensional-cuckoo-hashi","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI via Cuckoo Hashing","summary":"In two-dimensional cuckoo hashing one party places each item in both subtables of one of two tables, using an iterative insertion that moves evicted items to the other table, and the other party places each item in one subtable of each table by standard cuckoo hashing, so that an item held by both meets in exactly one subtable; with one item per bin and tables of more than \\( 4(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) bins the probability that some item cannot be placed is \\( O(1/n) \\) (Theorem 1); the variant with two-item bins and tables of \\( 2.4n \\) bins was measured to fail with probability at most \\( 2^{-37} \\) for \\( n = 2^{12} \\) at 99.9% confidence, with failure scaling as \\( n^{-3} \\), which the authors take as at most \\( 2^{-40} \\) for \\( n \\ge 2^{13} \\).","title":"Two-dimensional cuckoo hashing for circuit-based PSI: one shared bin per common item with \\( O(1/n) \\) failure above \\( 4(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) bins per table","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-2D-CUCKOO-HASHING"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-ALMOST-LINEAR-CIRCUIT","keywords":["atomic-result","mirror-cuckoo-hashing","circuit-based-psi","stash","almost-linear","symmetric-functions"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"almost-linear-circuit","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["circuit"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Circuit-based PSI pays for every comparison inside a secure computation, so the number of comparisons sets its cost. Sorting networks gave \\( n \\log n \\) comparisons, and the hashing approach of Circuit-Phasing, which pairs cuckoo hashing on one side with simple hashing on the other, gave slightly fewer, because simple hashing leaves logarithmically full bins. Pinkas, Schneider, Weinert, and Wieder let both parties use cuckoo hashing and resolved the problem that two parties may place a common item in different places by using a second set of tables whose hash functions mirror the first. Every common item then meets in exactly one bin, bins hold one item, and only a slowly growing stash must be compared with everything. Proving that such a stash keeps failure negligible completed an almost linear construction.","prior_boundary":"The best circuit constructions for PSI computed \\( O(n \\log n) \\) comparisons (sort-compare-shuffle of Huang, Evans, and Katz) or \\( O(n \\log n / \\log\\log n) \\) comparisons (Circuit-Phasing of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner), the latter because one party maps items by cuckoo hashing and the other by simple hashing into bins of \\( O(\\log n / \\log\\log n) \\) items (abstract, p. 1; §1.4, p. 5; §2, p. 7). The known stash bound \\( O(n^{-(s+1)}) \\) for cuckoo hashing was stated only for constant stash size s (§3.3, p. 9).","significance_at_publication":"Against the best known circuit constructions named in the abstract, the number of comparisons becomes arbitrarily close to linear, so that symmetric functions such as cardinality, threshold tests, or sums over the intersection need circuits of total size \\( \\omega(n) \\) instead of \\( O(n \\log n) \\) (abstract, p. 1; §1.3, p. 4; §6.2, p. 22).","technical_delta":"Two sets of tables are used, the right set reusing the left hash functions with the lower row in mirror order; Bob cuckoo-hashes each item into one subtable of each of the four tables, and Alice cuckoo-hashes into the two left rows and moves every item that lands in different columns to the right tables without new insertions (§4.1, pp. 10-12; Figure 1 and Algorithm 1). Appendix C shows that the Kirsch-Mitzenmacher-Wieder bound also holds for non-constant stash size, so a stash of size \\( \\omega(1) \\), for example \\( \\log\\log n \\), gives negligible failure (Appendix C, p. 34; §4.1, p. 13)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-ALMOST-LINEAR-CIRCUIT","keywords":["mirror-cuckoo-hashing","circuit-based-psi","stash","almost-linear","symmetric-functions"],"limitations":["the analysis is asymptotic and not intended to be tight (§4, p. 10)","concretely the mirror construction is worse than the 2D construction and was not implemented or timed (§6.2, p. 22; §6.3, p. 24)","no security against malicious adversaries (§5.4, p. 18)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties (§2, p. 6)","both sets have n items of bitlength σ; the parties agree on a symmetric function f and a circuit for it (§2, p. 5)","the hash functions are chosen at random and independently of the inputs (§3, pp. 7-8)","any symmetric function of the intersection; non-symmetric functions or the intersection itself need an \\( O(n \\log n) \\) shuffle (§1.3, p. 4)","the stash bound for non-constant s is derived from Theorem 5.5 of Wieder's survey (Appendix C, p. 34)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.2 (p. 3); §1.3 (pp. 3-4); §1.4 (p. 5); §2 (pp. 6-7); §3.3 (p. 9); §4 (pp. 10-13), Figure 1 (p. 11), Algorithm 1 (p. 12); §6.2 (p. 22); Table 3 (p. 23); Appendix C (p. 34). IACR ePrint 2018/120, PDF dated 2019-01-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/120","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"For two parties with n items of σ bits each, mirror cuckoo hashing places every item of each party into four standard cuckoo tables of total size \\( 8(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) so that an item held by both meets in exactly one subtable, with stashes of size \\( \\omega(1) \\); a circuit that compares items bin by bin and compares every stashed item with all items of the other party then computes any symmetric function of the intersection with \\( \\omega(n) \\) comparisons, that is \\( o(f(n)) \\) for every \\( f(n) \\in \\omega(n) \\), and circuit size \\( \\omega(\\sigma n) \\), against \\( O(\\sigma n \\log n) \\) for sort-compare-shuffle and \\( O(\\sigma n \\log n / \\log\\log n) \\) for Circuit-Phasing, with failure probability negligible in n.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Circuit-based PSI with \\( \\omega(n) \\) comparisons and negligible failure probability from mirror cuckoo hashing, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/120","sections":[{"content":"Mirror cuckoo hashing for circuit-based PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The circuit (§4.2, p. 13) compares, for each bin, the item Alice mapped there with the item Bob mapped there, and compares every stashed item of either party with all items of the other. Mirror hashing guarantees that a common item is input to exactly one comparison, and the comparisons number \\( \\omega(n) \\) (§4.2, p. 13). The tables have total size \\( 8(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\); Bob keeps four stashes and Alice two (§4.1, p. 12). Concretely the circuit has \\( 4 \\cdot 2.4n(\\sigma - \\log_2(2.4n) + 1) + 3s'n(\\sigma - 1) \\) AND gates with a combined stash \\( s' \\le 2s \\) (§6.2, p. 22).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-YAO and MPC-COMP-EVAL-GMW: the circuit may be evaluated with Yao's protocol or GMW, both named as the two main approaches (§2, p. 6).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"psww18-circuit-based-psi-with","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI via Cuckoo Hashing","summary":"For two parties with n items of σ bits each, mirror cuckoo hashing places every item of each party into four standard cuckoo tables of total size \\( 8(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) so that an item held by both meets in exactly one subtable, with stashes of size \\( \\omega(1) \\); a circuit that compares items bin by bin and compares every stashed item with all items of the other party then computes any symmetric function of the intersection with \\( \\omega(n) \\) comparisons, that is \\( o(f(n)) \\) for every \\( f(n) \\in \\omega(n) \\), and circuit size \\( \\omega(\\sigma n) \\), against \\( O(\\sigma n \\log n) \\) for sort-compare-shuffle and \\( O(\\sigma n \\log n / \\log\\log n) \\) for Circuit-Phasing, with failure probability negligible in n.","title":"Circuit-based PSI with \\( \\omega(n) \\) comparisons and negligible failure probability from mirror cuckoo hashing, semi-honest two-party","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2018","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSWW-ALMOST-LINEAR-CIRCUIT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-BIT-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-OT-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","ot-based-psi","bch-code","one-out-of-n-ot","kolesnikov-kumaresan","permutation-based-hashing","cuckoo-hashing","bit-length-independence"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"bit-length-independent-ot-psi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing","permutation_based_hashing","simple_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"In the authors' earlier OT-based protocol, each bin's oblivious PRF used transfers proportional to the item length, so longer identifiers cost more. Kolesnikov and coauthors had just removed that dependence with a pseudorandom code. Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner reached the same goal differently in their journal version: they kept a genuine error-correcting code and fixed one BCH code with 512-bit codewords, large enough that, after shortening items with permutation-based hashing, sets of up to about 100 billion items need no larger code. Communication ends up slightly above the pseudorandom-code version but, the authors note, the structured code allows a published extension to malicious receivers. On a single platform comparing many paradigms, it ran fastest, with communication close to that of the public-key protocols.","prior_boundary":"The authors' conference protocols scaled linearly in the bit length of the inputs (§1.4, 'Optimizations', p. 7). Kolesnikov et al. had replaced the error-correcting code of the implicit OPRF by a pseudorandom code of 424-448 bits, making its cost independent of σ (§1.2, p. 4; Table 1, p. 5).","significance_at_publication":"The authors report communication 1.10-1.15 times that of Kolesnikov et al. and note that their code choice permits the malicious-receiver extension of Orrù, Orsini, and Scholl, which the pseudorandom code does not (§1.2, pp. 4-5); in their benchmarks it is the fastest protocol, with about the communication of public-key protocols (§6.2.2, p. 28), and it intersects two billion-element sets in 34.2 hours (§6.2.4, p. 31).","technical_delta":"The OPRF keeps an error-correcting code but fixes one linear BCH code that encodes up to \\( 2^{77} \\) words into 512-bit codewords with distance 129 (§2.2.3, p. 11); with permutation-based hashing this covers sets of up to \\( 2^{37} \\) elements for \\( \\lambda = 40 \\) regardless of σ (§2.2.3, p. 11; §5, pp. 21-23; Protocol 3, p. 22). Smaller custom codes of 255-271 bits would suffice when \\( \\sigma - \\log_2(n) \\) is 8 to 13 (Table 1, p. 5)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-BIT-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-OT-PSI","keywords":["ot-based-psi","bch-code","one-out-of-n-ot","kolesnikov-kumaresan","permutation-based-hashing","cuckoo-hashing","bit-length-independence"],"limitations":["communication is 1.10-1.15 times that of Kolesnikov et al. for the fixed 512-bit code (§1.2, p. 5)","smaller custom-tailored codes would lower communication for small \\( \\sigma - \\log_2(n) \\), but generating them is described as a non-trivial problem (§1.2, p. 5)","no security against malicious parties is claimed (§2.1, p. 8)","for small sets it is slower than the Bloom-filter protocols because of the base OTs (§6.2.2, pp. 28-29)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","qualifiers":["semi-honest P1 and P2; the proof assumes an ideal 1-out-of-N random OT functionality (§5, 'Security', p. 23)","efficient random OT extension relies on a function modeled as a random oracle (§2.1, p. 8; §2.2.2, p. 10)","only P2 learns the intersection (Protocol 3, 'Output', p. 22)","the 1-out-of-N OT extension needs 512 base OTs, four times as many as 1-out-of-2 OT extension, which dominates for small sets (§2.2.3, p. 11; §6.2.2, pp. 28-29)","the OT-based protocol first appeared in the USENIX 2014 and 2015 versions; independence of σ is an addition of this version (§1.4, p. 7; §5, p. 21)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.2 'OT-Based PSI' and Table 1 (pp. 4-5); §1.3 'A Novel OT-Based PSI Protocol' (pp. 5-6); §1.4 'Optimizations' (p. 7); §2.1 'The random oracle model' (p. 8); §2.2.3 and Protocol 2 (pp. 10-11); §3.3 (pp. 17-18); §5 and Protocol 3, 'Efficiency', 'Correctness', 'Security' (pp. 21-23); Table 8 (p. 26); §6.2.2 (pp. 27-29); Tables 9-10 (p. 29); §6.2.4 (pp. 30-31). IACR ePrint 2016/930, PDF dated 2019-07-28.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/930","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"P1 hashes its items into b bins by simple hashing with k hash functions and P2 by cuckoo hashing with a stash of size s, with permutation-based hashing shortening stored items to \\( \\mu = \\sigma - \\log b + \\log k \\) bits; for each bin and stash position the parties run one 1-out-of-\\( 2^{\\mu} \\) random OT of the Kolesnikov-Kumaresan OT extension with a fixed linear \\( [2^{77}, 512, 129] \\) BCH code, which serves as an OPRF; P1 evaluates the OPRF on its own items and sends the permuted outputs, and P2 outputs its items whose outputs appear. Against semi-honest adversaries, P2 sends \\( 512(s + b) \\) bits and P1 sends about \\( k \\cdot n_1 \\cdot \\ell \\) bits with \\( \\ell = \\lambda + \\log_2(kn_1) + \\log_2(n_2) \\), independent of σ for sets of up to \\( 2^{37} \\) items at \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), and P1 performs \\( (k + s)n_1 \\) correlation-robust function evaluations.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"OT-based PSI with communication independent of item bit length for up to \\( 2^{37} \\) items, from per-bin OPRFs on 1-out-of-N random OT over a 512-bit BCH code, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/930","sections":[{"content":"Bit-length-independent OT-based PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"For each bin i, P1 obtains a random OPRF key \\( t_i \\), which corresponds to the values \\( q_i \\) and s of the 1-out-of-N OT extension, and P2 obtains \\( F_{t_i}(T2[i]) \\); P1 computes \\( M1[i][j] = H(q_i \\oplus (s \\wedge c_{v_j})) \\) for each of its items \\( v_j \\) in bin i, collects all values, permutes them, and sends them; stash positions are handled by evaluating the OPRF on P1's whole set (Protocol 3, p. 22; §5, p. 22). Mask length \\( \\ell = \\lambda + \\log_2(kn_1) + \\log_2(n_2) \\) gives correctness with probability \\( 1 - 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (§5, 'Correctness', p. 23).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: the 1-out-of-2 OT extension of Ishai et al., on which the 1-out-of-N OT extension builds (Protocol 1, p. 10; §2.2.3, p. 10). The Kolesnikov-Kumaresan 1-out-of-N OT extension, which this protocol instantiates (§2.2.3, Protocol 2, pp. 10-11), has no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"psz18-ot-based-psi-with","status":"published","subtitle":"Scalable Private Set Intersection Based on OT Extension","summary":"P1 hashes its items into b bins by simple hashing with k hash functions and P2 by cuckoo hashing with a stash of size s, with permutation-based hashing shortening stored items to \\( \\mu = \\sigma - \\log b + \\log k \\) bits; for each bin and stash position the parties run one 1-out-of-\\( 2^{\\mu} \\) random OT of the Kolesnikov-Kumaresan OT extension with a fixed linear \\( [2^{77}, 512, 129] \\) BCH code, which serves as an OPRF; P1 evaluates the OPRF on its own items and sends the permuted outputs, and P2 outputs its items whose outputs appear. Against semi-honest adversaries, P2 sends \\( 512(s + b) \\) bits and P1 sends about \\( k \\cdot n_1 \\cdot \\ell \\) bits with \\( \\ell = \\lambda + \\log_2(kn_1) + \\log_2(n_2) \\), independent of σ for sets of up to \\( 2^{37} \\) items at \\( \\lambda = 40 \\), and P1 performs \\( (k + s)n_1 \\) correlation-robust function evaluations.","title":"OT-based PSI with communication independent of item bit length for up to \\( 2^{37} \\) items, from per-bin OPRFs on 1-out-of-N random OT over a 512-bit BCH code, semi-honest","type":"result","venue":"ACM Transactions on Privacy and Security 21(2)","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-BIT-LENGTH-INDEPENDENT-OT-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-HASHING-PARAMETERS","keywords":["atomic-result","cuckoo-hashing","simple-hashing","stash-size","bin-size","hashing-failure","parameter-estimation","unequal-set-sizes"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"hashing-parameters","contribution_kind":"measurement_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"technique":["cuckoo_hashing","simple_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Hashing items into bins is what makes OT-based and circuit-based PSI run in linear time, but its parameters decide both correctness and cost: bins that overflow leak information or lose items, while oversized bins waste comparisons. Earlier work had given only asymptotic bounds for these schemes, with constants too vague to pick a concrete setting. Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner computed the bounds numerically and ran about a billion cuckoo-hashing trials per configuration, extrapolating to a failure probability of two to the minus forty. Their tables list bin sizes, stash sizes, and bin counts, show that three hash functions work best when the parties' sets are equally large, and give low-stash choices for a small client set against a large server set, which other protocols adopted as well.","prior_boundary":"Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas, and the later journal version with Hazay, suggested hashing to bins for PSI but analyzed its parameters only asymptotically and left setting them to future work (§1.3, p. 5; §3, p. 12); cuckoo hashing with a stash fails with probability \\( O(n^{1-c(s+1)}) \\) with unclear constants, and the utilization gains for more than two hash functions were known only asymptotically (§3.2.2, p. 15).","significance_at_publication":"The parameters let PSI protocols meet a stated hashing failure probability with concrete costs, show that three hash functions minimize comparisons for equal set sizes (§3.2.2, p. 16), and give low-stash settings for unequal set sizes (§3.2.2, p. 17; Table 11, p. 30); the authors note that the protocols of Kolesnikov et al. and of Orrù et al. benefit from this analysis (§1.2, p. 5).","technical_delta":"The paper computes the bin-size bound with 1,024-bit floating-point precision and measures cuckoo-hashing failures over \\( 2^{30} \\) repetitions, extrapolating by linear or logarithmic regression or a 4/3 margin, and tabulates trade-offs among stash size, number of hash functions, and number of bins (§3.1.2-3.2.2, pp. 13-18; Tables 3-7, Figures 1-2)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-HASHING-PARAMETERS","keywords":["cuckoo-hashing","simple-hashing","stash-size","bin-size","hashing-failure","parameter-estimation","unequal-set-sizes"],"limitations":["the \\( 2^{-40} \\) values are extrapolated from \\( 2^{-30} \\) experiments, because \\( 2^{40} \\) trials were too costly (§3, p. 13; §3.2.2, pp. 15-17)","the cuckoo-hashing parameters are empirical estimates, not proved bounds (§3.2.2, p. 15)","the unequal-set parameters were computed for client sets of \\( 2^{8} \\) and \\( 2^{12} \\) items, while the text of §6.2.3 mentions \\( 2^{8} \\) and \\( 2^{16} \\) (Table 4, p. 14; §6.2.3, p. 29)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","qualifiers":["the hashing failure parameter η is kept separate from the statistical security parameter λ (§3, p. 13)","cuckoo hashing is used by P2 and simple hashing with the same k functions by P1, who pads bins with dummy items (§3.2.1, p. 14)","part of the analysis appeared in the conference versions, which bounded the failure for one parameter set; the trade-offs and unequal-set parameters are additions of this version (§1.4, p. 7)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.3 'Concrete Parameter Estimation for Hashing' (p. 5); §1.4 'Extended Hashing Parameter Analysis' (p. 7); §3 intro (pp. 12-13); §3.1.2, Eq. (3), Tables 3-4 (pp. 13-14); §3.2.1-3.2.2, Figure 1, Tables 5-6 (pp. 14-16); 'Adjusting the Number of Bins', Figure 2, Table 7 (pp. 17-18); Table 11 (p. 30). IACR ePrint 2016/930, PDF dated 2019-07-28.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/930","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"By evaluating the balls-into-bins bound numerically and running \\( 2^{30} \\) cuckoo-hashing trials, the paper gives concrete hashing parameters for PSI: maximum simple-hashing bin sizes for n items in n bins (16 to 20 for \\( n = 2^{8} \\) to \\( 2^{24} \\) at failure probability \\( 2^{-40} \\)) and for unequal sets; stash sizes for two hash functions and \\( 2.4n \\) bins (12, 6, 4, 3, 2 for \\( n = 2^{8}, 2^{12}, \\ldots, 2^{24} \\) at \\( 2^{-40} \\)); the fewest bins needing no stash for \\( k = 3, 4, 5 \\) hash functions (\\( 1.27n \\), \\( 1.09n \\), \\( 1.05n \\) at \\( 2^{-40} \\)); and, for 256 items and two hash functions, the bins needed for stash sizes 0 to 4 (\\( 2{,}500n \\) down to \\( 3.8n \\) at \\( 2^{-40} \\)). Three hash functions give the fewest comparisons for equal set sizes. Values for \\( 2^{-40} \\) are extrapolated from \\( 2^{-30} \\) experiments.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Concrete simple- and cuckoo-hashing parameters for PSI at hashing failure probability \\( 2^{-30} \\) and \\( 2^{-40} \\), covering bin sizes, stash sizes, hash-function count, and bins for unequal set sizes","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","year":2018},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/930","sections":[{"content":"Concrete hashing parameters for PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Simple hashing: maximum bin sizes \\( \\mathrm{max}_b \\) from Eq. (3) for \\( n = b \\) (Table 3, p. 14) and for \\( n = 2n_1 \\) items into \\( b = 2.4n_2 \\) bins (Table 4, p. 14). Cuckoo hashing: stash sizes for \\( k = 2 \\) and \\( b = 2.4n \\) (Figure 1 and Table 5, p. 16); \\( \\varepsilon_{\\min} \\) for \\( k = 3, 4, 5 \\) without a stash and the resulting \\( \\mathrm{max}_b \\) for P1 (Table 6, p. 16); bins for fixed stash sizes when \\( n = 256 \\) (Figure 2 and Table 7, p. 18).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"psz18-concrete-simple-and-cuckoo","status":"published","subtitle":"Scalable Private Set Intersection Based on OT Extension","summary":"By evaluating the balls-into-bins bound numerically and running \\( 2^{30} \\) cuckoo-hashing trials, the paper gives concrete hashing parameters for PSI: maximum simple-hashing bin sizes for n items in n bins (16 to 20 for \\( n = 2^{8} \\) to \\( 2^{24} \\) at failure probability \\( 2^{-40} \\)) and for unequal sets; stash sizes for two hash functions and \\( 2.4n \\) bins (12, 6, 4, 3, 2 for \\( n = 2^{8}, 2^{12}, \\ldots, 2^{24} \\) at \\( 2^{-40} \\)); the fewest bins needing no stash for \\( k = 3, 4, 5 \\) hash functions (\\( 1.27n \\), \\( 1.09n \\), \\( 1.05n \\) at \\( 2^{-40} \\)); and, for 256 items and two hash functions, the bins needed for stash sizes 0 to 4 (\\( 2{,}500n \\) down to \\( 3.8n \\) at \\( 2^{-40} \\)). Three hash functions give the fewest comparisons for equal set sizes. Values for \\( 2^{-40} \\) are extrapolated from \\( 2^{-30} \\) experiments.","title":"Concrete simple- and cuckoo-hashing parameters for PSI at hashing failure probability \\( 2^{-30} \\) and \\( 2^{-40} \\), covering bin sizes, stash sizes, hash-function count, and bins for unequal set sizes","type":"result","venue":"ACM Transactions on Privacy and Security 21(2)","year":2018,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2018-PSZ-HASHING-PARAMETERS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","threshold-psi","intersection-cardinality-testing","polynomial-sparsity-test","hankel-matrix","noisy-polynomial-addition","ole","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"ahe-threshold-psi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["threshold"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["additively_homomorphic_encryption","ole","polynomial_sparsity_test","set_reconciliation"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Threshold PSI from fully homomorphic encryption reached communication close to the lower bound but was computationally out of reach, while generic garbled circuits paid a cubic price for solving the interpolation system. Ghosh and Simkin separated the problem in two. First the parties test, privately, whether their sets are close: writing each set as a sum of monomials makes the difference polynomial sparse exactly when the sets nearly agree, and sparsity reduces to whether a Hankel matrix is singular, which can be checked under additively homomorphic encryption. Only if the test passes do they compute randomized evaluations from which each party recovers its own exclusive elements and nothing about the other's. The second step is already cheap, so the cardinality test became the remaining bottleneck.","prior_boundary":"Threshold PSI protocols of Freedman-Nissim-Pinkas, Hallgren-Orlandi-Sabelfeld, Ghosh-Nilges, Pinkas et al., and Zhao-Chow first computed PSI or its cardinality and so communicated at least linearly in the smaller set (§1, p. 2). Sublinear communication was available only from FHE, or from generic garbled circuits at \\( \\tilde O(t^3) \\) because interpolation needs Gaussian elimination (§3, pp. 8-9).","significance_at_publication":"The authors present it as the first threshold PSI protocols whose communication depends mainly on t and only logarithmically on n, from assumptions weaker than FHE and with less computation than the FHE protocol (abstract, p. 1; §3, p. 7). Since the intersection step already costs \\( \\tilde O(t) \\), an \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) cardinality test would give \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) threshold PSI (§6.3, p. 14; §9, p. 19).","technical_delta":"Threshold PSI is split into a cardinality test and an intersection step (§1.2, pp. 2-4). The test uses a monomial set encoding, so sparsity of \\( p_A - p_B \\) measures the symmetric difference, and checks singularity of an encrypted Hankel matrix (Theorem 1, p. 9; Figure 5 and Theorem 3, p. 11). The intersection step uses generic computation only to produce noisy evaluations, which the parties interpolate in the clear; Lemma 1 of Kissner and Song makes the noise uniformly random (Figure 7, p. 13; Theorem 4, p. 14; Theorem 5, p. 16)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI","keywords":["threshold-psi","intersection-cardinality-testing","polynomial-sparsity-test","hankel-matrix","noisy-polynomial-addition","ole","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"limitations":["the authors do not claim practical efficiency (§1.1, p. 2; §9, p. 20)","the \\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\) cost comes from the private cardinality test; the intersection step alone is \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) (§6.3, p. 14)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","qualifiers":["static passive adversaries in the UC framework (§2.2, p. 6)","Theorem 4 is in the \\( (F_{\\mathrm{PICT}}, F_{\\mathrm{NPA}}) \\)-hybrid model and Theorem 5 in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{OLE}} \\)-hybrid model (pp. 14, 16); the \\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\) bound holds in the plain model after instantiation (Lemmas 3-4, pp. 12, 14)","the cardinality test needs a field \\( \\mathbb{F}_q \\) with \\( q > (4t^2 + 2t)(p - 1)2^{\\kappa} \\) and fails with probability at most \\( 2^{-\\kappa} \\) (Theorem 1, p. 9)","the parties' sets have equal size n; unequal sizes are handled by padding, at most doubling the threshold (§2, p. 4; §6.4, p. 15)","both parties learn the intersection or ⊥ (Figure 6, p. 12)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 2); §1.2 (pp. 2-4); §2.1, Lemmas 1-2 (p. 5); §3 (pp. 7-9); §4, Figure 3, and Theorem 1 (pp. 9-10); §5, Theorems 2-3, Figures 4-5, and Lemma 3 (pp. 10-12); §6, Figures 6-7, Theorem 4, and Lemma 4 (pp. 12-14); §7, Figures 9-10, and Theorem 5 (pp. 15-17); §9 (pp. 19-20). IACR ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Against passive adversaries, two-party threshold PSI is realized with communication \\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\) in the plain model from IND-CPA additively homomorphic encryption and OLE: a private intersection cardinality test encodes each set as the polynomial with one monomial \\( x^a \\) per element, so the difference of the two encodings has at most \\( 2t \\) monomials exactly when the symmetric difference is at most \\( 2t \\), and tests this with the Hankel-matrix sparsity test of Grigorescu, Jung, and Rubinfeld under encryption and the singularity test of Kiltz, Mohassel, Weinreb, and Franklin (\\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\)); if the test passes, an OLE-based noisy polynomial addition gives both parties \\( 3t + 1 \\) evaluations of \\( p_A R_1 + p_B R_2 \\) for random degree-t polynomials \\( R_1, R_2 \\), from which each interpolates its exclusive elements and hence the intersection (\\( \\tilde O(t) \\)).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Two-party threshold PSI with \\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\) communication from additively homomorphic encryption and OLE, via private intersection cardinality testing","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","sections":[{"content":"Threshold PSI from additively homomorphic encryption","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"\\( \\Pi_{\\mathrm{TPSI}} \\) (Figure 7, p. 13): the parties call \\( F_{\\mathrm{PICT}} \\) and output ⊥ if it returns different; otherwise they call \\( F_{\\mathrm{NPA}} \\) with \\( \\ell = 3t + 1 \\) and receive \\( V(\\alpha_i) = p_A(\\alpha_i)R_1(\\alpha_i) + p_B(\\alpha_i)R_2(\\alpha_i) \\). Alice divides by \\( p_A(\\alpha_i) \\), interpolates a rational function whose denominator is \\( p_{A \\setminus B} \\), and removes its roots from \\( S_A \\); Bob does the same. \\( \\Pi_{\\mathrm{PICT}} \\) (Figure 5, p. 11) sends Alice's encrypted Hankel matrix, Bob subtracts his own homomorphically, and \\( F_{\\mathrm{INV}} \\) decides singularity. \\( \\Pi_{\\mathrm{NPA}} \\) (Figure 10, p. 17) uses \\( 2\\ell \\) OLE calls, adapting a protocol of Ghosh and Nilges (§7.2, p. 15).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"HE-RESULT-1999-PAILLIER-PRACTICAL-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-PUBLIC-KEY-ENCRYPTION: Paillier and its Damgård-Jurik generalization are named as possible instantiations of the additively homomorphic encryption (§2.3, p. 6).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"gs19-threshold-psi-from-additive","status":"published","subtitle":"The Communication Complexity of Threshold Private Set Intersection","summary":"Against passive adversaries, two-party threshold PSI is realized with communication \\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\) in the plain model from IND-CPA additively homomorphic encryption and OLE: a private intersection cardinality test encodes each set as the polynomial with one monomial \\( x^a \\) per element, so the difference of the two encodings has at most \\( 2t \\) monomials exactly when the symmetric difference is at most \\( 2t \\), and tests this with the Hankel-matrix sparsity test of Grigorescu, Jung, and Rubinfeld under encryption and the singularity test of Kiltz, Mohassel, Weinreb, and Franklin (\\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\)); if the test passes, an OLE-based noisy polynomial addition gives both parties \\( 3t + 1 \\) evaluations of \\( p_A R_1 + p_B R_2 \\) for random degree-t polynomials \\( R_1, R_2 \\), from which each interpolates its exclusive elements and hence the intersection (\\( \\tilde O(t) \\)).","title":"Two-party threshold PSI with \\( \\tilde O(t^2) \\) communication from additively homomorphic encryption and OLE, via private intersection cardinality testing","type":"result","venue":"CRYPTO 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-AHE-THRESHOLD-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-FHE-UPPER-BOUND","keywords":["atomic-result","threshold-psi","fhe","set-reconciliation","rational-function-interpolation","upper-bound"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"fhe-upper-bound","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["threshold"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption","set_reconciliation"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Set reconciliation, a technique from distributed systems, lets two parties learn the union of their sets by exchanging a number of polynomial evaluations proportional to how much the sets differ, but it reveals both sets' differences. Ghosh and Simkin placed it under fully homomorphic encryption: one party sends encrypted evaluations of the polynomial whose roots are its elements, and the other interpolates the ratio of the two set polynomials on ciphertexts, verifies the result at a random point, and returns only the part describing the first party's exclusive elements. Communication becomes linear in the threshold and logarithmic in the field size, close to the lower bound, although interpolating homomorphically is expensive enough that the authors treat the protocol as a feasibility result.","prior_boundary":"Before this work all threshold PSI protocols had communication \\( \\Omega(n) \\) in the set size (abstract, p. 1; §1, p. 2). The set reconciliation protocol of Minsky, Trachtenberg, and Zippel computes a set union with communication proportional to the symmetric difference but is not private (§1.2, p. 3).","significance_at_publication":"It is an upper bound within polylogarithmic factors of the \\( \\Omega(t) \\) lower bound, establishing that threshold PSI communication can depend on the threshold rather than the set size; the authors consider it of mainly theoretical interest because of the homomorphic interpolation cost (§3, pp. 7-8; Figure 2, p. 7).","technical_delta":"The set reconciliation protocol is run under fully homomorphic encryption, so the rational-function interpolation and the consistency check at z happen on ciphertexts and only the numerator encoding Alice's exclusive elements is returned (§3, p. 8)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-FHE-UPPER-BOUND","keywords":["threshold-psi","fhe","set-reconciliation","rational-function-interpolation","upper-bound"],"limitations":["not claimed practical: homomorphic rational-function interpolation is computationally heavy (§3, p. 8)","relies on fully homomorphic encryption; whether \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) is possible from weaker assumptions is left open (§9, p. 19)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","qualifiers":["a high-level sketch; the details are left to the reader (§3, p. 8)","assumes FHE ciphertexts larger than plaintexts by at most a constant factor and elements from \\( \\mathbb{F}_p \\) (§3, p. 8)","security follows from the security of FHE; known instantiations rest on lattice assumptions such as LWE or ring-LWE (§3, p. 8)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 2); §1.2 (pp. 3-4); §3, upper bound paragraph and Figure 2 (pp. 7-8). IACR ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Against passive adversaries, two parties can run threshold PSI with communication \\( O(t \\log p) \\) bits for set elements in \\( \\mathbb{F}_p \\), that is \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) independent of the set size n: Alice sends FHE encryptions of her set polynomial at \\( 2t + 1 \\) points plus a random point z, Bob homomorphically interpolates the rational function \\( p_A/p_B \\) from the first \\( 2t \\) points, checks the candidate at z, and returns t ciphertexts holding the numerator \\( p_{A \\setminus B} \\) or ⊥, from which Alice computes the intersection.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Two-party threshold PSI with \\( O(t \\log p) \\) communication from fully homomorphic encryption, almost matching the \\( \\Omega(t) \\) bound","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","sections":[{"content":"\\( \\tilde O(t) \\) threshold PSI from FHE","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Alice encodes \\( S_A \\) as \\( p_A(x) = \\prod(x - a_i) \\) and sends \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(p_A(\\alpha_1)), \\ldots, \\mathrm{Enc}(p_A(\\alpha_{2t})) \\), \\( \\mathrm{Enc}(p_A(z)) \\), and z. Bob interpolates \\( p_A/p_B \\), whose numerator and denominator have gcd 1, on ciphertexts, and returns \\( p_{A \\setminus B} \\) if the candidate agrees with \\( p_A(z)/p_B(z) \\) and ⊥ otherwise. Total communication is \\( 2t + 1 \\) ciphertexts from Alice and t from Bob (§3, p. 8).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"gs19-two-party-threshold-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"The Communication Complexity of Threshold Private Set Intersection","summary":"Against passive adversaries, two parties can run threshold PSI with communication \\( O(t \\log p) \\) bits for set elements in \\( \\mathbb{F}_p \\), that is \\( \\tilde O(t) \\) independent of the set size n: Alice sends FHE encryptions of her set polynomial at \\( 2t + 1 \\) points plus a random point z, Bob homomorphically interpolates the rational function \\( p_A/p_B \\) from the first \\( 2t \\) points, checks the candidate at z, and returns t ciphertexts holding the numerator \\( p_{A \\setminus B} \\) or ⊥, from which Alice computes the intersection.","title":"Two-party threshold PSI with \\( O(t \\log p) \\) communication from fully homomorphic encryption, almost matching the \\( \\Omega(t) \\) bound","type":"result","venue":"CRYPTO 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-FHE-UPPER-BOUND"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-LOWER-BOUND","keywords":["atomic-result","lower-bound","communication-complexity","threshold-psi","set-disjointness","symmetric-difference"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"lower-bound","contribution_kind":"boundary_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["threshold"],"parties":["two_party"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The communication of ordinary set intersection had long been known to grow at least linearly with the sets, through the hardness of deciding set disjointness. Threshold PSI asks for less: the intersection is released only when the two sets differ in at most t elements. Ghosh and Simkin asked what that relaxation is worth and showed that it cannot be worth everything. A threshold protocol that communicated less than order t bits would, after both parties pad small sets with the same public dummy elements, compute an ordinary intersection too cheaply. The bound depends only on t, leaving room for protocols whose cost is independent of the set size, and it frames the upper bounds of the same paper.","prior_boundary":"Freedman, Nissim, and Pinkas observed that the \\( \\Theta(n) \\) communication complexity of set disjointness yields an \\( \\Omega(n) \\) lower bound for any PSI protocol on sets of size n, which extends to intersection cardinality (§1, p. 1; §3, p. 8). All earlier threshold PSI protocols started from PSI and communicated at least linearly in the smaller set (§1, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"With the FHE-based protocol it gives the first characterization of the communication complexity of threshold PSI, showing that communication depending on t rather than n is not ruled out (§1.1, p. 2; §3, p. 7; Figure 2, p. 7).","technical_delta":"The bound is restated in terms of the threshold: padding both inputs with a common set of dummy elements turns any threshold PSI protocol into a PSI protocol for the unpadded sets, so threshold PSI inherits an \\( \\Omega(t) \\) bound, measured by the symmetric set difference rather than the set size (§3, pp. 7-8)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-LOWER-BOUND","keywords":["lower-bound","communication-complexity","threshold-psi","set-disjointness","symmetric-difference"],"limitations":["the bound concerns protocols that output the intersection when the threshold condition holds, not cardinality testing alone (§3, p. 8)","it is stated for two parties (§3, p. 7)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","qualifiers":["unconditional: the bound holds for any protocol, without computational assumptions (§3, p. 7)","t is an upper bound on the size of the symmetric set difference (§3, p. 7)","the argument is a sketch that relies on the \\( \\Omega(n) \\) bound for set intersection derived from set disjointness (§3, p. 8)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1 (pp. 1-2); §1.1 (p. 2); §3, lower bound paragraph and Figure 2 (pp. 7-8). IACR ePrint 2019/175, PDF dated 2020-05-27.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Any two-party protocol that outputs the intersection of two sets whenever their symmetric difference is at most t must communicate \\( \\Omega(t) \\) bits, unconditionally: a protocol with \\( o(t) \\) communication, run on two size-t sets each padded with the same \\( n' - t \\) public dummy elements, would compute the intersection of size-t sets with \\( o(t) \\) communication, contradicting the \\( \\Omega(n) \\) set-intersection bound that follows from the communication complexity of set disjointness.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"\\( \\Omega(t) \\) communication lower bound for two-party threshold PSI, t the bound on the symmetric set difference","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/175","sections":[{"content":"\\( \\Omega(t) \\) lower bound for threshold PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Suppose protocol Π computes the intersection of sets of size \\( n' \\) that share at least \\( n' - t \\) elements with \\( o(t) \\) communication. For sets \\( S_A \\) and \\( S_B \\) of size t, both parties run Π on \\( S_A \\cup S_D \\) and \\( S_B \\cup S_D \\) for a fixed public set \\( S_D \\) of \\( n' - t \\) dummies; the threshold condition always holds, and removing \\( S_D \\) from the output yields \\( S_A \\cap S_B \\) with \\( o(t) \\) communication, contradicting the \\( \\Omega(t) \\) bound for PSI on size-t sets (§3, p. 8).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"gs19-omega-t-communication-lower","status":"published","subtitle":"The Communication Complexity of Threshold Private Set Intersection","summary":"Any two-party protocol that outputs the intersection of two sets whenever their symmetric difference is at most t must communicate \\( \\Omega(t) \\) bits, unconditionally: a protocol with \\( o(t) \\) communication, run on two size-t sets each padded with the same \\( n' - t \\) public dummy elements, would compute the intersection of size-t sets with \\( o(t) \\) communication, contradicting the \\( \\Omega(n) \\) set-intersection bound that follows from the communication complexity of set disjointness.","title":"\\( \\Omega(t) \\) communication lower bound for two-party threshold PSI, t the bound on the symmetric set difference","type":"result","venue":"CRYPTO 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-GS-LOWER-BOUND"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION","keywords":["atomic-result","correlated-ot","ot-precomputation","free-xor","garbled-circuits","naor-reingold-prf","oprf","unbalanced-psi","precomputation"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"cot-precomputation","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension","oprf"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Precomputed unbalanced PSI pushes all heavy work into phases that run before the client's contacts are known, leaving an online phase in which the client fetches the keys that let it evaluate a precomputed oblivious PRF on each item. Those fetches were ordinary oblivious transfers. Free-XOR garbling invites correlated transfers, which cost half as much, but precomputed correlated transfers seemed to require regarbling whenever the client's random choice bit differed from its real input. Kales and coauthors fixed the label pair from the correlated transfer, added a fresh random mask per label, and let a one-bit correction decide which masked value the server returns. The same idea applies to the Naor-Reingold OPRF, and in both protocols the online transfers shrink by half.","prior_boundary":"Kiss et al. split unbalanced PSI into base, setup, online, and update phases and precomputed OTs and garbled circuits in the base phase with ordinary OTs (§3.5, p. 6; §4.3, p. 8). Correlated OTs suit Free-XOR labels \\( l_1 = l_0 \\oplus \\Delta \\), but with OT precomputation the sender must swap the precomputed messages whenever the client's random choice differs from its input, which would force re-garbling at least the first layer of each precomputed circuit (§4.3, p. 8).","significance_at_publication":"OT messages are the only online communication of GC-PSI, so its online phase sends half as much; the authors state the technique is of independent interest for Yao's protocol with precomputation (§1.1, p. 2; §4.3, p. 8). The measured online phase for 1024 client items sends 2,048 KiB from server to client in GC-PSI and 4,147 KiB in NR-PSI (Table 5, p. 13).","technical_delta":"For GC-PSI the input labels are derived from correlated OTs through per-label masks δ, so the swap moves into the masks: the client sends \\( b = c \\oplus y \\), the garbler replies \\( B = r_b \\oplus l_0 \\), and the client sets its label to \\( r_c \\oplus B \\) (§4.3, p. 8; Figure 1, p. 9). For NR-PSI the correlation between r and \\( r \\cdot a_j \\) in the OPRF of Hazay and Lindell lets correlated OTs replace ordinary ones, combined with OT precomputation (§4.4, p. 10; Figure 2, p. 10)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION","keywords":["correlated-ot","ot-precomputation","free-xor","garbled-circuits","naor-reingold-prf","oprf","unbalanced-psi","precomputation"],"limitations":["the factor 2 concerns the online OT messages; base-phase garbled circuits and the setup-phase filter are unchanged (§4.3, p. 8; Tables 3-4, p. 13)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","qualifiers":["applies to the precomputation setting, in which the number of precomputed OTs and circuits bounds the client items checked before the base phase must be rerun (Figure 1 caption, p. 9)","security is argued from the arguments for standard OT precomputation and the uniformity of the fresh masks δ; no proof is given (§4.3, p. 8)","the GC-PSI variant relies on Free-XOR labels with a global offset Δ known only to the garbler (§4.3, p. 8)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 2); §3.1 (pp. 4-5); §3.5 (p. 6); §4.3 (p. 8); Figure 1 (p. 9); §4.4 (pp. 9-10); Figure 2 (p. 10); Table 5 (p. 13). IACR ePrint 2019/517, full version, PDF dated 2019-05-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"In unbalanced PSI whose client-side OPRF evaluations rely on OTs precomputed in an input-independent base phase, the online OT messages can come from correlated OTs: in garbled-circuit OPRF evaluation the garbler builds each input-wire label pair as \\( l_0 = r_0 \\oplus \\delta \\) and \\( l_1 = l_0 \\oplus \\Delta \\) from a correlated OT \\( (r_0, r_1 = r_0 \\oplus \\Delta) \\) and a fresh mask δ, and answers the client's correction bit \\( b = c \\oplus y \\) with the single value \\( r_b \\oplus l_0 \\); in Naor-Reingold OPRF evaluation, where the client chooses between r and \\( r \\cdot a_j \\), a correlated OT combined with OT precomputation sends one message of the symmetric security parameter instead of two. In both cases online OT communication halves, and garbled circuits stay precomputable before the client's input is known.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Correlated-OT precomputation that halves the online OT communication of precomputed garbled-circuit and Naor-Reingold OPRF evaluation in unbalanced PSI","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","sections":[{"content":"Correlated-OT precomputation","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"In the base phase the parties run random correlated OTs by OT extension: the garbler, with input Δ, learns \\( r_0 \\) and \\( r_1 = r_0 \\oplus \\Delta \\), and the evaluator, with a random choice c, learns \\( r_c \\) (§4.3, p. 8). Each precomputed circuit's input labels are \\( l_0 = r_0 \\oplus \\delta \\) and \\( l_1 = l_0 \\oplus \\Delta \\) with δ fresh per label. Online, the evaluator sends \\( b = c \\oplus y \\) and receives \\( B = r_b \\oplus l_0 \\), and \\( r_c \\oplus B \\) is the label for its input bit y in all four cases of \\( (c, b) \\) (§4.3, p. 8; Figure 1, p. 9). For NR-PSI the client's correction bit decides which precomputed random message the server treats as r and which masks the correlated message \\( r \\cdot a_j \\) (§4.4, p. 10).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: the base phase runs correlated and random OTs by OT extension, which the paper introduces with IKNP (§3.1, p. 4); the implementation uses the correlated-OT extension of Keller, Orsini, and Scholl (§5.1, p. 12). MPC-CONTRIB-2008-FREEXOR: the GC-PSI variant depends on Free-XOR labels with a global offset (§4.3, p. 8). Beaver's OT precomputation (CRYPTO 1995), on which the technique builds, has no record in any topic (§3.1, p. 5; §4.3, p. 8).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"krssw19-correlated-ot-precomputation","status":"published","subtitle":"Mobile Private Contact Discovery at Scale","summary":"In unbalanced PSI whose client-side OPRF evaluations rely on OTs precomputed in an input-independent base phase, the online OT messages can come from correlated OTs: in garbled-circuit OPRF evaluation the garbler builds each input-wire label pair as \\( l_0 = r_0 \\oplus \\delta \\) and \\( l_1 = l_0 \\oplus \\Delta \\) from a correlated OT \\( (r_0, r_1 = r_0 \\oplus \\Delta) \\) and a fresh mask δ, and answers the client's correction bit \\( b = c \\oplus y \\) with the single value \\( r_b \\oplus l_0 \\); in Naor-Reingold OPRF evaluation, where the client chooses between r and \\( r \\cdot a_j \\), a correlated OT combined with OT precomputation sends one message of the symmetric security parameter instead of two. In both cases online OT communication halves, and garbled circuits stay precomputable before the client's input is known.","title":"Correlated-OT precomputation that halves the online OT communication of precomputed garbled-circuit and Naor-Reingold OPRF evaluation in unbalanced PSI","type":"result","venue":"USENIX Security 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-COT-PRECOMPUTATION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION","keywords":["atomic-result","cuckoo-filter","filter-compression","filter-updates","false-positive-probability","precomputation","unbalanced-psi","private-contact-discovery"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"cuckoo-filter-compression","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["cuckoo_filter"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"In precomputed unbalanced PSI the server encrypts its entire user base under a PRF once and ships it to every client as a compact filter, so the size of that filter and of its daily updates decides whether the approach works on a phone plan. Resende and Aranha had used cuckoo filters with short tags, accepting a false-positive rate near one in eight thousand, and sent every update as a full elliptic-curve point. Kales and coauthors observed that empty entries need not be sent, that the position of a tag within its bucket carries no information, and that a tag plus one bucket index is enough to insert or delete. With longer tags the false-positive rate drops to about one in half a billion, while the filter shrinks and updates become four times smaller.","prior_boundary":"Resende and Aranha represented the server's encrypted set by a cuckoo filter with 16-bit tags, bucket size 3, and a false-positive probability of about \\( 2^{-13} \\), which the authors call impractical, and sent each updated element as a 256-bit compressed point on GLS-254 (§2, p. 4; §4.1, pp. 6-7). Kiss et al. used Bloom filters (§2, p. 4). Dictionary compression with LZMA, suggested for Bloom-filter updates, takes over 8 min to compress a filter for \\( 2^{28} \\) items (§4.1, p. 6).","significance_at_publication":"The setup transfer for \\( 2^{28} \\) server items is 1,072 MiB (Table 4, p. 13), and a filter at 10% occupancy, as in a fast-growing service, compresses 8.3 times (§4.1, p. 6). The encoding runs on the fly, while LZMA would be infeasible on a phone for regular updates (§4.1, pp. 6-7). The authors still name the filter size as the bottleneck for very large server sets (§4.6, p. 11).","technical_delta":"The filter is sent as a bit map of occupied entries plus the list of tags, or with a per-bucket tag count, since tag positions within a bucket do not matter (§4.1, p. 6). An update carries a tag and one bucket index, from which the client computes the other candidate bucket for relocations or deletion (§4.1, p. 7). Tags of 32 bits with bucket size 3 set the false-positive probability per item near \\( 2^{-29} \\) (§4.1, p. 6; Table 4, p. 13)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION","keywords":["cuckoo-filter","filter-compression","filter-updates","false-positive-probability","precomputation","unbalanced-psi","private-contact-discovery"],"limitations":["the filter remains linear in the server set, about 1 GiB for \\( 2^{28} \\) items and about 8 GiB for \\( 2^{31} \\), and must be stored by the client (§4.6, p. 11)","the paper does not discuss what updates reveal; PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR notes that in filter-based protocols of this kind the receiver learns how many items were inserted and removed and that deletions cannot be enforced (§8.3, p. 21 of that paper)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","qualifiers":["the 30% figure is for load ≈66%, tag size 32 bits, and bucket size 3; sparser filters compress more (§4.1, p. 6)","the 59-bit update assumes \\( 2^{28} \\) items, 27-bit bucket indices, and 32-bit tags (§4.1, p. 7)","false-positive probabilities are per item; the authors note that one false positive per ten clients querying \\( 2^{10} \\) items follows from \\( 2^{-13} \\) (§4.1, p. 6)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 2); §2 (pp. 3-4); §3.4 (pp. 5-6); §3.5 (p. 6); §4.1 (pp. 6-7); §4.6 (p. 11); Table 4 (p. 13). IACR ePrint 2019/517, full version, PDF dated 2019-05-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"In unbalanced PSI where the server sends its set once as a cuckoo filter of PRF values, transmitting an occupancy bit per entry plus the nonempty tags, or the number of tags per bucket in \\( \\log_2(b + 1) \\) bits, reduces the filter by about 30% at load ≈66% (from 6 MiB to 4.19 MiB for \\( 2^{20} \\) items with 32-bit tags and bucket size 3), and sending only a tag and one candidate bucket index per inserted or deleted item makes an update 59 bits for \\( 2^{28} \\) items instead of a 256-bit group element; 32-bit or 42-bit tags give false-positive probabilities of about \\( 2^{-29} \\) or \\( 2^{-39} \\) instead of about \\( 2^{-13} \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Smaller transfer of the server set in precomputed unbalanced PSI: compressed cuckoo filters, 4.3x smaller updates, and false-positive rates near \\( 2^{-29} \\)","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","sections":[{"content":"Compressed cuckoo filters","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"A cuckoo filter stores a v-bit tag of each item in one of two buckets of b entries; lookups compare tags in both candidate buckets, so equal tags cause false positives at a rate set mainly by v (§3.4, pp. 5-6). The compressed encoding sends an occupancy bit map and the stored tags, or a \\( \\log_2(b + 1) \\)-bit tag count per bucket followed by the tags (§4.1, p. 6). Updates send \\( (t_i, p_i) \\) for each changed item; the client inserts or deletes the tag at \\( p_i \\) or at \\( p_i \\oplus H(t_i) \\) (§4.1, p. 7; Figure 1, update phase, p. 9).","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"krssw19-smaller-transfer-of-the","status":"published","subtitle":"Mobile Private Contact Discovery at Scale","summary":"In unbalanced PSI where the server sends its set once as a cuckoo filter of PRF values, transmitting an occupancy bit per entry plus the nonempty tags, or the number of tags per bucket in \\( \\log_2(b + 1) \\) bits, reduces the filter by about 30% at load ≈66% (from 6 MiB to 4.19 MiB for \\( 2^{20} \\) items with 32-bit tags and bucket size 3), and sending only a tag and one candidate bucket index per inserted or deleted item makes an update 59 bits for \\( 2^{28} \\) items instead of a 256-bit group element; 32-bit or 42-bit tags give false-positive probabilities of about \\( 2^{-29} \\) or \\( 2^{-39} \\) instead of about \\( 2^{-13} \\).","title":"Smaller transfer of the server set in precomputed unbalanced PSI: compressed cuckoo filters, 4.3x smaller updates, and false-positive rates near \\( 2^{-29} \\)","type":"result","venue":"USENIX Security 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-CUCKOO-FILTER-COMPRESSION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-LOWMC-OPRF","keywords":["atomic-result","lowmc","garbled-circuits","oprf","and-gate-count","aes-128","key-schedule","unbalanced-psi","precomputation"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"lowmc-oprf","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["garbled_circuits","oprf"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"In the garbled-circuit flavor of precomputed unbalanced PSI, the server sends one garbled PRF circuit per client contact before the contacts are known, so the PRF's AND-gate count sets the cost of the base phase. Every implementation had used AES-128, with about five thousand AND gates, and LowMC had been named only as a possibility. Kales and coauthors chose LowMC parameters for this setting: a single S-box per round and many rounds, which costs nothing extra in Yao's constant-round protocol, and a data-complexity bound tied to how many queries the service will ever answer. The circuit shrinks to about six hundred AND gates, the key schedule is garbled once because the key never changes, and precomputation traffic falls roughly sevenfold.","prior_boundary":"All earlier GC-PSI implementations evaluated AES-128, whose circuit with the Boyar-Peralta S-box has 5,120 AND gates without key schedule (§4.2, p. 7). Kiss et al. had mentioned LowMC as a possible replacement without fixing parameters (§1.1, p. 2; §4.2, p. 7). With Free-XOR and half-gates only AND gates cost communication, two κ-bit labels each (§4.2, p. 7; §5.2, p. 12).","significance_at_publication":"In the base phase, which sends the garbled circuits, LowMC reduces communication 7.4 times relative to AES while the online phase stays comparable; the one-time server setup becomes slower than hardware-accelerated AES (§6.1, pp. 12-13; Tables 3-4, p. 13). The \\( 2^{64} \\) data complexity allows \\( 2^{20} \\) contact discoveries of \\( 2^{10} \\) items for each of \\( 2^{28} \\) clients (§4.2, p. 7).","technical_delta":"The authors compare five LowMC instances under the round formula updated after recent cryptanalysis and recommend instance (3), with one S-box per round and 624 AND gates, accepting 208 rounds because Yao's protocol has constant round complexity (§4.2, pp. 7-8; Table 2, p. 8). Data complexity \\( 2^{64} \\) is matched to the number of queries the service allows, and bundling all client circuits lets the fixed key schedule be garbled once, saving about 2 KiB per further item (§4.2, p. 7)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-LOWMC-OPRF","keywords":["lowmc","garbled-circuits","oprf","and-gate-count","aes-128","key-schedule","unbalanced-psi","precomputation"],"limitations":["the one-time server setup takes 1,869.13 s with LowMC against 23.94 s with AES for \\( 2^{28} \\) items, because AES has hardware support (Table 4, p. 13; §6.1, p. 13)","the number of client queries must be bounded to stay within the data complexity (§4.2, p. 7)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","qualifiers":["security of the OPRF rests on LowMC instance (3) being a secure PRF up to data complexity \\( 2^{64} \\), with rounds from the LowMC designers' updated formula (§4.2, pp. 7-8)","the 8.2x figure counts AND gates; the measured base-phase communication ratio is 7.4x (abstract, p. 1; §6.1, p. 12)","for small data complexity the authors recommend instance (4), with \\( 2^{32} \\) and 576 AND gates, which needs re-keying once the bound is reached (§4.2, pp. 7-8)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 2); §4.2 (pp. 7-8); Table 2 (p. 8); §6.1 (pp. 12-13); Tables 3-4 (p. 13). IACR ePrint 2019/517, full version, PDF dated 2019-05-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Instantiating the PRF evaluated by garbled circuits in GC-PSI with the LowMC instance of block and key size 128, one S-box per round, data complexity \\( 2^{64} \\), and 208 rounds gives 624 AND gates per client item, against 5,120 for AES-128 without key schedule (8.2x fewer); since the server's key is fixed, the key schedule is garbled once for all client circuits, and the data-complexity bound is enforced by limiting client queries. In the authors' measurement the base-phase communication for \\( 2^{10} \\) client items falls from 162.52 MiB to 22.01 MiB.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"LowMC instead of AES-128 as the PRF of the garbled-circuit OPRF in unbalanced PSI: 8.2x fewer AND gates per client item","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","sections":[{"content":"LowMC as the garbled OPRF","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Table 2 (p. 8) lists LowMC instances with block and key size 128: (1) 42 S-boxes and 13 rounds, 1,638 AND gates; (2) 31 S-boxes and 13 rounds, 1,209; (3) one S-box and 208 rounds, 624; (4) one S-box, data complexity \\( 2^{32} \\), 192 rounds, 576; (5) one S-box, data complexity \\( 2^{128} \\), 287 rounds, 861; and AES-128 with 5,120. Instance (3) is used in all experiments (§6.1, p. 12). Other MPC- and FHE-oriented ciphers are set aside: three of them are stream ciphers designed for FHE ciphertext expansion, and MiMC works over large prime fields, which does not fit Boolean garbled circuits (§4.2, p. 8).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-1982-YAO-2PC and MPC-CONTRIB-2015-HALFGATES: the OPRF is evaluated by Yao's garbled circuits with Free-XOR and half-gates, so AND gates determine the communication (§3.2, p. 5; §5.2, p. 12). SYM-RESULT-2001-AES-AES-128-BIT-BLOCK-CIPHER-STANDARD: AES-128 is the replaced PRF and the baseline of Table 2 (§4.2, p. 7). MPC-PAPER-2009-PRACTICAL2PC: OPRF evaluation by garbled circuits follows Pinkas, Schneider, Smart, and Williams (§4.3, p. 8). LowMC (Albrecht et al., EUROCRYPT 2015) has no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"krssw19-lowmc-instead-of-aes","status":"published","subtitle":"Mobile Private Contact Discovery at Scale","summary":"Instantiating the PRF evaluated by garbled circuits in GC-PSI with the LowMC instance of block and key size 128, one S-box per round, data complexity \\( 2^{64} \\), and 208 rounds gives 624 AND gates per client item, against 5,120 for AES-128 without key schedule (8.2x fewer); since the server's key is fixed, the key schedule is garbled once for all client circuits, and the data-complexity bound is enforced by limiting client queries. In the authors' measurement the base-phase communication for \\( 2^{10} \\) client items falls from 162.52 MiB to 22.01 MiB.","title":"LowMC instead of AES-128 as the PRF of the garbled-circuit OPRF in unbalanced PSI: 8.2x fewer AND gates per client item","type":"result","venue":"USENIX Security 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-LOWMC-OPRF"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION","keywords":["atomic-result","android","armv8","arm-cryptography-extensions","fixed-key-aes","malicious-client","libote","p-256","private-contact-discovery","signal","unbalanced-psi"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"mobile-implementation","contribution_kind":"implementation_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious","semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["garbled_circuits","ot_extension","oprf","cuckoo_filter"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Private contact discovery had a protocol design that suited phones, with an input-independent precomputation, a one-time transfer of the server's encrypted user base, and a cheap online phase, but its only phone implementation ran in Java with semi-honest security and needed close to a minute to check a single contact. Kales and coauthors rewrote both variants natively for ARMv8, garbling with the hardware AES instructions of recent phones and running the algebraic variant on an elliptic curve. Malicious clients came almost for free, because a client only sends oblivious-transfer messages, so swapping in actively secure transfers suffices. A thousand contacts against a quarter of a billion users then took about three seconds on WiFi, and the protocols ran inside a modified Signal client.","prior_boundary":"Kiss et al. gave the only unbalanced PSI implementation on real phones, in Java and with semi-honest security; their fastest protocol that can easily be made secure against malicious clients needed more than 52 s on WiFi to check one contact against \\( 2^{20} \\) entries, and one garbled AES-128 circuit took over a second (§1, p. 2; §5, p. 11). The FHE-based protocols of Chen et al. had been evaluated only on servers in a 10 Gbit/s network (§6.2, p. 13).","significance_at_publication":"For 1024 contacts the combined base and online time improves by more than 300 times (GC-PSI) and 30 times (NR-PSI) over the implementations of Kiss et al. run in the same environment, with 7.5 and 10.9 times less communication (Table 6, p. 14). A prototype was integrated into the Signal Android client (§1.1, p. 3; Appendix C, p. 19), although the authors state that Signal's stated requirements for a billion users are not yet met (§7, p. 14).","technical_delta":"Garbling uses fixed-key AES through the ARM Cryptography Extensions, which speed AES up 35 times, and NEON registers for 128-bit labels; NR-PSI runs over P-256, which shrinks online values 8 times relative to a finite field; libOTe is ported to ARMv8 (§5.1-5.3, pp. 11-12). Because the client sends only OT messages and correction bits, maliciously secure base OTs and OT extension suffice for malicious clients (§4.5, pp. 10-11; §5.1, pp. 11-12)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION","keywords":["android","armv8","arm-cryptography-extensions","fixed-key-aes","malicious-client","libote","p-256","private-contact-discovery","signal","unbalanced-psi"],"limitations":["the server setup takes 52,332.38 s for ECC-NR-PSI and 1,869.13 s for LowMC-GC-PSI on \\( 2^{28} \\) items, single-threaded (Table 4, p. 13)","enumeration attacks by repeated queries are not prevented; the authors recommend rate limiting (§4.5, p. 11)","a malicious server can learn about non-matching client items whenever the client reveals its intersection to the server, so a semi-honest server is assumed (§4.5, p. 11)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","qualifiers":["malicious clients and a semi-honest server; security is argued in prose, with no simulation proof (§4.5, pp. 10-11)","the reported 2.92 s and 6.53 s are base plus online time (Table 6, p. 14), which the abstract calls the online phase (p. 1)","server on an Intel Core i7-4600U, client a Google Pixel XL 2, one thread each; WiFi with about 230 Mbit/s and 70 ms RTT, LTE with 42 Mbit/s down, 4 Mbit/s up, and 80 ms RTT; 128-bit items (§6, p. 12)","the numbers for Kiss et al. come from running their implementations in the authors' environment (Table 6, p. 14)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1 (pp. 1-2); §1.1 (pp. 2-3); §4.5 (pp. 10-11); §5 (pp. 11-12); §6 (pp. 12-14); Tables 3-6 (pp. 13-14); §7 (p. 14); Appendices C-D (pp. 19-20); Table 8 (p. 20). IACR ePrint 2019/517, full version, PDF dated 2019-05-24.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"C/C++ implementations of precomputed LowMC-GC-PSI and ECC-NR-PSI for Android, using ARMv8 Cryptography Extensions for fixed-key AES garbling, the P-256 curve for the Naor-Reingold PRF, and an ARMv8 port of libOTe with Chou-Orlandi base OTs and Keller-Orsini-Scholl OT extension, are secure against malicious clients when the server is semi-honest, as argued in prose; checking 1024 client contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) server items takes 2.92 s on WiFi and 6.53 s on LTE of base plus online time with 6.07 MiB of communication for ECC-NR-PSI, after a one-time 1,072 MiB filter transfer, and a garbled AES-128 circuit is evaluated more than 1,000 times faster than in the earlier Java implementation of Kiss et al.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Native smartphone implementation of precomputed unbalanced PSI with malicious-client security: 1024 contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) users in about 3 s","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/517","sections":[{"content":"Smartphone implementation of precomputed unbalanced PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Both protocols follow the four phases of precomputed unbalanced PSI: an input-independent base phase, a setup phase in which the server sends a compressed cuckoo filter of PRF values of its whole set, an online phase of OPRF evaluations on the client's items followed by local filter lookups, and an update phase (§3.5, p. 6). The implementation measures each phase separately (Tables 3-5, p. 13) and compares with the Android implementations of Kiss et al. (Table 6, p. 14) and, on x86 hardware, with the FHE-based and Diffie-Hellman protocols using numbers taken from Chen et al. (Appendix D, Table 8, p. 20).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: OT extension, which the paper introduces with IKNP (§3.1, p. 4); the implementation uses the actively secure extension of Keller, Orsini, and Scholl, which has no record in any topic (§5.1, p. 12). MPC-CONTRIB-2008-FREEXOR and MPC-CONTRIB-2015-HALFGATES: the garbling scheme of the GC-PSI implementation (§5.2, p. 12).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"krssw19-native-smartphone-implementa","status":"published","subtitle":"Mobile Private Contact Discovery at Scale","summary":"C/C++ implementations of precomputed LowMC-GC-PSI and ECC-NR-PSI for Android, using ARMv8 Cryptography Extensions for fixed-key AES garbling, the P-256 curve for the Naor-Reingold PRF, and an ARMv8 port of libOTe with Chou-Orlandi base OTs and Keller-Orsini-Scholl OT extension, are secure against malicious clients when the server is semi-honest, as argued in prose; checking 1024 client contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) server items takes 2.92 s on WiFi and 6.53 s on LTE of base plus online time with 6.07 MiB of communication for ECC-NR-PSI, after a one-time 1,072 MiB filter transfer, and a garbled AES-128 circuit is evaluated more than 1,000 times faster than in the earlier Java implementation of Kiss et al.","title":"Native smartphone implementation of precomputed unbalanced PSI with malicious-client security: 1024 contacts against \\( 2^{28} \\) users in about 3 s","type":"result","venue":"USENIX Security 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-KRSSW-MOBILE-IMPLEMENTATION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF","keywords":["atomic-result","opprf","programmable-prf","batched-opprf","polynomial-interpolation","correlated-targets","hint"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"batched-opprf","contribution_kind":"mechanism","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"technique":["opprf","batched_oprf"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"An oblivious programmable PRF lets one party fix the outputs on chosen inputs while the other evaluates it without learning which inputs were chosen. In its original form each programmed input needed its own random target and its own polynomial coefficient, so programming every item that simple hashing places in a bin cost as much as the largest bin allowed. Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai observed that a single query suffices in circuit-based PSI, and that the targets may then be correlated, even identical, without loss of security. They also merged the polynomials of all bins into one, so the hint grows with the total number of items rather than with the number of bins times the maximum load. Programming every bin then costs linear communication overall.","prior_boundary":"The OPPRF was introduced by Kolesnikov, Matania, Pinkas, Rosulek, and Trieu with a polynomial-based construction and a security definition that allows many queries but requires an independent target per programmed point (§1, p. 2; §3.2, p. 12). One standalone OPPRF per bin costs communication linear in the number of programmed values, which for bins of maximal size \\( O(\\log n / \\log \\log n) \\) gives \\( O(n \\log n / \\log \\log n) \\) overall (§1.1, p. 3; §3.3, p. 13).","significance_at_publication":"The total hint size becomes \\( K \\cdot n \\) coefficients for cuckoo hashing with K hash functions (footnote 6, p. 13), at most 3% of the protocol's communication (§7.3, p. 24), which removes the logarithmic factor that bounded earlier circuit-based PSI (§1.1, pp. 3-4).","technical_delta":"The definition is changed to one query but correlated targets, including all targets equal (Definition 1, p. 11; §3.2, p. 12), which lets every item of a bin be programmed to the same value. The hints of all bins are merged into one polynomial over all programmed points, keyed per bin (Definition 2 and Construction 4, pp. 13-14), and security is reduced to a batch PRF (Theorem 5, pp. 14-15)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF","keywords":["opprf","programmable-prf","batched-opprf","polynomial-interpolation","correlated-targets","hint"],"limitations":["the definition is weaker than the earlier one in allowing a single query (§3.2, p. 12)","interpolating a polynomial of degree \\( O(n) \\) costs \\( O(n^2) \\) or \\( O(n \\log^2 n) \\) operations, which the protocol reduces by splitting bins into mega-bins (§4.3, p. 18)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","qualifiers":["one query per programmed function; querying twice could break security when targets are correlated (§3.2, p. 11)","the sets \\( X_1, \\ldots, X_\\beta \\) must be disjoint; the protocol appends the bin index to each item (Definition 2, p. 14; Protocol 9, p. 17)","security reduces to a pseudorandom function (Theorems 3 and 5, pp. 12-15) and is proved in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{OPRF}} \\)-hybrid model (Theorem 8, p. 15)","semi-honest parties (§2.2, p. 8)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY § Contributions","primary_source":"§1 (p. 2); §1.1 (pp. 3-4); §3.1-3.2 with Definition 1, Construction 2, and Theorem 3 (pp. 10-13); §3.3 with Definition 2, Construction 4, and Theorem 5 (pp. 13-15); §3.4 with Theorem 8, Functionality 6, and Protocol 7 (pp. 15-16); §7.3 (p. 24). IACR ePrint 2019/241, PDF dated 2019-02-28.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"A one-time programmable PRF publishes as hint the polynomial interpolated through the points \\( (x, F'_k(x) \\oplus t) \\) for each programmed input x with target t, so that a single evaluation returns t on programmed inputs and a pseudorandom value elsewhere even when the targets are correlated (Construction 2, Theorem 3); interpolating one polynomial through the points of β such functions with independent keys gives a hint of \\( \\kappa N \\) bits for N programmed points in total (Construction 4, Theorem 5), and combined with β one-time OPRF instances it securely realizes a batched oblivious programmable PRF in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{OPRF}} \\)-hybrid model (Protocol 7, Theorem 8), so the hints for bins of \\( O(\\log n / \\log \\log n) \\) items cost \\( O(\\kappa n) \\) in total instead of \\( O(\\kappa n \\log n / \\log \\log n) \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Batched OPPRF with a single polynomial hint of \\( \\kappa N \\) bits over all bins, from a one-time programmable PRF with correlated targets","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","sections":[{"content":"Batched OPPRF","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"In Protocol 7 (p. 16) the parties run β one-time OPRF instances in which the sender receives the keys \\( k_j \\) and the receiver learns \\( F'(k_j, x_j) \\); the sender then sends the batched hint polynomial p, and the receiver outputs \\( F'(k_j, x_j) \\oplus p(x_j) \\). The receiver learns \\( t_j \\) when \\( x_j \\) is a programmed point of bin j and a pseudorandom value otherwise (Functionality 6, p. 16).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None: the OPPRF and the one-time OPRF are PSI-specific mechanisms.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"psty19-batched-opprf-with-one","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI with Linear Communication","summary":"A one-time programmable PRF publishes as hint the polynomial interpolated through the points \\( (x, F'_k(x) \\oplus t) \\) for each programmed input x with target t, so that a single evaluation returns t on programmed inputs and a pseudorandom value elsewhere even when the targets are correlated (Construction 2, Theorem 3); interpolating one polynomial through the points of β such functions with independent keys gives a hint of \\( \\kappa N \\) bits for N programmed points in total (Construction 4, Theorem 5), and combined with β one-time OPRF instances it securely realizes a batched oblivious programmable PRF in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{OPRF}} \\)-hybrid model (Protocol 7, Theorem 8), so the hints for bins of \\( O(\\log n / \\log \\log n) \\) items cost \\( O(\\kappa n) \\) in total instead of \\( O(\\kappa n \\log n / \\log \\log n) \\).","title":"Batched OPPRF with a single polynomial hint of \\( \\kappa N \\) bits over all bins, from a one-time programmable PRF with correlated targets","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-BATCHED-OPPRF"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION","keywords":["atomic-result","circuit-based-psi","linear-communication","opprf","stash","dual-execution","stash-less-cuckoo-hashing","psi-cat"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"linear-communication","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["circuit"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["opprf","cuckoo_hashing","gmw"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Circuit-based PSI had come within a slowly growing factor of linear cost, but two obstacles kept it there: the party that copies each item into all its candidate bins needs bins with room for a logarithmic number of items, and the cuckoo-hashing party has a stash whose items must be compared with everything. Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai removed the first obstacle by moving the work out of the circuit: an oblivious programmable PRF maps all of a bin's items to one secret value, so the circuit checks a single equality per bin. They removed the second either by running the protocol again with roles swapped on the stashed items or by using three hash functions and no stash. Communication became linear in the set size, and the measured cost fell several times.","prior_boundary":"Circuit-Phasing needed \\( O(n \\log n / \\log \\log n) \\) comparisons because the simple-hashing party fills bins of up to \\( O(\\log n / \\log \\log n) \\) items, and the private set membership protocol of Ciampi and Orlandi kept that overall communication (§1.1, p. 3). Two-dimensional cuckoo hashing reached \\( \\omega(n) \\) comparisons, with \\( s \\cdot n \\) of them spent on a stash of size \\( \\omega(1) \\) (§1, p. 2; §5, p. 19), and the linear protocol of Falk, Noble, and Ostrovsky achieved \\( O(n) \\) only when the output is the intersection itself (§5, p. 19).","significance_at_publication":"The authors call it the first circuit-based PSI with linear communication, matching the obvious lower bound on the number of comparisons (abstract, p. 1; §1.1, p. 4). The stash-less protocol ran 2.8 to 4.2 times faster in the LAN and 3.9 to 5.8 times faster in the WAN setting than the 2D cuckoo-hashing protocol (Table 5, p. 27).","technical_delta":"Circuit-Phasing's hashing is kept but the per-bin sub-circuit of S comparisons is replaced by a batched OPPRF whose output the circuit compares with one target per bin (§1.1, p. 3; Protocol 9, p. 17). The stash is handled by dual execution, comparing \\( X_T \\times Y \\), \\( X_S \\times Y_T \\), and \\( X_S \\times Y_S \\) in three groups of circuit sections (§5.1, pp. 19-20; Protocol 10), or avoided with \\( K = 3 \\) hash functions, whose cost the OPPRF makes independent of the bin load (§5.2, pp. 20-21)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION","keywords":["circuit-based-psi","linear-communication","opprf","stash","dual-execution","stash-less-cuckoo-hashing","psi-cat"],"limitations":["the \\( O(n) \\) bound concerns symmetric functions; non-symmetric functions or the intersection itself need an \\( O(n \\log n) \\) shuffle (§1.3, p. 5)","the stash-less variant has no analytic failure bound (§5, p. 19)","only the stash-less protocol was implemented; times for the other variants are estimates (§7.4, p. 26)","the stash-less protocol was 41 times slower than BaRK-OPRF PSI for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) in the WAN setting (§7.4, p. 27)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties (§2.2, p. 8)","both sets have n items of bitlength λ; outputs \\( f(X \\cap Y) \\) to both parties for a symmetric function f (§1, p. 1; §2.1, p. 8; §1.3, p. 5)","Protocol 10 assumes a stash of size \\( O(\\log n) \\) (§5.1, p. 19)","the stash-less parameters \\( K = 3 \\) and \\( \\beta = 1.27n \\) rely on the empirical failure analysis of Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner (§5.2, p. 20; §7.1, p. 22)","OPPRF outputs are truncated to \\( \\gamma = 40 + \\log_2 \\beta \\) bits, so a false positive occurs with probability at most \\( \\beta \\cdot 2^{-\\gamma} \\) (§4.2, p. 18; §7.1, p. 22)","security follows from that of the OPPRF and the two-party computation functionality (§4.1, p. 17; §5.1, p. 20)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1 (pp. 1-2); §1.1 (pp. 3-4); §1.5 (pp. 7-8); §4.1-4.2 with Protocol 9 (pp. 16-18); §5 with Protocol 10 (pp. 19-21); §7.1 and Table 1 (p. 22); §7.3 and Table 3 (pp. 24-25); §7.4 and Table 5 (pp. 26-28). IACR ePrint 2019/241, PDF dated 2019-02-28.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Against semi-honest parties with n items each, letting the sender program a batched OPPRF so that all of its items in a cuckoo bin map to one random target lets the circuit test a single equality per bin; handling the receiver's stash by a second OPPRF phase with the roles reversed, restricted to the stashed items, plus \\( s^2 \\) comparisons between the two stashes (Protocol 10) yields \\( O(n) \\) comparisons and \\( O(n\\kappa) \\) communication with failure probability negligible in the statistical security parameter, and the stash-less variant with \\( K = 3 \\) hash functions and \\( 1.27n \\) bins, whose failure is below \\( 2^{-40} \\) by earlier experiments, needs 2,540 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and 2.6 to 12.8 times less communication than two-dimensional cuckoo hashing for \\( n = 2^{12} \\) to \\( 2^{20} \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Circuit-based PSI with \\( O(n) \\) communication through one OPPRF comparison per bin, semi-honest two-party","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","sections":[{"content":"Circuit-based PSI with linear communication","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"In Protocol 9 (p. 17) the receiver P1 cuckoo-hashes its items into \\( \\beta = 2(1 + \\varepsilon)n \\) bins and P2 places each item in both candidate bins; the batched OPPRF gives P1 the value \\( y^*_j \\), equal to P2's target \\( t_j \\) when P1's item in bin j is among P2's items there; the circuit compares the first \\( \\gamma \\) bits of \\( y^*_j \\) and \\( t_j \\) per bin and every stashed item with all of P2's items. Protocol 10 (p. 20) removes the stash comparisons with a role-reversed second phase. The stash-less version uses \\( K = 3 \\) hash functions, so the circuit compares \\( 1.27n \\) values; the OPRF and the hint polynomials are about 4% of its communication (Table 3, p. 25).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-GMW: the circuit is evaluated with GMW (Table 5 caption, p. 27; §2.3, pp. 8-9). MPC-PAPER-2015-ABY and MPC-CONTRIB-2015-ABY-MIXED: the secure circuit evaluation uses the ABY framework (§7.4, p. 27). MPC-PAPER-1987-GMW: GMW protocol, cited as [GMW87] (§2.3, p. 9; §7.3, p. 25).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"psty19-linear-communication-circuit","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI with Linear Communication","summary":"Against semi-honest parties with n items each, letting the sender program a batched OPPRF so that all of its items in a cuckoo bin map to one random target lets the circuit test a single equality per bin; handling the receiver's stash by a second OPPRF phase with the roles reversed, restricted to the stashed items, plus \\( s^2 \\) comparisons between the two stashes (Protocol 10) yields \\( O(n) \\) comparisons and \\( O(n\\kappa) \\) communication with failure probability negligible in the statistical security parameter, and the stash-less variant with \\( K = 3 \\) hash functions and \\( 1.27n \\) bins, whose failure is below \\( 2^{-40} \\) by earlier experiments, needs 2,540 MB for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) and 2.6 to 12.8 times less communication than two-dimensional cuckoo hashing for \\( n = 2^{12} \\) to \\( 2^{20} \\).","title":"Circuit-based PSI with \\( O(n) \\) communication through one OPPRF comparison per bin, semi-honest two-party","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-LINEAR-COMMUNICATION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-PAYLOAD","keywords":["atomic-result","payload","associated-data","opprf","circuit-based-psi","intersection-sum"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"payload","contribution_kind":"capability_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["circuit"],"parties":["two_party"],"technique":["opprf","cuckoo_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Many uses of circuit-based PSI need more than the matching identifiers: an advertiser and a merchant want the total spent by common customers, so each item carries a value that must reach the circuit only when it matches. In the OPPRF approach a bin collapses all of the sender's items to one secret value, which leaves no room for their individual payloads. Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai added a second programmable PRF in which each item's target is the bin's mask combined with that item's payload. The receiver's output for a matching item therefore carries the right payload under a mask that only the sender knows and inputs to the circuit, while the circuit still performs one comparison per bin.","prior_boundary":"Circuit-Phasing and the private set membership approach of Ciampi and Orlandi did not consider payloads (§1.1, p. 4). Two-dimensional cuckoo hashing handles payloads naturally because each comparison involves one item from each party, but the basic OPPRF protocol maps all of a bin's items to one output and cannot encode their different payloads in one value (§6, p. 21).","significance_at_publication":"It supports functions of data associated with intersecting items, such as aggregate revenue over common users, which the authors list among their contributions as unsupported by the Phasing circuit (§1.5, p. 7; §1.2, pp. 4-5).","technical_delta":"A second batched OPPRF uses non-equal targets, \\( \\tilde t_j \\) XORed with each item's payload, so the receiver's output for a matching item hides exactly that item's payload under \\( \\tilde t_j \\), which the sender inputs to the circuit (§6, p. 21)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-PAYLOAD","keywords":["payload","associated-data","opprf","circuit-based-psi","intersection-sum"],"limitations":["the section describes the payload variant for the cuckoo table and does not discuss stashed items separately (§6, pp. 21-22)","no measurement of the payload variant is reported (§7, pp. 22-28)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","qualifiers":["all payloads have the same length δ (§6, p. 21)","semi-honest parties (§2.2, p. 8)","the circuit has \\( O(n(\\lambda + \\rho)) \\) input wires for items of λ bits and payloads of ρ bits (§2.1, p. 8)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.1 (p. 4); §1.2 (pp. 4-5); §1.5 (p. 7); §2.1 (p. 8); §6 (pp. 21-22). IACR ePrint 2019/241, PDF dated 2019-02-28.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"In OPPRF-based circuit-PSI, where the sender maps several items with different payloads to one bin, a second batched OPPRF in which the sender programs each item y of bin j to the target \\( \\tilde t_j \\oplus V(y) \\) lets the receiver feed its output \\( \\tilde y_j \\) into the circuit, which forwards the receiver's item and payload and the sender's payload \\( \\tilde y_j \\oplus \\tilde t_j \\) to the function sub-circuit exactly when the per-bin equality test succeeds; the number of comparisons and the asymptotic complexity stay those of the protocol without payloads.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Payloads of both parties in OPPRF-based circuit-PSI through a second batched OPPRF, at the asymptotic cost of the basic protocol","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","year":2019},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/241","sections":[{"content":"Payloads in OPPRF-based circuit-PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"For bin j the receiver inputs its item, its own payload U(item), and the two OPPRF outputs \\( y^*_j \\) and \\( \\tilde y_j \\); the sender inputs \\( t_j \\) and \\( \\tilde t_j \\). If \\( y^*_j = t_j \\) the circuit forwards the item, U(item), and \\( \\tilde y_j \\oplus \\tilde t_j \\), which equals the sender's payload V(item) (§6, p. 21). The efficiency is that of the basic protocol (§6, p. 22).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"psty19-payloads-of-both-parties","status":"published","subtitle":"Efficient Circuit-based PSI with Linear Communication","summary":"In OPPRF-based circuit-PSI, where the sender maps several items with different payloads to one bin, a second batched OPPRF in which the sender programs each item y of bin j to the target \\( \\tilde t_j \\oplus V(y) \\) lets the receiver feed its output \\( \\tilde y_j \\) into the circuit, which forwards the receiver's item and payload and the sender's payload \\( \\tilde y_j \\oplus \\tilde t_j \\) to the function sub-circuit exactly when the per-bin equality test succeeds; the number of comparisons and the asymptotic complexity stay those of the protocol without payloads.","title":"Payloads of both parties in OPPRF-based circuit-PSI through a second batched OPPRF, at the asymptotic cost of the basic protocol","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2019","year":2019,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2019-PSTY-PAYLOAD"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-DDH-INTERSECTION-SUM","keywords":["atomic-result","intersection-sum","cardinality","diffie-hellman","pohlig-hellman","paillier","damgard-jurik","deployment","ad-conversion"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"ddh-intersection-sum","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["cardinality","sum"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["diffie_hellman","additively_homomorphic_encryption"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Measuring ad conversions requires two companies to learn how many users both saw an ad and bought, and how much those users spent, without exchanging customer lists. Commutative Diffie-Hellman masking had long answered the first half: hashed identifiers raised to both parties' secret exponents can be compared, and shuffling hides which ones matched. Ion and colleagues at Google attached an additively homomorphic encryption of each purchase value to the masked identifier, so the party that finds the matches can add the corresponding ciphertexts without learning any individual value, and only the holder of the key learns the total. The protocol is simple, uses widely deployed primitives, and keeps communication small, which the authors identify as the dominant monetary cost in batch computation between companies.","prior_boundary":"The Diffie-Hellman set-intersection protocol of Meadows computes the intersection, and Huberman, Franklin, and Hogg added a shuffle so that only its size is learned (§3.1, p. 5; §4.2, p. 8). Neither aggregates values attached to the intersecting items (§3.1, p. 5).","significance_at_publication":"The protocol had been used in practice for several years to attribute aggregate ad conversions (abstract and §1, p. 1); with Paillier encryption a run on 100,000 items per party cost 0.084 US cents in the authors' cost model, and it was 20 to 30 times cheaper in that model than the cheapest garbled-circuit protocol compared (§1, p. 3; Table 2, p. 8; §5.3, p. 12; §6, p. 13).","technical_delta":"The party holding values sends additively homomorphic encryptions of them alongside its doubly masked identifiers, and the other party sums the ciphertexts whose identifiers match in the shuffled intersection (§3.1, p. 5; Figure 2, p. 6). Security against each party is proved by simulation from the intersection size, and for P2 also the sum (Theorems 1-2, p. 6; Appendix D)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-DDH-INTERSECTION-SUM","keywords":["intersection-sum","cardinality","diffie-hellman","pohlig-hellman","paillier","damgard-jurik","deployment","ad-conversion"],"limitations":["the intersection size is revealed in addition to the sum (§4.2, p. 9)","no security against malicious parties is claimed (§2.3, p. 4)","the threshold variant only lets P1 abort before the sum is computed if the intersection is too small (§3.4, p. 7)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties (§2.3, p. 4; Theorems 1-2, p. 6)","the hash function H into a DDH group is modeled as a random oracle; a fresh oracle per execution is simulated by prepending a common random seed (§3.1, p. 5; §3.2, pp. 6-7)","P1 learns the intersection size and P2 learns the size and the sum; a reverse variant gives the sum to P1 (Figure 1, p. 3; §3.4, p. 7)","associated values and their sum are assumed small enough not to wrap around the plaintext space (§3.2, p. 7; Appendix A.4, footnote 5)","both parties use the same identifier space; finding one is outside the scope (§2.1, p. 3)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract and §1 (pp. 1-3); §2.1 and Figure 1 (p. 3); §2.3 (p. 4); §3.1, Figure 2, and Theorems 1-2 (pp. 5-6); §3.2-3.4 and Table 2 (pp. 6-8); §4.2 (pp. 8-9); Tables 3-6 (pp. 12-13); §6 (p. 13); Appendix D. IACR ePrint 2019/723, PDF dated 2020-09-05.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"In the private intersection-sum-with-cardinality functionality, P1 holds identifiers, P2 holds identifier-value pairs, both learn the intersection size, and P2 also learns the sum of values over the intersection; a three-round protocol realizes it against semi-honest parties under DDH with the hash modeled as a random oracle and a CPA-secure additively homomorphic encryption scheme: both parties raise hashed identifiers to secret exponents, the sets are exchanged in shuffled order, P2's identifiers travel with encryptions of their values, and P1 homomorphically adds the ciphertexts of matching items and returns a rerandomized ciphertext for P2 to decrypt; for M items per party it sends \\( 3M \\) group elements and \\( M + 1 \\) ciphertexts.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Private intersection-sum with cardinality from Diffie-Hellman double masking and additively homomorphic tags, semi-honest, random oracle model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","sections":[{"content":"Deployed DDH-based intersection-sum","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Round 1: P1 sends \\( H(v_i)^{k_1} \\) in shuffled order. Round 2: P2 returns \\( H(v_i)^{k_1 k_2} \\) shuffled, and sends pairs \\( (H(w_j)^{k_2}, \\mathrm{AEnc}(t_j)) \\) shuffled. Round 3: P1 raises the first components to \\( k_1 \\), finds the matches, sums their ciphertexts, rerandomizes, and sends the result to P2 (Figure 2, p. 6). The deployment uses the NIST curve prime256v1 with SHA-256 hashing and Paillier with the Damgård-Jurik optimization, packing 65 values per ciphertext (§3.2, pp. 6-7). Variants allow a threshold abort, segmented inputs, and several values per item (§3.4, p. 7).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"PKE-RESULT-1976-DH-INTERACTIVE-PUBLIC-CHANNEL-KEY-AGREEMENT: the DDH assumption is stated with reference to Diffie and Hellman (Appendix A.2, p. 15). HE-RESULT-1999-PAILLIER-PRACTICAL-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-PUBLIC-KEY-ENCRYPTION and he_paillier99: the deployment encrypts the values with Paillier and the Damgård-Jurik optimization (§1, p. 2; §3.2, p. 7; Appendix B.1).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"ikn20-deployed-diffie-hellman-inte","status":"published","subtitle":"On Deploying Secure Computing: Private Intersection-Sum-with-Cardinality","summary":"In the private intersection-sum-with-cardinality functionality, P1 holds identifiers, P2 holds identifier-value pairs, both learn the intersection size, and P2 also learns the sum of values over the intersection; a three-round protocol realizes it against semi-honest parties under DDH with the hash modeled as a random oracle and a CPA-secure additively homomorphic encryption scheme: both parties raise hashed identifiers to secret exponents, the sets are exchanged in shuffled order, P2's identifiers travel with encryptions of their values, and P1 homomorphically adds the ciphertexts of matching items and returns a rerandomized ciphertext for P2 to decrypt; for M items per party it sends \\( 3M \\) group elements and \\( M + 1 \\) ciphertexts.","title":"Private intersection-sum with cardinality from Diffie-Hellman double masking and additively homomorphic tags, semi-honest, random oracle model","type":"result","venue":"IEEE EuroS&P 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-DDH-INTERSECTION-SUM"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM","keywords":["atomic-result","intersection-sum","cardinality","random-ot","batched-oprf","shuffled-oprf","tag-shuffle-aggregate"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"rot-intersection-sum","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["cardinality","sum"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["batched_oprf","ot_extension","cuckoo_hashing","additively_homomorphic_encryption"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The fastest set-intersection protocols of the late 2010s replace each party's items by outputs of a batched oblivious PRF computed with random oblivious transfer, after cuckoo hashing places the items into bins. Such protocols expose which items match, so they could not directly compute a sum over a hidden intersection. Ion and colleagues inserted a shuffle: the PRF outputs of the receiving party are encrypted under its own key, masked and permuted by the other party, and so become fresh pseudorandom values in an order the receiver does not know. Encrypted purchase values ride along as tags, and the matching values are summed homomorphically. The result computes the same aggregate as the deployed protocol from symmetric-key oblivious transfer plus homomorphic encryption.","prior_boundary":"The most efficient PSI protocols evaluated a batched OPRF from random OT per cuckoo bin, building on the hashing of Pinkas, Schneider, Segev, and Zohner and the batched OPRF of Kolesnikov, Kumaresan, Rosulek, and Trieu, but they reveal which items intersect (§1, p. 2; §4.1, pp. 7-8). Circuit-based approaches hid the intersection with a garbled-circuit sub-protocol per bucket (§4.3.1, p. 9).","significance_at_publication":"The authors believe it is the first construction of private intersection-sum with cardinality from random OT (§4.3.1, p. 9). In their cost model it was cheaper than every garbled-circuit protocol compared but about 15 times more expensive than the deployed Diffie-Hellman protocol (§5.2-5.3, p. 12; Table 7, p. 13).","technical_delta":"A shuffle step is inserted after the batched OPRF: P1's encrypted OPRF outputs are additively re-masked and permuted by P2, so P1 learns the new OPRF values of its inputs only in shuffled order, and homomorphically encrypted values travel as tags with P2's items (§4.3.1, p. 9; Figure 3, p. 10). Security against each party is proved by simulation from the intersection size and sum (Theorems 3-4, pp. 9-10; Appendix E)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM","keywords":["intersection-sum","cardinality","random-ot","batched-oprf","shuffled-oprf","tag-shuffle-aggregate"],"limitations":["the intersection size is revealed (§4.2, p. 9)","communication \\( 3.6M + 1 \\) homomorphic ciphertexts plus random OTs and \\( 96M \\) bytes for M items per party, about 15 times that of the deployed protocol in the measurements (Table 4, p. 12; §5.2, p. 12)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","qualifiers":["semi-honest parties (Theorems 3-4, pp. 9-10)","correctness assumes cuckoo hashing fails and random-OT outputs collide only with negligible probability (§4.3.1, p. 9)","the batched random OT of Kolesnikov et al. yields a relaxed PRF with related keys, which the authors say does not affect their protocol or proofs (Appendix A.6, p. 16)","P1 learns the intersection size and P2 the size and the sum (Figure 1, p. 3; Figure 3, p. 10)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION § Contributions","primary_source":"§1 (p. 2); §4.1 (pp. 7-8); §4.2 (pp. 8-9); §4.3.1, Figure 3, and Theorems 3-4 (pp. 9-10); §5.1 (p. 11); Tables 3-5 and 7 (pp. 12-13); Appendices A.5-A.6 and E. IACR ePrint 2019/723, PDF dated 2020-09-05.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Against semi-honest parties, private intersection-sum with cardinality can be computed from batched random OT: P1 cuckoo-hashes its identifiers and obtains one OPRF value per bin and stash slot, encrypts these values under its own additively homomorphic key, and P2 adds a fresh random mask to each ciphertext and returns them shuffled, which turns them into evaluations of new, still pseudorandom functions in unknown order; P2 sends, for each of its items, the masked evaluations at all candidate positions together with an encryption of its value under P2's key, and P1 adds the value ciphertexts of items that hit a decrypted evaluation and returns the rerandomized sum for P2 to decrypt.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Private intersection-sum with cardinality from batched random OT with shuffled, re-masked OPRF outputs, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/723","sections":[{"content":"Random-OT-based intersection-sum","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"P1 cuckoo-hashes into n bins and a stash of size s; for each of the \\( n + s \\) positions a random OT gives P2 a key \\( k_i \\) and P1 the value \\( f_{k_i}(v_i) \\). P1 sends \\( \\mathrm{AEnc}(pk_1, f_{k_i}(v_i)) \\); P2 adds random \\( r_i \\), rerandomizes, and returns the ciphertexts shuffled. For each \\( (w_j, t_j) \\), P2 sends the set \\( \\{f_{k_i}(w_j) + r_i\\} \\) over the hash positions and stash slots, padded and in permuted order, with \\( \\mathrm{AEnc}(pk_2, t_j) \\). P1 decrypts the shuffled values, sums the tags of matching items, and sends the rerandomized sum (Figure 3, p. 10). The measured instance uses three hash functions, no stash, at most \\( 1.5n \\) bins, and the batched random OT of Kolesnikov et al. with 128 base OTs and a 448-bit pseudorandom code (§5.1, p. 11).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: the paper describes random OT as a modification of OT extension (§4.1, p. 8) and instantiates it with the batched random OT of Kolesnikov et al. on 128 base public-key OTs (§5.1, p. 11); IKNP itself is not cited by name.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"ikn20-private-intersection-sum-wit","status":"published","subtitle":"On Deploying Secure Computing: Private Intersection-Sum-with-Cardinality","summary":"Against semi-honest parties, private intersection-sum with cardinality can be computed from batched random OT: P1 cuckoo-hashes its identifiers and obtains one OPRF value per bin and stash slot, encrypts these values under its own additively homomorphic key, and P2 adds a fresh random mask to each ciphertext and returns them shuffled, which turns them into evaluations of new, still pseudorandom functions in unknown order; P2 sends, for each of its items, the masked evaluations at all candidate positions together with an encryption of its value under P2's key, and P1 adds the value ciphertexts of items that hit a decrypted evaluation and returns the rerandomized sum for P2 to decrypt.","title":"Private intersection-sum with cardinality from batched random OT with shuffled, re-masked OPRF outputs, semi-honest","type":"result","venue":"IEEE EuroS&P 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2020-ION-ROT-INTERSECTION-SUM"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO","keywords":["atomic-result","garbled-cuckoo-table","paxos","cuckoo-graph","two-core","cyclomatic-number","linear-time-encoding","constant-rate"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"garbled-cuckoo","contribution_kind":"mechanism","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"technique":["paxos"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Cuckoo hashing had made semi-honest PSI fast, but a party's choice of bin leaks information about its other items, and garbled Bloom filters, the encodings that suited malicious protocols, cost a factor of λ in size. The garbled cuckoo table removes the choice. Each item becomes an edge between two table positions, and the encoder sets the positions so that their XOR, together with a short random tail, equals the item's value. Positions on acyclic parts of the graph are fixed by a simple traversal, and the few constraints on cycles are solved jointly using the tail. Because a random cuckoo graph has only logarithmically many cycles, the table keeps constant rate with linear-time encoding and probes of \\( O(\\lambda) \\) positions.","prior_boundary":"Garbled Bloom filters have rate \\( \\Theta(1/\\lambda) \\) with \\( O(n\\lambda) \\) encoding and λ-position decoding; a random Boolean matrix has rate \\( n/(n + \\lambda) \\) but \\( O(n^3) \\) encoding; the Bloomier-filter variant of Charles and Chellapilla encodes in linear time only by re-sampling hash functions until the graph is acyclic, which makes them depend on the input (§3.5, pp. 8-9; Figure 2, p. 9; §5, p. 16).","significance_at_publication":"The authors present it as matching the encoding and decoding costs of garbled Bloom filters at constant rate, about \\( 1/(2 + \\varepsilon) \\), with hash functions chosen independently of the keys (§5, p. 16; §5.3, pp. 20-21); with \\( 2.4n \\) main positions it is the encoding of the variant that the authors report as their faster implementation (§7.1, p. 22; §7.3, p. 23).","technical_delta":"Each key is an edge \\( \\{h_1(x), h_2(x)\\} \\), and the table is \\( D = L \\| R \\) with \\( |R| = d + \\lambda \\) (§5.1-5.2, pp. 17-19; Figure 6, p. 19). Theorem 8 (p. 20) bounds the failure probability using Lemma 12 (p. 31), which shows that a cuckoo graph has at most \\( \\log n \\) cycles except with negligible probability. A DFS-based variant handles each back edge with one constraint on R (Appendix B, Figure 7, p. 30; Theorem 11, p. 29)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO","keywords":["garbled-cuckoo-table","paxos","cuckoo-graph","two-core","cyclomatic-number","linear-time-encoding","constant-rate"],"limitations":["the rate is about \\( 1/(2 + \\varepsilon) \\), well below 1 (§5, p. 16; Figure 2, p. 9)","the paper gives asymptotic failure bounds and no concrete failure probability for the \\( 2.4n \\) setting used in its implementation (Theorem 8, p. 20; §7.1, p. 22)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","qualifiers":["the failure bound rests on bounding the cyclomatic number of a random \\( (n, m) \\)-cuckoo graph by \\( d = (1 + \\varepsilon) \\log n \\) (Theorem 8, p. 20; Lemma 12, p. 31)","Lemma 12 is stated for a cuckoo graph with \\( (2 + \\varepsilon)n \\) nodes; the implementation uses \\( 2.4n \\) main positions for the 2-core variant and \\( 2n \\) for the DFS variant (§7.1, p. 22)","items are placed in a global order determined by the graph, unlike the online insertion of garbled Bloom filters (§5.3, p. 20)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); Figure 2 (p. 9); §5.1-5.3 (pp. 17-21); Figure 6 (p. 19); Theorem 8 (p. 20); Appendix B, Theorem 11 and Figure 7 (pp. 29-31); Appendix C, Lemma 12 (pp. 31-32); §7.1 (p. 22). IACR ePrint 2020/193, PDF dated 2020-02-16.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Two hash functions map each key to an edge of a cuckoo graph on the m main positions L, and a random vector r(x) selects positions among \\( d + \\lambda \\) extra positions R. The encoder solves the constraints of the edges in the graph's 2-core by Gaussian elimination over the 2-core positions and R, then fixes every other position by traversing the remaining forest away from the 2-core. With \\( d = (1 + \\varepsilon) \\log n \\) this is a PaXoS whose failure probability is the probability that the cyclomatic number of a random cuckoo graph exceeds \\( \\log n + O(1) \\), plus \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (Theorem 8). Encoding costs \\( O((d + \\lambda)^3 + n\\lambda) \\), and decoding one key XORs about \\( (\\lambda + d + 2)/2 \\) positions on average.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Garbled cuckoo table: a PaXoS of size \\( (2 + \\varepsilon)n + d + \\lambda \\) with \\( O(n\\lambda) \\) encoding and \\( O(\\lambda) \\)-position decoding, hash functions independent of the keys","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","sections":[{"content":"Garbled cuckoo table","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$1f","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"prty20-garbled-cuckoo-table-a","status":"published","subtitle":"PSI from PaXoS: Fast, Malicious Private Set Intersection","summary":"Two hash functions map each key to an edge of a cuckoo graph on the m main positions L, and a random vector r(x) selects positions among \\( d + \\lambda \\) extra positions R. The encoder solves the constraints of the edges in the graph's 2-core by Gaussian elimination over the 2-core positions and R, then fixes every other position by traversing the remaining forest away from the 2-core. With \\( d = (1 + \\varepsilon) \\log n \\) this is a PaXoS whose failure probability is the probability that the cyclomatic number of a random cuckoo graph exceeds \\( \\log n + O(1) \\), plus \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (Theorem 8). Encoding costs \\( O((d + \\lambda)^3 + n\\lambda) \\), and decoding one key XORs about \\( (\\lambda + d + 2)/2 \\) positions on average.","title":"Garbled cuckoo table: a PaXoS of size \\( (2 + \\varepsilon)n + d + \\lambda \\) with \\( O(n\\lambda) \\) encoding and \\( O(\\lambda) \\)-position decoding, hash functions independent of the keys","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","paxos-psi","malicious-psi","oos-ot-extension","linear-code","xor-homomorphism","random-oracle","linear-communication"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"malicious-paxos-psi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest","malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["paxos","ot_extension"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Malicious PSI lagged far behind semi-honest PSI. The leading semi-honest protocols relied on cuckoo hashing, which leaks through the choice of bin, and the leading malicious protocol, Rindal and Rosulek's dual execution, paid \\( O(n \\log n) \\) communication for hashing into small bins. Pinkas, Rosulek, Trieu, and Yanai noticed that the outputs of the malicious OOS OT extension are XOR-homomorphic, the property that PaXoS decoding respects. If the receiver feeds an encoding of its hashed items into OOS, both parties can compute the same masked value for a common item, and the sender sends one hash per item. Random-oracle hashing on both sides lets a simulator extract inputs, and changing only the code turns the semi-honest protocol into a malicious one.","prior_boundary":"Semi-honest OT-based PSI used cuckoo hashing, but in the malicious setting a party's choice of cuckoo location leaks information about its other items, and how to use cuckoo hashing there was unknown (§1, p. 2). The malicious protocol that the authors name the most concretely efficient, Rindal and Rosulek's dual execution, hashes items into \\( O(n/\\log n) \\) bins and has \\( O(n \\log n) \\) communication (§1, p. 2; §1.1, p. 3; Appendix D, pp. 34-35).","significance_at_publication":"The authors call it the first concretely efficient PSI with linear communication and malicious security in the OT-hybrid model with a non-programmable random oracle, and the first to use cuckoo hashing for malicious PSI (abstract, p. 1; §1.1, pp. 2-3). For \\( 2^{20} \\) items the malicious 2-core variant sends 202.04 MB against 1,322 MB for the encode-commit variant of Rindal and Rosulek (Table 2, p. 24).","technical_delta":"The protocol combines the XOR-homomorphism of OOS outputs, which OOS itself uses for a consistency check, with PaXoS decoding (§4.1, pp. 9-10; Figure 3, p. 11). H1 lets the simulator extract a malicious receiver's set from D, and including x in H2 lets it extract a malicious sender's set (§4.2, p. 10); Theorem 4 (p. 10) is proved in Lemmas 5-6 (pp. 12-14). The code and hash lengths select the semi-honest or malicious instantiation (§4.4, pp. 14-16; Figures 4-5, pp. 15-16)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI","keywords":["paxos-psi","malicious-psi","oos-ot-extension","linear-code","xor-homomorphism","random-oracle","linear-communication"],"limitations":["the introduction states a 25% communication improvement over KKRT for the semi-honest instantiation (§1.1, p. 2), but Table 1 gives about \\( 1268n \\) bits against \\( 1018n \\) for KKRT at \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (p. 21) and §7.3 reports the best variant within 10% above KKRT (p. 23)","in the semi-honest setting the 2-core variant is 18% slower than KKRT on a LAN and about 80% slower over the two WANs for \\( 2^{20} \\) items (§7.3, p. 23)","the protocol is designed for sets of roughly equal size (Appendix D, p. 36)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","qualifiers":["static corruption of one of the two parties (§2, p. 5; Theorem 4, p. 10)","OOS is used as an ideal functionality, and H1, H2 are non-programmable random oracles (abstract, p. 1; §4.2, p. 10)","the ideal functionality lets a corrupt party contribute a set of size \\( n' > n \\); for a malicious receiver the paper bounds \\( n' \\) between \\( 2m \\) and \\( 5m \\) in its parameter tables (§2, p. 5; Claim 7, p. 15; Figure 5, p. 16)","only the receiver learns the intersection (Figure 1, p. 6)","linear communication means \\( O(n\\kappa) \\); the authors note that concrete hash lengths may involve an \\( O(\\log n) \\) term, as in any intersection protocol (§6, p. 22)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1-1.1 (pp. 2-3); §2, OOS functionality and Lemma 1 (pp. 4-5); Figure 1 (p. 6); §4.1-4.3 (pp. 9-10); Theorem 4 (p. 10); Figure 3 (p. 11); Lemmas 5-6 (pp. 12-14); §4.4 and Figures 4-5 (pp. 14-16); §6 and Table 1 (pp. 21-22); §7.3 and Table 2 (pp. 23-24). IACR ePrint 2020/193, PDF dated 2020-02-16.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"The receiver encodes the pairs \\( (y, H_1(y)) \\) for its set into a PaXoS D and uses the strings of D as its choice inputs to the OOS 1-out-of-N OT extension. Because the code C is linear and PaXoS decoding is XOR-homomorphic, the receiver's value \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(R, x) \\) equals \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, x) \\oplus C(H_1(x)) \\wedge s \\), which the sender can compute, whenever x is in the receiver's set; the sender sends \\( H_2(x, \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, x) \\oplus C(H_1(x)) \\wedge s) \\) for each of its items. The protocol is a secure two-party PSI against a malicious sender or receiver in the random oracle model with OOS as an ideal functionality, where a corrupt party may use a set of size \\( n' > n \\) (Theorem 4). The semi-honest instantiation is the same protocol with a shorter code and hash outputs.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious-secure two-party PSI with communication linear in the set size, from any PaXoS and OOS OT extension, random oracle model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","sections":[{"content":"PaXoS-PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$20","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: the protocols compared in the paper reduce PSI to many OTs implemented with OT extension (§1, p. 2); the OOS extension that PaXoS-PSI runs is a 1-out-of-N extension in the IKNP paradigm (§2, p. 4; §4.1, p. 9). OOS itself and the Kolesnikov-Kumaresan paper behind Lemma 1 (p. 4) have no record in any topic yet.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"prty20-malicious-psi-with-linear","status":"published","subtitle":"PSI from PaXoS: Fast, Malicious Private Set Intersection","summary":"The receiver encodes the pairs \\( (y, H_1(y)) \\) for its set into a PaXoS D and uses the strings of D as its choice inputs to the OOS 1-out-of-N OT extension. Because the code C is linear and PaXoS decoding is XOR-homomorphic, the receiver's value \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(R, x) \\) equals \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, x) \\oplus C(H_1(x)) \\wedge s \\), which the sender can compute, whenever x is in the receiver's set; the sender sends \\( H_2(x, \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, x) \\oplus C(H_1(x)) \\wedge s) \\) for each of its items. The protocol is a secure two-party PSI against a malicious sender or receiver in the random oracle model with OOS as an ideal functionality, where a corrupt party may use a set of size \\( n' > n \\) (Theorem 4). The semi-honest instantiation is the same protocol with a shorter code and hash outputs.","title":"Malicious-secure two-party PSI with communication linear in the set size, from any PaXoS and OOS OT extension, random oracle model","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-PAXOS","keywords":["atomic-result","paxos","probe-and-xor-of-strings","key-value-encoding","garbled-bloom-filter","linear-system","homomorphic-decoding"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"paxos","contribution_kind":"definition","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"technique":["paxos"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Garbled Bloom filters already let a party hide a set of keys inside a table whose values are recovered by XOR, but the table grew with the statistical parameter, and a random linear system, the compact alternative, was too slow to solve for large sets. Pinkas, Rosulek, Trieu, and Yanai isolated what such encodings must provide: a public probing rule, fixed before the inputs are known, whose rows for any n keys are linearly independent with overwhelming probability, and a decoder that commutes with XOR, linear maps, and bitwise AND. Treating garbled Bloom filters and random matrices as instances of one object let them write a PSI protocol once for any encoding and made rate and encoding time explicit targets for new constructions.","prior_boundary":"Garbled Bloom filters, introduced by Dong, Chen, and Wen, encode n items into \\( \\Theta(\\lambda n) \\) positions with \\( O(n\\lambda) \\) encoding and decode by XOR of at most λ positions; a random Boolean matrix reaches \\( m = n + \\lambda \\) but needs \\( O(n^3) \\) Gaussian elimination and decodes by XOR of about \\( n/2 \\) positions; a Bloomier-filter variant reaches linear time only by choosing hash functions that depend on the input set (§3.5, pp. 8-9; Figure 2, p. 9).","significance_at_publication":"The abstraction separates the PSI protocol from the data structure: the protocol of Figure 3 is written for any PaXoS (§4, pp. 9-11). The authors require the map to be independent of the inputs, since an input-dependent choice would leak them, which excludes the Bloomier-filter variant (§3.1, p. 6; §3.5, p. 9).","technical_delta":"Definition 3 (p. 6) abstracts these structures as a probe-and-XOR of strings, with Encode solving a linear system and Decode computing the inner product of v(x) with the data structure (§3.2, pp. 6-7); §3.3 (p. 7) states the homomorphic properties that the PSI protocol uses, and §3.4 (pp. 7-8) names rate, encoding cost, and decoding cost as the efficiency measures."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-PAXOS","keywords":["paxos","probe-and-xor-of-strings","key-value-encoding","garbled-bloom-filter","linear-system","homomorphic-decoding"],"limitations":["Definition 3 requires only linear independence of the probe vectors; it defines no hiding property for the encoded data structure (Definition 3, p. 6)","rate and encoding cost are efficiency measures, not part of the definition; a random matrix satisfies the definition with poor encoding cost (§3.1, p. 6; §3.5, p. 8)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","qualifiers":["failure probability \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) over the choice of the random function H (Definition 3, p. 6)","the probing rule must not depend on the inputs; the Bloomier-filter variant of Charles and Chellapilla is not a PaXoS for this reason (§3.1, p. 6; §3.5, pp. 8-9)","values are bit strings and decoding uses only 0/1 coefficients (§3.2, pp. 6-7)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 2); Definition 3 (p. 6); §3.2-3.4 (pp. 6-8); §3.5 and Figure 2 (pp. 8-9). IACR ePrint 2020/193, PDF dated 2020-02-16.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"An \\( (n, m, 2^{-\\lambda}) \\)-PaXoS is a randomized map v from strings to m-bit vectors such that, for any n distinct inputs, the vectors are linearly independent except with probability \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) over the random function. It encodes n key-value pairs as m strings D by solving \\( M \\cdot D = (y_1, \\ldots, y_n) \\), and decodes a key x as the XOR of the positions of D selected by v(x). Decoding commutes with XOR of two encodings, with linear maps, and with bitwise AND by a fixed string, and the map is fixed independently of the inputs.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Probe-and-XOR of strings (PaXoS): an input-independent binary key-value encoding with XOR-homomorphic decoding","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","sections":[{"content":"Probe-and-XOR of strings","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$21","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"prty20-paxos-key-value-encoding","status":"published","subtitle":"PSI from PaXoS: Fast, Malicious Private Set Intersection","summary":"An \\( (n, m, 2^{-\\lambda}) \\)-PaXoS is a randomized map v from strings to m-bit vectors such that, for any n distinct inputs, the vectors are linearly independent except with probability \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) over the random function. It encodes n key-value pairs as m strings D by solving \\( M \\cdot D = (y_1, \\ldots, y_n) \\), and decodes a key x as the XOR of the positions of D selected by v(x). Decoding commutes with XOR of two encodings, with linear maps, and with bitwise AND by a fixed string, and the map is fixed independently of the inputs.","title":"Probe-and-XOR of strings (PaXoS): an input-independent binary key-value encoding with XOR-homomorphic decoding","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-PAXOS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND","keywords":["atomic-result","input-extraction","incompressibility","random-oracle","malicious-receiver","set-size-slack","paxos"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"receiver-set-size-bound","contribution_kind":"security_analysis","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"technique":["paxos"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"In malicious PSI a simulator must read off the set that a corrupt receiver effectively used, and an encoding sized for n items might be crafted to agree with a random oracle on more of them. Pinkas, Rosulek, Trieu, and Yanai bounded this slack without any computational assumption. A PaXoS of m strings that matched the oracle on many queried points could be used to describe the oracle in fewer bits than it contains, which is possible only with small probability. For practical parameters the extracted set exceeds a small multiple of m with probability below \\( 2^{-40} \\), at the price of longer oracle outputs and longer codewords in the malicious instantiation.","prior_boundary":"Malicious PSI functionalities, as in Rindal and Rosulek's protocols, already let a corrupt party use more items than it advertises (§2, p. 5). In PaXoS-PSI the simulator extracts a malicious receiver's set as the queried y with \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(D, y) = H_1(y) \\), and the protocol by itself does not limit how many such y a crafted D satisfies (§4.4, p. 15).","significance_at_publication":"The bound fixes the slack between the advertised and extracted set sizes in the malicious instantiation and is why its hash outputs and codewords are longer than in the semi-honest one (§4.4, pp. 14-16; Figures 4-5, pp. 15-16); it applies only once \\( n' \\) exceeds m, since a structure of m strings can always encode m values (§4.4, p. 16).","technical_delta":"Claim 7 (p. 15), proved as Lemma 10 (pp. 27-29), shows that a D agreeing with H1 on many queried points would compress H1, which Proposition 9 (p. 27) rules out; Figure 5 (p. 16) turns the bound into parameters with probability below \\( 2^{-40} \\) of extracting more than \\( n' \\) items, for \\( n' \\) from \\( 2m \\) to \\( 5m \\) and \\( 2^{128} \\) queries."},"historical_context_status":"curator_synthesis","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND","keywords":["input-extraction","incompressibility","random-oracle","malicious-receiver","set-size-slack","paxos"],"limitations":["the bound allows the extracted set to exceed the advertised size by a constant factor; it does not enforce \\( n' = n \\) (§4.3, p. 10; §4.4, p. 16)","the sender side is handled separately by choosing H2 outputs of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits so that collisions are negligible (§4.4, p. 15)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","qualifiers":["random oracle model; the probability is over H1 and holds against unbounded adversaries making q queries (Claim 7, p. 15; Lemma 10, pp. 27-29)","the parameters of Figure 5 assume \\( 2^{128} \\) queries; the authors note that \\( 2^{80} \\) queries would allow \\( \\ell_1 \\) of 70 to 90 bits (p. 16, footnote 4)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"§2 (p. 5); §4.3 (p. 10); §4.4, Claim 7 (p. 15); Figure 5 (p. 16); Appendix A, Proposition 9 and Lemma 10 (pp. 27-29). IACR ePrint 2020/193, PDF dated 2020-02-16.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"If an adversary makes q queries to a random oracle H1 with \\( \\ell_1 \\)-bit outputs and then outputs a PaXoS D of m strings, the probability that \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(D, y) = H_1(y) \\) holds for at least \\( n' \\) of the queried values y is at most the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{q}{n'} \\) divided by \\( 2^{(n' - m)\\ell_1} \\). The bound is information-theoretic and bounds the set that the simulator extracts from a malicious receiver in PaXoS-PSI; it constrains \\( n' \\) only when \\( n' \\) exceeds m.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Bound on the number of random-oracle values a malicious receiver can encode in a PaXoS of m strings, by an incompressibility argument","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","year":2020},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/193","sections":[{"content":"Bound on a malicious receiver's effective set","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The proof builds a compression scheme for the random oracle H1 from any adversary that produces a PaXoS consistent with H1 on \\( n' \\) queried points (Lemma 10, pp. 27-29). The compressed description lists D (\\( m \\cdot \\ell_1 \\) bits), the indices of the \\( n' \\) consistent queries, and the oracle answers for all other points; the decompressor recovers each consistent answer by decoding D. Proposition 9 (p. 27) bounds the success probability of any such scheme by the ratio of description sizes, which yields the stated bound. The simulator of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-MALICIOUS-PAXOS-PSI extracts exactly this set from a malicious receiver (Lemma 5, p. 12), so the bound determines how many items a corrupt receiver can test. Figure 5 (p. 16) lists, for PaXoS sizes \\( m \\) from \\( 2^{12} \\) to \\( 2^{24} \\), output lengths \\( \\ell_1 \\) from 129 to 233 bits and the corresponding codes for extracted sets of size \\( 2m \\) to \\( 5m \\).","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"prty20-bound-on-the-number","status":"published","subtitle":"PSI from PaXoS: Fast, Malicious Private Set Intersection","summary":"If an adversary makes q queries to a random oracle H1 with \\( \\ell_1 \\)-bit outputs and then outputs a PaXoS D of m strings, the probability that \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(D, y) = H_1(y) \\) holds for at least \\( n' \\) of the queried values y is at most the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{q}{n'} \\) divided by \\( 2^{(n' - m)\\ell_1} \\). The bound is information-theoretic and bounds the set that the simulator extracts from a malicious receiver in PaXoS-PSI; it constrains \\( n' \\) only when \\( n' \\) exceeds m.","title":"Bound on the number of random-oracle values a malicious receiver can encode in a PaXoS of m strings, by an incompressibility argument","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2020","year":2020,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-RECEIVER-SET-SIZE-BOUND"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES","keywords":["atomic-result","threshold-psi","multiparty","ideal-functionality","cardinality-testing","definition"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"functionalities","contribution_kind":"definition","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["threshold"],"parties":["multiparty"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Threshold PSI had been defined for two parties, where saying that each set is close to the intersection and saying that the two sets are close to each other amount to the same thing. With more parties the two readings separate: if each of n parties holds T elements that no one else has, every set lies within T of the intersection, yet the union exceeds it by \\( nT \\) elements. Badrinarayanan, Miao, Raghuraman, and Rindal therefore defined two multiparty functionalities, one requiring every party's set to lie within T of the intersection and one requiring the union to lie within T of it, with matching tests that only reveal whether the condition holds. Applications choose between them, and the known techniques reach them from different assumptions.","prior_boundary":"Ghosh and Simkin defined two-party threshold PSI, releasing the intersection when the symmetric set difference is small, and studied its communication (§1, p. 2). Earlier threshold PSI protocols computed the cardinality first and compared it with the threshold (§1, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"The distinction organizes the paper's results: both functionalities need \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) communication and admit \\( O(nT) \\) from threshold FHE, but only the union-based one has a protocol from threshold additively homomorphic encryption (Table 1, p. 3).","technical_delta":"The two-party condition is generalized in two inequivalent ways, by each party's distance to the intersection and by the union's distance to it, with ideal functionalities for threshold PSI and for cardinality testing in the UC framework (§1.1, p. 2; §2.3-2.4, pp. 6-7; Figures 1-4)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES","keywords":["threshold-psi","multiparty","ideal-functionality","cardinality-testing","definition"],"limitations":["the functionalities are defined for sets of equal size (§2.3, p. 6)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","qualifiers":["all sets have the same size m and elements lie in a field \\( \\mathbb{F}_p \\) with p a \\( \\Theta(\\lambda) \\)-bit prime (§2.3, p. 6)","with equal set sizes, under \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) either all parties learn the output or none does (§2.3, p. 6)","which functionality and threshold to use depends on the application (§1.1, pp. 2-3)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (pp. 2-3); §2.3 with Figures 1-2 (p. 6); §2.4 with Figures 3-4 (pp. 6-7); Table 1 (p. 3). IACR ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"For n parties each holding m elements with intersection I, \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) releases I to every party if and only if \\( |S_i \\setminus I| \\le T \\) for all i, equivalently \\( |I| \\ge m - T \\), and \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) releases I if and only if \\( |(S_1 \\cup \\cdots \\cup S_n) \\setminus I| \\le T \\); for two parties the conditions coincide up to the factor 2 in \\( |(S_1 \\cup S_2) \\setminus I| = 2|S_i \\setminus I| \\), while for \\( n > 2 \\) only \\( 2|S_i \\setminus I| \\le |(\\cup S_i) \\setminus I| \\le n|S_i \\setminus I| \\) holds, so the two can lead to very different outcomes; matching cardinality-testing functionalities \\( F_{\\text{CTest-int}} \\) and \\( F_{\\text{CTest-diff}} \\) output only similar or different.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Two multiparty threshold PSI functionalities: release when every party's set is close to the intersection, or when the union is close to it","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"Multiparty threshold PSI functionalities","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Figure 1 (p. 6) defines \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) and Figure 2 (p. 6) \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\); Figures 3-4 (p. 7) define the cardinality tests \\( F_{\\text{CTest-int}} \\) and \\( F_{\\text{CTest-diff}} \\), which output similar or different. The paper's protocols first run a cardinality test and, if it passes, a common second phase that lets each party learn \\( S_i \\setminus I \\) (§1.1, pp. 3-4).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"bmrr21-two-multiparty-threshold-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"Multi-Party Threshold Private Set Intersection with Sublinear Communication","summary":"For n parties each holding m elements with intersection I, \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) releases I to every party if and only if \\( |S_i \\setminus I| \\le T \\) for all i, equivalently \\( |I| \\ge m - T \\), and \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) releases I if and only if \\( |(S_1 \\cup \\cdots \\cup S_n) \\setminus I| \\le T \\); for two parties the conditions coincide up to the factor 2 in \\( |(S_1 \\cup S_2) \\setminus I| = 2|S_i \\setminus I| \\), while for \\( n > 2 \\) only \\( 2|S_i \\setminus I| \\le |(\\cup S_i) \\setminus I| \\le n|S_i \\setminus I| \\) holds, so the two can lead to very different outcomes; matching cardinality-testing functionalities \\( F_{\\text{CTest-int}} \\) and \\( F_{\\text{CTest-diff}} \\) output only similar or different.","title":"Two multiparty threshold PSI functionalities: release when every party's set is close to the intersection, or when the union is close to it","type":"result","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-FUNCTIONALITIES"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-LOWER-BOUND","keywords":["atomic-result","lower-bound","communication-complexity","multiparty-threshold-psi","point-to-point","broadcast","set-disjointness"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"lower-bound","contribution_kind":"boundary_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["threshold"],"parties":["multiparty"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Once two-party threshold PSI was known to need communication linear in the threshold, the multiparty question was whether more parties must pay proportionally more. Badrinarayanan, Miao, Raghuraman, and Rindal answered with a simple simulation argument: if any one party could take part while exchanging fewer than order T bits, a two-party protocol could be built in which one side plays that party and the other plays everyone else, and that protocol would beat the two-party bound. Every party therefore carries order T communication, and with only point-to-point channels the total is order \\( nT \\). A separate reduction from multiparty set disjointness gives a weaker bound when broadcast is available, a setting the authors leave for further study.","prior_boundary":"Ghosh and Simkin proved an \\( \\Omega(T) \\) lower bound for two-party threshold PSI (§1, p. 2; §3.5, p. 15). No multiparty bound was known; the paper asks whether sublinear lower and upper bounds extend to more parties (§1, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"Together with the paper's \\( O(nT) \\) protocols from threshold FHE, the bound settles the point-to-point communication complexity of both functionalities up to the omitted \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) factor (Table 1, p. 3).","technical_delta":"A protocol in which some party \\( P_{i^*} \\) communicates \\( o(T) \\) is turned into a two-party threshold PSI protocol by letting one party emulate \\( P_{i^*} \\) and the other emulate all remaining parties with the same input, contradicting the two-party bound (Theorem 4.1, pp. 15-16; Theorem 4.3, pp. 16-17). The broadcast bound reduces multiparty set disjointness, whose complexity Braverman and Oshman showed to be \\( \\Omega(N \\log n + n) \\), to \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) with \\( T = N - 1 \\) (Theorem C.1, pp. 40-42)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-LOWER-BOUND","keywords":["lower-bound","communication-complexity","multiparty-threshold-psi","point-to-point","broadcast","set-disjointness"],"limitations":["the point-to-point bound does not necessarily apply with broadcast channels (Appendix C, p. 40)","a broadcast lower bound for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) is left open (Appendix C, p. 42)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","qualifiers":["semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) parties (Theorems 4.1 and 4.3, pp. 15-16)","a party's communication counts both incoming and outgoing messages (footnote 7, p. 15)","the \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) total holds for point-to-point networks, which include the star network (§1.1, p. 4; §4, p. 15)","the broadcast bound is proved only for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) (Theorem C.1, p. 40)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 and Table 1 (pp. 3-4); §3.5 (pp. 14-15); §4.1 with Theorem 4.1 and Corollary 4.2 (pp. 15-16); §4.2 with Theorem 4.3 and Corollary 4.4 (pp. 16-17); Appendix C with Theorem C.1 (pp. 40-42). IACR ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"In any multiparty threshold PSI protocol for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) or \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) secure against a semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) of n parties, every party sends and receives \\( \\Omega(T) \\) bits, so in a network of point-to-point channels the total communication is \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) (Theorems 4.1 and 4.3, Corollaries 4.2 and 4.4); in the broadcast model any protocol for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) needs \\( \\Omega(T \\log n + n) \\) (Theorem C.1).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"\\( \\Omega(nT) \\) communication lower bound for multiparty threshold PSI in point-to-point networks, semi-honest with up to \\( n - 1 \\) corruptions","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"\\( \\Omega(nT) \\) lower bound for multiparty threshold PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"For \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\), the emulating parties learn the output iff \\( |X_1 \\setminus I| \\le T \\), which for equal set sizes is the two-party condition \\( |(X_1 \\setminus X_2) \\cup (X_2 \\setminus X_1)| \\le 2T \\); for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) the reduction yields the two-party condition with threshold T (§4.1-4.2, pp. 15-17). Corruption of either emulating party is simulated by corrupting \\( P_{i^*} \\) alone or all other parties (Theorem 4.1, p. 15).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"bmrr21-omega-nt-lower-bound","status":"published","subtitle":"Multi-Party Threshold Private Set Intersection with Sublinear Communication","summary":"In any multiparty threshold PSI protocol for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) or \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) secure against a semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) of n parties, every party sends and receives \\( \\Omega(T) \\) bits, so in a network of point-to-point channels the total communication is \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) (Theorems 4.1 and 4.3, Corollaries 4.2 and 4.4); in the broadcast model any protocol for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) needs \\( \\Omega(T \\log n + n) \\) (Theorem C.1).","title":"\\( \\Omega(nT) \\) communication lower bound for multiparty threshold PSI in point-to-point networks, semi-honest with up to \\( n - 1 \\) corruptions","type":"result","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-LOWER-BOUND"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","threshold-psi","threshold-additive-homomorphic-encryption","hankel-matrix","half-gcd","singularity-testing","polynomial-sparsity-test","garbled-circuits"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"tahe-threshold-psi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["threshold"],"parties":["multiparty","two_party"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["additively_homomorphic_encryption","garbled_circuits","polynomial_sparsity_test"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The two-party threshold PSI protocol from additively homomorphic encryption had communication quadratic in the threshold because its cardinality test checked singularity of a general encrypted matrix. Badrinarayanan, Miao, Raghuraman, and Rindal observed that the matrix in question is a Hankel matrix, determined by a linear number of entries, and that a classical result of Brent, Gustavson, and Yun ties its singularity to a half-GCD computation that runs in quasi-linear time. Shared among the parties and tested with a garbled circuit or homomorphic evaluation, the test costs quasi-linear communication. Combined with an encoding whose monomials count the elements outside the intersection across all parties, this gave a multiparty protocol without fully homomorphic encryption and, for two parties, quasi-linear communication in the threshold.","prior_boundary":"Ghosh and Simkin's two-party threshold PSI from additively homomorphic encryption cost \\( \\tilde O(T^2) \\), dominated by the Kiltz-Mohassel-Weinreb-Franklin singularity test for arbitrary matrices, and they left \\( \\tilde O(T) \\) from assumptions weaker than FHE open (§1, p. 2; §1.2, p. 4; §3.3, p. 13).","significance_at_publication":"The paper states that the case \\( n = 2 \\) solves the Ghosh-Simkin open problem (abstract, p. 1; §1.2, p. 4; §3.3, p. 13). Its multiparty protocol is computationally more efficient than the threshold-FHE protocol and almost matches the \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) bound (abstract, p. 1; §1.1, p. 3).","technical_delta":"Hankel matrices have only \\( 2k + 1 \\) distinct entries, and by Brent, Gustavson, and Yun their singularity reduces to a half-GCD computation of \\( O(k \\log^2 k) \\) cost (Imported Theorems 6.1-6.3, p. 25). The parties share the Hankel matrix additively and run \\( F_{\\mathrm{SingTest}} \\), instantiated either by homomorphic evaluation following Franklin-Haber and Cramer-Damgård-Nielsen, with \\( O(k \\log^2 k) \\) rounds, or by jointly generating a Yao garbled circuit for the test, with \\( O(1) \\) rounds (§6.1, pp. 25-26; Figure 8 and Theorem 6.5, p. 27)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI","keywords":["threshold-psi","threshold-additive-homomorphic-encryption","hankel-matrix","half-gcd","singularity-testing","polynomial-sparsity-test","garbled-circuits"],"limitations":["only \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) is covered; for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) a protocol from threshold additively homomorphic encryption is marked unknown (Table 1, p. 3; §1.1, p. 4)","no security against malicious parties is claimed (§1.1, p. 3)","no implementation or measurement is reported (§§6-7, pp. 24-30)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","qualifiers":["semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) parties (Theorem 7.1, p. 28)","Theorem 6.5 is proved in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{SingTest}} \\)-hybrid model and needs a prime \\( q > T(T + 1)(p - 1)2^{\\kappa} \\); the sparsity test errs with probability at most \\( 2^{-\\kappa} \\) (Imported Theorem 6.4 and Theorem 6.5, p. 27)","the constant-round singularity test assumes a PRG computable in \\( \\mathrm{NC}^1 \\) (§6.1, p. 26)","threshold additive homomorphic encryption can be obtained from ElGamal under DDH or Paillier under decisional composite residuosity (§2.6, p. 10)","\\( \\tilde O \\) hides polylogarithmic factors and the bounds omit \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) (footnote 1, p. 3)","in the two-party case \\( F_{\\mathrm{SingTest}} \\) uses Yao's garbled circuits with communication \\( \\tilde O(k \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) (§6.1, p. 26), and the second phase uses additively homomorphic encryption with Alice holding the secret key, at \\( O(T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) (§7.1, p. 29)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 with Tables 1-2 (pp. 3-4); §1.2 (p. 4); §2.6 (p. 10); §3.3 (pp. 12-13); §6.1 with Imported Theorems 6.1-6.3 and Figure 7 (pp. 24-26); §6.2 with Imported Theorem 6.4, Figure 8, Theorem 6.5, and Corollary 6.6 (pp. 26-28); §7 with Theorem 7.1, Corollary 7.2, §7.1, and Figure 9 (pp. 28-30). IACR ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Assuming n-out-of-n threshold additively homomorphic encryption with distributed setup, \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) is securely realized against a semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) parties in a star network with communication \\( \\tilde O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) and \\( O(1) \\) rounds: the parties obtain additive shares of the \\( (T + 1) \\times (T + 1) \\) Hankel matrix of the monomial encoding \\( p(x) = (n - 1)p_1(x) - p_2(x) - \\cdots - p_n(x) \\), whose number of monomials is \\( |(\\cup S_i) \\setminus I| \\), and test its singularity with an MPC protocol of communication \\( \\tilde O(T \\cdot n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) built on the half-GCD connection; for \\( n = 2 \\) the singularity test runs in Yao's garbled circuits and the intersection step uses additively homomorphic encryption alone, which the authors state yields two-party threshold PSI with communication \\( \\tilde O(T) \\) from assumptions weaker than FHE.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Threshold PSI with \\( \\tilde O(nT) \\) communication from threshold additively homomorphic encryption via Hankel singularity testing in quasi-linear cost; \\( \\tilde O(T) \\) for two parties","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"\\( \\tilde O(nT) \\) threshold PSI from threshold additively homomorphic encryption","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"\\( \\Pi_{\\text{CTest-diff}} \\) (Figure 8, p. 27): the central party picks u; party 1 encodes its set as \\( \\sum (n - 1)x^a \\) and every other party as \\( -\\sum x^a \\); each party evaluates its polynomial at \\( u^0, \\ldots, u^{2T} \\), and these values define additive shares of the Hankel matrix H; \\( F_{\\mathrm{SingTest}} \\) reports whether H is singular, which happens exactly when the encoding has at most T monomials, except with probability \\( 2^{-\\kappa} \\). If the test passes, the second phase of Figure 9 (p. 30) computes the intersection. The communication is \\( \\tilde O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) with \\( O(1) \\) rounds (Corollaries 6.6 and 7.2, p. 28).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-YAO and MPC-COMP-ENC-GARBLED: the constant-round singularity test generates and evaluates a Yao garbled circuit, and the two-party test uses Yao's garbled circuits, cited as [Yao86] (§6.1, p. 26; §3.3, p. 13). HE-RESULT-1999-PAILLIER-PRACTICAL-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-PUBLIC-KEY-ENCRYPTION: Paillier is named as a basis for threshold additive homomorphic encryption (§2.6, p. 10).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"bmrr21-tilde-o-nt-threshold","status":"published","subtitle":"Multi-Party Threshold Private Set Intersection with Sublinear Communication","summary":"Assuming n-out-of-n threshold additively homomorphic encryption with distributed setup, \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) is securely realized against a semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) parties in a star network with communication \\( \\tilde O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) and \\( O(1) \\) rounds: the parties obtain additive shares of the \\( (T + 1) \\times (T + 1) \\) Hankel matrix of the monomial encoding \\( p(x) = (n - 1)p_1(x) - p_2(x) - \\cdots - p_n(x) \\), whose number of monomials is \\( |(\\cup S_i) \\setminus I| \\), and test its singularity with an MPC protocol of communication \\( \\tilde O(T \\cdot n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) built on the half-GCD connection; for \\( n = 2 \\) the singularity test runs in Yao's garbled circuits and the intersection step uses additively homomorphic encryption alone, which the authors state yields two-party threshold PSI with communication \\( \\tilde O(T) \\) from assumptions weaker than FHE.","title":"Threshold PSI with \\( \\tilde O(nT) \\) communication from threshold additively homomorphic encryption via Hankel singularity testing in quasi-linear cost; \\( \\tilde O(T) \\) for two parties","type":"result","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TAHE-THRESHOLD-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND","keywords":["atomic-result","multiparty-threshold-psi","threshold-fhe","cardinality-testing","rational-function-interpolation","degree-reduction","star-network"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"tfhe-upper-bound","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["threshold"],"parties":["multiparty"],"security_model":["standard_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["threshold_fhe","additively_homomorphic_encryption","set_reconciliation"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Two-party threshold PSI from fully homomorphic encryption had shown that communication can depend on the threshold alone, but extending it to many parties raised a subtle problem: adding the set polynomials of several parties may cancel roots by accident, so a test based on the degree of a rational function can wrongly report that the sets are close. Badrinarayanan, Miao, Raghuraman, and Rindal multiplied each party's polynomial by a random linear factor, which makes such cancellations negligible, and ran the test under threshold fully homomorphic encryption with one central party. A second phase, built only on threshold additively homomorphic encryption and economical in randomizing polynomials, then lets every party recover its own excess elements, so total communication meets the lower bound.","prior_boundary":"For two parties, Ghosh and Simkin obtained communication \\( O(T) \\) from FHE by homomorphically interpolating the ratio of the two set polynomials and checking it at a random point (§1, p. 2; §3.1, p. 11). Summing several parties' polynomials in the numerator can cancel roots, so the natural multiparty extension may accept sets that differ too much (§3.1, pp. 11-12).","significance_at_publication":"The protocols match the \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) lower bound for both functionalities up to the omitted \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) factor, and their communication does not depend on the set size (Table 1, p. 3; §1.2, p. 4).","technical_delta":"Each party multiplies its set polynomial by a random linear factor \\( (x - r_i) \\), which makes accidental cancellation negligible (Lemma 2.4, p. 10; §3.1, p. 12); for \\( F_{\\text{CTest-diff}} \\) the central party instead interpolates \\( p_i/p_1 \\) for every i and counts the union of differences under encryption (§3.2, p. 12; §5.2, pp. 21-23). The second phase lets each party contribute two randomizing polynomials, reducing \\( O(n^2 T) \\) to \\( O(nT) \\) communication (§3.4, p. 14; Figure 9, p. 30)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND","keywords":["multiparty-threshold-psi","threshold-fhe","cardinality-testing","rational-function-interpolation","degree-reduction","star-network"],"limitations":["no security against malicious parties is claimed (§1.1, p. 3)","relies on threshold FHE for the cardinality test; for \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) no protocol from weaker assumptions is given (Table 1, p. 3)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","qualifiers":["semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) parties (Theorems 7.1 and B.1, pp. 28, 40)","n-out-of-n threshold FHE with distributed setup, available from circular-secure LWE (Imported Theorem 2.2, p. 9; footnote 4)","star network with a designated central party; not all parties need be online at once (§1.1, p. 4; §2.3, p. 6)","the bounds omit a \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) factor (footnote 1, p. 3)","equal set sizes m and a field of order \\( \\Omega(2^{\\lambda}) \\) (§2.3, p. 6; Figure 5, p. 18)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 with Tables 1-2 (pp. 3-4); §2.5 with Definition 2.1 and Imported Theorem 2.2 (pp. 7-9); Lemmas 2.4-2.5 (p. 10); §3.1-3.2 and §3.4 (pp. 11-14); §5.1 with Theorem 5.1 and Figure 5 (pp. 17-21); §5.2 with Theorem 5.4 and Figure 6 (pp. 21-23); §7 with Theorem 7.1, Corollary 7.2, and Figure 9 (pp. 28-30); Appendix B with Theorem B.1, Corollary B.2, and Figure 10 (pp. 39-41). IACR ePrint 2020/600, PDF dated 2021-02-25.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Assuming n-out-of-n threshold FHE with distributed setup, both \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) and \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) are securely realized against a semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) parties in a star network with communication \\( O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) and \\( O(1) \\) rounds: each party sends encrypted evaluations of its set polynomial, randomized by a factor \\( (x - r_i) \\), to a central party that homomorphically interpolates and tests a rational function, the parties jointly decrypt one bit, and if the test passes a second phase from threshold additively homomorphic encryption gives every party \\( 3T + 4 \\) evaluations of a randomized combination of all set polynomials, from which it interpolates \\( S_i \\setminus I \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI with \\( O(nT) \\) communication and \\( O(1) \\) rounds from threshold FHE, matching the \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) bound, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/600","sections":[{"content":"\\( O(nT) \\) multiparty threshold PSI from threshold FHE","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"\\( \\Pi_{\\text{TFHE-CTest-int}} \\) (Figure 5, p. 18): each party sends encryptions of \\( p'_i(j) = p_i(j)(x - r_i) \\) evaluated at \\( j = 1, \\ldots, 2T + 3 \\) and at a random z; the central party interpolates \\( (p'_2 + \\cdots + p'_n)/p'_1 \\) homomorphically, compares it with the value at z, and the parties decrypt the resulting bit (§5.1, p. 17). \\( \\Pi_{\\text{TFHE-CTest-diff}} \\) (Figure 6) interpolates \\( p_i/p_1 \\) for each i (§5.2, pp. 21-23). The second phase, \\( \\Pi_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) (Figure 9, p. 30) and \\( \\Pi_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) (Figure 10, p. 41), evaluates \\( V(x) = \\sum p'_i(x) U_i(x) \\) at \\( 3T + 4 \\) points under threshold additively homomorphic encryption.","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"TSIG-PAPER-2018-BGGJKRS: the threshold FHE with distributed setup follows the definition of Boneh et al. and is instantiated by their scheme (§2.5, p. 7; Imported Theorem 2.2, p. 9). HE-RESULT-1999-PAILLIER-PRACTICAL-ADDITIVELY-HOMOMORPHIC-PUBLIC-KEY-ENCRYPTION: Paillier, like ElGamal, can be converted into the threshold additively homomorphic encryption used in the second phase (§2.6, p. 10).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"bmrr21-multiparty-threshold-psi-wit","status":"published","subtitle":"Multi-Party Threshold Private Set Intersection with Sublinear Communication","summary":"Assuming n-out-of-n threshold FHE with distributed setup, both \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-int}} \\) and \\( F_{\\text{TPSI-diff}} \\) are securely realized against a semi-honest adversary corrupting up to \\( n - 1 \\) parties in a star network with communication \\( O(n \\cdot T \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\) and \\( O(1) \\) rounds: each party sends encrypted evaluations of its set polynomial, randomized by a factor \\( (x - r_i) \\), to a central party that homomorphically interpolates and tests a rational function, the parties jointly decrypt one bit, and if the test passes a second phase from threshold additively homomorphic encryption gives every party \\( 3T + 4 \\) evaluations of a randomized combination of all set polynomials, from which it interpolates \\( S_i \\setminus I \\).","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI with \\( O(nT) \\) communication and \\( O(1) \\) rounds from threshold FHE, matching the \\( \\Omega(nT) \\) bound, semi-honest","type":"result","venue":"PKC 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-BMRR-TFHE-UPPER-BOUND"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS-POWERS","keywords":["atomic-result","frobenius-automorphism","depth-free-exponentiation","low-communication","bgv","helib","fully-homomorphic-encryption","unbalanced-psi"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"frobenius-powers","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"In homomorphic PSI with a small client set, the client's upload is dominated by the extra encrypted powers it sends so that the server can stay within a shallow circuit, and their number grows with the logarithm of the server's bin size. Cong and coauthors observed that the Frobenius automorphism, available in BGV and BFV, raises an encrypted value to a power of the plaintext modulus without consuming depth. With a small modulus, the server can then build every power it needs from a constant number of ciphertexts, and the upload becomes nearly independent of the server's set size. Their prototype in HElib kept online communication under one megabyte for server sets of up to tens of millions of items, but the server computation took minutes, so the variant demonstrates a communication bound rather than a deployable protocol.","prior_boundary":"With windowing, the earlier protocols need \\( O(\\log B) \\) encrypted powers for the sender to compute all powers up to the bin size B at depth \\( O(\\log \\log B) \\), which gives communication \\( \\tilde O(2^C C^2) \\) for \\( C = \\log \\log |X| \\) (§1.2, p. 3; §3.1, p. 11).","significance_at_publication":"For 210 receiver items, online communication stays at 0.76 MB for sender sets of \\( 2^{22} \\) to \\( 2^{26} \\) items, which the authors compare with 18.32 MB for 128 receiver items in the earlier protocol at \\( 2^{24} \\) (abstract, p. 1; Table 6, p. 20). Evaluation keys of 6 to 9 MB are sent once (Table 5, p. 19). Sender online time is 38.7 s at \\( 2^{20} \\) and 354 s at \\( 2^{24} \\) on 24 threads (Table 8, p. 27), and the authors call the variant not practical today (§6, p. 20).","technical_delta":"A Frobenius operation costs about one key switch, adds only additive noise, and is counted as depth-free (§2.3, p. 5). Lemma 2 (p. 12, proved in Appendix C) gives the number \\( e_D \\) of consecutive powers reachable at depth D, so \\( O(1) \\) encrypted powers suffice (§3.1, pp. 11-12). The HElib implementation over BGV, whose cyclotomic order can be chosen freely, demonstrates the effect without partitioning or windowing and without implementing the OPRF or networking (§5, p. 15; §5.4, pp. 18-19)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS-POWERS","keywords":["frobenius-automorphism","depth-free-exponentiation","low-communication","bgv","helib","fully-homomorphic-encryption","unbalanced-psi"],"limitations":["the HElib implementation does not implement the OPRF or networking; communication is computed from ciphertext sizes plus 32 bytes of OPRF per receiver item (§5, p. 15)","sender online computation is impractically high on current CPUs (abstract, p. 1; Table 8, p. 27; §6, p. 20)","the comparison with the earlier protocol uses public-key-mode sizes supplied by its authors and different receiver set sizes (§5.4, p. 19; Table 6, p. 20)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","qualifiers":["requires a small plaintext modulus t; the number of SIMD slots is \\( n/d \\) with d the order of t modulo m, so smaller t gives fewer slots (§3.1, p. 12)","the receiver sends \\( \\lfloor \\log_t B \\rfloor \\) Frobenius evaluation keys, or one key at the cost of more Frobenius operations; keys are independent of the receiver's set and can be cached (§3.1, p. 12; §5.4, p. 19)","SEAL's fixed ring orders leave no suitable small primes, so the technique was implemented only in HElib (§5, p. 15)","the HElib runs use 80-bit hashed items, \\( \\alpha = 1 \\), no windowing or postage-stamp bases, and parameters with at least 128-bit security except one set at 106 bits (§5.4, p. 19; Table 6, p. 20)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.2 (p. 3); §2.3 (pp. 4-5); §3.1 Low depth exponentiation via the Frobenius operation, Examples 2-4, and Lemma 2 (pp. 11-12); §5 (pp. 14-15); §5.4 and Tables 5-6 (pp. 18-20); §6 (p. 20); Appendix C (p. 25); Appendix D and Tables 7-8 (pp. 25-27). IACR ePrint 2021/1116, PDF dated 2021-11-14.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"Since the Frobenius automorphism maps an encryption of y to an encryption of \\( y^{t^i} \\) without consuming a multiplicative level, the sender can compute all powers \\( y, y^2, \\ldots, y^{e_D} \\) within depth D from the single ciphertext of y (Lemma 2), so evaluating the intersection circuit at depth \\( O(\\log \\log |X|) \\) needs only \\( O(1) \\) query ciphertexts, and the communication complexity becomes \\( \\tilde O(C^2) \\) with \\( C = \\log \\log |X| \\) instead of \\( \\tilde O(2^C C^2) \\); the receiver must also send Frobenius evaluation keys, which the sender can cache, and a small plaintext modulus reduces the number of SIMD slots.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Online communication nearly constant in the larger set for FHE-based unbalanced PSI, from depth-free Frobenius powers","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","sections":[{"content":"Depth-free Frobenius powers","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"With plaintext modulus t, the Frobenius operation maps an encryption of y to an encryption of \\( y^{t^i} \\) by a permutation of the Double-CRT representation and a key switch (§2.3, p. 5). For \\( t = 2 \\), from y alone the sender obtains \\( y^2 \\), \\( y^4 \\), \\( y^8 \\) at depth zero, the odd powers up to 15 at depth at most two, and the remaining low and high powers of Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation by further Frobenius maps, so a degree-255 polynomial is evaluated at depth three from one ciphertext (Example 2, p. 11). Lemma 2 (p. 12) generalizes the count to any t and depth D.","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"HE-PAPER-2012-GHS-AES: the Frobenius operation and the Double-CRT representation are used as described by Gentry, Halevi, and Smart, cited as [27] (§2.3, p. 5). HE-PAPER-2012-BGV: the low-communication variant is implemented over BGV in HElib (§2.3, p. 4; §5, p. 14).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"cmg21-online-communication-nearly","status":"published","subtitle":"Labeled PSI from Homomorphic Encryption with Reduced Computation and Communication","summary":"Since the Frobenius automorphism maps an encryption of y to an encryption of \\( y^{t^i} \\) without consuming a multiplicative level, the sender can compute all powers \\( y, y^2, \\ldots, y^{e_D} \\) within depth D from the single ciphertext of y (Lemma 2), so evaluating the intersection circuit at depth \\( O(\\log \\log |X|) \\) needs only \\( O(1) \\) query ciphertexts, and the communication complexity becomes \\( \\tilde O(C^2) \\) with \\( C = \\log \\log |X| \\) instead of \\( \\tilde O(2^C C^2) \\); the receiver must also send Frobenius evaluation keys, which the sender can cache, and a small plaintext modulus reduces the number of SIMD slots.","title":"Online communication nearly constant in the larger set for FHE-based unbalanced PSI, from depth-free Frobenius powers","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-FROBENIUS-POWERS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER","keywords":["atomic-result","paterson-stockmeyer","non-scalar-multiplication","polynomial-evaluation","partitioning","fully-homomorphic-encryption","unbalanced-psi","labeled-psi"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"paterson-stockmeyer","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection","labeled"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Homomorphic PSI with a small client set evaluates, for every bin of the server's hash table, a polynomial whose roots are the bin's items, and the earlier protocols did so by computing every power of the client's encrypted value, one ciphertext multiplication per power. With bins of a few thousand items these multiplications dominated the server's online work. Cong and coauthors applied the classic Paterson-Stockmeyer method: compute about the square root of the degree in low powers and as many high powers, combine the low powers with plaintext coefficients, which is cheap, and multiply only by the high powers. The number of expensive multiplications drops from linear to square root in the bin size, costing at most one extra level of depth, and it combines with partitioning.","prior_boundary":"In the protocols of Chen et al., the sender computes all powers of the receiver's encrypted item up to \\( \\lceil B/\\alpha \\rceil \\) for α partitions of a bin of size B, using \\( \\lceil B/\\alpha \\rceil - 1 \\) non-scalar multiplications, so computation is \\( O(|X|) \\) homomorphic multiplications with depth \\( O(\\log \\log |X|) \\) (§1.1, p. 3; §3.1, p. 7). When B is in the thousands and α about ten, these multiplications can dominate the online time (§3.1, p. 7).","significance_at_publication":"The authors call the result a quadratic improvement in homomorphic multiplications over the earlier labeled-PSI protocol (§1.2, p. 3). With all their optimizations, for \\( 2^{28} \\) sender items and 2048 receiver items they report 4.75 s sender online time on 24 threads against 28.5 s on 32 threads, and 15.09 MB against 22.28 MB of online communication (Table 2, p. 17).","technical_delta":"The intersection polynomial is rewritten as a sum over i of \\( y^{iL} \\) times an inner sum of low powers with plaintext coefficients, so only the multiplications by high powers are non-scalar (eq. (2), p. 8). The count is \\( L + 2H - 4 \\) (eq. (3), p. 8), or \\( L_\\alpha + (\\alpha + 1)H_\\alpha - (\\alpha + 3) \\) with partitioning (eq. (4), p. 8), with the optimum derived in Appendix A; Lemma 1 (p. 9) bounds the receiver powers needed for a target depth."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER","keywords":["paterson-stockmeyer","non-scalar-multiplication","polynomial-evaluation","partitioning","fully-homomorphic-encryption","unbalanced-psi","labeled-psi"],"limitations":["the reported speedups combine this method with the other optimizations of the paper and different FHE parameters, so they are not attributable to it alone (§5.1, p. 15; Table 2, p. 17)","the comparison runs use 24 threads against the earlier protocol's 32, on machines the authors assume comparable (§5, p. 14; Table 1, p. 16)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","qualifiers":["costs are counted in non-scalar multiplications, which dominate both running time and noise growth (§2.3, p. 5)","the slightly faster original Paterson-Stockmeyer variant is not used, because the coefficient ring \\( R_t \\) is not Euclidean (§3.1, p. 8)","with partitioning the naive method can need fewer multiplications for larger α; Example 1 (p. 8) shows the crossover at \\( \\alpha = 6 \\) for \\( B = 81 \\), and Appendix A gives the general condition"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 (p. 3); §1.2 (p. 3); §3.1 Partitioning and Paterson-Stockmeyer (pp. 7-8); eq. (2)-(4) and Example 1 (p. 8); §3.1 Windowing and Lemma 1 (p. 9); Appendix A (pp. 23-24); Tables 1-2 (pp. 16-17). IACR ePrint 2021/1116, PDF dated 2021-11-14.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"In FHE-based unbalanced PSI, the sender can evaluate each bin's intersection polynomial of degree \\( B = LH - 1 \\) on the receiver's encrypted item by computing the low powers \\( y^2, \\ldots, y^{L-1} \\) and the high powers \\( y^L, \\ldots, y^{(H-1)L} \\) and summing high powers times scalar combinations of low powers, which costs \\( L + 2H - 4 \\) ciphertext-ciphertext (non-scalar) multiplications, minimal \\( O(\\sqrt{B}) \\) for \\( L \\approx \\sqrt{2(B + 1)} \\), instead of the \\( \\lceil B/\\alpha \\rceil - 1 \\) needed to compute all powers for α partitions; with partitioning the count is \\( L_\\alpha + (\\alpha + 1)H_\\alpha - (\\alpha + 3) \\), the depth grows by at most one, and the protocol needs \\( O(\\sqrt{|X|}) \\) homomorphic multiplications.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"\\( O(\\sqrt{B}) \\) ciphertext-ciphertext multiplications per sender bin in FHE-based unbalanced PSI, by Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation of the intersection polynomial","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","sections":[{"content":"Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation of the intersection polynomial","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Choose L and H with \\( B = LH - 1 \\). The sender computes \\( y^2, \\ldots, y^{L-1} \\) and \\( y^L, y^{2L}, \\ldots, y^{(H-1)L} \\), and evaluates P(y) as the sum over \\( i < H \\) of \\( y^{iL} \\cdot \\sum_{j<L} a_{iL+j} y^j \\), where the inner sums use only scalar multiplications and additions (eq. (2), p. 8). The depth is \\( \\lceil \\log((H - 1)L + 1) \\rceil + 1 \\), at most one more than computing a degree-B polynomial directly, and window powers sent by the receiver lower it further (§3.1, p. 9). For \\( B = 81 \\), the method needs 23 multiplications without partitioning against 80 for computing all powers (Example 1, p. 8).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"HE-PAPER-2012-FV and HE-PAPER-2012-BGV: the method is implemented over BFV in SEAL and over BGV in HElib (§2.3, p. 4; §5, p. 14). The Paterson-Stockmeyer algorithm (SIAM J. Computing 1973) has no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"cmg21-o-sqrt-b-multiplications","status":"published","subtitle":"Labeled PSI from Homomorphic Encryption with Reduced Computation and Communication","summary":"In FHE-based unbalanced PSI, the sender can evaluate each bin's intersection polynomial of degree \\( B = LH - 1 \\) on the receiver's encrypted item by computing the low powers \\( y^2, \\ldots, y^{L-1} \\) and the high powers \\( y^L, \\ldots, y^{(H-1)L} \\) and summing high powers times scalar combinations of low powers, which costs \\( L + 2H - 4 \\) ciphertext-ciphertext (non-scalar) multiplications, minimal \\( O(\\sqrt{B}) \\) for \\( L \\approx \\sqrt{2(B + 1)} \\), instead of the \\( \\lceil B/\\alpha \\rceil - 1 \\) needed to compute all powers for α partitions; with partitioning the count is \\( L_\\alpha + (\\alpha + 1)H_\\alpha - (\\alpha + 3) \\), the depth grows by at most one, and the protocol needs \\( O(\\sqrt{|X|}) \\) homomorphic multiplications.","title":"\\( O(\\sqrt{B}) \\) ciphertext-ciphertext multiplications per sender bin in FHE-based unbalanced PSI, by Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation of the intersection polynomial","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PATERSON-STOCKMEYER"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES","keywords":["atomic-result","postage-stamp-basis","windowing","query-powers","multiplicative-depth","fully-homomorphic-encryption","unbalanced-psi"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"postage-stamp-bases","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection","labeled"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"To keep the circuit shallow, FHE-based PSI lets the client send several encrypted powers of each query value, from which the server builds all the powers it needs. The earlier protocols chose these powers by windowing, sending digits of a fixed base, which is simple but sends more ciphertexts than necessary. Cong and coauthors recognized the choice as the global postage-stamp problem from additive number theory: find few integers whose sums of at most h terms cover every value up to a bound. Sending powers at an extremal basis keeps the depth at the logarithm of h while needing fewer ciphertexts, for example three instead of five to reach the twenty-sixth power at depth two, and a scaled second basis serves the high powers of their polynomial evaluation.","prior_boundary":"Windowing, as in the earlier protocols, sends the \\( (b - 1)(\\lfloor \\log_b B \\rfloor + 1) \\) powers \\( y^{i \\cdot b^j} \\) so that all powers up to B are reachable at depth \\( \\lceil \\log(\\lfloor \\log_b B \\rfloor + 1) \\rceil \\), with b derived from the target depth (§3.1, p. 9). The authors call it easy to use but far from optimal (§3.1, p. 10).","significance_at_publication":"In the \\( B = 26 \\) example the receiver sends three powers instead of five at the same depth (§3.1, p. 10). Combined with Paterson-Stockmeyer, the six powers {1, 5, 8, 27, 135, 216} support polynomials of degree 728 at depth three (§3.1, p. 11). The authors note that no efficient method for finding such bases is known and that bases with large h are hard to find (§3.1, pp. 10-11).","technical_delta":"Choosing which powers to send is cast as the global postage-stamp problem (Definition 1, p. 10). The sender iterates over the exponents and computes each missing power depth-optimally as a product of two lower ones (Figure 1, p. 10). Brute-forced bases for small instances are taken from the number-theory literature, one for the low powers and one scaled by L for the high powers of Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation (§3.1, pp. 10-11)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES","keywords":["postage-stamp-basis","windowing","query-powers","multiplicative-depth","fully-homomorphic-encryption","unbalanced-psi"],"limitations":["bases with large h can be hard to find and may raise online computation because many powers are computed by non-scalar multiplication (§3.1, p. 11)","the paper reports no measurement isolating this change; the SEAL experiments combine it with the other optimizations (§5, p. 15)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","qualifiers":["the depth bound \\( \\lceil \\log_2 h \\rceil \\) follows from computing each power as a product of two lower powers (§3.1, p. 10)","only small instances are used, for which extremal bases have been found by brute force in the literature (§3.1, p. 11)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.2 (p. 3); §3.1 Windowing (p. 9); §3.1 Extremal postage-stamp bases, Definition 1, and Figure 1 (pp. 10-11). IACR ePrint 2021/1116, PDF dated 2021-11-14.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"If the receiver sends encryptions of \\( y^{a_1}, \\ldots, y^{a_k} \\) for an extremal postage-stamp basis \\( \\{a_1 = 1 < \\cdots < a_k\\} \\), in which every integer up to n is a sum of at most h basis elements, the sender can compute all powers of y up to n within multiplicative depth \\( \\lceil \\log_2 h \\rceil \\); this replaces the windowing of earlier FHE-based PSI, for example with three powers {1, 5, 8} instead of five {1, 2, 4, 8, 16} for all powers up to 26 at depth two, a 40% reduction of receiver-to-sender ciphertexts, and a second basis multiplied by L supplies the high powers of Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Fewer encrypted query powers at a fixed multiplicative depth in FHE-based unbalanced PSI, from extremal postage-stamp bases instead of windowing","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","sections":[{"content":"Extremal postage-stamp bases for the receiver's powers","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Definition 1 (p. 10) asks, for given h and k, for k integers \\( 1 = a_1 < \\cdots < a_k \\) such that every integer up to n is a sum of at most h of them, with n maximal. If the receiver sends encryptions of y at the basis exponents, the sender reaches all powers up to n in depth \\( \\lceil \\log_2 h \\rceil \\). The basis {1, 5, 8} is the unique extremal basis for \\( h = 4 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\) (§3.1, p. 10). For Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation one basis covers the low powers up to \\( L - 1 \\) and the same or another basis multiplied by L covers the high powers (§3.1, p. 11).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"Extremal postage-stamp bases (Challis and Robinson, Journal of Integer Sequences 2010) belong to additive number theory and have no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"cmg21-fewer-encrypted-query-powers","status":"published","subtitle":"Labeled PSI from Homomorphic Encryption with Reduced Computation and Communication","summary":"If the receiver sends encryptions of \\( y^{a_1}, \\ldots, y^{a_k} \\) for an extremal postage-stamp basis \\( \\{a_1 = 1 < \\cdots < a_k\\} \\), in which every integer up to n is a sum of at most h basis elements, the sender can compute all powers of y up to n within multiplicative depth \\( \\lceil \\log_2 h \\rceil \\); this replaces the windowing of earlier FHE-based PSI, for example with three powers {1, 5, 8} instead of five {1, 2, 4, 8, 16} for all powers up to 26 at depth two, a 40% reduction of receiver-to-sender ciphertexts, and a second basis multiplied by L supplies the high powers of Paterson-Stockmeyer evaluation.","title":"Fewer encrypted query powers at a fixed multiplicative depth in FHE-based unbalanced PSI, from extremal postage-stamp bases instead of windowing","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-POSTAGE-STAMP-BASES"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS","keywords":["atomic-result","prime-field-slots","simd-packing","mandatory-oprf","labeled-psi","label-encryption","interpolation","fully-homomorphic-encryption","unbalanced-psi"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"prime-field-slots","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"output":["intersection","labeled"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["unbalanced"],"technique":["fully_homomorphic_encryption","oprf"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The first labeled FHE-based PSI protocol handled long items by packing each hashed item into one slot over an extension field, which made every homomorphic operation slower, most painfully when long labels multiplied the work. Splitting an item across several small prime-field slots looks natural, but each slot value is short enough to guess, so partial matches would leak whenever the oblivious PRF step was skipped, as the earlier protocol allowed. Cong and coauthors made the PRF step mandatory, after which item chunks reveal nothing and prime fields suffice. For labels they encrypt each label under its item's PRF output, interpolate the ciphertext chunk by chunk, add a fresh nonce on updates, and move items whose chunks collide within a bin to another partition.","prior_boundary":"The earlier labeled-PSI protocol supported arbitrary-length items by hashing them into extension-field slots, which the authors say can be devastating for performance, especially with labels, and constrains parameter choices (§3.1, p. 7). It treated the OPRF step as optional, so small slot values could be guessed and partial matches would leak (§3.1, p. 7), and its label interpolation relied on extension fields, where non-zero values can be randomized and items never repeat (§4.1, p. 13; §4.2, p. 14).","significance_at_publication":"The authors expect prime fields to be cheaper than extension fields, while interpolation remains \\( O(B^2) \\) field operations (§4.2, p. 13). With all their optimizations, for 288-byte labels, \\( 2^{20} \\) sender and 256 receiver items, they report 0.66 s sender online time on 16 threads and 11.2 MB, against 4.6 s and 17.6 MB for the earlier protocol (Table 3, p. 18). They also sketch the labeled-case security proof that the earlier paper left implicit (§4.2, p. 14).","technical_delta":"Hashed items are split into prime-field chunks and the OPRF is always applied, so chunks of non-matching items look random (§3.1, p. 7). Labels are encrypted under part of the OPRF output and interpolated chunk by chunk (§4.2, p. 13; Figure 2, p. 13). Appending a random nonce on each update avoids XOR leakage between label versions, and an item whose chunks collide in a partition goes to another or a new partition, since interpolation would otherwise be impossible (§4.2, pp. 13-14)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS","keywords":["prime-field-slots","simd-packing","mandatory-oprf","labeled-psi","label-encryption","interpolation","fully-homomorphic-encryption","unbalanced-psi"],"limitations":["interpolation still costs \\( O(B^2) \\) field operations per bin, mitigated only by the updatability of the precomputation (§4.2, p. 13)","labeled PSI uses more partitions and fills them less than unlabeled PSI because of chunk collisions (§4.2, p. 14)","the labeled-case security is sketched as an extension of the earlier unlabeled proof, not proved in full (§4.2, p. 14)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","qualifiers":["relies on the OPRF of the earlier protocol, which gives security against a malicious receiver and privacy against a malicious sender in the random oracle model; the paper keeps those guarantees and proof (§3, p. 6)","labels longer than the item are split into item-length fragments, each with its own interpolation polynomial, so sender-to-receiver communication grows linearly in the number of fragments (§4.2, p. 14; §5.3, p. 18)","the labeled protocol returns the intersection result together with the label results, so the receiver knows which labels hold valid data (§4.2, p. 14)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.2 (p. 3); §3 (pp. 5-6); §3.1 SIMD packing (p. 7); §4.1-4.2 and Figure 2 (pp. 12-14); §5.3 and Tables 3-4 (pp. 18-19). IACR ePrint 2021/1116, PDF dated 2021-11-14.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"If the OPRF step is always applied, each hashed item can be split across several consecutive prime-field SIMD slots, with the intersection polynomial evaluated on each part independently, instead of occupying one extension-field slot, because partial matches of OPRF outputs reveal nothing about the sender's items; for labeled PSI, each label is encrypted under a key derived from its item's OPRF output, with a fresh random nonce on every update, and the encrypted label is interpolated per slot-sized part, while the sender re-places any item whose parts collide with an existing item in the same bin and partition, at the cost of more partitions and a lower fill rate than unlabeled PSI.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Arbitrary-length items and labels in FHE-based unbalanced PSI over prime-field SIMD slots, enabled by mandatory OPRF hashing","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1116","sections":[{"content":"Prime-field slots for long items and labels","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"After the OPRF step, the receiver places each hashed item in a cuckoo bin and spreads it over several consecutive prime-field slots; the sender evaluates the intersection polynomial of each chunk position independently (§3.1, p. 7). For labels, the OPRF output is extended so that one part represents the item and another keys a symmetric cipher; the sender interpolates the encrypted label chunks (Figure 2, p. 13), and the receiver decrypts the label with the key derived from its own OPRF value (§4.2, p. 13). When inserting an item, the sender checks that none of its chunks already appears in the same positions of the target partition (§4.2, p. 14).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"HE-RESULT-2012-SV-SIMD-CIPHERTEXT-SLOT-PACKING-AND-PARALLEL-RECRYPTION: SIMD packing, cited as [60] (§2.3, p. 5).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"cmg21-arbitrary-length-items-and","status":"published","subtitle":"Labeled PSI from Homomorphic Encryption with Reduced Computation and Communication","summary":"If the OPRF step is always applied, each hashed item can be split across several consecutive prime-field SIMD slots, with the intersection polynomial evaluated on each part independently, instead of occupying one extension-field slot, because partial matches of OPRF outputs reveal nothing about the sender's items; for labeled PSI, each label is encrypted under a key derived from its item's OPRF output, with a fresh random nonce on every update, and the encrypted label is interpolated per slot-sized part, while the sender re-places any item whose parts collide with an existing item in the same bin and partition, at the cost of more partitions and a lower fill rate than unlabeled PSI.","title":"Arbitrary-length items and labels in FHE-based unbalanced PSI over prime-field SIMD slots, enabled by mandatory OPRF hashing","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-CONG-PRIME-FIELD-SLOTS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT","keywords":["atomic-result","3h-gct","garbled-cuckoo-table","hypergraph-peeling","two-core","binary-okvs","okvs-rate"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"3h-gct","contribution_kind":"mechanism","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"technique":["okvs"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The PaXoS encoding placed each item on an edge of a random graph, which keeps encoding linear but leaves more than half of the table slack, and in PaXoS-PSI every table position costs one oblivious transfer. Garimella and coauthors observed that hypergraphs with three positions per item stay peelable down to about \\( 1.23n \\) positions, so the same peel-and-back-substitute strategy works with a table little larger than the data. They kept the short random tail that absorbs the few unpeelable items and argued that, with current techniques, three positions per item is the right choice for peeling. Plugged into PaXoS-PSI, the smaller table cut communication by about forty percent, although the parameter \\( 1.3n \\) rested on experiments rather than a proven bound.","prior_boundary":"PaXoS used a cuckoo graph with two hash functions and about \\( 2.4n \\) positions, a heuristic choice with no proven failure probability, giving rate \\( 0.4 - o(1) \\) (§3, p. 9; Figure 1, p. 8); polynomial OKVS had rate 1 but \\( O(n \\log^2 n) \\) encoding (§3, p. 7).","significance_at_publication":"Instantiating PaXoS-PSI with it, the authors report 1.61 times less communication than PaXoS-PSI with malicious security, because \\( 1.3n \\) OTs replace \\( 2.4n \\) (§8.2, p. 23; Table 1, p. 24), and an encoding of 20.80 MB against 38.40 MB for PaXoS at \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 2, p. 29).","technical_delta":"The table moves from graphs to 3-hypergraphs, which are asymptotically peelable with at least about \\( 1.23n \\) nodes (§4.1, pp. 9-10), keeps the \\( L \\| R \\) structure of PaXoS for the 2-core (§4.1, pp. 10-11), and is specified in Figure 2 (p. 12) with Lemma 6 (p. 11; proof sketch in Appendix A.1, p. 28). The implementation uses \\( 1.3n \\) nodes (§8.1, p. 23)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT","keywords":["3h-gct","garbled-cuckoo-table","hypergraph-peeling","two-core","binary-okvs","okvs-rate"],"limitations":["the authors call \\( m = 1.3n \\) an acceptable heuristic, and their table lists the error analysis of 3H-GCT as heuristic (§8.1, p. 23; Table 2, p. 29)","the rate 0.91 possible with Walzer's hypergraph construction takes effect only for very large n (Figure 1 caption, p. 8; §4.1, p. 10)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","qualifiers":["values are combined by XOR, so the OKVS is binary; the extra positions R are needed because small 2-cores often consist of two identical hyperedges (§4.1, p. 11, footnote 6)","Lemma 6 is conditional on a bound d(n) on the 2-core, which the paper does not derive concretely; known analyses do not give concrete parameters for failure \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (§4.1, p. 10)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Figure 1 (p. 8); §3, PaXoS (p. 9); §4.1 with footnotes 4-6 (pp. 9-11); Lemma 6 (p. 11); Figure 2 (p. 12); §8.1-8.2 (p. 23); Table 1 (p. 24); Appendix A.1 (p. 28); Table 2 (p. 29). IACR ePrint 2021/883, PDF dated 2021-11-30.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Each key is mapped to a hyperedge of three distinct positions in a table L and to a random vector r(k) over \\( d(n) + \\lambda \\) extra positions R. Encoding peels the hypergraph, solves the constraints of the remaining 2-core by Gaussian elimination in \\( O((d + \\lambda)^3) \\) time, and fills the peeled positions in reverse order. If the 2-core exceeds d(n) hyperedges with probability at most \\( \\varepsilon_1 \\), the result is an OKVS with error \\( \\varepsilon_1 + 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (Lemma 6). The authors list its rate as \\( 0.81 - o(1) \\) with \\( O(n\\lambda) \\) encoding and batch decoding, against \\( 0.4 - o(1) \\) for PaXoS.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Three-hash garbled cuckoo table: binary OKVS with about \\( 1.3n \\) main positions instead of the \\( 2.4n \\) of PaXoS, by peeling a random 3-hypergraph","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"Three-hash garbled cuckoo table","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Each key k selects three distinct positions \\( h_1(k), h_2(k), h_3(k) \\) of L, and its value is the XOR of those positions and of the positions of R selected by r(k) (§4.1, pp. 9-11). Encoding repeatedly removes a hyperedge that touches a position used by no other remaining key, pushing it onto a stack; it then solves the system for the keys left in the 2-core, and finally pops the stack, setting for each key a position not yet defined so that the key decodes correctly (Figure 2, p. 12). Hyperedges of size three are used because larger hyperedges improve the orientability threshold but make linear-time peeling fail more often (footnote 5, p. 10). The construction improves the rate of the PaXoS table of PSI-CONTRIB-2020-PRTY-GARBLED-CUCKOO, and its failure probability is made concrete by the amplification of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION. The PSI rows that use it are PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-3HGCT and, through the star architecture, PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-STAR.","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"gprty21-three-hash-garbled-cuckoo","status":"published","subtitle":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","summary":"Each key is mapped to a hyperedge of three distinct positions in a table L and to a random vector r(k) over \\( d(n) + \\lambda \\) extra positions R. Encoding peels the hypergraph, solves the constraints of the remaining 2-core by Gaussian elimination in \\( O((d + \\lambda)^3) \\) time, and fills the peeled positions in reverse order. If the 2-core exceeds d(n) hyperedges with probability at most \\( \\varepsilon_1 \\), the result is an OKVS with error \\( \\varepsilon_1 + 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (Lemma 6). The authors list its rate as \\( 0.81 - o(1) \\) with \\( O(n\\lambda) \\) encoding and batch decoding, against \\( 0.4 - o(1) \\) for PaXoS.","title":"Three-hash garbled cuckoo table: binary OKVS with about \\( 1.3n \\) main positions instead of the \\( 2.4n \\) of PaXoS, by peeling a random 3-hypergraph","type":"result","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION","keywords":["atomic-result","okvs-amplification","star-architecture","failure-probability","clopper-pearson","empirical-validation","cuckoo-hashing-parameters"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"amplification","contribution_kind":"transform","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"technique":["okvs"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Cuckoo-style encodings are fast, but no one could say concretely how large they must be to fail with probability \\( 2^{-40} \\). The asymptotic analyses hide their constants, and confirming so small a probability by experiment needs more than \\( 2^{40} \\) trials. Garimella, Pinkas, Rosulek, Trieu, and Yanai turned the problem around. A failure rate near \\( 2^{-30} \\) can be measured on ordinary hardware, and a composite encoding whose central part repairs any single failed component fails only when two components fail, roughly squaring the probability. Splitting the keys among many small leaves keeps the total size close to that of one table. The security parameter then rests on a measured quantity and an exact combinatorial bound instead of extrapolation.","prior_boundary":"Randomized structures in PSI, such as cuckoo hashing, have asymptotic analyses with unclear constants, so parameters for failure \\( 2^{-40} \\) were set heuristically or, as by Pinkas, Schneider, and Zohner, extrapolated from experiments costing millions of core hours that could not validate such small probabilities (§1.2, p. 3; §5, p. 13, footnote 7).","significance_at_publication":"The authors describe the star-architecture OKVS as the only construction in their comparison with an empirically proven error probability (Appendix A.2, p. 28; Table 2, p. 29); it is the encoding of their 'star arch.' PSI, which sends 1.43 times less than PaXoS-PSI with malicious security (§8.2, p. 23).","technical_delta":"Three architectures (§5.1-5.3, pp. 13-17) build a new OKVS from instances of an underlying OKVS with independent hash functions; the concrete instantiation (§5.4, pp. 17-18) validates ε for the three-hash table with \\( 2^{33} \\) runs at \\( n' = 6600 \\) and amplifies it with \\( q = 10 \\) or 160 leaves."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION","keywords":["okvs-amplification","star-architecture","failure-probability","clopper-pearson","empirical-validation","cuckoo-hashing-parameters"],"limitations":["encoding calls the underlying encoder up to \\( 2q + 1 \\) times and decoding calls it twice (§5.2, p. 16)","the failure probability of the instantiation depends on an empirical measurement, not a proof (§5.4, p. 17)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","qualifiers":["ε is an empirical upper bound at confidence 0.9999 from the Clopper-Pearson method, measured at \\( n' = 6600 \\) and assumed to hold for larger leaf loads (§5.4, p. 17; footnote 9, p. 18)","a failure of the underlying table is a 2-core larger than \\( 0.5 \\log n' \\) after peeling (§5.4, p. 17)","the maximum leaf load \\( n' \\) is bounded by a balls-into-bins tail bound with probability \\( 2^{-\\lambda} \\) (Eq. (5), p. 16)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.2 (p. 3); §5 with footnotes 7-8 (p. 13); §5.1-5.3 (pp. 13-17); Eq. (3)-(5) (pp. 15-16); §5.4 with footnote 9 (pp. 17-18); Appendix A.2 and Table 2 (pp. 28-29). IACR ePrint 2021/883, PDF dated 2021-11-30.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"From an OKVS that fails with probability ε, replication with c copies fails with probability \\( \\varepsilon^c \\) at c times the size. The star architecture hashes keys into q leaf OKVS and adds one central OKVS; a single failed component is set at random and corrected through the others, so the scheme fails only if two of the \\( q + 1 \\) components fail, about \\( \\binom{q + 1}{2} \\cdot \\varepsilon^2 \\), at size \\( (q + 1) \\cdot \\mathrm{size}(n') \\) with \\( n' \\) the maximum leaf load. The generalized star uses a replicated central OKVS to tolerate \\( d - 1 \\) leaf failures, failing with probability \\( O(\\varepsilon^d) \\). Over the three-hash table with an empirically measured \\( \\varepsilon = 2^{-29.355} \\) at confidence 0.9999, the star gives failure \\( 2^{-52.9} \\) for \\( n = 2^{16} \\) and \\( 2^{-45.05} \\) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) at about \\( 1.3n \\) size.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"OKVS correctness amplification: replication and star architectures turn an empirically validated failure probability ε into about \\( \\varepsilon^2 \\), or \\( O(\\varepsilon^d) \\), at nearly the same size","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"OKVS correctness amplification","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"All three architectures rely on two facts: whether an OKVS encoding fails depends only on its keys and hash functions, and a random vector of the right length is a valid encoding of random values (§5, p. 13). Replication tries a second instance whenever the first fails (§5.1, pp. 13-14). The star architecture decodes a key as the sum of its leaf's value and the central value; if one component fails, it is filled at random and the others are encoded to compensate (§5.2, pp. 14-16; failure probability in Eq. (3)-(4), p. 15). The generalized star replaces the central node by a d-fold replicated OKVS that can absorb up to \\( d - 1 \\) failed leaves (§5.3, pp. 16-17). In the concrete instantiation, each leaf is a three-hash table with \\( 1.3n' + \\lambda + 0.5 \\log n' \\) positions (PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-3H-GCT), and the total size is about \\( 1.3n \\) (§5.4, pp. 17-18). The PSI row that uses it is PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MAL-STAR.","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"gprty21-okvs-correctness-amplificati","status":"published","subtitle":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","summary":"From an OKVS that fails with probability ε, replication with c copies fails with probability \\( \\varepsilon^c \\) at c times the size. The star architecture hashes keys into q leaf OKVS and adds one central OKVS; a single failed component is set at random and corrected through the others, so the scheme fails only if two of the \\( q + 1 \\) components fail, about \\( \\binom{q + 1}{2} \\cdot \\varepsilon^2 \\), at size \\( (q + 1) \\cdot \\mathrm{size}(n') \\) with \\( n' \\) the maximum leaf load. The generalized star uses a replicated central OKVS to tolerate \\( d - 1 \\) leaf failures, failing with probability \\( O(\\varepsilon^d) \\). Over the three-hash table with an empirically measured \\( \\varepsilon = 2^{-29.355} \\) at confidence 0.9999, the star gives failure \\( 2^{-52.9} \\) for \\( n = 2^{16} \\) and \\( 2^{-45.05} \\) for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) at about \\( 1.3n \\) size.","title":"OKVS correctness amplification: replication and star architectures turn an empirically validated failure probability ε into about \\( \\varepsilon^2 \\), or \\( O(\\varepsilon^d) \\), at nearly the same size","type":"result","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-AMPLIFICATION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-LINEAR-OKVS-VOLE","keywords":["atomic-result","vector-ole","linear-okvs","paxos-psi","malicious-psi","error-correcting-code","field-correlation"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"linear-okvs-vole","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["okvs","vole"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"PaXoS-PSI took its correlated strings from a malicious OT extension, whose correlation mixes XOR with bitwise AND, and that mix is why it needed a binary encoding and a long error-correcting code. Garimella and coauthors observed that vector OLE supplies the same kind of correlation with a genuine field multiplication by the sender's secret. Any linear encoding then commutes with the correlation, and a hashed value with a nonzero offset already looks random without a code. They restated the PaXoS-PSI protocol for this setting, so that any linear OKVS can serve, including non-binary ones. At the time no malicious-secure vector OLE had been implemented, and the authors left open whether this route would beat the OT-extension version in practice.","prior_boundary":"PaXoS-PSI works only with a binary OKVS, because the bitwise AND in the OOS correlation is not a field operation, and it needs an error-correcting code of minimum distance κ so that \\( C(d_i) \\wedge s \\) masks at least κ bits of s (§7.1, pp. 20-21).","significance_at_publication":"The generalization admits non-binary OKVS and removes one source of overhead (§1.3, p. 4). The authors note that it requires a malicious-secure vOLE, none of which had been implemented, and leave to future work whether it is competitive with the OT-extension version (§7.1, p. 21).","technical_delta":"With the field correlation of vector OLE, \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(R, k) = \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, k) \\oplus \\mathrm{Decode}(D, k) \\cdot s \\) holds for any linear OKVS, and \\( H(a \\oplus b \\cdot s) \\) looks random to the receiver for every nonzero b, so no code is needed (§7.1, p. 21); the protocol is Figure 3 (p. 22), adapted from PaXoS-PSI, with Theorem 7 (p. 21)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-LINEAR-OKVS-VOLE","keywords":["vector-ole","linear-okvs","paxos-psi","malicious-psi","error-correcting-code","field-correlation"],"limitations":["no implementation or concrete cost is given, and no malicious-secure vOLE had been implemented (§7.1, p. 21)","the proof is not written out; the paper states that it follows closely the proof of PaXoS-PSI (§7.1, p. 21)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","qualifiers":["malicious security in the vOLE-hybrid, random oracle model, with the hash lengths \\( \\ell_1, \\ell_2 \\) of PaXoS-PSI (Theorem 7, p. 21; Figure 3, p. 22)","the OKVS must be linear over \\( \\mathrm{GF}(2^{\\ell_1}) \\), and only the receiver learns the intersection (Figure 3, p. 22)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.3 (p. 4); §7.1 with Eq. (6)-(7) (pp. 20-21); Theorem 7 (p. 21); Figure 3 (p. 22). IACR ePrint 2021/883, PDF dated 2021-11-30.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"The OOS correlation \\( r_i = q_i \\oplus C(d_i) \\wedge s \\) of PaXoS-PSI is replaced by the vector-OLE correlation \\( q_i = r_i \\oplus d_i \\cdot s \\) over \\( \\mathrm{GF}(2^{\\ell_1}) \\). The receiver's vOLE input is a linear OKVS D encoding the pairs \\( (y, H_1(y)) \\) for its set, and the sender sends \\( H_2(x, \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, x) \\oplus H_1(x) \\cdot s) \\) for each of its items. For any linear OKVS and the hash lengths of PaXoS-PSI, the protocol realizes two-party PSI against malicious adversaries (Theorem 7).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious two-party PSI from any linear OKVS and vector OLE, generalizing PaXoS-PSI and removing its error-correcting code","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"PSI from a linear OKVS and vector OLE","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$22","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-PAPER-2019-PCG: the paper cites the pseudorandom correlation generators of Boyle et al. among the vector-OLE protocols that produce this correlation (§7.1, p. 21; reference [BCG+19], p. 24).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"gprty21-malicious-two-party-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","summary":"The OOS correlation \\( r_i = q_i \\oplus C(d_i) \\wedge s \\) of PaXoS-PSI is replaced by the vector-OLE correlation \\( q_i = r_i \\oplus d_i \\cdot s \\) over \\( \\mathrm{GF}(2^{\\ell_1}) \\). The receiver's vOLE input is a linear OKVS D encoding the pairs \\( (y, H_1(y)) \\) for its set, and the sender sends \\( H_2(x, \\mathrm{Decode}(Q, x) \\oplus H_1(x) \\cdot s) \\) for each of its items. For any linear OKVS and the hash lengths of PaXoS-PSI, the protocol realizes two-party PSI against malicious adversaries (Theorem 7).","title":"Malicious two-party PSI from any linear OKVS and vector OLE, generalizing PaXoS-PSI and removing its error-correcting code","type":"result","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-LINEAR-OKVS-VOLE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-MALICIOUS-MULTIPARTY","keywords":["atomic-result","multiparty-psi","malicious","zero-sharing","oprf","okvs","augmented-semi-honest","star-topology"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"malicious-multiparty","contribution_kind":"security_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["multiparty"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"technique":["okvs","vole","secret_sharing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Kolesnikov and coauthors had built multiparty PSI from two-party oblivious PRFs: each party masks its PRF outputs with shares of zero, and a central party sees the shares cancel only on items that everyone holds. Their most efficient variant was proven only against augmented semi-honest adversaries, in a model where the simulator may change corrupt inputs. Garimella and coauthors showed that after each PRF output is hashed together with the item through a random oracle, the same protocol is secure against malicious parties. Changing a corrupt party's input is harmless in that model, and the oracle lets a simulator read off the items an adversary encoded. Writing the protocol over any OKVS let it use the new fast encodings instead of polynomials.","prior_boundary":"Kolesnikov et al.'s most efficient multiparty PSI, built from an OPPRF that encodes zero shares with a polynomial or a garbled Bloom filter, had only augmented semi-honest security; other concretely efficient malicious multiparty PSI relied on garbled Bloom filters with cut-and-choose checks (§6.2, p. 19; §7.2, pp. 21-22; Appendix B.5, p. 35).","significance_at_publication":"The authors call it the most efficient malicious multiparty PSI to date, at nearly the cost of the augmented semi-honest protocol (§1.3, p. 4; §7.2, p. 22), against garbled-Bloom-filter protocols that they report as about 10 and 2 times slower than that protocol (§7.2, p. 22; Table 4, p. 36).","technical_delta":"Figure 5 (p. 39) writes Kolesnikov et al.'s construction over an arbitrary OKVS and wraps each OPRF output in a random oracle so that the simulator can extract inputs (Appendix B.2, p. 30); Theorem 9 (p. 31) is proved in Appendix B.4 (pp. 30-35), with a compression argument bounding the extracted set by \\( 2m \\) (p. 34). Appendix B.6 (pp. 36-37) explains why the protocol is malicious-secure though not semi-honest-secure."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-MALICIOUS-MULTIPARTY","keywords":["multiparty-psi","malicious","zero-sharing","oprf","okvs","augmented-semi-honest","star-topology"],"limitations":["the protocol does not strictly enforce corrupt parties' advertised set sizes; restricting them to m items is left open (Appendix B.4, pp. 34-35; Appendix B.7, p. 37)","no implementation of the multiparty protocol is reported (§8, pp. 22-24)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","qualifiers":["secure in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{oprf}} \\)-hybrid model with a malicious-secure two-party OPRF, for an exponentially large field F (Theorem 9, p. 31; Figure 5, p. 39)","star topology; only P0 receives output (Figure 5, p. 39)","all parties hold sets of the same size m; with unequal sizes only a weaker functionality, in which corrupt parties may use the largest advertised size, is achieved (Appendix B.7, p. 37)","the \\( 2m \\) bound on corrupt parties' sets assumes \\( |F| = 2^{2\\kappa} \\) and \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\) oracle queries (Appendix B.4, p. 34)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.3 (p. 4); §6.2 (pp. 18-19); §7.2 (pp. 21-22); Appendix B.2-B.4 (pp. 29-35); Theorem 9 (p. 31); Appendix B.5 and Table 4 (pp. 35-36); Appendix B.6-B.7 (pp. 36-38); Figure 5 (p. 39). IACR ePrint 2021/883, PDF dated 2021-11-30.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"A central party P0 runs a malicious-secure two-party OPRF with every other party, and each party \\( P_i \\) sends P0 an OKVS mapping each of its items h to \\( H(\\mathrm{PRF}_i(h), h) + sh_i(h) \\), where the \\( sh_i \\) are non-interactive additive shares of zero derived from pairwise PRF keys; P0 outputs the items of its set whose decoded values sum to zero. Over an exponentially large field the protocol realizes UC-secure multiparty PSI against malicious adversaries corrupting any subset of parties in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{oprf}} \\)-hybrid model, with output to P0 only, and corrupt parties may contribute sets of up to about \\( 2m \\) items (Theorem 9).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious security for the zero-sharing multiparty PSI of Kolesnikov et al., once OPRF outputs are hashed with a random oracle, over any OKVS","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"Malicious multiparty PSI from OPRF and OKVS","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$23","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"gprty21-malicious-multiparty-psi-fro-2","status":"published","subtitle":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","summary":"A central party P0 runs a malicious-secure two-party OPRF with every other party, and each party \\( P_i \\) sends P0 an OKVS mapping each of its items h to \\( H(\\mathrm{PRF}_i(h), h) + sh_i(h) \\), where the \\( sh_i \\) are non-interactive additive shares of zero derived from pairwise PRF keys; P0 outputs the items of its set whose decoded values sum to zero. Over an exponentially large field the protocol realizes UC-secure multiparty PSI against malicious adversaries corrupting any subset of parties in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{oprf}} \\)-hybrid model, with output to P0 only, and corrupt parties may contribute sets of up to about \\( 2m \\) items (Theorem 9).","title":"Malicious security for the zero-sharing multiparty PSI of Kolesnikov et al., once OPRF outputs are hashed with a random oracle, over any OKVS","type":"result","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-MALICIOUS-MULTIPARTY"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS","keywords":["atomic-result","okvs","oblivious-key-value-store","linear-okvs","binary-okvs","overfitting","polynomial-encoding","paxos"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"okvs","contribution_kind":"definition","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"technique":["okvs"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Many PSI protocols hid a party's set by interpolating a polynomial through its items, which is compact but costs quasi-linear field operations, and PaXoS had shown that a sparse XOR-based encoding could do the same job faster in one protocol. Garimella, Pinkas, Rosulek, Trieu, and Yanai named the common requirement: an encoding of key-value pairs that decodes correctly on the encoded keys and reveals nothing about which keys were used when the values are random. They separated linear and binary variants and formalized the overfitting game that limits how many keys a malicious party can squeeze into an encoding. Polynomials, garbled Bloom filters, and PaXoS became instances, and protocols written against the interface could adopt any faster instance.","prior_boundary":"PSI and related protocols hid input sets by interpolating polynomials, as in OPPRF-based multiparty PSI, circuit PSI, and SpOT-light, at \\( O(n \\log^2 n) \\) field operations (§1.1, pp. 2-3). PaXoS took a step toward an abstraction but covered only binary encodings (§1.1, p. 3), and its overfitting bound was proved for PaXoS (§2.4, p. 7).","significance_at_publication":"With one interface the authors argue that an OKVS can replace polynomials in sparse OT extension, OPPRF and its multiparty and circuit-PSI uses, PaXoS-PSI, and covert computation (§6, pp. 18-20), so that a better OKVS improves all of them (§1.3, p. 4).","technical_delta":"Definitions 1-3 (p. 5) define key-value stores, obliviousness, and linear OKVS; §2.3 (p. 6) identifies binary OKVS with PaXoS; Definition 4 (pp. 6-7) defines the overfitting game, and Lemma 5 (p. 7) restates the PaXoS bound for any key-value store. Section 3 and Figure 1 (pp. 7-9) place polynomials, random matrices, garbled Bloom filters, and PaXoS in one comparison of rate and encoding and decoding cost."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS","keywords":["okvs","oblivious-key-value-store","linear-okvs","binary-okvs","overfitting","polynomial-encoding","paxos"],"limitations":["whether overfitting is computationally hard for polynomial-time adversaries, beyond the information-theoretic bound, is only conjectured (§2.4, p. 7)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","qualifiers":["obliviousness is required only when the encoded values are uniformly random (Definition 2, p. 5)","whether Encode fails depends only on the keys and the hash functions, not on the values (§2.1, p. 5)","the overfitting bound of Lemma 5 is information-theoretic and limits \\( n' \\) only beyond the size of the encoding (§2.4, p. 7)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 and §1.3 (pp. 2-4); Definitions 1-3 (p. 5); §2.3 (p. 6); Definition 4 and Lemma 5 (pp. 6-7); §2.5 and §3 with Figure 1 (pp. 7-9); §6 (pp. 18-20). IACR ePrint 2021/883, PDF dated 2021-11-30.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"A key-value store is a pair of algorithms (Encode, Decode) such that \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(S, k) = v \\) for every pair (k, v) used to create S, where Encode may fail with small probability depending only on the keys. It is oblivious if, when the values are uniformly random, encodings of any two key sets of the same size are computationally indistinguishable. It is linear over a field F if Decode(S, k) is the inner product of a key-dependent vector d(k) with S, and binary if d(k) is a 0/1 vector, which is a PaXoS. The \\( (n, n') \\)-overfitting game asks an adversary for an S that agrees with a random oracle on more than \\( n' \\) keys.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Oblivious key-value store (OKVS): key-value encodings that hide the keys when the values are random, with linear, binary, and overfitting notions","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/883","sections":[{"content":"Oblivious key-value store","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$24","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"gprty21-oblivious-key-value-store","status":"published","subtitle":"Oblivious Key-Value Stores and Amplification for Private Set Intersection","summary":"A key-value store is a pair of algorithms (Encode, Decode) such that \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(S, k) = v \\) for every pair (k, v) used to create S, where Encode may fail with small probability depending only on the keys. It is oblivious if, when the values are uniformly random, encodings of any two key sets of the same size are computationally indistinguishable. It is linear over a field F if Decode(S, k) is the inner product of a key-dependent vector d(k) with S, and binary if d(k) is a 0/1 vector, which is a PaXoS. The \\( (n, n') \\)-overfitting game asks an adversary for an S that agrees with a random oracle on more than \\( n' \\) keys.","title":"Oblivious key-value store (OKVS): key-value encodings that hide the keys when the values are random, with linear, binary, and overfitting notions","type":"result","venue":"CRYPTO 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-GPRTY-OKVS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-NO-COLLUSION","keywords":["atomic-result","no-collusion","honest-majority","server-aided-psi","okvs","paxos","symmetric-key-only","reduction-to-two-party"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"malicious-no-collusion","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["multiparty"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["okvs","paxos","server_aided_psi"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Multiparty PSI protocols that tolerate malicious parties had paid for the worst case: they defended against all but one party colluding and made every party run interactive subprotocols built on oblivious transfer. Nevo, Trieu, and Yanai isolated the setting in which at most one party is corrupt. One party hands out fresh PRF keys and a key-value encoding that maps its items to combined PRF values; the others turn their items into PRF images, and only two designated parties still compare sets. That comparison can use server-aided two-party PSI, with a third party as the server that does not collude, so no oblivious transfer is needed at all. The paper also shows that a coalition of any two parties breaks the protocol, which marks the boundary of this setting.","prior_boundary":"Concretely efficient multiparty PSI protocols run OPPRFs on OT extension (Kolesnikov et al., Garimella et al.) or garbled Bloom filters with OT (Ben Efraim et al.), so every party runs interactive two-party subprotocols, and the malicious ones protect against \\( n - 1 \\) colluding parties even when fewer corruptions are expected (§1.1, pp. 1-3; §1.2, p. 4; Table 1, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"The authors state that, to their knowledge, this is the only multiparty PSI construction relying only on symmetric-key primitives (§1.2, p. 4). For three parties with \\( 2^{20} \\) items, server-aided two-party PSI lowers the running time from about 9 seconds with plain malicious PSI to about 4.8 seconds, and 32 parties with \\( 2^{20} \\) items finish in 10 seconds (§1.2, p. 4).","technical_delta":"With at most one corrupted party, PRF keys and OKVS encodings reduce n-party PSI to two-party PSI, either recursively with \\( O(n) \\) rounds (Protocol 3.1, p. 6; Theorem 3.2, p. 7) or between \\( P_{n-1} \\) and \\( P_n \\) in constant rounds (Protocol 3.3, p. 8; Theorem 3.4, p. 8). Items are concatenated with their PRF or OKVS values to stop a malicious P1 from forcing false matches (§3.1, p. 6; §3.2, p. 7), and the base case is server-aided two-party PSI with another party as server (§3.1.1, p. 6; Appendix A, pp. 13-14)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-NO-COLLUSION","keywords":["no-collusion","honest-majority","server-aided-psi","okvs","paxos","symmetric-key-only","reduction-to-two-party"],"limitations":["insecure as soon as two parties collude, since a party with both the key and the OKVS can test arbitrary inputs (§3.1.2, p. 6; §3.2.1, p. 8)","the computation of P1 and \\( P_{n-1} \\) grows as \\( O(nm) \\) (§3.2.2, p. 8)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","qualifiers":["at most one corrupted party; a collusion of two parties such as P1 and \\( P_{n-1} \\) breaks Protocol 3.3, which stays secure against any subset of \\( P_2, \\ldots, P_{n-2} \\) together with one of P1, \\( P_{n-1} \\), \\( P_n \\) (§3.2.1, p. 8)","Theorem 3.2 is stated in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{psi}}^{n-1,1,m} \\)-hybrid and random oracle model (p. 7); Theorem 3.4 is stated in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{psi}}^{2,1,m} \\)-hybrid model (p. 8), and in the malicious case parties hash items with a random oracle H whose queries the simulator observes (Protocol 3.3, note a, p. 8; pp. 8-9)","the symmetric-key-only property depends on instantiating the two-party PSI with server-aided PSI, in which the server does not collude with either party (§1.2, p. 4; §3.1.1, p. 6)","the output goes to \\( P_n \\) only (Functionality 2.1, p. 4)","Protocol 3.3 has 4 rounds by the count in §3.2.2 (p. 8); Table 1 lists 5 (p. 2)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 with Table 1 (pp. 1-3); §1.2 (pp. 3-4); Functionality 2.1 (p. 4); §2.0.4 OKVS (p. 5); §3.1 with Protocol 3.1 (p. 6); Theorem 3.2 and proof (p. 7); §3.2 with Protocol 3.3 and Theorem 3.4 (pp. 7-9); §3.2.1-3.2.2 (p. 8); Appendix A with Protocols A.1-A.2 (pp. 13-14); §5 with Tables 3-5 (pp. 11-12). IACR ePrint 2021/1221, PDF dated 2021-09-17.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1221","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Against a malicious adversary corrupting at most one of n parties, P1 sends PRF keys \\( k_i \\) to parties \\( P_2, \\ldots, P_{n-2} \\) and sends \\( P_n \\) an OKVS mapping each of its items to the XOR of the PRF values under those keys; each \\( P_i \\) encodes its items under \\( F(k_i, \\cdot) \\) in an OKVS sent to \\( P_{n-1} \\); \\( P_{n-1} \\) and \\( P_n \\) then run a two-party PSI on items concatenated with their decoded values, and \\( P_n \\) outputs the intersection. With an ideal two-party PSI functionality the protocol is secure, takes a constant number of rounds, and gives clients \\( O(m \\kappa) \\) communication and computation; instantiating the two-party PSI with server-aided PSI, in which a party that does not collude acts as the server, removes oblivious transfer, so the protocol uses only symmetric-key primitives. A recursive variant reduces n-party to \\( (n - 1) \\)-party PSI with \\( O(n) \\) rounds.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious multiparty PSI with at most one corrupted party from symmetric-key primitives only, by reducing n-party PSI to server-aided two-party PSI over OKVS encodings","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1221","sections":[{"content":"Malicious multiparty PSI without collusion","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"In Protocol 3.3 (p. 8), P1 picks keys \\( k_2, \\ldots, k_{n-2} \\), gives \\( k_i \\) to \\( P_i \\), and sends \\( P_n \\) an OKVS that maps each of its items a to the XOR of \\( F(k_i, a) \\); each \\( P_i \\) with \\( 2 \\le i \\le n - 2 \\) sends \\( P_{n-1} \\) an OKVS mapping its items a to \\( F(k_i, a) \\). \\( P_{n-1} \\) XORs its decodings of these OKVSs and \\( P_n \\) decodes P1's OKVS; for an item in every set both obtain the same pseudorandom value. They append these values to their items and call a two-party PSI functionality, and \\( P_n \\) outputs the plain items of the result (Protocol 3.3, steps 4-6, p. 8). The round count is two rounds plus those of the two-party PSI, which the server-aided protocol of Kamara et al. completes in two (§3.2.2, p. 8). Section 3.2.1 shows why a direct variant in which every \\( P_i \\) sends its OKVS to \\( P_n \\) would leak more than the intersection (pp. 7-8).","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"nty21-malicious-multiparty-psi-wit","status":"published","subtitle":"Simple, Fast Malicious Multiparty Private Set Intersection","summary":"Against a malicious adversary corrupting at most one of n parties, P1 sends PRF keys \\( k_i \\) to parties \\( P_2, \\ldots, P_{n-2} \\) and sends \\( P_n \\) an OKVS mapping each of its items to the XOR of the PRF values under those keys; each \\( P_i \\) encodes its items under \\( F(k_i, \\cdot) \\) in an OKVS sent to \\( P_{n-1} \\); \\( P_{n-1} \\) and \\( P_n \\) then run a two-party PSI on items concatenated with their decoded values, and \\( P_n \\) outputs the intersection. With an ideal two-party PSI functionality the protocol is secure, takes a constant number of rounds, and gives clients \\( O(m \\kappa) \\) communication and computation; instantiating the two-party PSI with server-aided PSI, in which a party that does not collude acts as the server, removes oblivious transfer, so the protocol uses only symmetric-key primitives. A recursive variant reduces n-party to \\( (n - 1) \\)-party PSI with \\( O(n) \\) rounds.","title":"Malicious multiparty PSI with at most one corrupted party from symmetric-key primitives only, by reducing n-party PSI to server-aided two-party PSI over OKVS encodings","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-NO-COLLUSION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-THRESHOLD-CALIBRATED","keywords":["atomic-result","malicious","dishonest-majority","corruption-threshold","zeroxor","okvs","paxos","opprf","unconditional-zero-sharing","client-server","pivot"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"malicious-threshold-calibrated","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["multiparty"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["okvs","paxos","opprf","secret_sharing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Malicious multiparty PSI against a dishonest majority had become concretely efficient through garbled Bloom filters and oblivious key-value stores, but those protocols cost the same whether one party or all but one might collude, and every party took part in the whole computation. Nevo, Trieu, and Yanai tied the cost to the collusion bound t. Most parties act as light clients: each sends one PRF key per server and a single key-value encoding of its items to a pivot. The pivot and t servers then hold values that cancel only on items common to all sets, and a zero test built from programmable OPRFs and pseudorandom zero-sharing reveals those items. Against the largest coalition the protocol coincides with the earlier malicious one, and smaller bounds make it faster.","prior_boundary":"The augmented semi-honest protocol of Kolesnikov et al. and the malicious protocols of Ben Efraim et al. and Garimella et al. protect against \\( n - 1 \\) colluding parties at the same cost for every t, and the semi-honest protocol of Chandran et al. tolerates fewer than \\( n/2 \\); the paper states that it was not known how to improve these protocols for smaller t (§1.1, pp. 1-3; §1.2, p. 4; Table 1, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"Client communication is almost constant in n and in t below \\( n - 1 \\), and for \\( (n, t) = (15, 7) \\) with \\( 2^{20} \\) items a client needs 3.4 seconds on a LAN, which the authors report as 10-23 times less than the semi-honest protocols of Chandran et al. and Kolesnikov et al. (§5.1, p. 12; Table 3, p. 12). Total running time is 1.2-6.5 and 1.2-8.5 times lower than those protocols, and with t fixed the cost is nearly constant in n (§5.1-5.2, p. 12).","technical_delta":"Clients deliver a conditional zero-sharing of their items to the pivot and t servers through one OKVS and t PRF keys each (§4, p. 9; Protocol 4.4, p. 10). ZeroXOR outputs the keys common to all \\( t + 1 \\) parties whose values XOR to zero, using OPPRFs to \\( P_n \\) masked by unconditional zero-sharing so that values of non-matching keys stay hidden (Functionality 4.1, p. 9; Protocol 4.2 and Theorem 4.3, p. 10). Theorem 4.5 proves malicious security against \\( t < n \\) (p. 11), and at \\( t = n - 1 \\) the protocol coincides with that of Garimella et al. up to the OKVS instantiation (§1.2, p. 4)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-THRESHOLD-CALIBRATED","keywords":["malicious","dishonest-majority","corruption-threshold","zeroxor","okvs","paxos","opprf","unconditional-zero-sharing","client-server","pivot"],"limitations":["at \\( t = n - 1 \\) there are no clients and the cost equals that of the augmented semi-honest protocol of Kolesnikov et al. (§5.1, p. 12)","comparisons with semi-honest protocols cross security models, and runtimes of Ben Efraim et al. are taken from their paper for \\( n \\in \\{8, 12\\} \\) rather than rerun (§5.1, p. 11)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","qualifiers":["malicious adversary that statically corrupts any strict subset of the parties, \\( t < n \\) (abstract, p. 1; §1, p. 1; Theorem 4.5, p. 11)","Theorem 4.5 is in the ZeroXOR-hybrid and random oracle model with a proof sketch; Theorem 4.3 for ZeroXOR is in the zero-sharing and OPPRF hybrid model, and its proof is omitted (pp. 10-11)","the OPPRF is the malicious construction of Pinkas et al. (PaXoS), described in Appendix B (p. 14; §1.1.1, p. 2)","the output goes to \\( P_n \\) only (Functionality 2.1, p. 4)","the \\( t + 1 \\) parties that process the intersection can be chosen, and only a collusion of exactly that set reveals information, whereas any \\( t + 1 \\) parties can do so in the protocol of Chandran et al. (§1.1.2, p. 2)","the implementation uses PaXoS with expansion 2.5 as the OKVS and the table-based OPPRF code of Kolesnikov et al., which footnote 2 says is semi-honest secure and about three times slower than the malicious PaXoS-based OPPRF (§5, p. 11)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 with Table 1 (pp. 1-3); §1.2 (pp. 3-4); Functionality 2.1, §2.0.4 OKVS, §2.0.5 zero-sharing (pp. 4-5); §4 with Functionality 4.1 (p. 9); Protocol 4.2, Theorem 4.3, Protocol 4.4 (p. 10); §4.2.1 and Theorem 4.5 (pp. 10-11); §5 with Tables 2-5 (pp. 11-12); Appendix B (p. 14). IACR ePrint 2021/1221, PDF dated 2021-09-17.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1221","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"For n parties with m items each and at most \\( t < n \\) corrupted and colluding, the parties split into \\( n - t - 1 \\) clients, one pivot, and t servers. Each client sends a PRF key to each server and one OKVS to the pivot that maps its items to the XOR of the PRF values under those keys; the pivot and the t servers then hold values that XOR to zero exactly on items in every set, and a ZeroXOR protocol among these \\( t + 1 \\) parties, built from OPPRFs and unconditional zero-sharing, outputs those items to \\( P_n \\). In the ZeroXOR-hybrid and random oracle model the protocol is secure against a malicious adversary corrupting \\( t < n \\) parties and runs in 4 rounds; a client sends \\( O(m \\kappa) \\) bits and computes \\( O(m t \\kappa) \\), the leader communicates \\( O(m \\kappa \\max\\{t, n - t\\}) \\) and computes \\( O(m \\kappa (n - t)) \\), and at \\( t = n - 1 \\) the protocol reduces to ZeroXOR among all parties.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious multiparty PSI against \\( t < n \\) colluding parties whose cost decreases with t, with client communication \\( O(m \\kappa) \\), from OKVS encodings and a ZeroXOR test over OPPRFs","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1221","sections":[{"content":"Malicious multiparty PSI calibrated to the collusion bound","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Let \\( v = n - t \\). Clients \\( P_1, \\ldots, P_{v-1} \\) send key \\( k_i^j \\) to each server \\( P_j \\), \\( j > v \\), and send the pivot \\( P_v \\) an OKVS \\( S_i \\) mapping each item to the XOR over servers of \\( F(k_i^j, \\text{item}) \\) (Protocol 4.4, steps 2-3, p. 10). The pivot pairs each of its items with the XOR of its decodings, and each server pairs each of its items with the XOR of \\( F(k_i^j, \\text{item}) \\) over clients (steps 4-5). For an item in every set the \\( t + 1 \\) values XOR to zero, otherwise to a pseudorandom value, and ZeroXOR among \\( P_v, \\ldots, P_n \\) finds the items with zero XOR (step 6; §4.2, p. 10). In ZeroXOR every party except \\( P_n \\) programs an OPPRF to \\( P_n \\) with its value masked by its unconditional zero-share, and \\( P_n \\) keeps the keys whose outputs and own value XOR to zero (Protocol 4.2, p. 10). A client communicates only its OKVS and keys, so its load depends on m alone (§4.2.1, p. 10).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None. The OPPRF, OKVS, and zero-sharing building blocks are PSI mechanisms.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"nty21-malicious-multiparty-psi-cal","status":"published","subtitle":"Simple, Fast Malicious Multiparty Private Set Intersection","summary":"For n parties with m items each and at most \\( t < n \\) corrupted and colluding, the parties split into \\( n - t - 1 \\) clients, one pivot, and t servers. Each client sends a PRF key to each server and one OKVS to the pivot that maps its items to the XOR of the PRF values under those keys; the pivot and the t servers then hold values that XOR to zero exactly on items in every set, and a ZeroXOR protocol among these \\( t + 1 \\) parties, built from OPPRFs and unconditional zero-sharing, outputs those items to \\( P_n \\). In the ZeroXOR-hybrid and random oracle model the protocol is secure against a malicious adversary corrupting \\( t < n \\) parties and runs in 4 rounds; a client sends \\( O(m \\kappa) \\) bits and computes \\( O(m t \\kappa) \\), the leader communicates \\( O(m \\kappa \\max\\{t, n - t\\}) \\) and computes \\( O(m \\kappa (n - t)) \\), and at \\( t = n - 1 \\) the protocol reduces to ZeroXOR among all parties.","title":"Malicious multiparty PSI against \\( t < n \\) colluding parties whose cost decreases with t, with client communication \\( O(m \\kappa) \\), from OKVS encodings and a ZeroXOR test over OPPRFs","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-NTY-MALICIOUS-THRESHOLD-CALIBRATED"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","circuit-psi","opprf","xopaxos","doubly-oblivious","cuckoo-hashing","gmw","secret-shared-output"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"circuit-psi","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["circuit"],"parties":["two_party"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["opprf","vole","paxos","cuckoo_hashing","gmw"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Circuit-PSI hides even the intersection: the parties end with secret shares that a later computation can consume. The leading linear-communication protocol hashed the receiver's items into a cuckoo table and used an oblivious programmable PRF, built from polynomial interpolation, so that each bin yields equal strings exactly when its item is shared, before a small comparison circuit. Rindal and Schoppmann kept that structure and replaced the programmable PRF with one built from their VOLE-based OPRF and a sparse encoding of the sender's corrections. Because the PaXoS encoder sets some positions to zero, which could leak the sender's keys, they randomized every free position so that the encoding is uniform whenever its values are.","prior_boundary":"The linear-communication circuit-PSI of Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai combined an OPPRF based on polynomial interpolation with a small GMW circuit (§1.4, p. 6); the PaXoS encoder sets some positions to zero, so its output can depend on the encoded keys (§1.3, p. 5; §2, p. 10).","significance_at_publication":"The authors report circuit-PSI up to 3 times faster than the previous protocol (abstract, p. 1), for example 23.3 s against 72.0 s at 5 Gbps for \\( 2^{20} \\) items with IKNP-based GMW, and 277 MB with SilentOT against an estimated 2,540 MB (Table 4, p. 29).","technical_delta":"XoPaXoS (Figure 1, p. 7) assigns random values to the free positions of the 2-core system and to one vertex per tree, making the encoding uniform for uniform values (§2, pp. 10-11); the OPPRF is Figure 8 (p. 38) with Theorem 5.1 (p. 21), and the circuit-PSI protocol is Figure 10 (pp. 38-39), whose proof the authors defer to the earlier work (§6, pp. 22-24)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI","keywords":["circuit-psi","opprf","xopaxos","doubly-oblivious","cuckoo-hashing","gmw","secret-shared-output"],"limitations":["the communication figures for the earlier protocol in Table 4 are computed from its theoretical costs, not measured (Table 4 caption, p. 29)","no malicious security is claimed for circuit-PSI (abstract, p. 1)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","qualifiers":["semi-honest security; the circuit-PSI proof is deferred to Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai, and the OPPRF is proved in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{oprf}} \\)-hybrid model (§6, p. 23; Theorem 5.1, p. 21)","cuckoo hashing with expansion 1.27 and three hash functions, following the earlier protocol; the authors note disagreement in the literature about these parameters (§7.2, pp. 29-30)","the final comparison uses generic two-party computation, implemented with GMW whose OTs come from IKNP or SilentOT (§7.2, p. 30)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 and §1.3 (pp. 3-6); §1.4 (p. 6); Figure 1 (p. 7); §2, X-oblivious PaXoS (pp. 10-11); §5 and Theorem 5.1 (pp. 21-22); §6 (pp. 22-24); Table 4 and §7.2 (pp. 29-30); Figures 7-10 (pp. 37-39). IACR ePrint 2021/266, PDF dated 2024-08-08.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"An OPPRF is obtained from any OPRF by having the sender encode the corrections \\( z_i - F_k(y_i) \\) with XoPaXoS, a PaXoS variant whose output is uniformly distributed when the encoded values are uniform. In the circuit-PSI protocol of Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai, this OPPRF, built on the VOLE-based OPRF, replaces their polynomial-based OPPRF: the receiver places its items in a cuckoo table, the sender places its items in a simple hash table, and a generic two-party circuit compares one programmed value per bin. Both parties obtain secret shares of a membership bit and of associated values for each bin, against semi-honest adversaries.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Semi-honest circuit-PSI from a VOLE-based OPPRF programmed with a doubly oblivious PaXoS (XoPaXoS), cuckoo hashing, and a GMW comparison circuit","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","sections":[{"content":"Circuit-PSI from a VOLE-based OPPRF","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$25","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-EVAL-GMW: the comparison circuit is evaluated with the GMW implementation of libOTe (§7.2, pp. 26 and 30). MPC-CONTRIB-2003-IKNP-OTEXT: one variant generates the GMW offline OTs with IKNP OT extension (§7.2, p. 30). MPC-CONTRIB-2019-PCG-SILENT: the other variant uses SilentOT, cited to Boyle et al., CCS 2019 (§7.2, p. 30; Table 4, p. 29); that CCS paper is distinct from the CRYPTO 2019 paper MPC-PAPER-2019-PCG and has no record of its own.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rs21-circuit-psi-from-vole","status":"published","subtitle":"VOLE-PSI: Fast OPRF and Circuit-PSI from Vector-OLE","summary":"An OPPRF is obtained from any OPRF by having the sender encode the corrections \\( z_i - F_k(y_i) \\) with XoPaXoS, a PaXoS variant whose output is uniformly distributed when the encoded values are uniform. In the circuit-PSI protocol of Pinkas, Schneider, Tkachenko, and Yanai, this OPPRF, built on the VOLE-based OPRF, replaces their polynomial-based OPPRF: the receiver places its items in a cuckoo table, the sender places its items in a simple hash table, and a generic two-party circuit compares one programmed value per bin. Both parties obtain secret shares of a membership bit and of associated values for each bin, against semi-honest adversaries.","title":"Semi-honest circuit-PSI from a VOLE-based OPPRF programmed with a doubly oblivious PaXoS (XoPaXoS), cuckoo hashing, and a GMW comparison circuit","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-CIRCUIT-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS","keywords":["atomic-result","oprf-to-psi","input-extraction","malicious-sender","output-length","collision","communication"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"short-oprf-outputs","contribution_kind":"security_analysis","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"technique":["batched_oprf"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Turning an oblivious PRF into malicious PSI is simple: the sender evaluates the function on its items and sends the results. The difficulty lies in the proof, where a simulator must identify the sender's items from these values. The standard argument asked for outputs long enough that no two of the sender's queries ever collide, which doubles their length to \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits. Rindal and Schoppmann pointed out that collisions among the sender's own queries are harmless unless they land on the receiver's set, which is fixed before the function is chosen. Extracting only queries without collisions keeps the simulation sound with κ-bit outputs, so the sender's message shrinks by half.","prior_boundary":"The folklore analysis of the malicious OPRF-to-PSI transformation, as used by De Cristofaro, Kim, and Tsudik and by PaXoS-PSI, extracts every queried y whose output appears in the sender's message and therefore needs \\( 2\\kappa \\)-bit outputs so that collisions among the sender's queries are negligible (§1.3, p. 5; §4, p. 19).","significance_at_publication":"It halves the sender's message in the malicious protocol; the authors list it, with the shorter encoding, as a reason their malicious protocol sends less than PaXoS-PSI, whose analysis states \\( 2\\kappa \\)-bit values (§7.1, p. 26).","technical_delta":"The new simulator discards colliding queries; a collision matters only if it hits the receiver's set, which is fixed before F is sampled, so for \\( \\kappa = 128 \\) and \\( n_x = 2^{30} \\) the sender would need about \\( 2^{98} \\) queries to distinguish, against \\( 2^{49} \\) under the folklore analysis (§4, pp. 19-20; Theorem 4.1, p. 20; Figure 6, p. 37)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS","keywords":["oprf-to-psi","input-extraction","malicious-sender","output-length","collision","communication"],"limitations":["the saving concerns the sender's message only; the cost of the OPRF itself is unchanged (§4, p. 19)","the paper's percentages differ by passage: about 33%, up to 50% (§1.3, p. 5), and 20% to 50% (§4, p. 19)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","qualifiers":["malicious security in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{oprf}} \\)-hybrid model, in which F is a truly random function (Theorem 4.1, p. 20; §4, p. 19, footnote 3)","semi-honest instantiations use outputs of \\( \\lambda + \\log_2(n_x) + \\log_2(n_y) \\) bits (Figure 6, p. 37)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS § Contributions","primary_source":"§1.1 (p. 3); §1.3 (p. 5); §4 (pp. 18-20); Theorem 4.1 (p. 20); §7.1 (p. 26); Figures 5-6 (p. 37). IACR ePrint 2021/266, PDF dated 2024-08-08.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"In the transformation where the receiver learns F(x) for its set from a batched OPRF and the sender sends {F(y)} for its set, a simulator for a malicious sender that extracts only those queried y whose output collides with no other query suffices. Hence OPRF outputs of κ bits, instead of the \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits required by the earlier analysis, give malicious security in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{oprf}} \\)-hybrid model (Theorem 4.1). The authors estimate the saving at 20% to 50% of total communication, the larger figure when the sender's set is much larger than the receiver's.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious OPRF-to-PSI transformation with κ-bit instead of \\( 2\\kappa \\)-bit sender messages, by extracting only non-colliding OPRF queries","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","sections":[{"content":"κ-bit OPRF outputs for malicious PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The transformation is the standard one (Figure 6, p. 37): the receiver queries the OPRF on its set, the sender evaluates it on its own items and sends the values in random order, and the receiver outputs the items whose values appear. For a malicious sender, the simulator records the set \\( Y^* \\) of the sender's OPRF queries, keeps only queries whose outputs are distinct from those of all other queries, and extracts those whose outputs appear in the message (Theorem 4.1 proof, p. 20). A colliding pair can cause a wrong extraction only if one of its outputs equals the output of a receiver item, an event bounded by a target preimage probability of \\( O(2^{-\\kappa}) \\) per item because the receiver's set is fixed before the function is sampled (§4, pp. 19-20). This analysis is applied to the OPRF of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF in PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-RS-MAL.","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"rs21-malicious-oprf-to-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"VOLE-PSI: Fast OPRF and Circuit-PSI from Vector-OLE","summary":"In the transformation where the receiver learns F(x) for its set from a batched OPRF and the sender sends {F(y)} for its set, a simulator for a malicious sender that extracts only those queried y whose output collides with no other query suffices. Hence OPRF outputs of κ bits, instead of the \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits required by the earlier analysis, give malicious security in the \\( F_{\\mathrm{oprf}} \\)-hybrid model (Theorem 4.1). The authors estimate the saving at 20% to 50% of total communication, the larger figure when the sender's set is much larger than the receiver's.","title":"Malicious OPRF-to-PSI transformation with κ-bit instead of \\( 2\\kappa \\)-bit sender messages, by extracting only non-colliding OPRF queries","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-SHORT-OPRF-OUTPUTS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF","keywords":["atomic-result","vole-oprf","vector-ole","batched-oprf","paxos-solver","lpn","sublinear-vole","malicious"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"vole-oprf","contribution_kind":"mechanism","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"technique":["batched_oprf","vole","paxos"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Oblivious PRFs built on OT extension paid several κ-bit strings for every position of the receiver's encoded set, and the malicious PaXoS protocol paid more, because its correlation required a long error-correcting code. Rindal and Schoppmann observed that new vector-OLE generators, which expand a short correlated seed into a long correlation, give a correlation over a field at a cost that grows sublinearly in its length. The receiver solves a sparse linear system so that its encoding decodes to a hash of each of its items, then sends the encoding masked by its VOLE share. Linearity lets both parties derive the same value on common items, and hashing with a fresh random offset turns that value into an oblivious PRF.","prior_boundary":"OPRF-based PSI from OT extension, including PaXoS-PSI and SpOT-light, sent about \\( 3.5\\kappa \\) bits per encoded position; malicious PaXoS-PSI needed \\( 5\\kappa \\) bits per position because of its error-correcting code, an encoding of \\( 11.8\\kappa n \\) bits (§7.1, pp. 25-26; Table 1, p. 25).","significance_at_publication":"With the PSI transformation, the authors report under 450 bits per element for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) with malicious security, which they call the lowest for any published PSI protocol (abstract, p. 1), and 57.86 MB against 247.6 MB for PaXoS-PSI at \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (Table 3, p. 29).","technical_delta":"Vector OLE with sublinear communication, from LPN-based generators that expand a short seed correlation (§3.1, pp. 13-14), replaces OT extension: the encoding P is sent masked by a VOLE vector, so each position costs κ bits, and linearity of decoding makes both parties' values agree on the receiver's set (§1.3, pp. 3-4; §3.2, pp. 14-15; Figure 4, p. 36). Lemmas 3.2-3.3 (pp. 16-18) prove security, with a malicious receiver's effective set bounded by the PaXoS size bound."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF","keywords":["vole-oprf","vector-ole","batched-oprf","paxos-solver","lpn","sublinear-vole","malicious"],"limitations":["the 2024 revision states that the earlier version did not correctly address extraction of a malicious receiver's effective input (§2, p. 13)","the rate-1 variant with polynomial interpolation is analyzed in theory only (§7.1, p. 24; Table 1, p. 25)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","qualifiers":["random oracle and \\( F_{\\mathrm{vole}} \\)-hybrid model; the simulators program the random oracle H (Theorem 3.1, p. 16; Lemmas 3.2-3.3, pp. 16-18)","a malicious receiver's effective input may have \\( n' = O(n) \\) elements by the PaXoS size bound, and \\( n' = m \\) under the authors' Strong OKVS Extraction Conjecture (§2, pp. 11-12; Lemma 3.3, p. 18)","the implementation instantiates VOLE from LPN with regular noise, with iterative bootstrapping and a consistency check for malicious security (§3.1, p. 14; §7.2, p. 26)","the implemented VOLE costs about \\( 2^{17} \\kappa n^{0.05} \\) bits, which dominates for small sets: 27,800 bits per element at \\( n = 2^{10} \\) against 38 at \\( n = 2^{20} \\) (§7.1, pp. 24-25)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1 and §1.3 (pp. 2-4); §2 (pp. 8-12); §3.1 and Figure 2 (pp. 13-14); §3.2 (pp. 14-15); Figure 3 and Theorem 3.1 (p. 16); Lemmas 3.2-3.3 (pp. 16-18); §7.1 and Table 1 (pp. 24-26); Table 3 (p. 29); Figure 4 (p. 36). IACR ePrint 2021/266, PDF dated 2024-08-08.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"The receiver solves for P with \\( \\langle \\mathrm{row}(x), P \\rangle = H_F(x) \\) for every x in its set using a PaXoS solver, and sends \\( A = P + A' \\), where the receiver's \\( (A', C) \\) and the sender's \\( (\\Delta, B) \\) form a random VOLE correlation \\( C = A'\\Delta + B \\) of length m. The sender sets \\( K = B + A\\Delta \\); after a coin-tossed offset w, the sender's function is \\( F(x) = H(\\mathrm{Decode}(K, x) - \\Delta H_F(x) + w, x) \\), which the receiver computes as \\( H(\\mathrm{Decode}(C, x) + w, x) \\) for its own inputs. The protocol realizes a batched OPRF against malicious adversaries in the random oracle, \\( F_{\\mathrm{vole}} \\)-hybrid model (Theorem 3.1); the encoding costs \\( \\rho\\kappa \\) bits per input, with ρ about 2.4 for PaXoS, plus a VOLE whose communication is sublinear in its length.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Batched OPRF from vector OLE and a PaXoS encoding, malicious-secure in the random oracle and VOLE-hybrid model, sending about \\( \\rho\\kappa \\) bits per input plus a sublinear VOLE","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/266","sections":[{"content":"Batched OPRF from vector OLE","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$26","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-PCG: the VOLE generators used are pseudorandom correlations that expand a short seed correlation into a long one; the implementation extends Schoppmann et al.'s primal VOLE with the iterative bootstrapping of Yang et al. (§3.1, pp. 13-14; §7.2, p. 26). These VOLE papers have no record in any topic yet.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rs21-batched-oprf-from-vector","status":"published","subtitle":"VOLE-PSI: Fast OPRF and Circuit-PSI from Vector-OLE","summary":"The receiver solves for P with \\( \\langle \\mathrm{row}(x), P \\rangle = H_F(x) \\) for every x in its set using a PaXoS solver, and sends \\( A = P + A' \\), where the receiver's \\( (A', C) \\) and the sender's \\( (\\Delta, B) \\) form a random VOLE correlation \\( C = A'\\Delta + B \\) of length m. The sender sets \\( K = B + A\\Delta \\); after a coin-tossed offset w, the sender's function is \\( F(x) = H(\\mathrm{Decode}(K, x) - \\Delta H_F(x) + w, x) \\), which the receiver computes as \\( H(\\mathrm{Decode}(C, x) + w, x) \\) for its own inputs. The protocol realizes a batched OPRF against malicious adversaries in the random oracle, \\( F_{\\mathrm{vole}} \\)-hybrid model (Theorem 3.1); the encoding costs \\( \\rho\\kappa \\) bits per input, with ρ about 2.4 for PaXoS, plus a VOLE whose communication is sublinear in its length.","title":"Batched OPRF from vector OLE and a PaXoS encoding, malicious-secure in the random oracle and VOLE-hybrid model, sending about \\( \\rho\\kappa \\) bits per input plus a sublinear VOLE","type":"result","venue":"EUROCRYPT 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-KA-TO-MALICIOUS-PSI","keywords":["atomic-result","key-agreement","polynomial-encoding","ideal-permutation","oracle-diffie-hellman","elligator","malicious","uc-security","small-sets"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"ka-to-malicious-psi","contribution_kind":"transform","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["malicious"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["ideal_permutation_model","random_oracle_model"],"technique":["key_agreement"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Diffie-Hellman PSI was the cheapest option in communication, but making it secure against malicious parties had cost several times more messages and exponentiations. Cho, Dachman-Soled, and Jarecki had shown that protocol messages can be hidden by placing them as values of a polynomial at the parties' items, though they embedded full equality tests and needed an ideal cipher. Rosulek and Trieu kept the polynomial but placed in it only responses of an ordinary key agreement, let the other party send a single opening message, and replaced the ideal cipher by an ideal permutation, with random oracles giving the simulator a way to extract inputs. Using elliptic curves, the result costs about one group element per item, and on sets of a few hundred items it ran faster than any protocol in their comparison.","prior_boundary":"Cho, Dachman-Soled, and Jarecki embedded the messages of n malicious-secure string equality tests in polynomials, using an ideal cipher (§1.2, p. 5). The most efficient malicious Diffie-Hellman PSI, by De Cristofaro, Kim, and Tsudik, sends \\( 6n \\) group elements plus n hashes and computes \\( 2n \\) fixed-base and \\( 4n \\) variable-base exponentiations (§1.2, p. 6); Jarecki and Liu's protocol relaxes input independence (§1, p. 1).","significance_at_publication":"The authors report the malicious protocol over 30 times faster than De Cristofaro-Kim-Tsudik with 80% less communication (§1.2, p. 6), and, for \\( n = 256 \\), 18-30% faster with 10% less communication than the next best protocol, which is semi-honest (§1.2, p. 5); for sets of at most 512 items they find it best in both communication and computation (§5.2, p. 20).","technical_delta":"Plain two-round key agreement replaces string equality tests, the sender sends a single key-agreement message instead of a polynomial, and an ideal permutation replaces the ideal cipher (§1.2, p. 5); random oracles H1 and H2 let the simulator extract inputs (§4, p. 10; Lemmas 5-6, pp. 10-12). A corrupt receiver's effective set size is bounded unconditionally through a polynomial overfitting game (§4.1, pp. 15-16)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-KA-TO-MALICIOUS-PSI","keywords":["key-agreement","polynomial-encoding","ideal-permutation","oracle-diffie-hellman","elligator","malicious","uc-security","small-sets"],"limitations":["the receiver's set-size slack is not removed; a tighter bound would follow from a conjecture that the authors state without proof (§4.1, p. 16)","computation is linear in exponentiations; on 10 Gbps networks OT-extension protocols overtake it between 500 and 1,000 items (§1, p. 2) and from \\( n = 2^{10} \\) in the LAN experiments (§5.2, p. 20)","the optimizations of Appendix B are not proved; the paper leaves their security to the reader (p. 28)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","qualifiers":["UC security against a malicious sender (Lemma 5, p. 10) and a malicious receiver (Lemma 6, p. 12), in the ideal permutation plus random oracle model (abstract, p. 1)","only the receiver learns the intersection (Figure 2, p. 6)","a corrupt sender's set is enforced to size n; a corrupt receiver may input up to \\( n' \\) items, for example fewer than \\( 8n + 4 \\) for \\( n = 2^{10} \\) or \\( 2^{20} \\) and \\( |F| = 2^{256} \\), by an unconditional bound (§4.1, Lemma 10, pp. 15-16)","for a one-round key agreement such as Diffie-Hellman, the sender's two messages combine into a two-round protocol (§4, p. 15)","the implementation instantiates Π with fixed-key Rijndael-256 and the random oracles with SHA2 over Curve25519 with elligator encoding (§5.1, pp. 16-18)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1 (pp. 1-2); §1.2 (pp. 4-6); Table 1 (p. 4); Figure 2 (p. 6); §2.4 (p. 7); §3.1 Definitions 1-4 (p. 8); §3.2 (pp. 8-9); §4, Lemma 5 (p. 10); Figure 4 (p. 11); Lemma 6 (p. 12); Lemma 7 (p. 14); §4 'Optimizations' and 'Costs' (p. 15); §4.1, Definition 8, Proposition 9, Lemma 10 (pp. 15-16); §5.1 (pp. 16-18); Table 2 (p. 17); Table 3 (p. 18); §5.2 (pp. 19-20). IACR ePrint 2021/1159, version of October 17, 2021.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"The sender sends one key-agreement message m; for each item \\( y_i \\) the receiver computes a fresh response \\( m'_i \\) and sends the polynomial P with \\( P(H_1(y_i)) = \\Pi^{-1}(m'_i) \\); the sender computes \\( k_i = \\mathrm{KA.key1}(a, \\Pi(P(H_1(x_i)))) \\) for each item and sends the shuffled values \\( H_2(x_i, k_i) \\); the receiver outputs the items whose own key yields a value in that set. The protocol is UC-secure against a malicious sender if the key agreement has pseudorandom second messages, and against a malicious receiver if it is non-malleable with at least \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\) keys, with Π an ideal permutation and H1, H2 random oracles. Instantiated with hashed elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman and elligator encoding, non-malleability is the oracle Diffie-Hellman assumption, and communication is one group element, n field elements, and n strings of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Malicious two-party PSI from any two-round key agreement with pseudorandom second messages and non-malleability, by polynomial encoding of the responses, ideal permutation and random oracle model","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","sections":[{"content":"Key agreement to malicious PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Figure 4 (p. 11) needs a key agreement whose second-message space is a field F with pseudorandom messages, an ideal permutation Π on F, and random oracles \\( H_1: \\{0,1\\}^* \\to F \\) and \\( H_2: \\{0,1\\}^* \\times F \\to \\{0,1\\}^{2\\kappa} \\). The simulator against a corrupt sender programs Π so that every output is a key-agreement message with known randomness and extracts from H2 queries (Lemma 5, p. 10); against a corrupt receiver it extracts the items y with an H1 query whose \\( P(H_1(y)) \\) was obtained from \\( \\Pi^{-1} \\) (Lemma 6, p. 12), relying on non-malleability to replace the other keys by random ones (Lemma 7, p. 14). Costs: one key-agreement message and n keys for the sender, n responses and n keys for the receiver, plus interpolation and multi-point evaluation of a degree-n polynomial in \\( O(n \\log^2 n) \\) field operations (§4, p. 15).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"PKE-RESULT-1976-DH-INTERACTIVE-PUBLIC-CHANNEL-KEY-AGREEMENT: the key agreement used in the instantiation, in its hashed variant with elligator encoding (§3.2, pp. 8-9). Cho-Dachman-Soled-Jarecki (CT-RSA 2016), the stated starting point (§1.2, p. 5), the ideal permutation model, elligator encodings, and the oracle Diffie-Hellman assumption have no record in any topic.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rt21-key-agreement-to-malicious","status":"published","subtitle":"Compact and Malicious Private Set Intersection for Small Sets","summary":"The sender sends one key-agreement message m; for each item \\( y_i \\) the receiver computes a fresh response \\( m'_i \\) and sends the polynomial P with \\( P(H_1(y_i)) = \\Pi^{-1}(m'_i) \\); the sender computes \\( k_i = \\mathrm{KA.key1}(a, \\Pi(P(H_1(x_i)))) \\) for each item and sends the shuffled values \\( H_2(x_i, k_i) \\); the receiver outputs the items whose own key yields a value in that set. The protocol is UC-secure against a malicious sender if the key agreement has pseudorandom second messages, and against a malicious receiver if it is non-malleable with at least \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\) keys, with Π an ideal permutation and H1, H2 random oracles. Instantiated with hashed elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman and elligator encoding, non-malleability is the oracle Diffie-Hellman assumption, and communication is one group element, n field elements, and n strings of \\( 2\\kappa \\) bits.","title":"Malicious two-party PSI from any two-round key agreement with pseudorandom second messages and non-malleability, by polynomial encoding of the responses, ideal permutation and random oracle model","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-KA-TO-MALICIOUS-PSI"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION","keywords":["atomic-result","diffie-hellman","communication","semi-honest","polynomial-encoding","elligator","ideal-permutation"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"semi-honest-dh-communication","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["ideal_permutation_model","random_oracle_model"],"technique":["key_agreement"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"The two-message Diffie-Hellman intersection protocol from the late 1990s remained the reference point for low communication, and later work had trimmed only its computation. Rosulek and Trieu observed that in their key agreement construction one party needs to send only a single opening message, while the other packs one response per item into a polynomial. For honest-but-curious parties the hashing used to extract inputs can be dropped and the returned keys shortened to a statistical length. Two variants result: one relies on an ideal permutation and the computational Diffie-Hellman assumption, the other avoids the permutation at the cost of one extra field element and a stronger oracle assumption. Both send about one group element per item in total, which the authors present as the first communication gain over the classic protocol.","prior_boundary":"The classic semi-honest Diffie-Hellman PSI of Huberman, Franklin, and Hogg, as the paper describes it, sends \\( 2n \\) group elements plus n hashes and computes \\( 4n \\) variable-base exponentiations (§1.2, pp. 5-6); the only improvement the authors know, due to Jarecki and Liu, lowers computation by about 5-15% (§1, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"The authors call this the first improvement in the communication of Diffie-Hellman PSI in over 20 years, by over 40% (§1, p. 2), with 45% less communication than the next best protocol for \\( n = 256 \\) (§1.2, p. 5); for \\( n_1 = 2^{16} \\) and \\( n_2 = 2^{20} \\) it sends 12.58 MB against 46.14 MB (§5.2, p. 19).","technical_delta":"One party sends a single key-agreement message and the other encodes its n responses in one polynomial, so the total is \\( n + 1 \\) group elements plus n hashes, with \\( 3n \\) fixed-base and \\( 2n \\) variable-base exponentiations and two degree-n interpolation or evaluation steps (§1.2, p. 6; Appendix A.1, Figure 5, p. 24; Appendix A.2, Figure 6, p. 26)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION","keywords":["diffie-hellman","communication","semi-honest","polynomial-encoding","elligator","ideal-permutation"],"limitations":["the hardness assumptions in Table 1 do not list the random oracle or the ideal permutation (Table 1, p. 4)","computation remains one or two exponentiations per item, so OT-extension protocols are faster on large sets in the LAN setting (§5.2, p. 20)","the comparison with classic Diffie-Hellman PSI uses the paper's own description of that protocol, with n hashes in place of n group elements in one direction (§1.2, pp. 5-6; Table 1, p. 4)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","qualifiers":["variant 1: secure against a semi-honest sender if the key agreement has pseudorandom messages and Π is an ideal permutation (Lemma 11, p. 24), and against a semi-honest receiver if the key agreement is secure against eavesdroppers (Lemma 12, p. 25)","variant 2: no ideal permutation; secure against a semi-honest receiver if the key agreement is non-malleable with pseudorandom second messages (Lemma 13, p. 27)","hashed Diffie-Hellman key agreement is secure against eavesdroppers under CDH in the random oracle model (§3.2, p. 8)","only the receiver learns the intersection (Figure 5, step 7, p. 24)","the October 2021 revision repaired a bug in the original semi-honest proof and added variant 2 (§1.3, p. 6)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT § Contributions","primary_source":"§1 (p. 2); §1.2 (pp. 5-6); §1.3 (p. 6); Table 1 (p. 4); §3.2 (pp. 8-9); §4 (p. 15); Appendix A.1, Figure 5, Lemmas 11-12 (pp. 23-26); Appendix A.2, Figure 6, Lemma 13 (pp. 26-28); Table 2 (p. 17); Table 3 (p. 18); §5.2 (pp. 19-20). IACR ePrint 2021/1159, version of October 17, 2021.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Dropping the random oracles used only for extraction, the receiver interpolates P with \\( \\Pi(P(y_i)) = m'_i \\) and the sender returns its keys shortened to \\( \\lambda + 2 \\log n \\) bits (variant 1), which is secure against semi-honest parties if the key agreement is secure against eavesdroppers with pseudorandom second messages and Π is an ideal permutation; variant 2 drops the ideal permutation by fixing P(0) to a random value, at one extra field element, and needs a non-malleable key agreement. With hashed elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman, communication is \\( (\\lambda + \\log(n_1 n_2))n_1 + \\phi n_2 + \\phi \\) bits for variant 1 and one more ϕ for variant 2, against \\( (\\phi + \\lambda + \\log(n_1 n_2))n_1 + \\phi n_2 \\) bits for classic Diffie-Hellman PSI, under CDH (variant 1) or oracle Diffie-Hellman (variant 2).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Semi-honest Diffie-Hellman PSI communication of one key-agreement message, one field element per receiver item, and one short key per sender item, from polynomial-encoded key-agreement responses","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","year":2021},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/1159","sections":[{"content":"Lower-communication semi-honest Diffie-Hellman PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Variant 1 (Figure 5, p. 24): the sender sends \\( m = \\mathrm{KA.msg1}(a) \\); the receiver sends P interpolated on \\( (y_i, \\Pi^{-1}(\\mathrm{KA.msg2}(b_i, m))) \\); the sender sends the shuffled keys \\( k_i = \\mathrm{KA.key1}(a, \\Pi(P(x_i))) \\) of length \\( \\lambda + 2 \\log n \\); the receiver outputs \\( y_i \\) with \\( \\mathrm{KA.key2}(b_i, m) \\) in the set. Variant 2 (Figure 6, p. 26): \\( P(y_i) \\) is the key-agreement message itself and \\( P(0) = R \\) for a random R, which lets the simulator program one evaluation point at a time (Appendix A.2, p. 26). Correctness fails with probability at most about \\( n^2/|\\mathrm{KA.K}| \\) (Appendix A.1, p. 24).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"PKE-RESULT-1976-DH-INTERACTIVE-PUBLIC-CHANNEL-KEY-AGREEMENT: the hashed Diffie-Hellman key agreement used in both variants (§3.2, p. 8).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rt21-semi-honest-diffie-hellman","status":"published","subtitle":"Compact and Malicious Private Set Intersection for Small Sets","summary":"Dropping the random oracles used only for extraction, the receiver interpolates P with \\( \\Pi(P(y_i)) = m'_i \\) and the sender returns its keys shortened to \\( \\lambda + 2 \\log n \\) bits (variant 1), which is secure against semi-honest parties if the key agreement is secure against eavesdroppers with pseudorandom second messages and Π is an ideal permutation; variant 2 drops the ideal permutation by fixing P(0) to a random value, at one extra field element, and needs a non-malleable key agreement. With hashed elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman, communication is \\( (\\lambda + \\log(n_1 n_2))n_1 + \\phi n_2 + \\phi \\) bits for variant 1 and one more ϕ for variant 2, against \\( (\\phi + \\lambda + \\log(n_1 n_2))n_1 + \\phi n_2 \\) bits for classic Diffie-Hellman PSI, under CDH (variant 1) or oracle Diffie-Hellman (variant 2).","title":"Semi-honest Diffie-Hellman PSI communication of one key-agreement message, one field element per receiver item, and one short key per sender item, from polynomial-encoded key-agreement responses","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2021","year":2021,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RT-SEMI-HONEST-DH-COMMUNICATION"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-SUBFIELD-VOLE","keywords":["atomic-result","subfield-vole","vole-psi","communication","statistical-security","okvs","semi-honest"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"subfield-vole","contribution_kind":"optimization","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["two_party"],"security_model":["random_oracle_model"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["subfield_vole","okvs","batched_oprf"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"In VOLE-based PSI the receiver sends its encoded set masked by a VOLE vector, so every encoded position costs one field element, and the field had to be as large as the computational security parameter. Raghuraman and Rindal separated the two roles of that field. The sender's secret multiplier must be hard to guess, but the encoded values only need to avoid accidental collisions among all pairs of items, which takes about \\( \\lambda + \\log n \\) bits. Subfield VOLE, in which one vector lies in a small base field and everything else in a large extension, provides exactly this split. The receiver's message shrinks to statistical-parameter size per position, and communication no longer grows with κ times the set size.","prior_boundary":"VOLE-PSI performed all VOLE arithmetic over a field of about \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\) elements, so derandomizing the correlation cost about κ bits per OKVS position, and its communication scaled with κ times the set size (abstract, p. 1; §1.2, p. 4).","significance_at_publication":"The authors call it the first PSI with communication \\( O(n\\lambda + n \\log n) \\), not scaling with \\( \\kappa n \\) (abstract, p. 1), and report \\( 180n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) against \\( 426n \\) for VOLE-PSI (§1.1, p. 3; Table 2, p. 25).","technical_delta":"Only Δ must come from a large field for security; the encoded values need only be long enough to avoid spurious matches among the \\( n_x n_y \\) pairs, so the OKVS lives in the base field of a subfield VOLE (§1.2, p. 4; §4, pp. 21-22; Figure 9, p. 23; Figure 12, p. 32); the proof is Theorem 3 (pp. 33-35)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-SUBFIELD-VOLE","keywords":["subfield-vole","vole-psi","communication","statistical-security","okvs","semi-honest"],"limitations":["the abstract reports \\( 187n \\) bits for \\( n = 2^{20} \\), while §1.1 (p. 3) and Table 2 (p. 25) give \\( 180n \\)","the low-communication instantiation is slower than the fast one: 1,353 ms against 369 ms for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) on one thread (Table 2, p. 25)","the malicious protocol of the paper does not use subfield VOLE (§4, p. 23)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","qualifiers":["semi-honest security only, in the \\( F_{\\text{sub-vole}} \\)-hybrid model with random oracles \\( H_B \\) and \\( H_o \\) (Theorem 3, p. 33)","requires \\( |B| \\ge 2^{\\lambda + \\log_2(n_x) + \\log_2(n_y)} \\) and \\( |F| \\ge 2^{\\kappa} \\) (Theorem 3, p. 33)","the low-communication instantiation uses the OKVS without clustering, \\( m = 1.23n \\) (§4, p. 22)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.1-1.2 (pp. 3-4); §4 (pp. 21-23); Figure 9 (p. 23); Table 2 (p. 25); Figure 12 (p. 32); Appendix C.1, Theorem 3 (pp. 33-35). IACR ePrint 2022/320, PDF dated 2022-11-02.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"In semi-honest VOLE-based PSI, a subfield VOLE \\( C = A\\Delta + B \\), with A over a small base field B and \\( \\Delta, B, C \\) over an extension F of size at least \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\), lets the receiver derandomize the correlation by sending \\( A - P \\), where P is an OKVS over B that decodes to \\( H_B(x) \\) on its items. If \\( |B| \\ge 2^{\\lambda + \\log_2(n_x n_y)} \\), the protocol realizes PSI against semi-honest adversaries in the \\( F_{\\text{sub-vole}} \\)-hybrid model (Theorem 3). The receiver's message is \\( m \\cdot \\log_2 |B| \\) bits, and total communication is \\( O(n\\lambda + n \\log n) \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Subfield VOLE in VOLE-based PSI: OKVS entries in a base field of about \\( \\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y) \\) bits, giving \\( O(n\\lambda + n \\log n) \\) communication, semi-honest","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","year":2022},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","sections":[{"content":"Subfield VOLE in VOLE-based PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"The receiver encodes \\( L = \\{(x, H_B(x, r'))\\} \\) with the OKVS of PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS over the base field B, obtains a random subfield VOLE with A over B and \\( \\Delta, B, C \\) over F, and sends \\( A' = A - P \\), which costs \\( m \\cdot \\log_2 |B| \\) bits (Figure 9, steps 1-3, p. 23; §4, p. 22). The sender's \\( B' = B + A'\\Delta \\) satisfies \\( C - B' = \\Delta P \\), so by linearity \\( \\mathrm{Decode}(C, x) = \\mathrm{Decode}(B', x) + \\Delta H_B(x) \\) for each x in the receiver's set; both parties hash these values with \\( H_o \\), and the sender sends its hashed values for Y (§4, p. 22; Figure 9, steps 4-5, p. 23). Because Δ is uniform in a field of size at least \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\), the receiver cannot predict the sender's values outside the intersection (Theorem 3 proof, Hybrid 6, p. 34). The protocol is the semi-honest OPRF-based PSI of PSI-CONTRIB-2021-RS-VOLE-OPRF with the VOLE moved to a subfield. The complete row is PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-SD.","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"MPC-COMP-CORR-PCG: the starting point is the subfield VOLE of Couteau, Rindal, and Raghuraman (Silver) and of Boyle et al., CCS 2019 (§4, p. 22); neither paper has a record yet.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"rr22-subfield-vole-psi","status":"published","subtitle":"Blazing Fast PSI from Improved OKVS and Subfield VOLE","summary":"In semi-honest VOLE-based PSI, a subfield VOLE \\( C = A\\Delta + B \\), with A over a small base field B and \\( \\Delta, B, C \\) over an extension F of size at least \\( 2^{\\kappa} \\), lets the receiver derandomize the correlation by sending \\( A - P \\), where P is an OKVS over B that decodes to \\( H_B(x) \\) on its items. If \\( |B| \\ge 2^{\\lambda + \\log_2(n_x n_y)} \\), the protocol realizes PSI against semi-honest adversaries in the \\( F_{\\text{sub-vole}} \\)-hybrid model (Theorem 3). The receiver's message is \\( m \\cdot \\log_2 |B| \\) bits, and total communication is \\( O(n\\lambda + n \\log n) \\).","title":"Subfield VOLE in VOLE-based PSI: OKVS entries in a base field of about \\( \\lambda + \\log(n_x n_y) \\) bits, giving \\( O(n\\lambda + n \\log n) \\) communication, semi-honest","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2022","year":2022,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-SUBFIELD-VOLE"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS","keywords":["atomic-result","okvs","triangulation","gap","back-substitution","phase-transition","clustering","doubly-oblivious","failure-probability"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"triangulation-okvs","contribution_kind":"mechanism","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"technique":["okvs"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Sparse encodings such as the three-hash garbled cuckoo table were fast in principle, but their concrete parameters were shaky. The unpeelable core was solved wholesale with many dense columns, and a failure bound of \\( 2^{-40} \\) was reached only through an amplification that doubled the work. Raghuraman and Rindal recast encoding as triangulating a sparse matrix. A greedy pass finds the rows that back-substitution can handle and leaves a gap of a few rows, which a handful of dense columns over a large field resolve. They proved the greedy pass optimal in several cases and measured how the gap behaves around the known phase transition of random hypergraphs, which let them choose the expansion directly for a target security level.","prior_boundary":"The three-hash table of Garimella et al. solved its whole 2-core with \\( \\lambda + |\\text{2-core}| \\) dense columns, needed two graph traversals, and reached failure \\( 2^{-40} \\) only through the star amplification, at expansion 1.32 for \\( n = 2^{20} \\); its 2-core bound came from an experiment (§1, p. 3; §5.1, p. 24).","significance_at_publication":"The authors report encoding \\( 2^{20} \\) items in 653 ms and decoding them in 54 ms, 13 and 64 times faster than the three-hash table, and 143 ms with clustering against 4,912 ms for the star construction (§5.1, p. 23; Table 1, p. 25); with this OKVS their PSI runs in 369 ms for \\( n = 2^{20} \\) on one thread, against 2,073 ms for KKRT on the same laptop (Table 2, p. 25).","technical_delta":"Figure 1 (p. 7) triangulates by repeatedly selecting the sparse column of minimum nonzero weight, so only the rows left in the gap need dense columns (§2, pp. 5-8; Lemmas 1-2, p. 8); clustering confines rows to blocks of about \\( 2^{14} \\) columns for memory locality and threads (§2.1, pp. 9-10). Section 3 proves optimality for \\( w = 2 \\) (Lemma 4, p. 12), for \\( g = 0 \\) (Lemma 9, p. 19), and for duplicate rows (§3.3, p. 20), and derives parameters from Equations (1)-(3) (pp. 14-19; §3.4, p. 21)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS","keywords":["okvs","triangulation","gap","back-substitution","phase-transition","clustering","doubly-oblivious","failure-probability"],"limitations":["for \\( w > 2 \\) optimal triangulation is argued to be hard in general, and in the worst case the greedy gap can be as large as the 2-core minus one (§3.2, p. 15; §3.3, p. 20)","the fitted bound of Equation (3) is stated for \\( 3 \\le w \\le 20 \\) and becomes an upper bound outside that range (§3.2, p. 19)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","qualifiers":["concrete security levels come from empirical curves fitted for \\( 30 \\le \\lambda \\le 128 \\), \\( 3 \\le w \\le 10 \\), and \\( 2^6 \\le n \\le 2^{30} \\), and the line through a common point is a conjectured trend (§3.1, p. 14; §3.2, pp. 17-19)","with dense columns over a large field the values must be field elements; the binary dense variant encodes arbitrary strings (§2, p. 9)","a doubly oblivious variant, used for circuit PSI, appends random rows so that the output is uniform (Figure 1, step 5, p. 7; Theorem 2, p. 31)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1-1.2 (pp. 2-4); §2 and Figure 1 (pp. 5-8); Lemmas 1-2 (p. 8); §2.1 (pp. 9-10); §3.1, Lemmas 3-6 and Figures 2-3 (pp. 10-14); §3.2, Lemmas 7-8 and Figures 4-8 (pp. 14-19); §3.3, Lemmas 9-10 (pp. 19-21); §3.4 (p. 21); §5.1 and Table 1 (pp. 23-25); Table 2 (p. 25); Figure 10 (p. 30); Appendix A (pp. 30-31). IACR ePrint 2022/320, PDF dated 2022-11-02.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Each key's row has w ones among \\( m' = en \\) sparse positions plus a short dense part, either random bits or powers of a hashed element of a large field. The encoder permutes rows and columns into approximately lower-triangular form, leaving a gap of g rows that it solves with the dense columns in \\( O(g^2) \\) time, and back-substitutes the rest, for \\( O(m + g^2) \\) time in total. The greedy triangulation is optimal for \\( w = 2 \\), where \\( g = O(\\log n) \\) at \\( e = 2 \\), and finds a gap of zero whenever one exists; for \\( w \\ge 3 \\) the authors fit empirical curves around the 2-core phase transition into a formula giving the statistical security from n, w, and e. For \\( w = 3 \\) they use \\( e = 1.23 \\) without clustering and \\( e = 1.28 \\) with clusters of about \\( 2^{14} \\) columns.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"OKVS by sparse triangulation with a small gap: about \\( 1.23n \\) positions for row weight 3, linear-time encoding, and concrete failure-probability bounds","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","year":2022},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/320","sections":[{"content":"Triangulation OKVS","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"$27","heading":"Exact contribution"}],"slug":"rr22-triangulation-okvs","status":"published","subtitle":"Blazing Fast PSI from Improved OKVS and Subfield VOLE","summary":"Each key's row has w ones among \\( m' = en \\) sparse positions plus a short dense part, either random bits or powers of a hashed element of a large field. The encoder permutes rows and columns into approximately lower-triangular form, leaving a gap of g rows that it solves with the dense columns in \\( O(g^2) \\) time, and back-substitutes the rest, for \\( O(m + g^2) \\) time in total. The greedy triangulation is optimal for \\( w = 2 \\), where \\( g = O(\\log n) \\) at \\( e = 2 \\), and finds a gap of zero whenever one exists; for \\( w \\ge 3 \\) the authors fit empirical curves around the 2-core phase transition into a formula giving the statistical security from n, w, and e. For \\( w = 3 \\) they use \\( e = 1.23 \\) without clustering and \\( e = 1.28 \\) with clusters of about \\( 2^{14} \\) columns.","title":"OKVS by sparse triangulation with a small gap: about \\( 1.23n \\) positions for row weight 3, linear-time encoding, and concrete failure-probability bounds","type":"result","venue":"ACM CCS 2022","year":2022,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2022-RR-TRIANGULATION-OKVS"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY-QUASILINEAR","keywords":["atomic-result","multiparty-threshold-psi","quasilinear-communication","cardinality-testing","oblivious-transfer","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"multiparty-quasilinear","contribution_kind":"construction","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["threshold"],"parties":["multiparty"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["secret_sharing","simple_hashing","additively_homomorphic_encryption"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Threshold PSI among many parties had two kinds of protocols that avoid paying for the full sets: those built on fully homomorphic encryption, nearly linear in the threshold, and those built on additively homomorphic encryption, quadratic in it. One protocol was nearly linear without fully homomorphic encryption, but only under a stricter condition that bounds all elements outside the intersection together. Ghosh and Simkin applied their bucketing compiler to the quadratic multiparty cardinality test of Branco, Döttling, and Pu, after rewriting it with generic secure computation, and then reused their own earlier step for computing a large intersection. The result releases the intersection when every set is close to it, communicates quasilinearly in the threshold, and needs only oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption.","prior_boundary":"Ghosh and Simkin gave \\( O(\\lambda t^2 \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) for two parties from oblivious transfer and additively homomorphic encryption and \\( O(\\lambda t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) from FHE; Branco, Döttling, and Pu gave \\( O(\\lambda\\ell t^2 \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) for ℓ parties from threshold additively homomorphic encryption; Badrinarayanan et al. gave \\( O(\\lambda\\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) when the union exceeds the intersection by at most t, and FHE-based protocols matching \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) up to polylog factors (abstract, p. 1; §1, pp. 1-2). The authors state that all three left asymptotically optimal multiparty protocols from assumptions other than FHE open (§1, p. 2).","significance_at_publication":"It gives multiparty threshold PSI for the large-intersection condition with quasilinear rather than quadratic dependence on t from simple assumptions, which the authors describe as a significant theoretical step (abstract, p. 1; §1.2, pp. 2-3).","technical_delta":"The compiler of the paper reduces the threshold handled by the inner multiparty cardinality test to \\( O(\\ln t\\ell) \\), so the quadratic inner protocol contributes only polylogarithmic factors (§4.1, p. 16; Corollary 5, p. 16); the intersection is then computed with the earlier Ghosh-Simkin protocol at \\( \\tilde O(\\lambda\\ell t) \\) (§1.2, p. 2; Corollary 6, p. 16)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY-QUASILINEAR","keywords":["multiparty-threshold-psi","quasilinear-communication","cardinality-testing","oblivious-transfer","additively-homomorphic-encryption"],"limitations":["no round complexity is stated for the multiparty protocol (Corollaries 5-6, p. 16)","not claimed practical; concretely efficient protocols are left open (§1.2, p. 3)","the bound carries a \\( \\lambda^2 \\) factor and \\( \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) factors relative to the \\( \\Omega(\\ell t) \\) lower bound shown in the earlier works (§1, p. 2; abstract, p. 1)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","qualifiers":["passive security against a static adversary corrupting all but one party, with pairwise private channels and a unit-cost broadcast channel (§2.1, p. 4; Corollary 6, p. 16)","all sets have exactly n elements, and the output condition is an intersection of at least \\( n - t \\) elements (Figure 9, p. 11; abstract, p. 1)","the inner multiparty cardinality test of Branco, Döttling, and Pu is assumed adaptable to generic secure computation at \\( O(\\lambda\\ell \\,\\mathrm{poly}(t)) \\) (§4.1, p. 16)","\\( \\epsilon/\\lambda \\) is in \\( O(1) \\), so the bound is \\( O(\\lambda^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) in the security parameter (§2, p. 4; abstract, p. 1)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1 (pp. 1-2); §1.2 (pp. 2-3); §2.1 (p. 4); §4 with Figure 9 (p. 11); §4.1 with Theorem 8, Corollaries 5-6 (pp. 15-16). IACR ePrint 2023/919, PDF dated 2023-06-12.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/919","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Assuming oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption, there is a passively secure protocol for ℓ parties holding sets of n elements that outputs the intersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) elements, with communication \\( O(\\epsilon^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits (Corollary 6): the multiparty cardinality-test compiler is instantiated with the protocol of Branco, Döttling, and Pu, rewritten to use generic secure computation at \\( O(\\lambda\\ell \\,\\mathrm{poly}(t)) \\), run \\( O(\\ln t) \\) times at threshold \\( O(\\ln t) \\) in each of \\( \\epsilon t \\) buckets, and followed by the Ghosh-Simkin protocol for computing a large intersection.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI with communication quasilinear in t, \\( O(\\epsilon^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits, from oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption, passive security","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","year":2023},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/919","sections":[{"content":"Quasilinear multiparty threshold PSI without FHE","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Corollary 5 (p. 16) gives an ℓ-party cardinality test with communication \\( O(\\epsilon^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits from oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption; Corollary 6 (p. 16) combines it with the Ghosh-Simkin intersection step into passively secure ℓ-party threshold PSI with the same communication. The functionality is the large-intersection one, \\( |\\cap X_i| \\ge n - t \\) (Figure 9, p. 11), which for \\( \\ell > 2 \\) differs from the union-based condition under which Badrinarayanan et al. reach \\( O(\\lambda\\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) without FHE (§1, p. 2).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"gs23-multiparty-threshold-psi-wit","status":"published","subtitle":"Threshold Private Set Intersection with Better Communication Complexity","summary":"Assuming oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption, there is a passively secure protocol for ℓ parties holding sets of n elements that outputs the intersection if it has at least \\( n - t \\) elements, with communication \\( O(\\epsilon^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits (Corollary 6): the multiparty cardinality-test compiler is instantiated with the protocol of Branco, Döttling, and Pu, rewritten to use generic secure computation at \\( O(\\lambda\\ell \\,\\mathrm{poly}(t)) \\), run \\( O(\\ln t) \\) times at threshold \\( O(\\ln t) \\) in each of \\( \\epsilon t \\) buckets, and followed by the Ghosh-Simkin protocol for computing a large intersection.","title":"Multiparty threshold PSI with communication quasilinear in t, \\( O(\\epsilon^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) bits, from oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption, passive security","type":"result","venue":"PKC 2023","year":2023,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-MULTIPARTY-QUASILINEAR"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER","keywords":["atomic-result","cardinality-testing","compiler","random-partitioning","divide-and-conquer","padding","secret-sharing","threshold-psi"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"pict-compiler","contribution_kind":"transform","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest"],"output":["threshold"],"parties":["two_party","multiparty"],"set_size_regime":["balanced"],"technique":["secret_sharing","simple_hashing"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Threshold PSI protocols spend most of their effort on a single bit: are the parties' sets close enough that their intersection should be released? Known tests for that bit cost a polynomial in the threshold, quadratic in the best multiparty protocols that avoid fully homomorphic encryption. Ghosh and Simkin observed that random partitioning shrinks the threshold. After the elements are hashed into as many buckets as the threshold, each bucket of nearly identical sets differs in only logarithmically many elements, so an expensive test run per bucket with a logarithmic threshold costs only polylogarithmically much. Repeating the partition a security parameter's worth of times makes it likely that one partition works, and secret sharing lets the parties add bucket counts without revealing them. Any polynomial-cost test thus becomes quasilinear.","prior_boundary":"Every threshold PSI protocol, following Ghosh and Simkin, first runs a cardinality test that outputs one bit and then computes the intersection; the second step already costs \\( \\tilde O(\\lambda\\ell t) \\) from oblivious transfer or additively homomorphic encryption (abstract, p. 1; §1.2, p. 2). Multiparty cardinality tests from assumptions weaker than FHE, such as that of Branco, Döttling, and Pu, cost \\( O(\\lambda\\ell t^2 \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) (§1, p. 1).","significance_at_publication":"The efficiency of the inner protocol only affects the constant in the \\( \\mathrm{polylog}(t) \\) exponent, so multiparty cardinality tests with quasilinear dependence on t follow from effectively any assumption implying secure computation (§1.2, p. 2).","technical_delta":"Random partitioning into t buckets leaves each bucket, with constant probability per partition, at most \\( O(\\ln t\\ell) \\) elements outside its intersection (Lemma 2, p. 7; Theorem 5, p. 12); ϵ partitions make some partition good except with probability \\( 2^{-\\epsilon} \\) (Theorems 2 and 6, pp. 8, 14). Padding keeps bucket sizes equal so intersection sizes stay meaningful (Lemmas 3 and 5, pp. 11-14), and the exact bucket intersection size is assembled from cardinality tests at each small threshold (Figures 8 and 12; Theorems 4 and 8, pp. 10, 16)."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER","keywords":["cardinality-testing","compiler","random-partitioning","divide-and-conquer","padding","secret-sharing","threshold-psi"],"limitations":["the authors consider the results too inefficient for most real-world parameters (§1.2, p. 3)","the two-party instantiation does not improve asymptotically on the existing \\( O(\\lambda t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\) (p. 4)","Theorems 3 and 7 are stated in hybrid models with ideal functionalities for the bucket counts and comparisons (pp. 9, 15)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","qualifiers":["static passive adversary corrupting all but one party; pairwise authenticated private channels and a broadcast channel of unit cost; standalone simulation-based security (§2.1, p. 4)","the input PICT must output secret shares, which the authors say all existing protocols can easily be adapted to do (§1.2, footnote 3, p. 2)","the partitions use public random functions; pseudorandom functions with a key known to all parties are said to work equally well (footnote 4, p. 3)","ϵ is the statistical security parameter with \\( \\epsilon/\\lambda \\) in \\( O(1) \\), and the field has at least \\( 2^{\\epsilon} \\) elements (§2, p. 4)","the multiparty version requires all sets to have exactly n elements (Figure 9, p. 11); the two-party version allows sets of at most n elements (Figure 1, p. 5)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1.2 (p. 2); §1.3 (pp. 3-4); §2 with Figures 1-5 (pp. 4-6); §3 with Lemmas 1-2, Theorems 2-4, Corollary 1, and Figures 6-8 (pp. 6-10); §4 with Lemmas 3-5, Theorems 5-8, Corollary 4, and Figures 9-12 (pp. 11-16). IACR ePrint 2023/919, PDF dated 2023-06-12.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/919","status":"theorem_checked"},"statement":"Against a static passive adversary corrupting all but one of ℓ parties, any private intersection cardinality test (PICT) with secret-shared output and communication \\( O(\\lambda\\ell \\,\\mathrm{poly}(t)) \\) yields a PICT for threshold t with communication \\( O(\\lambda^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\): the parties hash their sets ϵ times with public random functions into t buckets, pad every bucket to a common size, obtain for each bucket a secret sharing of its intersection size (or of a default value when the bucket's threshold \\( \\tilde t = O(\\ln t\\ell) \\) is exceeded) by running the given PICT at each threshold up to \\( \\tilde t \\), sum these per partition, and test with generic secure computation whether some partition's sum shows an intersection of at least \\( n - t \\); failure probability is at most \\( 2^{-\\epsilon} \\).","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Compiler from any intersection cardinality test with secret-shared output and \\( O(\\lambda\\ell \\,\\mathrm{poly}(t)) \\) communication to one with \\( O(\\lambda^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\), passive security","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","year":2023},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/919","sections":[{"content":"Compiler for intersection cardinality tests","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Two-party version (Figure 7, p. 9): for each of ϵ partitions and each of t buckets, the parties call \\( F_{\\triangle} \\) with threshold \\( \\tilde t = 1 + 3 \\ln 2t \\) and default \\( t + 1 \\), add the shares per partition, and call \\( F_{\\text{vec-leq}} \\) to test whether some sum is at most t (Theorem 3, p. 9). \\( F_{\\triangle} \\) is realized from \\( \\tilde t \\) cardinality tests at thresholds \\( 1, \\ldots, \\tilde t \\) (Figure 8, Theorem 4, p. 10). Multiparty version (Figure 11, p. 15): buckets are padded to size \\( n + 1 \\), \\( F_{\\ell\\text{-}\\cap} \\) returns the bucket intersection size or the default \\( n + 1 \\), \\( F_{\\ell\\text{-min}} \\) removes the padding offset, and \\( F_{\\ell\\text{-vec-intrvl}} \\) tests whether some partition's total lies between \\( n - t \\) and n (Theorem 7, p. 15); \\( F_{\\ell\\text{-}\\cap} \\) is realized from cardinality tests at each threshold (Figure 12, Theorem 8, p. 16).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"None. Cramer and Damgård's constant-round secure linear algebra, used to instantiate the two-party inner test (p. 10), has no entry yet.","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"gs23-bucketing-compiler-for-cardi","status":"published","subtitle":"Threshold Private Set Intersection with Better Communication Complexity","summary":"Against a static passive adversary corrupting all but one of ℓ parties, any private intersection cardinality test (PICT) with secret-shared output and communication \\( O(\\lambda\\ell \\,\\mathrm{poly}(t)) \\) yields a PICT for threshold t with communication \\( O(\\lambda^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\): the parties hash their sets ϵ times with public random functions into t buckets, pad every bucket to a common size, obtain for each bucket a secret sharing of its intersection size (or of a default value when the bucket's threshold \\( \\tilde t = O(\\ln t\\ell) \\) is exceeded) by running the given PICT at each threshold up to \\( \\tilde t \\), sum these per partition, and test with generic secure computation whether some partition's sum shows an intersection of at least \\( n - t \\); failure probability is at most \\( 2^{-\\epsilon} \\).","title":"Compiler from any intersection cardinality test with secret-shared output and \\( O(\\lambda\\ell \\,\\mathrm{poly}(t)) \\) communication to one with \\( O(\\lambda^2 \\ell t \\,\\mathrm{polylog}\\, t) \\), passive security","type":"result","venue":"PKC 2023","year":2023,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2023-GS-PICT-COMPILER"},{"evidence":"fulltext_checked","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2024-VCE-ANALYTICAL-COST-COMPARISON","keywords":["atomic-result","sok","operation-count","elliptic-curve-elgamal","oblivious-transfer","ole","collusion-resistance","state-of-the-art","use-cases"],"metadata":{"claim_slug":"analytical-cost-comparison","contribution_kind":"research_result","dossier_type":"contribution","evidence":"fulltext_checked","facet_status":"normalized","facets":{"adversary":["semi_honest","augmented_semi_honest"],"output":["intersection"],"parties":["multiparty"]},"historical_context":{"narrative":"Each new multiparty PSI paper had compared itself with a few recent protocols, so older designs based on homomorphic encryption dropped out of view on the strength of measurements taken in other settings. Vos, Conti, and Erkin put the semi-honest, collusion-resistant protocols on one footing. They instantiate the homomorphic designs with elliptic-curve ElGamal and count curve multiplications per element, and they express the protocols built from two-party subprotocols in calls to oblivious transfer, programmable OPRFs, and oblivious linear evaluation. Counted this way, older homomorphic designs become competitive with recent proposals, and the cheapest choice changes with the collusion bound, the error an application tolerates, and the computing power of the assistants. The authors conclude that no single protocol is the state of the art.","prior_boundary":"Each new multiparty PSI paper compared itself with a small subset of earlier protocols, and older works received less attention because of performance comparisons that do not cover all points of comparison (abstract, p. 1; §1, p. 2). The surveyed papers report experiments on different hardware, which the authors caution against comparing directly (§5.1.1, p. 10; §7, p. 12).","significance_at_publication":"The counts show that Hazay and Venkitasubramaniam is cheaper for weak assistants, Cheon et al. for equal computing power and small t, and Vos et al. for t close to n with a tolerated error (§7.1, p. 13), and the authors conclude that it is not possible to name one state-of-the-art protocol and that direct comparisons cannot be conclusive until realistic parameters are known (§8, p. 13; §9, p. 14).","technical_delta":"The authors derive or collect asymptotic communication, computation, and rounds for every protocol without known attacks (Table 2, p. 10; Appendix A, pp. 16-17), and worst-case counts of elliptic-curve multiplications for the PHE-based protocols of Sang and Shen, Cheon et al., Hazay and Venkitasubramaniam, and Vos et al., and of OT, OPPRF, or OLE calls for those of Inbar et al., Kolesnikov et al., Chandran et al., Kavousi et al., Nevo et al., Ghosh and Nilges, and Gordon et al. (§7.1-7.2, pp. 12-13; Table 3, p. 13; Appendix B, pp. 17-18), modifying some protocols so that only the leader receives the output."},"historical_context_status":"source_explicit","id":"PSI-CONTRIB-2024-VCE-ANALYTICAL-COST-COMPARISON","keywords":["sok","operation-count","elliptic-curve-elgamal","oblivious-transfer","ole","collusion-resistance","state-of-the-art","use-cases"],"limitations":["counts of primitive operations, not measured running times or communication; no implementation is evaluated, which the meta-review lists as a concern (§7, p. 12; Appendix C.4, p. 19)","no count is given for Garimella et al., whose multiparty analysis was deferred to an unavailable full version (Table 3, p. 13; Appendix B.2, p. 18)","OT, OPPRF, and OLE counts are not converted into elliptic-curve operations, so the two families are compared separately (§7, p. 12)"],"paper_id":"PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE","qualifiers":["semi-honest protocols with collusion resistance t and output to the leader only; server-aided protocols are excluded (§1, p. 2; Definition 1, p. 2)","PHE-based protocols are counted with elliptic-curve ElGamal, with precomputed multiplications taken as four times cheaper and homomorphic additions ignored (§7.1, pp. 12-13; Appendix B.1, p. 17)","2PC-based protocols are counted in sub-protocol calls, with one OPPRF as one OPRF and one OPRF as about 3.5 OTs; OLEs are counted separately, and setup such as base OTs is ignored (§7.2, p. 13; Appendix B.2, p. 18)","where possible, protocols are analyzed in the augmented semi-honest model (§7.2, p. 13)","some protocols are modified before counting, for example so that only the leader receives the output (Appendix B.1, pp. 17-18)"],"source_locator":{"dossier_section":"PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE § Contributions","primary_source":"Abstract (p. 1); §1 (pp. 1-2); Table 2 (p. 10); §7 with Table 3 and the use-case table (pp. 12-13); §8 (pp. 13-14); §9 (p. 14); Appendix A (pp. 16-17); Appendix B (pp. 17-18). IACR ePrint 2023/1777, PDF dated 2023-11-16.","primary_source_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1777","status":"section_checked"},"statement":"For semi-honest multiparty PSI protocols that resist t colluding parties and give the output to a leader, the latest homomorphic-encryption-based protocols are instantiated with elliptic-curve ElGamal and costed in elliptic-curve multiplications per element, and protocols built from two-party subprotocols are costed in calls to OT, OPPRF (counted as 3.5 OTs), or OLE, for the leader, an assistant, and in total, in the worst case; derived asymptotic communication, computation, and round counts accompany them. On these counts the cheapest protocol changes with the assistants' computing power, the collusion bound t, the tolerated error, and the number of parties, older homomorphic-encryption protocols instantiated with modern cryptosystems compete with recent proposals, and no single protocol is the state of the art.","statement_status":"source_normalized_statement","status":"published","title":"Worst-case operation counts per element for semi-honest collusion-resistant multiparty PSI protocols, showing that the cheapest protocol depends on the setting","work_id":"PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE","year":2024},"primaryUrl":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1777","sections":[{"content":"Analytical cost comparison of multiparty PSI","heading":"Overview"},{"content":"Table 3 (p. 13) gives, per element, the worst-case elliptic-curve multiplications of the leader, an assistant, and in total for four PHE-based protocols, and the OTs or OLEs for seven 2PC-based protocols; for example, Kolesnikov et al. need \\( 7(n - 1)k \\) OTs at the leader and \\( 3.5(n + 1)(n - 1)k \\) in total, and Nevo et al. \\( 3.5tk \\) at the leader (Table 3, p. 13; Appendix B.2, p. 18). Three use cases with assumed values of n, t, set size, and error (mutual customers, common IP addresses, consensus voting) are matched to protocols (§7.3, p. 13). The authors identify improvements in oblivious key-value stores and homomorphic cryptosystems as the most impactful technical advances (§8, p. 13).","heading":"Exact contribution"},{"content":"PKE-PAPER-1985-ELGAMAL: the PHE-based protocols are instantiated with elliptic-curve ElGamal for the count (§7.1, p. 12; Appendix B.1, p. 17).","heading":"Related records in other topics"}],"slug":"vce24-operation-counts-for-semi","status":"published","subtitle":"SoK: Collusion-resistant Multi-party Private Set Intersections in the Semi-honest Model","summary":"For semi-honest multiparty PSI protocols that resist t colluding parties and give the output to a leader, the latest homomorphic-encryption-based protocols are instantiated with elliptic-curve ElGamal and costed in elliptic-curve multiplications per element, and protocols built from two-party subprotocols are costed in calls to OT, OPPRF (counted as 3.5 OTs), or OLE, for the leader, an assistant, and in total, in the worst case; derived asymptotic communication, computation, and round counts accompany them. On these counts the cheapest protocol changes with the assistants' computing power, the collusion bound t, the tolerated error, and the number of parties, older homomorphic-encryption protocols instantiated with modern cryptosystems compete with recent proposals, and no single protocol is the state of the art.","title":"Worst-case operation counts per element for semi-honest collusion-resistant multiparty PSI protocols, showing that the cheapest protocol depends on the setting","type":"result","venue":"IEEE S&P 2024","year":2024,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#PSI-CONTRIB-2024-VCE-ANALYTICAL-COST-COMPARISON"},{"evidence":"registry","id":"ASM-DCR","keywords":["factoring","standard"],"metadata":{"aliases":["Decisional Composite Residuosity","composite_residuosity"],"falsifiable":true,"family":"factoring","id":"ASM-DCR","implied_by":[],"implied_by_records":[],"implies":["ASM-FACTORING"],"implies_records":[{"id":"ASM-FACTORING","name":"Integer factorization (factoring)"}],"introduced_in":{"citation":"Pascal Paillier, Public-Key Cryptosystems Based on Composite Degree Residuosity Classes, EUROCRYPT 1999","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-48910-X_16"},"kind":"standard","name":"Decisional composite residuosity (DCR)","post_quantum":false,"references":[{"citation":"Pascal Paillier, Public-Key Cryptosystems Based on Composite Degree Residuosity Classes, EUROCRYPT 1999","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-48910-X_16"}],"status":"As of 2026-10-10 no way to decide composite residuosity is known other than factoring the modulus; it is broken in quantum polynomial time by Shor's algorithm."},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-48910-X_16","sections":[{"content":"Let \\( N = pq \\) be an RSA modulus. An element \\( z \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{N^2}^* \\) is\nan \\( N \\)-th residue if \\( z = y^{N} \\bmod N^2 \\) for some\n\\( y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{N^2}^* \\). The decisional composite residuosity assumption\nstates that, given \\( N \\), no probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm\ndistinguishes a uniformly random \\( N \\)-th residue modulo \\( N^2 \\) from a\nuniformly random element of \\( \\mathbb{Z}_{N^2}^* \\) with non-negligible\nadvantage.","heading":"Definition"},{"content":"Factoring \\( N \\) breaks DCR, and no other attack is known. DCR gives\nsemantic security of Paillier encryption (`[Paillier99]`), which is\nadditively homomorphic, and is the encryption assumption in Paillier-based\ntwo-party and threshold ECDSA (`[Lindell17]`, `[GG18]`,\n`[CGGMP20]`) and in the LWE-to-Paillier compression of\n`[ADK26]`.","heading":"Status"},{"content":"DCR implies factoring; the converse is open. Paillier (1999)\nalso defines the computational composite residuosity class problem and shows it\nis no harder than RSA with exponent \\( N \\). DCR and quadratic residuosity are not known to be related.","heading":"Relations"}],"slug":"dcr","status":"registry","subtitle":"factoring","summary":"Let \\( N = pq \\) be an RSA modulus.","tags":["factoring","standard"],"title":"Decisional composite residuosity (DCR)","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#ASM-DCR"},{"evidence":"registry","id":"ASM-DDH","keywords":["group","standard"],"metadata":{"aliases":["Decisional Diffie-Hellman","decisional Diffie-Hellman","Decisional Diffie–Hellman","ddh"],"falsifiable":true,"family":"group","id":"ASM-DDH","implied_by":["ASM-SXDH"],"implied_by_records":[{"id":"ASM-SXDH","name":"Symmetric external Diffie-Hellman (SXDH)"}],"implies":["ASM-CDH","ASM-DLIN","ASM-KLIN"],"implies_records":[{"id":"ASM-CDH","name":"Computational Diffie-Hellman (CDH)"},{"id":"ASM-DLIN","name":"Decision linear (DLIN)"},{"id":"ASM-KLIN","name":"k-linear assumption (k-Lin)"}],"introduced_in":{"citation":"Stefan Brands, An Efficient Off-line Electronic Cash System Based on the Representation Problem, CWI Technical Report CS-R9323, 1993","url":"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/5303"},"kind":"standard","name":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman (DDH)","post_quantum":false,"references":[{"citation":"Dan Boneh, The Decision Diffie-Hellman Problem, ANTS-III 1998","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0054851"},{"citation":"Stefan Brands, An Efficient Off-line Electronic Cash System Based on the Representation Problem, CWI Technical Report CS-R9323, 1993","url":"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/5303"},{"citation":"Antoine Joux and Kim Nguyen, Separating Decision Diffie-Hellman from Computational Diffie-Hellman in Cryptographic Groups, J. Cryptology 16, 2003","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00145-003-0052-4"},{"citation":"Alex Escala, Gottfried Herold, Eike Kiltz, Carla Ràfols, Jorge Villar, An Algebraic Framework for Diffie-Hellman Assumptions, CRYPTO 2013","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-40084-1_8"}],"status":"As of 2026-10-10 DDH is believed hard in prime-order elliptic-curve groups without an efficient pairing and in prime-order subgroups of finite fields; it is false in groups with a symmetric pairing and in full groups \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p^* \\), and it is broken in quantum polynomial time by Shor's algorithm."},"primaryUrl":"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/5303","sections":[{"content":"Let \\( \\mathbb{G} = \\langle g \\rangle \\) be a cyclic group of prime order\n\\( p \\). The DDH assumption states that no probabilistic polynomial-time\nalgorithm distinguishes\n\n\\[\n(g, g^{a}, g^{b}, g^{ab}) \\quad \\text{from} \\quad (g, g^{a}, g^{b}, g^{c})\n\\]\n\nfor uniform \\( a, b, c \\in \\mathbb{Z}_p \\) with non-negligible advantage. In the\nmatrix Diffie-Hellman framework DDH is the case \\( k = 1 \\) of k-Lin.","heading":"Definition"},{"content":"DDH fails whenever a group has extra structure that tests the relation: in the\nfull group \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p^* \\) the Legendre symbol leaks whether exponents are\neven, so DDH is used in prime-order subgroups; in groups with an efficiently\ncomputable symmetric pairing \\( e(g^a, g^b) = e(g, g^{ab}) \\) tests the tuple,\nso DDH is easy while CDH may remain hard (Joux and Nguyen 2003). In\nprime-order elliptic-curve groups without an efficient pairing, and in the\nsource groups of asymmetric (Type III) pairings, no attack better than\ncomputing discrete logarithms is known. Boneh's 1998 survey credits Brands\n(1993) with observing that earlier undeniable-signature proposals implicitly\nrelied on DDH.","heading":"Status"},{"content":"DDH implies CDH and hence DL. It implies k-Lin for every \\( k \\ge 1 \\)\nin the same group, in particular DLIN; the converse fails\nin groups with symmetric pairings, where DLIN is believed to hold but DDH does\nnot. SXDH is DDH in both source groups of an asymmetric pairing.\nElGamal (`[ElGamal85]`) and Cramer-Shoup (`[CS98]`)\nencryption, TreePIR (`[LP23b]`) and the Glacius threshold\nSchnorr signature (`[BDLR24]`) rely on DDH.","heading":"Relations"}],"slug":"ddh","status":"registry","subtitle":"group","summary":"Let \\( \\mathbb{G} = \\langle g \\rangle \\) be a cyclic group of prime order \\( p \\).","tags":["group","standard"],"title":"Decisional Diffie-Hellman (DDH)","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#ASM-DDH"},{"evidence":"registry","id":"ASM-LWE","keywords":["lattice","standard"],"metadata":{"aliases":["Learning With Errors","Learning With Errors (LWE)","LWE","lwe"],"falsifiable":true,"family":"lattice","id":"ASM-LWE","implied_by":["ASM-GAPSVP","ASM-SUCCINCT-LWE"],"implied_by_records":[{"id":"ASM-GAPSVP","name":"Worst-case approximate lattice problems (GapSVP and SIVP)"},{"id":"ASM-SUCCINCT-LWE","name":"Succinct learning with errors (succinct LWE)"}],"implies":["ASM-SIS","ASM-AGCD"],"implies_records":[{"id":"ASM-SIS","name":"Short integer solution (SIS)"},{"id":"ASM-AGCD","name":"Approximate greatest common divisor (AGCD)"}],"introduced_in":{"citation":"Oded Regev, On Lattices, Learning with Errors, Random Linear Codes, and Cryptography, STOC 2005; J. ACM 56(6), 2009","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1060590.1060603"},"kind":"standard","name":"Learning with errors (LWE)","post_quantum":true,"references":[{"citation":"Oded Regev, On Lattices, Learning with Errors, Random Linear Codes, and Cryptography, J. ACM 56(6), 2009","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1568318.1568324"},{"citation":"Chris Peikert, Public-Key Cryptosystems from the Worst-Case Shortest Vector Problem, STOC 2009","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1536414.1536461"},{"citation":"Zvika Brakerski, Adeline Langlois, Chris Peikert, Oded Regev, Damien Stehlé, Classical Hardness of Learning with Errors, STOC 2013","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2488608.2488680"},{"citation":"Sanjeev Arora and Rong Ge, New Algorithms for Learning in Presence of Errors, ICALP 2011","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-22006-7_34"},{"citation":"Martin R. Albrecht, Rachel Player, Sam Scott, On the Concrete Hardness of Learning with Errors, J. Math. Cryptol. 9(3), 2015","url":"https://doi.org/10.1515/jmc-2015-0016"},{"citation":"Yilei Chen, Quantum Algorithms for Lattice Problems, IACR ePrint 2024/555 (claim withdrawn, April 2024)","url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/555"}],"status":"As of 2026-10-10 no classical or quantum polynomial-time algorithm is known for LWE with the parameters covered by the worst-case reductions; a claimed polynomial-time quantum algorithm (Chen, April 2024) was withdrawn by its author after a bug was found."},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1060590.1060603","sections":[{"content":"Fix a dimension \\( n \\), a modulus \\( q \\), a number of samples \\( m \\), and an\nerror distribution \\( \\chi \\) over \\( \\mathbb{Z} \\), typically a discrete\nGaussian of width \\( \\alpha q \\) with \\( \\alpha q > 2\\sqrt{n} \\). Sample\n\\( A \\leftarrow \\mathbb{Z}_q^{m \\times n} \\), a secret\n\\( s \\leftarrow \\mathbb{Z}_q^{n} \\) (uniform, or from \\( \\chi^n \\) in the\nequivalent \"normal form\"), and \\( e \\leftarrow \\chi^{m} \\). Set\n\n\\[\nb = A s + e \\bmod q .\n\\]\n\nSearch LWE asks to recover \\( s \\) from \\( (A, b) \\). Decision LWE asks to\ndistinguish \\( (A, b) \\) from \\( (A, u) \\) with \\( u \\) uniform in\n\\( \\mathbb{Z}_q^{m} \\). For polynomial \\( q \\) the two versions are equivalent\nup to polynomial factors.","heading":"Definition"},{"content":"$28","heading":"Status"},{"content":"Worst-case hardness of GapSVP and SIVP implies LWE hardness (quantumly in\ngeneral; classically for the moduli above). An algorithm for SIS on\nthe transpose of \\( A \\) with norm bound \\( \\beta \\) such that\n\\( \\beta \\alpha q \\ll q \\) distinguishes LWE samples from uniform, so LWE with\nmatching parameters implies SIS.\n\nRing-LWE (ASM-RLWE) and Module-LWE are structured variants over\npolynomial rings; LWE is the rank-\\( n \\), degree-1 case of Module-LWE.\nCheon and Stehlé (2015) reduce LWE to a variant of the approximate GCD problem. Succinct LWE gives the adversary extra trapdoor\ninformation and therefore implies LWE. Evasive LWE is a\nconditional assumption about LWE samples together with preimages and is not\nimplied by LWE.","heading":"Relations"}],"slug":"lwe","status":"registry","subtitle":"lattice","summary":"Fix a dimension \\( n \\), a modulus \\( q \\), a number of samples \\( m \\), and an error distribution \\( \\chi \\) over \\( \\mathbb{Z} \\), typically a discrete Gaussian of width \\( \\alpha q \\) with \\( \\alpha q > 2\\sqrt{n} \\).","tags":["lattice","standard"],"title":"Learning with errors (LWE)","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#ASM-LWE"},{"evidence":"registry","id":"ASM-PRF","keywords":["symmetric","standard"],"metadata":{"aliases":["Pseudorandom functions","pseudorandom-function"],"falsifiable":true,"family":"symmetric","id":"ASM-PRF","implied_by":["ASM-PRG","ASM-AES-PRP","ASM-CHACHA20"],"implied_by_records":[{"id":"ASM-PRG","name":"Pseudorandom generators (PRG)"},{"id":"ASM-AES-PRP","name":"AES as a pseudorandom permutation (AES PRP)"},{"id":"ASM-CHACHA20","name":"ChaCha20 block function as a PRF (ChaCha20)"}],"implies":["ASM-PRG"],"implies_records":[{"id":"ASM-PRG","name":"Pseudorandom generators (PRG)"}],"introduced_in":{"citation":"Oded Goldreich, Shafi Goldwasser, Silvio Micali, How to Construct Random Functions, J. ACM 33(4), 1986 (FOCS 1984)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/6490.6503"},"kind":"standard","name":"Pseudorandom functions (PRF)","post_quantum":true,"references":[{"citation":"Oded Goldreich, Shafi Goldwasser, Silvio Micali, How to Construct Random Functions, J. ACM 33(4), 1986","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/6490.6503"},{"citation":"Johan Håstad, Russell Impagliazzo, Leonid A. Levin, Michael Luby, A Pseudorandom Generator from any One-way Function, SIAM J. Comput. 28(4), 1999","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/S0097539793244708"},{"citation":"Mark Zhandry, How to Construct Quantum Random Functions, FOCS 2012; J. ACM 2021","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/FOCS.2012.37"}],"status":"As of 2026-10-10 PRFs exist if and only if one-way functions exist, the Goldreich-Goldwasser-Micali construction is secure against quantum queries, and practical instantiations (AES, ChaCha20, SHA-2/SHA-3-based PRFs) have no known attacks on full rounds."},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1145/6490.6503","sections":[{"content":"A keyed function family \\( F : \\{0,1\\}^{\\lambda} \\times \\{0,1\\}^{n} \\to\n\\{0,1\\}^{m} \\) is a pseudorandom function if, for a uniformly random key\n\\( k \\), no probabilistic polynomial-time adversary with oracle access to\n\\( F(k, \\cdot) \\) distinguishes it from a uniformly random function\n\\( \\{0,1\\}^n \\to \\{0,1\\}^m \\) with non-negligible advantage. Quantum-secure\nPRFs require indistinguishability even when the adversary queries the oracle\nin superposition.\n\nAs used in topic results, the assumption is that some efficient PRF exists,\ntypically instantiated with a block cipher, a stream-cipher core, or a hash\nfunction in a keyed mode.","heading":"Definition"},{"content":"Goldreich, Goldwasser and Micali (1984) built PRFs from any length-doubling\npseudorandom generator (ASM-PRG); with Håstad, Impagliazzo, Levin and Luby\n(1999) this shows PRFs exist if and only if one-way functions exist. Zhandry\n(2012) proved the GGM construction secure against quantum superposition\nqueries when the underlying generator is quantum-secure. For concrete\ninstantiations, see AES and ChaCha20.","heading":"Status"},{"content":"PRFs and PRGs imply each other. Concrete primitives modelled as PRFs (AES via\nthe PRP/PRF switching lemma, the ChaCha20 block function) imply that PRFs\nexist. PRFs are the assumption behind client-preprocessing PIR such as\n`[ZPSZ24]` and `[LP24]`, behind the\nsymmetric-key pseudorandom correlation functions of `[IKR26]`,\nand behind the PRF and PRF_msg components of SPHINCS+ (`[HKB+19]`).","heading":"Relations"}],"slug":"prf","status":"registry","subtitle":"symmetric","summary":"A keyed function family \\( F : \\{0,1\\}^{\\lambda} \\times \\{0,1\\}^{n} \\to \\{0,1\\}^{m} \\) is a pseudorandom function if, for a uniformly random key \\( k \\), no probabilistic polynomial-time adversary with oracle access to \\( F(k, \\cdot) \\) distinguishes it from a uniformly random function \\( \\{0,1\\}^n \\to \\{0,1\\}^m \\) with non-negligible advantage.","tags":["symmetric","standard"],"title":"Pseudorandom functions (PRF)","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#ASM-PRF"},{"evidence":"registry","id":"ASM-PRG","keywords":["symmetric","standard"],"metadata":{"aliases":["Pseudorandom generator security"],"falsifiable":true,"family":"symmetric","id":"ASM-PRG","implied_by":["ASM-PRF"],"implied_by_records":[{"id":"ASM-PRF","name":"Pseudorandom functions (PRF)"}],"implies":["ASM-PRF"],"implies_records":[{"id":"ASM-PRF","name":"Pseudorandom functions (PRF)"}],"introduced_in":{"citation":"Manuel Blum and Silvio Micali, How to Generate Cryptographically Strong Sequences of Pseudo-Random Bits, SIAM J. Comput. 13(4), 1984 (FOCS 1982)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/0213053"},"kind":"standard","name":"Pseudorandom generators (PRG)","post_quantum":true,"references":[{"citation":"Manuel Blum and Silvio Micali, How to Generate Cryptographically Strong Sequences of Pseudo-Random Bits, SIAM J. Comput. 13(4), 1984","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/0213053"},{"citation":"Andrew C. Yao, Theory and Application of Trapdoor Functions, FOCS 1982","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SFCS.1982.45"},{"citation":"Johan Håstad, Russell Impagliazzo, Leonid A. Levin, Michael Luby, A Pseudorandom Generator from any One-way Function, SIAM J. Comput. 28(4), 1999","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/S0097539793244708"}],"status":"As of 2026-10-10 PRGs exist if and only if one-way functions exist, and practical instantiations (block ciphers in counter mode, stream ciphers such as ChaCha20) have no known attacks on full rounds."},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1137/0213053","sections":[{"content":"A deterministic polynomial-time function\n\\( G : \\{0,1\\}^{\\lambda} \\to \\{0,1\\}^{\\ell(\\lambda)} \\) with\n\\( \\ell(\\lambda) > \\lambda \\) is a pseudorandom generator if, for a uniformly\nrandom seed \\( s \\), no probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm distinguishes\n\\( G(s) \\) from a uniformly random \\( \\ell(\\lambda) \\)-bit string with\nnon-negligible advantage.","heading":"Definition"},{"content":"Blum and Micali (1982) and Yao (1982) introduced the notion and built\ngenerators from specific one-way permutations; Håstad, Impagliazzo, Levin and\nLuby (1999) built a PRG from any one-way function. Practical instantiations\nare block ciphers in counter mode and stream ciphers; their status is tracked\nunder AES as a pseudorandom permutation (AES PRP) and ChaCha20 block function as a PRF (ChaCha20).","heading":"Status"},{"content":"A length-doubling PRG yields a PRF by the Goldreich-Goldwasser-Micali tree\nconstruction, and a PRF yields a PRG by evaluation on distinct inputs, so the\ntwo assumptions are equivalent as existence statements (ASM-PRF). Distributed\npoint functions, the basis of two-server PIR in `[GI14]`, are\nbuilt from any PRG.","heading":"Relations"}],"slug":"prg","status":"registry","subtitle":"symmetric","summary":"A deterministic polynomial-time function \\( G : \\{0,1\\}^{\\lambda} \\to \\{0,1\\}^{\\ell(\\lambda)} \\) with \\( \\ell(\\lambda) > \\lambda \\) is a pseudorandom generator if, for a uniformly random seed \\( s \\), no probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm distinguishes \\( G(s) \\) from a uniformly random \\( \\ell(\\lambda) \\)-bit string with non-negligible advantage.","tags":["symmetric","standard"],"title":"Pseudorandom generators (PRG)","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#ASM-PRG"},{"evidence":"registry","id":"ASM-RLWE","keywords":["lattice","standard"],"metadata":{"aliases":["Ring Learning With Errors","Ring-LWE","ring-lwe","rlwe"],"falsifiable":true,"family":"lattice","id":"ASM-RLWE","implied_by":["ASM-IDEAL-SVP"],"implied_by_records":[{"id":"ASM-IDEAL-SVP","name":"Worst-case approximate SVP on ideal lattices (Ideal-SVP)"}],"implies":["ASM-RSIS"],"implies_records":[{"id":"ASM-RSIS","name":"Ring short integer solution (Ring-SIS)"}],"introduced_in":{"citation":"Vadim Lyubashevsky, Chris Peikert, Oded Regev, On Ideal Lattices and Learning with Errors over Rings, EUROCRYPT 2010; J. ACM 60(6), 2013","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-13190-5_1"},"kind":"standard","name":"Ring learning with errors (Ring-LWE)","post_quantum":true,"references":[{"citation":"Vadim Lyubashevsky, Chris Peikert, Oded Regev, On Ideal Lattices and Learning with Errors over Rings, J. ACM 60(6), 2013","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2535925"},{"citation":"Damien Stehlé, Ron Steinfeld, Keisuke Tanaka, Keita Xagawa, Efficient Public Key Encryption Based on Ideal Lattices, ASIACRYPT 2009","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-10366-7_36"},{"citation":"Yara Elias, Kristin E. Lauter, Ekin Ozman, Katherine E. Stange, Provably Weak Instances of Ring-LWE, CRYPTO 2015","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-47989-6_4"},{"citation":"Chris Peikert, How (Not) to Instantiate Ring-LWE, SCN 2016","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-44618-9_22"},{"citation":"Ronald Cramer, Léo Ducas, Benjamin Wesolowski, Short Stickelberger Class Relations and Application to Ideal-SVP, EUROCRYPT 2017","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-56620-7_12"}],"status":"As of 2026-10-10 no attack on Ring-LWE over cyclotomic rings with error distributions covered by the worst-case reduction is known to beat generic lattice reduction; known breaks apply only to non-standard rings or error distributions narrower than the reductions require."},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-13190-5_1","sections":[{"content":"Let \\( K \\) be a number field of degree \\( n \\) with ring of integers\n\\( R \\) (the standard choice is the power-of-two cyclotomic ring\n\\( R = \\mathbb{Z}[x]/(x^n + 1) \\)), let \\( q \\) be a modulus and\n\\( R_q = R / qR \\). Let \\( \\chi \\) be an error distribution on \\( R \\) (a\ndiscrete Gaussian in the canonical embedding, on \\( R \\) or on its dual\n\\( R^\\vee \\)). For a secret \\( s \\in R_q \\), a sample is\n\n\\[\n(a, \\; b = a \\cdot s + e) \\in R_q \\times R_q, \\qquad a \\leftarrow R_q,\\; e \\leftarrow \\chi .\n\\]\n\nSearch Ring-LWE asks to recover \\( s \\) from polynomially many samples.\nDecision Ring-LWE asks to distinguish such samples from uniform pairs in\n\\( R_q \\times R_q \\).","heading":"Definition"},{"content":"Lyubashevsky, Peikert and Regev (2010) gave a quantum reduction from the\nworst-case approximate shortest vector problem on ideal lattices in \\( K \\) to search Ring-LWE, and a search-to-decision reduction for\ncyclotomic rings and suitable moduli. Each sample gives \\( n \\) correlated LWE\nsamples, which is the source of the efficiency gain over plain LWE.\n\nElias, Lauter, Ozman and Stange (2015) broke decision Ring-LWE for explicit\nfamilies of non-cyclotomic fields; Peikert (2016) showed these weak instances\nuse spherical errors far narrower than the hardness theorems require, and that\ninstantiations meeting the worst-case reduction are immune to that attack.\nQuantum algorithms for Ideal-SVP with approximation factor\n\\( 2^{\\tilde O(\\sqrt n)} \\) in cyclotomic fields (Cramer, Ducas and Wesolowski\n2017) do not reach the polynomial factors behind Ring-LWE, and that paper states\nthat Ring-LWE does not appear to be directly affected.","heading":"Status"},{"content":"Worst-case Ideal-SVP hardness implies Ring-LWE by a quantum reduction. Ring-LWE\nis the rank-1 case of Module-LWE. As with LWE and SIS, a Ring-SIS\nsolver with small enough norm distinguishes Ring-LWE samples, so\nRing-LWE with matching parameters implies Ring-SIS. Hardness of plain LWE\n(ASM-LWE) is not known to imply Ring-LWE, and conversely.","heading":"Relations"}],"slug":"rlwe","status":"registry","subtitle":"lattice","summary":"Let \\( K \\) be a number field of degree \\( n \\) with ring of integers \\( R \\) (the standard choice is the power-of-two cyclotomic ring \\( R = \\mathbb{Z}[x]/(x^n + 1) \\)), let \\( q \\) be a modulus and \\( R_q = R / qR \\).","tags":["lattice","standard"],"title":"Ring learning with errors (Ring-LWE)","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#ASM-RLWE"},{"evidence":"registry","id":"ASM-ROM","keywords":["idealized_model","idealized_model"],"metadata":{"aliases":[],"falsifiable":false,"family":"idealized_model","id":"ASM-ROM","implied_by":[],"implied_by_records":[],"implies":[],"implies_records":[],"introduced_in":{"citation":"Mihir Bellare and Phillip Rogaway, Random Oracles are Practical: A Paradigm for Designing Efficient Protocols, ACM CCS 1993","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/168588.168596"},"kind":"idealized_model","name":"Random oracle model (ROM)","post_quantum":"not_applicable","references":[{"citation":"Mihir Bellare and Phillip Rogaway, Random Oracles are Practical: A Paradigm for Designing Efficient Protocols, ACM CCS 1993","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/168588.168596"},{"citation":"Ran Canetti, Oded Goldreich, Shai Halevi, The Random Oracle Methodology, Revisited, STOC 1998; J. ACM 51(4), 2004","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1008731.1008734"},{"citation":"Dan Boneh, Özgür Dagdelen, Marc Fischlin, Anja Lehmann, Christian Schaffner, Mark Zhandry, Random Oracles in a Quantum World, ASIACRYPT 2011","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-25385-0_3"}],"status":"As of 2026-10-10 the random oracle heuristic is not known to fail for any natural scheme in wide use, but there are artificial schemes that are secure in the ROM and insecure under every concrete hash function (Canetti, Goldreich, Halevi 1998)."},"primaryUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1145/168588.168596","sections":[{"content":"A proof in the random oracle model gives every party, honest or adversarial,\noracle access to a uniformly random function\n\\( H : \\{0,1\\}^* \\to \\{0,1\\}^{\\ell} \\) (or to a random function into a group or\nanother range). The reduction may observe the adversary's oracle queries and\nchoose the answers (\"programming\"), as long as the answers are distributed\nuniformly. A scheme is then deployed with \\( H \\) replaced by a concrete hash\nfunction such as SHA-2 or SHA-3. The model is a heuristic: it is not a\nhardness assumption about any concrete function.\n\nIn the quantum random oracle model (QROM) the adversary may query \\( H \\) in\nsuperposition, which is the appropriate model when the adversary is quantum\nand \\( H \\) is a public hash function.","heading":"Definition"},{"content":"Canetti, Goldreich and Halevi constructed signature and encryption schemes that\nare secure in the random oracle model but insecure for every efficiently\ncomputable instantiation of the oracle. Their schemes are artificial; no\ncomparable failure is known for natural schemes such as RSA-OAEP\n(`[BR94]`), Fiat-Shamir signatures (`[FS86]`) or the\nFujisaki-Okamoto transform (`[FO99]`) when instantiated with\nstandard hash functions. Because the instantiation claim is not a\nstatement about a fixed computational problem, it is not falsifiable in the\nusual sense.\n\nClassical ROM proofs that rewind the adversary or inspect its queries do not\nautomatically carry over to quantum adversaries; Boneh et al. (2011)\nintroduced the QROM for that reason, and many Fiat-Shamir and\nFujisaki-Okamoto results have since been re-proved there.","heading":"Status"},{"content":"The ROM is often combined with a concrete assumption (for example RSA with\nrandom oracles for OAEP, RSA assumption (RSA); or generic groups with random oracles,\nGeneric group model (GGM), including the generic bilinear group model). A security proof \"in the ROM under assumption X\" assumes both the\nidealized hash function and the hardness of X; it says nothing about security\nwith a concrete hash function beyond the heuristic.","heading":"Relations"}],"slug":"rom","status":"registry","subtitle":"idealized model","summary":"A proof in the random oracle model gives every party, honest or adversarial, oracle access to a uniformly random function \\( H : \\{0,1\\}^* \\to \\{0,1\\}^{\\ell} \\) (or to a random function into a group or another range).","tags":["idealized_model","idealized_model"],"title":"Random oracle model (ROM)","type":"assumption","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/psi-catalog.json#ASM-ROM"}],"primer":{"id":"PSI-PRIMER","keyProblems":["PSI-OP-001","PSI-OP-011","PSI-OP-005"],"sections":[{"content":"$29","heading":"What it is"},{"content":"$2a","heading":"Core notions"},{"content":"$2b","heading":"Main approaches"},{"content":"| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| \\(X\\), \\(Y\\) | Sets of the sender and of the receiver |\n| \\(n\\) | Set size; in multiparty threshold PSI, each party's set size (PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR writes \\(m\\)) |\n| \\(\\ell\\) | Number of parties in multiparty threshold PSI (PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR writes \\(n\\)) |\n| \\(t\\) | Threshold: the most elements of a set allowed outside the intersection (PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR writes \\(T\\)) |\n| \\(\\lambda\\) | Security parameter; papers on OT- and VOLE-based PSI write \\(\\kappa\\) for the computational and \\(\\lambda\\) for the statistical parameter, while PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR and PSI-PAPER-2023-GS write \\(\\lambda\\) for the computational one |","heading":"Notation"},{"content":"This topic covers two-party and multiparty PSI and functions of the\nintersection (cardinality, intersection-sum, threshold, circuit outputs,\nlabels) in five subfields: public-key-based, OT- and VOLE-based,\ncircuit-based, unbalanced and labeled, and multiparty PSI. Server-aided and\ndelegated PSI, fuzzy and updatable PSI, private set union, and PSI from trusted\nhardware are outside it; OT extension, VOLE, and garbling belong to secure\nmultiparty computation, and keyword PIR to private information retrieval. The\ncovered papers date from 1999 to 2024; later work, including maliciously\nsecure circuit-PSI, is not yet covered.","heading":"Scope of this atlas"}],"sources":["PSI-PAPER-2018-EKR","PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR","PSI-PAPER-1999-HFH","PSI-PAPER-2010-DT","PSI-PAPER-2012-HEK","PSI-PAPER-2017-KMPRT","PSI-PAPER-2017-RR-CCS","PSI-PAPER-2018-CHLR","PSI-PAPER-2018-PSWW","PSI-PAPER-2019-GS","PSI-PAPER-2019-KRSSW","PSI-PAPER-2019-PSTY","PSI-PAPER-2020-ION","PSI-PAPER-2020-PRTY","PSI-PAPER-2021-BMRR","PSI-PAPER-2021-CONG","PSI-PAPER-2021-NTY","PSI-PAPER-2021-RS","PSI-PAPER-2021-RT","PSI-PAPER-2022-RR","PSI-PAPER-2023-GS","PSI-PAPER-2024-VCE"],"startHere":["PSI-PAPER-2018-EKR","PSI-PAPER-2004-FNP","PSI-PAPER-2018-PSZ","PSI-PAPER-2016-KKRT","PSI-PAPER-2021-GPRTY","PSI-PAPER-2017-CLR"],"summary":"Protocols that let parties holding private sets learn the intersection of those sets, or a function of it, without revealing anything else about the items outside it.","title":"Private set intersection"},"propertyAssertions":[{"dimension":"parties","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-ADVANCED.md","id":"PSI-PROP-0001292EDDF896","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-ADVANCED","value":"two_party"},{"dimension":"output","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-NO-STASH.md","id":"PSI-PROP-0036C37565671A","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-PSTY-NO-STASH","value":"circuit"},{"dimension":"technique","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-SD.md","id":"PSI-PROP-0068A390CF4B24","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2022-RR-SD","value":"batched_oprf"},{"dimension":"primitive","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-BF.md","id":"PSI-PROP-006D53AB8BD14B","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-BF","value":"PSI cardinality and intersection-sum"},{"dimension":"security_model","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-GS-FHE.md","id":"PSI-PROP-010E35C16E4971","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2019-GS-FHE","value":"not_reported"},{"dimension":"setup","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-MAL-LEAKAGE.md","id":"PSI-PROP-012BCE520EA43A","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-MAL-LEAKAGE","value":"zero-knowledge proof that all OPRF answers use the same key (Figure 8, step 1b, p. 25), replaceable by aborting on degenerate answers (remarks, p. 26); jointly sampled hash functions, since sender-chosen ones with reused pre-processing leak one bit (§7.3, p. 18); random oracles H and the label hash (Figure 8, step 1a, p. 25)"},{"dimension":"primitive","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-EC-ROM.md","id":"PSI-PROP-0133C74E79F6E2","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2017-RR-CCS-EC-ROM","value":"two-party PSI"},{"dimension":"parties","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MULTIPARTY.md","id":"PSI-PROP-014C85D181C555","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2021-GPRTY-MULTIPARTY","value":"multiparty"},{"dimension":"primitive","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-LABELED.md","id":"PSI-PROP-01BEA913298CB4","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2018-CHLR-LABELED","value":"labeled PSI"},{"dimension":"parties","evidence_ref":"knowledge/primitives/psi/schemes/PSI-CONSTRUCTION-2020-ION-ROT.md","id":"PSI-PROP-020F2633ABF787","review_status":"fulltext_checked","scope":"construction","subject_id":"PSI-CONSTRUCT